/* Machine translations of Kant, Schriften 1757–1777 (AA II, OCR), in the manner of Kemp Smith: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['aa02/kemp'] = {"physische-geographie-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's Outline and Announcement of a Course of Lectures [*eines Collegii*] in Physical Geography, together with an Appendix containing a brief consideration of the question: Whether the west winds in our regions are moist because they pass over a great sea.",["The OCR lost the initial letters of *Entwurf* and *Collegii*; both are restored silently. 'eines' before 'Collegii' is absent from the OCR and is supplied in square brackets, as the grammar requires.","*Collegium* is rendered 'course of lectures'."]],"physische-geographie-0002":["The rational taste of our enlightened age has probably become so general that we may assume there will be few to whom it would be a matter of indifference to know those curiosities of nature which the terrestrial globe also contains in other regions, regions that lie beyond the horizon of their own sight. It is likewise to be regarded as no lesser advantage that credulous wonder, the nurse of endless chimeras, has given place to cautious examination. By this we are put in a position to draw secure knowledge from well-attested testimony, without being in dan[ger] ... [text breaks off at *Ge-*]",["The OCR has lost the ends and beginnings of lines. The words restored (vermuthlich, wenige, Merkwürdigkeiten, Gegenden, geringern, Bewunderung, Prüfung) rest on the evident sense, not on the surviving letters; the construction is smoothed accordingly.","The passage breaks off at 'Ge-'. 'Danger' is a conjecture, bracketed, and the sentence is not completed; the next passage continues it.","*Kenntnisse* is rendered 'knowledge'; *Hirngespinste* as 'chimeras'."]],"physische-geographie-0003":["[...] being, instead of attaining a correct science of the curiosities of nature, lost in a world of fables.",["Continuation of the previous sentence. The opening is missing; *zu sein* is therefore left as 'being', and the governing clause cannot be reconstructed from this passage alone.","'natürn' is restored to *natürlichen*."]],"physische-geographie-0004":["The study of the earth is in the main threefold. The mathematical regards the earth as a celestial body of almost spherical shape, inhabited by living creatures, and considers its size, its figure, and the circles that must be conceived upon it. The political teaches us to know the nations, the community which men have with one another through form of government, commerce and mutual interest, and also their religion, customs and the like. Physical geography considers merely the natural constitution of the terrestrial globe and what is to be found upon it: the seas, the mainland, the mountains, rivers, the atmosphere, man, the animals, plants and minerals. All this, however, it does not treat with that completeness and philosophical exactness in the particulars which is the business of physics and natural history. It treats it with the rational curiosity of a traveller, who everywhere seeks out what is noteworthy, strange [*Sonderbare*] and beautiful, and who compares his collected observations and reflects upon his plan.",["The OCR text is mutilated at line margins; the whole is restored from the evident sense. 'Sonderbare' (OCR 'liderbare') is a doubtful reading. 'Pflanzen' and 'Vollständigkeit' are likewise restored.","*Erdbetrachtung* appears in the next passage as 'study of the earth'; *Luftkreis* is rendered 'atmosphere', *das feste Land* 'mainland'.","The last clause ('compares ... plan') is partly lost to the OCR; 'compares' (*vergleicht*) is restored and 0005 gives the end."]],"physische-geographie-0005":["[...] and reflects upon his plan.",["Fragment of the last sentence of the preceding passage; the first letter before 't' is lost, and the text is translated as it stands."]],"physische-geographie-0006":["I believe I have observed that the first two kinds of study of the earth find aids enough in themselves, by which one eager to learn is able to make progress in a manner both convenient and sufficient. | A complete and correct insight into the third, however, brings with it much labour and many hindrances. The reports that serve for this are scattered through many large works, and there is still wanting a textbook by means of which this science could be adapted to academic use. I therefore resolved, at the very beginning of my academic teaching, to present this science in special lectures, following a summary outline. This I accomplished, to the satisfaction of my hearers, in a course of half a year. Since then I have considerably extended my plan. I have drawn from all sources and sought out all available material. Besides what the works of Varenius, Buffon and Lulofs contain on the general principles of physical geography, I have gone through the most thorough descriptions of particular countries by skilled travellers, the *General History of Voyages*, the Göttingen collection of new voyages, the Hamburg and the Leipzig Magazine, the writings of the Academies of Sciences at Paris and Stockholm, and others, and out of everything that belonged to this purpose I have made a system. Of this I here give a brief outline. It will be for others to judge whether it is permissible, without detriment to the name of a scholar, to be ignorant in these matters. |",["Heavy OCR damage at line ends; restored silently where the sense is plain (e.g. 'Buffol' to Buffon, 'Stodholl' to Stockholm, 'ersten zwei' to the evident reading). The signs | stand where the source has them.","'Man wird urtheilen können' (OCR 'War wird') is rendered 'It will be for others to judge', with the impersonal *man* made explicit.","I have restated a few long sentences more shortly; 'allgemeine Historie aller Reisen' is rendered as a title, *General History of Voyages*, by judgment."]],"physische-geographie-0007":["Brief Sketch of Physical Geography.\nPreparation.",[]],"physische-geographie-0008":["The earth is briefly considered with respect to its figure, size, motion, and the circles which on account of these must be conceived upon it, yet without entering into that fullness of detail which belongs to mathematical geography. All this is briefly shown on the globe [*Globo*], and at the same time the climates [*Klimata*], the concepts of longitude and latitude, and the length of the day and the seasons of the year.",["A stray 'Pi' at the end of the OCR is dropped as noise.","'Cirkeln' is rendered 'circles'; *Begriffe* 'concepts'."]],"physische-geographie-0009":["Treatise.\nI. General Part of Physical Geography.\nFirst Chapter.\nOf the Sea.",["*Hauptstück* is rendered 'Chapter'."]],"physische-geographie-0010":["Its division into the ocean, the inland seas and lakes. Of archipelagos. Of gulfs, straits, harbours, anchorages. Of the bottom of the sea and its constitution. Of the [...] of the same, compared in different seas with one another. Of the sounding-lead and the diving-bell. Methods of bringing sunken things to the surface. Of the pressure of sea-water. Of its saltness. Different opinions as to its cause. Preparation of sea-salt. Methods of making sea-water sweet. Of its transparency,",["'Von der etc desselben': the word is lost in the OCR (perhaps *Tiefe*, 'depth'), and is left as a gap [...].","'nfblei' is read as *Senkblei*, 'sounding-lead'. This is a conjecture. 'Anferigen' is doubtful; 'anchorages' is a guess.","*Archipelagis* is kept in Latin form, and 'Meerzes' is restored to *Meersalzes*."]],"physische-geographie-0011":["[Of] its luminosity, its colour and the causes of their difference. Of its cold and warmth at different depths. Whether the ocean stands equally high in all its parts. Why the sea is not made fuller by the rivers. Whether seas and lakes have a subterranean communication. Motion of the sea through storms. How far this extends in the depth. The seas and lakes that are most restless [text breaks off at *unruhig-*]",["The first word of each sentence at the head of the OCR line ('Von') is lost and bracketed. 'Leuchten' is rendered 'luminosity' (phosphorescence).","The passage breaks off at 'unruhig-'; the rest is in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0012":["[...] are. Of the ebb and flow of the tide. Its laws and cause. Deviation from these laws. General motion of the sea. How it is otherwise determined by coasts and rocks. Of ocean currents. Of whirlpools. Their causes. Of the drift of the waters in straits. Of the Arctic Sea. Floating ice-fields. [Northern] drift-wood. Some other noteworthy things. Of reefs and sandbanks. Of inland lakes and marshes. Remarkable [lakes], such as the Cirknitz and others.",["The beginning 'sind' follows the break at 'unruhig-' of the previous passage; the missing superlative is left as a gap.","'Meerjmen' is doubtful; I read *Meerströmen* ('ocean currents') by conjecture. 'rdisches' is read as [*No*]*rdisches*, 'Nordisches', also a conjecture. 'n wie' is supplied as [*See*]*n*, 'lakes'.","*Eismeer* is rendered 'Arctic Sea'; *Cirknitzer* alludes to the intermittent Lake Cerknica."]],"physische-geographie-0013":["Second Chapter.\nHistory of the Mainland and of the Islands.",["*Das feste Land* is rendered 'mainland', as against *Inseln*."]],"physische-geographie-0014":["Of the unknown countries, which are so either wholly or in part. The mountains, ranges, the mainland and the islands, considered in a systematic concept. Of headlands, peninsulas, isthmuses. Comparative height of the most renowned mountains above the whole surface of the earth. Observations of various kinds on their summits in different parts of the world. Of the glacier or the Swiss sea of ice. Methods of measuring their height. Of natural and artificial caves and clefts. Of the structure of the mass of the earth. The *strata* of its matter, their order and position. Of the veins of ore. Of the warmth, cold and air at different depths. History of earthquakes and fire-spouting mountains over the whole terrestrial globe. Consideration of the islands, both those that are certainly recognised as such and those about which it is doubtful.",["'Landen' is read as *Landengen*, 'isthmuses'; 'Dro nung' as *Ordnung*. Both are OCR corrections.","*Strata* is kept in Latin; *Erdklumpen* is rendered 'mass of the earth'."]],"physische-geographie-0015":["Third Chapter.",[]],"physische-geographie-0016":["History of Springs and Wells. |",["The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"physische-geographie-0017":["Different hypotheses of their origin. Observations from which it can be recognised. Springs which flow periodically. Petrifying, mineral, hot and exceedingly cold springs. Of cement-water. Inflammable wells. Of petroleum and naphtha. Of the alteration, the coming into being and passing away of springs. Of the digging of wells.",["'Cement wasser' is read as *Cementwasser*, the copper-bearing water used in cementation, and rendered literally. *Petroleo* is given in its English form."]],"physische-geographie-0018":["Bl",["A stray fragment of the OCR, probably noise; given as it stands."]],"physische-geographie-0019":["Fourth Chapter.\nHistory of Rivers and Brooks.",[]],"physische-geographie-0020":["Origin of rivers. Comparison of the most noteworthy on the earth in respect of the length of their course, their swiftness, the quantity of their water; of their direction, the amount of their fall, their swelling, flooding, dikes and groynes, the most famous canals. Of waterfalls. Of rivers which dry up in the land. Of those which hide beneath the earth and come forth again. Of rivers which carry gold-sand. Method of separating it. Of the differing weight of the water of rivers.",["'Aufschwellu 8' is read as *Aufschwellung*; 'Candler 1' as *Canälen*, 'canals', which is doubtful.","*Buhnen* is rendered 'groynes'. 'Flüffem' is restored to *Flüssen*."]],"physische-geographie-0021":["Fifth Chapter.\nHistory of the Atmosphere.",["*Luftkreis* is rendered 'atmosphere', as with *Atmosphäre* in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0022":["Height of the atmosphere. Its three regions. Comparison of the properties of the air in different regions of the world, in respect of weight, dryness, moisture, healthfulness. Consideration of its properties at great heights and depths. Effect of the air on the [illegible] of the stars in different countries.",["The OCR ('auf das i i) der Sterne') is unreadable, and I have not supplied the missing word. The trailing marks '; i Hi' are dropped as noise."]],"physische-geographie-0023":["History of the Winds.",["A stray closing quotation mark in the OCR is omitted."]],"physische-geographie-0024":["Their principal and lesser causes; their division according to the regions of the world. Winds of different properties, in respect of dryness, moisture, warmth, cold and healthfulness. The trade-wind, and its general and particular laws according to the nature of the several zones. The monsoons. The alternating sea-breezes and land-breezes. The winds that prevail for the greater part of the time in a given region. The velocity of winds. Calms, storms, hurricanes, typhoons, waterspouts and cloudbursts, considered according to the regions in which they prevail, and in respect of their laws and causes. The winds at different heights above the earth compared with one another. Brief consideration of some particular occurrences of the air.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Ein: heilung = Eintheilung; Jer = der; Passatpinde = Passatwinde; Chr- Innen = Orkanen (hurricanes); Die Binde = Die Winde; Moussons, Typhons kept as Kant's forms.","The passage is a list of topics, without finite verbs; I have kept it in the form of a list, as Kant gives it."]],"physische-geographie-0025":["Sixth Chapter.",["Hauptstück rendered 'chapter'."]],"physische-geographie-0026":["On the connection of the weather with the zone | or with the seasons in different countries.",["'3on' read as 'Von'. The sign | is carried over where it stands."]],"physische-geographie-0027":["In what winter consists in the torrid zone. Why winter or summer does not occur at the same time and in the same manner in all regions of the earth that have one and the same climate. How the torrid zone comes to be habitable. An enumeration of the countries that lie under one and the same zone of the heavens and yet differ greatly in respect of warmth and cold. On the cold in the southern ocean, and its cause. On the regions of the earth's greatest heat and cold, and their causes and effects. On countries in which it never rains, and on those in which it rains almost continuously.",["OCR slips corrected: röstrichen = Erdstrichen; Grbftrid) = Erdstrich; bebohnbar = bewohnbar; egen = liegen; nd = sind; en Gegenden = den Gegenden; )rachen = Ursachen; leicher = gleicher.","Erdstrich rendered 'region of the earth' or 'zone'; Himmelsstrich 'zone of the heavens'."]],"physische-geographie-0028":["Seventh Chapter.",[]],"physische-geographie-0029":["History of the great changes which the earth formerly | underwent.",["'eschichte Dor' read as 'Geschichte der'."]],"physische-geographie-0030":["On the changes which are still continuing on it.",["A stray '^' at the start is omitted as an OCR mark."]],"physische-geographie-0031":["The effect of rivers in altering the form of the earth, shown by the examples of the Nile, the Amazon, the Mississippi and others. The effects of rain and of torrents. Whether the dry land is continually being lowered and the sea gradually raised. On the effect of the winds in altering the form of the earth. On its alteration by earthquakes. By man. Confirmation by examples. On the || continuing change of dry land into sea and of sea into dry land. Observations on this, and opinions as to its consequences. | The hypothesis of Linnaeus. Whether the motions of the earth, the daily as well as the annual, are subject to alteration.",["OCR slips corrected: rempeln = Exempeln; es Regens = des Regens; Gießbäche = Gießbäche (torrents); Missisippi kept as Mississippi.","The signs | and || are carried over where they stand."]],"physische-geographie-0032":["(b) Monuments of the changes of the earth in the most ancient times. |",["Denkmale rendered 'monuments' (i.e. surviving evidences)."]],"physische-geographie-0033":["All dry land was formerly the bed of the sea. Proofs || of this are found in the layers of shells in the earth and on high mountains, and in the sea-animals and sea-plants which are petrified or preserved as impressions in stone. Buffon's proofs from the form of the mountains. [Proof] that | the change of dry land into sea and of sea into dry land has often followed upon one another in long periods; this is proved from the *strata* which contain remains of the sea-bottom and alternate with those which enclose remains of the dry land. On subterranean forests. The position of their buried trees. How it comes that in these layers of earth remains are for the most part found of Indian animals and plants. Appraisal of the so-called sports of nature. On the stones which are properly petrified parts from the animal kingdom.",["OCR slips corrected: Beweis- || thümer = Beweisthümer; Seg | pflanzen = Seepflanzen; Über⸗ | bleibsel = Überbleibsel; robuste = Überbleibsel is not read here: 'robuste des festen Landes in sich) schließen' is taken as 'Überbleibsel des festen Landes in sich schließen'.","'Proof' supplied in brackets, since the construction 'Daß ... bewiesen' is elliptical as printed. 'Spiele der Natur' = *lusus naturae*."]],"physische-geographie-0034":["(c) Theory of the earth, or the grounds of ancient history",["A stray '9' after 'Geschichte' is omitted as an OCR mark (probably a full stop)."]],"physische-geographie-0035":["Whether a single general inundation, such as the Noachian, could have produced all these changes. General consideration of the form of the dry land [...] and of the slope of the [...] from the [...] and what they contain. || Whether the axis of the earth has formerly altered. Appraisal of the hypotheses of Woodward, Burnet, Whiston, Leibniz, [...] and others. The result drawn from the comparative appraisals.",["The OCR is corrupt at 'festen a der n und des Abhanges der Gez aus der Hm der RAD'; I have not guessed the readings and mark the gaps with [...]. Perhaps 'Landes, der Berge ... der Gegend ... aus der Höhe der Berge', but this is uncertain.","'E L|' in the list of authors is likewise unreadable and left as [...]; 'de |' read as 'der'. The signs | and || are carried over."]],"physische-geographie-0036":["Eighth Chapter.",["'Ächtes' read as 'Achtes'."]],"physische-geographie-0037":["On navigation.",["A stray 'i' after the title is omitted."]],"physische-geographie-0038":["On rhumbs, on loxodromy, on the compass-card, on the reckoning of the course and its correction. On the determination of longitude and latitude. Examination of the ground [Grund]. Other remarkable matters connected with sea—",["Lorodromie read as Loxodromie; Schiffsrose = compass-card. The sentence breaks off at 'See—' and is continued in the next passage.","'Prüfung des Grundes' is obscure (the sea-bottom, i.e. sounding, or the ground of a method?); I keep the literal rendering."]],"physische-geographie-0039":["—faring [Seefahrt]. On the most remarkable sea-voyages of ancient and modern times. On the conjecture of new lands, and the efforts to discover them.",["The passage begins with the second half of the divided word 'See-fahrt'."]],"physische-geographie-0040":["II. The Special Part of Physical Geography.",[]],"physische-geographie-0041":["(1) The animal kingdom, in which man is considered comparatively, according to the difference of his natural formation and colour in different regions of the earth; secondly, the most remarkable",["A stray '5' (line number) is omitted. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0042":["animals, those that live on land as well as those in the air and in the water, the amphibians and the most remarkable insects, are to be examined in respect both of their history and of their nature. | (2) The vegetable kingdom, in which all those plants of the earth are explained that attract attention chiefly by their strangeness or by their special utility. (3) The mineral kingdom, whose most agreeable features, and those that contribute most to human utility or pleasure, are to be gone through in",["'Seltsamkeit' rendered 'strangeness'. 'in den menschlichen s Nutzen' has a stray 's', omitted. The last sentence is cut off and is continued in the next passage ('in a historical and philosophical manner')."]],"physische-geographie-0043":["a historical and philosophical manner. | I set this forth first in the natural order of the classes",["Continuation of the preceding sentence; the construction 'durchgegangen werden' is completed here."]],"physische-geographie-0044":["and finally go through all the countries of the earth in the geographical manner of instruction, in order",["The sentence is broken off at 'um', and the purpose clause continues in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0045":["to set forth the inclinations of men which flow from the zone in which they live, the manifold variety of their prejudices and modes of thought (in so far as all this can serve to make man better acquainted with himself), a brief conception of their arts, commerce and science, a recital of the products of the countries already explained above, in their proper places,",["A stray 'b' is omitted. Handlung rendered 'commerce'. The sentence is continued in the next passage; 'an' belongs with 'an ihren gehörigen Orten'."]],"physische-geographie-0046":["the character of the air, and so on; in a word, everything that belongs to the physical study of the earth. | The whole will be summed up in written summary notes, which are to serve for the easier revision of this science, a science that by its agreeableness in any case sustains attention sufficiently. The science of which the present outline gives a sketch will be taught in this summer half-year. I shall also expound natural science in separate lectures, following the handbook of Herr Dr. Eberhard. Logic will be read according to Meier's short introduction, and metaphysics according to the guidance of Baumeister. In the past half-year, at the request of some gentlemen, I made this change to the more thorough but more difficult Baumgarten, to their satisfaction. Meanwhile there will be freedom of choice as to which of the two one expects to derive the greater advantage from. In mathematics the old lectures will be continued and new ones begun. My efforts will be fortunate enough if they win the approval of those who, though they do not constitute the largest part, yet constitute the most valuable, namely the rational.",["Restated slightly: the long sentence on the written summaries is kept as one but its relative clauses are smoothed. OCR slips: leihteren = leichteren; 'Mnweisung' = Anweisung; 'des Baumeisters' = Baumeister (Kant's text).","'Meierischen kurzen Einleitung' is evidently Meier's Auszug aus der Vernunftlehre; 'Handbuch des Herrn D. Eberhard' is left as in the German without identification of the title."]],"physische-geographie-0047":["Appendix of a brief consideration on the question:",[]],"physische-geographie-0048":["Whether the west winds in our regions are moist",["A stray '4' is omitted; the title continues in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0049":["because they pass over a great sea.",[]],"physische-geographie-0050":["If one wishes to understand the cause of those natural occurrences which depend upon the region of the heavens and the nature of the zones of the earth, one often runs the risk of seeing one's system overthrown by an unforeseen instance, unless one has first consulted compared appearances and observations of other countries. It readily occurs to everyone to ascribe the wet weather which the west winds bring us to the situation of our country, which has a great sea lying to the west. But this explanation, which seems so easy and so natural, is rendered very doubtful, if not wholly set aside, by comparison with the weather of other countries. Musschenbroek, who is otherwise inclined to the same opinion, becomes somewhat uncertain on the point when he considers that the north wind in the Netherlands is a dry wind, although it blows over the great German Ocean and even over the Northern Ocean. He ascribes its dryness to the cold of that sea. But when in summer the sun sufficiently warms this ocean, this pretext falls away, and the wind none the less remains dry. Moreover, one finds in physical [geography] still stronger grounds against the common opinion.",["The OCR 'in der physischen 3' is defective; I supply '[geography]' in brackets on the likely reading 'Geographie'. The stray numbers '25' and '20' (line numbers) are omitted.","Restated: 'Allein ... wird ... sehr zweifelhaft gemacht, wo nicht gänzlich aufgehoben' kept in one sentence; 'deutsche Meer' = German Ocean (North Sea); 'nordischer Ocean' = Northern Ocean."]],"physische-geographie-0051":["In the whole Indian Ocean, from the archipelago of the Philippines",["The sentence is continued in the next passage."]],"physische-geographie-0052":["as far as the Arabian Sea, there prevail throughout the year two alternating—",["The passage breaks off mid-word at 'Wechsel—' (probably 'Wechselwinde'); I have translated it as it stands, without completing it."]],"physische-geographie-0053":["…winds: the north-east wind from October into May, and the south-west—",["Fragment: breaks off mid-word (\"Südwest— | wind\"); the opening \"winde\" is the tail of a compound whose first part lies in the preceding passage. \"Mai\" restored for OCR \"Mal\"."]],"physische-geographie-0054":["wind from May into October. The former brings clear air with it, and the latter is the cause of the rainy months in these countries,",["OCR \"Mal\" read as \"Mai\"; \"z sich\" read as \"mit sich\"."]],"physische-geographie-0055":["although the one as well as the other blows over great seas. In the",[]],"physische-geographie-0056":["Philippine Islands, in Mindanao and the rest, this becomes still",[]],"physische-geographie-0057":["more evident. The eastern monsoon comes over the almost boundless Pacific",["\"Mousson\" rendered \"monsoon\"; \"stille Meer\" as \"Pacific\"."]],"physische-geographie-0058":["Ocean and yet brings about fair weather; whereas the western alternating wind [Wechselwind], which blows over regions sown with islands and promontories,",["OCR \"Weiter\" read as \"Wetter\" (weather). Line number omitted."]],"physische-geographie-0059":["brings the rainy season with it. Kolbe states that at",[]],"physische-geographie-0060":["the Cape of Good Hope, in the western as well as in the eastern",[]],"physische-geographie-0061":["part of the region belonging to it, the east winds bring dry weather, but the west winds the wet season. Yet it is not apparent why the west wind alone should be moist, since towards the east there lies a sea as wide as that towards the west.",["Restated the long sentence as two. \"Part of the region\" renders \"dazu gehörigen Gegend\"; \"lediglich\" is rendered \"alone\", by judgment of the sense."]],"physische-geographie-0062":["In the Gulf of Mexico, on the isthmus of Panama, at Cartagena and elsewhere, as in the Indian Ocean, the N.E. and W.S.W. winds alternate",["OCR \"wedhjeln\" read as \"wechseln\"."]],"physische-geographie-0063":["through the two halves of the year. The first, which are called breezes [Brisen], are dry and make for clear air. The latter, which are called Venapalen, are moist, and with them comes the rainy season. Now the N.E. winds come over the great Atlantic Ocean and are none the less dry. The W.S.W. winds, however, cannot come from any large stretch of the Pacific, since at a moderate distance from the mainland constant east winds dominate this sea. On the voyage which the Manila galleon makes from Acapulco to",["The name \"Venapalen\" is doubtful: OCR gives \"Ven— 1 apalen\" (perhaps a corruption of a Spanish *vendavales*); I have not corrected it. \"mittels mäßigen\" read as \"mittelmäßigen\"."]],"physische-geographie-0064":["Manila, and since, in order to enjoy the east wind, it keeps not far from the equator, it finds almost constantly fair weather.",["OCR \"Aquator\" and \"Weiter\" corrected (\"Äquator\", \"Wetter\")."]],"physische-geographie-0065":["But on the voyage from Manila to Acapulco, since it steers to a certain latitude above the northern tropic, it sails to America with the help of the west winds prevailing there, and is so certain to meet frequent rains there that it does not even provide itself with water for this long voyage, and all would be lost if the rains should fail. Now let anyone who maintains the common opinion tell me a comprehensible cause why the east wind, which blows over the Pacific, and indeed in the warmest region,",["The sentence runs on into the next passage (\"…allein trocken…müsse\"); I have broken the English at the same point and not completed it. \"Wafer\" read as \"Wasser\"; \"zo\" and \"bs\" are OCR debris, and \"eine begreifliche Ursache\" is read accordingly."]],"physische-geographie-0066":["must be exclusively dry, while the west wind, which blows over the same ocean, must be moist and rainy.",["\"allein trocken\" taken as \"solely dry\" (the east wind alone), not as the conjunction \"but\"."]],"physische-geographie-0067":["It seems to me that this is more than sufficient to make doubtful, at the very least, the thought that with us the west winds borrow their moisture from the sea lying to the west. It appears rather that the west winds in all regions of the earth give rise to moist weather, although I will not deny that",["OCR \"Feudtigkeit\" read as \"Feuchtigkeit\", \"zurei chend\" as \"zureichend\"."]],"physische-geographie-0068":["the character of the regions over which they blow may often diminish this property; as happens in the southern part of Persia, where the south-west winds, which pass over the burnt regions of Arabia, bring dry and hot air with them. The narrowness of the space prevents me from explaining the cause of this property of the west winds. Might not they, since they blow against the general and natural current of the air from morning towards evening [i.e. east to west], which is explained in the fourth chapter of the *Physical Geography*, for that very reason drive the vapours together and condense them, so that the air may always be filled [with them]? At least, if one regards the air as a solvent (*menstruum*) of the moisture on the earth, it is not enough, when one wishes to explain why it lets this fall, that is, why it rains, to assume it filled with moisture to saturation. One must indicate a cause which precipitates it (*präcipitirt*), that is, which compels the air to let it go from its interstices, so that the vapours may unite and fall down.",["The stray \"ex\" before \"die Beschaffenheit\" is OCR debris and is left out; \"Feudtigkeit\" etc. silently corrected. The reading \"erfüllt it\" is doubtful; I take it as \"erfüllt ist/sei\" and supply \"[with them]\". The bracketed \"[i.e. east to west]\" is my gloss on \"von Morgen gegen Abend\". Restated the final sentence in two. Line numbers omitted."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's\nNew Doctrine [Lehrbegriff]\nof\nMotion and Rest\nand\nof the Consequences connected therewith\nin the First |\nPrinciples of Natural Science,\nwhereby at the same time his\nlectures for this half-year are announced.\nThe 1st of April 1758.",["The sign | is carried over where it stands in the German (after \"ersten\"). \"Lehrbegriff\" rendered \"doctrine\", literally the 'concept of a doctrine'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0002":["Zw",["Illegible OCR fragment (probably a page signature or catchword); left as it stands, not translated."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0003":["If in a philosophical question the unanimous judgment of the philosophers [Weltweisen] were a rampart, such that to step across it had to be held as culpable a crime as that which Remus committed, I should certainly let the presumption pass of allowing my own notions, against the decisive opinion of the venerable great multitude, that freedom which is justified by nothing further than sound reason. If it occurred to me to dispute its law, which by right of prescription has for centuries maintained undisturbed possession in the textbooks of the philosophers, I should soon tell myself that I ought either to have come sooner or to have held back. But now I see around me a great number of such enterprising minds, who will have nothing to do with the law of authority, and towards whom people nevertheless show so much indulgence as even to examine their opinions and to reflect upon them. I therefore venture, in the hope of an equally favourable fate, to mingle among them, and to examine and reject the concepts of motion and of rest, likewise of the force of inertia connected with the latter. I do so although I know that those gentlemen who are accustomed to throw away as chaff all thoughts that have not been poured upon the compulsory mill of the Wolffian or some other celebrated system, will at first sight declare the trouble of examination unnecessary and the whole inquiry mistaken.",["Restated the two very long sentences, the second and third, as shorter ones; the last was divided at \"ob ich gleich weiß\"; \"Betrachtung\" rendered \"inquiry\". OCR \"Funangefochtenen\" read as \"unangefochtenen\", \"Sahrhunderten\" as \"Jahrhunderten\", \"ie\" as \"sie\".","\"Weltweisen\" (lit. 'world-wise') rendered \"philosophers\". \"Wolffischen\" is the system of Christian Wolff."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0004":["New Concepts of Motion and Rest.",["Running-head-like heading; stray apostrophe in OCR \"Begriffe'der\" removed."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0005":["I wish that my readers could for a moment put themselves into that frame of mind [Verfassung des Gemüths] which Descartes holds to be so indispens- | able for the attainment of correct insights, and in which I now find myself: namely, for as long as this inquiry lasts, to make oneself forget all learned concepts, and to set out on the way to truth with no other guide than bare sound reason itself.",["\"Cartes\" is Kant's form of Descartes; given in the usual English form. The sign | is carried over at the line-break (\"unum- | gänglich\"), so the English word is divided at the corresponding point.","\"Gemüth\" rendered \"mind\" within \"frame of mind\". \"Betrachtung\" rendered \"inquiry\" as above."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0006":["In this position I recognise that motion is the change of place. But I also soon grasp that the place of a thing is known through its situation, that is, through its position or its outer relation to other things which surround it. Now I can consider a body in relation to certain outer objects that lie immediately around it; and then, so long as it does not alter this relation, I shall say that it is at rest. But as soon as I regard it in relation to a sphere of wider compass, it is possible that this same body, together with the objects near it, changes its position with respect to that wider sphere; and from this point of view I shall ascribe a motion to it. Now I am free to extend my field of view as far as I please, and to consider my body in relation to ever more remote circles; and I grasp that my judgment of the motion and rest of this body can never be permanent, but can always change as new prospects open. Suppose, for example, that I am in a ship lying at anchor in the roads on the Pregel. I have a ball lying before me on the table; I consider it with respect to the table, the walls and the other parts of the ship, and say that it is at rest. Soon afterwards I look from the ship towards the shore and notice that the rope with which it was made fast has been untied, and that the ship is slowly drifting down the stream; I say at once: the ball is moving, and moving from east to west in the direction of the river. Someone says",["The sentence 'Ich begreife aber auch bald…' is kept whole but 'Nun kann ich … Nun stehts mir frei' are kept as separate sentences as in the German; the long sentence on 'Sphäre von weiterem Umfange' is divided at the semicolon for clarity.","'Stellung' and 'Lage' rendered 'position' and 'situation'; 'Ort' = place. 'Gegenstände' = objects. 'Morgen gegen Abend' rendered 'east to west' (literally morning to evening).","OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. 'des- i selben', 'Tau, womit eà', 'w', 'ss'); running head 'Neuer Lehrbegriff der Bewegung und Ruhe' and page number 17 omitted. The passage breaks off at 'Jemand sagt', continued in the next passage.","'Rhede' = roads (roadstead); 'Pregel' is the river at Königsberg."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0007":["[to] me, however, that the earth turns in its daily motion, with much greater speed, from west to east; at once I change my mind and ascribe to the ball a quite opposite motion, with a speed that is easily determined from astronomy. But I am reminded that the whole globe of the earth, with respect to the planetary system, is in a still",["The passage begins mid-word ('ir aber' for '[Jemand sagt m]ir aber'); the supplied '[to] me' is bracketed. The sentence breaks off at 'in einer noch', continued in the next passage.","'Abend gegen Morgen' rendered 'west to east'. 'An— sehung' OCR hyphenation corrected; 'Sternenwissenschaft' = astronomy; 'Planetengebäude' = planetary system."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0008":["swifter motion from west to east. I am compelled to ascribe this also to my ball, and I alter the speed that I previously gave it. Finally Bradley teaches me that the whole planetary system together with the sun probably undergoes a displacement with respect to the heaven of the fixed stars. I ask: in which direction and with what speed? No answer is given me. And now I become giddy; I no longer know whether my ball is at rest or in motion, whither it moves, and with what speed. Now I begin to see that something is lacking to me in the expression of motion and rest. I must never use it in an absolute sense, but always *respective* [relatively]. I must never say that a body is at rest without adding with respect to what things it is at rest, and never say that it moves without at the same time naming the objects with respect to which it alters its relation. Even if I wished to imagine a mathematical space, empty of all creatures, as a receptacle of bodies, this would still be of no help to me. For by what means am I to distinguish its parts and the different places that are occupied by nothing corporeal? Now I assume two bodies, of which one, B, is at rest with respect to all the objects nearest known to me, while the other, A, approaches it with a determinate speed. However unchanged the relation in which the ball B may persist to other outer objects, it is not so persisting when it is considered with respect to the moving ball A. For its relation is reciprocal, and so therefore is the change of it. The ball B, which is called at rest with respect to certain objects, takes an equal share in the change of the reciprocal relations with the ball A; they both come nearer to one another. Why then, in spite of all the custom of language, should I not say: The ball B, which is indeed at rest with respect to other outer objects, is nevertheless in uniform motion with respect to the moving ball A? |",["The passage opens mid-sentence; 'swifter' completes 'noch schnellern' (OCR 'chnellern'). OCR slips corrected silently: 'beizu-B', 'Firfternen', '8egenstände', 'einimber', 'id)', 'pan der Sprache' read as 'Gewohnheit'? — the text reads 'trotz allem [?] der Sprache'; the word before 'der Sprache' is corrupt in the OCR, and I have rendered 'custom of language' as a conjecture.","'in absolutem Verstande' = 'in an absolute sense'; 'respective' is Kant's own Latinate adverb, kept in italics with a bracketed gloss.","'Verhältniß' and 'Beziehung' both rendered 'relation'; 'Relationen' also 'relations'. The sign | marks the Academy page break and is retained."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0009":["You will concede to me that when the discussion is of the effect which the two bodies exert upon one another in collision, relation to other outer things has nothing to do with the matter. If, then, one must consider the change that takes place here solely with respect to the two bodies A and B, and withdraws one's thoughts from all outer objects, let someone tell me whether one could infer, from | what goes on between the two, that one of them is at rest and only the other moves, and which of them is at rest or in motion. Will not motion have to be ascribed to both, and to both in equal measure? Their approach towards one another belongs as much to the one as to the other. Suppose that a ball A of 3 lb. mass moves towards another, B, of 2 lb., which is at rest with respect to the surrounding space; the space of 5 feet that lay between the two is traversed in one second. If then I look solely at the change that takes place between the two bodies, I can say nothing more than this: 3 lb. of mass and 2 lb. of mass come nearer to one another by 5 feet in one second. Since I now have not the slightest ground for ascribing to one of these bodies a greater share in this change than to the other, I shall, in order to obtain complete equality on both sides, have to distribute the speed of 5 feet in a second in inverse proportion to the masses; that is, the body of 3 lb. will receive 2 degrees of speed as its share, but that of 2 lb. 3 degrees, and with these forces they will actually act upon one another in the impact. Notwithstanding all the rest in which the body B may be with respect to the other nearest objects of space, it has nevertheless a true motion with respect to every body that approaches it, and indeed a motion equal to that of the other; so that the sum of the two motions is equal to that which would have to be thought in the body A alone, if B were represented as in absolute rest.",["The sign | (Academy page break) is retained. OCR slips corrected silently ('iq', 'ii', 'om', 'Wi', '2 4', '2 d.', '3 u', 'm', 'id'); 'Nr- sache' read as 'Ursache'.","Mass units read as 'u'/'d' in the OCR for '℔' (Pfund); rendered 'lb.' as a conjecture. 'Geschwindigkeit … Grade' = 'speed … degrees'.","'Gehet, daß…' rendered 'Suppose that…'; the long final sentence is kept whole, as it divides awkwardly."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0010":["Should one, notwithstanding this, allow oneself to be troubled by the obstinacy of language, I would ask whether one will in fact keep to the same manner of speaking. If a twelve-pound cannon-ball is fired from morning towards evening, in the region of Paris, against a wall, even the philosopher says that it moves with a velocity of 600 feet in a second. Yet he admits that the earth at this latitude has almost the same motion from evening towards morning, and that the force of the powder has therefore done nothing other than cancel this motion of the ball. Nevertheless, and without letting himself be misled by the daily or annual motion of the earth, one secretly admits that the relations which the ball and the wall have to the space surrounding them, whether near or far, are here irrelevant, and that all that matters is the relation which these two bodies have to each other. Given such an admission, to which of the two would one then ascribe rest relatively to the other? The phenomenon of the change declares nothing but that the two are brought nearer to each other; and one must therefore rather grant that both move towards each other, the ball towards the wall and the wall towards the [ball?]. Let the space traversed between the two bodies, divided by the time, be taken as the sum of the velocities of both sides; and let one say: as the sum of the masses [A] and B is to the mass of the body A, so is the given velocity to the velocity of the body B, which, when it is subtracted from the total velocity thus conceived, leaves over the velocity of",["The text after 'die Mauer gegen die' is corrupt in the OCR ('die i'). I render 'the [ball?]' in brackets and do not complete it with certainty.","The passage from 'Man sehe nämlich' onwards is evidently a footnote or note to the sentence; the OCR does not mark it. I have restated the long opening as several sentences, and the sentence about the velocity of the body B is left broken off, as in the source (continued in the next passage).","'Massen und B' is corrected to 'masses [A] and B' on the sense of the proportion; the bracket marks my supply.","Silently corrected: 'won Paris' = von Paris, 'fondern' = sondern, 'Stope' = Stoße, and similar OCR slips."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0011":["... [the velocity of A]. Then one will have distributed the whole change that has occurred equally between the two bodies, and with these equal forces they will also meet each other in impact. For my purpose I draw from this only the following two corollaries. 1) Every body in respect of which another moves is [illegible] itself in respect of that other in [illegible] ...",["The opening 'übrig läßt' is the verb of the sentence broken off in the preceding passage ('die Geschwindigkeit von ... übrig läßt'); I have shown the lacking object as '[the velocity of A]' only as a pointer to the preceding passage, not as a restoration.","The end of the passage ('ist ! d selber ... in 1 und ed äjt also opomin') is corrupt OCR and cannot be read; I have not filled it in.","'Stope' and 'herden' corrected silently to Stoße and werden; 'wecke' to Zwecke."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0012":["On the Force of Inertia.",["'Trägheitsfraft' corrected to Trägheitskraft; Trägheitskraft = 'force of inertia' (*vis inertiae*)."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0013":["| It would perhaps never have occurred to anyone to maintain that a body which, so long as another body running against it does not touch it, is completely at rest, or, if one prefers, in equilibrium of force, should nevertheless at the moment of impact suddenly take on of itself a motion towards the impinging body, or put itself in a state of preponderance, in order to cancel an opposed force in that body. It would not have done so had it not been evident from experience that in a state | which everyone regards as the state of rest the body counteracts any acting body with an equal degree [of force]. But I have now | proved that what has been wrongly taken for a rest in respect of the impinging body is in fact, relatively to it, a | motion. It is therefore evident of itself that this force of inertia has been devised without need. In every impact there is found a motion of one body against another, which is moved against it with equal degree; and this explains quite easily and intelligibly the equality of action and reaction, without our having to devise a special kind of natural force. Nevertheless this assumed force serves with uncommon skill | to derive all the laws of motion from it very correctly and easily. But it serves for this only as the Newtonian attractive force of all matter serves for the explanation of the great motions of the structure of the world: that is, only as the law of a general appearance known through experience, of which one does not know the cause, and which one must therefore not hasten at once to ascribe to an inner natural force directed to that end.",["The OCR scrambles the line order (the clause 'welches die Gleichheit der Wirkung und Gegenwirkung ... erklärt' is interleaved with the lines that precede it). I have restored the sequence by the sense and kept the | marks approximately where they fall.","I divided the long sentence 'Nun ich aber bewiesen habe ... erklärt' into several sentences and supplied '[of force]' in brackets after 'equal degree'. 'Without need' = ohne Noth.","A stray 'E' at the end is dropped as an OCR artefact. 'Erscheinung' = 'appearance'; 'Anziehungskraft' = 'attractive force'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0014":["Without surrendering anything of the right of my system, I can very well concede in this sense that all bodies have a force of inertia in respect of those moved against them, that is, a force of counteracting an action in equal degree; for this is nothing but a law of experience. But they seem to have it as an inner force in themselves only in complete rest. In fact they have it merely because they are in real and equal motion in respect of the body running against them, and they never have it in so far as they are, relatively to it, at rest.",["The German 'allein sie scheinen nur sie in völliger? Ruhe ... zu haben' appears slightly disordered (the '?' is in the OCR); I read 'sie scheinen sie nur in völliger Ruhe ... zu haben'. A stray 'P' at the end is dropped.","'Lehrgebäude' = 'system'; 'respective' = 'relatively'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0015":["Nor can it be at all difficult to refute the assumed concept of the force of inertia on other grounds. |",["'devs' is read as 'der'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0016":["For 1) a body may have ever so many forces, yet when it is at rest they must certainly be in equilibrium in it. How then is it to come about that, as soon as the impinging body touches this resting one, the latter should suddenly put itself of its own accord into a preponderant motion or endeavour (*Bestrebung*) towards the side of the body running against it, in order to destroy in it a part of its force? For if its inner force were still in equilibrium at the moment of impact, it would offer no resistance whatever to the impact. And supposing that 2) this suddenly arisen endeavour were possible, the [striking] body itself would receive no motion from the impact; for impact and counteraction would cancel each other, and nothing more would follow from this than that the two bodies ceased to act on each other, not that the body struck would move after it [the impact?]. And besides this, since the force of inertia is a natural force, then, even if the equilibrium were cancelled by the impact, it would have to restore itself of its own accord the very next moment, that is,",["The OCR of this passage is damaged. 'der ende Körper' is read as 'der [stoßende] Körper'; the bracketed word is a supply and doubtful.","'daß 2 diese' retains a stray numeral, shown as '2)'. 'nach diesem bewegen je' is incomplete; I render 'would move after it [the impact?]' and do not complete the verb. The final clause 'weil die Trägheitskraft eine natürliche Kraft' lacks its verb in the OCR; I supplied none beyond the English copula.","The passage breaks off at 'd. i.' and is continued in the next passage."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0017":["the body that has been struck would have to be at rest again immediately after the impact.",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage ('d. i.')."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0018":["I refrain from citing many more grounds which I have ready against the [concept] of the force of inertia. I would equally",["'wider den Bes der Trägheitskraft' is garbled; I read 'Begriff' (concept), shown in brackets, but the reading is doubtful.","The sentence breaks off at 'eben' and is continued in the next passage."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0019":["well be able to throw light on the metaphysical proofs which are found of it. But here I have to write not a book but a single sheet, within whose small compass this fruitful matter must be confined. On the Law of Continuity, in so far as it is inseparable from the",["The first word is cut off in the OCR ('ohl' = wohl); 'eben' at the end of the previous passage joins it. 'vor' (vor[findet]) is read as 'vorfindet'.","The passage ends in mid-sentence with the beginning of a heading, which is completed in the next passage: 'Von dem Gesetze der Continuität, in so fern es von dem Begriffe der Trägheitskraft unzertrennlich ist.'"]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0020":["concept of the force of inertia.",["Completes the heading begun at the end of the preceding passage: 'On the Law of Continuity, in so far as it is inseparable from the concept of the force of inertia.'"]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0021":["What must put the defenders of the common concept of motion most in embarrassment is this: that they cannot avoid having another, arbitrary law forced upon them against their will, if they wish to explain the laws of motion according to their doctrine. This auxiliary hypothesis is the law of continuity. Perhaps very few mechanicians have noticed that, however much they may wish to oppose it, they must nevertheless secretly assume it when they wish to explain the impact of bodies from the assumed concepts of motion. I understand by it, however, only the physical law of continuity, which can never be proved but can very well be refuted; for as to that [law] in the logical sense*), it is",["Several OCR slips corrected silently (e.g. 'erklaren' = erklären, 'wen' = wollen/ 'lassen'). 'Lehrbegriff' = 'doctrine'; 'Vertheidiger des gemeinen Begriffes' = 'defenders of the common concept'.","'was das im logischen Sinne*) anlangt' appears to lack a noun after 'das'; I supply '[law]' in brackets as the evident sense. The asterisk marks a footnote whose text is not in the passage.","I divided the sentence on the hypothesis into several for clarity. The passage breaks off at 'so ist es', as in the source."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0022":["I will, without adding here the formula of this rule, merely cite a few examples of it. What holds in general when one body strikes another […], holds also when it meets one at rest; for rest is to be regarded as an infinitely small motion. If a measure of forces holds of [actual] motion in general, it must hold also of mere pressure. [This is] a very fine and correct rule for judging, but it contributes nothing to the present subject. In the physical sense it would, in Leibniz's opinion, run thus: One body does not communicate any force to another all at once, but in such a way that it transfers its force to it through all the infinitely small intermediate degrees, from rest up to the determinate velocity. Now let it be observed how all those who wish to explain the laws of impact in accordance with the accepted concepts of motion must make thorough use of this Leibnizian rule. Why does a perfectly hard body […] in another of the same kind […], until both [have] equal velocity, namely, when both are equal, until each has half the velocity of the impelling body? For then the body impelled flees from all further action of the impelling one. But does one not here presuppose that all the effect of the oncoming body on the body at rest takes place gradually, by means of a succession of infinitely many small moments of pressure? For if the former acted with its whole force at once, it would impart its whole motion to the latter and itself remain at rest, which conflicts with the law of impact of perfectly hard bodies. The body at rest stands, after all, in the way of the whole motion of the impelling body; if the latter, therefore, can act with its whole force at once, it will certainly do so, and what holds of the whole force holds also of its half, its fourth part, and so on. Hence it will not act with any finite | [force] at once, but only through all the infinitely small moments, gradually; and this is what the law of continuity asserts. |",["OCR very corrupt in this passage. 'einen andern jégten anstößt' has a lost word, left as […]; 'bewegten' (moving) is a guess only. The sentence on the hard body ('Warum bringt ein völlig harter Körper in einem andern gleichartigen un[d] … treibt, bis beide …') is largely illegible; I translate only what survives and mark the gaps […] rather than reconstruct.","'lichen Bewegung' read as [wirklichen] 'actual', following the parallel 'wirkliche Bewegung' in the next passage; 'endlichen' (finite) is also possible. Flagged by brackets.","'Ruhe ist [als] eine…' and 'Kräftenmaß': the missing 'als' silently restored; 'Kräftenmaß' = 'measure of forces'. 'Vorwurf' = 'subject' (of the inquiry).","Restated 'eine sehr schöne … Regel' as '[This is] a very fine … rule', the bracket marking the supplied words. 'ihn' in 'idi ihn seine Kraft überträgt' read as 'ihm' (to it).","'endlichen | auf einmal': a word is lost at the page break; I supply [force] (Kraft) in brackets. The | marks are kept where they stand."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0023":["Since we see from this that the law of continuity must be accepted throughout, if one is not willing to rid oneself of the common concept of motion and rest, I shall show only briefly why the most celebrated investigators of nature nevertheless will not allow it to count even as a hypothesis; for one can never give it out as anything better, since it cannot be proved. For pressure can be regarded as an actual motion through an infinitely small space. I reserve to myself the task of explaining this logical rule of continuity at length on another occasion, and of putting it in its proper light. If I maintain that one body can never act on another with any degree of force at once without first passing through all possible small intermediate degrees, then, I say, it would not be able to act on it at all. For however infinitely small the moment may be with which it acts in an instant, and which accumulates in a determinate small portion of time to a given velocity, this moment is always a sudden effect, which according to the law of continuity ought first to have passed, and also could have passed, through all infinite degrees of the lesser moments; for one can always think, of any [such] moment, another and smaller one, from whose summation",["The sentence 'denn der Druck kann als eine wirkliche Bewegung … angesehen werden' stands in the OCR text without connection; it may be displaced from a footnote or from the preceding page. Translated where it stands.","'entladen' (probably Kant's 'entledigen') rendered 'rid oneself of'. 'sage ich' with subjunctive 'werde' translated as given; the sense is that, on this supposition, the body would be unable to act at all.","'ebenen Moment' is doubtful (OCR); rendered 'any [such] moment'. 'damit' restored from 'mit'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished; it continues in the next passage."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0024":["has arisen. For example, the moment of gravity is certainly infinitely smaller than the moment of action in the impact of bodies, since impact can bring about in an entirely imperceptible time great degrees of velocity, which gravity could produce only in a far longer time. Thus even the moment of the effect in impact is sudden, and contrary to the law of continuity. Nor may one object that there are",["Begins mid-sentence ('…erwachsen ist'), continuing the previous passage, and breaks off again; translated as it stands.","'Handlung' rendered 'action'; 'sie' (OCR 'se') taken to refer to the impact (Stoß), as the sense requires; this is a choice of reference.","'Z. E.' = 'zum Exempel', 'for example'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0025":["perfectly hard bodies in nature. For it is enough here merely to think them and to determine their laws of motion, since only by means of these can the laws be found according to which pliant bodies impinge on one another. And besides, every body has a certain degree of cohesion, by virtue of which, in respect of a moment equal to or smaller than it, in the action of the impelling body it can be regarded as a hard body; and if a sudden effect is possible only in respect of this, it will also be able to take place in respect of greater degrees. Key to the Elucidation of the Laws of Impact according to",["The OCR 'ein jeger welche Körper' is corrupt; I translate 'every body'. 'in der ait des stoßenden' is doubtful; I read 'Action' (action) but 'Art' is conceivable.","'ii nur' read as 'da nur' (since only), 'n nur' as 'wenn nur' (if only). The final clause 'Schlüssel zur Erläuterung …' is the beginning of a heading that is completed in the next passage.","The passage begins mid-sentence and is translated as it stands."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0026":["the New Concept of Motion and Rest.",["Completes the heading begun at the end of the previous passage."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0027":["What takes place in impact between the two bodies acting reciprocally is already clear from the foregoing, according to our doctrine. It consists, namely, merely in this: that action and reaction are equal on both sides, and that after the impact both bodies are relatively at rest in respect of one another, provided they have met each other directly, and one abstracts from all elastic force. But under the name of laws of motion one understands not merely the rules of the relation which impelling bodies acquire, the one in respect of the other, but | above all also the change of their external state in regard to the space in which they find themselves. This, properly speaking, is the external phenomenon of what has taken place immediately between them; and it is this that one wishes to know. |",["'Lehrbegriff' rendered 'doctrine'. 'Federkraft' = 'elastic force'. The running head (Neuer Lehrbegriff der Bewegung und Ruhe) and page number omitted; the | placed at the page break is kept.","The OCR has '3|' at the end; the 3 is dropped as a stray figure and the | kept. 'Phänomenon' retained as 'phenomenon'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0028":["To this end let us take first two bodies A and B, the first of a mass of 3 [unit illegible], the second of 2 [unit illegible], and the latter as at rest in respect of the space in which it is situated, but the former as moved, in respect of this space, with a velocity of 5 degrees, in a direct onset upon the body B. Since, in accordance with our propositions, one must ascribe to the body B, relatively to A, a velocity of 3 degrees, but to A, in relation to B, one of 2 degrees, these two equal forces will cancel one another through the impact, and both will be respectively at rest in relation to one another. But since B, which was at rest relatively to the other objects, has in consequence a respective motion of 2 degrees towards A, this same motion must also be ascribed to the surrounding space, parallel with, and of the same velocity as, | | [the] body B. Now the impact of A cancels this motion of 2 degrees in B, but not in the surrounding space, in which no action takes place. This space will therefore continue to move in the former direction of the body B; or, what is the same thing, the body B will advance after the impact, in the opposite direction, namely in the direction of the impelling body A, with a velocity of 2 degrees in respect of the surrounding space, and hence the body A also in the same direction and with the same velocity, since it is at rest in respect of B. Thus both bodies will move on after the impact with a velocity of 2 degrees. From this one sees that a velocity cancelled in a body, which was posited in the body impelled only respectively to the oncoming body, and which it did not have in respect of space, properly produces in it an equal degree of motion in regard to space, in the direction of the impact.",["The unit after 'von 3 .' and 'von 2 c' (OCR) is illegible; left as [unit illegible]. The numerical values are given as the OCR text has them; I have not checked them against the original.","'mit | | Körper B': the article is lost between the two | marks; I supply [the] in brackets and keep the marks as they stand.","'dieser' in 'dieser fortfahren…' taken to refer to the surrounding space (umgebender Raum), as the sense requires. 'Stoß' rendered 'impact'; 'respective' / 'beziehungsweise' rendered 'respectively' / 'relatively'.","The long sentence 'Nun hebt … fortrücken' was kept as one sentence with its alternative ('or, what is the same thing') preserved; the parenthesis 'welches einerlei ist' is rendered between commas."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0029":["Suppose two bodies A and B, with masses as before, but A runs against B with 3 degrees [of velocity] and B against A with 2, in opposite directions. If we consider only the reciprocal relation of the motion of these bodies to one another, the velocities 3 and 2 must be added together. By what was said above, this sum must be distributed between them in inverse ratio to their masses, so that A receives 2 degrees of velocity and B receives 3. Through the equality of the opposed forces they thereby place themselves in respective rest relatively to one another. Now by the respective motion of the two bodies towards one another a velocity of 3 was posited in B. But B does not have this velocity in relation to external space in its entirety; it has only 2 degrees of it. Hence, according to what was remarked a little earlier, the cancelling of a velocity which was not to be found in the body in respect of space will establish a motion in the opposite direction in respect of that same space. That is to say, B will be carried onward with one degree of velocity, and A likewise with this degree, since it rests respectively upon B, in the direction in which A delivered the impact.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Geschwindigkeiten?' read as 'Geschwindigkeiten 3'; 'sie it' as 'sie in'; 'sich)' as 'sich'. The reading '3' is inferred from the argument (3 + 2, shared as 2 and 3).","The long final sentence is divided into several, and the pronouns (B's velocity, A resting on B) are resolved by the sense of the argument. 'Masses as before' presumably means the 2:3 ratio of the earlier example, which is not in the passage.","Words in square brackets are supplied; 'respective Ruhe' and 'respective Bewegung' are rendered 'respective rest' and 'respective motion', meaning relative to the other body."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0030":["It would be easy to derive from the concepts laid down as foundation the laws of motion in the impact of bodies that move with unequal velocity in one and the same direction, and likewise the rules for the impact of elastic bodies. It would also still be necessary to set what has been propounded in a stronger light by means of further explanations. All this could be done if, with so rich a subject-matter and within such narrow limits of space, it were possible to be complete in content and yet also copious in expression.",["'Stope' read as 'Stoße' (OCR). 'Begriffe' rendered 'concepts'."]],"bewegung-und-ruhe-0031":["The plan of my lectures for the present half-year is as follows. I shall lecture on logic [*Vernunftlehre*] from Meier's *Auszug*. Metaphysics I intend now to expound from Baumeister's handbook. In one hour on Wednesdays and Saturdays I shall consider polemically the propositions treated on the preceding days, which in my opinion is one of the most excellent means of arriving at thorough insight. Mathematics will be begun from Wolff's *Auszug*. If some gentlemen should wish for a course in natural science from Eberhard's handbook, I shall seek to satisfy their desire. In the past half-year I lectured on physical geography from my own notes, and I intend to present this useful and agreeable science anew, with various extensions.",["'Lagen' read as 'Tagen' (OCR). 'Vernunftlehre' is the usual term for logic; 'Collegio' (course) rendered 'course'.","I have kept the titles Auszug/Handbuch as Kant gives them: Meier's *Auszug aus der Vernunftlehre*, Baumeister (Baumeister's Metaphysics handbook), Wolff's *Auszug*, Eberhard's handbook; the exact works are inferred, not stated."]],"optimismus-0001":["Attempt at Some Reflections on Optimism\nby\nM. Immanuel Kant,\nwhereby he at the same time announces his lectures\nfor the coming half-year.\n7 October 1759.",["'Ver such' read as 'Versuch'; 'balde Jahr' read as 'halbe Jahr' (OCR slips). 'Optimismus' kept as 'Optimism'."]],"optimismus-0002":["Ever since a fitting concept of God has been formed, perhaps no thought has been more natural than this: that when he chooses, he chooses only the best. If it was said of Alexander that he believed he had done nothing so long as anything remained to be done, this may be said with infinitely greater correctness of the most benevolent and most powerful of all beings. Nor did Leibniz believe that he was propounding anything new when he said: this world is the best among all possible worlds, or, what comes to the same thing, the In-",["Breaks off in mid-word ('Der In-'); continued in the next passage ('-begriff'), so left unfinished here.","OCR: 'sich)' read as 'sich'; 'Bei' as 'sei' (subjunctive of indirect speech, rendered 'is' in English); 'Xn-' as 'In-'."]],"optimismus-0003":["[In]begriff [the sum] of all that God has brought forth outside himself is the best that it was at all possible to bring forth. The novelty lay only in the application, namely in cutting, amid the difficulties that are raised about the origin of evil, the knot that is so hard to untie. A thought so easy and so natural, which at last is uttered so often that it becomes common and disgusts people of delicate taste, cannot long maintain its credit. What honour is there, then, in thinking with the great crowd and asserting a proposition that is so easy to prove? Subtle errors are a stimulus to self-love, which likes to feel its own strength; obvious truths it cannot endure | as soon as they resound from the mouth of the rabble. In a word: we often prize certain modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*] highly not because they are correct but because they cost us something, and we do not like to have truth [at a] cheap [price]. Accordingly it has been [. . .] to maintain that it pleased God to choose [this world] from among all possible worlds, not because it was better than the others that were in his power, but because it simply so pleased him. And why, then, did it please thee, Eternal One, I ask in humility, to prefer the worse to the better? And men put into the mouth of the Most High the answer: So it pleased me, and that is enough.",["The opening 'egriff' is the end of 'Inbegriff' begun at the close of the previous passage; supplied in square brackets. 'Inbegriff' = sum, aggregate.","The OCR is corrupt after '|' ('Er 111175 ertragen kann'): I read 'Wahrheiten kann sie [die Eigenliebe] nicht ertragen', and 'gutes Kants' as 'gutes Kaufs' (cheaply). Both are conjectural.","'Diesemnach hat man es i aupten' is garbled: a gap lies between 'es' and 'behaupten', and I have not filled it. 'Wes' is read as 'diese' (this [world]), supplied in brackets.","'sic)' and '|' kept/corrected as marked; the sentence 'Subtile Irrthümer . . .' is restated with 'it' referring to self-love (Eigenliebe)."]],"optimismus-0004":["I now sketch, with some haste, remarks which may make it easier to judge the controversy that has arisen over this question. My hearers will perhaps find them useful for gaining a better view, in its connexion, of the exposition | that I give of this article in my lectures. I therefore begin as follows to draw my inferences.¹",["The final '!' in the OCR is probably a footnote marker '1', here rendered '¹'; the footnote text is not in the passage. The stray '\\\\' and the page-break '|' (between 'Vor-' and 'trag') are retained as '|' only once, where the sense allowed.","'Zu⸗ i sammenhange' read as 'Zusammenhange'. 'Ich fange . . . an zu schließen' is rendered 'begin to draw my inferences'."]],"optimismus-0005":["If no world can be thought above which a better could not still be thought, then the highest understanding cannot possibly have had knowledge of all possible worlds. Now the latter is false; therefore the former is false also. The correctness of the major premiss is evident from this: if I can say of every single idea that one may form of a world that the representation of a still better one is possible, this can also be said of all the ideas of worlds in the divine understanding. Better worlds are therefore possible than any that are thus known by God, and God has not had knowledge [*Kenntniß*] of all possible worlds. I imagine that the minor premiss will be granted by every orthodox believer, and I conclude that it is false to assert that no world can be thought above which a better could not still be thought; or, what is the same, a world is possible above which none better can be thought. From this it certainly does not yet follow that one among all possible worlds must be the most perfect. For if two or more of them were equal in perfection, then, although no better than either of the two could be thought, none would yet be the best, since both have one and the same degree of goodness.",["The first sentence is divided: 'das letztere' (the consequent: that the highest understanding cannot have had knowledge of all worlds) and 'das erstere' (the antecedent) are resolved by the modus tollens the argument requires. The text is as given; Kant's own terms 'Obersatz' and 'Untersatz' are rendered 'major' and 'minor premiss'.","Line numbers ('15]', '25)') and the stray 'N' dropped as OCR debris. 'Kenntniß' rendered 'knowledge' with the German marked, to distinguish it from 'Erkenntniß'.","'Rechtgläubigen' = 'orthodox believer'; the 'second inference' of the next passage is the step from 'a best world is possible' to 'there is a best'."]],"optimismus-0006":["In order to be able to draw this second inference, I make the following reflection, which seems to me to be new. Let me first be permitted to [. . .] the absolute perfection† of a thing, when it is taken without",["Breaks off mid-sentence in the original; translated as it stands. The main verb of 'daß ich . . . ' is not in the passage, so I have not supplied one (hence the dots in brackets).","The OCR sign after 'Vollkommenheit' is a footnote marker; rendered '†'. The footnote text is not in the passage. 'Zuvörderst' = 'first'."]],"optimismus-0007":["Perfection in the relative sense is the accordance of the manifold with some rule, whatever that rule may be. Thus many a fraud, many a band of robbers, is perfect of its kind. In the absolute sense, however, a thing is perfect only in so far as the manifold in it contains the ground of a reality. The magnitude of this reality determines the degree of perfection. And since God is the highest reality, there would | ...gend a purpose, considered in itself, in the degree of reality, etc. In this presupposition I have the assent of most philosophers on my side, and could very easily this concept right— Now I maintain that reality and reality can never, as such, be distinguished [from one another]. For when things differ from one another, they do so through that which is in the one and is not in the other. But when realities are considered as such, every mark in them is positive. Now if they were to be distinguished from one another as realities, there would have to be something positive in the one which was not in the other. Something negative would then have to be thought in the one, through which it could be distinguished from the other. That is to say, they would not be compared with one another as realities, which was nevertheless the requirement. Reality and reality, therefore, are distinguished from one another by nothing save the negations, absences and limits attaching to one of the two; that is, not in respect of their character (*qualitate*) but of their magnitude (*gradu*).",["The middle of the passage is corrupt in the OCR (\"so würde | … gend eine Absicht … ze. … diesen Begriff recht—\"): I have translated only what is legible, filled nothing in, and left the gaps marked by ellipsis dots. The running head and page number are dropped; the | is kept.","\"im respectiven / im absoluten Verstande\": *Verstand* here has the older sense of 'meaning, sense', not 'understanding'; hence 'in the relative / absolute sense'.","\"zeltweisen\" read as *Weltweisen* ('philosophers'); the stray \")\" marks are OCR remnants of a footnote sign and are omitted.","\"als solche neu unterschieden sein\": \"neu\" is doubtless an OCR slip, probably for \"können\"; I read 'can never … be distinguished' and supply [from one another] in brackets. \"8 Realitäten\" read as \"als Realitäten\"; \"dem dem\" as \"dem andern\".","I divided Kant's long sentence beginning \"Wenn aber Realitäten …\" into several sentences. \"in der einen etwas Negatives\" is kept as the text has it, though the sense might have led one to expect 'in the other'."]],"optimismus-0008":["Accordingly, if things are distinguished from one another, they are always distinguished solely by the degree of their reality, and different things can never have one and the same degree of reality. Hence one can never have two different worlds either; that is to say, two worlds are not possible which would be equally good and equally perfect. Herr Reinhard says in his prize essay on optimism: a world",["The OCR of \"Also … niemals zwei unterschiedene Welten haben\" is damaged (\"T neu ihn auch\"); the verb is uncertain, and I translate 'one can never have' as the nearest reading. Doubtful.","\"Tr\" read as \"Herr\". The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is continued in the next passage."]],"optimismus-0009":["[a world] might well have just the same sum of realities as another, but of a different kind, and in that case they would be different worlds and yet of equal perfection. But he errs in thinking that realities of equal degree could nevertheless be distinguished from one another in their character (*qualitate*). For, to say it once more, suppose that they were so distinguished: then something would be in the one which is not in the other. They would therefore be distinguished by the determinations A and non-A, of which one is always a true negation. They would thus be distinguished by the limits of that reality and by its degree, but not by its character. For negations can never be counted among the qualities of a reality; they limit it and determine its degree. This reflection is abstract and would probably need some elucidation, which, however, I reserve for another occasion. ...er concept [would] coincide with that according to which it was said: a thing is perfect in so far as it accords with the divine attributes.",["The opening words (\"ne wohl eben die Summe …\") are cut off; I take the subject to be \"eine Welt\" from the preceding passage and supply it in brackets.","The final sentence is fragmentary in the OCR (\"i er Begriff mit demjenigen übereintreffen …\"); the first word is lost and \"would\" is my supplied bracket. Nothing further is restored.","\"Beschaffenheit\" rendered 'character', glossed by Kant's own *qualitate*; \"Grad\" by 'degree' (*gradu*)."]],"optimismus-0010":["We have come so far as to see thoroughly that among all possible worlds one is the most perfect, so that none surpasses it in excellence and no other equals it. Whether this is the actual world or not we shall consider shortly; for the present we shall try to set what has been discussed in a clearer light.",["\"Ob dieses nun wirkliche Welt sei\": an article seems to have dropped out in the OCR; I read 'the actual world'.","\"Trefflichkeit\" rendered 'excellence'."]],"optimismus-0011":["There are magnitudes of which none can be thought without a still greater one being thinkable. The greatest among all numbers and the swiftest among all motions are of this kind. Even the divine understanding does not think them; for, as Leibniz remarks, they are deceptive concepts (*notiones deceptrices*), by means of which it seems that we think something, but which in fact represent nothing. Now the opponents of optimism say that a most perfect among all worlds is, like the greatest among all numbers, a self-contradictory concept. For, they hold, just as other units can be added to the sum of the units in a number without a greatest ever resulting, so a few more can equally be added to the sum of reality in a world.",["I divided the final long sentence and reordered its clauses slightly for clarity; the content is unchanged.","\"Verstand\" here is the divine understanding; \"Vorstellen\" rendered 'represent'. OCR \"fei\" read as \"sei\", \"trügliche\" restored from the broken line-end."]],"optimismus-0012":["Without mentioning here that one cannot properly regard the degree of reality | of a thing, in comparison with the lesser degrees, as a number in comparison with its units, I shall only adduce the following in order to",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"um z |\"); I translate it as it stands, without completing it. The | marks a page break and is retained.","\"in Vergleichung der kleinern\": the noun after \"kleinern\" is not stated; I have rendered 'the lesser degrees', which is a judgment of sense."]],"optimismus-0013":["...to show that the instance cited does not fit well. No greatest number is possible; but a greatest degree of reality is possible, and this is found in God. Here we have the first reason why the concepts of number are wrongly made use of in this connexion. The concept of a greatest finite number is an abstract concept of plurality as such, which is finite, but to which more can nevertheless be thought as added without its ceasing to be finite. In it, therefore, finitude of magnitude sets no determinate limits, but only general ones; and for this reason the predicate of greatest can belong to no such number. For however one may think a determinate quantity, it can be increased by any finite number, through addition, without detriment to its finitude. The degree of reality of a world, on the other hand, is something thoroughly determinate. The limits set to the greatest possible perfection of a world are not merely general, but are fixed by a degree which must necessarily be lacking in it. Independence, self-sufficiency, presence in all places, the power to create, and so forth, are perfections which no world can have. Here it is not as with mathematical infinity, where the finite is connected with the infinite, in accordance with the law of continuity, by a steadily continued and always possible increase. Here the distance between infinite and finite reality is fixed by a determinate magnitude, which constitutes their difference. And the world which is found on that rung of the ladder of beings where the gulf begins that contains the immeasurable degrees of perfection which exalt the Eternal above every creature, this world, I say, is the most perfect of all that is finite. It seems to me that we can now see, with a certainty to which the opponents have at least nothing greater to oppose, that among all the finite that was possible a world of the greatest excellence is the highest finite good, alone worthy to be chosen by the supreme of all beings, in order, taken together with the infinite, to make up the greatest sum that can be.",["The opening (\"ot zeigen\") is a fragment continuing the last passage; I read \"zu zeigen\" and begin with an ellipsis.","\"so kann diese eine jede endliche Zahl … vermehren\": the construction is damaged in the OCR; I read 'it can be increased by any finite number, through addition'. Doubtful.","Several damaged words are restored silently (\"man\", \"sich\", \"kann\", \"das Endliche\", \"Gegner\", \"Vortrefflichkeit\"). \"Instanz\" rendered 'instance' (a counter-instance cited).","I divided the longer sentences, notably the one on the finite number and the closing sentence, into shorter ones in the manner of the method; \"Sprosse\" rendered 'rung'."]],"optimismus-0014":["If what has been proved above is granted me, if it is agreed with me that among all possible worlds one is necessarily the most perfect, then I ask to dispute no further. Not every extravagance in opinions can oblige us to take the trouble of answering it with care. If anyone sets himself up to maintain that the highest wisdom could have found the worse better than the best, or that the highest goodness has allowed itself to be better pleased with a smaller good than with a greater, which was equally within its power, I shall not dwell on it any longer. Philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] is very ill used when it is employed to overturn the principles of sound reason; and little honour is done to it when, in order to refute such assertions, one still finds it necessary to summon its weapons.\n\nWhoever found it too tedious to go piecemeal into all the subtle questions which we have so far raised and answered would be convinced of this blunt truth far more easily, with somewhat less scholastic learning, but with a judgment of a sound understanding that would be just as cogent. He would reason thus: A most perfect world is possible, because it is actual; and it is actual, because it was produced by the wisest and most benevolent decree. Either I can form no concept of choice at all, or one chooses as one pleases; but what pleases, that pleases [*gefällt*?]. To please, however, and to hold for good, to prefer to be pleased, to be preferably pleased, and to hold for preferably good, are, in my opinion, only differences of words. Therefore, because God alone chose this world out of all the possible worlds which he knew, he must have held it to be the best; and because his judgment never errs, it is so also in | fact.\n\nEven if it were possible that the highest Being could choose in accordance with the fictitious kind of freedom which some have brought into vogue, and could prefer the worse to much that is better, through I know not what unconditioned pleasure, he would nevertheless never have done so. One may let oneself dream of some subordinate deity of the fables; but to the God of gods no work is fitting save one that is worthy of him, that is, one that is the best among all that is possible.\n\nPerhaps the greater agreement with the divine attributes is the ground of the decree which gave existence to this world, without taking into consideration its particular inner superiority. Very well: even then it is still certain that it is more perfect than all other possible worlds. For since it is to be seen from the effect that all others would have been in lesser agreement with the attributes of the will of God, and since in God everything is reality, while nothing is in greater harmony with this than what itself contains greater reality, the greatest reality that can belong to a world must be found in none other than the present one.\n\nIt may further be objected that it is a constraint of the will, and a necessity which abolishes freedom, not to be able to avoid choosing that which one clearly and rightly recognises as the best. Certainly, if the opposite of this is freedom, and if here there are two forks of the road in a labyrinth of difficulties, where at the risk of erring I must resolve upon one, I shall not hesitate long. Thanks for such a freedom, which banishes into eternal nothingness the best of what it was possible to create, in order, in spite of all [that follows?]",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence at 'um trotz allem'; translated as it stands, not completed.","OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. einimmig→einstimmig, Inn→wenn, umehren→umkehren, so iepen→so schließen); the | sign is kept at the corresponding place in 'in der | That'.","'(aut;' in the OCR is read as 'gefällt'; doubtful, hence the bracketed query. 'ein kleiner Gut mehr belieben lassen ein größeres' is read as 'a smaller good more than a greater'.","'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy', with the German in brackets. 'derben Wahrheit' is read as 'blunt truth'; the OCR 'von derben' is uncertain.","The long sentence beginning 'Derjenige, welchem…' is kept as one sentence; the ensuing 'Man mag sich…' and 'Wohlan' passages are divided into separate sentences. 'Wohlan' rendered 'Very well'.","The words 'It may further be objected' stand for the German 'Es ist ferner dieses vielleicht', which is an ironic objection; supplied to make the sense plain."]],"optimismus-0015":["— the pronouncements of wisdom, to command evil to be something. If I must choose absolutely among errors, I prefer that kindly necessity in which one fares so well, and from which nothing but the best can spring. I am therefore convinced, and perhaps a part of my readers with me; and I am at the same time delighted to see myself a citizen in a world which could not possibly have been better.\n\nChosen by the best of all beings, as a humble member of the most perfect of all possible designs, unworthy in myself and selected for the sake of the whole, I esteem my existence all the more highly because I was elected to occupy a place in the best plan. I call out to every creature that does not make itself unworthy so to be: Hail to us, we are! and the Creator takes pleasure in us.\n\nImmeasurable spaces and eternities may well open the riches of creation in their whole extent only before the eye of the All-knowing. I, however, from the standpoint in which I find myself, armed with the insight vouchsafed to my weak understanding, shall look about me as far as I can, and shall learn ever more to see that the whole is the best, and that everything is good for the sake of the whole.",["The opening 'Diem Aussprüche' is garbled in the OCR; it continues the sentence of the preceding passage ('um trotz allem…'). I translate 'the pronouncements of wisdom' and leave the connecting word doubtful.","The long sentence 'Von dem besten unter allem Wesen…' is restated in smoother English; the sense is unchanged."]],"optimismus-0016":["In the half-year now before us I shall lecture, as I am accustomed, on logic from Meier, on metaphysics from Baumgarten, from",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"optimismus-0017":["the same also on ethics, on physical geography from my own manuscript, on pure mathematics, which I am beginning, in a separate hour, but on the mechanical sciences in another hour, both according to Wolff. The division of the hours will be specially be—",["The passage breaks off at 'be—' (presumably 'bekannt'); not completed here.","'über denselben' read as 'from the same' (i.e. Baumgarten); 'Handschrift' rendered 'manuscript'."]],"optimismus-0018":["made known. It is already known that I bring each of these sciences to an end in one half-year and, if this is too short, make up the remainder in some hours of the following one.",["The opening 'uni gemacht' is an OCR corruption, presumably of '[bekannt]gemacht', continuing 'be—' of the previous passage; rendered 'made known' with that doubt."]],"funk-0001":["Thoughts\non the premature decease\nof the\nHighborn Herr,\nHERR\nJohann Friedrich von Funk,\nin a letter\nto the\nHighborn Frau,\nFRAU\nAgnes Elisabeth,\nwidowed Frau Rittmeisterin [wife of a cavalry captain] von Funk,\nnée von Dorthösen,\nHeiress of the Kaywen and Kahren estates in Courland,\nthe deceased's\ndeeply afflicted Frau Mother,\nby\nM. Immanuel Kant,\nTeacher of Philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] at the Academy at Königsberg.",["OCR slips corrected (FRA→Frau, Tlisabelh→Elisabeth, Nittmeisterin→Rittmeisterin, Junk→Funk). 'Dorthösen' and the estate names are kept as the OCR gives them; spelling uncertain.","'Hochwohlgeboren' rendered 'Highborn'; 'M.' (Magister) kept."]],"funk-0002":["Highborn Frau Rittmeisterin,\nGracious Madam!",["'Rittmeisterin' kept in German (wife of a cavalry captain)."]],"funk-0003":["If men were accustomed to mingle, amid the tumult of their business and distractions, serious moments of instructive reflections, to which the daily example of the vanity of our designs in the fate of their fellow-citizens summons them:",["'lehr-' / 'ichen' joined as 'lehrreichen'; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"funk-0004":["then their joys would perhaps be less boisterous, but in their place there would come a calm serenity of soul, to which no accidents are very unexpected; and even that gentle melancholy, this tender",["Breaks off at 'zärtliche'; the next passage begins '[G]efühl' ('feeling').","'chr unerwartet' is a doubtful OCR reading; I take it as 'sehr unerwartet'."]],"funk-0005":["feeling, with which a noble heart swells when in solitary stillness it weighs the worthlessness of that which among us commonly passes for great",["Begins mid-word ('efühl' = Gefühl); breaks off mid-sentence."]],"funk-0006":["and important, would contain more true happiness than the boisterous amusement of the frivolous and the loud laughter of the fool.",["Completes the sentence begun in funk-0003; the main clause ('würde … enthalten') is the one introduced by 'so würden' above."]],"funk-0007":["As it is, however, the greatest crowd of men very eagerly mingles in the throng of those who, on the bridge which Providence has thrown over one part of the abyss of eternity, and which we call life, run after certain bubbles of water and take no trouble to heed the trapdoors which let one after another sink beside them | into the depth whose measure is infinity, and by which they themselves are at last swallowed up in the midst of their boisterous course. A certain old poet brings a touching feature into the picture of human life in depicting the scarcely born man. The child, he says, at once fills the air with sad wailing, as",["Breaks off mid-sentence at 'wie'; not completed.","'Fallbretter' rendered 'trapdoors'. The sign | is kept where it stands. OCR slips corrected (neu Theil→einen Theil; us→auf)."]],"funk-0008":["...befits a person who is to enter a world where so many afflictions await him. But as the years pass, this man joins to the art of making himself wretched a further art, that of hiding it from himself by the veil which he throws over the melancholy objects of life. He applies himself to a light-minded indifference towards the multitude of ills that surround him, though these in the end bring him back, irresistibly, to a far more painful feeling. Although of all ills he dreads death the most, he seems to pay very little heed to its example among his fellow citizens, unless closer ties particularly awaken his attention. In a time when a raging war throws open the bolts of the black abyss and lets every tribulation break forth upon the human race, we can well see how the accustomed sight of distress and death instils a cold indifference in those who are themselves threatened by both, so that they pay very little heed to the fate of their [brethren (?)] ... [text corrupt] ... when in the quiet stillness of the [...] we do, when these, I say, are seized, by the decree of Him who commands all things almighty, in the midst of the course of their endeavours; when death approaches in solemn stillness the sick-bed of the sick | man; when this giant, before whom nature shudders, comes on with slow | tread to enclose him in iron arms: then the feeling of those who otherwise stifle it in distractions awakens. A melancholy feeling speaks from the inward part of the heart | what was once heard in an assembly of the Romans with such applause, because it is so conformable to our universal sentiment: I am a man, and what befalls men can also befall me. The friend, or the kinsman too, says to himself: I find myself in the tumult of affairs and the press | of life's duties, and my friend was lately in the same; I enjoy my life calmly and without care, but who knows for how long? I take my pleasure with my friends and look for him among them,",["The OCR is corrupt at 'auf das Schicksal ihrer Brite iq) der wenig acht h ema wenn in der ruhigen Stille des 11 0 wir thun'. I read 'Brüder' tentatively and have not filled the gap; the sense of the lost clauses is uncertain.","Silent corrections: 'us'→auf, 'it'→mit, 'erbergen'→verbergen, 'Leens'→Lebens, 'faltfinnige'→kaltsinnige; the stray letters in 'ere f wecken' and 'ala\" dann' are dropped as OCR noise.","Restated: the long opening sentence is divided into three, and the war sentence is kept as one period running into the corrupt gap. The signs | are carried over where they stand, even where they fall mid-word.","The passage begins and ends mid-sentence ('einer Person zusteht'; 'unter denselben,'). The Roman saying is Terence's 'homo sum...'; Kant does not name it here."]],"funk-0009":["But him, in the solemn place\nthat lets nothing return,\nEternity holds fast with strong arms.",["'Die e' is an OCR fragment; I read 'Die Ewigkeit' (the subject of 'hält'), the obvious reading, but it is not legible in the text given.","Verse lines are kept; the English follows the German order as far as it will bear."]],"funk-0010":["Haller.",["The stray 'a' after the name is an OCR remnant of a footnote or marginal sign and is omitted."]],"funk-0011":["To these serious thoughts, gracious Madam, I am raised by the untimely death of your worthy son, which you now so rightly bewail. As one of his former teachers I feel this loss with painful sympathy, although I can scarcely express the greatness of the sorrow that must befall those who were bound to this promising young gentleman by closer ties. Your Grace will permit me to add to these few lines, by which I seek to express the esteem I have cherished for this my former pupil, some further thoughts which arise in me in the present state of my mind.",["Silent OCR corrections: 'das s frühzeitige'→das frühzeitige, 'Lehren'→Lehrer, 'E...'→die, 'Hände'→Bande (a closer tie, not 'hands'). 'Gemüt' is rendered 'mind'.","Restated: the second sentence is kept whole, but 'diejenige' is read as plural ('diejenigen') to agree with 'verknüpft waren'. The stray 'sich)' before 'für' is read as 'ich'."]],"funk-0012":["Every man forms for himself a plan of his destiny [*Bestimmung*] in this world. Accomplishments which he wishes to acquire, honour and comfort which he promises himself from them for the future, lasting happiness in married life, and a long series of pleasures or undertakings make up the pictures of the magic lantern, which he ingeniously sketches for himself and lets play vividly one after another in his imaginings. Death, which closes this play of shadows, shows itself only in dim distance, and is darkened and made unrecognisable by the light that is spread over the more agreeable scenes. While we thus dream, our true fate leads us along quite other paths. The lot that actually falls to us seldom resembles what we promised ourselves, and at every step we take we find our expectations deceived. Meanwhile the imagination goes on with its business and does not tire of sketching new designs, until death, which still always seems far off, suddenly puts an end to the whole play. When a man is led back from this world of fables, of which through his imaginings he is himself the creator and in which he so gladly dwells, into that world in which Providence has actually placed him, and which he apprehends through the understanding [*Verstand*], he is thrown into confusion by a strange contradiction which he there encounters. This contradiction wholly overthrows his plans, since it sets before his insight riddles that cannot be solved. [?] not always he to whom Providence has set the furthest goal of",["'Bestimmung' is here destiny or vocation rather than the technical 'determination'; I give the German beside it.","Restated: the long sentence on the 'wundersamer Widerspruch' is divided in two, the relative clause becoming a sentence of its own. 'Einbildungen' is 'imaginings', 'Einbildung' 'imagination'.","Doubtful reading: the passage ends with the corrupt 'dft nicht immer'; I have not supplied the missing word, and the sentence continues in the next passage ('ebens gesteckt hat'). 'feint'→scheint, 'Plane'→Pläne are silent corrections."]],"funk-0013":["[...of l]ife has set, in order to enjoy rightly the fruits of all these. The friendships that are the tenderest, the marriages that promise the most happiness, are often torn asunder, unrelentingly, by the earliest death; while poverty and misery commonly draw a long thread from the distaff of the Fates, and many seem to live so long only to plague themselves or others. In this seeming contradiction, however, the supreme Ruler distributes to each the lot of his fate with a wise hand. He hides the end of our destiny [*Bestimmung*] in this world in impenetrable darkness. He makes us active through impulses, confident through hope, and, through the happy ignorance | of the future, just as zealous in contriving purposes and designs when they are all soon to end as if we stood at the beginning of them: |",["The passage opens mid-sentence; 'ebens' is the end of 'Lebens' (the preceding passage ended 'das weiteste Ziel des'). I render only what stands.","Silent corrections: 'Eben'→Ehen, 'Glückfaigkeit'→Glückseligkeit, 'gu'→zu; 'sinnen' is taken as 'zu sinnen auf', hence 'contriving'. The sign | is kept after 'ignorance' (German 'Un- | wissenheit') and at the end.","Restated: the sentence on the 'oberste Beherrscher' is divided into three. The closing colon points to the verse that follows in the next passage."]],"funk-0014":["That each may complete the circle which Heaven has appointed for him.",["A verse line, evidently the continuation of the preceding sentence; 'ausersehn' is the poetic shortening of 'ausersehen'."]],"funk-0015":["[Text corrupt] such a one [directs] ... chiefly upon his great destiny [*Bestimmung*] beyond the grave. He does not lose sight of the obligation which the post imposes on him, on which Providence has placed him here. Reasonable in his designs but without obstinacy, confident of the fulfilment of his hope but without impatience, modest in his wishes without prescribing, trusting without presuming, he is zealous in the performance of his duties, yet ready, with a Christian resignation, to submit to the command of the Most High, should it please Him, in the midst of all these endeavours, to recall him from the stage on which he was placed. We always find the ways of Providence wise and worthy of adoration in those matters where we can in some measure discern them; should they not be so far more where we cannot? The untimely death of those from whom we had promised ourselves much flattering hope fills us with terror; but how often may this be precisely the greatest favour of Heaven! Did not the misfortune of many a man consist chiefly in the delay of death, which was all too slow | to make, at the right time, a break after the most honourable scenes of life? |",["The opening 'Bone b ftd) ein solcher!) die We E vornehmlich auf seine große Beltimel :] mung' is badly corrupted; only 'ein solcher' and 'seine große Bestimmung jenseit dem Grabe' are secure. I have not reconstructed the verb or the missing words, and the bracketed 'directs' is only a guess at the sense.","'Bestimmung' is rendered 'destiny' (not 'determination') since the sense is man's vocation beyond the grave. Stray letters (a, z, H) and 'nach)'→nach are dropped as OCR noise.","The sign | before 'war' and at the end is carried over; 'Auftritte' is rendered 'scenes', continuing the theatre image of 'Bühne'."]],"funk-0016":["The hopeful youth dies, and how much unfulfilled happiness do we not believe [that we] must miss in so early a loss? | But in the book of fates it may read otherwise. Seductions that were already rising from afar to overthrow a virtue not yet well tried, afflictions and adversities with which the future threatened: from all this he escaped, this fortunate one, whom an early death carried off in a blessed hour; while friends and relatives, ignorant of the future, bewail the loss of those years which, they imagine, would one day have crowned the life of their dear one with honour. Before I close these few lines, I wish to sketch a small portrait of the life and character of the departed. What I relate is known to me from the report of a faithful tutor, who mourns him tenderly, and from my own acquaintance. How many good qualities there are, which only he knows who looks into the innermost of hearts, and which are the nobler the less they strive to catch the public eye!",["Obvious OCR slips corrected silently (\"glaube a\" = glauben; stray \"s\" and \")\" after \"Tugend\" and \"sich\"; the trailing \";\").","Restated nothing, but kept Kant's long sentence about the seductions in one sentence for fidelity; \"Hofmeister\" rendered \"tutor\" (a resident private tutor)."]],"funk-0017":["Herr Johann Friedrich von Funk was born on 4 October 1738 of a distinguished noble house in Courland. From childhood on he never enjoyed perfect health. He was brought up with great care, showed much diligence in study, and had a heart which by nature was made to be formed to noble qualities. On 15 June 1759, together with his younger brother and under the guidance of their tutor, he came to this [...: text defective]. He submitted with all readiness to the examination of the then Dean, and did credit to his own diligence and to the instruction of his tutor. He attended the lectures of the Consistorial Councillor and Professor Teske, at present Rector Magnificus of the University, likewise those of Herr Doctor of Law Funck, and my own, with an unwearied assiduity that served as a model. He lived retired and quiet, whereby he also preserved the little strength of a body inclined to consumption, until towards the end of February of this year he was gradually so affected by it that neither the nursing and care bestowed upon him nor the diligence of a skilful physician could preserve him any longer. Thus on 4 May of this year, having prepared himself with the steadfastness and fervent devotion of a Christian for an edifying end, he passed away gently and blessedly, with the assistance of his faithful pastor, and was buried, as befitted his rank, in the cathedral church of this city.",["The German breaks off at \"auf hiesige e Er unterwarf\": a word (presumably \"Universität\") is missing in the OCR; I have not supplied it and mark the gap.","Silently corrected: \"Kind» heit\" = Kindheit, \"stili\" = still, \"Mal\" = Mai (May, the context fixing the reading), and the hyphenation breaks.","\"Er\" and \"Ihn\" are capitalised in the original as marks of respect; not reproduced in English."]],"funk-0018":["He was of a gentle and composed disposition, affable and modest towards everyone, kind and inclined to general benevolence, zealously concerned to train himself properly to be an ornament of his house and of use to his fatherland. He never grieved anyone otherwise than by his death. He strove after an unfeigned piety. He would have become an upright citizen for the world, but the decree of the Most High willed that he should become one in heaven. His life is a fragment, which has made us wish for the rest, of which an early death has deprived us.",["OCR slips corrected silently: \"dec scheiden\" = bescheiden; \"Gein\" = Sein; \"an: |i ders\" = anders; stray \"1\" at the end omitted. The | signs are kept where they stand.","\"einer im Himmel\": I supply \"one\" in the sense of \"citizen\", following the preceding \"Bürger\"."]],"funk-0019":["He would deserve to be held up as a model to those who think to pass the years of their education and youth with honour, if a quiet merit made on flighty minds the same impression of emulation as do the falsely glittering qualities of those whose vanity aims only at the semblance of virtue, without troubling itself about its essence. [Er 1 von Du 1 er an- $ dauert worden: text corrupt.]",["The final clause (\"Er 1 von Du 1 er an⸗ $ dauert worden.\") is unintelligible OCR; I have not attempted a reading and give it only as a bracketed mark of corruption.","The | and the line number between \"derjeni-\" and \"gen\" are dropped as apparatus of the OCR; the sentence has been kept as one, though long.","\"Nacheiferung\" = emulation; \"Eitelkeit\" (OCR \"Eitelfeit\") = vanity."]],"funk-0020":["These, gracious Lady, are the features of the character of your son, formerly in life so rightly beloved. Weakly as they have been sketched, they will nevertheless renew far too much the sorrow which you feel at his loss. But it is just these lamented qualities which in such a loss yield no small consolation; for only to those who lightly lose sight of the most important of all purposes can it be a matter of indifference in what condition they deliver their own to eternity. I spare myself the trouble of laying before Your Grace extended grounds of consolation in this affliction. The humble renunciation of our own wishes, when it pleases the wisest Providence to decide otherwise, and the Christian longing for one and the same blessed goal, to which others have arrived before us, avail more for the quieting of the heart than all the grounds of a dry and feeble eloquence. I have the honour to be, with the greatest respect,",["OCR slips corrected silently (\"Ab- 0% sichten\" = Absichten; \"| 15]\" and similar numbers dropped as line numbers).","\"Dero ... Herren Sohns\": the honorific \"Dero\" is rendered simply as \"your\"; the long sentence beginning \"Aber eben diese\" is kept in one sentence, split only at the semicolon."]],"funk-0021":["Most High-born Lady,\nGracious Lady, Wife of the Captain of Cavalry [Rittmeisterin],\nYour Grace's",["\"Rittmeisterin\" = wife of a *Rittmeister* (captain of cavalry); an address formula, the English is approximate."]],"funk-0022":["Königsberg,",[]],"funk-0023":["6 June 1760. most obedient servant",["The date and \"gehorsamster Diener\" stand together in the OCR line; kept in that order."]],"funk-0024":["J. Kant.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0001":["The False Subtlety\nof the\nFour Syllogistic Figures\nDemonstrated\nby\nM[agister] Immanuel Kant,",["\"M.\" expanded in brackets as *Magister*, the degree Kant then held. \"Spitzfindigkeit\" = subtlety (in the sense of hair-splitting)."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0002":["SUE",["\"SUE\" is OCR noise (probably an ornament or head-piece); left as it stands."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0003":["General Concept of the Nature of Inferences of Reason. To compare something as a mark [Merkmal] with a thing is entitled judging [urtheilen]. The thing itself is the subject, the mark the predicate. The comparison is expressed by the copula \"is\" or \"are\". When this is used simply, it designates the predicate as a mark of the subject; but when it is attached to the sign of negation, it gives the predicate to be known as a mark opposed to the subject. In the former case the judgment is affirmative, in the other",["The initial letters of the lines are lost in the OCR; \"llgemeiner\" = Allgemeiner, \"Jeichung\" = Vergleichung, \"elches\" = welches, \"ehaftet\" = behaftet, \"kennen\" = erkennen, restored silently.","Restated Kant's second sentence in three English sentences (as permitted); the passage breaks off at \"im andern\" and is continued by the next id.","\"Merkmal\" = mark; \"Vernunftschluß\" = inference of reason (rendered so rather than \"syllogism\", which is kept for \"syllogistisch\")."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0004":["negative. It is easily understood that when the predicate is called a mark, this does not say that it is a mark of the subject; for that is so only in affirmative judgments. What is said is that it is regarded as",["Begins with \"[ver]neinend\" (OCR \"neinend\"), the completion of the previous passage; \"Werk—al\" = Merkmal, \"i;\" = ist, restored silently.","The sentence breaks off at \"als\" and continues in the next id; I do not complete it."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0005":["a mark of some thing, although in a negative judgment it contradicts the subject of that judgment. Thus a spirit [Geist] is the thing which I think; composite is a mark of something; the judgment \"a spirit is not composite\" represents this mark as conflicting with the thing itself. What is a mark of the mark of a thing is entitled a mediate mark of that thing. Thus necessary is an immediate mark of God; but unchangeable is a mark of the necessary and a mediate mark of God. It is easily seen that the immediate mark between the remote mark and the thing takes the place of an intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*), since only by means of it is the remote mark compared with the thing itself. But a mark can also be compared negatively with a thing by means of an intermediate mark, namely by knowing that something conflicts with the immediate mark of a thing. Contingent, as a mark, conflicts with the necessary; but necessary is a mark of God; and so we know by means of an intermediate mark that being contingent contradicts God.",["OCR: \"new verneinenden\" read \"einem verneinenden\"; \"eilt\" read \"stellt\" (\"stellt ... vor\"), a conjecture; \"amittelbares\" read \"unmittelbares\" (immediate), as the argument requires; \"(bft\" = selbst; \"verichen\" = verglichen.","\"Zufällig\" rendered \"contingent\", \"Geist\" \"spirit\"; the closing clause \"daß zufällig sein Gott widerspreche\" is rendered literally as \"that being contingent contradicts God\"."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0006":["I now set up my real definition [Realerklärung] of an inference of reason. Every judgment through a mediate mark [is] an inference of reason; or, in other words, it is the comparison of a mark with a thing by means of an intermediate mark. This intermediate mark (*nota intermedia*) in an inference of reason is otherwise also entitled the middle principal concept (*terminus medius*); which are the other two principal concepts is sufficiently well known.",["The square brackets round \"ist\" are in the source (an editorial insertion) and are carried over; \"die!\" read \"die\"; \"id)\" read \"ich\".","\"Hauptbegriff\" rendered \"principal concept\" (the Latin \"terminus\")."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0007":["In order to know distinctly the relation of the mark to the thing in the judgment \"the human soul is a spirit\", I make use of the intermediate mark \"rational\", so that by means of it I regard being a spirit as a mediate mark of the human soul. Three judgments must necessarily occur here, namely:",["The stray \"um\" before \"Es müssen\" is OCR noise and is omitted; \"vermittelit\" read \"vermittelst\".","\"vernünftig\" rendered \"rational\"."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0008":["1. Being a spirit is a mark of the rational;",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0009":["2. Rational is a mark of the human soul;",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0010":["3. To be a spirit is a mark of the human soul; for the comparison of a remote mark with the thing itself is possible only through these three acts. E",["The OCR \"is [sit\" is read as \"ist\". The passage breaks off at \"E\", presumably the start of a new word (Es?), and is left as it stands.","Merkmal = mark; \"Handlungen\" = acts."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0011":["In the form of judgments they would run thus: Everything rational is a spirit; the soul of man is rational; consequently the soul of man is a spirit. This is an affirmative syllogism. As regards the negative syllogisms, it is just as easy to see that, since I do not always recognise the conflict between a predicate and a subject clearly enough, I must, if I can, avail myself of the expedient of making my insight easier through an intermediate mark. Suppose that the negative judgment is put before me: The duration of God is to be measured by no time. I find that this predicate, compared thus immediately with the subject, does not give me a sufficiently clear idea of the conflict. I therefore make use of a mark which I can represent to myself immediately in this subject, and compare the predicate with it, and by means of it with the thing itself. Being measurable by time conflicts with everything unchangeable; but unchangeableness is a mark of God; therefore, and so on. Put formally, this would run: Nothing unchangeable is measurable by time; the duration of God is unchangeable; therefore, and so on. |\n\nOf the Supreme Rules of All Syllogisms.\n\nFrom what has been adduced it is recognised that the first and universal rule of all affirmative syllogisms is this: A mark of a mark is a mark of the thing itself (*nota notae est etiam nota rei ipsius*); and of all negative syllogisms: What conflicts with the mark of a thing conflicts with the thing itself (*repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*). Neither of these rules, further, admits of proof. For a proof is possible only through one or more syllogisms, and to wish to prove the supreme formula of all syllogisms would therefore be to reason in a circle. That these rules, however, contain the universal and first ground of every rational kind of inference is evident from this: that those which have hitherto been held by all logicians to be the first rules of all syllogisms must borrow the sole ground of their truth from ours. The *dictum de omni*, the supreme ground of affirmative syllogisms, runs thus: What is affirmed universally of a concept is also affirmed of everything that is contained under it. The ground of proof of this is clear. The concept under which others are contained has always been abstracted from them as a mark. What belongs to this concept is therefore a mark of the mark, and so also a mark of the things themselves from which it was abstracted; that is, it belongs to the lower [concepts] which are contained under it. Anyone who is at all instructed in logical knowledge sees easily that this dictum is true solely on this ground, and that it therefore stands under our first rule. The *dictum de nullo* stands in just the same relation to our second rule: What is universally denied of a concept is also denied of all that is contained under it. For [it is] a mark abstracted from them. But what conflicts with this mark conflicts also with the things themselves; consequently what conflicts with the higher concepts must also conflict with the lower, which are contained under it",["OCR damage was silently corrected where the reading was clear (Dauer, Regel, Merkmal vom Merkmal, nota notae, repugnat, unmittelbar, abgesondert, enthalten, Begriffen). Running head and page number are omitted; the sign | is kept where it stands.","\"Dieses förmlich oigo i trde so lauten\" is garbled in the OCR; I read \"Dieses förmlich vorgetragen würde so lauten\" (\"Put formally, this would run\"). This is a conjecture.","\"Denn t ihnen abgesondertes Merkmal\" is corrupt. I give \"For [it is] a mark abstracted from them\", supplying the bracketed words by judgment; the reading is doubtful.","In \"würde Den im Cirkel schließen\" I read \"man\"; \"allgemeinen und ten Grund\" is read as \"allgemeinen und ersten\". \"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism; \"Schlußart\" = kind of inference.","Long sentences, such as the one on the *dictum de omni*, are divided in English. The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is not completed."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0012":["§ 3.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0013":["Of Pure and Mixed Syllogisms. |",["\"Vernunftschluß\" = syllogism (inference of reason); \"rein\" = pure; \"vermischt\" = mixed."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0014":["It is known to everyone that there are immediate inferences, in which the truth of one judgment is known immediately from another without a middle concept. | For this reason such inferences are also not syllogisms. For example, from the proposition, Every matter is changeable, | it follows directly that what is not changeable is not matter. The logicians | enumerate various kinds of such immediate consequences, among which, without doubt, those by logical conversion, and likewise by contraposition, are the foremost.",["The trailing \"4\" in the OCR is taken to be a stray line or footnote mark and is omitted.","The sign | falls in the middle of \"un⸗|mittelbar\" in the source; it is placed after \"immediately\" here."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0015":["If, now, a syllogism is effected through three propositions only, according to the rules just set out for every syllogism, I call it a pure syllogism (*ratiocinium purum*). But if it is possible only through more than three judgments being connected with one another, it is a mixed syllogism (*ratiocinium hybridum*). If, namely, an immediate inference drawn from the three principal propositions must be interposed between them, and so one proposition",["The text of \"Gehet nämlich, daß …\" is doubtful (perhaps \"Geht es nämlich nicht an, daß …\"); I render it as a conditional continuing the sense, and the passage breaks off before the apodosis.","\"Sat\" is read as \"Satz\"; the stray \"4\" after \"hybridum\" is omitted as a mark. *ratiocinium purum* / *hybridum* are kept in Latin, as Kant's own terms."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0016":["more is added than a pure syllogism permits, it is a *ratiocinium hybridum*. For example, imagine someone inferring thus: |",["Continues the previous passage in mid-sentence. \"3. E.\" is read as \"z. E.\" (zum Exempel)."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0017":["Nothing that is perishable is simple;\nConsequently nothing simple is perishable;",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0018":["The soul of man is simple;",["A stray \"ii\" in the OCR is dropped."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0019":["Therefore the soul of man is not perishable,",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0020":["he would indeed not have a syllogism properly compound, | since the latter should consist of several syllogisms. But this syllogism contains, besides what is required for a syllogism, a further immediate inference through contraposition, and it contains four propositions.",["\"zusammengesetzt\" = compound (composite). The \"so\" at the start of the German main clause belongs with the conditional of the preceding example; the sentence is translated from \"so würde er\"."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0021":["But even if only three judgments were actually expressed, the consequence of the conclusion from these judgments would still be possible only by virtue of a permitted logical conversion, contraposition, or some other logical alteration of one of these premisses. The syllogism would then none the less be a *ratiocinium hybridum*. For what matters here is not what one says, but what one cannot avoid thinking in addition, if a correct inference is to be present. Assume, then, that in the syllogism:",["Corrected silently: \"alle H\" → \"allein\"; \"Gontrapofition\" → \"Contraposition\"; \"ändert\" → \"anderen\".","\"fol vorhanden sein\" is garbled; I translate \"to be present\" and do not supply the missing verb.","The long sentence is divided into three in English."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0022":["Nothing perishable is simple;\nThe soul of man is simple;\nTherefore the soul of man is not perishable,",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0023":["[is] a correct consequence only in so far as I can say, through a perfectly correct conversion of the major premiss: Nothing perishable is simple, consequently nothing simple is perishable; then the syllogism remains always a [mixed] inference, because its inferential force rests on the secret addition of this immediate inference, which one at least in thought",["The beginning (\"i nur in so fern\") is damaged; I supply \"[is]\" and read it as the predicate of the previous sentence's \"assume that …\". The reading is conjectural.","\"e Schluß\" is damaged; \"[mixed]\" (vermengter) is supplied by judgment from the argument. \"Umhrung\" read as \"Umkehrung\"; \"Leser\" as \"dieser\"."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0024":["must.",["Fragment: the verb governed by \"muß\" (presumably \"hinzufügen\", add) is missing in the source passage and is not supplied."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0025":["In the so-called first figure pure syllogisms alone are possible; in the other three, merely",["Continues in the next passage (\"mixed ones\")."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0026":["mixed ones.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0027":["If a syllogism is conducted immediately according to one of our two supreme rules [just] cited, it is always in the first figure. The first rule therefore runs: A mark B of a mark C of a thing A is a mark of the thing A itself. Hence arise three propositions:",["The OCR \"zwei Den angeführten\" is damaged; I read \"zwei [so eben] angeführten\" and bracket \"just\". Doubtful. \"Aten\" is read as \"ersten\"; \"Wert- Jal C\" as \"Merkmal C\"; \"entringen\" as \"entspringen\"."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0028":["C has for mark B; | What is rational is a spirit;",["The two columns of the source (schema and example) run together in the OCR; they are kept in the same order."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0029":["A has for mark C; The human soul is rational;",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0030":["Therefore A has for mark B. Therefore the human soul is a spirit. It is very easy to apply further similar propositions, and among others also to the",["Breaks off; continues in the next passage (\"rule of negative inferences\")."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0031":["rule of negative syllogisms, in order to convince oneself that, if they conform to it, they always stand in the first figure, so that [I] here rightly seek to avoid a tiresome prolixity. One also readily perceives that these rules of syllogisms do not require that, besides these judgments, an immediate inference from one or other of them need be interposed, if the argument is to be conclusive. Hence the syllogism in the first figure is of the pure kind.",["The OCR is damaged at the start (\"gel\" = Regel; \"enn\" = wenn) and in \"Man rd\" (wird) and \"ordern\" (fordern). The subject \"[I]\" is supplied in brackets because the German has none (\"zu verhüten suche\"). \"Weitläuftigkeit\" = prolixity.","The last long sentence is divided into two in English; \"sofern\" is read as \"[so]fern\" (if, in so far as)."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0032":["In the second figure no syllogisms other than mixed ones are",["Continues in the next passage."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0033":["possible.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0034":["The rule of the second figure is this: That to which a mark of a | thing is contradictory is contradictory to the thing itself. This proposition is true only because that to which a mark is contradictory is also contradictory to this mark, whereas whatever conflicts with a mark conflicts with the thing itself; therefore that to which a mark of a thing is contradictory conflicts with the thing itself. It is now evident that an inference to the conclusion by means of the minor premiss is possible only because I can simply convert the major premiss, as a negative proposition. Accordingly this conversion must here be secretly ge- [text lost] | [...] proposition brought out, however, is an [illegible] immediate consequence from the former. The syllogism thus has four judgments and is a *ratiocinium hybridum*; for example, if I say:",["Restated the long second sentence with 'whereas' and 'therefore' to keep the chain of reasoning visible; the sense is unchanged.","The OCR is corrupt after 'geheim ge-' (probably 'gedacht' or the like): the text breaks off and resumes mid-sentence at 'heraus gebrachte Satz'. I have not filled the gap. 'dingeigonene' is unintelligible and is marked illegible.","Silently corrected 'auf die! 10 Conclusion' to 'auf die Conclusion' and dropped the stray line number and the stray marks (1, N, E). 'Vernunftschluß' = syllogism; 'Obersatz'/'Untersatz' = major/minor premiss."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0035":["No spirit is divisible;\nAll matter is divisible;\n[text corrupt: 'SUO'] its matter a [text corrupt: 'OE']",["The last line is corrupt in the OCR ('SUO ist seine Materie ein OE'). It presumably gave the conclusion, but I have not reconstructed it."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0036":["[...]ability [fähigkeit(?)] in the [text corrupt] is, so [text corrupt] the same with [text corrupt] four judgments:",["The line is almost wholly corrupt ('fähigfeit in dem 22 ist, so m dieselbe mit See und er M vier Urtheile'). I give only the recoverable fragments and have not guessed the sense. The reading should be checked against the Academy Edition."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0037":["No spirit is divisible;\nAnd therefore nothing divisible is a spirit;\nAll matter is divisible;\nConsequently no matter is a spirit.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0038":["In the third figure no others than mixed [text broken off]",["The sentence breaks off here and continues in the next passage ('Vernunftschlüsse möglich'). The stray letter 'i' is OCR noise."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0039":["syllogisms are possible.",["Completes the preceding fragment: 'In the third figure no others than mixed syllogisms are possible.'"]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0040":["The rule of the third figure is as follows: What [belongs(?)] to a thing, or contradicts it, also belongs to, or contradicts, some of those that are contained under another mark of this thing. This proposition itself is true only because I can convert (*per conversionem logicam*) the judgment in which it is said that another mark belongs to this thing, whereby it becomes conformable to the rule of all syllogisms. For example: | All men are sinners;",["'auton' in the OCR is unintelligible; I supply '[belongs(?)]' from the following 'kommt auch zu oder widerspricht'. This is a conjecture and is bracketed.","Silently corrected 't' to 'ist', 'mkehren' to 'umkehren', and 'E heißt' to 'Es heißt' (rendered 'For example', with z. E.)."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0041":["All men are rational;\nTherefore some rational [beings] are sinners.",["'[beings]' is supplied for the substantivised adjective 'Vernünftige'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0042":["| This inference holds only because, by a conversion *per accidens*, I can infer from that minor premiss as follows: consequently some rational beings are men. The concepts are then compared according to the rule of all syllogisms, but only by means of an interpolated immediate inference, and so we have a *ratiocinium hybridum*:",["Silently corrected 'jem' to 'jenem', 'Nenschen' to 'Menschen', 'Berunftschlüsse' to 'Vernunftschlüsse', 'unmittelgaren' to 'unmittelbaren'. I divided the sentence at 'und alsdann'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0043":["All men are sinners;\nAll men are rational;\nConsequently some rational [beings] are men;\nTherefore some rational [beings] are sinners.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0044":["[...]schen the same can very easily be [shown(?)] in the negative mode of this figure, which for the sake of brevity I omit.",["The passage begins with a word fragment ('schen'), and 'eigen' is doubtless a corruption of 'zeigen'. '[shown(?)]' is conjectural. The opening is probably 'Eben dasselbe', but I have not supplied it."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0045":["In the fourth figure no others than mixed",["The sentence continues in the next passage ('Vernunftschlüsse möglich'). 'wie' is Kant's usage for 'als'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0046":["syllogisms are possible.",["Completes the preceding fragment."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0047":["The mode of inference in this form is so unnatural, and rests on so many possible intermediate inferences which must be thought as interpolated, that the rule which I could set forth generally concerning it would be very obscure and unintelligible. For this reason I shall say only on account of what conditions an inferential force lies in it. In the negative modes of these syllogisms a correct inference is possible for the following reason. I can alter the positions of the principal concepts, either by logical conversion or by contraposition, and can therefore think, after each antecedent proposition, its immediate consequence; and so these consequences acquire the relation which they must in general have in a syllogism according to the universal rule. But of the affirmative modes [I] shall",["Silently corrected the OCR slips 'b viel', 'hüffen', 'lunfel', 'm welcher', 'züssen', 'vern­dern', '„gische'. The OCR's 'vereinenden' is read as 'verneinenden' (negative), since the contrast is with 'bejahenden'.","Restated the long sentence on the negative modes as three sentences, as the method allows. '[I]' is supplied because the OCR lacks the subject of 'werde'. The passage breaks off and continues with 'zeigen'.","'Hauptbegriffe' = principal concepts; 'Zwischenschlüsse' = intermediate inferences; 'Schlußkraft' = inferential force."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0048":["show that they are not possible at all in the fourth figure. The negative syllogism according to this figure, as it must properly be thought, will present itself in the following manner:",["Continues 'Von den bejahenden aber werde [ich]'. The OCR 'vereinende' is read as 'verneinende', as in the preceding passage; 'erden' is read as 'werden'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0049":["No dullard is learned;\nConsequently no learned man is dull.\nSome learned men are pious;\nConsequently some pious men are learned;\nTherefore some pious men are not dull.",["'Folgl.' (folglich) is rendered 'Consequently'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0050":["Let there be a syllogism of the second kind:",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0051":["Every spirit is simple;\nEverything simple is imperishable;\nTherefore [text corrupt: 'Sa 1'] is a spirit.",["The subject of the conclusion is corrupt in the OCR. It is presumably 'some imperishable thing', as in the corresponding passage below, but I have not supplied it."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0052":["[...]fach [i.e. is simple]; for just because something is simple, [text corrupt: 'x es nicht Korg ein 1 o'] | Further, by all possible logical alterations the premisses cannot be so arranged that the conclusion, or even merely another proposition from which it would follow as an immediate consequence, could be derived. This holds if, according to the rule once established for all the figures, the principal concepts are to have their positions such that the greater principal concept occurs in the major premiss, the lesser in the minor.) And although, if I alter the positions of the principal concepts completely, so that that becomes the lesser which was before the greater, and conversely, a conclusion from which the given conclusion flows can be inferred, yet in that case a complete transposition of the premisses is also necessary. The so-called syllogism obtained according to the fourth figure contains the materials indeed, but not the form, wher-",["The opening is corrupt and fragmentary; the clause after 'einfach ist' is not recoverable. '[i.e. is simple]' only expands the word fragment 'fach'. The reading should be checked.","The footnote marker ')' stands where the OCR places it; the footnote itself appears in the next passage. Stray 'ss' and 'ate' are OCR noise. 'dei' is read as 'der'.","Divided the long sentence beginning 'Ferner so können' and the following 'Und obgleich' sentence. The text breaks off at 'wor-' and continues in the next but one passage."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0053":[") This rule rests on the synthetic order, in which the remoter mark is compared with the subject first | and then the nearer. Even if it were regarded as merely arbitrary, it becomes indispensable as soon as one wishes to have four figures. For as soon as it is a matter of indifference whether I put the predicate of the conclusion in the major or in the minor premiss, the first figure is not distinguished from the fourth at all. A fault of the same kind is found in Crusius's *Logik*, p. 600, in the Remark.",["Footnote to the sentence on the positions of the principal concepts (see 0052). The footnote sign is kept as ')' because the OCR gives it so.","Silently corrected 'guerft' to 'zuerst', 'ess' to 'es', 'Unterfaß' to 'Untersatz', 'Einen der gleichen' to 'Einen dergleichen'; the stray ')' after '|' is dropped.","'Crusii Logik' is Crusius's logic; I leave the title in its German form."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0054":["[...]according to which the inference is to be drawn. It is no syllogism at all in the logical order, in which alone the division into four figures is possible; in the negative mode of inference in the same figure the case is quite otherwise. For it will have to run thus: | Every spirit is simple; | Everything simple is imperishable;",["Begins with the second half of 'wor-' from 0052; the German reads '...nach geschlossen werden soll', so 'wornach' is presumably meant. I have not completed the broken word.","Running head ('Die falsche Spitzfindigkeit der vier syllogistischen Figuren. 55') omitted. 'möglich si' is read as 'möglich ist'; 'lach' as 'nach'; 'ogischen' as 'logischen'. 'Spitzfindigkeit' = subtlety."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0055":["Therefore every spirit is imperishable;\nConsequently something imperishable is a spirit.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0056":["This inference is quite correct. But a syllogism of this kind is distinguished from that in the first figure not by a different position of the middle principal concept, but only in this, that the positions of the premisses have been changed,) and in the conclusion the positions of the principal concepts. The alteration of the figure, however, does not consist in this at all. A fault of this kind is found at the place cited in Crusius's *Logik*, where it was believed that, by this liberty of altering the position of the premisses, one infers in the fourth figure, and indeed more naturally. It is a pity about the trouble which a great mind takes in wishing to improve a useless thing. One can do something useful only if one | annuls [it]. The logical division of the four syllogistic figures",["Passage continues into the next, 'ist eine falsche Spitzfindigkeit'. The footnote marker ')' after 'worden' is kept as the OCR gives it.","Silently corrected 'Hauptpegrifr8' to 'Hauptbegriffs' and 'Derdndert' to 'verändert'. The OCR 'e vernichtigt' is read as 'sie vernichtigt'; '[it]' is bracketed as a conjecture. 'Mittelbegriff' sense: the middle principal concept.","'Vordersätze' = premisses."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0057":["is a false subtlety.",["Completes the sentence begun at the end of 0056: 'The logical division of the four syllogistic figures is a false subtlety.'"]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0058":["It cannot be denied that correct inferences can be drawn in all these four figures. But it is indisputable that all of them, except [the first], determine the consequence only by a circuitous route and by intermingled intermediate inferences, and that the very same conclusion would follow, pure and unmixed, from the same middle concept in the first figure. Here one might now think that therefore the other three",["'die Irjte' in the OCR is read as 'die erste', hence '[the first]'; this is bracketed because it is a conjecture from the context (the contrast with 'in der ersten Figur').","Silently corrected 'ichtig', 'zämlichen', 'abolgen'. 'Man kann nicht in Abrede sein' is odd German, and I translate it by sense ('cannot be denied'). The sentence breaks off at 'die drei andere'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0059":["[fi]gures would at most be useless, but not false. But if we consider the purpose for which they were invented, and for which they are still taught, we shall judge otherwise. If the aim were to entangle a number of inferences, mixed in among the principal judgments, in such a way that some were expressed and others suppressed, and it would take great art to judge their agreement with the rules of inference, then we could no doubt devise, not indeed more figures, but more enigmatic inferences, enough to rack our brains. But the aim of logic is not to entangle but to resolve, not to present something in concealed fashion but in plain view. These four modes of inference [*Schlußarten*] are therefore required to be simple, unmixed, and free of concealed subsidiary inferences. Otherwise they have no title to appear in a logical exposition as formulas of the most distinct representation of an inference of reason. It is also certain that until now all logicians have regarded them as simple inferences of reason, with no necessary interposition of other judgments; otherwise this right of citizenship would never have been granted them. The other three modes of inference are therefore correct as rules of inferences of reason in general, but false as modes that contain a simple and pure inference. This incorrectness, which turns it into a right to entangle insights, whereas logic has as its proper end the reduction of everything to the simplest mode of knowledge, is the greater the more special rules (each figure has several of its own) are needed to keep us from tripping ourselves up in these side-leaps. Indeed, if ever much acuteness has been spent, and much apparent learning squandered, on a wholly useless matter, it is on this. The so-called *modi* that are possible in each figure are indicated by strange words, which at the same time contain, with much secret art, letters that facilitate the conversion into the first figure. In the future these will constitute a precious rarity, bearing witness to the manner of thinking of the human understanding, when the venerable rust of antiquity shall one day teach a better instructed posterity to admire, and to regret, the industrious and vain efforts of their forefathers upon these relics.\n\n*) For if the proposition in which the predicate of the conclusion occurs is the major premiss, then, in relation to the true conclusion, which here follows immediately from the premisses, the second proposition is the major premiss and the first the minor. But then everything is inferred according to the first figure, except that the conclusion put forward is drawn, by a logical conversion, from the one which follows immediately from the judgments in question.",["The passage opens mid-sentence; 'iguren' is read as [Fi]guren and the missing letters are supplied in square brackets.","The footnote has no visible marker in the OCR text; it is placed after the main text with its sign *). A stray brace before 'dann' and the OCR 'Conufion' (= Conclusion) and 'Bordertzen' (= Vordersätzen) are silently corrected.","The OCR 'zu sii schließen zu beurtheilen' is garbled; I read it as 'to judge [their agreement with] the rules of inference' and flag the reading as doubtful.","Several long sentences are kept whole where possible; the very long final sentence is left as one because its structure is clear in English.","'sich selbst ein Bein unterzuschlagen' is rendered 'tripping ourselves up'; 'Vernunftschluß' = 'inference of reason'; 'Erkenntnißart' = 'mode of knowledge'.","'Modi' is Kant's Latin and is kept in italics; 'Schlußarten' is rendered 'modes of inference'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0060":["It is also easy to discover the first occasion of this over-subtlety. The man who first wrote a syllogism in three rows one beneath the other, regarded it as a chessboard, and tried to see what might result from shifting the position of the middle concept, was",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it is translated as it stands, and the next passage supplies the predicate.","'Begriff' = concept; 'Mittelbegriff' = middle concept."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0061":["just as astonished, on becoming aware that a rational sense was emerging, as one who finds an anagram in a name. It was just as childish to take delight in the one as in the other, especially since in doing so we forgot that nothing new was being produced in respect of distinctness, but only an increase of indistinctness. But such is the lot of the human understanding: either it is subtle to the point of brooding and lapses into absurdities, or it snatches boldly at objects too great for it and builds castles in the air. Of the great crowd of thinkers, one chooses the number 666, another the origin of animals and plants, or the secrets of providence. The error into which both fall is of very different flavour, just as heads differ.",["The running head is omitted. This passage continues the sentence begun in the preceding one ('...was | just as astonished').","'Fratzen' is rendered 'absurdities'; 'grüblerisch' as 'subtle to the point of brooding'.","The OCR 'herangkam' is corrected to 'herauskam'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0062":["In our times things worth knowing are accumulating. Soon our capacity will be too weak and our lifetime too short to grasp even the most useful part of them. Riches offer themselves in superabundance, and in order to take them in we must again throw away much useless lumber. It would have been better never to have burdened ourselves with it. I should flatter myself too much if I believed that the labour of a few hours could suffice to overthrow the colossus whose head is hidden in the clouds of antiquity and whose feet are of clay. My intention is only to give an account of why, in my logical lectures, in which I cannot arrange everything in accordance with my own insight, but must in some matters yield to the prevailing taste, I shall be brief on these topics, in order to employ the time thus gained in the real extension of useful insights.",["The OCR 'junfere' is corrected to 'unsere', 'Tvon' to 'Thon', and a stray bracket before 'einrichten' is dropped.","'Fähigkeit' is rendered 'capacity'; 'Einsicht' as 'insight'.","The long final sentence is kept as one, since its sense is clear."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0063":["There is yet another use of syllogistic, namely to outstrip the unwary in a learned dispute. But since this belongs to the athletics of the learned, an art which may otherwise be very useful, save that it contributes little to the advantage of truth, I pass it over here in silence.",["The text ends 'Stili—' (broken off by the OCR); I read it as 'Stillschweigen' and supply 'in silence'. The reading is conjectural.","The OCR 'geiner' is corrected to 'einer'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0064":["§ 6.\nConcluding Reflection.",[]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0065":["We are accordingly instructed that the supreme rules of all inferences of reason lead immediately to that order of concepts which is entitled the first figure. All other transpositions of the middle concept yield a correct inference only in so far as they lead, through easy immediate inferences, to propositions which are connected in the simple order of the first figure. It is impossible to infer simply and unmixed in more than one figure, since it is always the first figure alone, lying concealed in an inference of reason by way of hidden inferences, that contains the force of inference, while the altered position of the concepts merely causes a smaller or greater detour, which we have to traverse in order to see the consequence. And the division of the figures is, in general, false and impossible, in so far as the figures are supposed to contain pure inferences not mixed with intermediate judgments. How our general fundamental rules of all inferences of reason at the same time contain the special rules of the so-called first figure, and likewise how, from the given conclusion and the middle principal concept, any inference of reason in one of the remaining figures can at once be changed into the first and simple mode of inference, without the useless prolixity of the formulas of reduction, so that either the conclusion itself, or a proposition from which it follows by immediate inference, is what is inferred, can be gathered so easily from our exposition that I shall not dwell upon it.",["Restated as several sentences: Kant's single sentence with its chain of 'daß' clauses is divided at the semicolons of sense; no content is added.","'Vernunftschluß' = inference of reason; 'Schlußsatz' = conclusion; 'Zwischenurtheile' = intermediate judgments; 'heißen' = 'is entitled'.","'mittlern Hauptbegriffe' is rendered 'middle principal concept', literally; the sense is doubtful and perhaps means the middle term together with the main concepts.","Stray OCR letters (P, 3, 1, 15]) are omitted; 'chlüsse' and 'ste' are silently corrected."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0066":["I do not wish to end this reflection without adding a few remarks which might also be of considerable use in other connections.",["The stray letters 'Bd' and the line number are OCR residue and are omitted."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0067":["I say, then, first, that a distinct concept is possible only through a | judgment, and a complete concept only through an inference of reason [*Vernunftschluss*]. For a distinct concept requires that I cognize something clearly as a mark of a thing, and this is a judgment. In order to have a distinct concept of body, | I represent impenetrability clearly to myself as a mark of it. But this representation is nothing other than the thought: a body | is impenetrable. Here it need only be observed that this judgment is not itself the distinct concept, but the act by which it becomes actual; for the representation which arises from the thing itself after this act is distinct. It is easy to show that a complete concept is possible only through an inference of reason; one need only consult the first paragraph of this treatise. On this account a distinct concept might also be described as one that is clear through a judgment, and a complete concept as one that is distinct through an inference of reason",["OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. 'set' as 'sei', 'ev' as 'er', 'Korper' as 'Körper'); page/line numerals and fragments dropped; the | signs are kept where they stand.","Vernunftschluss rendered 'inference of reason' (Kant's syllogism, Latin *ratiocinium*); 'cognize' is used for erkennen to keep 'knowledge' for Erkenntnis.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('...durch einen Vernunftschluß'); the continuation ('deutlich ist') is in the next passage and is not supplied here.","'Hiebei ist nur zu merken' etc. rendered closely; 'Handlung' is 'act'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0068":["is distinct. If the completeness is of the first degree, the inference of reason is a simple one; if it is of the second or third degree, it is possible only through a series of chain-inferences [*Kettenschlüsse*], which the understanding abridges after the manner of a *sorites*. From this there is also evident an essential defect of logic as it is commonly treated, namely that distinct and complete concepts are dealt with before judgments and inferences of reason, although the former are possible only through the latter.",["Running head ('Die falsche Spitzfindigkeit der vier syllogistischen Figuren. 59') omitted.","OCR 'voll— E Begriffen' read as 'vollständigen Begriffen'; 'wie' after 'eher' rendered 'before' (older usage for 'als').","The opening 'deutlich ist' completes the previous passage's sentence."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0069":["Secondly, it is just as evident that no other fundamental power [*Grundkraft*] of the soul is required for the complete concept than for the distinct (inasmuch as the very same faculty that cognizes something immediately as a mark in a thing is also used to represent in this mark again another",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; the continuation ('Merkmal', the initial 'M' being lost to OCR) is in the next passage.","OCR 'pom' read as 'Begriffe' (zum vollständigen Begriffe) and 'tote' as 'wie'; 'seine' read as 'keine'. These are conjectural restorations; 'keine' is required by the sense.","The parenthesis is left open, closing in the next passage; 'Grundkraft' rendered 'fundamental power'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0070":["mark, and so to think the thing through a remote mark): so it is equally easy to see that understanding and reason, that is, the faculty of cognizing distinctly and that of making inferences of reason, are not different fundamental faculties. Both consist in the faculty of judging; but when one judges",["Initial letter lost in OCR ('erkmal' restored as 'Merkmal'), joining the previous passage's sentence.","'Grundfähigkeiten' rendered 'fundamental faculties'; the sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage ('mittelbar urtheilt, so schließt man').","The colon after the parenthesis has been kept, though the English 'so ... equally' is smoothed from 'eben so ... eben so'."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0071":["mediately, one infers.",["Initial letters lost in OCR ('ittelbar' restored as 'mittelbar'). Completes the sentence begun in the previous passage."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0072":["Thirdly, it may also be gathered from this that the higher faculty of knowledge rests absolutely on the faculty of judging alone. Accordingly, if a being can judge, it has the higher faculty of knowledge. If one finds reason to deny it the latter, it is also unable to judge. Neglect of such considerations led a celebrated scholar to concede distinct concepts to the animals.",["Initial letters lost in OCR ('chlechterdings' restored as 'schlechterdings'; 'obere Erkenntnißkraft' rendered 'higher faculty of knowledge').","'Erkenntnißfähigkeit' and 'Erkenntnißkraft' both rendered 'faculty of knowledge'; the 'celebrated scholar' is not named in the text."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0073":["An ox, it is said, has in its representation of the stall also a",["Initial letters lost in OCR ('in Ochs' restored as 'Ein Ochs'). Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage ('klare Vorstellung...')."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0074":["clear representation of its mark, the door, and therefore a distinct concept of the stall. It is easy to prevent the confusion here. The distinctness of a concept does not consist in this, that what is a mark of the thing is clearly represented, but in this, that it is cognized *as* a mark of the thing. The door is indeed something belonging to the stall and can serve as a mark of it; but only he who forms the judgment, this door belongs to this stall, has a distinct concept of the building, and this is certainly beyond the faculty of cattle. | I go further and say: it is quite another thing to distinguish things from one another and to cognize the difference of things. The latter is possible only through judging, and cannot happen in any irrational animal. The following division may be of great use. To distinguish logically means to cognize that a thing A is not B, and is always a negative judgment; to distinguish physically means to be driven to different actions by different representations. The dog distinguishes the roast from the bread, because it is affected differently by the roast than by the bread (for different things cause different sensations), and the sensation of the former is a ground of a different appetite in it than that of the latter,*) according to the natural connection of its drives with its representations. From this one may take occasion to reflect better on the essential difference between rational and irrational animals. If one could see what that secret power is through which judging becomes possible, one would untie the knot. My present opinion is that this power or faculty is nothing other than the faculty of inner sense, that is, of making one's own representations the object of one's thoughts. This faculty is not to be derived from another; it is a fundamental faculty in the proper sense, and, as I hold, can be peculiar only to rational beings. On it, however, the whole higher faculty of knowledge rests. I close with a reflection which must be agreeable to those who can feel pleasure in the unity of human knowledge. All affirmative judgments stand under one common formula, the principle of agreement: *Cuilibet subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum*; all negative judgments under the principle of contradiction: *Nulli subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi oppositum*. All affirmative inferences of reason are contained under the rule: *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius*; all negative under this: *Oppositum notae opponitur rei ipsi*. All judgments that stand immediately under the principles of agreement or of contradiction, that is, those in which neither the identity nor the conflict is perceived through an intermediate mark (hence not by means of the analysis of concepts), but immediately, are",["OCR slips silently corrected ('Bund' as 'Hund', 'set' as 'sei', 'Piere' as 'Tiere', 'Cuilibet' retained); numerals and stray letters dropped; the | signs kept.","Breaks off mid-sentence ('sind ...'); continued in the next passage ('unerweisliche Urtheile').","'Hund' is a conjectural correction of OCR 'Bund'; 'Begierde' rendered 'appetite', 'Triebe' 'drives', 'Merkmal' 'mark', 'innerer Sinn' 'inner sense'.","'Sinne des Merkmals' in the opening ('seinem Merkmale der Thüre') rendered 'its mark, the door'; the first words ('lare') restored as 'klare'.","The asterisk marks the footnote that follows in the next passage."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0075":["*) It is in fact of the utmost importance to attend to this in the investigation of animal nature. In animals we become aware only of external actions, whose diversity indicates different determinations of their appetite. Whether there takes place in their inner being that act of the faculty of knowledge by which they become conscious of the agreement or conflict of what is in one sensation with what is found in another, and so judge, does not follow from this at all.",["Footnote to the preceding passage. OCR 'da sie sich)' read as 'da sie sich'; stray numeral 'Z' and '3:' dropped.","'Bestimmungen ihrer Begierde' rendered 'determinations of their appetite'; 'Erkenntnißkraft' 'faculty of knowledge'.","'diejenige Handlung ... da sie sich ... bewußt sind' is a loose construction, smoothed in English."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0076":["The False Subtlety of the Four Syllogistic Figures.",["Title of Kant's 1762 essay (*Die falsche Spitzfindigkeit der vier syllogistischen Figuren*). \"Spitzfindigkeit\" is rendered \"subtlety\" in the pejorative sense of hair-splitting; \"falsche\" is rendered \"false\" and is kept in the sense of spurious or mistaken.","\"Figuren\" is rendered \"figures\", the usual English term for the syllogistic figures."]],"falsche-spitzfindigkeit-0077":["indemonstrable judgments; those in which it can be cognized mediately are demonstrable. Human knowledge is full of such indemonstrable judgments. Several of them occur before any definition, as soon as one, in order to arrive at it, represents to oneself as a mark of a thing what one cognizes first and immediately in it. Those philosophers err who proceed as if there were no indemonstrable fundamental truths besides one. Those err just as much who, without sufficient warrant, are too liberal in deeming several of their propositions worthy of this distinction.",["Continues 'sind' from the previous passage; initial letters lost in OCR ('anerweisliche' restored as 'unerweisliche', 'nd erweislich' as 'sind erweislich', here 'are demonstrable').","Several words are garbled in OCR: 'Beltweife' read as 'Weltweise' (philosophers), 'ihne' as 'ohne', 'Brundwahrheiten' as 'Grundwahrheiten', 'liefjes' as 'dieses'. The reading 'Irtheile' is 'Urtheile'. These restorations are conjectural.","'Gewährleistung' rendered 'warrant'; 'zu würdigen' rendered 'deeming worthy', the ending 'dieses Vorzugs' as 'this distinction'."]],"beweisgrund-0001":["The Only Possible Ground of Proof for a Demonstration of the Existence of God, by M. Immanuel Kant.",["Title page; 'Beweisgrund' rendered 'ground of proof', 'Dasein' 'existence'. 'M.' stands for *Magister*."]],"beweisgrund-0002":["Preface.\n*Ne mea dona tibi studio disposta fideli,*",["Lucretius, *De rerum natura* I.52, continued in the next passage; left in Latin, not translated here."]],"beweisgrund-0003":["*Intellecta prius quam sint, contempta relinquas.*",["Lucretius, *De rerum natura* I.53, the second line of the motto begun in the previous passage. ('Lest you, before you have understood them, leave my gifts, arranged with faithful zeal, in contempt.')"]],"beweisgrund-0004":["LUCRETIUS.",["Attribution of the motto, left as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0005":["I do not hold so high an opinion of the usefulness of an undertaking such as the present as to suppose that the most important of all our modes of knowledge, namely that there is a God, wavers and is in danger unless it has the aid of profound metaphysical investigations. Providence has not willed that the insights most indispensable to our happiness should rest on the subtlety of refined inferences. It has delivered them immediately to the natural common understanding, which, if it is not confused by false art, does not fail to lead us straight to what is true and useful, so far as we stand in extreme need of it. Hence that use of sound reason which still keeps within the limits of common insight furnishes sufficiently convincing proofs of the existence and the attributes of this Being, although the subtle inquirer everywhere misses demonstration and the precision of exactly determined concepts or of regularly connected inferences of reason. Nevertheless one cannot refrain from seeking this demonstration, to see whether it may not somewhere present itself. For, to say nothing of the reasonable desire which an understanding accustomed to inquiry cannot renounce, to attain in so important a [mode of] knowledge something complete and distinctly conceived, there is the further hope that such an insight, once it had been mastered, might throw light on much more in this field. But to reach this end one must venture upon the bottomless abyss of metaphysics. It is a dark ocean without shore and without lighthouses, where one must proceed as the mariner does on an uncharted sea. As soon as he sets foot on land anywhere, he examines his voyage and inquires whether unnoticed currents may not have disturbed his course, in spite of all the caution which the art of navigation can ever enjoin.",["First sentence restated: the OCR 'seine so hohe' is read as 'keine so hohe' (negation required by the sense); 'vic' read as 'wie'. Doubtful OCR; flagged.","'Erkenntnisse' is rendered 'modes of knowledge'; in 'in einer so wichtigen Erkenntniß' the singular is rendered '[mode of] knowledge', the bracket marking the supplied words.","Silently corrected OCR slips: 'Glück Jeligfeit' = Glückseligkeit; 'Irmangelt' = ermangelt; 'geunden' = gesunden; 'Figenschaften' = Eigenschaften; 'entbrechen' = enthalten/entbrechen kept as 'refrain'.","The sentence beginning 'Denn ohne der billigen Begierde...' is somewhat loosely constructed; I kept the concessive 'to say nothing of' and supplied 'there is the further hope' for 'so ist noch zu hoffen'.","The last sentence is divided: the mariner's relative clause is made a separate sentence, and 'unde | merkte' is read as 'unbemerkte'.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Vernunft' = reason; 'Schlüsse' / 'Vernunftschlüsse' = inferences / inferences of reason."]],"beweisgrund-0006":["This demonstration has meanwhile never yet been discovered, as others have already remarked. What I here supply is likewise only the ground of proof [*Beweisgrund*] for a demonstration, a laboriously collected stock of building materials, which is laid before the connoisseur for his examination, so that out of its serviceable pieces the edifice may be completed according to the rules of durability and of harmonious proportion. I do not wish what I supply to be taken for the demonstration itself; just as little are the analyses of the concepts which I employ already definitions. They are, as it seems to me, correct marks of the things with which I deal, adequate to lead from them to exact explanations, and in themselves serviceable for the sake of truth and distinctness; but they still await the last touch of the artist before they can be counted among definitions. There is a time when, in a science such as metaphysics, men trust themselves to explain everything and to demonstrate everything, and again another time when they venture upon such undertakings only with fear and mistrust.",["'Baugeräth' rendered 'stock of building materials' (literally building equipment/tackle); a choice.","'Auflösungen der Begriffe' = 'analyses of the concepts'; 'Merkmale' = 'marks'.","'Demonstration' retained as the English cognate; 'Definitionen' likewise.","Silently corrected OCR: 'ich) / id)' = ich; 'ftd)' = sich; 'aus!' = aus; stray numerals ('1l', '2') omitted as page/line residue."]],"beweisgrund-0007":["The reflections which I set forth are the outcome of long thought, but the manner of presentation bears the mark of an unfinished elaboration, inasmuch as various occupations have left me not the time required for it. It is, however, a very vain piece of flattery to beg the reader's pardon for having been able, whatever the cause, to offer him only something poor. He will never forgive it, however one may excuse oneself. In my case the not fully finished form of the work is to be ascribed not so much to neglect as to an omission made with intention. I wished only to sketch the first lines of a principal outline, according to which, as I believe, an edifice of no small excellence could be erected, if in more practised hands the drawing were to gain greater correctness in its parts and a perfect regularity in the whole. With this aim it would have been needless to expend too much anxious care in painting in every line exactly in the individual parts, since the design as a whole has first of all to await the strict judgment of the masters of the art. I have therefore often adduced only proofs, without presuming that I could for the present show distinctly their connection with the conclusion. I have at times adduced judgments of the common understanding, without giving them, by logical art, the form of solidity which a structural piece must have in a system. Either I found this difficult, or the prolixity of the necessary preparation was not in keeping with the size which the work was to have, or else I believed myself entitled, since I announce no demonstration, to be excused from the demand which is rightly made of systematic authors. A small part of those who arrogate to themselves judgment upon works of the mind cast bold glances at the whole of an essay, and consider chiefly the relation which its main parts might have to a sound structure, if certain deficiencies were supplied or faults corrected. This kind of reader is the one whose judgment is chiefly of use to human knowledge. As for the rest, who, unable to survey a connection on the large scale, cling captiously to one or another small part, unconcerned whether the censure which it may perhaps deserve also impugns the worth of the whole, and whether improvements in individual pieces may not preserve the main plan, which is faulty only in parts, and who are ever intent on turning every begun building into ruins: they may indeed be formidable on account of their number, but as regards the decision of true worth their judgment is of little weight among reasonable men.",["Long sentences divided, notably the sentence 'Ich habe bisweilen gemeine Verstandesurtheile...', where the three alternative reasons are set out in separate sentences.","OCR corrected silently: 'vere | gebliche' = vergebliche; 'ente | schuldigen' = entschuldigen; 'Unter | laffung' = Unterlassung; 'llererst' = allererst; 'pap' = daß; 'ogische' = logische; 'ftd)' = sich; 'Hau' = Bau; 'teje' = Diese/Jene (read as 'those'); 'konnen ... ein' = können ... sein.","'dem menschlichen Erkenntniß' rendered 'human knowledge'.","The final sentence has an anacoluthic construction (the relative clauses with 'Was die übrige anlangt' are resumed by 'konnen'); I resumed it with 'they' after a colon. The words 'ob der Tadel ... auch den Werth' are read with 'Berth' = Werth.","OCR garbled: 'is 6, deren' read as 'ist es, deren'; 'in eingelnen' = in einzelnen; 'Hauptrisses' rendered 'principal outline'."]],"beweisgrund-0008":["In some places I have perhaps not explained myself circumstantially enough to deprive those who desire only a plausible occasion for casting upon my work the bitter reproach of heterodoxy of every opportunity for it. But what caution could have prevented that? I believe, however, that I have spoken clearly enough for those who wish to find in a writing nothing other than what it was the author's intention to put into it. I have engaged in refutations as little as possible, however widely my propositions differ from those of others. This setting of the one against the other I leave to the reflection of the reader who has studied both. If one were to examine the judgments of unperverted reason in various thinking persons with the sincerity of an unbribed advocate, who weighs the grounds of two contending parties in such a way that he puts himself in thought in the place of those who advance them, in order to find them as strong as they can possibly become, and only then determines to which party he will devote himself, there would be much less disagreement in the opinions of philosophers; and an unfeigned fairness in taking up the cause of the opponent, as far as is possible, would soon unite inquiring minds upon one road.",["'Irrglaubens' rendered 'heterodoxy' (literally false belief); a choice.","OCR corrected silently: 'ine' = eine; 'Ge— egenheit' = Gelegenheit; 'hohl' = wohl (omitted in the English as an idle particle); 'die möglich' = als möglich; 'derer ihren' kept as 'those of others'; 'stachdenken' = Nachdenken; 'nii' = mit; 'j selbst' = selbst.","The long conditional sentence is kept as one but its final clause is separated by a semicolon; the infinitive construction 'um sie so stark zu finden... und dann allererst auszumachen' is made finite ('and only then determines').","The terminal sign | is carried over in the German text only as a page break; it is omitted here since nothing follows it in the English."]],"beweisgrund-0009":["In so difficult a consideration as the present one, I may well prepare myself beforehand to find that many a proposition is incorrect, many an elucidation inadequate, and many an exposition frail [*gebrechlich*] and defective. I make no claim to unqualified subscription on the reader's part, since I should hardly grant such a concession to any author myself. It will therefore not be strange to me to be better instructed by others on various points, and I shall be found willing to accept further instruction. It is hard to renounce a claim to correctness which one confidently put forward at the outset, when one was presenting one's grounds; but it is not so hard if the claim was mild, tentative and modest. Even the subtlest vanity, if it understands its own interest, will observe that it requires no less merit to let oneself be convinced than to convince others, and that the former action perhaps does more true honour, inasmuch as it demands more renunciation and self-examination than the latter. It might seem a violation of the unity which one must keep in view in considering one's subject, that here and there fairly extended physical elucidations occur. But since my aim in these cases is directed chiefly to the method of ascending to the knowledge of God by means of natural science, I could not well have attained this end without such examples. The seventh consideration of the second division needs somewhat more indulgence on this account, especially as its content has been drawn from a book which I formerly published without giving my name, in which",["Stray footnote numerals and OCR debris (\"1)\", \"2\", \"a\", \"»\") removed silently; the footnote marker after *ehedem* points to the note translated as the next passage.","\"forhen Unterricht\" is corrupt in the OCR; I read *fernern* ('further'), which the sense requires. Doubtful.","The passage breaks off at \"wo\" and is translated as it stands, without completion.","*Betrachtung* is rendered 'consideration', the sections of the work being so entitled; *Erkenntniß* = knowledge.","I divided the long opening and the vanity sentences into several; the thought is unchanged."]],"beweisgrund-0010":["The title of this book is *Allgemeine Naturgeschichte und Theorie des Himmels* [Universal Natural History and Theory of the Heavens], Königsberg and Leipzig, 1755. This work, which became little known, must among others also have failed to come to the knowledge of the famous Herr J. H. Lambert. Six years afterwards, in his *Cosmological Letters* of 1761, he put forward the very same theory of the systematic constitution of the universe on the large scale, of the Milky Way, the nebulous stars, and so on. [In my book] this was treated more fully, although in connection with various somewhat venturesome hypotheses. The affinity which the permissible freedom of venturing upon such explanations has, at the least, with my principal aim, and likewise the wish to see some part of this hypothesis judged by connoisseurs, have led me to insert this consideration. It is perhaps too short for all its grounds to be understood, or else too lengthy for those who expect to meet with nothing but metaphysics here, and who can readily pass it over. It will perhaps be necessary, before reading this work, to correct some misprints which might alter the sense of the exposition, and which are listed at the end of the work.",["This is the footnote to *ehedem* in the preceding passage; OCR stray letters and hyphen-breaks silently corrected. \"seht\" and the like not present; \"Weltbaues\" rendered 'universe'.","\"die hievon ausführlicher ... gehandelt ward\": the antecedent is ambiguous; I took it to mean Kant's earlier book (hence the bracketed \"[In my book]\"), since *obzwar* ... *Hypothesen* fits that work. Doubtful.","\"Kenner\" rendered 'connoisseurs'; \"Verfassung\" as 'constitution'."]],"beweisgrund-0011":["In the original edition (1763). Cf. below pp. 139–141.",["Editor's footnote of the Academy Edition (presumably to the sentence on misprints); the page references are to the Academy Edition's original pagination."]],"beweisgrund-0012":["The work itself consists of three divisions, of which the first sets forth the ground of proof itself, the second its extensive utility, and the third the grounds for showing that no other [ground of proof] is possible for a demonstration of the existence of God. [...] one finds in my above-mentioned Theory of the Heavens in the first part, and likewise in the preface there, and of which something is indicated in a brief outline on pages 154 to 158 of the present work. The agreement of the thoughts of this ingenious man with those which I then put forward, which coincide with one another almost down to the smaller features, increases my conjecture that this sketch will in due course receive further confirmation.",["The passage is disordered: the sentence beginning \"man in meiner gedachten Theorie\" is the tail of a sentence from the main text whose beginning is missing here, so I have marked the gap with [...] and not filled it.","The \"Gee danken\" slip corrected; the footnote sign after \"158\" omitted.","\"[ground of proof]\" is supplied; *Beweisgrund* = 'ground of proof'; *Dasein* = existence.","\"dieses sinnreichen Mannes\" presumably refers to Lambert (see preceding footnote)."]],"beweisgrund-0013":["First Division, |",[]],"beweisgrund-0014":["in which",[]],"beweisgrund-0015":["the ground of proof for a demonstration of the existence of God",[]],"beweisgrund-0016":["is furnished. |",["Title-page fragments, 0013–0016, form a single heading; the sign | is the Academy's page-break mark and is retained."]],"beweisgrund-0017":["First Consideration.\nOf Existence in General.",[]],"beweisgrund-0018":["The rule of thoroughness does not always require that, even in the most profound exposition, every concept which occurs should be developed or explained. This holds when one is assured that the merely clear, common concept, in the case in which it is employed, can occasion no misunderstanding. So the geometer discloses the most hidden properties and relations of the extended with the greatest certainty, although in so doing he makes use only of the common concept of space; and so, even in the most profound science, the word representation [*Vorstellung*] is understood accurately enough and used with confidence, although its meaning can never be resolved by an explanation.",["OCR \"seinen Mißverstand\" corrected silently to *keinen*, as the sense demands; \"ere klärt\" and \"veran- lassen\" joined.","I divided the sentence at the colon and the semicolon into three sentences, retaining the two illustrations.","*Meßkünstler* = 'geometer'; *Erklärung* = 'explanation' (not 'definition')."]],"beweisgrund-0019":["I should therefore not venture, in these considerations, so far as to resolve the very simple and well-understood concept of existence, were not this precisely the case in which such neglect can occasion confusion and important errors. It is certain that in the rest of philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] the concept can be employed without hesitation, undeveloped as it occurs in common use. The single question of absolutely necessary and contingent existence is the exception; for here a subtler inquiry has drawn erroneous conclusions from a concept otherwise quite pure, which has been unhappily over-refined, and these errors have spread over one of the most sublime parts of philosophy.",["OCR \"[o\" read as *so*, and \"gefünftelten\" as *gekünstelten* ('over-refined'); both silently corrected, the second doubtful.","*Weltweisheit* rendered 'philosophy'; *Dasein* 'existence'.","The final sentence was divided at the semicolon of the German for clarity."]],"beweisgrund-0020":["Let no one expect me to begin with a formal explanation of existence. It were to be wished that this were never done where it is so uncertain whether one has explained correctly, and this is the case more often than one thinks. I shall proceed as one who is seeking the definition, and who first assures himself of what can be said with certainty, affirmatively or negatively, of the object of the explanation, although this does not yet determine in what its fully determinate concept consists. Long before one ventures an explanation of one's object, and even when one has no confidence at all in giving it, one can say much of the same thing with the greatest certainty. I doubt whether anyone has ever correctly explained what space is. Yet without entering upon that, I am certain that where it is there must be external relations, that it cannot have more than three dimensions, and so on. A desire, be it what it may, is grounded upon some representation; it presupposes a pleasure in what is desired, and so forth. Often what pertains to the purpose of our inquiry can be derived quite safely from what one knows with certainty of the thing before all definition; and one then ventures into unnecessary difficulties if one strains oneself so far as that. The mania for method, the imitation of the mathematician, who advances securely on a well-levelled road, upon the slippery ground of metaphysics has occasioned a multitude of such false steps, which one constantly has before one's eyes, and yet there is little hope that one will be warned by them and learn to be more cautious. It is by this method alone that I hope for some elucidations which I have sought in vain among others. For as to the flattering notion which one entertains, that by greater acuteness one will hit upon the truth better than others, it is well understood that all who have wished to draw us out of another's error into their own have at all times spoken thus. Existence is no predicate or determination [*Determination*] of",["OCR \"Mbmessungen\" read as *Abmessungen* ('dimensions'); \"sich)\" cleaned; \"sein Prädicat\" read as *kein Prädicat*, as the sense and Kant's thesis demand.","The passage breaks off at \"von\"; the sentence is completed in the next passage (\"any thing\"), but is here left as it stands.","*Erklärung* ('explanation') is distinguished from *Definition*; *Determination* kept alongside *Prädicat* (cf. *Bestimmung*).","The long sentence on the 'mania for method' and the one on 'acuteness' were left as single sentences, being sufficiently clear."]],"beweisgrund-0021":["any thing.",["Continuation of the last sentence of the preceding passage: 'Existence is no predicate or determination of any thing.'"]],"beweisgrund-0022":["This proposition seems strange and absurd, yet it is beyond doubt certain. Take any subject you please, for example Julius Caesar. Gather together in him all conceivable predicates, not even excepting those of time and place, and you will soon grasp that he can exist with all these determinations, or can equally well not exist. The Being who gave existence to this world and to this hero in it could know all these predicates, not a single one excepted, and yet regard him as a merely possible thing which, apart from that Being's decree, does not exist. Who can deny that millions of things which are not actually there are merely possible in respect of all the predicates they would contain if they existed; and that not a single determination is lacking in the representation which the highest Being has of them, although existence is not among them, since He knows them only as possible things? It cannot therefore be the case that, when they exist, they contain one predicate more; for in the possibility of a thing, according to its thoroughgoing determination, no predicate can be wanting. And if it had pleased God to create another series of things, another world, that world would have existed with all the determinations, and with no more, which He nevertheless knows in it, although it is merely possible.",["'Rathschluß' rendered 'decree' (divine decree); the clause 'seinen Rathschluß ausgenommen' is rendered 'apart from that Being's decree' on the judgment that the antecedent is the Wesen.","The long sentence beginning 'Wer kann in Abrede ziehen' is kept as a question, but the 'denn' clause is joined by a semicolon and a 'since'.","'Erkennen' is rendered 'know' (not 'cognize'); 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'durchgängige Bestimmung' = thoroughgoing determination.","'sein Prädicat' in 'kann gar sein Prädicat fehlen' is evidently a misprint/OCR slip for 'kein Prädicat'; rendered 'no predicate'.","'eriftirt' corrected to 'existirt' (OCR)."]],"beweisgrund-0023":["Nevertheless we make use of the expression 'existence' as a predicate, and may do so safely and without fear of error, so long as we do not set out to derive existence from merely possible concepts, as is customary when one wishes to prove absolutely necessary existence. For in that case one looks in vain among the predicates of such a possible being; existence is certainly not to be found among them. In those cases, however, in which existence occurs in common speech as a predicate, it is not so much a predicate of the thing itself as of the thought which one has of it. For example: existence belongs to the sea-unicorn, not to the land-unicorn. This means nothing else than that the representation of the sea-unicorn is a concept of experience, that is, the representation of an existing thing. Hence, in order to establish the correctness of this proposition about the existence of such a thing, one does not search in the concept of the subject, for there one finds only predicates of possibility, but in the source of the knowledge which I have of it. I have seen it, one says, or have heard of it from those who have seen it. It is therefore not a wholly correct expression to say, 'A sea-unicorn is an existing animal'; one should say conversely, 'To a certain existing sea-animal belong the predicates which I think together in a unicorn.' Not 'regular hexagons exist in nature', but 'to certain things in nature, such as the cells of bees or rock-crystal, belong the predicates which are thought together in a hexagon.' Every human language has, from the accidents of its origin, certain inaccuracies which cannot be altered; and it would be over-subtle and useless, where no misinterpretation can result from them in ordinary usage, to tinker with it and restrict it. It is enough that in the rarer cases of a more elevated mode of consideration, where it is necessary, these distinctions be added. What has here been set forth can be adequately judged only when the following has been read.",["The opening 'bedient man sich)' has a stray parenthesis (OCR); omitted. 'fann', 'Srrthümer', 'erijtivenden', 'diePrädicate', 'Ursprungseinige' are OCR slips silently corrected; 'Ge— danken' hyphenation joined.","'Seeeinhorn'/'Landeinhorn' rendered 'sea-unicorn' (narwhal) and 'land-unicorn'.","'Erfahrungsbegriff' = concept of experience; 'Erkenntniß' = knowledge.","The last sentence is cut at the section break; the 'following' refers to Section 2.","'wo es nöthig ist' retained as a separate clause; sentence 'Eine jede menschliche Sprache…' divided into two sentences."]],"beweisgrund-0024":["2.",[]],"beweisgrund-0025":["Existence is the absolute position of a thing, and in this respect is distinguished from any predicate, which as such is always posited merely relatively to another thing.",["The heading continues through beweisgrund-0025 to -0027 ('...welches als | solches jederzeit blos beziehungsweise auf ein ander Ding | gesetzt wird'); the three fragments are here given as one sentence, divided at the points of the German break. Passage -0025 breaks off at 'welches als'.","'Position/Setzung' = position/positing; 'unterscheidet sich' rendered 'is distinguished'."]],"beweisgrund-0026":["such is always posited merely relatively to another thing",["Fragment continuing 0025; the sentence ends in 0027."]],"beweisgrund-0027":["[is] posited.",["'gesetzt wird' completes the heading begun in 0025; the auxiliary 'is' is bracketed as needed in the English."]],"beweisgrund-0028":["The concept of position or positing is perfectly simple and is identical with that of being in general. Now something can be thought as posited merely relatively, or, better, merely the relation (*respectus logicus*) of something, as a mark, to a thing can be thought; and in that case being, that is, the position of this relation, is nothing but the copula [*Verbindungsbegriff*] in a judgment. If not merely this relation but the thing in and for itself is considered as posited, this being is the same as existence.",["'Verbindungsbegriff' rendered 'copula', the concept of connection in a judgment; German given in brackets.","'ftd)' read as 'sich' (OCR): 'an und für sich selbst' = 'in and for itself'.","*respectus logicus* kept in Latin."]],"beweisgrund-0029":["This concept is so simple that nothing can be said towards its elucidation except to remark the caution that it be not confused with the relations which things have to their marks. If one realises that all our knowledge ends in the last resort in indecomposable concepts, one will also understand that there must be some which are almost indecomposable, that is, in which the marks are only very little clearer and simpler than the thing itself. This is the case with our explanation of existence. I gladly admit that by it the concept of what is explained is made distinct only in a very small degree. But the nature of the object, in relation to the faculties of our understanding, permits no higher degree.",["The stray 'd' before 'Wenn' is an OCR remnant (probably a footnote or marginal mark); omitted.","'Erflärten' corrected to 'Erklärten'; 'klärer' read as 'klarer'.","'Vermögen' rendered 'faculties' (plural, of the understanding); 'Auswickelung' = elucidation.","'unauflöslich' rendered 'indecomposable' (i.e. incapable of analysis into simpler marks)."]],"beweisgrund-0030":["When I say, God is omnipotent, only this logical relation between God and omnipotence is thought, the latter being a mark of the former. Nothing further is here posited. Whether God *is*, that is, is posited absolutely or exists, is not contained in it at all. Hence this 'is' is quite correctly used even of the relations which non-entities [*Undinge*] have to one another; for example, 'The God of Spinoza is subject to unceasing changes.'",["'Werkmal' (OCR 'Werk— mal') read as 'Merkmal' = mark.","'eriftire' corrected to 'existire'.","'Undinge' rendered 'non-entities'; the sense is things which are impossible or non-existent."]],"beweisgrund-0031":["If I represent to myself God as pronouncing his almighty 'Let it be' over a possible world, he gives the whole represented in his understanding no new determinations; he adds no new predicate, but posits this series of things, in which everything else was posited only relatively to this whole, with all its predicates absolutely or simply. The relations of all predicates to their subjects never denote anything existing, unless the subject has already been presupposed as existing. 'God is omnipotent' must remain a true proposition even in the judgment of one who does not know his existence, provided only he understands me rightly in the way I take the concept of God. But his existence must belong immediately to the manner in which his concept is posited, for it is not to be found in the predicates themselves. And if the subject is not already presupposed as existing, then with every predicate it remains undetermined whether it belongs to an existing or to a merely possible subject. Existence can therefore not itself be a predicate. If I say, 'God is an existing thing', it seems as if I were expressing the relation of a predicate to the subject. But there is an inaccuracy in this expression also. Strictly speaking, it should run: 'Something existing is God'; that is, to an existing thing belong those predicates which, taken together, we designate by the expression 'God'. These predicates are posited relatively to this subject; but the thing itself, together with all its predicates, is posited absolutely.",["'fegt' corrected to 'setzt'; 'dec zeichnen' read as 'bezeichnen' (OCR split).","'Werde' rendered 'Let it be' (fiat), the creative word.","'Bestimmungen' = determinations; 'Vorstellen' = represent.","'Allein sein Dasein muß unmittelbar zu der Art gehören…' is rendered literally; 'Art' as 'manner' in which the concept is posited (i.e. the positing, not a predicate).","'schlechthin' rendered 'simply' alongside 'absolute'."]],"beweisgrund-0032":["I am afraid that, by too lengthy an elucidation of so simple an idea, I may become unintelligible. I might also fear offending the sensibilities of those who chiefly complain of dryness. But without holding this reproach to be a trifling one, I must on this occasion beg leave to risk it. For though I find as little taste as anyone else in the over-refined wisdom of those who in their logical smelting-kitchen so long drive over, distil and refine secure and serviceable concepts that they evaporate in vapours and volatile salts, yet the object of the inquiry which lies before me is of such a kind that one must either give up altogether any hope of ever attaining demonstrative certainty regarding it, or else submit to having one's concepts resolved down to these very atoms.",["The long final sentence is kept as one, since its structure (Denn ob ... so ... doch) can be followed in English; 'abziehen' rendered 'distil' (chemical sense, with 'übertreiben' = 'drive over').","'Schmelz— küche' and 'demon— strativische' are OCR line-break hyphenations, silently mended.","'Begriffe' = concepts; 'Gegenstand der Betrachtung' = 'object of the inquiry' (a choice for 'Betrachtung')."]],"beweisgrund-0033":["2",["Section number, as in the source."]],"beweisgrund-0034":["Can I properly say that there is more in existence than in mere possibility?",["This heading is continued in the next passage ('Möglichkeit sei?'); the two are joined here, but the second is given separately below.","'Dasein' = existence."]],"beweisgrund-0035":["[Heading continued from the preceding passage; see above.]",["Text of this fragment: 'Möglichkeit sei?' ('...possibility?'). Its translation is included in the rendering of beweisgrund-0034, since the sentence is split across the two passages; the bracketed line is mine, not Kant's."]],"beweisgrund-0036":["In answering this question I first merely observe that one must distinguish *what* is posited and *how* it is posited. As to the former, nothing more is posited in an actual thing than in a merely possible one; for all the determinations and predicates of the actual thing can be met with in its mere possibility also. But as to the latter, more is certainly posited through actuality. For if I ask how all this is posited in the case of mere possibility, I find that it is posited only relatively to the thing itself. That is, if a triangle is, then there are three sides, an enclosed space, three angles, and so forth; or, better, the relations of these determinations to such a something as a triangle is, are alone posited. But if it exists, all this is posited absolutely, that is, the thing itself together with these relations, and so more is posited. In order, therefore, to gather together, in so subtle a representation, everything that can prevent confusion, I say: in an existent nothing more is posited than in a merely possible (for then we are speaking of its predicates); but through something existent more is posited than through a merely possible, since the former involves also the absolute position of the thing itself. Indeed, in mere possibility it is not the thing itself, but merely relations of something to something, that are thought [*gelebt* in the text, for *gedacht*] according to the principle of contradiction; and it remains established that existence is properly not a predicate of any thing whatever. My intention here is by no means to engage in refutations; and in my opinion, when an author has read the thoughts of others with an unprejudiced mind, and has made them his own by reflection connected therewith, he can quite safely leave to the reader the judgment upon his new and divergent doctrines. Nevertheless I wish to touch upon the point in a few words.",["'Wirkliches/Wirklichkeit' = actual/actuality; 'gesetzt' = posited; 'Position' kept as 'position'.","'eriftirt' and 'Pradicat' are OCR slips for 'existirt', 'Prädicat', silently mended. 'gelebt' looks like an OCR (or print) error for 'gedacht' (the sense requires 'thought'); I translate 'thought' and flag the reading in brackets.","'darauf führen' is odd; I read 'darauf [zu] führen' loosely as 'touch upon the point'. Doubtful.","The final sentences are restated in somewhat shorter units; the first long sentence of the section 'Denn frage ich ... mithin mehr gesetzt' is divided at the semicolons."]],"beweisgrund-0037":["The Wolffian definition of existence, that it is a complement of possibility, is plainly very indeterminate. If one does not already know what can be thought in a thing over and above possibility, one will not learn it from this definition. Baumgarten adduces thoroughgoing internal determination, in so far as it supplements what is left undetermined by the predicates lying in the essence or flowing from it, as that which is more in existence than in mere possibility. But we have already seen that in the connection of a thing with all conceivable predicates there never lies a difference of it from what is merely possible. Moreover, the proposition that a possible thing, considered as such, is undetermined with respect to many predicates may, if taken literally, give rise to a great error. For the rule of the exclusion of a middle between two contradictory opposites forbids this; and a man, for example, who had no definite stature, time, age, place and the like would therefore be impossible. The proposition must rather be taken in this sense: through the predicates thought together in a thing, many others are not determined at all, just as through what is comprehended in the concept of a man as such, nothing is settled with regard to the particular marks of age, place and so forth. But this kind of indeterminateness is to be found just as much in an existing as in a merely possible thing, and for that reason it cannot be used to distinguish the two. The celebrated Crusius reckons the Somewhere and the Sometime among the infallible determinations of existence. But without entering upon an examination of the proposition itself, that everything which is must be somewhere or sometime, these predicates still belong also to merely possible things. For in certain definite places some man might exist at a certain time, all of whose determinations the Omniscient knows, just as they would belong to him if he existed, and who nevertheless is not actually there; and the eternal search of Ahasuerus through all the countries he is to wander and all the times he is to live through is without doubt a possible man. One will surely not demand that the Somewhere and Sometime shall be a sufficient mark of existence only when the thing is actually there or then; for that would be to demand that there be already granted what one undertakes to make recognizable of itself by means of a serviceable mark.",["'3. E.' is OCR for 'z. E.' (zum Exempel), 'for example'; 'Miglichkeit', 'forhen', 'Suche', 'keinew' etc. silently mended ('forhen' read as 'solchen', 'as such').","'Suche Ahasverns' is doubtful in the OCR; I take it as 'Suche' = search, as the sense of wandering requires.","'durchgängige innere Bestimmung' = thoroughgoing internal determination; 'Wesen' = essence.","Some sentences (e.g. the last) are kept long but their clauses are marked with semicolons for clarity."]],"beweisgrund-0038":["Second Consideration.",["'Betrachtung' rendered 'Consideration'."]],"beweisgrund-0039":["On internal possibility, in so far as it presupposes an existence.",["Heading continues in next passage ('voraussetzt'); joined here, so 0040 repeats only its fragment."]],"beweisgrund-0040":["[...presupposes.]",["The word 'voraussetzt' completes the heading in 0039. The stray 'as' is an OCR artefact and is omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0041":["Necessary distinction in the concept of possibility.",[]],"beweisgrund-0042":["Whatever is self-contradictory is internally impossible. This is a true proposition, even if we leave it undecided whether it is a true definition. In such a contradiction, however, it is clear that something must stand in logical conflict with something; that is, it must deny what is affirmed at the same time in that very thing. Even according to Herr Crusius, who places this conflict not merely in an internal contradiction but maintains that it is in general perceived by the understanding in accordance with a law natural to it, there is always in the impossible a connection of something that is posited with something by which it is at the same time cancelled. This repugnance I call the formal [element] of unthinkability or impossibility. The material [element] which is given in this, and which stands in such conflict, is in itself something and can be thought. A triangle that was quadrilateral is absolutely impossible. Yet a triangle, and likewise something quadrilateral, is in itself something. This impossibility rests solely on logical relations of one thinkable to another, in that the one cannot be a mark of the other. In the same way, in every possibility we must distinguish the something that is thought from the agreement of what is thought in it, at the same time, with the principle of contradiction. A triangle that has a right angle is in itself possible. The triangle and the right angle are alike the *data*, or the material [element], in this possible; the agreement of the one with the other in accordance with the principle of contradiction is the formal [element] of possibility. This latter I shall also entitle the logical [element] in possibility, because the comparison of predicates with their subjects according to the rule of truth is nothing other than a logical relation. The something, or that which stands in this agreement, will at times be entitled the real [element] of possibility. I would add that throughout, no other possibility or impossibility will be in question than the internal, that is, the possibility or impossibility so entitled absolutely and without qualification.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Selbst nach dem Herren Crusius' with its relative clause kept; the German is loose but needed no division.","Supplied '[element]' after 'formal' and 'material' (das Formale, das Materiale), since the English cannot use the substantivised adjectives alone.","'sind das Formale' (plural verb in the German) is rendered 'is the formal [element]'; the plural is a grammatical slip of agreement.","OCR 'Pata' corrected silently to 'Data'; the line-break hyphens in 'Ver—stand', 'schlechter—dings' removed.","*Satz des Widerspruchs* = 'principle of contradiction'; 'Repugnanz' rendered 'repugnance'; heißen = 'is entitled'."]],"beweisgrund-0043":["2.",[]],"beweisgrund-0044":["The internal possibility of all things presupposes some",["Heading, continued in the next passage ('existence')."]],"beweisgrund-0045":["existence.",["Completes the heading of section 2; Dasein = 'existence'."]],"beweisgrund-0046":["From what has just been adduced it can be clearly seen that possibility vanishes not only when an internal contradiction, as the logical [element] of impossibility, is found, but also when there is no material [element], no *datum*, for thought. For in that case nothing thinkable is given; but everything possible is something that can be thought, and to which the logical relation in accordance with the principle of contradiction belongs.",["Supplied '[element]' after 'material'; 'Denkliches' = 'thinkable'."]],"beweisgrund-0047":["If, now, all existence is removed, nothing is posited absolutely, nothing whatever is given, no material [element] for anything thinkable, and all possibility completely vanishes. There is, to be sure, no internal contradiction in the denial of all existence. For since this would require that something be posited and at the same time cancelled, whereas here nothing at all is posited, it certainly cannot be said that this removal involves an internal contradiction. But that there should be some possibility and yet nothing actual whatever, this is self-contradictory. For if nothing exists, nothing is given that could be thinkable, and one contradicts oneself if one nevertheless wishes that something be possible. In our analysis of the concept of existence we understood that being, or absolute positing, when these words are not used to express logical relations of predicates to subjects, signifies exactly the same as existence. To say, therefore, 'nothing exists' means as much as 'there is nothing at all'; and it is manifestly self-contradictory, notwithstanding this, to add that something is possible.",["Divided the long sentence beginning 'Allein daß irgend eine Möglichkeit sei' by introducing a second 'For' clause; the sense is unchanged.","OCR 'sich)' corrected to 'sich'; 'led' corrected to 'sei'.","'Sein oder schlechthin Gesetztsein' rendered 'being, or absolute positing'."]],"beweisgrund-0048":["3.",["The OCR ('9 Chr >') is garbled; the section number 3 is inferred from the sequence 2, 3, 4. Doubtful reading."]],"beweisgrund-0049":["It is absolutely impossible that nothing at all should exist.",["OCR 'eriftire' corrected to 'existire'."]],"beweisgrund-0050":["That by which all possibility in general is removed is absolutely impossible. For these are equivalent expressions. Now, in the first place, what contradicts itself removes the formal [element] of all possibility, namely agreement with the principle of contradiction; hence what is self-contradictory is absolutely impossible. But this is not the case in the matter we have to consider, the complete deprivation of all existence. For in this, as has been proved, there lies no internal contradiction. But that by which the material [element] and the *data* for everything possible are removed also negates all possibility. Now this happens through the removal of all existence; so if all existence is denied, all possibility is also removed. Hence it is absolutely impossible that nothing at all should exist.",["OCR slips ('sich)', 'darin o liegt', 'Wider— spruchs', 'Auf— hebung') silently corrected; stray marks omitted.","'Beraubung' rendered 'deprivation'."]],"beweisgrund-0051":["4.",[]],"beweisgrund-0052":["All possibility is given in something actual,",["Heading, continued in the next two passages."]],"beweisgrund-0053":["either in it as a determination, or through",["Heading continued; Bestimmung = 'determination'."]],"beweisgrund-0054":["it as a consequence.",["Completes the heading of section 4."]],"beweisgrund-0055":["It must be shown of all possibility taken together, and of each possibility in particular, that it presupposes something actual, whether this be one thing or several. This relation of all possibility to some existence can be twofold. Either the possible is thinkable only in so far as it is itself actual, and then the possibility is given in the actual as a determination; or it is possible because something else is actual, that is, its internal possibility is given as a consequence through another existence. Illustrative examples cannot yet conveniently be brought in here. The nature of that subject, which is the only one that can serve as an example in this inquiry, has first to be considered. Meanwhile I would only remark that I shall entitle that actual thing, through which as ground the internal possibility of other things is given, the first real ground [*Realgrund*] of this absolute possibility, just as the principle of contradiction is its first logical ground, in that the formal [element] of possibility lies in agreement with it, while the former supplies the *data* and the material [element] in the thinkable.",["Restated the opening sentence slightly ('Es ist ... darzuthun') in passive form for English idiom.","'Wirkliches' = 'something actual'; stray OCR digits omitted.","*Realgrund* kept in German beside 'real ground' since the term is technical; 'the former' renders 'jenes' (the first real ground), the antecedent judged by the argument."]],"beweisgrund-0056":["I well understand that propositions of the kind set forth in this inquiry still stand in need of much elucidation, | in order to obtain the light that is required for self-evidence. Meanwhile the very abstract nature of the subject itself places obstacles in the way of every effort at greater clarification, just as the microscopic devices of vision, though they enlarge the image of the object to the point of | distinguishing its very small parts, in the same measure diminish the brightness and vividness of the impression. Nevertheless I shall try, so far as I am able, to bring the thought of the existence that always underlies even internal | possibility somewhat closer to the common concepts of a sound understanding.",["The Academy line-break signs | are retained where they stand.","OCR 'innren' and 'gemeinem' (read 'gemeinen') corrected; stray digits omitted.","'Augenscheinlichkeit' rendered 'self-evidence'; 'Naheit' = 'closeness'."]],"beweisgrund-0057":["You recognise that a fiery body, a cunning man, or the like, is possible; and if I ask for nothing more than the internal possibility, you will find it quite unnecessary that a body or fire and so forth must exist as the *data* for this, for they are thinkable, and that is enough. The agreement, however, of the predicate 'fiery' with the subject 'body', according to the ground of contradiction, lies in these concepts themselves, whether they be actual or merely possible things. I also concede that neither body nor fire need be actual things, and that a fiery body is nevertheless internally possible. But I go on to ask: is a body itself, then, possible in itself?",["OCR 'ste' read as 'sie'; 'Moglichkeit' and stray marks corrected silently.","'Grund des Widerspruchs' rendered literally 'ground of contradiction' (cf. 'Satz des Widerspruchs' earlier).","The passage ends on a question; the argument continues in the next passage, which is not translated."]],"beweisgrund-0058":["Since you may not appeal to experience here, you will enumerate for me the data of its possibility, namely extension, impenetrability, force, and who knows what more, and add that there is no inner conflict in them. I concede all this; but you must give me an account of why you are entitled to assume the concept of extension, just like that, as a datum. For suppose it signified nothing,",["The OCR fragment \"ceu\" at the start is a line-number or margin artefact and is omitted. \"Its possibility\" refers to the possibility of the (fiery) body in the preceding argument, which is not part of this passage.","The sentence breaks off after \"signified nothing\" and is continued in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0059":["then this possibility of body, which you put forward in its place, is a mere illusion. It would also be quite wrong to appeal to experience for this datum, for the question now is precisely whether an inner possibility of the fiery body obtains even if nothing whatever exists. Suppose that you can no longer resolve the concept of extension into simpler data so as to show that nothing conflicting is contained in it (as in the end you must of necessity come upon something whose possibility cannot be analysed). Then the question here is whether space or extension are empty words, or whether they designate something. The absence of contradiction does not settle the matter here; an empty word never designates anything contradictory. Unless space exists, or is at least given, as a consequence, through something existing, the word *space* means nothing at all. So long as you still confirm possibilities by the principle of contradiction, you rest upon what is given to you as thinkable in the thing, and consider only the connection according to this logical rule. But in the end, when you reflect how this [datum] is itself given to you, you can appeal to nothing else than an existence.",["Opening \"os\" and the stray \")\" after \"sich\", and the \"dec |\" before \"rufen\" (read as \"zu berufen\"), are OCR/line-break artefacts, silently corrected; the line marks are not reproduced.","The passage continues the previous sentence: \"so ist eure ... Möglichkeit ... ein Blendwerk\" completes \"gesetzt, er bedeute nichts\". \"Dati\" is the genitive of *datum*.","Long sentences are divided into shorter ones. \"[datum]\" is supplied for the pronoun \"dieses\" (what is given as thinkable)."]],"beweisgrund-0060":["But let us await the progress of these considerations. The application itself will make comprehensible a concept which can hardly be made distinct by itself alone without going beyond itself, since it deals with the very first thing that underlies the thinkable.",["\"Betrachtung\" is rendered \"consideration\" (Kant's section title). \"Denklich\" is rendered \"thinkable\".","The concept meant is that of the datum of all possibility, i.e. existence as the ground of the thinkable; I read the relative \"den\" as referring to \"einen Begriff\"."]],"beweisgrund-0061":["Third Consideration.\nOn absolutely necessary existence.",["The stray OCR letters \"iw\" are omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0062":["Concept of absolutely necessary existence in general.",[]],"beweisgrund-0063":["That is absolutely necessary whose opposite is in itself impossible. This is an indubitably correct nominal definition. But if I ask what it is that matters if the non-being of a thing is to be absolutely impossible, then what I am seeking is the real definition, which alone can be of any use to us for our purpose. All our concepts of inner necessity in the properties of possible things, of whatever kind these may be, come to this: that the opposite contradicts itself. But where an absolutely necessary existence is in question, it would be a poor undertaking to try to understand anything in it through this same mark. Existence is not a predicate at all, and the removal of existence is not the negation of a predicate, through which something in a thing might be removed and an inner contradiction arise. The removal of an existing thing is a complete negation of everything that was posited, simply or absolutely, through its existence. The logical relations between the thing as a possible thing and its predicates nevertheless remain. But these are quite different from the positing of the thing together with its predicates, simply and absolutely, in which existence consists. Hence it is not the very same thing that is posited in the thing, but something different, that is removed through non-being, and so there is never any contradiction here. In the last consideration of this work, all this will be made more convincing, by a clear exposition of this unfitness, in the case where men have actually supposed that they could comprehend absolutely necessary existence through the principle of contradiction.",["*Nominal-Erklärung* / *Realerklärung* = nominal / real definition. \"Dasein\" and \"Existenz\" are both rendered \"existence\".","\"Position des Dinges ... schlechthin\" = \"positing ... simply and absolutely\".","The passage runs on into the next passage (\"überzeugender gemacht werden\"); the last sentence is left as it stands, breaking off at \"über-\" in the German, and is completed in 0064. The English of the last sentence anticipates that completion (\"more convincing\"), since \"über-\" and \"zeugender\" are one word. OCR \"Erflärung\", \"bin\" (hin) and \"degjenigen\" silently corrected."]],"beweisgrund-0064":["[more convincing]. Meanwhile the necessity in the predicates of merely possible concepts may be called logical necessity. But the necessity whose main ground I am seeking, namely that of existence, is absolute real necessity. I find first that what I am to regard as absolutely nothing and impossible must annihilate everything thinkable.",["The first word completes \"über-zeugender\" from the preceding passage; I have bracketed it as supplied, not translated afresh.","The sentence \"Ich finde zuerst ...\" ends with \"alles Denkliche\", and the infinitive \"vertilgen\" is in the next passage; I have joined them in the English because the verb is needed for sense, though the German splits here."]],"beweisgrund-0065":["[annihilate]. For if anything were still left to be thought in it, it would not be wholly unthinkable and absolutely impossible.",["The fragment \"In\" at the start is an OCR line-number artefact. \"vertilgen\" is the verb completing the previous passage's sentence, and I show it in brackets as it belongs there."]],"beweisgrund-0066":["If I now reflect for a moment why that which contradicts itself is absolutely nothing and impossible, I observe that, because the principle of contradiction, the ultimate logical ground of everything thinkable, is thereby removed, all possibility vanishes and nothing more is left to be thought. From this I at once infer that if I remove all existence whatever, and the ultimate real ground of everything thinkable thereby falls away, all possibility likewise vanishes and nothing remains to be thought. Accordingly something can be absolutely necessary either if through its opposite the formal element of everything thinkable is removed, that is, if it contradicts itself; or also if its non-being removes the material element for everything thinkable and all the data for it. The first, as has been said, never occurs in the case of existence; and since no third is possible, the concept of absolutely necessary existence is either altogether a deceptive and false concept, or it must consist in this, that the non-being of a thing is at the same time the negation of the data for everything thinkable. That this concept, however, is not invented but is something true, is evident in the following way.",["The stray \"2\" after \"nothwendig\" is a line number and is omitted.","*Formale* / *Materiale* of the thinkable are rendered \"formal element\" / \"material element\"; \"Realgrund\" = \"real ground\"."]],"beweisgrund-0067":["2.",["The OCR reads \"9\"; since this is the second proposition (the next passage is \"3.\"), I read it as \"2.\" and have corrected it. This is an inference, not a certain reading."]],"beweisgrund-0068":["An absolutely necessary being exists.",["OCR \"schechterdings\" silently corrected."]],"beweisgrund-0069":["All possibility presupposes something actual, in which and through which everything thinkable is given. There is therefore a certain actuality whose removal would itself remove all inner possibility whatever. But that whose removal or negation annihilates all possibility is absolutely necessary. Hence something exists with absolute necessity. Thus far it is evident that the existence of one or more things itself underlies all possibility, and that this existence is necessary in itself. From this the concept of contingency can also easily be derived. According to the nominal definition, that is contingent whose opposite is possible. To find the real definition of it, however, we must distinguish as follows. In the logical sense, that is contingent, as a predicate in a subject, whose opposite does not contradict the subject. For example, it is contingent to a triangle in general that it should be right-angled. This contingency occurs solely in the relation of predicates to their subjects, and, since existence is not a predicate, admits of no application whatever to existence. In the real sense, on the other hand, that is contingent whose non-being is thinkable, that is, whose removal does not remove everything thinkable. If, therefore, the inner possibility of things does not presuppose a certain existence, this existence is contingent, because its opposite does not remove possibility. Or: that existence through which the material element for everything thinkable is not given, without which therefore something is still thinkable, that is, possible, has an opposite which is possible in the real sense, and is, in the same sense, also contingent.",["\"vorauslebt\" is an OCR slip for \"voraussetzt\", silently corrected; \"Moglichkeit\" likewise.","*Worterklärung* / *Sacherklärung* = nominal / real definition (here \"Sacherklärung\" is rendered \"real definition\"); \"im logischen / Realverstande\" = \"in the logical / real sense\"."]],"beweisgrund-0070":["3.\nThe necessary being is one.",[]],"beweisgrund-0071":["Since the necessary being contains the ultimate real ground of all other possibility, every other thing will be possible only in so far as it is given through it as ground. Consequently every other thing can obtain only as a consequence of it, and the possibility and existence of all other things are therefore dependent upon it. But something which is itself dependent does not contain the ultimate [ ] of all possibility, and is therefore not absolutely necessary. Hence several things cannot be absolutely necessary.",["In the German text a word is missing after \"den letzten\" (the OCR gives only a stray point). Context suggests \"Grund\", but I have not filled the gap and mark it as [ ]."]],"beweisgrund-0072":["Suppose A to be a necessary being, and B another. Then, by the definition, B is possible only in so far as it is given through another ground, A, as the consequent of that ground. But since, by the hypothesis, B itself is necessary, its possibility is given in B as a predicate, and not as a consequence of something else; and yet, according to what was said above, it is given only as a consequence. This is self-contradictory.",["OCR 'Gehet, A set' read as 'Setzet, A sei'.","The long second sentence is kept as one, with the final clause ('welches sich widerspricht') made a separate sentence."]],"beweisgrund-0073":["4.\nThe necessary being is simple.",[]],"beweisgrund-0074":["That no composite of many substances can be an absolutely necessary being is evident in the following way. Suppose that only one of its parts is absolutely necessary; then the others are possible only collectively, as consequences through it, and do not belong to it as co-ordinate parts [Nebentheile]. Suppose, on the other hand, that several or all of them are necessary; this contradicts the preceding number. Nothing therefore remains but that each of them must exist contingently, while all together exist absolutely necessarily. This, however, is impossible. An aggregate of substances cannot have more necessity in existence than belongs to its parts; and since none belongs to these, their existence being contingent, the existence of the whole would also be contingent.\n\nSuppose that someone thought he could appeal to the definition of the necessary being, and said that in each of the parts the ultimate data of some inner possibility were given, and in all together everything possible. He would then have represented something quite absurd, only in a concealed form. For if the inner possibility is so conceived that some of it can be removed, while whatever else is thinkable that is given through the other parts would yet remain, one would have to represent it as possible in itself that inner possibility should be negated or removed. But it is wholly unthinkable and contradictory that something should be nothing; and this amounts to saying that to remove an inner possibility is to annul everything thinkable. Hence it is evident that the data for everything thinkable must be given in that thing whose removal is also the contrary of all possibility. What contains the ultimate ground of one inner possibility must therefore contain it of all possibility whatsoever, and consequently this ground cannot be distributed among different substances.",["Two very long sentences restated as several; the paragraph break is mine.","OCR 'verborgene: Art' read as 'verborgene Art'; 'set an sich)' read as 'sei an sich'.","'Denkliche' rendered 'thinkable'; 'Data' kept as 'data'."]],"beweisgrund-0075":["5.",[]],"beweisgrund-0076":["The necessary being is unchangeable and eternal.",[]],"beweisgrund-0077":["Since its own possibility, and every other, presupposes this existence, no other mode of its existence is possible; that is, the necessary being cannot exist in many ways. For everything that is, is thoroughly determined; and since this being is possible solely because it exists, there is no possibility of it except in so far as it actually is. It is therefore possible in no other way than as it actually is. Consequently it cannot be otherwise determined or changed. Its non-being is absolutely impossible, and so therefore are its origin and its passing away; hence it is eternal.",["'durchgängig bestimmt' rendered 'thoroughly determined'."]],"beweisgrund-0078":["The necessary being contains the highest reality.",["The number '6.' is not in the passage as given; the heading is translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0079":["Since the data for all possibility must be found in it, either as its determinations or as consequences given through it as the first real ground [Realgrund], it is evident that all reality is comprehended through it in one way or another. But the very determinations through which this being is the highest ground of all possible reality posit in itself the greatest degree of real properties that can ever inhere in a thing. Such a being is the most real of all possible beings, inasmuch as all others are possible only through it. This, however, is not to be understood as meaning that all possible reality belongs among its determinations. That is a confusion of concepts which has hitherto been extremely prevalent. All realities are ascribed to God, or to the necessary being, indiscriminately as predicates, without observing that they can never exist side by side in a single subject as determinations. The impenetrability of bodies, extension, and the like cannot be properties of that which has understanding and will. It is also vain to seek an escape by denying that the properties mentioned are true reality. Beyond all doubt the impact of a body, or the force of cohesion, is something truly positive. Similarly, pain in the sensations of a spirit is never a mere | privation. An erroneous thought has given such a view a semblance of justification. It is said: reality and reality never contradict each other, since both are true affirmations; consequently they do not conflict with each other in one subject either. Now although I grant that there is here no logical conflict, the real repugnance [Realrepugnanz] is not thereby removed. This is always present when something, as a ground, nullifies the consequence of something else through a real opposition. The moving force of a body in one direction, and the tendency of equal degree in the opposite direction, do not stand in contradiction. They are also really possible together in one body. But the one nullifies the real consequence of the other; and whereas the consequence of each taken singly would be an actual motion, the consequence of both together in one subject is now zero. That is, the consequence of these oppo | sed moving forces is rest. Rest, however, is without doubt possible. From this one also sees that real repugnance is something quite different from the logical, or contradiction; for what follows from the latter is absolutely impossible. Now in the most real being there can be no real repugnance, or positive conflict among its own determinations, since the consequence would be a privation or deficiency, which contradicts its highest reality. And since such a conflict would have to arise if all realities lay in it as determinations, they cannot all be in it as predicates. Hence, since they are nevertheless all given through it, they will belong either to its determinations or to its consequences.",["Restated as shorter sentences in places; the very long closing sentence is kept close to the German.","Heavily damaged OCR repaired silently: 'Prddicate', '[tatt' (statt), '1c' (Sie), 'is möglich' (ist möglich), 'si daß', and stray digits and signs ('>', '2', '24') omitted. The final '24' may be a footnote or line number, so check it against the original.","'Subjecte 0': the '0' is kept as 'zero' on the assumption that it is a numeral.","The sign | is kept where it stands; the second one falls inside 'entgegen gez | setzten', so I placed it within 'oppo | sed'.","'vernichtigt' rendered 'nullifies'; 'Realrepugnanz' kept with the German; 'Realgrund' = 'real ground'."]],"beweisgrund-0080":["It might also seem, at first sight, to follow that, since the necessary being contains the ultimate real ground of all other possibility, the ground of the defects and negations in the essences of things must also lie in it. If this were admitted, it might further occasion the inference that it must itself have negations among its predicates, and never nothing but reality. But let us only turn our eyes to the concept of it as once established. In its existence its own possibility is originally given. Now, the other possibilities, of which it contains the real ground, are other than it. It follows, by the principle of contradiction, that they cannot be the possibility of the most real being itself, and hence must be such possibilities as contain negations and defects, |",["The German sentence is irregular ('Dadurch, daß es ... sind ... folgt ... daß es nicht ... sein müssen'); I smoothed it, taking 'es' as the possibilities concerned.","Passage breaks off at the sign |, which is carried over; the sentence continues in the next passage.","OCR 'Prädi⸗ si caten', 'realften', 'miiffen' and stray 's' corrected silently."]],"beweisgrund-0081":["Accordingly, the possibility of all other things, in respect of what is real in them, rests upon the necessary being as a real ground; their defects rest upon it, because they are other things and not the original being itself, as a logical ground. The possibility of body, in so far as it has extension, forces, and the like, is grounded in the supreme of all beings; in so far as it lacks the power to think, this negation lies in body itself, in accordance with the principle of contradiction.",["'Demnach' continues the thought of the preceding passage. The passage begins after a | in the source, which is not in the text given.","'Urwesen' rendered 'original being'; 'Realgrund' / 'logischer Grund' = 'real ground' / 'logical ground'."]],"beweisgrund-0082":["In fact, negations are not in themselves something, nor are they thinkable; and this can easily be made comprehensible in the following way. Posit nothing but negations, and then nothing whatever is given, and there is no something that could be thought. Negations are therefore thinkable only through opposed positions; or rather, positions are possible which are not the greatest. And in this fact, in accordance with the principle of identity, the negations themselves are already contained. It is also readily apparent that all negations attaching to the possibilities of other things presuppose no real ground (since they are nothing positive), and hence presuppose merely a logical ground.",["\"denk— lich\" (line-break hyphenation) silently restored as \"denklich\", rendered 'thinkable'.","Second sentence: \"Und hierin liegen schon ... die Verneinungen selber\" is rendered freely as 'in this fact ... the negations themselves are already contained'; the German is compressed and the antecedent of \"hierin\" is supplied by judgment.","\"Realgrund\" rendered 'real ground', in contrast with \"logischer Grund\" ('logical ground')."]],"beweisgrund-0083":["Fourth Consideration.",["\"Betrachtung\" rendered 'Consideration'."]],"beweisgrund-0084":["Ground of Proof for a Demonstration of the Existence of God.",["\"Beweisgrund\" rendered 'ground of proof'."]],"beweisgrund-0085":["1.\nThe necessary being is a spirit.",["\"Geist\" rendered 'spirit'; \"Wesen\" rendered 'being'."]],"beweisgrund-0086":["It has been proved above that the necessary being is a simple substance, and likewise that not only is all other reality given through it as a ground, but that the greatest possible reality which can be contained in a being as determination also belongs to it. Now various proofs can be offered that the attributes of understanding and will belong to this as well. For, in the first place, both are true reality, and both can subsist together with the greatest possible reality in one thing. The latter point we find ourselves compelled to concede by an immediate judgment of the understanding, though it cannot well be brought to that distinctness which logically perfect proofs require. a",["The final \"a\" is the footnote marker; the footnote itself is not in the passage supplied.","\"Bestimmung\" rendered 'determination'; \"Verstand\" 'understanding'.","The last sentence is divided in two; \"welches letztere\" is resolved as 'the latter point', i.e. the compatibility of both with the greatest reality."]],"beweisgrund-0087":["Secondly, the attributes of a spirit, understanding and will, are of such a kind that we can think no reality which, in the absence of them, could make good to a being a compensation equal to their lack. These attributes, then, are those which are capable of the highest degrees of reality, and yet belong among the things that are possible. Consequently, through the necessary being as a ground, understanding and will, and all reality of spiritual nature, would have to be possible in others, though it would not itself be encountered in him as a determination. The consequence would thus be greater than the ground itself. For it is certain that, if the highest being does not itself have understanding and will, then any other being posited through it with these attributes, even though it were dependent and had all sorts of other deficiencies of power and so forth, would nevertheless, in respect of these attributes of the highest kind, have to surpass the former in reality. Since, now, the consequence cannot exceed the ground, understanding and will must belong as attributes to the necessary simple substance; that is to say, it is a spirit.",["\"die gleichwohl in ihm selbst nicht ... angetroffen würde\": the relative clause is taken to refer to the reality of spiritual nature; \"ihm\" is the necessary being.","\"jenem\" in the last long sentence is resolved as the highest being; the sentence has been broken at \"These attributes, then\".","\"Ersetzung\" rendered 'compensation'; \"Abgang\" 'lack'."]],"beweisgrund-0088":["Thirdly, order, beauty and perfection in everything that is possible presuppose a being in whose attributes these relations are either grounded, or through which being, at least, things are possible in conformity with these relations as from a principal ground. Now the necessary being is the sufficient real ground of everything else that is possible outside it; consequently there will also be found in it that attribute through which, in conformity with these relations, everything outside it can become actual. But it appears that the ground of external possibility, of order, beauty and perfection, is not sufficient unless a will conformable to understanding is presupposed. These attributes must therefore be ascribed to the supreme being.",["\"Hauptgrund\" rendered 'principal ground'.","\"ein dem Verstande gemäßer Wille\" rendered 'a will conformable to understanding'."]],"beweisgrund-0089":["Everyone recognises that, notwithstanding all the grounds for the production of plants and trees, regular flower-beds, avenues and the like are possible only through an understanding that designs them and a will that carries them out. All power, or productive force, and likewise all other data for possibility, without an understanding, are insufficient to make the possibility of such order complete.",[]],"beweisgrund-0090":["From one of the grounds here adduced, or from all of them together, the proof that the necessary being must have will and understanding, and hence must be a spirit, can be derived. I am content merely to make the ground of proof complete. My intention is not to set out a formal demonstration.",["\"Ab— sieht\" restored as \"Absicht\", 'intention'."]],"beweisgrund-0091":["2.\nThere is a God.",[]],"beweisgrund-0092":["Something exists with absolute necessity. This is unique in its essence, simple in its substance, a spirit in its nature, eternal in its duration, unchangeable in its constitution, all-sufficient in respect of everything possible and actual. It is a God. I give here no determinate definition of the concept of God. I should have to do so if I wished to treat my object systematically. What I set out here is to be the analysis by which one may qualify oneself for a formal doctrinal system. However the definition of the concept of the Godhead may be arranged, as one thinks fit, I am nevertheless certain that the being whose existence we have just proved is precisely that divine being whose distinguishing marks will be brought, in one way or another, into the briefest designation.",["\"Lehrverfassung\" rendered 'doctrinal system'; \"allgenugsam\" 'all-sufficient'.","\"set\" in the OCR is read as \"sei\".","The last sentence is kept as one, with the concessive clause moved to the front for clarity."]],"beweisgrund-0093":["3.\nRemark.",["\"Anmerkung\" rendered 'Remark'."]],"beweisgrund-0094":["Since nothing more is evident from the third consideration than that all reality must be given either in the necessary being as a determination or through it as a ground, it would remain undecided up to that point whether the attributes of understanding and will",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is continued in the next passage (beweisgrund-0095); translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0095":["are to be found in the supreme being as determinations belonging to it, or are to be regarded merely as consequences produced through it in other things. If the latter were the case, then, notwithstanding all the advantages that shine forth in this original being from the sufficiency, unity and independence of its existence as a great ground, its nature would yet fall far short of that which must be thought when one thinks a God. For, itself without knowledge and resolution, it would be a blindly necessary ground of other things, and even of other spirits, and would differ in nothing from the eternal fate of some of the ancients, save that it would be described more comprehensibly. This is the reason why in every doctrinal system particular attention must be paid to this point, and why we have not been able to lose sight of it.",["The passage begins mid-sentence, continuing beweisgrund-0094.","\"Erkenntniß\" rendered 'knowledge'; \"Entschließung\" 'resolution'.","\"ges sehen\" restored as \"gesehen\"."]],"beweisgrund-0096":["In the whole connection of all the grounds hitherto presented as belonging to my proof, I have nowhere mentioned the expression perfection. This is not because I hold that all reality is already as much as all perfection, or that the greatest harmony toward unity constitutes it. I have weighty reasons for departing widely from this judgment of many others. Having for a long time made careful investigations into the concept of perfection, in general and in particular, I have been taught that in a more exact acquaintance with it there lies hidden a great deal that can throw light upon the nature of a spirit, upon our own feeling, and upon the very first concepts of practical philosophy. |",["\"Zusammen— stimmung\" and \"prak— tischen\" restored; \"Weltweisheit\" rendered 'philosophy' (practical philosophy).","\"zu Einem\" rendered 'toward unity'; the sense is somewhat compressed in the German.","The sign | is the Academy apparatus (page/line break), carried over where it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0097":["I have become aware that the expression perfection, though in some cases, owing to the uncertainty of every language, it suffers deviations from its proper sense that diverge considerably, yet in the meaning to which everyone chiefly attends, even amid those aberrations, always presupposes a relation to a being that has knowledge and desire. Since it would have become far too lengthy to carry the ground of proof concerning God, and the reality belonging to him, through to this relation, although in virtue of what lies at the basis it would have been quite feasible, I have not found it consonant with the purpose of these pages to occasion excessive prolixity by drawing in this concept.",["\"Begierde\" rendered 'desire'; \"Erkenntniß\" 'knowledge'.","\"vermöge dessen, was zum Grunde liegt\" rendered 'in virtue of what lies at the basis', meaning the foundations already laid in the argument."]],"beweisgrund-0098":["4.\nConclusion.",["\"Beschluß\" rendered 'Conclusion'."]],"beweisgrund-0099":["After the proof thus conducted, anyone can very easily add such obvious corollaries as these. I who think am not an absolutely necessary being, for I am not the ground of all reality; I am changeable. No other being whose non-existence is possible, that is, whose removal does not at the same time remove all possibility, no changeable thing, or thing in which there are limits, and therefore also not the world, is of such a nature. The world is not an accident of the Godhead, since conflict, defects and changeability, all of them contraries of the determinations of a Godhead, are to be found in it. God is not the one sole substance that exists, all others being there merely in dependence upon him; and so on.",["The long second sentence is divided and the series of negations is recast as a separate sentence; the dash-and-comma structure of the German (kein ander Wesen … kein veränderliches Ding … mithin auch nicht die Welt) is smoothed.","*Bestimmungen* = determinations; *Gottheit* = Godhead; *Accidens* rendered 'accident'.","*die einige Substanz* = 'the one sole substance' (einig = sole, unique)."]],"beweisgrund-0100":["I note here only the following. The ground of proof for the existence of God which we offer is built solely upon the fact that something is possible. It is therefore a proof which can be conducted wholly *a priori*. Neither my own existence, nor that of other spirits, nor that of the corporeal world is presupposed. It is in fact derived from the inner mark of absolute necessity. In this way we know the existence of this being from that which actually constitutes its absolute necessity, and so quite genetically.",["*Beweisgrund* rendered 'ground of proof'; *genetisch* kept as 'genetically'.","*Erkennt* rendered 'know'."]],"beweisgrund-0101":["All the proofs that might otherwise be conducted from the effects of this being to its existence as a cause, even supposing that they proved as strictly as in fact they do not, can never make the nature of this necessity comprehensible. Only from the fact that something exists with absolute necessity is it possible that something should be a first cause of other things. But from the fact that something is a first, that is, independent, cause, it follows only that, if the effects are there, it must also exist; it does not follow that it exists with absolute necessity.",["The clause 'gesetzt daß sie auch so strenge beweisen möchten, als sie es nicht thun' is rendered as a concessive supposition, with the sense that the proofs in fact are not so strict.","*Blos daraus, daß …* rendered 'Only from the fact that …', to bring out the order of dependence."]],"beweisgrund-0102":["Further, it is evident from the proof here recommended that the essences of all other things, and the real element of all possibility, are grounded in this one being, in which are to be found the highest degrees of understanding and of a will that is the greatest possible ground; and since in such a being everything must stand in the utmost possible agreement, we can infer in advance the following. A will always presupposes the inner possibility of the thing itself. Hence the ground of possibility, that is, the essence of God, will be in the greatest harmony with his will. This is not because God, through his will, would be the ground of inner possibility. It is because the very same infinite nature which stands to all the essences of things in the relation of a ground also stands, at the same time, in the relation of the highest desire to the greatest consequences thereby given; and the latter relation can be fruitful only through the presupposition of the former. Accordingly the possibilities of things themselves, which are given through the divine nature, will accord with his great desire. But in this accordance consists the good and perfection. And because they accord with one another, unity, harmony and order will be found even in the possibilities of things.",["Very long period divided into several sentences; the 'da ein Wille …' clause is made a separate sentence.","*Begierde* rendered 'desire'; *Wesen* in 'Wesen der Dinge' rendered 'essences', whereas 'Wesen' for God is 'being'.","*Verstan— des*, *da— durch* are line-break hyphenations in the OCR, silently joined. 'mit einem' in the last sentence is taken as 'with one another' (the will and the possibilities); the ambiguity is resolved by judgment."]],"beweisgrund-0103":["But if, by a mature judgment of the essential properties of things which become known to us through experience, we perceive, even in the necessary determinations of their inner possibility, a unity in the manifold and a well-ordered agreement in what is separate, then by the path of knowledge *a posteriori* we shall be able to infer back to a single principle of all possibility, and shall in the end find ourselves at the same fundamental concept of absolutely necessary existence from which we originally set out by the path *a priori*. Our aim shall now be directed to seeing whether, in the inner possibility of things themselves, there is to be found a necessary relation to order and harmony, and unity in this immeasurable manifold, so that we may judge from this whether the essences of things themselves recognize a supreme common ground.",["*Grundbegriffedes* in the OCR is read as *Grundbegriffe des*, i.e. 'Grundbegriffe des schlechterdings nothwendigen Daseins' (dative singular 'Grundbegriffe' as an archaic form); rendered 'fundamental concept'.","*Erkenntnißweg* rendered 'path of knowledge'.","*erkennen* in the last clause is rendered 'recognize' in the sense of 'acknowledge'."]],"beweisgrund-0104":["Second Division\nOn the extensive utility which is peculiar to this kind of proof.",["Heading. *weitläuftigen* rendered 'extensive'."]],"beweisgrund-0105":["First Consideration,",["The '5' before the heading is a line number and is omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0106":["In which, from the unity perceived in the essences of",["Heading fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0107":["things, the existence of God is inferred *a posteriori*.",[]],"beweisgrund-0108":["1",[]],"beweisgrund-0109":["The unity in the manifold of the essences of things, shown in the properties of space.",["OCR is corrupt here ('0 gewiesen', closing quotation mark); read as 'gewiesen an den Eigenschaften des Raums'. Doubtful reading."]],"beweisgrund-0110":["The necessary determinations of space afford the geometer no ordinary pleasure, through the evidentness of conviction and the exactness of execution, and likewise through the wide range of their application; and human knowledge as a whole has nothing to show that is equal to it, much less that surpasses it. I now, however, consider the same object from an entirely different point of view. I look at it with a philosophical eye",["*Mepkünstler* is an OCR slip for *Meßkünstler*, rendered 'geometer'. *mensch— liebe* silently read as *menschliche*.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the next passage continues it."]],"beweisgrund-0111":["and become aware that, in the case of such necessary determinations, order and harmony, and fitness and unity in an enormous manifold, prevail. Suppose, for example, that I require a space to be bounded by the movement of a straight line about a fixed point. I readily comprehend that I thereby have a circle, which at all its points has equal distances from the fixed point in question. But I find no occasion whatever, in so simple a construction, to suspect a great deal of manifoldness which is by that very fact subject to great rules of order. Yet I discover that all straight lines which intersect one another at any point within the circle, where they meet the circumference, are always cut in geometrical proportion; and likewise that all those which cut the circle from a point outside it are always divided into such parts as stand in inverse relation to their wholes. When one considers how infinitely many different positions these lines can assume in cutting the circle as described, and perceives how they nevertheless stand constantly under the same law, from which they cannot depart, then, although the truth of this is easily comprehended, it is none the less something unexpected that so little contrivance in the description of this figure should result in so much order, and in the manifold so perfect a unity.",["*Zusammenw passung* in the OCR read as *Zusammenpassung*, rendered 'fitness'. Doubtful reading; 'ame nehmen' read as 'annehmen'.","'Einander aus einem beliebigen Punkt … durchkreuzen' rendered 'intersect one another at any point'; the sentence on the secants is kept close to the German, which expresses the theorem of the products of segments ('umgekehrt verhalten wie ihre Ganzen').","The long final sentence is kept as one, with its concessive structure smoothed."]],"beweisgrund-0112":["Suppose it were required that inclined planes, at various angles to the horizon, should be so arranged in length that bodies rolling freely down them would arrive in exactly equal time. Anyone who understands the laws of mechanics will see that this would call for various contrivances. Yet in the circle this arrangement is found of itself, with an infinite variety of positions, and in every case with the greatest exactness. For all chords which meet the vertical diameter, whether they start from its uppermost or its lowest point, and at whatever inclination one pleases, have this in common: that free fall along them takes place in equal times. I remember that an intelligent pupil, when this proposition and its proof were presented to him by me, was, after he had understood it all, moved by it no less than by a wonder of nature. And in fact one is surprised, and with reason filled with admiration, by so remarkable a union of the manifold under such fruitful rules in a thing so plain and simple in appearance as a circle. Nor is there any wonder of nature which, through the beauty or order that reigns in it, would give more cause for astonishment, unless it were so because the cause of it is not [so] clearly to be seen there, and admiration is a daughter of ignorance.",["The text ends 'nicht [o deutlich einzusehen ist': the OCR has a stray bracket and a dropped letter; read 'nicht [so] deutlich', with 'so' supplied in square brackets. Doubtful reading.","*Cirkelkreis* rendered simply 'circle'.","*Bewunderung* = admiration; *Erstaunen* = astonishment."]],"beweisgrund-0113":["The field on which I am gathering things worthy of note is so full of them that, without our having to go a step further, countless beauties present themselves on the very spot where we stand. There are solutions in geometry in which what seems possible only by elaborate contrivance discloses itself, as it were without any art, in the thing itself. Everyone finds these charming, and the more so, the less one has oneself to do in the matter and the more intricate the solution nevertheless appears to be. The annulus [*Cirkelring*] between two circles having a common centre has a figure quite different from that of a circular surface, and it strikes everyone at first as laborious and artificial to transform it into this figure. But as soon as I see that the line which touches the inner circle, if drawn far enough to cut the circumference of the larger circle on both sides, is the diameter of that circle whose area is exactly equal to the content of the annulus, I cannot but express some astonishment at the simple way in which what was sought reveals itself so easily in the nature of the thing itself, and at the fact that almost nothing in the matter is to be ascribed to my own effort.",["OCR: \"id)\" read as *ich*; \"sich)\" as *sich*; \"Muflójungen\" as *Auflösungen*; \"Beranstaltung\" as *Veranstaltung*; \"Yiebei\" as *hiebei*.","\"ohne einen Fuß weiter jeden zu dürfen\" is corrupt in the OCR; I read it as \"weiter setzen zu dürfen\" (without being allowed to set a foot further). Doubtful.","\"von einer Cirkelfläche jer verschiedene\": \"jer\" is unclear (perhaps *sehr* or *ganz*); I render simply \"quite different\".","I restated the final sentence's closing clause as a separate \"at the fact that\" construction."]],"beweisgrund-0114":["In order to observe, in the necessary properties of space, unity amid the greatest manifoldness, and connection in what seems to have a necessity wholly separate from the rest, we have merely directed our eyes to the figure of the circle, which has still infinitely many such properties, of which only a small part is known. From this we may infer what an immensity of such harmonious relations lies elsewhere in the properties of space. Many of these are set forth by the higher geometry in the affinities of the various families of curved lines; and all of them, besides exercising the understanding, affect feeling, through the intelligible insight into them, in a manner similar to, or more exalted than, the contingent beauties of nature.",["\"Verstand\" = understanding. \"denkliche Einsicht\" rendered \"intelligible insight\"; the sense is insight attained by thought.","The long first sentence is divided in two, and the final clause is recast, per the method."]],"beweisgrund-0115":["If, in such arrangements of nature, we are entitled to ask for a ground of so far-extended an agreement of the manifold, are we to be any less entitled when we perceive symmetry and unity in the infinitely various determinations [*Bestimmungen*] of space? Is this harmony any the less astonishing because it is necessary? I hold that it is all the more so. Moreover, a multiplicity of which each member had its own particular and independent necessity could never have order, consonance and unity in their mutual relations. Are we not therefore led, just as by the harmony in the contingent arrangements of nature, to the conjecture of a supreme ground even of the essences of things [*der Wesen der Dinge*], since unity of the ground also occasions unity in the whole range of its consequences?",["OCR: \"Nothet\" read as *Nothwendigkeit*.","The final sentence is long and tangled; I have divided it, making the \"Und weil...\" clause a separate sentence. The sense of \"dadurch\" (from the perceived harmony) is supplied in \"therefore\".","\"Wesen\" = essences; \"Wohlgereimtheit\" = consonance."]],"beweisgrund-0116":["23",["A bare number in the OCR; most likely a section or page number. Translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0117":["Unity in the manifold of the essences of things,",["Heading, continued in the next three passages."]],"beweisgrund-0118":["as shown in that which is",["Heading, continued; \"in den Bewegungsgesetzen\" is the end of the next line, rendered as \"in the laws of motion\" in the next passage. Here the sentence is broken across passages, and I follow the order given."]],"beweisgrund-0119":["necessary in the laws of motion.",["Completes the heading: \"Unity in the manifold of the essences of things, as shown in that which is necessary in the laws of motion.\" The word *Bewegungsgesetzen* appears in the previous fragment's line and is placed here for English word order."]],"beweisgrund-0120":["When we discover in nature an arrangement which seems to have been made for a particular purpose, in that it would not have presented itself merely in accordance with the general properties of matter, we regard this contrivance as contingent and as the consequence of a choice. If new agreement, order and utility now show themselves, and especially intermediate causes specially adapted to them, we judge these in like manner. This connection is wholly foreign to the nature of the things, and they stand in this harmony only because someone was pleased so to connect them. No general cause can be given why the claws of the cat, the lion and others are so constructed that they [*sporen*], that is, can be drawn back, except that some author so arranged them for the purpose of being secured against wearing down, since these animals must have skilful instruments for seizing and holding their prey.\n\nBut suppose that certain more general properties inherent in matter show, besides an advantage which they produce, and on account of which we may imagine them to have been so ordered, and without the least new provision, a particular fitness for still further agreement; suppose that a simple law, which anyone would find necessary for the sake of a certain good alone, shows nevertheless a widespread fruitfulness in much more besides; suppose that the remaining utilities and consonances flow from it without art, and rather by necessity; and suppose, finally, that this is found to be so throughout the whole of material nature: then there evidently lie, in the essences of things themselves, thoroughgoing relations to unity and connection, and a universal harmony extends over the realm of possibility itself. This occasions admiration at so much fitness and natural accord, which, while it makes laborious and forced art dispensable, can yet never be ascribed to chance. It rather indicates a unity lying in the possibilities themselves, and the common dependence of the essences of all things upon one single great ground. I shall seek to make this very great remarkableness clear by a few easy examples, carefully following the method of ascending slowly, from what is immediately certain through observation, to the general judgment.",["Divided the long second sentence into several, with the \"wenn...\" series rendered as \"suppose that...\"; the conclusion \"so liegen...\" is kept as the main clause after \"then\".","\"sporen\" is doubtful in the OCR (perhaps *spornen*, or another word); left in the original in brackets. \"ihren Rand\" is almost certainly *ihren Raub* (prey), and so rendered.","\"Wesen der Dinge\" = essences of things; \"Möglichkeit\" = possibility; \"Mittelursachen\" = intermediate causes."]],"beweisgrund-0121":["One may choose one utility from a thousand on account of which one may regard it as necessary that there be an atmosphere, if one absolutely demands a purpose as the ground by which an arrangement in nature was first occasioned. I therefore concede this, and name, say, the breathing of men and animals as the final aim of this arrangement. Now this air, through the very same properties, and no more than it would require for breathing alone, gives occasion at the same time to an infinity of fine consequences, which necessarily accompany it and need not be promoted by special provisions. The very same elastic force and weight of the air make sucking possible, without which young animals would have to go without nourishment, and the possibility of pumps is a necessary consequence of it. By them it comes about that moisture is drawn up in vapours, which thicken above into clouds, which beautify the day, often temper the excessive heat of the sun, but serve chiefly to moisten the dry regions of the earth's surface by the [*Rand*] from the water-beds of the low [*wilde*]. The twilight, which lengthens the day and, by gradual intermediate degrees, makes the passage from night to day harmless to the eye, and above all the winds, are quite natural and unforced consequences of the same properties.",["OCR: \"Lag\" read as *Tag*. \"durch den Rand von den Wasserbetten der niedrigen wilde\" is corrupt: \"Rand\" and \"wilde\" are doubtful (perhaps *Zug* or *Strich*, and *Gegenden*); I leave both in brackets and do not guess.","\"Luftkreis\" rendered \"atmosphere\"; \"Endabsicht\" \"final aim\".","\"die ... dürfen\" is rendered \"need not be\" for \"nicht dürfen ... befördert werden\" (i.e. *brauchen nicht*); the sense is clear."]],"beweisgrund-0122":["Suppose someone to draw up a plan by which the coasts of the countries of the torrid zone, which would otherwise have to be hotter than the regions lying farther inland, might enjoy a somewhat more tolerable warmth. He will most naturally hit upon a sea-breeze, which would have to blow for this purpose during the hottest hours of the day. But at night the air over the sea grows cold much more quickly than that over the land, and it would not be advisable that the same wind should blow continuously. He would therefore wish that it had pleased Providence so to arrange matters that in the middle hours of the night the wind should turn back again from the land, which might also promote many other benefits. The only question would then be by what mechanism and contrived arrangement this alternation of winds could be obtained. Here one would have great cause to fear that, since man cannot demand that all laws of nature should accommodate themselves to his convenience, this means, though possible, might fit so ill with the other necessary provisions that the supreme wisdom would for that reason not see fit to ordain it. All this misgiving, however, is unnecessary. What an arrangement made by deliberate choice would accomplish is here performed by the air in accordance with the universal laws of motion; and the very same simple principle of its other uses produces this one also, without any new and special provisions. The air over the burning soil of such a country, rarefied by the heat of the day, necessarily yields to the denser and heavier air over the cool sea, and so causes the sea-breeze, which accordingly blows from the hottest hours of the day until late in the evening. The sea air, which for the same reasons has not been heated so strongly by day as that over the land, cools more quickly at night, contracts, and occasions the retreat of the land air during the night. Everyone knows that all the coasts of the torrid continent enjoy this alternating wind.",["The long sentence beginning 'Weil aber...' was divided into several sentences; the causal clause about night cooling was made a separate statement.","'heißen Weltstrichs' rendered 'torrid zone'; 'Welttheils' later rendered 'continent' (the German varies, and the later phrase is loose).","Stray OCR characters ('sich)', 'cheißesten') silently corrected; 'Anord— nung' etc. rejoined.","'Vorsehung' = Providence; 'oberste Weisheit' = supreme wisdom.","'Principium' is Kant's Latinism; rendered 'principle'."]],"beweisgrund-0123":["In order to show the relations which simple and very general laws of motion have, through the necessity of their essence, to order and harmony [*Wohlgereimtheit*], I have cast my glance only upon a small part of nature, namely the effects of the air. It will easily be seen that the whole immeasurable extent of the great order of nature lies open before me in just the same respect. I reserve to myself the right to add something further in what follows toward widening this fair prospect. For the present I should be omitting something essential if I did not mention the important discovery which Monsieur de Maupertuis has made concerning the harmony of the necessary and most universal laws of motion.",["'Wohlgereimtheit' (literally 'rhyming-well-together') rendered 'harmony', with the German given at first occurrence; elsewhere I use 'harmony' for it and for 'Harmonie' where they coincide.","'aus der acht lassen' is a mis-OCR for 'aus der Acht lassen' (to omit); corrected silently.","'Herr v. Maupertuis' rendered 'Monsieur de Maupertuis' as a conventional equivalent."]],"beweisgrund-0124":["What we have adduced by way of proof concerns, it is true, widely extended and necessary laws, but only for one particular kind of the materials of the world. Monsieur de Maupertuis, on the other hand, proved that even the most universal laws according to which matter in general acts, whether in equilibrium or in impact, whether of elastic or inelastic bodies, in the attraction of light in refraction no less than in its repulsion in reflection, are subject to one ruling rule, in accordance with which the greatest economy in action is always observed. By this discovery the effects of matter, notwithstanding the great differences which they may in themselves display, are brought under one universal formula, which expresses a relation to fitness, beauty and harmony. Yet the laws of motion are themselves of such a nature that matter can never be thought without them, and they are so necessary that they can be derived with the greatest distinctness from the universal and essential constitution of all matter, even without the least experiments. The learned and acute author at once perceived that, since unity is thereby produced in the infinitely manifold of the universe, and order in what is blindly necessary, there must be some supreme principle from which all this can derive its harmony and fitness. He rightly believed that so universal a connection in the simplest natures of things affords a far more suitable ground for finding with certainty, in some perfect original being, the ultimate cause of everything in the world, than does any observation of various contingent and changeable arrangements according to particular laws. It now remained to be seen what use the higher philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] could make of this important new insight. I believe I am not mistaken in conjecturing that the Royal Academy of Sciences in Berlin had this in view in setting the prize-question whether the laws of motion are necessary or contingent, a question which no one has answered in accordance with expectation.",["The final sentence, with its relative clause 'welche niemand...', was recast: 'a question which no one has answered...'.","'Urwesen' = 'original being'; 'Principium' = 'principle'; 'Weltweisheit' = philosophy (the German kept for the sense).","'Anständigkeit' rendered 'fitness' (propriety/seemliness); a choice.","'Gleichgewichte' = equilibrium; 'Abprallung' = reflection, as the optical sense requires.","OCR 'sich)' silently corrected."]],"beweisgrund-0125":["If contingency is taken in the real sense [*im Realverstande*], as consisting in the dependence of the materials of possibility upon something else, then it is evident that the laws of motion, and the universal properties of matter which obey them, must depend upon some great common original being, the ground of order and harmony. For who would hold that, in a vast manifold in which each single thing had its own wholly independent nature, everything should nevertheless, by some strange chance, so accommodate itself that it fitted well together and unity showed itself in the whole? But that this principle must relate not merely to the existence of this matter and of the properties assigned to it, but to the very possibility of matter in general and to its essence itself, is clearly evident from the fact that what is to fill a space, and what is to be capable of the motion of impact and pressure, cannot be thought under other conditions than those from which the said laws necessarily flow. On this footing we see that these laws of motion of matter are absolutely necessary, that is, that if the possibility of matter is presupposed, it is self-contradictory that it should act according to other laws; and this is a logical necessity of the highest kind. We see also that the inner possibility of matter itself, namely the data and the real that underlies this [content] of thought [*Denkliche*], is not given independently or of itself, but is posited through some principle in which the manifold receives unity and the diverse receives connection; and this proves the contingency of the laws of motion in the real sense.",["'Realverstande' is rendered 'real sense', with the German; contrasted with logical necessity later in the passage.","The two long sentences were divided and the dependent clauses 'daß gleichwohl...' made a parallel assertion ('We see also that...').","'Materialen der Möglichkeit' = 'materials of possibility'; 'Data' kept as 'data'.","OCR 'ö Principium' read as 'dieses Principium'; 'set' read as 'sei'.","'[content]' supplied for the substantive adjective 'Denkliche'."]],"beweisgrund-0126":["Second Consideration.",[]],"beweisgrund-0127":["Distinction of the dependence of all things upon God into the",["The heading continues in the next passage ('moral and non-moral')."]],"beweisgrund-0128":["moral and non-moral.",["'unmoralisch' rendered 'non-moral' (not 'immoral'), as Kant's definition in the next passage requires."]],"beweisgrund-0129":["I call that dependence of a thing upon God in which he is a ground of it through his will, moral; all other dependence is non-moral. If, therefore, I maintain that God contains the ultimate ground even of the inner possibility of things, everyone will readily understand that this dependence can only be non-moral; for the will makes nothing possible, but only resolves upon what is already presupposed as possible. In so far as God contains the ground of the existence of things, I admit that this dependence is always moral, that is, that they exist because he has willed that they should be.",["'unmoralisch' rendered 'non-moral'; the definition makes clear that no ethical censure is meant.","OCR 'un moralisch' and 'Abhangigkeit' silently corrected."]],"beweisgrund-0130":["For the inner possibility of things offers to him who resolved upon their existence materials which contain an extraordinary aptitude for agreement, and a fitting-together, lying in their very essence, into a whole that is orderly and beautiful in manifold ways. That an atmosphere exists may, on account of the ends to be attained through it, be ascribed to God as to a moral ground. But that so great a fruitfulness lies in the essence of a single and so simple a ground, that there lies already in its possibility so much fitness and harmony, which needs no fresh provisions in order to accord with other possible things of a world according to manifold rules of order—this certainly cannot in turn be ascribed to a free choice; for every resolution of a will presupposes knowledge of the possibility of what is to be resolved upon.",["Last sentence kept as one long sentence with dashes; the 'daß'-clauses are subjects of 'das kann ... nicht beigemessen werden', which I resumed with 'this'.","'Erkenntniß' = knowledge; 'Wesen' rendered 'essence'; 'Grund' = ground; 'beimessen' = ascribe.","'ge— mäß' is a line-break hyphenation, silently joined."]],"beweisgrund-0131":["Everything whose ground is to be sought in a free choice must, so far, also be contingent. Now the union of many and manifold consequences with one another, which flow necessarily from a single ground, is not a contingent union; consequently it cannot be ascribed to a voluntary determination. Thus we have seen above that the possibility of pumps, of breathing, the rising of liquid matters (where there are any) into vapours, the winds, and so forth, are inseparable from one another, since they all depend upon a single ground, namely the elasticity and weight of the air; and this agreement of the manifold in one is therefore in no way contingent, and so is not to be ascribed to a moral ground.",["OCR 'von ; einander' corrected silently to 'von einander'; 'Ubereinstimmung' = agreement.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination'; 'freiwillige' = voluntary."]],"beweisgrund-0132":["I am here concerned always only with the relation which the essence of the air, or of any other thing, has to the possible production of so many beautiful consequences; that is, I consider only the aptitude of its nature for so many ends. In this respect the unity, owing to the agreement of a single ground with so many possible consequences, is certainly necessary, and these possible consequences are, so far, inseparable from one another and from the thing itself. As regards the actual production of these benefits, it is contingent in so far as one of the things to which the thing is related may be lacking, or a foreign force may hinder the effect. |",["The sign | is carried over as it stands at the end of the passage.","'Tauglichkeit' = aptitude; 'einigen Grundes' = 'single ground'."]],"beweisgrund-0133":["In the properties of space there lie beautiful relations, and in the immeasurable manifold of its determinations an admirable unity. The existence of all this harmony, in so far as matter was to fill space, is, with all its consequences, to be ascribed to the arbitrary will of the first cause; but as regards the union with one another of so many consequences, all of which stand in such great harmony with the things in the world, it would be absurd to seek this in turn in a will. Among the other necessary consequences from the nature of the air is also to be reckoned this, that resistance is offered by it to the matters moved within it. The raindrops, as they fall from a very great height, are checked by it and come down with moderate speed, whereas without this retardation they would have acquired, in falling from such a height, a most destructive force. This is an advantage which, since without it the air is not possible, has not been joined to its other properties by a special decree. Whether the cohesion of the parts of matter, in water for example, be a necessary consequence of the possibility of matter in general, or a specially arranged ordinance, its immediate effect is the round figure of small parts of it, such as the raindrops. Through this, however, the beautiful coloured arch becomes possible, according to very general laws of motion, which stands, with a moving splendour and regularity, above the horizon when the unobscured sun shines into the raindrops falling opposite. That fluid matters and heavy bodies exist can be ascribed only to the will of this mighty Author; but that a world-body in its fluid state, by entirely necessary means and in accordance with such general laws, strives to assume a spherical shape, which afterwards accords better than any other possible shape with the remaining ends of the universe, since for example such a surface is capable of the most uniform distribution of light—this lies in the essence of the thing itself.",["Restated 'da durch sie ... Widerstand geleistet wird' as 'this, that resistance is offered by it'.","OCR damage ('veran- | ftaltete', 'Wirfung', 'Ger | sichtskreise', 'herab- ı: fallende', '2c') silently corrected; line/page numbers and the stray '2c' dropped. The | marks inside words were removed as OCR break artefacts rather than Academy apparatus.","'Gesichtskreis' rendered 'horizon'; 'rührenden' = 'moving' (touching); 'Willkür' = 'arbitrary will'; 'Begehren' left as 'will' (desire), a choice.","'ganz nothwendiger Weise' rendered 'by entirely necessary means'; 'Weltkörper' = 'world-body'."]],"beweisgrund-0134":["The cohesion of matter, and the resistance which its parts combine with their separability, make friction necessary. Friction is of such great utility, and accords with the order in all the manifold changes of nature as well as anything could that had not flowed from such general grounds but had been added by a special provision. If friction did not retard motions, the preservation of the forces once produced, through communication to others, through rebounding and continually renewed impacts and shocks, would in the end bring everything into confusion. The surfaces on which bodies lie would always have to be perfectly horizontal (which they can only seldom be), otherwise the bodies would always slip. All twisted ropes hold only by friction. For",["Passage breaks off at 'Denn' and is translated as it stands.","Stray line number '25' dropped; 'Aufbehaltung' = 'preservation'; 'Anstalt' = 'provision'."]],"beweisgrund-0135":["the threads, which are not as long as the whole rope, would be drawn apart by the slightest force, were they not held back by the friction proportionate to the force with which they are pressed together by the twisting.",["Continues the previous sentence ('Denn ... die Fäden'); stray '2' (line number) dropped.","'gemäße Reibung' construed with 'der Kraft ... gemäß': friction proportionate to the pressing force."]],"beweisgrund-0136":["I cite here such little regarded and common consequences of the simplest and most general laws of nature for two reasons. First, so that one may infer from them both the great and infinitely widely extended agreement which the essences of things in general have with one another, and the great consequences to be ascribed to it, even in cases where one is not skilful enough to trace back some order of nature to such simple and general grounds. Second, so that one may feel the absurdity involved in naming the wisdom of God as the special ground of such agreements. That there are things which have so many beautiful relations is to be ascribed to the wise choice of him who brought them forth for the sake of this harmony. But that each of them contains so extended a fitness for manifold agreement through simple grounds, and that an admirable unity in the whole could thereby be obtained, lies in the very possibility of things; and since the contingent, which must be presupposed in every choice, here vanishes, the ground of this unity can indeed be sought in a wise being, but not by means of his wisdom.",["Divided the long opening sentence into 'first ... second' ('sowohl ... als auch'); the numbering is supplied for clarity.","Line-break hyphenations ('Naturord nung', 'Über— einstimmungen', 'Schicklich— keit', 'Zu— fällige') silently joined.","'Widersinnige' (OCR 'Widerfinnige') = 'absurdity'."]],"beweisgrund-0137":["Third Consideration.",["'Betrachtung' rendered 'Consideration'."]],"beweisgrund-0138":["On the Dependence of the Things of the World upon God by means of",[]],"beweisgrund-0139":["the Order of Nature, or without it.",[]],"beweisgrund-0140":["Division of World-Events, in so far as they stand under the",[]],"beweisgrund-0141":["Order of Nature or not.",[]],"beweisgrund-0142":["*) See [Kant's essay] on the World's Beginning, Change and Destruction.",["Footnote; the OCR 'Raj' is doubtful and may be a corruption of a reference to Kant's own work or a cited author; I have not resolved it and the bracketed words are my conjecture only.","'Veränd.' expanded as 'Change' ('Veränderung'); 'Untergang' = 'destruction'."]],"beweisgrund-0143":["A thing stands under the order of nature in so far as its existence or its alteration is sufficiently grounded in the forces of nature. Two things are required for this. First, the force of nature must be the efficient cause of it; second, the manner in which that force is directed to the production of this effect must itself be sufficiently grounded in a rule of the natural laws of operation. Events of this kind are also entitled, quite simply, natural events of the world. Where this is not so, the case, which does not stand under such a ground, is something supernatural. This occurs in two ways: either in so far as the proximate efficient cause lies outside nature, that is, in so far as the divine power produces the event immediately; or, secondly, when it is only the manner in which the forces of nature have been directed to this case that is not contained under a rule of nature. In the first case I call the event supernatural *materialiter*, in the second *formaliter*. Since only the latter case seems to require some explanation, the rest being clear of itself, I shall give examples of it.\n\nThere are many forces in nature which have the power to destroy single human beings, or states, or the whole human race: earthquakes, storms, the movements of the sea, comets, and so on. It is also sufficiently grounded, according to a general law, in the constitution of nature that some of these should occasionally happen. But among the laws according to which they happen, the vices and moral corruption of the human race are no natural grounds at all that could stand in connection with them. The misdeeds of a city have no influence upon the hidden fire of the earth, and the luxuries of the first world did not belong to the efficient causes which could draw comets down towards it in their orbits. And when such a case occurs, but is ascribed to a natural law, what is meant is that it is a misfortune, not that it is a punishment; for the moral conduct of men cannot be a ground of earthquakes according to a natural law, since here there is no connection of causes and effects.\n\nFor example, when the earthquake overturns the city of Port Royal in Jamaica,* he who calls this a natural event will mean by it the following. Although, by the testimony of their preacher, the vicious deeds of the inhabitants may well have deserved such a devastation as a judgment of punishment, nevertheless this case is to be regarded as one among many which occur from time to time according to a more general law of nature, in that regions of the earth, and among them sometimes cities, and among these now and then even very vicious cities, are shaken. If, on the other hand, it is to be regarded as a punishment, then these forces of nature, since according to a natural law they cannot have any connection with the conduct of men, must have been directed by the Supreme Being specially to each such single case. But then the event is supernatural in the formal sense, although the intermediate cause was a force of nature.\n\nAnd even if this event finally came about as a judgment of punishment through a long series of preparations specially laid down for it in the active forces of the world, and even if one were to suppose that God had already at the creation made all arrangements so that it should afterwards happen at the right time through the forces in nature directed to that end (as one may conceive this in Whiston's theory of the Flood, in so far as it is supposed to originate from a comet), the supernatural is not thereby diminished at all, but only removed far back into the creation, and thereby increased beyond description. For this whole series, in so far as the manner of its arrangement referred to the outcome, and in respect of that outcome was in no way to be regarded as a consequence of more general laws of nature, betokens an immediate and still greater divine care, directed upon so long a chain of consequences in order also to avoid the obstacles which might have caused the exact attainment of the effects sought to miscarry.",["Divided the long opening and closing sentences into shorter ones; the opening 'gegründet dt' is an OCR slip, read as 'gegründet ist'.","'heißen' rendered 'are entitled'; *materialiter* and *formaliter* kept in Latin and italicised.","'Verstande' in 'im formalen Verstande' rendered 'in the formal sense'; 'Nethenfolge' read as 'Reihenfolge' (series), an OCR slip.","'Uppigkeiten' read as 'Üppigkeiten' (luxuries); 'über natürlich' read as 'übernatürlich'; 'Meers— bewegungen' joined as 'Meeresbewegungen'.","An asterisk follows 'umkehrt' where the OCR shows a stray mark ('umkehrt,“'), probably a footnote sign whose text is not in the passage; 'sich)' is likewise an OCR slip for 'sich'.","'Es steht ... unter': 'Strafgericht' rendered 'judgment of punishment'; 'zureichend gegründet' is 'sufficiently grounded' (Kant's *ratio sufficiens*).","'Wenn man ihn einem natürlichen Gesetze beimisst' (OCR 'mißt ... bei') is rendered 'ascribed'; 'man' resolved as impersonal."]],"beweisgrund-0144":["On the other hand, there are punishments and rewards according to the order of nature, because the moral conduct of men stands in connection with them according to the laws of causes and effects. Wild lust and intemperance end in a sickly and tormented life. Intrigue and cunning come to grief in the end, and honesty is, after all, in the end the best policy. In all this the connection of the consequences takes place according to the laws of nature. But however many such punishments or rewards, or any other events in the world, there may be, for which the direction of the forces of nature has had in every case to take place in an extraordinary manner for each single instance, even if a certain uniformity prevails among many of them, they are subordinated indeed to an immediate divine law, namely that of His wisdom, but to no law of nature.",["'Jederzeit außerordentlich auf jeden einzelnen Fall hat geschehen müssen' restated in smoother English; the sentence is long and was kept as one with its concessive clause.","'Richtung der Naturkräfte' rendered 'direction of the forces of nature'; 'Verknüpfung' as 'connection'."]],"beweisgrund-0145":["2.",[]],"beweisgrund-0146":["Division of natural events, in so far as",["Heading continues in the next two passages."]],"beweisgrund-0147":["they stand under the necessary or the contingent order",[]],"beweisgrund-0148":["of nature.",["The three heading fragments together read: 'Division of natural events, in so far as they stand under the necessary or the contingent order of nature.'"]],"beweisgrund-0149":["All things of nature are contingent in their existence. The connection of different kinds of things, e.g. of air, earth and water, is likewise without doubt contingent, and so far is to be ascribed solely to the arbitrary will [*Willkür*] of the supreme Author. But although the laws of nature seem to possess no necessity, in so far as the things themselves to which they belong, and likewise the connections in which they can be exercised, are contingent, there nevertheless remains a kind of necessity which is very remarkable. For there are many laws of nature whose unity is necessary; that is to say, in them the very same ground of agreement for one law also makes other laws necessary. For example, the very same elastic force and weight of the air which is a ground of the laws of respiration is necessarily at the same time a ground of the possibility of pumps, of the possibility of the clouds that are to be produced, of the maintenance of fire, of the winds, and so forth. It is necessary that the ground for the rest should be found, as soon as the ground for even a single one of them is present.\n\nOn the other hand, if the ground of a certain kind of similar effects according to one law is not at the same time, in the same being, the ground of another kind of effects according to another law, then the union of these laws is contingent. In other words, a contingent unity prevails in these laws, and what takes place in the thing in accordance with them happens according to a contingent order of nature. Man sees, hears, smells, tastes, and so on; but the very same properties which are the grounds of seeing are not also those of tasting. He must have different organs for hearing and for tasting. The union of [s]o different faculties [*Vermögen*] is contingent and, since it aims at perfection, artificial. Within each organ, again, there is artificial unity. In the eye the part that admits the light is one, that which refracts it another, and yet another that which receives the image. On the other hand, there are not different causes which give the earth its spherical shape, and others again which hold bodies on the earth against the centrifugal force of its rotation, and yet another which keeps the moon in its orbit; gravity alone is one cause which necessarily suffices for all this.\n\nNow it is without doubt a perfection that grounds for all these effects are found in nature; and if the same ground which determines one of them is also sufficient for the others, so much the more unity accrues thereby to the whole. But this unity, and with it the perfection, is in the case here cited necessary and attaches to the essence of the thing; and all the suitability to ends [*Wohlgereimtheit*], fruitfulness and beauty which are in this respect to be thanked to it, depend upon God by means of the essential order of nature, that is, by means of that which is necessary in the order of nature. I hope I shall be understood when I say that I do not wish this necessity to extend to the existence of these things themselves, but solely to the agreement and unity lying in their possibility, as a necessary ground of so exceedingly great a fitness and fruitfulness.\n\nThe creatures of the vegetable and animal kingdoms throughout offer the most admirable examples of a unity which is contingent, yet in accord with great wisdom. There are vessels that absorb sap, vessels that absorb air, those which elaborate the sap, and those which exhale it, and so on: a great manifold, of which each taken singly has no fitness for the effects of the others, and where their union for the total perfection is artificial, so that the plant itself, with its relations to such different ends, constitutes a contingent and arbitrary unity.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'WiNkür' to Willkür, 'Satt' to Saft (sap), 'Ubereinstimmung', and the hyphenation breaks.","'Die Vereinbarung [o verschiedener Vermögen': the bracketed '[o' is a corrupt OCR reading, evidently 'so'; I render it '[s]o' and flag it as doubtful.","Divided the long passage into several paragraphs and sentences; 'Dagegen' at the opening of the second paragraph is rendered 'On the other hand'.","'Z. E.' rendered 'e.g.' / 'For example'; 'Wohlgereimtheit' rendered 'suitability to ends', a choice; 'Willkür' kept as 'arbitrary will' with the German.","'Wesen' rendered 'being' in 'in demselben Wesen' but 'essence' in 'dem Wesen der Sache'; 'wesentliche Ordnung' is 'essential order'.","'Drehungsschwung' rendered 'centrifugal force of its rotation', an interpretive gloss; literally 'the impetus of rotation'."]],"beweisgrund-0150":["On the other hand, inorganic nature in particular supplies an unspeakable number of proofs of a necessary unity in the relation of a single ground to many fitting consequences. So much is this the case that we are led to conjecture that perhaps even where, in organic nature itself, some perfection may seem to have its particular provision [*Anstalt*] as its ground, it may well be a necessary consequence of the very same ground which already, in its essential fruitfulness, connects it with many other beautiful effects; so that even in these realms of nature there may be more necessary unity than is commonly thought.",["Restated the single long sentence as two; 'anständige Folgen' rendered 'fitting consequences'.","OCR break 'organi— schen' silently repaired."]],"beweisgrund-0151":["Since the forces of nature and their laws of operation contain the ground of an order of nature which, in so far as it comprehends manifold harmony in a necessary unity, brings it about that the connection of many perfections in one ground becomes a law, we must consider various natural effects, in respect of their beauty and utility, as standing under the essential order of nature, and by means of it under God. On the other hand, since many perfections in a whole are not possible through the fruitfulness of a single ground, but require various grounds arbitrarily united for this purpose, many an artificial arrangement will in turn be the cause of a law. The effects which take place in accordance with it stand under the contingent and artificial order of nature, and by means of it under God.",["OCR 'Schön- E\" und Nützlichkeit' read as 'Schönheit und Nützlichkeit' (doubtful reading, sense secure).","'vermittelt' read as 'vermittelst'; 'Anordnung' rendered 'arrangement'."]],"beweisgrund-0152":["Fourth Consideration.",["'Betrachtung' rendered 'Consideration'."]],"beweisgrund-0153":["The Use of Our Ground of Proof in Judging the Perfection of a World According to the Course of Nature.",["Heading continues in the next passage (0154); the German 'Voll— / kommenheit' is split across the two."]],"beweisgrund-0154":["[The Use of Our Ground of Proof in Judging the Perfection of a World] according to the Course of Nature. 1.",["The bracketed words repeat the preceding heading, with which this fragment forms one sentence; I did not carry them as separate text. Only 'kommenheit einer Welt nach dem Laufe der Natur. 1.' is the passage proper, so 'Vollkommenheit' is completed from 0153."]],"beweisgrund-0155":["What Can Be Inferred from Our Ground of Proof in Favour of the Preference of the Order of Nature over the Supernatural.",["Heading split over 0155–0157; rendered here in full at the first fragment, with the remaining fragments given as their share."]],"beweisgrund-0156":["[of the order of nature over the supernatural, can be inferred]",["Continuation of the heading begun in 0155; the translation there already includes it, so this is given as a bracketed fragment."]],"beweisgrund-0157":["[…]",["Last word of the heading ('werden', 'be inferred'), already included in the rendering at 0155."]],"beweisgrund-0158":["It is a well-known rule of philosophers, or rather of sound reason in general, that nothing should be held to be a miracle or a supernatural event without the most weighty cause. This rule contains, first, that miracles are rare; secondly, that the entire perfection of the universe is attained, in accordance with the divine will, by the laws of nature, even without many supernatural influences. For everyone recognises that if the world, without frequent miracles, would miss the end of its existence, supernatural events would have to be something ordinary.\n\nSome hold the opinion that the formal element of the natural connection of consequences with their grounds is in itself a perfection, to which a better result, if it were not to be obtained otherwise than in a supernatural way, would on occasion have to be sacrificed. They immediately ascribe a preference to the natural as such, because everything supernatural seems to them, as an interruption of an order, to give rise in itself to an inconvenience. But this difficulty is merely imagined. The good lies only in the attainment of the end, and is attributed to the means only for the sake of the end. The natural order, if perfect consequences do not issue from it, has immediately no ground of preference in itself, because it can be considered only after the manner of a means, which admits of no value of its own, but only one borrowed from the greatness of the end thereby attained",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence at 'weil sie nur nach'; the continuation appears in 0159. I have rendered the text of 0158 through 'only after the manner of a means…' by borrowing from 0159, which is a slight overreach: strictly, 0158 ends at 'only according to'.","Restated: divided into two paragraphs and several sentences; 'Formale' rendered 'formal element', 'Erfolg' as 'result', 'Übelstand' as 'inconvenience'."]],"beweisgrund-0159":["the manner of a means, which admits of no value of its own, but only one borrowed from the magnitude of the end thereby attained.",["Continues the sentence of 0158. The marginal line number in the OCR was dropped."]],"beweisgrund-0160":["The representation of the toilsomeness which men feel in their immediate exertions",["Fragment; the sentence continues in 0161. 'Vorstellung' rendered 'representation'."]],"beweisgrund-0161":["secretly mingles in here, and gives a preference to what can be entrusted to alien forces,",["Continuation of 0160; line number '5' dropped."]],"beweisgrund-0162":["even where something of the intended utility would be missed in the result.",["'abgezweckten' rendered 'intended'; OCR break 'abge— zweckten' repaired."]],"beweisgrund-0163":["Meanwhile, if, without greater trouble,",[]],"beweisgrund-0164":["the man who sets the timber to a sawmill could just as well turn it directly",["Sentence continues in 0165."]],"beweisgrund-0165":["into planks, all the art of this machine would be merely a plaything, because its whole value can be found in it only as a means",["OCR 'unmittel— dar' read 'unmittelbar'; line number 'lo' (i.e. 10) dropped."]],"beweisgrund-0166":["to this end. Accordingly something is not good because",[]],"beweisgrund-0167":["it happens according to the course of nature, but the course of nature",[]],"beweisgrund-0168":["is good in so far as what flows from it is good. And since God [a world]",["Breaks off mid-sentence at 'Und da Gott eine Welt'; 'eine Welt' is rendered 'a world' and the bracket in the translation is superfluous: read 'And since God [chooses/…] a world' is NOT supplied. Nothing is completed."]],"beweisgrund-0169":["[...] comprised in his decree, in which for the most part everything fulfilled the rule of the best through a natural connection, he deemed them worthy of his choice. He did so not because the good consisted in their hanging together naturally, but because through this natural connection the perfect ends were most correctly attained without many miracles.",["The passage begins mid-sentence, and the antecedent of 'in der' and the subject of 'würdigte er sie' are not given. I render 'in which' and 'them' without guessing, and have restated the sentence's break as two sentences.","OCR 'native s lichen' read as 'natürlichen'."]],"beweisgrund-0170":["And now the question arises: how can it come about that the universal laws of nature correspond so beautifully to the will of the Supreme Being in the course of those events of the world which happen in accordance with them? And what ground have we for ascribing to them this fitness, so that we need not [more] often, as we observe [events], admit secret supernatural arrangements which would incessantly have to come to the aid of their defects?¹) Here our concept of the dependence of the essences of all things upon God serves us to a still wider purpose than that which one expects in this question. The things of nature bear, even in the most necessary determinations of their inner possibility, the mark of dependence upon",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence. It continues, after the intervening footnotes, in the next passage ('demjenigen Wesen an sich ...').","Doubtful reading: 'nicht öfter, mals man wahrnimmt' is garbled by OCR. I take it as 'öftermals, wie/so oft man wahrnimmt' and render loosely; the supplied '[more]' is conjectural.","Silent OCR corrections: 'allgemeine o Gesetze' to 'allgemeinen Gesetze'; 'Begeben— heilen' to 'Begebenheiten'. The footnote marker is given as ¹) because the source's asterisks would clash with the italic markup.","I divided the long question into two sentences. 'Wesen' is rendered 'essences' (the sense is Kant's essences of things, as in the later passage); 'Bestimmungen' is rendered 'determinations'."]],"beweisgrund-0171":["¹) If it is a necessary outcome of nature, as Newton supposes, that a world-system such as that of our sun should in the end come to a complete standstill and universal rest, I should not add with him that it is necessary for God to restore it by a miracle. For since it is a result to which nature is necessarily determined according to its most essential laws, I conjecture from this that it is also good. It need not strike us as a lamentable loss; for we do not know what immensity belongs to nature, which is forever forming itself afresh in other regions of the heavens, so that by great fruitfulness it may abundantly replace elsewhere this loss [*Abgang*] of the universe.",["This is the footnote to 'unausbleiblich ist' in the next passage; I have marked it ¹) for convenience, though the source marks it with an asterisk.","A stray 'b' at the end of the OCR is dropped as an artefact.","I read 'Abgang des Universum' as 'loss of [part of] the universe', and keep the German since the sense turns on it."]],"beweisgrund-0172":["²) This question is by no means sufficiently answered if one appeals to the wise choice of God, which had already so well ordered the course of nature that frequent repairs were unnecessary. For the greatest difficulty lies in how it could even have been possible to unite so great a perfection in a connection of world-events according to universal laws. This is especially so when one considers the multitude of natural things and the immeasurably long series of their changes, and asks how, in accordance with universal rules of their reciprocal efficacy, a harmony could have arisen that would require no frequent supernatural influences.\n\n[...] that Being in itself in which everything accords with the attributes of wisdom and goodness. We may expect of them agreement and beautiful connection, and a necessary unity in the manifold advantageous relations which a single ground has to many fitting laws. There will be no need for immediate divine corrections to intervene where nature works according to necessary laws. For in so far as the consequences are necessary according to the order of nature, nothing displeasing to God can ever occur, even under the most universal laws. How, indeed, could the consequences of things be contrary to his will? Their contingent connection depends on the will of God, while their essential relations, as the grounds of what is necessary in the order of nature, proceed from that in God which stands in the greatest harmony with his attributes in general. How, I say, can these be opposed to his will? And so all the changes of the world which are mechanical, and therefore necessary according to the laws of motion, must always be good just because they are naturally necessary; and it is to be expected that the consequence will be incapable of improvement as soon as it is inevitable according to the order of nature.¹)\n\nBut, in order that all misunderstanding may be prevented, I observe that the changes in the world are of two kinds. Either they are necessary in virtue of the first ordering of the universe and the general and particular laws of nature, as is everything that proceeds mechanically in the corporeal world; or they have, notwithstanding all this, a contingency not sufficiently comprehended, like actions springing from freedom, the nature of which is not properly understood. The latter kind of world-changes, in so far as they seem to involve an absence of constraint [*Ungebundenheit*] in respect of determining grounds and necessary laws, to that extent contain a possibility of deviating from the general tendency of natural things toward perfection. For this reason one may expect that supernatural supplements may be necessary, since it is possible that in this respect the course of nature may at times conflict with the will of God.\n\nNevertheless, the powers of freely acting beings are not wholly withdrawn from all laws in their connection with the rest of the universe. They are always subject to grounds which, though not necessitating, yet make their exercise certain in another way according to the rules of choice [*Willkür*]. Hence the universal dependence of the essences of things upon God remains here too a great ground for judging that the consequences which occur even among this kind of things in the course of nature are on the whole fitting and in accordance with the rule of the best (without our being misled by the apparent deviation in individual cases). The order of nature therefore only seldom requires an immediate supernatural correction or supplement; and revelation, too, makes mention of such only in respect of certain times and certain peoples.\n\nExperience also agrees with this dependence of even the freest actions upon a great natural rule. However contingent the resolution to marry may be, one finds in one and the same country that the proportion of marriages to the number of the living is fairly constant if one takes large numbers, and that, for example, among 110 persons of both sexes there is one married couple. Everyone knows how much human freedom contributes to the lengthening or shortening of life. Yet even these free actions must be subject to a great order, since on the average, when one takes large numbers, the number of the dying stands always in very exactly the same proportion to the living. I content myself with these few proofs, in order to make it somewhat intelligible that even the laws of freedom do not carry with them such an absence of constraint in respect of the rules of a universal order of nature as to prevent the very same ground from producing in the natural course of free conduct, without manifold miracles, at least a greater direction toward the good pleasure of the Supreme Being. This ground, in the rest of nature, has already fixed in the essences of things themselves an unfailing relation to perfection and harmony.\n\nMy attention, however, is directed rather to the course of natural changes, in so far as they are necessary through implanted laws. In such an order miracles will be necessary either not at all or only seldom, since it cannot reasonably be that such imperfections should naturally arise as would require them.",["The passage as printed interleaves footnote ²) (to the question in the previous passage) with the main text, which continues from the end of the previous passage ('von / demjenigen Wesen an sich'). I have separated the two and rendered the footnote first, as it stands; the main text begins mid-sentence.","Doubtful reading: OCR 'seiner öftern übernatürlichen Einflüsse bedürfe' should probably be 'keiner ...' (sense and mood of 'bedürfe'); I translate 'would require no frequent supernatural influences'. Other silent corrections: 'mad)' to 'nach'; 'Xundessen' to 'Indessen'; 'benbthigt' to 'benöthigt'; 'Eben' to 'Ehen'.","The footnote marker ¹) in the main text refers to the Newton footnote (previous passage); ²) is my marker for the footnote that opens this passage.","The very long sentences have been divided and the paragraphs broken for readability; the German is one unbroken block. The sentence 'Denn wie sollten doch ... entgegen sein?' is restated, with a parenthetical clause turned into a separate sentence, and 'wie können diese, sage ich' is retained as a resumption.","I supplied 'of two kinds' (the German has only 'entweder ... oder'), and 'the very same ground ... This ground' is a split of the final clause of the 'Ich begnüge mich' sentence.","Terms: *Willkür* kept in German, since it is a technical term here; 'Ungebundenheit' glossed as 'absence of constraint'; 'Wesen' is rendered 'essences' in 'Wesen der Dinge' but 'Being' for the Supreme Being; 'anständig' is rendered 'fitting'.","'unter 110 Menschen ... ein Ehepaar' is rendered literally; the figure is as printed."]],"beweisgrund-0173":["If I formed for myself the concept of the things of nature which is commonly held of them, namely, that their inner possibility is independent in itself and without any extraneous ground, I should not find it at all unexpected if it were said that a world of any considerable perfection is impossible without many supernatural effects. I should rather find it strange and incomprehensible how, without a constant series of miracles, anything fit for its purpose could be accomplished in such a world through a great natural connection. For it would have to be a startling chance that the essences of things, each of which would have its own separate necessity, should so adapt themselves to one another that even the highest wisdom could unite them into a great whole, in which, despite such manifold dependence, an unimprovable harmony and beauty shone forth according to universal laws. On the other hand, since I am instructed that something else [anders] is possible only because there is a God, I expect, even from the possibilities of things, an accord conformable to their great principle, and an aptitude to fit together, through universal arrangements, into a whole which is in right harmony with the wisdom of that very being from which they derive their ground. I find it indeed wonderful that, in so far as anything happens, or would happen, in the course of nature according to universal laws, it should displease God and need a miracle for its correction. And if such a miracle does happen, then even the occasion for it belongs among those things which occur now and again, but which we can never comprehend.",["'anders' (etwas anders möglich) is probably a slip for 'anderes' (something else); rendered 'something else [anders]'.","Long final sentence divided in two; the stray ')' after 'sich' is an OCR remnant and is dropped."]],"beweisgrund-0174":["It will also be understood without difficulty that, if one perceives the essential ground why miracles can seldom be necessary to the perfection of the world, this holds also of those events which in the preceding consideration we entitled supernatural events in the formal sense, and which in ordinary judgments are admitted very frequently, because through a perverse concept one believes one finds something natural in them.",["'im formalen Verstande' rendered 'in the formal sense'."]],"beweisgrund-0175":["2.",[]],"beweisgrund-0176":["What can be inferred from our argument as to the superiority of one or",["Heading split across two passages; the sentence is completed in the next id."]],"beweisgrund-0177":["the other order of nature.",[]],"beweisgrund-0178":["In the procedure of purified philosophy [Weltweisheit] there prevails a rule which, though not formally stated, is nevertheless always observed in practice. It is that in every search for the causes of given effects great care must be taken to preserve the unity of nature as far as possible; that is, to derive many kinds of effects from a single ground already known, and not, on account of some apparent greater dissimilarity, to assume forthwith new and different efficient causes for the different effects. One accordingly presumes that there is great unity in nature in respect of the sufficiency of a single ground for many kinds of consequences, and believes oneself to have reason for regarding the union of one kind of appearances with those of another kind, for the most part, as something necessary, and not as the effect of an artificial and contingent order. How many different effects are not derived from the single force of gravity, for which different causes were formerly thought to be needed: the rising of some bodies and the falling of others! The vortices, which were to keep the heavenly bodies in their orbits, have been abolished as soon as the cause of this was found in that simple force of nature. One presumes, with good reason, that the expansion of bodies by heat, light, electrical force, thunderstorms, and perhaps also magnetic force, are many different appearances of one and the same active matter spread through all spaces, namely the aether; and one is in general dissatisfied if one sees oneself compelled to assume a new principle for a certain kind of effects. Even where,",["Long opening sentence restated as several; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances.","A stray quotation mark before 'Ursache' (probably a footnote sign) is dropped; 'Athers' read as 'Äthers'.","The passage breaks off at 'Selbst da,'; the next passage begins without the expected 'wo', and I have not supplied it."]],"beweisgrund-0179":["a very exact symmetry seems to demand a special artificial arrangement, one is inclined to ascribe it to the necessary outcome of more general laws, and still to observe the rule of unity, before one lays an artificial disposition at the foundation. The figures of snow are so regular, and so far surpass in elegance all the crudity which blind chance can produce, that one should almost distrust the honesty of those who have given us drawings of them, were it not that every winter gives countless occasions for everyone to assure himself of them by his own experience. One will find few",["Begins mid-clause (the 'wo' is missing in the source text as given); I leave this unsupplied. 'gez geben' read as 'gegeben'.","Breaks off at 'Man wird wenig'; continued in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0180":["The Only Possible Ground of Proof for a Demonstration of the Existence of God.",["\"Demonitration\" in the OCR is silently corrected to \"Demonstration\" (Demonstration); the title is rendered in the form of Kant's full title, *Der einzig mögliche Beweisgrund zu einer Demonstration des Daseins Gottes*, but the passage as given reads only \"Beweisgrund zu einer Demonstration des Daseins Gottes\" (this is a running or section title, probably the heading repeated at the start of the text).","Because the passage as given lacks \"Der einzig mögliche\", the translation supplies \"The Only Possible\" from Kant's full title; if the heading is to be rendered strictly as it stands, read: \"Ground of Proof for a Demonstration of the Existence of God.\""]],"beweisgrund-0181":["flowers which, so far as one can perceive externally, show more neatness and proportion, and one sees nothing that art can produce which would contain more exactness than these generations which nature scatters with such prodigality over the surface of the earth. And yet no one has allowed it to enter his mind to derive them from a special seed of snow, and to devise an artificial order of nature; on the contrary, one ascribes them, as a by-product, to more general laws, which comprehend under themselves, with necessary unity, the formation of this product at the same time.",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage ('One will find few flowers ...'). The closing '“)' of the source is probably a footnote sign and is not rendered."]],"beweisgrund-0182":["Nevertheless nature is rich in a certain other kind of productions, in which all philosophy that reflects upon their mode of origin finds itself compelled to abandon this path. Great art, and a contingent union through free choice in accordance with certain purposes, is there evident, and becomes at the same time the ground of a special law of nature, which belongs to the artificial order of nature. The structure of plants and animals shows such a design, for which universal and necessary laws of nature are insufficient. Now since it would be absurd to regard the first generation of a plant or animal as a mechanical by-product of universal laws of nature, there still remains a twofold question, which on the ground stated is undecided. The first alternative is that every individual of these is built immediately by God and is therefore of supernatural origin, and that only propagation, that is, the passage from time to time to development, is entrusted to a natural law. The second is that some individuals of the vegetable and animal kingdoms are indeed of immediately divine origin, yet with a capacity, incomprehensible to us, of producing their like, and not merely of developing them, according to an ordinary law of nature. On both sides difficulties present themselves. It is perhaps impossible to determine which is the greater; but what concerns us here is only to note the preponderance of the grounds, in so far as they are metaphysical. How, for instance, a tree should be capable, through an inner mechanical constitution, of so forming and moulding the nutritive sap,",["Several long sentences divided; 'künstliche Naturordnung' rendered 'artificial order of nature'; 'Auswickelung' = development (unfolding).","The stray ')' after 'sich' is a footnote sign, here omitted; the footnote follows in the next id.","The sentence breaks off at 'zu modeln,' and is completed in the next passage, after the footnote text."]],"beweisgrund-0183":["*) The figure of mould, which resembles that of plants, had led many to count it among the products of the vegetable kingdom. According to other observations, however, it is much more probable that its apparent regularity cannot prevent us from regarding it, like the tree of Diana, as a consequence of the common laws of sublimation.\n\n[Main text resumed:] that in the eye of the leaves, or in its seed, something should arise which contained a similar tree in miniature, or from which such a tree could at least come to be, is by all our knowledge in no way to be understood. The inner forms [innerliche Formen] of Monsieur de Buffon, and the elements of organic matter which, in consequence of their memories and according to the laws of desire and aversion, combine, in the opinion of Monsieur de Maupertuis, are either just as unintelligible as the thing itself, or are devised quite arbitrarily. But, without troubling ourselves about such theories, must we on that account propound another which is just as arbitrary, namely that all these individuals are of supernatural origin, because we do not at all comprehend their natural mode of origin? Has anyone ever made the capacity of yeast to produce its like mechanically comprehensible? And yet in this case no appeal is made to a supernatural ground.",["The passage opens with footnote text (on mould, 'Schimmel') whose reference sign appears as ')'; I have prefixed '*)' as a footnote marker. After the first sentence the main text resumes mid-sentence ('daß in dem Auge der Blätter ...'), completing the preceding passage's 'Wie z. E. ein Baum ... soll vermögend sein ... zu modeln'; I have marked this in brackets.","'Baum der Diane' = the arbor Dianae (silver tree); 'Sublimirung' = sublimation; 'Hefens' read as 'Hefe' (yeast); 'änscheinende' read as 'anscheinende'; stray 's' before 'organischer' dropped.","'Kenntnissen' rendered 'knowledge'; 'Erinnerungen' as 'memories'."]],"beweisgrund-0184":["Since in this case the origin of all such organic products is regarded as wholly supernatural, people nevertheless believe that they are leaving something over for the natural philosopher, if they allow him to play with the manner of gradual propagation. But let it be well considered that in this way the supernatural is not diminished. For whether this supernatural generation takes place at the time of creation or little by little at different times, in the latter case there is no more of the supernatural than in the former; the whole difference comes down not to the degree of immediate divine action, but merely to the *when*. As for that natural order of evolution (*Auswickelung*), it is not a rule of the fruitfulness of nature but a method of useless circumlocution. For not the least degree of immediate divine action is saved by it. It therefore seems unavoidable either to ascribe the formation of the fruit at every act of generation immediately to a divine action, or to grant to the first divine ordering of plants and animals a capacity, in accordance with a natural law, not merely to develop their like in the sequel but truly to produce it.",["The sentence beginning 'Da in diesem Falle' is kept as one; 'alimählig' (OCR 'alimáblig') silently corrected to 'allmählich'; stray 'o' after 'daß' dropped as an OCR slip.","*Auswickelung* is the preformationist 'evolution' (unfolding), given in the German because the sense turns on it.","'das Wenn' rendered 'the *when*' to keep the point that only timing differs."]],"beweisgrund-0185":["My present purpose is only to show by this that a greater possibility of producing their consequences in accordance with universal laws must be conceded to natural things than is commonly done.",[]],"beweisgrund-0186":["Fifth Consideration,",["Heading; 'Betrachtung' rendered 'Consideration'."]],"beweisgrund-0187":["In which the insufficiency of the usual method of",["Heading, continued in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0188":["physico-theology is shown.\nOn physico-theology in general.",["'Physikotheologie' rendered 'physico-theology'."]],"beweisgrund-0189":["All ways of knowing the existence of God from its effects can be reduced to the following three. Either one arrives at this knowledge through the perception of that which interrupts the order of nature and immediately indicates the power to which nature is subject; this conviction is occasioned by miracles. Or the contingent order of nature, which one clearly sees to have been possible in many other ways, and in which nevertheless great art, power and goodness shine forth, leads to the divine author. Or, thirdly, the necessary unity perceived in nature, and the essential order of things, which accords with great rules of perfection, in short that which in the regularity of nature is necessary, leads to a supreme *principium* not merely of this existence but of all possibility itself.",["Long sentence divided at the semicolon and the 'oder' clauses for clarity.","OCR 'Ord— nung', 'gleic)wohl' silently corrected.","'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'; *Principium* kept in Latin."]],"beweisgrund-0190":["When men have grown wholly savage, or a stubborn malice closes their eyes, the first means alone seems to have some power to convince them of the existence of the supreme Being. On the other hand, the right reflection of a well-disposed soul finds in so much contingent beauty and purposive connection as the order of nature offers proofs enough to infer from it a will accompanied by great wisdom and power; and for this conviction, so far as it is to be sufficient for virtuous conduct, that is, morally certain, the common concepts of the understanding are adequate. For the third way of inference philosophy (*Weltweisheit*) is necessarily required, and only a higher degree of it is capable of reaching the same object with a clarity and conviction befitting the greatness of the truth.",["'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy', with the German given.","OCR 'gez langen' corrected to 'gelangen'."]],"beweisgrund-0191":["The two latter ways may be entitled physico-theological methods; for both show the path by which one may ascend from reflections on nature to the knowledge of God.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'knowledge'."]],"beweisgrund-0192":["9",["Section number, translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0193":["The advantages and also the defects of the usual",["Heading, continued in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0194":["physico-theology.",[]],"beweisgrund-0195":["The chief characteristic of the physico-theological method hitherto in use is this: the perfection and regularity are first duly comprehended as to their contingency, and the artificial order is then shown in them in all its purposive relations, in order to infer from it a wise and benevolent will. Afterwards, through the added consideration of the magnitude of the work, the concept of the immeasurable power of the author is united with this.",["Sentence divided in two; 'nadhher' read as 'nachher', 'ver— einigt' as 'vereinigt'. The final stray '.' omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0196":["This method is excellent: first, because the conviction is extremely sensible [*sinnlich*] and therefore very lively and engaging, and accordingly easy and comprehensible even to the commonest understanding; secondly, because it is more natural than any other, since without doubt everyone begins with it; thirdly, because it provides a very intuitive concept of the high wisdom, providence, or also power of the adorable Being, a concept which fills the soul and has the greatest force to produce astonishment, humility and reverence.*) This kind of proof is much more practical than any other, even with regard to the philosopher. For although for his inquiring or brooding understanding he does not here find the determinate abstract idea of the Godhead, and the cer-",["Passage breaks off mid-word and mid-sentence ('Gewiß⸗'); continued in the next passage. The footnote marker is retained as '*)'.","'anschauend' rendered 'intuitive'; 'sinnlich' rendered 'sensible' (relating to sensibility).","OCR 'überex aus' read as 'überaus'."]],"beweisgrund-0197":["*) When, among other things, I consider the microscopic observations of Doctor Hill, which are found in the *Hamburgisches Magazin*, and see numerous kinds of animals in a single drop of water, predatory species equipped with instruments of destruction, which are themselves destroyed by still mightier tyrants of this watery world while they are intent on pursuing others; when I look upon the intrigues, the violence and the scene of tumult in a drop of matter, and from there raise my eyes on high to see the immeasurable space of worlds swarming like specks of dust, no human language can express the feeling which such a thought arouses, and all subtle metaphysical analysis falls far short of the sublimity and dignity proper to such an intuition.\n\n[the text of the main passage resumes:] tainty itself is not mathematical but moral, yet so many proofs, each of such great impression, take possession of his soul, and speculation follows calmly, with a certain confidence, a conviction which has already taken its place. Hardly anyone would stake his whole happiness on the presumed correctness of a metaphysical proof, especially if lively sensible persuasions stood against it. But the force of the conviction which grows from this, precisely because it is so sensible, is also so settled and unshakeable that it fears no danger from syllogisms and distinctions, and sets itself far above the power of subtle objections. Nevertheless this method has its defects, which are considerable enough, although they are properly to be attributed only to the procedure of those who have made use of it.",["The OCR interleaves the footnote with the continuation of the main text ('heit selbst nicht mathematisch…'); I separated them and marked the join in brackets, continuing the sentence from the previous passage ('Gewiß⸗' + 'heit' = 'Gewißheit', 'certainty').","'Hamb. Magaz.' expanded to *Hamburgisches Magazin* as the evident reference; 'gefließen' read as 'geflissen'; 'Schlußreden' rendered 'syllogisms'.","'Anschauung' rendered 'intuition'; 'Überzeugung' rendered 'conviction'; 'Überredungen' rendered 'persuasions'."]],"beweisgrund-0198":["1. It regards all perfection, harmony and beauty of nature as contingent, and as an arrangement by wisdom, although many of them flow with necessary unity from the most essential rules of nature. What is most detrimental here to the aim of physico-theology is that it regards this contingency of natural perfection as highly necessary for the proof of a wise author; hence, on this presupposition, all the necessary harmonies [*Wohlgereimtheiten*] of the things of the world become dangerous objections.",["Second sentence kept whole with 'hence' resolving 'daher'; 'Wohlgereimtheiten' rendered 'harmonies' (literally 'well-rhymed agreements')."]],"beweisgrund-0199":["To be convinced of this fault, one need only attend to what follows. One sees how the authors, following this method, have exerted themselves to wrest the products of the vegetable and animal kingdoms, so rich in countless purposes, away not merely from the power of chance but also from mechanical necessity according to universal laws of material nature. In this they can have not the least difficulty, for the preponderance of the grounds on their side is far too decisive. But when they turn from organic nature to the inorganic, they still persist in the very same method, and yet they find themselves there, almost invariably, entangled by the altered nature of the matter in difficulties which they cannot avoid. They still speak of the arrangement, made with great wisdom, of so many useful properties of the atmosphere, of the clouds, the rain, the winds, the twilight, and so on, as if the property by which the air is fitted to produce winds had been united, by a wise choice, with that by which it draws up vapours, or by which it grows thinner at great heights, just as in a spider the various eyes with which it lies in wait for its prey are combined in one animal with the spinnerets from which the spider's silk is drawn as through draw-holes, and with the fine claws, or the pads of its feet, by which it glues the silk together or holds fast to it. In this latter case the unity among all the combined utilities (in which perfection consists) is plainly contingent, and is to be ascribed to a wise arbitrary will [*Willkür*]; in the former case, on the contrary, it is necessary, and if only one of the aforesaid aptitudes is attributed to the air, the other cannot possibly be separated from it. Precisely because no other way of judging the perfection of nature is admitted than by the contrivance of wisdom, every widely extended unity, so far as it is plainly recognised as necessary, will constitute a dangerous objection. We shall soon see that, according to our method, the divine wisdom is nevertheless inferred from such a unity; not, however, as derived from a wise choice as its cause, but from a ground in a supreme being which must at the same time be a ground of great wisdom in him. It is thus inferred from a being that is wise, but not through his wisdom.",["The opening 'Um sich)' is an OCR slip for 'Um sich'; 'be— harren', 'noth— wendig' restored; 'Rauhe' read as 'Raube' (prey); 'sie s zusammenklebt' read as 'sie zusammenklebt'.","Divided no sentence, but kept the long comparison in one sentence; the 'als in welcher die Vollkommenheit besteht' parenthesis is kept.","'Willkür' rendered 'arbitrary will'; 'Ziehlöcher' = draw-holes (wire-drawing plates); 'Spinnenseide' rendered 'spider's silk'.","'welcher ... ein Grund einer großen Weisheit in ihm sein muß': the antecedent of 'welcher' is 'Grunde', so the ground is in the supreme being."]],"beweisgrund-0200":["2. This method is not sufficiently philosophical, and has also often greatly hindered the extension of philosophical knowledge. As soon as a natural arrangement is useful, it is commonly explained directly from the purpose of the divine will, or at least by an order of nature specially contrived by art. This is either because men have once taken it into their heads that the effects of nature, according to its most universal laws, could not issue in such harmony, or because they hold that, if it were conceded that these laws had such consequences, this would be to ascribe the perfection of the world to blind chance, whereby the divine Author would be greatly misjudged. Hence there are",["Breaks off mid-sentence ('Daher werden'); translated as it stands, the continuation being in the next passage ('...einem solchen Falle der Naturforschung Grenzen gesetzt').","'Naturanstalt' rendered 'natural arrangement'; 'Ungefähr' rendered 'chance'. The 'oder wenn man einräumte' clause is irregular after 'entweder weil'; I smoothed it by reading both as reasons.","'Wohlgereimtheit' rendered 'harmony' (literally, well-rhymed agreement)."]],"beweisgrund-0201":["[Hence there are] set limits to natural inquiry in such a case. Reason, abased, readily desists from further investigation, because it here regards such investigation as presumptuous curiosity. The prejudice is the more dangerous because it gives the indolent a pre-eminence over the untiring investigator, under the pretext of devotion and of fitting submission to the great Author, in whose knowledge all wisdom must be united. One recounts, for example, the uses of mountains, of which there are countless many; and as soon as one has brought together a good number of them, among them such as the human race cannot do without, one believes one has reason to regard mountains as an immediate divine arrangement. For to regard them as a consequence of universal laws of motion (since one by no means supposes that these laws could have any relation to beautiful and useful consequences, unless it were perhaps by chance) would, in their opinion, be to let an essential advantage of mankind depend on blind accident. The consideration of the rivers of the earth is of the same kind. If one listens to the physico-theological authors, one is brought to imagine that their channels were all hollowed out by God. Nor is it philosophising when one regards each single mountain or each single stream as a particular purpose of God, which would not have been attained by universal laws, and then, I say, devises the means which God may perhaps have employed, by a special provision, to bring about these individual effects. For, according to what was shown in the third Consideration of this Division, such a product is always, to that extent, supernatural. Indeed, since it cannot be explained according to an order of nature (as it arose only as a single event through special arrangements), such a way of judging rests on a perverted representation of the excellence of nature in itself, even if nature had to be forced into a single case; and that, so far as we can see, can be regarded as a means of circuitous detour and not as a procedure of wisdom.¹) When Newton had convinced himself by infallible proofs that the body of the earth has that figure in which all the directions of gravity, altered by the centrifugal force of rotation, stand perpendicular, he concluded that the earth was originally fluid, and by the laws of statics, by means of rotation, assumed precisely this shape. [The sentence continues in the footnote's interposed text; see notes.]",["Begins mid-sentence, continuing 'Daher werden' of the previous passage; I supply '[Hence there are]' in brackets only to mark the join, and the word 'Grenzen' is the object of 'gesetzt'.","'Flows' of the OCR: 'sich)' and 'gründet sich)' have stray parentheses (the former a footnote mark or OCR slip), removed; the footnote mark after 'angesehen werden.“)' is rendered as ¹) and refers to the footnote printed in the next passage.","In the German the passage breaks off at 'nach den' and the sentence continues in the next passage ('Gesetzen der Statik...'), because the footnote text intervenes in the OCR. I have joined the continuation in the main text for readability, though it belongs to the following passage; the reader should treat 'and by the laws of statics...' as repeated there.","'Vorwitz' rendered 'presumptuous curiosity'; 'Anstalt' rendered 'arrangement'; 'Umschweif' rendered 'circuitous detour'; 'Betrachtung' of this Division = Consideration."]],"beweisgrund-0202":["¹) It were to be wished that in such cases, where revelation reports that a worldly event is an extraordinary divine relation, the presumptuous curiosity of philosophers in parading their physical insights might be moderated; for they do religion no service at all, and only make it doubtful whether the event was not after all a natural accident, as in the case in which the destruction of the army under Sennacherib is ascribed to the wind Samiel. Philosophy commonly gets into difficulties in this connection, as in Whiston's theory, which uses astronomical knowledge of comets to explain the Bible.\n\n[The text of the main passage resumes:] ...Laws of statics by means of rotation assumed precisely this shape. He knew as well as anyone the advantages that lie in the spherical roundness of a heavenly body, and also the highly necessary flattening, to forestall the harmful consequences of rotation on an axis. These are all arrangements worthy of a wise Author. Nevertheless he had no hesitation in ascribing them, as an effect, to the most necessary mechanical laws, and was not concerned that in doing so he would lose sight of the great Ruler of all things.",["The OCR interleaves the footnote with the main text: the footnote (from 'Es wäre zu wünschen' to 'Bibelerklärung zu gebrauchen') is translated first, and the main text resumes with 'Gesetzen der Statik...', which completes Newton's sentence begun in the preceding passage ('nach den | Gesetzen der Statik').","'Samyel' = Samiel (the hot desert wind); 'Whistonschen Theorie' = Whiston's theory. 'Vorwitz' rendered 'presumptuous curiosity'.","'Abplattung' rendered 'flattening' (of the poles); 'Drehungsschwung'/'Achsendrehung' rendered by 'rotation'.","My bracketed '[The text of the main passage resumes:]' is an editorial signpost, not in the German."]],"beweisgrund-0203":["It is therefore safe to presume that [Newton] would never have taken refuge directly in a divine arrangement [*Anstalt*], in regard to the structure of the planets, their revolutions and the position of their orbits, had he not judged a mechanical origin to be impossible here. He did not judge so because such an origin is insufficient for regularity and order in general (for why did he not fear this unsuitability in the case previously mentioned?). He judged so because the celestial spaces are empty, and in this condition no community of the planets' effects upon one another, such as could fix their orbits, is possible. Suppose, however, that it had occurred to him to ask whether these spaces had always been empty. Suppose he had asked whether, at least in the very first state, when these spaces were perhaps filled continuously, that effect might have been possible whose consequences have been preserved ever since. And suppose he had had a well-grounded conjecture about this most ancient condition. Then we may be assured that he would have sought, in a manner befitting philosophy, the grounds of the constitution of the world-structure in the universal mechanical laws. He would not have been anxious on that account that such an explanation delivers the origin of the world out of the hands of the Creator into the power of chance. Newton's famous example must therefore not serve as a pretext for slothful confidence, so that a hasty appeal to an immediate divine arrangement may be passed off as an explanation in the philosophical manner.",["OCR slips silently corrected: *Mnsehung* = *Ansehung*, *ge— nommen*, *mdglich*, *jeder— zeit*, *augzugeben* = *auszugeben*; the stray parenthesis in \"auch)\" is dropped.","\"[Newton]\" is supplied: the German has only \"er\", and Newton is named in the last sentence.","Restated as several sentences: the long conditional sentence beginning \"Wenn es ihm indessen beigefallen wäre\" is divided, and the \"wenn\" clauses are made explicit suppositions.","*Anstalt* is rendered \"arrangement\"; *Geschmacke* in \"in philosophischem Geschmacke\" as \"manner\"."]],"beweisgrund-0204":["In general, to be sure, countless dispositions of nature, since they are still contingent according to the most general laws, have no other ground than the wise purpose of him who willed that they should be connected in this way and not otherwise. But we cannot conversely infer that wherever a natural connection accords with what is in keeping with a wise choice, it is also contingent according to the universal laws of nature's operation, and has been fixed in an extraordinary manner by artificial contrivance. In this way of thinking it may often happen that the ends of the laws which one imagines are incorrect. One then suffers, besides this error, the further harm of having passed over the efficient causes and of having held directly to a purpose that is merely invented. Süßmilch once supposed that he had found the ground why more boys than girls are born in this purpose of providence: that the greater number of the male sex might make good the loss which this sex suffers, more than the other, through war and the more dangerous kinds of occupation. But later observations taught this same careful and rational man that this surplus of boys is so far removed by death in the years of childhood that a smaller number of the male than of the female sex reaches the years in which the causes mentioned above can first give rise to a loss. We have reason to believe that this remarkable fact may be a case falling under a much more general rule. The stronger part of the human species has also a greater share in the generative activity, so as to make its own kind predominate in the products of both sides. On the other hand, more is required if something which has the basis of a greater perfection is also to meet, in its development, all the circumstances belonging to the attainment of it. Hence a greater number of those of a less perfect kind will reach the degree of completeness than of those whose completeness requires a greater concurrence of grounds. But whatever the case may be with this rule, we can at least make the remark that it hinders the extension of philosophical insight to turn to moral grounds, that is, to explanation from ends, where it is still to be presumed that physical grounds determine the consequence through a connection with necessary, more general laws.",["OCR slips corrected: *Abfit* = *Absicht*, *größe: ren*, *Zeugungsthätigfeit*, *Süß milch* = Süßmilch.","\"die wirkende Ursachen vorbeigegangen ist\" is ungrammatical in the German (the plural noun with a singular verb); I render \"has passed over the efficient causes\" (*causae efficientes*).","The final long sentences are divided in English, and the antecedents of \"derselben\" and \"deren\" are settled by the argument (the greater perfection; the less perfect kind).","*Zwecke* = ends; *moralische Gründe* = moral grounds, which Kant glosses as \"explanation from ends\"."]],"beweisgrund-0205":["3. This method can serve only to prove an author of the connections and artificial combinations of the world. It cannot prove an author of matter itself, or of the origin of the constituent parts of the universe. This considerable defect must leave all who employ the method alone in danger of that error which is called the subtler atheism. According to this error, God is regarded, in the strict sense, as an architect [*Werkmeister*] and not as a creator of the world; he has indeed ordered and formed matter, but has not produced or created it. Since I shall consider this inadequacy in the next consideration, I am content here merely to have noted it.",["OCR slips corrected: *Betrach— iung* = *Betrachtung*, *an— dem* = *andern*.","\"und den Ursprung\" is left as in the German; the construction (\"der Materie selbst und den Ursprung\") is irregular, and I render the sense with a repeated \"of\".","*im eigentlichen Verstande* = \"in the strict sense\"; *Betrachtung* = \"consideration\"."]],"beweisgrund-0206":["For the rest, the method in question remains always one of those most in keeping both with the dignity and with the weakness of the human understanding. There are in fact countless dispositions in nature whose proximate ground must be a final purpose of their author. The easiest way that leads to him is to consider those arrangements which are immediately subordinate to his wisdom. It is therefore only fair to direct our efforts rather to supplementing the method than to attacking it, and to correcting its faults rather than holding it in contempt on that account. The following consideration is to be occupied with this aim.",["OCR slips corrected: *er— gänzen*, *ge— ringschätzig*, *Ab— sieht*.","\"Anstalten\" is rendered \"arrangements\"; \"seine Bemühungen\" (indefinite \"one's efforts\") is rendered \"our efforts\"."]],"beweisgrund-0207":["Sixth Consideration.\nImproved Method of Physico-theology.\n1.",["*Betrachtung* is rendered \"Consideration\"; *Physikotheologie* is rendered \"Physico-theology\"."]],"beweisgrund-0208":["Order and fitness [*Anständigkeit*], even when it is necessary,",["Heading continued in the next passage: \"... indicates an intelligent author.\" *Anständigkeit* is rendered \"fitness\" (propriety, suitability)."]],"beweisgrund-0209":["indicates an intelligent author.",["Continues the heading of the preceding passage."]],"beweisgrund-0210":["Nothing can be more prejudicial to the thought of a divine author of the universe, and at the same time more unreasonable, than to be ready to ascribe a great and fruitful rule of fitness, utility and harmony to blind chance. The *clinamen* of the atoms in the system of Democritus and Epicurus was of this kind. I shall not dwell upon the absurdity and deliberate blindness of this way of judging, since it has been made sufficiently evident by others. I observe instead that the necessity perceived in the relation of things to regular connections, and the coherence of useful laws with a necessary unity, furnish proof of a wise author just as much as the most contingent and arbitrary arrangement does. The dependence upon him must, however, be represented in another way from this point of view. To see this properly, I note that order and manifold advantageous accord in general indicate an intelligent author, even before we consider whether this relation is necessary or contingent to the things. According to the judgments of common sound reason, the sequence of the world's changes, or that connection in whose place another was possible, gives indeed a clear ground of proof of contingency. But it has little effect in leading the understanding to presume an author. Philosophy is required for that, and even its employment in this case is involved and slippery. Great regularity and congruity in a many-voiced harmony, on the other hand, give us pause, and common reason itself can never find them possible without an intelligent author. One rule of fitness may already lie essentially in another, or may be arbitrarily combined with it. Either way we find it downright impossible that order and regularity should come about by chance, or should of themselves occur among many things which have their separate existence. For extended harmony is never sufficiently given, as regards its possibility, without an intelligent ground. And here a great difference at once appears in the way in which perfection is to be judged as to its origin.",["OCR slip corrected: *an— dem* = *andern*.","Restated as a series of shorter sentences; the long sentence beginning \"Die eine Regel der Anständigkeit\" is divided, and \"so findet man es\" is rendered \"Either way we find it\".","*Ungefähr* / *ungefährer Zufall* is rendered \"chance\" / \"blind chance\"; *Wohlgereimtheit* as \"congruity\"; *stutzig macht* as \"give us pause\".","*clinamen* is kept in Latin and italicised, as Kant gives it. *Beweisthum* = \"proof\"; *Beweisgrund* = \"ground of proof\"."]],"beweisgrund-0211":["2.",[]],"beweisgrund-0212":["Necessary order of nature itself indicates an",["Heading continued in the next passage: \"author of the matter which is so ordered.\""]],"beweisgrund-0213":["author of the matter which is so ordered.",["Continues the heading of the preceding passage."]],"beweisgrund-0214":["Order in nature, so far as it is regarded as contingent and as springing from the arbitrary choice [*Willkür*] of an intelligent being, is no proof at all that the things of nature which are connected in such an order according to wisdom themselves owe their existence to this author. For it is only the connection that is of such a character as to presuppose an intelligent plan. Hence Aristotle and many other philosophers of antiquity derived from the Deity not the matter or stuff of nature, but only its form. Perhaps it is only since the time when revelation taught us the complete dependence of the world upon God that philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] has for the first time devoted proper effort to regarding the origin of the things themselves, which constitute the raw material of nature, as something that would not be possible without an author. I doubt whether anyone has succeeded in this, and in the last Division I shall adduce the grounds of my judgment. At the very least, the contingent order of the parts of the world, so far as it indicates an origin in arbitrary choice, can contribute nothing whatever to the proof of this.\n\nFor example: in the structure of an animal the organs of sensory perception are so artfully combined with those of voluntary movement and with the vital parts that one must be malicious (for no man can be so unreasonable) to fail, as soon as one is led to the point, to acknowledge a wise author who has brought into so excellent an order the matter of which an animal body is composed. More than this does not follow. Whether this matter is in itself eternal and independent, or has likewise been produced by the same author, is not decided by it at all.\n\nThe judgment turns out quite otherwise, however, if one perceives that not all perfection in nature is artificial, but that rules of great utility are also bound up with necessary unity, and that this union lies in the possibilities of the things themselves. What is one to judge on this perception? Is this unity, this fruitful harmony, possible without dependence upon a wise author? The formal character of so great and manifold a regularity forbids it. Since, however, this unity is nevertheless itself grounded in the possibilities of things, there must be a wise being without whom all these things of nature are themselves not possible, and in whom, as in a great ground, the essences of so many natural things are united in such regular relations. But it is then clear that not merely the manner of the connection, but the things themselves are possible only through this being; that is, they can exist only as its effects. This is what first gives sufficient indication of the complete dependence of nature upon God.\n\nIf one now asks: how do these natures depend upon such a being, so that I may understand from this their agreement with the rules of wisdom? I answer: they depend upon that in this being which, in containing the ground of the possibility of things, is also the ground of its own wisdom; for the latter in general presupposes the former.¹ But in this unity of the",["Footnote marker: the OCR shows 'Dies.“)' and '1 )'; I have rendered the marker as ¹ and placed the footnote text after the passage as it appears in the source, below.","FOOTNOTE (¹), as it stands, breaking off: Wisdom presupposes that agreement and unity in relations is possible. That being which is of wholly independent nature can be wise only in so far as there are contained in it grounds of the very possible harmony and perfections that offer themselves to its execution. Were [the text breaks off here and continues in the next passage].","Restated: the long sentence 'Denn lediglich diese Verbindung ...' was kept whole, but the example sentence ('Z. E. ...') was kept close to the German; 'boshaft sein muß ... so bald man darauf geführt wird einen weisen Urheber zu verkennen' is somewhat awkward and rendered as 'to fail ... to acknowledge' (verkennen).","I split the passage into paragraphs at 'Z. E.' and 'Ganz anders' and 'Frägt man nun' for readability; the German has no breaks (OCR). 'Willkür' kept in brackets at first occurrence; 'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy'.","'Ob diese Materie für sich)' — stray parenthesis in OCR silently dropped. 'Hau' read as 'Bau' (structure). The sentence ending 'Mehr folgt hieraus gar nicht' is rendered literally.","'Wesen' rendered 'essences' in 'die Wesen so mancher Naturdinge' and 'being' elsewhere, following the sense required; 'Möglichkeiten' = possibilities."]],"beweisgrund-0215":["[…] the possibilities of things no such relation to order and perfection [were present], then wisdom would be a chimera [sic: see note]. For through this unity of the ground, both of the essence of all things and of wisdom, goodness and power, it is necessary that all possibility should harmonise with these attributes.",["This passage is a jumble caused by the OCR running the footnote text together with the main text: it begins mid-footnote ('den Möglichkeiten der Dinge keine solche Beziehung auf Ordnung und Vollkommen-' continues, in the Academy page, with 'heit befindlich, so wäre Weisheit eine Chimäre', which the OCR has displaced to 0225). I have therefore translated only what stands here, with an ellipsis in brackets at the start.","The 'wisdom would be a chimera' clause in my translation is NOT in this passage as given; it belongs to the footnote text as transmitted in 0225 and I have retained it only as the visible sequel. A cautious reader should strike it; the passage proper ends with 'Grundes sowohl des Wesens aller Dinge, als der Weisheit, Güte und Macht ist es nothwendig: daß alle Möglichkeit mit diesen Eigenschaften harmonire.'","'Bei dieser Einheit aber des' (end of 0214) joins 'Grundes sowohl des Wesens ...' here: 'But in this unity of the ground both of the essence of all things and of wisdom, goodness and power, it is necessary that all possibility harmonise with these attributes.'"]],"beweisgrund-0216":["57",["Page number, translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0217":["Rules of the improved method of Physico-theology. 5",["'5' is probably the section number (the OCR also shows '4.' in 0227), left as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0218":["I summarise them briefly as follows: guided by confidence in the fruitfulness of the general laws of nature, on account of their dependence upon the divine being, one should seek",[]],"beweisgrund-0219":["1. The cause itself of the most advantageous arrangements in such general laws as stand, with a necessary unity, besides other",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in 0220 ('re anständigen Folgen' = 'suitable consequences', the 'andre' being split across the lines)."]],"beweisgrund-0220":["suitable consequences, in relation also to the production of these effects.",["The fragment 're' is the end of 'andre' (from 0219); I have joined it as 'other suitable consequences'. The sentence is compressed in the German and the sense of its last clause is somewhat uncertain."]],"beweisgrund-0221":["2. One should note what is necessary in this connection of various capacities of fitness in a single ground, both because the way in which one infers from it to dependence upon God differs from that which has as its proper aim the artificial and chosen unity, and also in order to distinguish the outcome according to constant and necessary laws from mere chance coincidence.",["'Tauglichkeiten' rendered 'capacities of fitness'; 'ungefähren Zufall' rendered 'mere chance coincidence'. The line number '15' in the OCR is omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0222":["3. One should presume, not only in unorganised but also in organised nature, a greater necessary unity than is immediately obvious. For even in the structure of an animal it is to be presumed that a single disposition will have a fruitful fitness for many advantageous consequences, for which we might at first find many special arrangements necessary. This attention is both very consonant with philosophy and advantageous to the physico-theological inference.",["OCR 'in 9» die Augen' read as 'in die Augen'; 'ss.' is a line-number remnant and omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0223":["4. One should make use of the manifestly artificial order in order to infer from it the wisdom of an author as a ground; but of the essential and necessary unity in the laws of nature, in order to infer from it a wise being as a ground, not by means of its wisdom, but",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in 0224."]],"beweisgrund-0224":["by virtue of that in it which must harmonise with this [wisdom].",["'AS' is a line-number remnant, omitted. 'mit dieser' refers to wisdom (Weisheit), supplied in brackets."]],"beweisgrund-0225":["5. One should infer from the contingent connections of the world to the author of the manner in which the universe is put together; from the necessary unity, however, to the same being as an author even of the matter and primal stuff of all natural things.\n\n[Footnote, continued from p. 125:] ... [being] found, wisdom would be a chimera. But if this possibility were not itself grounded in the wise being, this wisdom could never be independent in every respect.",["The OCR interleaves the main text with the end of the footnote begun in 0214 ('Wäre ... heit befindlich, so wäre Weisheit eine Chimäre. Wäre aber diese Möglichkeit in dem ... weisen Wesen nicht selbst gegründet, so könnte diese Weisheit nimmermehr in aller Absicht unabhängig sein.'). I have separated them. The footnote's connection to 0215 ('den Möglichkeiten der Dinge keine solche Beziehung auf Ordnung und Vollkommen-heit befindlich') makes the full footnote: 'Were no such relation to order and perfection to be found in the possibilities of things, wisdom would be a chimera. But were this possibility not itself grounded in the wise being, this wisdom could never be independent in every respect.'","'Ri' in the OCR is a stray fragment, omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0226":["6. One should extend this method by general rules which can make intelligible the grounds of the harmony of that which is mechanically, or even geometrically, necessary with the best of the whole; and one should not neglect to consider the properties of space themselves from this point of view, and from the unity in its great manifold to elucidate the same principal concept.",["'Wohlgereimtheit' rendered 'harmony' (also 'fruitful harmony' earlier); 'Hauptbegriff' = 'principal concept'."]],"beweisgrund-0227":["4. Elucidation of these rules.",["Heading; '4.' is presumably the section number."]],"beweisgrund-0228":["I shall give some examples to make the method I have in mind more intelligible. The mountains of the earth are among the most useful arrangements [*Verfassungen*] upon it, and Burnet, who regards them as nothing better than a wild devastation, imposed as a punishment for our sin, is without doubt in the wrong. According to the usual method of physico-theology, the extensive advantages of these mountain ranges are recounted, and they are then regarded as a divine provision, made through great wisdom for the sake of so many and various useful ends. This way of judging leads us to think that general laws, without a special and artificial ordering applied to this case, would not have produced such a shape of the earth's surface; and the appeal to the almighty will enjoins upon inquiring reason a reverent silence.\n\nAccording to a better instructed way of thinking, on the other hand, the utility and beauty of this arrangement of nature furnish no ground for passing over the general and simple laws of the operation of matter, so as not to regard this arrangement as a by-product [*Nebenfolge*] of them. It might perhaps be hard to determine whether the spherical figure of the earth as a whole is not of still greater advantage and more important consequences than those unevennesses which make its surface deviate somewhat from this measured roundness. Yet no philosopher has any scruple in regarding that figure as an effect of the most general statical laws, in the very earliest epoch of the world. Why should the inequalities and projections not also belong to such natural and unartificial effects?\n\nIt seems that in every great world-body the state in which it passes gradually from fluidity to solidity must be very necessarily bound up with the generation of extensive cavities, which must be found beneath its already hardened crust, when the lightest materials of its inner, still fluid mass (air being among them) rise up beneath this crust by gradual separation. And since the extent of these cavities must stand in proportion to the size of the world-body, the sinkings of the solid vaults will be spread over just as wide an area. Even a certain kind of regularity, at least the chain-like series of these unevennesses, need not seem foreign or unexpected in such a mode of generation. For it is known that the rising of the lighter kinds in a large mixture at one place has an influence on the same movement in the neighbouring part of the mixture. I do not dwell long on this manner of explanation; nor do I intend here to show any devotion to it, but only to set forth through it a small illustration of the method of judging.",["Silently corrected OCR slips: 'ftd) unter' read as 'sich unter', 'leid) testen' as 'leichtesten', 'Einfinfungen' as 'Einsenkungen' (my reading, rendered 'sinkings'), 'fültige' as 'einfältige' (rendered 'simple').","Restated: the long sentence on cavities is kept as two sentences in English; the passage is divided into paragraphs for clarity, which the German does not do.","'Verfassung' rendered 'arrangement' (once '[*Verfassungen*]' flagged); 'Anstalt' rendered 'provision' in the physico-theological sense and 'arrangement' in the second.","'Ergebenheit ... zu bezeigen' is rendered 'show any devotion to it', i.e. no commitment to the hypothesis; the sense is Kant's disclaimer, not a doubtful reading.","'Weltkörper' rendered 'world-body' (heavenly body); 'statische Gesetze' kept literally as 'statical laws'."]],"beweisgrund-0229":["The whole solid land of the earth is traversed in a very advantageous manner by the channels of the rivers, as by furrows. But there are on all the solid land so many unevennesses, valleys and flat regions that at first sight it seems necessary that the channels in which its waters run must have been specially constructed and ordered. Otherwise, given the irregularity of all the rest of the ground, the waters running down from the heights would have to wander far and wide, flood many plains, form lakes in the valleys, and make the country wild and unusable rather than beautiful and well ordered. Who will not here perceive a strong appearance of a necessary extraordinary provision? Yet all natural inquiry into the cause of rivers would be brought to an end by an assumed supernatural ordering.\n\nI, on the other hand, do not let this kind of regularity mislead me, and do not at once look for its cause outside the sphere of general mechanical laws; I therefore follow observation, in order to infer from it something about the mode in which these rivers were generated. I perceive that many river-beds are still forming up to the present time, and that they raise their own banks until these no longer flood the surrounding country as much as formerly. I become certain that all rivers in olden times really did wander as widely as we feared they would have to do without an extraordinary provision; and I infer from this that no such extraordinary arrangement ever took place. The Amazon, over a stretch of several hundred miles, shows clear traces that it formerly had no confined bed but must have flooded the land far and wide; for the soil on both sides, to great distances, is flat as a lake and consists of river-mud, in which a pebble is as rare as a diamond. The same is found with the Mississippi. And in general the Nile and other rivers show that these channels have in the course of time been much lengthened; and where the river seemed to have its outlet, because near the sea it spread out over the flat ground, it gradually builds out its channel and flows farther in a lengthened bed.\n\nThen, having been set on the track by experience, I believe I can reduce the whole mechanism of the formation of the channels of all rivers to the following simple grounds. The spring- or rain-water running down from the heights poured at first irregularly down the slope of the ground, filled many valleys, and spread over many flat regions. But in that tract where the current of the water was at any point swiftest, the water could not, because of its speed, deposit its mud so readily, whereas it let it fall far more abundantly on both sides. Thereby the banks were raised, while the strongest current of the water obtained its channel. In time, when the inflow of water itself became smaller (which in the course of time had finally to happen, from causes known to those versed in the history of the earth), the river no longer overstepped the banks which it had itself built up, and out of wild disorder regularity and order arose. One sees plainly that this goes on even to the present time, chiefly at the mouths of rivers, which are their youngest parts. And just as, on this plan, the deposit of mud had to be more abundant near the places where the river at first overstepped its new banks than farther from them, so one also perceives that in many places where a river runs through flat regions its bed actually lies higher than the surrounding plains.",["Silently corrected OCR slips (e.g. 'auch)' as 'auch', 'ftd)' as 'sich', 'gewiß.' as 'gewiß', 'Nr—sachen' as 'Ursachen', 'ab—setzen' joined); hyphenation breaks removed.","Restated: the long sentence on the river's lengthening and the 'Alsdann aber ...' sentence are kept separate, and paragraphs added; Kant's own text runs on continuously.","'Weilen' read as 'Meilen' (miles); the OCR 'W' is likely a slip, and I translate 'miles'.","'Anstalt' / 'Veranstaltung' / 'Einrichtung' rendered 'provision' / 'provision' / 'arrangement' to mark the physico-theological sense of design.","'Ich werde gewiß' rendered 'I become certain'; the original may have 'gewiß' as predicate, a sense not in doubt."]],"beweisgrund-0230":["There are certain general rules according to which the effects of nature take place, and which can throw some light on the relation of mechanical laws to order and harmony. One of them is this: the forces of motion and of resistance act upon one another until they offer each other the least hindrance. The grounds of this law are very easily seen; but the relation which its consequence bears to regularity and advantage is extensive and great to the point of wonder.\n\nThe epicycloid, an algebraic curve, is of such a nature that teeth and gearing rounded according to it suffer the least possible friction against one another. The celebrated Professor Kästner mentions in one place that he was shown by an experienced mining expert, on machines long in use, that this figure is actually produced in the end by long motion wearing away [the parts]. It is a figure which rests upon a fairly complicated construction, and which with all its regularity is a consequence of a common law of nature.",["Silently corrected OCR slips: 'as Gesetze' read as 'Gesetze' (stray 'as' dropped), 'SEE dieses Gesetzes' read as 'Gründe dieses Gesetzes' (conjectural, rendered 'grounds'), 'Cpicyfloide' as 'Epicykloide'.","'Gründe' is a conjectural restoration of a garbled OCR word ('SEE'); doubtful.","Restated: divided into two paragraphs; the final sentence is kept but with an inserted '[the parts]' to complete 'abschleife' (sich abschleifen, 'wear away'), a supplied object.","'Epicycloid' is described by Kant as 'algebraische Krümmung', rendered 'algebraic curve'; Kästner is Abraham Gotthelf Kästner."]],"beweisgrund-0231":["To cite, from among the cruder effects of nature, something which, because it stands under the law just mentioned, shows of itself a tendency towards regularity, I shall take one of the effects of rivers. In view of the great differences in the slope of the various regions of the solid land, it is much to be expected that the streams running down these slopes would here and there have steep falls and cataracts. Some such do in fact occur, though rarely, and they involve great irregularity and inconvenience. But it is easy to see that even if (as may be supposed) such cataracts were frequent in the first, unreclaimed condition, the force of the fall would nevertheless have dug into and washed away the loose soil, and even some kinds of rock not yet sufficiently hardened, until the stream had lowered its channel to a fairly uniform gradient. Hence, where cataracts still exist, the ground is rocky, and in very many regions the stream runs between two steeply cut banks, between which it has presumably itself cut its deep-lying bed. It is found very useful that almost all streams, throughout the greater part of their course, do not exceed a certain degree of speed, which is fairly moderate and by which they are navigable. Now at the outset this could hardly have been expected, without special art, from the very different slope of the ground over which they run; yet it is easy to see that in the course of time a certain degree of rapidity must have established itself of its own accord, which they cannot easily exceed, however steep the ground of the country may be, provided only that it is loose. For they will go on washing it away, working their way into it, and lowering their bed in some places and raising it in others, until what they carry off from the ground when swollen is fairly equal to what they let fall in times of more sluggish movement. The force here acts until it has brought itself to a more moderate degree, and until the reciprocal action [*Wechselwirkung*] of impact and resistance has settled into equality.",["Silently corrected OCR: 'stelle' to 'steile', 'ste' to 'sie', 'die s Wechselwirkung' to 'die Wechselwirkung', 'Abschusses' read as the slope of the ground, 'gleichförmichten' modernised.","Restated the long sentence beginning 'Es ist wegen...' and the one beginning 'Allein es fällt...' as several shorter sentences.","'Ausschlag auf Regelmäßigkeit' rendered 'tendency towards regularity' (the sense is that the balance tips towards regularity).","'Gewalt' rendered 'force'; 'Wechselwirkung' glossed with the German since it is a technical term (reciprocal action)."]],"beweisgrund-0232":["Nature offers innumerable examples of the extended usefulness of one and the same thing for manifold employments. It is quite perverse to regard these advantages forthwith as ends, and as those results which contained the motives [*Bewegungsgründe*] for which their causes were ordained in the world by divine choice [*Willkür*]. Among other advantages the moon provides this, that ebb and flood set ships in motion, even against the wind or without it, by means of the currents in the straits and near the mainland. By means of the moon and the satellites of Jupiter the longitude at sea is found. The products of all the realms of nature each have great utility, some of which men also turn to use. It is an absurd way of judging, when, as commonly happens, all these are reckoned among the motives of the divine choice, and when, on account of the advantage of Jupiter's moons, appeal is made to the wise arrangement of the Author, who thereby meant to put into men's hands a means of determining the longitude of places. Let us beware of justly drawing upon ourselves the mockery of a Voltaire, who says in a [?] tone: Behold why we have noses; doubtless in order that we might set spectacles upon them. Divine choice does not yet supply a sufficient ground why the very same means, which alone would be necessary to attain one end, should be advantageous also in so many other respects. That admirable community which prevails among the beings of all created things, so that their natures are not foreign to one another but, connected in manifold harmony, are of themselves suited to one another and contain in their essences an extended necessary agreement toward collective perfection, is the ground of such manifold utilities. According to our method these may be regarded as proofs of a supremely wise Author, but not in all cases as arrangements which have been joined to the rest, by special wisdom, for the sake of particular subsidiary advantages. Without doubt the motives for which Saturn [?] should have moons are complete, even if the moons were never used for the measurement of longitude through the invention of the telescope. These benefits, which are to be regarded as by-products, nevertheless count for something in enabling us to infer from them the immeasurable greatness of the Author of all things. For, together with millions of others of the same kind, they are proofs of the great chain which unites, even in the possibilities of things, the parts of creation that seem to have nothing to do with one another. For otherwise one cannot always reckon the benefits which follow from the result of a free arrangement, and which the Author knows and includes in his decree, among the motives of such a choice, namely when these motives were already complete irrespective of such by-products. Without doubt water has not the nature of setting itself level in order that one may be able to see one's reflection in it. If we wish to judge with reason, such observed utilities cannot be used at all, by the limited physico-theological method now in use, for the purpose we have here in view. Only the supplement which we have sought to give to it can make such collected observations fit to serve as grounds for the important inference to the universal subordination of all things under a supremely wise Being. Extend your aims as far as you can over the immeasurable benefits which a creature offers in a thousandfold relation, at least in respect of possibility (the coconut palm alone provides the Indian with countless such benefits); connect in such relations the most remote members of creation with one another. When you have duly admired the products of immediately artificial arrangements, do not neglect, in the pleasing sight of the fruitful relation which the possibilities of created things have to thoroughgoing harmony, and of the unforced sequence of such manifold beauty, which presents itself of its own accord, to admire and adore that power in whose eternal primal source the essences of things lie, as it were, ready for an excellent plan.",["Silently corrected OCR: 'Nukbarkeit' to 'Nutzbarkeit', 'Sehrohre' to 'Sehröhre', 'mochten' to 'möchten', 'auge-ordnet' to 'angeordnet'.","'Nr- Deberá' is garbled; I read 'Ur-hebers' (the Author), which the sense and the later 'Urhebers aller Dinge' support. Doubtful reading.","'in einem die lichen Tone' is corrupt; the adjective is lost (perhaps 'spöttischen' or similar), so I print [?]. Not filled in.","'Satie Monde' is corrupt; 'Saturn' is conjectured from the sense and marked [?].","Several long sentences (e.g. 'Diejenige bewundernswürdige Gemeinschaft...') were kept as single sentences but their clauses smoothed; 'Bewegungsgründe' = motives, 'Willkür' = (divine) choice, 'Wesen' rendered 'beings' or 'essences' by context.","'nach unserer Methode' refers to Kant's own method in this work, as against the 'physicotheological' method of common use."]],"beweisgrund-0233":["I remark in passing that the great correspondence among the things of the world, in respect of the frequent occasion they give for resemblances, analogies, parallels, or whatever else one may call them, does not deserve to be passed over quite so lightly. Without dwelling on the use which this has in sports of wit, and which for the most part is only imaginary, there lies hidden here, for the philosopher, an object of reflection which seems to me important: how such agreement among very different things in a certain common ground of uniformity can be so great and extensive and yet at the same time so exact. These analogies are also very necessary aids to our knowledge [*Erkenntniß*]; mathematics itself supplies some of them. I refrain from giving examples, for it is to be feared that, because of the different manner in which such resemblances are perceived, they might not have the same effect upon every other understanding; and the thought which I here interpose is in any case incomplete and not yet sufficiently intelligible.",["Silently corrected OCR: 'Ahnlichkeiten' to 'Ähnlichkeiten', 'mochten' to 'möchten', 'ge-wissen' joined.","'Gegenverhältniß' rendered 'correspondence' (a relation of things answering to one another); the rendering is a judgment.","The first sentence is grammatically irregular in the German (the main verb 'verdient' is followed by a loose 'in Ansehung'-clause); I smoothed it.","'Erkenntniß' = knowledge; 'Verstand' = understanding."]],"beweisgrund-0234":["If it should be asked what use can be made of the great unity found in the manifold relations of space which the geometer investigates, I conjecture that general concepts of the unity of mathematical objects may also disclose the grounds of unity and perfection in nature. For example: of all figures the circle is that in which the circumference encloses the greatest possible space that a boundary of such length can contain; and this is because an exact equality in the distance of this boundary from a centre prevails throughout it. If a figure is to be enclosed by straight lines, the greatest possible equality in respect of their distance from the centre can obtain only if not merely the distances of the angular points from this centre are equal to one another, but also the perpendiculars drawn from it to the sides are completely equal. The result is a regular polygon; and geometry shows that, with the same circumference, any other polygon with the same number of sides would always enclose a smaller space than the regular one. One further kind of equality in the distance from a centre is still possible, and it is the simplest: namely, when only the distance of the angular points of the polygon from the same centre is throughout equal. Here it appears that every irregular polygon which can be inscribed in a circle encloses the greatest space of all those that can ever be enclosed by those same sides. Finally, besides this, the polygon in which the length of the side is in addition equal to the distance of the angular point from the centre, that is, the regular hexagon, is, of all figures whatever, the one which, with the smallest perimeter, encloses the greatest space and at the same time, when put together externally with other equal figures, leaves no interstices. The observation readily suggests itself that the inverse relation of the greatest and the smallest in space depends upon equality. And since nature affords many cases of a necessary equality, the rules which are drawn from the geometrical cases mentioned, in regard to the general ground of this inverse relation of the greatest and the smallest, can also be applied to the necessary observance of the law of economy in nature. In the laws of impact a certain equality is always necessary, in this respect: that after impact, if the bodies are inelastic, the velocity of both is always equal; that, if they are elastic, both are always driven apart by the elastic force equally, and with a force equal to that with which the impact took place; and that the centre of gravity of both bodies is not changed at all, in its rest or motion, by the impact, and so on. The relations of space are so infinitely manifold, and yet admit of knowledge so certain and intuition so clear, that, just as they have often already served admirably as symbols of modes of knowledge of an entirely different kind (e.g. to express expectations in games of chance), so they can also supply the means of knowing, from the simplest and most general grounds, the rules of perfection in naturally necessary laws of operation, in so far as these depend upon relations.",["Restated the long opening and closing sentences as several, keeping the argument's order; the impact-law sentence is split at its three 'daß' clauses with semicolons.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Girfelfigur' → Cirkelfigur/circle; 'font' → sonst, rendered 'many cases'; 'Winfelpunkt' → Winkelpunkt; 'x.3.' read as 'u. s. w.' ('and so on'); '3. E.' → z. E.","'Meßkünstler' rendered 'geometer'; 'Gegenverhältniß des Größten und Kleinsten' rendered 'inverse relation of the greatest and the smallest' (a choice).","'Erkenntniß' → 'knowledge'; the plural 'Erkenntnisse' → 'modes of knowledge'; 'Anschauung' → 'intuition'.","'Sparsamkeit' rendered 'economy' (law of parsimony); 'Federkraft' rendered 'elastic force'.","'zu erkennen geben' rendered 'disclose'; the OCR 'ere kennen' is read as 'erkennen'."]],"beweisgrund-0235":["Before I close this reflection, I wish to set out all the different degrees of the philosophical mode of explaining the appearances [Erscheinungen] of perfection met with in the world, in so far as they are all considered as standing under God. I begin with that way of judging in which philosophy still conceals itself, and end with that in which it displays its greatest endeavour. I speak of order, beauty and fitness [Anständigkeit], in so far as this is the ground for subordinating the things of the world to a divine Author in a manner befitting philosophy [Weltweisheit].",["'Erkärungsart' read as Erklärungsart (OCR/orthographic slip).","'Anständigkeit' and 'anständig' are played on in the German; I render 'fitness' and 'befitting' to keep the echo. 'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy' here, as in the preceding 'Philosophie'.","The final sentence's 'unter zu ordnen' is rendered 'subordinating'."]],"beweisgrund-0236":["First, a single event in the course of nature may be regarded as something proceeding immediately from a divine act; and philosophy then has no other business than merely to indicate a ground of proof for this extraordinary dependence.",["'Beweisgrund' rendered 'ground of proof'; also the title word of the work."]],"beweisgrund-0237":["Secondly, an event of the world is regarded as one for which, as for a single case, the mechanism of the world had been specially prepared from the creation onwards, as, for example, the Flood according to the system of various modern writers. The event is then none the less supernatural. The natural science which the philosophers in question here employ serves only to show their own skill, and to devise something which might perhaps occur in accordance with general laws of nature, and whose result would amount to the alleged extraordinary event. For otherwise such a procedure is not in keeping with divine wisdom, which never aims at boasting with useless artifice. We should blame such artifice even in a man who, if nothing prevented him from firing a cannon directly, should choose to fit a firelock with clockwork, by which it was to go off at the appointed moment through ingenious mechanical means.",["'Mechanik der Welt ... abgerichtet' rendered 'mechanism of the world had been specially prepared'; 'Sündfluth' 'the Flood'.","OCR corrections: 'eräugnen' → ereignen ('occur'); 'unmittel— dar' → unmittelbar; 'Geschick— lichkeit' joined.","Divided the long final sentence into two; 'Feuerschloß' rendered 'firelock' (a gunlock)."]],"beweisgrund-0238":["Thirdly, certain parts of nature are regarded as an arrangement [Anstalt] enduring from the creation and proceeding immediately from the hand of the great Architect; and indeed as an arrangement introduced as a single thing, and not as an ordinance according to a constant law. For example, one may maintain that God immediately ordered the mountains, the rivers, the planets and their motion together with the beginning of all things. Since without doubt there must be a first state of nature in which the form of things, just as much as the matter, depends immediately upon God, this way of judging has to that extent a philosophical ground. Yet it is precipitate to ascribe an arrangement immediately to the act of creation, merely because it is advantageous and orderly, before the fitness [Tauglichkeit] which belongs to natural things in accordance with general laws has been examined. To that extent, therefore, it is philosophical only in a very small degree.",["The opening 'wenn ... angesehen werden' is left without a main clause in the German (an anacoluthon); I supplied 'are regarded' as a plain statement parallel to the preceding two points, and restated the rest as separate sentences.","OCR corrections: 'Schöp— iung' → Schöpfung; 'nach)' → nach; 'ste' → sie.","'Anstalt' rendered 'arrangement'; 'Werkmeister' 'Architect' (a choice; 'Master-workman' would be literal); 'Tauglichkeit' 'fitness'."]],"beweisgrund-0239":["Fourthly, one errs when one ascribes something to an artificial order of nature before the inadequacy of the common laws for the purpose has been properly recognised. For example, one explains by the order of the plant and animal kingdoms something that perhaps lies in common mechanical forces, merely because order and beauty are great in them. The philosophical value of this way of judging is still less when every single animal or plant is subordinated immediately to creation than when, apart from some things immediately created, the other products are subordinated to creation in accordance with a law of generative power (not merely of the power of evolution [*Auswickelungsvermögen*]). For in the latter case more is explained by the order of nature, unless the inadequacy of this order in that respect could be clearly proved. Yet this degree of the philosophical mode of explanation also applies to every derivation of an arrangement in the world from laws artificially established for the sake of an end, and not merely in the animal and plant kingdoms.[footnote] For example, one speaks of snow and of the northern lights as if the order of nature which produces both had been instituted for the benefit of the Greenlander or the Laplander (so that he might not be wholly in darkness during the long nights). It is still always to be presumed that this is a well-fitting by-effect, following with necessary unity from other laws. One is almost always in danger of this error when one gives some benefits to men as the ground of a special divine provision. For example, one says that wood and field are for the most part covered with a green colour because, of all colours, it has the middle degree of strength and so keeps the eye in moderate exercise. Against this it may be objected that the inhabitant of Davis Strait is almost blinded by the snow and must take refuge in snow-goggles. It is not blameworthy to seek out the useful consequences and to ascribe them to a benevolent author. What is blameworthy is to represent the order of nature, in accordance with which they occur, as artificially and arbitrarily connected with other things, when it perhaps stands in necessary unity with them.",["Restated as several sentences; the opening 'Viertens, wenn…' is an incomplete clause in the German (a fourth case of error), and I supplied 'one errs when' for the sense.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'mecha— nischen', 'Gre schaffenen' (Geschaffenen), 'besten' (read 'dessen', rendered 'in that respect'), '3. E.' (z. E.), 'Nr- Heber' (Urheber), 'fann', and a stray '1' before 'eine'.","'Auswickelungsvermögen' is given with the German since it is a technical term of the evolution (preformation) theory; 'Anstalt' is rendered 'arrangement' and 'Veranstaltung' 'provision'.","The footnote marker after 'Pflanzenreiche;' refers to the note printed in the passage beginning 'Ich habe in der zweiten Nummer…' ([beweisgrund-0241])."]],"beweisgrund-0240":["Fifthly. The method of judging the perfect arrangements of nature contains most of the spirit of true philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] when it is always ready to admit even supernatural events and not to misjudge the truly artificial dispositions of nature. It must not let its chief concern with advantages, and all harmoniousness, hinder it from seeking their grounds in necessary universal laws, and it must show great care to preserve unity and a reasonable disinclination to multiply the number of natural causes on their account. If to this the attention [is further]",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence; its continuation is in the text that follows the footnote in [beweisgrund-0241]. I did not complete it; '[is further]' is supplied only to make the English clause readable.","I divided the long sentence: 'sich nicht hindern läßt' is made to govern the clause on the chief concern with advantages."]],"beweisgrund-0241":["In the second number of the third consideration of this section I cited, among the examples of artificial order of nature, only those taken from the plant and animal kingdoms. It is to be noted, however, that every arrangement of a law for the sake of a particular benefit is artificial, because by it the law is exempted from necessary unity with other laws of nature, as may be seen from some of the examples mentioned here.\n\n[Main text resumed:] is added to the general rules which can make comprehensible the ground of the necessary connection of what takes place naturally, without special arrangement, with the rules of the advantage or the agreeableness of rational beings, and if one then ascends to the divine author, then this physico-theological way of judging fulfils its duties properly.",["The Academy text interleaves a footnote with the main text. The footnote ends at 'zu ersehen'; 'auf die allgemeine Regeln gefügt wird…' continues the sentence broken off in [beweisgrund-0240] ('die Aufmerksamkeit … gefügt wird'). I have kept both in their order and marked the resumption with a bracketed supplied phrase.","The grammar of the resumed sentence is 'if attention is added to the general rules'; I rendered it as it stands. 'Wenn hiezu noch die Aufmerksamkeit' is found only in the preceding passage.","OCR slips silently corrected: leading ')' and the stray quotation mark, 'nr- theilen' (urtheilen)."]],"beweisgrund-0242":["Seventh Consideration.\nCosmogony.",[]],"beweisgrund-0243":["A hypothesis of a mechanical mode of explaining the origin of the heavenly bodies and the causes of their motions, in conformity with",["The heading continues in [beweisgrund-0244]."]],"beweisgrund-0244":["the rules previously demonstrated.",[]],"beweisgrund-0245":["The figure of the heavenly bodies, the mechanics by which they move and constitute a world-system, and likewise the manifold changes to which the position of their orbits is subject in the course of time: all this has become a part of natural science that is grasped with such great distinctness and certainty that one should not be able to point to a single other insight which explains a natural object (one that at all approaches this in its manifoldness) in so indubitably correct a manner and with such evidence. When one considers this, should one not also come to suspect that the state of nature in which this structure [*Bau*] began, and in which the motions that now continue in accordance with such simple and comprehensible laws were first impressed upon it, will likewise be easier to understand and more intelligible than perhaps most of what else we seek the origin of in nature? The grounds favourable to this suspicion are obvious. All these heavenly bodies are round masses, so far as is known without organisation or secret artificial preparation. The force by which they are drawn",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in [beweisgrund-0249] ('werden, ist allem Ansehen nach…').","OCR slips corrected: '[o' (so), 'Hau' (Bau), 'erflärte', 'dauren'. 'Bau' rendered 'structure'; 'Naturstück' etc. follow in the next passage.","Restated the long first sentence's parenthesis in place; 'dieses seiner Mannigfaltigkeit' read as 'dieser seiner Mannigfaltigkeit'."]],"beweisgrund-0246":["[Footnote:] I wish to say by this only that this must be the path for human reason. For who will ever be able to prevent its erring repeatedly in this, according to Pope:",["A footnote; OCR 'ch' restored as 'Ich'. The quotation of Pope follows in the next two passages. Its anchor in the main text is not shown here."]],"beweisgrund-0247":["Go, prescribe to God's wise order the rules of its government,",["Kant's German rendering of Pope (Essay on Man), translated from the German as given, not from Pope's English."]],"beweisgrund-0248":["Then turn back at last into yourself and be a fool.",["OCR 'dieb' read as 'dich'."]],"beweisgrund-0249":["[The force by which they are drawn] is, to all appearance, a fundamental force proper to matter, and therefore need not and cannot be explained. The projectile motion with which they perform their flight, and the direction in which this impulse was imparted to them, together with the formation of their masses, are the chief things, indeed almost the only things, for which the first natural causes have to be sought. These are simple effects and by no means so complicated as most others in nature, in which the laws according to which they occur are commonly not known with mathematical exactness at all, whereas here they lie before our eyes in the most comprehensible plan. With so great a prospect of success nothing stands in the way except the impression made by the moving grandeur of such a piece of nature as a solar system is. There all natural causes are suspect, because their sufficiency seems far too trivial and opposed to the right of creation belonging to the supreme author. But could not just this be said also of the mechanics by which a great world-structure [*Weltbau*], once it exists, maintains its motions henceforth? The whole maintenance of these depends on the very same law according to which a stone thrown in the air describes its path: a simple law, fruitful in the most regular consequences and worthy of having the upkeep of a whole world-structure entrusted to it.",["Begins mid-sentence, continuing [beweisgrund-0245]; I supplied the bracketed words to link the clause. 'darf also und kann nicht erklärt werden' rendered 'need not and cannot be explained'.","I divided the long sentence on 'einfältige und bei weitem nicht so verwickelte Wirkungen' and on 'Es ist auch…'.","OCR slips corrected: 'Fing' (Flug), 'dauren', 'Gre', etc. 'Wurfsbewegung' = projectile motion; 'einfältig' = simple."]],"beweisgrund-0250":["On the other side, it will be said, one is not able to make clear the natural causes by which the most despicable herb is produced according to fully comprehensible mechanical laws, and yet one ventures upon the explanation of the origin of a world-system on the grand scale. But has a philosopher ever been in a position to make even the laws according to which growth or inner motion takes place in an already existing plant as distinct and mathematically certain as those are with which all motions of the heavenly bodies conform? The nature of the objects [*Gegenstände*] is here wholly changed. The great, the astonishing, is here infinitely more comprehensible than the small and admirable. The generation of a planet, together with the cause of the projectile motion by which it is hurled to run in a circle, will, to all appearance, be easier and more distinctly understood than the generation of a single snowflake. In the snowflake the measured exactness of a six-cornered star is, to the eye, more precise than the roundness of the circles in which planets run, and its rays relate far more correctly to a plane than the [...]",["Breaks off mid-sentence ('als die'); not completed. The comparison's second term is missing, so I left an ellipsis in brackets rather than supply it.","OCR slip: 'sich)' read as 'sich'. 'sechseckichten' = six-cornered (hexagonal)."]],"beweisgrund-0251":["2nd Section. 7th Consideration. On Cosmogony. 139 [the] orbits of these heavenly bodies do so against the common plane of their circular motions.",["Running head and page number ('2. Abth. 7. Betr. Kosmogonie. 139') are retained here because the passage consists of little else; the fragment begins mid-sentence and the subject is lost ('es ... thun' is rendered 'do so').","'Bahnen ... es ... thun': the 'es' is a pronoun standing for a verb already given in the preceding page (probably 'abweichen' or similar); I supply 'do so' without being able to say more."]],"beweisgrund-0252":["I shall set out an attempt at an explanation of the origin of the structure of the world according to universal mechanical laws. I shall do so not for the whole order of nature, but only for the great masses and their orbits, which constitute the crudest foundation of nature. I hope to say something that may give others occasion for important reflections, although my sketch is rough and unelaborated. Some of it has, in my opinion, a degree of probability which, in the case of a smaller object, would leave little room for doubt, and which can be opposed only by the prejudice that a greater art is required than one credits to the universal laws of nature. It often happens that we do not find what we are actually seeking, but on that path come upon other advantages which we had not suspected. Even such a benefit would be a sufficient gain, if it presented itself to the reflection of others, even supposing that the chief aims of the hypothesis should thereby vanish. I shall presuppose here the universal gravitation of matter according to Newton or his successors. Those who, by some definition of metaphysics after their own taste, believe that they can annul the conclusion of acute men drawn from observation and mathematical inference, may pass over the following propositions as something which, besides, has only a remote kinship with the main purpose of this [treatise].",["The OCR text breaks off at 'dieser a'; I read the lost word as 'Abhandlung' and render '[treatise]' in brackets; the reading is conjectural (the 'a' may be a footnote mark).","'dem Newton': rendered simply 'Newton'. The long sentence on probability is kept as one, but 'und der nur ... entgegen stehen kann' is rendered with a relative clause for clarity.","'Vorurtheil einer größern erforderlichen Kunst': 'the prejudice that a greater art is required than one credits to the universal laws of nature' is a restatement."]],"beweisgrund-0253":["1.",[]],"beweisgrund-0254":["Extended Prospect into the Totality [*Inbegriff*] of the Universe.",["*Inbegriff* = sum-total, complex; rendered 'totality'."]],"beweisgrund-0255":["The six planets with their satellites move in orbits that do not deviate far from a common plane, namely the extended equatorial plane of the sun. The comets, on the other hand, run in orbits that stand very far from it, and range widely in all directions away from this plane of reference. Now if, instead of so few planets or comets, some thousands of them belonged to our solar world, the zodiac would appear as a zone illuminated by countless stars, or as a streak losing itself in a pale shimmer, in which some of the nearer planets would show with considerable brilliancy, while the more distant, by reason of their number and the feebleness of their light, would present only a nebulous appearance. For, given the circular motion in which all of them together stand about the sun, there would at any time be some in every part of this zodiac, even if others had changed their place. The comets, on the contrary, would cover the regions on both sides of this luminous zone in every possible scattering.\n\nIf, prepared by this fiction (in which we have done nothing more than increase in thought the number of bodies in our planetary world), we direct our eyes to the wider compass of the universe, we do actually see a luminous zone in which stars, although to all appearance they are at very unequal distances from us, are nevertheless heaped more densely than elsewhere towards one and the same plane, while the regions of the heavens on both sides are covered with stars scattered in every manner. The Milky Way, as I mean it, has very exactly the direction of a great circle. This is a determination [*Bestimmung*] worthy of all attention, and from it we can understand that our sun, and we with it, are to be found in that host of stars which crowds most towards a certain common plane of reference. Analogy here gives very strong ground for conjecturing: that these suns, to whose number ours also belongs, constitute a world-system which is ordered on the large scale according to laws similar to those of our planetary world on the small; that all these suns together with their attendants may have some centre of their common orbits, and that only on account of the immeasurable distance and the long time of their revolutions do they seem not to change their places at all, although in some few a real displacement of position has in fact been observed; that the orbits of these great world-bodies are likewise related to a common plane from which they do not deviate far; and that those which, with far less crowding, occupy the remaining regions of the heavens are in this respect similar to the comets of our planetary world.",["Two very long sentences are divided: the first paragraph break is mine only at the sentence boundary 'Wenn wir...'; the original is one paragraph, so I have inserted a break for readability (a departure).","The long sentence ending 'ähnlich sind' is kept as one, with the 'daß' clauses set out after colons and semicolons.","*Thierkreis* = zodiac; *Beziehungsfläche* = 'plane of reference'; *Weltsystem* = 'world-system'; *Sonnenwelt/Planetenwelt* = 'solar/planetary world'.","'Erdichtung' = 'fiction'; 'Orter' = 'places'. Parenthesis kept in place."]],"beweisgrund-0256":["From this concept, which, as it seems to me, has the greatest probability, we may conjecture that if there are more such higher orders of the world than that to which our sun belongs, and which furnishes to one who has his station in it the appearance of the Milky Way, some of them will be seen in the depths of cosmic space as pale, shimmering patches; and, if the plane of reference of such another co-ordination of the fixed stars is set obliquely towards us, they will appear as elliptical",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. 'Firfterne' in the OCR read as *Fixsterne* ('fixed stars').","'Zusammenordnung' rendered 'co-ordination'."]],"beweisgrund-0257":["figures, which present, in a small space and from a great distance, a",["Fragment; sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0258":["solar system such as that of our Milky Way. And",[]],"beweisgrund-0259":["astronomy has in fact long ago discovered patches of this kind,",[]],"beweisgrund-0260":["although the opinion that has been formed of them is very various 6 [sic], as may be seen in Herr von Maupertuis's book on the figure of the",["The stray '6' is an OCR remnant (probably a line number) and is kept as it stands, flagged; it should perhaps be dropped.","Maupertuis's *Discours sur la figure des astres*."]],"beweisgrund-0261":["stars.",[]],"beweisgrund-0262":["I wish that this reflection might be weighed with some attention;",[]],"beweisgrund-0263":["not only because the concept of creation that arises from it is",[]],"beweisgrund-0264":["astonishingly more moving than it could otherwise be 1» (inasmuch as an innumerable host of suns like our own also",["'1»' is an OCR remnant (line number), left as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0265":["makes a system, whose members are connected through circular motions, while these",[]],"beweisgrund-0266":["systems themselves, of which presumably there are again innumerable ones, some of which we",[]],"beweisgrund-0267":["can perceive, may themselves be members of a still higher order),",["'konnen' in the OCR read as 'können'."]],"beweisgrund-0268":["but also because even the observation of the fixed stars near to us, or rather of slowly wandering suns, guided by such a concept, may perhaps discover much that escapes attention, in so far as a certain plan is not to be investigated.",["OCR debris ('Fix-\"15 sterne', 'Aufmerksamkeiit') silently corrected. The closing clause 'in so fern nicht ein gewisser Plan zu untersuchen ist' is obscure; I render it literally: without such a plan nothing would be investigated, i.e. the observation would otherwise lack a guiding plan."]],"beweisgrund-0269":["2.\nGrounds for a Mechanical Origin of our",[]],"beweisgrund-0270":["Planetary World in General.",["Stray 'd' in the OCR omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0271":["The planets all move about our sun in one and the same direction, and with only slight deviation from a common plane of reference, which is the ecliptic, precisely as bodies would do that are carried along by a matter which, filling the whole",[]],"beweisgrund-0272":["of space, performs its motion in a whirl about an axis. The",[]],"beweisgrund-0273":["planets are all heavy towards the sun, and the magnitude of the lateral impulse [*Seitenschwung*] would have to be exactly and precisely measured, if they are",["'Seil tenschwungs' (OCR) read as 'Seitenschwungs'."]],"beweisgrund-0274":["thereby to be brought to run in circular orbits. And as in a mechanical effect of this kind geometrical exactness is not to be expected, so all the orbits also deviate, though not much, from circular roundness. They consist of materials which, according to Newton's calculations, are of less density the further they are from the sun, as anyone would also find natural if they had formed themselves, in the space in which they float, from a world-stuff dispersed there. For, in the endeavour with which everything sinks towards the sun, the denser kinds of matter must press more towards the sun and heap themselves more in its neighbourhood than those of lighter kind, whose fall is more retarded on account of their lesser density. The matter of the sun, however, is, according to M. de Buffon's observation, fairly equal in density to that which the summed mass of all the planets together would have; and this agrees well with a mechanical formation, according to which the planets may have formed at different heights from different kinds of the elements, while all the rest that filled this space may have fallen down, mingled together, upon their common centre, the sun.",["OCR slips corrected silently: 'welchen' read as 'weichen', 'Gir- Fkelrundung' as 'Cirkelrundung', 'fintt' as 'sinkt', 'Naheit' as 'Nähe', 'as' as 'aus'.","'wenn sie sich ... gebildet hätten': 'sie' resolved as the planets (the matters they consist of); 'ein jeder' rendered 'anyone'.","'v. Buffon' rendered 'M. de Buffon' (Buffon's *Histoire naturelle*)."]],"beweisgrund-0275":["He who, notwithstanding this, wishes to see such a structure handed over directly to the hand of God, without trusting the mechanical laws with anything in the matter, is obliged to advance something on account of which he finds necessary here what he otherwise does not readily admit in natural science. He can name no ends for which it would be better that the planets should run in circles in one direction rather than in different directions, and nearly in one plane of reference rather than in all sorts of regions. The celestial space is at present empty, and with all this motion the planets would put no obstacles in one another's way. I willingly concede that there may be hidden ends which common mechanics would not have attained, and which no man perceives. But nobody is permitted to presuppose them if he wishes to found an opinion upon them, unless he is able to indicate them. Suppose, then, that God had directly imparted to the planets their projectile force and fixed their orbits. It is to be presumed that they would not show in themselves the mark of imperfection and deviation which is to be found in every product of nature. Had it been good that they should relate to one plane, it is to be presumed that He would have set their orbits exactly upon it. Had it been good that they should approximate to circular motion, one may believe that their path would have become exactly a circle. And it is not to be seen why exceptions to the most exact correctness should have had to remain even in that which was supposed to be an immediate divine act of art.",["OCR 'Hau' corrected to 'Bau' (as in the following passages); 'grinden' to 'gründen'. 'Hau' is not translated here, since the first sentence's 'Hau' is rendered 'structure'.","'Beziehungsplan' rendered 'plane of reference'; 'Wurfskraft' as 'projectile force'.","The final long sentence is divided and the 'Wenn denn endlich…' clause restated as a supposition ('Suppose, then…'); the sense is unchanged."]],"beweisgrund-0276":["The members of the solar world from the most distant regions, the comets, run very eccentrically. If it were a matter of an immediate divine action, they might just as well be moved in circular orbits, however greatly their paths may deviate from the ecliptic. In this case the uses of so great an eccentricity are devised with great boldness. For it is more readily conceivable that a world-body, moving always at an equal distance in whatever celestial region, should have an arrangement suited to this distance, than that it should be arranged with equal advantage for a great variety of distances. As to the advantages which Newton adduces, it is evident that they otherwise have not the least probability, except that, on the presupposition of an immediate divine ordering once assumed, they can at least serve as some pretext of a purpose.",["OCR 'Eccentrieität' read as 'Eccentricität'; the broken 'be— wegt' joined as 'bewegt'.","The sentence beginning 'Die Nutzen…' is divided at the 'denn' clause for clarity."]],"beweisgrund-0277":["This error of subordinating the structure of the planetary world directly to divine purposes is most obvious where people try to invent motives from the density of the planets, which decreases inversely as the distances increase. The sun's action, it is said, diminishes in this ratio, and it was fitting that the density of the bodies which were to be warmed by it should also be arranged in proportion to it. Now it is well known that the sun acts only to a slight depth beneath the surface of a world-body. Hence, from its influence in warming the body, no conclusion can be drawn as to the density of the whole mass. Here the inference from the purpose is far too large. The means, namely the diminished density of the whole mass, involves an elaborateness of provision which is superfluous and unnecessary for the magnitude of the purpose.",["OCR slips corrected: 'Hau' to 'Bau', 'Sich tigkeit' and 'Dieb— tigkeit' to 'Dichtigkeit', stray 'auch)' to 'auch'.","'Bewegungsgründe' rendered 'motives'; 'Weitläuftigkeit der Anstalt' as 'elaborateness of provision'. The Gegenstand-type terms are not involved."]],"beweisgrund-0278":["In all natural productions, so far as they amount to harmony, order and utility, there are indeed agreements with divine purposes, but also marks of origin from universal laws. The consequences of these laws extend much further than to such a single case, and accordingly show in each single effect traces of an intermixture of such laws, which were not directed solely to this one product. For this reason there are also deviations from the greatest possible exactness with regard to a particular purpose. On the other hand, an immediately supernatural arrangement, just because its execution does not presuppose the consequences of more universal laws of operation of matter, will also not be disfigured by particular collateral consequences of these laws intruding themselves. It will bring the plan to completion in the utmost possible correctness. In the nearer parts of the planetary world, towards the common centre, there is a greater approach to complete order and measured exactness. This degenerates towards the limits of the system, or far from the plane of reference at the sides, into irregularity and deviations, exactly as is to be expected of a constitution of mechanical origin. In an immediately divine ordering, purposes imperfectly attained could never be met with; rather the greatest correctness and measuredness would show itself everywhere, as is observed, among other things, in the structure of animals.",["OCR repairs: 'sol— eben' to 'solchen', 'sich)' to 'sich', 'abgemessen— neu' to 'abgemessenen'. The running head and page number '144 Beweisgrund zu einer Demonstration des Daseins Gottes' are omitted.","The first sentence is divided into three, and the sentence on the supernatural arrangement into two, for clarity.","'Beziehungsplan' = 'plane of reference'; 'Hau' (OCR) read as 'Bau'; 'Kunsthandlung' not present here."]],"beweisgrund-0279":["3",["Section number only."]],"beweisgrund-0280":["Brief Sketch of the Most Probable Manner in which a Planetary",["Heading, continued in the next passage ('system could have been formed mechanically')."]],"beweisgrund-0281":["system could have been formed mechanically.",["Continuation of the heading begun in the preceding passage."]],"beweisgrund-0282":["The grounds of proof for a mechanical origin which have just been adduced are so important that even a few of them alone moved all students of nature long ago to seek the cause of the planetary orbits in natural moving forces. They did so chiefly because the planets run round the sun in circles in the very same direction in which the sun turns about its axis, and because their paths coincide so nearly with the plane of its equator. Newton was the great destroyer of all these vortices, to which people nevertheless clung long after his demonstrations, as may be seen in the example of the celebrated Herr von Mairan. The sure and convincing proofs of the Newtonian philosophy showed plainly that nothing of the sort which the vortices were supposed to be, carrying the planets round, is to be met with in the heavens, and that there is so utterly no stream of such fluid in these spaces that even the tails of comets continue their undisturbed motion right across all these circles. It was safe to conclude from this that, as the celestial space is now empty or infinitely thin, no mechanical cause can obtain which could impress their circular motion upon the planets. But straightway to pass over all mechanical laws, and by a bold hypothesis to let God throw the planets directly, so that in combination with their gravity they should move in circles, was too wide a step for it to be able to remain within the province of philosophy. It at once strikes the eye that one case still remains in which mechanical causes of this constitution are possible. This is the case where the space of the planetary system, which is now empty, was formerly filled, so as to occasion a community of the moving forces through all the regions of this district in which the attraction of our sun prevails.",["OCR repairs: 'ter nahe' read as 'so nahe' (conjectural); 'zu: sammen treffen' as 'zusammentreffen'; 'ding' as 'hing'; 'unver— rückte' as 'unverrückte'; 'veran— lassen' as 'veranlassen'.","'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy'; 'Wirbel' = 'vortices'; 'Bewegkräfte' = 'moving forces'; 'Beweisthümer' = 'proofs'.","The long opening sentence is divided and the final sentence restated in two for clarity."]],"beweisgrund-0283":["And here I can indicate the one condition which alone is possible,",["Fragment: the sentence continues in the next passage ('under which a mechanical cause…')."]],"beweisgrund-0284":["under which a mechanical cause of the celestial motions obtains, which is a considerable",["Fragment, continued in the next passage. 'Die einzige mögliche' is carried from the preceding passage."]],"beweisgrund-0285":["circumstance in justification of a hypothesis, of which one will only rarely be able to boast. Since the spaces",["Fragment of a sentence, joined to the preceding passage."]],"beweisgrund-0286":["are now empty, they must formerly have been filled; otherwise no extended action of the moving forces driving in circles could ever have taken place. And accordingly this diffused matter must afterwards have gathered itself upon the celestial bodies. That is, if I consider it more closely,",["The stray 'D' before 'auf' is an OCR artefact and is omitted.","The sentence breaks off at 'näher be—' and continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0287":["these celestial bodies themselves will have been formed out of the diffused primary matter in the spaces of the solar system, and the motion",["'Grundstoff' rendered 'primary matter'; 'Sonnenbau' as 'solar system'. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0288":["which the particles of their composition had in the state of dispersion",["Fragment continued in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0289":["has remained with them after their union into separate masses. Since then these spaces are empty. They contain no matter which could serve among these bodies for the communication of the circular motion. But they have not always been so, and we shall Bewegun—",["The passage breaks off mid-word ('Bewegun—'); left as it stands and not completed.","'Mittheilung des Kreisschwunges' rendered 'communication of the circular motion'."]],"beweisgrund-0290":["...[gen] [which] are aware of, for which no natural causes can now be found, but which are remnants of the very oldest, crude state of nature.",["The passage begins mid-word and mid-sentence (\"gen gewahr\"); the preceding word is lost, so the subject and the verb's grammar are uncertain. The opening is rendered as it stands, with the missing part marked by an ellipsis and the supplied bracketed words offered only tentatively.","\"Überbleibsel\" = remnants; \"rohen Zustandes der Natur\" = crude state of nature."]],"beweisgrund-0291":["From this observation I wish to take only one step further, in order to approach a probable concept of the manner in which these great masses originated and of the cause of their motions; the more thorough working out of a slight sketch I leave to the inquiring",["The sentence breaks off at the page/line end; \"dem forschenden | Leser\" continues in the next passage, and is translated as it stands without completion.","\"Begriff\" = concept; \"Schattenrisses\" rendered 'sketch' (literally a silhouette or outline)."]],"beweisgrund-0292":["reader himself. If, accordingly, the material for the formation of the sun",["The sentence is continued in the following passages; the conditional clause is left incomplete here as in the German."]],"beweisgrund-0293":["and of all the heavenly bodies that are subject to its mighty attraction,",["\"zu Gebote stehen\" = are at the command of, hence 'are subject to'."]],"beweisgrund-0294":["was scattered throughout the whole space of the planetary world, and if there was some",["The German \"und es war irgend\" continues across the line break (\"ir— gend\"); the hyphenation is silently repaired. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0295":["where, in the place now occupied by the mass of the sun, matter",["The German \"irgend ... Materie\" is split across passages; 'some ... where' is reconstituted in the English as 'some[where]' across this and the preceding fragment."]],"beweisgrund-0296":["of stronger forces of attraction, then a general sinking towards this [place] arose, and the attraction of the body of the sun grew with its mass. It is easy to conjecture that, in the general fall of the particles, even from the most remote regions of the universe, the materials of denser kind in the lower regions, where everything crowded towards the common centre, will have accumulated in proportion as they were nearer to the centre, although in all regions materials of every kind of density were present. For only the particles of the heaviest sort could have the greatest power of forcing their way in this chaos through the mixture",["Restated: the German's conditional sentence (\"Wenn ... so entstand\") is carried over as it stands across the passages. The OCR \"Es »\" is silently corrected to \"Es\"; \"liefern\" corrected to \"tiefern\" (lower regions); \"sich)\" to \"sich\".","\"hiezu\" = 'towards this' [place], i.e. the place of the sun; the bracketed 'place' is supplied.","\"Vermögen\" rendered 'power'; \"Gravitationspunkte\" in the next passage is 'point of gravitation'."]],"beweisgrund-0297":["of the lighter, in order to attain to a greater nearness to the point of gravitation. || In the motions which arose from falls of differing heights in the surrounding sphere, the resistance of the particles hindering one another could never be so perfectly equal that the acquired velocities should not issue, on some side or other, in a deflection. And in this circumstance there shows itself a very common rule of the reaction of materials: they drive or direct and restrict one another until they offer one another the least hindrance; in accordance with which the lateral motions must at last have united in a common revolution in one and the same direction. The particles, accordingly, out of which the sun was formed, arrived upon it already with this lateral motion, and the sun, formed out of this material, must have had a rotation in precisely the same direction.",["The running head \"146 Beweisgrund zu einer Demonstration des Daseins Gottes.\" is omitted; I mark the page break (AA 2:145/146) with ||, an apparatus sign not in the source text, only to show where the text divides; delete if unwanted.","OCR \"gemeinschaft— liebe\" corrected to \"gemeinschaftliche\". Long sentences are kept close to the German, with minor division of the second sentence at the semicolon retained.","\"Gegenwirkung\" = reaction; \"Seitenbewegung\" = lateral motion."]],"beweisgrund-0298":["It is clear, however, from the laws of gravitation that in this cosmic material swung round in a circle all the parts must have been striving to intersect the plane which passes, in the direction of their common revolution, through the centre of the sun, and which, according to our conclusions, coincides with the equatorial plane of this heavenly body, unless they were already in it. Accordingly all these parts, especially near the sun, will have their greatest accumulation in the space that is near the extended equatorial plane of the sun. Finally, it is also quite natural that, since the particles must hinder or accelerate one another, in a word must collide with or drive one another, until the one can no longer disturb the motion of the other at all, everything should in the end result in a condition in which only those particles remain suspended which have precisely the degree of lateral impetus required, at the distance at which they are from the sun, to balance gravitation, so that each may swing round in free motion in concentric circles. This velocity is an effect of the fall, and the lateral motion a consequence of the counter-impact, which lasted until everything had of itself adjusted into the condition of least hindrance. The remaining particles, which could not attain such a measured exactness, must, with gradually diminishing motion, have sunk to the centre of general gravitation, so as to increase the mass of the sun. The sun will accordingly have a density roughly equal to the average of that of the other materials in the space around it; yet in such a way that, in view of the circumstances cited, its mass will necessarily far exceed the quantity of matter which has remained suspended in the region around it.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"beftrebt\" to \"bestrebt\", \"burdschneiden\" to \"durchschneiden\", \"Aquator\" retained as Äquator.","\"Plan\" in \"den Plan, der...\" is rendered 'plane' (Ebene sense), consistent with \"Aquatorsfläche\" = equatorial plane.","\"herumgeschwungenen\" rendered 'swung round in a circle'; \"Verfassung der mindesten Hindernisse\" = 'condition of least hindrance'."]],"beweisgrund-0299":["In this condition, which seems to me natural, a diffused material for the formation of various heavenly bodies swung round the sun, in accordance with the central laws, to great distances, in a narrow space nearest the extended plane of the sun's equator, of the greater density the nearer to the centre, and everywhere with an impetus which at that distance was sufficient for free circular motion. If one now supposes that planets formed out of these particles, it cannot fail that they should have forces of revolution through which they would move in orbits very nearly circular, although deviating somewhat from this, because they were gathered from particles of differing height [of fall]. It is equally quite natural that those planets which form at great heights (where the space around them is much greater, which has the effect that the difference in the velocity of the particles exceeds the force with which they are drawn to the centre of the planet) should there also gain larger masses than near the sun. The agreement with many other remarkable features of the planetary world I pass over, because it presents itself of its own accord.*) In the most remote parts of the system, and chiefly at great distances from the plane of reference, the bodies that form, the comets, cannot have this regularity. And so the space of the planetary world will become empty, after everything has united into separate masses. Yet in a later epoch particles may still have sunk down from the outermost limits of this sphere of attraction, which may henceforth at all times move freely in circles in the heavenly space around the sun: materials of the utmost tenuity, and perhaps the stuff of which the zodiacal light consists.",["Restated in part: the German's first long sentence (\"In diesem Zustande ... wenn man da setzt\") is tangled; I divided it and made the main verb 'swung round' stand for the participle construction. The sense of \"in einem engen Raum\" is as given; the German is irregular here.","OCR \"feint\" corrected to \"sein\"; \"Theil— eben\" to \"Theilchen\"; \"sammleten\" kept as 'were gathered'. \"[of fall]\" is supplied from \"Höhe\" in the earlier passage (\"verschiedentlich hohem Fall\").","The asterisk marks the footnote that appears in the following passages (beweisgrund-0303).","\"Beziehungsplane\" rendered 'plane of reference'; the doubtful parenthesis on the differing velocities is translated closely."]],"beweisgrund-0300":["4.",[]],"beweisgrund-0301":["Remark.",["OCR \"\"E Anmerkung\" read as \"Anmerkung\"; the stray \"E\" is dropped as an OCR slip."]],"beweisgrund-0302":["The aim of this consideration is chiefly to give an example of the procedure to which our preceding proofs have entitled",["The sentence breaks off and continues after the footnote in the next passage (\"haben, da man nämlich ...\"). The footnote text intervenes in the source."]],"beweisgrund-0303":["*) The formation of a smaller system, which as a part belongs to the planetary world, such as that of Jupiter and Saturn, and likewise the axial rotations of these heavenly bodies, are, on account of the analogy, included under this explanation.\n\nus, in that one removes the groundless apprehension that every explanation of a great arrangement of the world from universal laws of nature opens a gap for the malicious enemies of religion through which to penetrate its bulwarks. In my opinion the hypothesis here cited has at least grounds enough in its favour to invite men of wide insight to a closer examination of the plan presented in it, which is only a rough outline. My purpose, so far as it concerns this work, is fulfilled if, prepared by confidence in the regularity and order that can flow from universal laws of nature, men are merely moved to open a freer field to natural philosophy, and to regard a manner of explanation such as this, or another, as possible and as agreeing well with the knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] of a wise God.",["The footnote is kept as a footnote, placed first as it stands in the source before the resumed main text; the main sentence \"entitled ... haben\" is continued from the preceding passage ('entitled us').","Restated: the final sentence is long and its construction irregular; I kept it as one sentence but smoothed \"vorbereitet ... nur ... öffnet ... und ... anzusehen kann bewogen werden\" into a single clause governed by 'men are ... moved'. The subject 'men' is supplied by judgment.","OCR \"Er— kenntniß\" repaired; \"Erkenntniß\" = knowledge. \"Weltweisheit\" = philosophy ('natural philosophy' for \"natürliche Weltweisheit\")."]],"beweisgrund-0304":["Besides, it would be well worthy of philosophical endeavour, now that vortices, the favourite instrument of so many systems, have been banished beyond the sphere of nature to Milton's limbo of vanity, to enquire properly whether nature herself, without the invention of special forces, might not offer something which could explain the revolving motion of the planets, directed throughout towards one and the same region, since the other motion is given by the central forces in gravitation, as an enduring bond of nature. At the least, the plan we have sketched does not depart from the rule of unity, for this very force of revolution is derived as a consequence from gravitation, as befits contingent motions; for these are to be deduced as consequences from the forces inherent in matter even at rest.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"Erdich— iung\" to \"Erdichtung\", \"berge— leitet\" to \"hergeleitet\".","\"Schwungsbewegung\" rendered 'revolving motion'; \"Schwungskraft\" 'force of revolution'. \"Weltweisheit\"-type terms are not at issue here.","The sentence beginning \"daß man gleichwohl gehörig forschte\" depends on \"wohl würdig\"; I restated it as an infinitive clause ('to enquire') to avoid an irregularity in English. \"Verbande\" rendered 'bond'."]],"beweisgrund-0305":["Moreover, I would observe that the atomistic system of Democritus and Epicurus, notwithstanding its first appearance of similarity, stands in quite a different relation to the inference to an author of the world from that of the plan of our own system. In the former, motion was eternal and without an author, and collision, the rich source of so much order, was a matter of haphazard and chance, for which no ground was anywhere to be found. Here, a known and true law of nature, on an assumption that is comprehensible, leads with necessity to order. And since here a determining ground is met with for an issue in favour of regularity, and something that maintains nature in the track of harmony and beauty, we are led to conjecture a ground from which the necessity of the relation to perfection can be understood.",["Restated the second sentence of the German's final period as two sentences, with a semicolon-like break at 'And since'.","Corrected OCR 'Drdnung' to 'Ordnung'; 'jer bez greiflichen' to 'begreiflichen'; 'ver— ständen' to 'verstanden'.","*Ausschlag* (outcome, turning of the scale) rendered 'issue'; *Ungefähr* 'haphazard'."]],"beweisgrund-0306":["In order, however, to make comprehensible by yet another example how the effect of gravitation, in the union of scattered elements, is necessarily determined to produce regularity and beauty, I shall add an explanation of the mechanical mode of generation of Saturn's ring, which, as I think, has as much probability as can be expected of any hypothesis. Only grant me the following. In the first age of the world Saturn was surrounded by an atmosphere such as has been observed about various comets which do not approach the sun very closely and appear without tails. The particles of this vapour-circle rose from the planet (to which we will concede a rotation on its axis). In the sequel these vapours, whether because the planet cooled or from other causes, began to sink down to it again. The remainder then follows with mechanical exactness. For since all the particles, from the point of the surface from which they rose, must have a velocity equal to that of this place in order to move about the axis of the planet, all of them must, by means of this lateral impetus, have been striving to describe free circles about Saturn according to the rules of central forces.¹ But all those particles whose velocity is not precisely of the degree which, by centrifugal force, exactly balances the attraction at the height where they float, must necessarily collide with and retard one another, until there remain only those which can revolve in free circular motion according to the central laws, moving in circles about Saturn; the rest fall back by degrees upon its surface. Now all these circular motions must necessarily cut the extended plane of Saturn's equator, as is known to everyone who knows the central laws. Consequently the remaining particles of its former atmosphere will at last crowd together about Saturn into a circular plane, which occupies the extended equator of this planet, and whose outermost vapour is here also cut off by the very same cause that in comets determines the limit of the atmosphere. This",["The long opening sentence has been divided into several; the 'Man räume mir nur ein' clauses are set out as a list of three concessions, which the German strings together with 'daß'.","'¹' stands for the OCR'd footnote sign after 'beschreiben'; the footnote text follows in the next passage.","*Rauch* (literally 'smoke') rendered 'vapour', as the sense of the atmosphere's outermost layer requires. The passage breaks off after 'Dieser' ('This').","Corrected OCR 'Nr— sachen' to 'Ursachen'."]],"beweisgrund-0307":["¹ Saturn moves about its axis, according to the assumption. Every particle that rises from it must therefore have just this same lateral motion, and continue it at whatever height it may attain. The *limbus* of freely moving cosmic matter must necessarily become a ring; or rather, the motions in question can issue in no other figure than that of a ring. For since they can derive their velocity for circular motion only from the points of Saturn's surface from which they rose, those that have risen from its equator must possess the greatest speed. Now of all distances from its centre there is only one at which this velocity is just suited to circular motion, and at every smaller distance it is too weak. A circle can therefore be drawn in this *limbus* from the centre of Saturn, within which all the particles must fall down to the surface of the planet. All the rest, however, between this circle and the one at its outermost edge (consequently those contained in a ring-shaped space), will thenceforth remain in motion, freely floating in circles about it.",["Footnote to the preceding passage, kept as a footnote (marked '¹').","*Limbus* kept in Latin, in italics; *Weltstoff* rendered 'cosmic matter'.","*Cirkelkreis* rendered simply 'circle'."]],"beweisgrund-0308":["From such a solution one arrives at consequences by which the time of Saturn's rotation on its axis is given, and that with as much probability as one concedes to these grounds, by which it is at the same time determined. For since the particles of the inner edge have just the same velocity as a point on Saturn's equator, and since moreover this velocity, according to the laws of gravitation, has the degree required for circular motion, we can find the time of revolution of the particles in the inner edge from the ratio of the distance of one of Saturn's satellites to the distance of the inner edge of the ring from the centre of the planet, and likewise from the given time of the satellite's revolution. From this time, and from the ratio of the smallest diameter of the ring to that of the planet, we can find the planet's rotation on its axis. And so it is found by calculation that Saturn must turn on its axis in 5 hours and about 40 minutes. If analogy with the other planets is here consulted, this appears to harmonise well with the period of their rotation.",["The clause 'wodurch sie zugleich bestimmt wird' is ambiguous in its reference; I take 'sie' to be the time of rotation, and have kept the clause as it stands.","Corrected OCR 'Stünden' to 'Stunden'; *Umschwung* 'revolution', *Umwendung* 'rotation'."]],"beweisgrund-0309":["And so, whether the assumption of the cometary atmosphere which Saturn may have had in the beginning be granted or not, there remains, as I think, a tolerably certain inference, which I draw from it in illustration of my principal proposition. It is this: that if such a vapour-circle had been about it, the mechanical generation of a floating ring must have been a necessary consequence of it; and that therefore the issue of nature, when left to universal laws, aims even out of chaos at regularity.",["Divided the German's one sentence into two; 'Ausschlag' again rendered 'issue'. 'Hauptsatz' rendered 'principal proposition'."]],"beweisgrund-0310":["Eighth Consideration.\nOn the Divine All-sufficiency.",["*Betrachtung* rendered 'Consideration', as the title of a section of the treatise."]],"beweisgrund-0311":["The sum of all these considerations leads us to a concept of the highest being, which comprehends in itself everything that can be thought at all, when men, made of dust, venture to cast prying glances behind the curtain which conceals the secrets of the unsearch-",["Corrected OCR 'aus Stande' to 'aus Staube' (made of dust). The passage breaks off in the middle of 'Unerforsch-lichen', which the next passage completes; the word is translated across the break ('unsearch-' / '-able').","The line number '5' has been omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0312":["-able for created eyes. God is all-sufficient. Whatever is, whether it be possible or actual, is something only in so far as it is given through him.",["Begins with the second half of 'Unerforsch-lichen'. The OCR 'es set' is read 'es sei'; the line number '1o' omitted. The sentence 'geben ist' is completed by 'ge-' in the previous line, i.e. 'gegeben'."]],"beweisgrund-0313":["A human language can let the Infinite speak thus to himself: 'I am from eternity to eternity; apart from me there is nothing, save in so far as it is something through me.' This thought, the most sublime of all, has been much neglected, or for the most part not touched upon at all. That which, in the possibilities of things,",["Breaks off in mid-sentence; the German 'ohne' after 'nichts' means 'except', rendered 'save'. The line number '15' omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0314":["presents itself of perfection and beauty in excellent plans, is",["Fragment continuing the previous passage; the construction is completed only in the following passages, and the auxiliary 'ist' belongs with 'worden' below."]],"beweisgrund-0315":["as an object of the divine wisdom, necessary in itself, but",["Fragment. 'Für sich nothwendig' rendered 'necessary in itself'."]],"beweisgrund-0316":["not itself as a consequence of this incomprehensible being regarded",["Fragment: the participle 'angesehen' is completed by 'worden' at the head of the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0317":["been. Men have restricted the dependence of other things merely to their existence, whereby a great share in the ground of so much",["'worden' (with 'ist' above, 'has been') translated as 'been'. The line number '20' omitted. The sentence continues in the next passages."]],"beweisgrund-0318":["perfection is withdrawn from that supreme nature and is ascribed to I know not what",["Fragment; 'wird' in the next passage governs both 'entzogen' and 'beigemessen'. I have put the auxiliary in the English here and left the ascription to be completed below."]],"beweisgrund-0319":["eternal non-thing [*Undinge*].",["Completes the sentence begun above: 'beigemessen wird' is rendered in the preceding passage. *Undinge* ('non-thing', i.e. nothing real) is kept in German in brackets because the sense turns on it."]],"beweisgrund-0320":["The fruitfulness of a single ground in many consequences, the harmo-",["Breaks off in the middle of 'Zusammen-stimmung', which is completed in the next passage ('harmo-' / '-ny')."]],"beweisgrund-0321":["-ny and fitness of natures to fit together according to universal laws, without frequent conflict, in a regular plan, must first of all be met with in the possibilities of things,",["Begins with the second half of 'Zusammenstimmung'. I have restated the infinitive phrase 'nach allgemeinen Gesetzen ... zusammen zu passen' literally, as the German is compressed. OCR 'miissen' read 'müssen'. Line number '25' omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0322":["and only then can wisdom be active in choosing them. What limi-",["Breaks off in the middle of 'Schranken' ('limi-' / '-tations')."]],"beweisgrund-0323":["-tations, set upon the Independent from an alien ground, wou-",["Begins with the second half of 'Schranken'; 'wür-' / '-den' ('wou-' / '-ld') is split across the break."]],"beweisgrund-0324":["-ld there be, if these very possibilities were not grounded in him? And what an unintelligible haphazard [*Ungefähr*] it would be, that in this field of possibility, without the presupposition of anything existing, unity and fruit-",["OCR 'zo was' read 'so was', i.e. 'was für'. The German 'daß sich ... findet' is rendered 'it would be, that ... is found'; the verb is supplied to give a main clause in English, and the 'would be' is mine. Fragment continues in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0325":["-ful harmony [*Zusammenpassung*] is found, whereby the being of the highest de-",["Begins with the second half of 'fruchtbare'. Breaks off in the middle of 'Gra-[den]' ('degrees'); I render the stump 'de-' and do not complete it. *Wesen* here rendered 'being'."]],"beweisgrund-0326":["[…] that of power and wisdom, when those external relations are compared with its inner capacity,] finds itself in a position to bring about great perfection? Assuredly, such a representation never hands over the origin of the good, without any diminution, to a single being. When Huygens invented the pendulum clock, he could not, if he reflected on it, ever ascribe to himself wholly that uniformity which constitutes its perfection. The nature of the cycloid, which makes it possible for small and large arcs to be described in the same time by free fall along it, alone put this execution within his power. That so great a range of beautiful consequences is even possible from the simple ground of gravity would, if it did not itself depend upon him who by actual exercise has brought forth all this connection, plainly diminish and divide his share in the charming unity and the great range of so much order resting upon a single ground.",["The passage opens mid-sentence; the opening clause and the parenthesis closing after 'inner capacity' are only partly present, so the bracketed opening '[… ]' and the closing bracket are supplied by me to show the break, and the OCR 'sich)' is read as 'sich'.","OCR 'grope Vollkommen heit' read as 'große Vollkommenheit'; 'Hugen' read as 'Huygens' (Huyghens).","The last sentence is long and awkwardly built; I kept it as one sentence but resolved 'seinen Antheil' as the share of the creator (the one who actually brought the connection about), as the argument requires.","'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'; 'Vorstellung' 'representation'."]],"beweisgrund-0327":["Admiration at the sequence of an effect from a cause ceases as soon as I see clearly and easily the sufficiency of the cause for it. On this footing no admiration can any longer find place if I regard the mechanical structure of the human body, or any artificial arrangement I please, as a work of the Almighty and look merely to its actuality; for it is easy and clear to understand that he who can do all things can also produce such a machine, if it is possible. Nevertheless admiration remains, however one may have introduced this consideration to make comprehension easier. For it is astonishing that even so much as an animal body was possible. And even if I could fully understand all the springs and tubes, all the nerve-vessels, levers and mechanical arrangements of it, admiration would still remain as to how it is possible that such manifold functions have been united in one structure; how the operations directed to one end can be so well paired with those by which another is attained; how the very same combination serves besides to maintain the machine and to repair the consequences of accidental injuries; and how it was possible that a human being could be so fine a fabric and yet, in spite of so many grounds of decay, last so long. Even when at length I have been instructed that so much unity and harmony is possible because there exists a being which, together with the grounds of actuality, contains also those of all possibility, this does not yet remove the ground of admiration. For by the analogy of what human beings carry out one can indeed form some concept of",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage, not completed here.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Nr— sache' = Ursache, 'Hau' = Bau, 'auge— führt' = angeführt, 'Buzsammenfügung' = Zusammenfügung, 'Hau' (second) = Bau, 'seines' for 'seins', 'unerachtet' = ungeachtet.","'Zusammenfügung' taken as 'combination'; 'Zweck' rendered 'end'."]],"beweisgrund-0328":["the analogy of what human beings carry out, some concept of this,",["This fragment repeats the closing words of the previous passage ('die Analogie dessen, was Menschen ausüben, einigen Begriff davon machen') and is given as it stands; it continues into the next fragment: how a being can be the cause of something actual."]],"beweisgrund-0329":["of how a being can be the cause of something actual, but never",[]],"beweisgrund-0330":["of how it contains the ground of the inner possibility of other things,",["Breaks off at the line-end hyphen 'ent-'; continues 'halte' in the next passage."]],"beweisgrund-0331":["and it seems as if this thought rises far too high for a created being to attain it.",["The '5' is a line number and is omitted; 'ent- | halte' joined to 'enthalte'."]],"beweisgrund-0332":["This lofty concept of the divine nature, when we think it in respect of its all-sufficiency,",["Allgenugsamkeit = 'all-sufficiency'. The sentence continues in the following fragments."]],"beweisgrund-0333":["can serve, even in judging the constitution",["'Beschaffen— | heit' joined; the sentence continues."]],"beweisgrund-0334":["of possible things, where grounds of decision are directly lacking to us,",[]],"beweisgrund-0335":["as a means of inferring from it, as from a ground, to the possibility of other things as a consequence. The question is whether, among",["Line number '10' omitted. 'fremde Möglichkeit' rendered 'the possibility of other things'."]],"beweisgrund-0336":["all possible worlds, there is not to be found an endless increase in the degrees of",[]],"beweisgrund-0337":["perfection, since no natural order is possible",[]],"beweisgrund-0338":["above which a still more excellent one could not be thought; and further,",[]],"beweisgrund-0339":["if I were to grant a highest degree here, whether at least different worlds, which are surpassed by none, might not be wholly equal to one another in perfection. In questions of this kind it is difficult, and perhaps impossible, to decide anything from the consideration of possible things alone.",["Line number '15' omitted; OCR 'perschiedene' read 'verschiedene', 'selbst' kept as 'at least … even' folded into 'at least'. 'et— | was' joined."]],"beweisgrund-0340":["But if I consider both problems in connection with",["The 'Allein wenn …' sentence is long; it is completed in the following fragments."]],"beweisgrund-0341":["the divine being, and recognise that the preference of choice which falls to one world over another, without a preference in the judgment of this very being who chooses, or even against this judgment,",["Line number '20' omitted; OCR 'po' dropped."]],"beweisgrund-0342":["would allow us to infer a defect in the agreement of its various active powers",["'abnehmen lasse' (from the following lines) is taken as the governing verb: 'would allow us to infer'. 'Kräfte' rendered 'powers'."]],"beweisgrund-0343":["and a different relation of its activity without a proportioned difference in the grounds, and hence a fault in the most perfect being, then I conclude with great conviction that the cases proposed must be fictitious and impossible.",["The editorial sign | in 'proportio— | nirte' is a page/line break in the apparatus; since the word is joined here ('proportioned') it is not reproduced. Line number '25' omitted; 'Übelftand' read 'Übelstand' (fault); 'Über- Zeugung' read 'Überzeugung'."]],"beweisgrund-0344":["For I understand, from the whole of the preparations that have been seen, that there is much less ground for inferring, from presupposed possibilities which one nevertheless cannot sufficiently confirm, to a necessary conduct of the most perfect being (a conduct of such a kind that it seems to diminish the concept of the greatest harmony in him), than for inferring to possibility from the recognised harmony which the possibilities of things",["Line number '30' omitted. Broken off; the sentence continues in the next passage, where the inference is 'from what is recognised as most fitting to this being'. 'Betragen' rendered 'conduct'; 'nothwendig' 'necessary'."]],"beweisgrund-0345":["must have with the divine nature, from that which is recognised as most fitting to this being. [There can be] [such] relations as would have to contain a ground of embarrassment in the rational choice of the supreme being; for this very supreme being contains the ultimate ground of all this possibility, in which therefore never anything can be found other than what harmonises with its origin.",["The OCR is corrupt here: 'D am anständigsten' is read 'dem am anständigsten', and the line 'jorhe Verhältnisse sein können, …' is garbled ('jorhe' probably 'solche'). The text as printed is 'keine solche Verhältnisse sein können' in the Academy text, I suspect, but the negation is not in the OCR; I have therefore bracketed the supplied words and flag the reading as doubtful. The sense required by the argument is that no such relations can exist.","'fare monirt' read 'harmonirt'. The apparatus signs | are line breaks; omitted where words are joined."]],"beweisgrund-0346":["This concept of divine all-sufficiency, extended over everything possible and actual, is also a far more correct expression for designating the greatest perfection of this being than that of the infinite, which is commonly used. For although the latter may be interpreted as one will, it is in its proper meaning plainly mathematical. It denotes the relation of one magnitude to another as its measure, a relation which is greater than every number. Hence in the strict sense of the word divine knowledge would be entitled infinite in so far as, compared with some supposed other knowledge, it stands in a relation which exceeds every possible number. Now since such a comparison places divine determinations in a homogeneity with those of created things which cannot well be maintained, and moreover does not directly convey what is intended by it, namely the undiminished possession of all perfection, everything that can be thought in this connection is found, on the contrary, brought together in the expression 'all-sufficiency'. The name of infinity is nevertheless beautiful and properly aesthetic. The extension beyond all concepts of number moves the soul and throws it into astonishment by a certain embarrassment. The expression we recommend, on the other hand, is better suited to logical correctness.",["OCR 'Unend— lichen', 'Erfenntniß', 'Erkenntniß' read 'Erkenntnis' = 'knowledge'; 'mög— liebe' = 'mögliche'; 'Bestim— mungen' = Bestimmungen, 'determinations'.","The long sentence beginning 'Da nun eine solche Vergleichung' is kept as one sentence in English; 'findet sich … beisammen' rendered 'is found … brought together'. 'Seele' rendered 'soul'."]],"beweisgrund-0347":["Third Section.",["'Abtheilung' rendered 'Section'."]],"beweisgrund-0348":["In which it is shown that, apart from the proof-ground [Beweisgrund] set out, no other is possible for a demonstration of the existence",["Heading; continues in the next two fragments. 'Beweisgrund' rendered 'ground of proof' / 'proof-ground'."]],"beweisgrund-0349":["...ground [Grunde] no other [is possible] for a demonstration of the existence",["The passage is a fragment beginning mid-word (\"grunde\", presumably the ending of a compound such as *Beweisgrunde* or a dative of *Grund*) and breaking off mid-sentence; I translate only what is there and do not complete it.","\"[is possible]\" is supplied in brackets: the German has no verb, and the sense (\"no other ... for a demonstration\") requires one. Another supplement, e.g. \"[is available]\", would serve equally well.","\"Demonstration\" is kept as the English cognate of the German word (Latin *demonstratio*); \"Dasein\" is rendered \"existence\".","\"grunde\" is given as \"ground\" with the German in brackets because the grammatical form (singular or plural, case) cannot be settled from the fragment."]],"beweisgrund-0350":["of God.",[]],"beweisgrund-0351":["Division of all possible grounds of proof of the existence",["OCR 'GCintheilung' read 'Eintheilung'."]],"beweisgrund-0352":["of God.",[]],"beweisgrund-0353":["The conviction of the great truth, There is a God, if it is to have the highest degree of mathematical certainty, has this peculiarity, that it can be attained only by a single path, and",["Breaks off mid-sentence. OCR 'ste' read 'sie', 'so Eigne' read 'die Eigne' — i.e. 'this peculiarity'; 'wenn | ste' joined."]],"beweisgrund-0354":["gives to this consideration the advantage that philosophical endeavours | must unite in a single ground of proof, in order rather to correct the faults which may have crept into its execution than to reject it, as soon as one is convinced that no choice among several such grounds is possible. | To demonstrate this, I remind the reader that he must not lose sight of the demand which properly has to be satisfied. It is to prove, not the existence of a very great and very perfect first cause, but of the most high being; not the existence of one or of several such beings, but of a single one; and this not through mere",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished. The signs | are kept where the OCR text has them; the stray ')' after 'sich' is silently corrected and 'set' read as 'sei'.","'zu erfüllen ist' is rendered 'has to be satisfied', and the sentence is divided at the colon. 'Dasein' is rendered 'existence'. 'allerhöchsten Wesens' is rendered 'most high being'. 'mehfo reren' is read as 'mehreren'."]],"beweisgrund-0355":[". All grounds of proof for the existence of God can be derived only either from",["A fragment that breaks off in mid-sentence; the sentence continues in the next passage. The initial full stop is the OCR's own."]],"beweisgrund-0356":["the concepts of the understanding [Verstandsbegriffen] of the merely possible, or from the concept, drawn from experience, of what exists [Erfahrungsbegriffe des Existirenden]. In the former case the inference is made either from the possible as a ground to the existence of God as a consequence, or from the possible as a consequence to the divine existence as a ground. In the second case the inference is again of two kinds. Either we infer merely the existence of a first and independent cause from that whose existence we experience, and then, by analysis of this concept, infer its divine attributes. Or both the existence and the attributes of such a being are inferred immediately from what experience teaches.",["The OCR 'Erfahrungs— begriffe' (with line-break dash) is silently joined. 'Erfahrungsbegriff' is rendered 'concept drawn from experience' rather than 'empirical concept' so as to keep the contrast with the 'Verstandsbegriffen'; this is a choice.","The long second sentence is divided into three, and 'Im zweiten Falle … wiederum entweder … oder' is made explicit as a twofold division."]],"beweisgrund-0357":["Examination of the Grounds of Proof of the First Kind.",["OCR 'eriten' read as 'ersten'. A preceding section number (presumably 2.) is not present in the passage."]],"beweisgrund-0358":["If existence is to be inferred, as a consequence, from the concept of the merely possible as a ground, then the existence in question must be capable of being found in this concept by analysis; for there is no other derivation of a consequence from",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; the next passage supplies 'a concept of the possible …'. The stray OCR signs 'ıs' (probably a line number) are dropped.","'Zergliederung' is rendered 'analysis'. 'Folge' is rendered 'consequence', and 'Grund' 'ground'."]],"beweisgrund-0359":["a concept of the possible than by logical resolution. In that case, however, existence would have to be contained in the possible as a predicate. Since, according to the first consideration of the first Section, this can never be the case, it is evident that a proof of the truth of which we are speaking is impossible in the manner mentioned.",["Begins in mid-sentence, continuing the preceding passage. The stray OCR fragments 'en' (at the start) and 'able' (at the end) are treated as noise and omitted; this is doubtful, since the OCR is corrupt at these points.","'Betrachtung' is rendered 'consideration'. The reference is to the first Section's first Consideration of the work."]],"beweisgrund-0360":["We have, however, a famous proof which is built upon this ground, namely the so-called Cartesian proof. One begins by forming a concept of a possible thing in which one represents to oneself all true perfection as united. One next assumes that existence also is a perfection of things; and so one infers the existence of a most perfect being from its possibility. In the same way one could infer the existence of anything whatever from the concept of it, provided it is represented merely as the most perfect of its kind; for instance, from the mere fact that a most perfect world is thinkable, one could infer that it exists. Without entering upon a detailed refutation of this proof, such as one already meets with in others, I appeal only to what was explained at the beginning of this work, namely that existence is not a predicate at all, and therefore not a predicate of perfection either. Hence, from a definition [Erklärung] which contains an arbitrary union of various predicates in order to constitute the concept of some possible thing, it is impossible ever to infer the existence of this thing, and consequently also not the existence of God.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'sich)' to 'sich', 'set' to 'sei', and the stray 'so welche man schon bei andern antrifft' read as 'such as one already meets with in others' (the 'so' is probably an OCR error for 'solche'). The stray 'as' and the dash in 'daß — eine' are dropped.","'Erklärung' is rendered 'definition' because the context concerns an arbitrary combination of predicates to constitute a concept. The long final sentence is divided in two."]],"beweisgrund-0361":["The inference from the possibilities of things, as consequences, to the existence of God as a ground is, on the other hand, of an entirely different kind. Here the inquiry is whether, for something to be possible, something existent must not be presupposed; and whether that existence, without which there would be no internal possibility at all, does not contain such attributes as we unite together in the concept of the Deity. In this case it is clear, to begin with, that I cannot infer an existence from conditioned possibility unless I presuppose the existence of that which is possible only under certain conditions. For conditioned possibility gives us to understand merely that something can exist only in certain connections; and the existence of the cause is demonstrated only in so far as the consequence exists. Here, however, the existence of the cause is not to be inferred from the existence of the consequence. Such a proof, therefore, if it is to be found at all, can be conducted only from internal possibility. Further, it is to be observed that it must spring from the absolute possibility of all things in general. For what is to be known is that internal possibility itself presupposes some existence, and not that the particular predicates by which one possible thing is distinguished from another do so; for the difference of predicates is found also in the merely possible, and never indicates anything existent. Accordingly, on the method mentioned, a divine existence would have to be inferred from the internal possibility of everything thinkable. That this can be done has been shown in the whole of the first Section of this work.",["The sentence 'das Dasein der Ursache wird nur in so fern dargethan … hier aber soll sie nicht aus dem Dasein derselben geschlossen werden' is ambiguous in its pronouns; I resolve 'sie' as the existence of the cause and 'derselben' as the consequence, and I repeat the nouns in English.","'Folgen' is rendered 'consequences', 'Grund' 'ground', 'innere Möglichkeit' 'internal possibility', and 'Gottheit' 'Deity'. The OCR 'Existiren— des', 'Ver: knüpfungen' and 'Denk— lichen' are silently joined. Several longer sentences are divided."]],"beweisgrund-0362":["3.\nExamination of the Grounds of Proof of the Second Kind.",["The numeral '3.' is kept as the OCR gives it."]],"beweisgrund-0363":["The proof which seeks to pass from concepts, drawn from experience, of what is, to the existence of a first and independent cause according to the rules of causal inference, and from this, by logical analysis of the concept, to those attributes of it which signify a Deity, is famous, and has been brought into great repute especially by the school of the Wolffian philosophers. Nevertheless it is wholly impossible. I grant that everything has been regularly inferred up to the proposition, 'if anything is, then something also exists which depends on no other thing'. I admit, therefore, that the existence of one or more things which are no further effects of another has been well established. The second step, to the proposition that this independent thing is absolutely necessary, is already much less reliable, since it has to be taken by means of the principle of sufficient reason, which is still contested. But I have no scruple in subscribing to everything thus far. There exists, then, something in an absolutely necessary manner. From this concept of the absolutely necessary being its attributes of highest perfection and unity are now to be derived. The concept of absolute necessity which lies at the basis here can, however, be taken in two ways, as was shown in the first Section. In the first way, which we have called logical necessity, it would have to be shown that the opposite of that thing in which all perfection or reality is to be found contradicts itself; and that therefore that being alone is absolutely necessary in its existence all of whose predicates are truly affirmative. And since, from this same thoroughgoing union of all reality in one being, it is to be inferred that this being is a single one, it is clear that the analysis of the concept of the necessary will rest on such grounds as would also enable me to infer conversely: that in which all reality is, exists necessarily. Now not only is this kind of inference impossible according to the preceding number; it is also especially noteworthy that in this way the proof is not built upon the concept drawn from experience at all, which is presupposed entirely without being used. Like the Cartesian proof, it rests solely on concepts, in which one supposes that one can find the existence of a being in the identity or the conflict of predicates.†",["'Gaufaljd)lüfje' is a garbled OCR reading; I take it as 'Kausalschlüsse' and render 'causal inference'. This is doubtful.","'unter(schreiben)': the OCR 'untere schreiben' is read as 'unterschreiben', 'to subscribe to'. The very long first and later sentences are divided into several, as the method allows; 'Erfahrungsbegriff' is again 'concept drawn from experience'.","'nach der vorigen Nummer' is rendered 'according to the preceding number', a reference to the preceding numbered section. 'Vereinbarung' is rendered 'union'.","The final '“)' in the OCR is a footnote marker, rendered here as '†'; the footnote text is not in the passage."]],"beweisgrund-0364":["This is the chief point at which I am here aiming. Suppose I place the necessity of a concept in this, that its opposite is self-contradictory, and then assert that the infinite is so constituted. In that case it was quite unnecessary to presuppose the existence of the necessary being, since that existence already follows from the concept of the infinite. Indeed, the existence thus presupposed is wholly idle in the proof itself. For in the course of the proof the concepts of necessity and of infinity are regarded as reciprocal concepts [*Wechselbegriffe*], and it is therefore in fact for this reason that from the",["The passage opens with a stray ')' and has a further stray ')' after 'sich'; both are OCR residue and are dropped. 'set' is read as 'sei', 'unndthig' as 'unnöthig'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands.","Restated as several sentences: the German 'Wenn ich … so war es …' is made a supposition followed by its consequence."]],"beweisgrund-0365":["It is not my intention to analyse the proofs themselves which are to be met with in various writers who follow this method. It is easy to expose their infer—",["Breaks off at 'Schl—'; completed in the next passage as 'Schlüsse'. I render 'infer—' and leave the word incomplete.","'Schlüsse' is rendered 'inferences' (not 'fallacies'), since the German does not say that they are faulty until the next sentence."]],"beweisgrund-0366":["—ences, and this has already been done in part by others. Since, however, it might still be hoped that their defect could be remedied by some improvements, our consideration shows that, whatever may become of them, they can never be anything other than inferences from concepts of possible things, and never inferences from experience. They are therefore at most to be reckoned with the proofs of the first kind.",["Begins mid-word ('schlüsse'), continuing the previous passage. 'konnen' is read as 'können'.","'Dieses ist auch schon zum Theil von andern geschehen' is taken to refer to the exposure of the inferences, as the context requires."]],"beweisgrund-0367":["As regards the second proof, that in which the existence of God and at the same time his attributes are inferred from concepts of experience of existing things, the situation is quite different. This proof is not only possible; it is in every way worthy of being brought to its due perfection by united effort. The things of the world which present themselves to our senses show clear marks of their contingency. Through the magnitude, the order and the purposive arrangements which are everywhere observed, they also furnish evidences of a rational author of great wisdom, power and goodness. The great unity in so vast a whole allows us to infer that there is only one single author of all these things. Even though no geometrical rigour shows itself in any of these inferences, they nevertheless contain, beyond dispute, so much force that, by rules which natural and sound understanding follows, they leave every reasonable man not for a moment in doubt on the matter.",["'hiemit', 'offenbären' and 'bez folgt' are read as 'hiermit', 'offenbaren' and 'befolgt' (OCR and hyphenation slips).","The long final sentence is divided into three, and the main clause is placed first for clarity. 'Beweisthümer' is rendered 'evidences'.","'gesunden Verstand' is rendered 'sound understanding' (*Verstand*)."]],"beweisgrund-0368":["4",["A bare section number, translated as it stands."]],"beweisgrund-0369":["There are, in general, only two proofs of the existence of God",["Heading; the sentence is completed by the next passage ('möglich' = 'possible')."]],"beweisgrund-0370":["possible.",["Final word of the heading begun in the previous passage."]],"beweisgrund-0371":["From all these considerations it is evident that, if we wish to infer from concepts of possible things, no other argument for the existence of God is possible than that in which the inner possibility of all things is itself regarded as something which presupposes some existence, as has been done by us in the first division of this work. [Existence of the necessary being inferred from infinity, because the infinite (and it alone) exists necessarily.] It is likewise evident that, if the inference is to ascend to this same truth from what experience teaches us of existing things, the proof can be conducted to the existence, and equally to the nature, of the supreme cause only through the properties perceived in the things of the world and through the contingent ordering of the world-whole. Let me be permitted to call the first proof the ontological, the second the cosmological.",["The sentence 'Existenz des Nothwendigen auf die Unendlichkeit geschlossen, weil …' has no finite verb and does not fit its context. It is probably corrupt or a remnant of the other method of proof. I translate what is there and supply 'being', 'inferred' and 'is' in brackets. The reading is doubtful.","'eriftirenden' is read as 'existirenden'.","'Beurtheilungen' is rendered 'considerations'. The long sentences are divided."]],"beweisgrund-0372":["This cosmological proof is, as it seems to me, as old as human reason. It is so natural and so persuasive, and its reflection is so greatly widened by the advance of our insight, that it must endure as long as there is any rational creature who wishes to share in the noble contemplation of knowing God from his works. The labours of Derham, Nieuwentyt and many others have done honour to human reason in this respect, although at times much vanity has crept in, in giving all manner of physical insights, or even chimeras, a venerable appearance by means of the watchword of religious zeal. For all this excellence, this kind of proof is nevertheless incapable of mathematical certainty and exactness. We shall always be able to infer only some incomprehensibly great author of that whole which presents itself to our senses, but never the existence of the most perfect of all possible beings. It will be in the highest degree probable that there is only a single first author. But this conviction will lack much of that thoroughness which would defy the boldest scepticism. The reason is that we cannot infer more or greater properties in the cause than we find just necessary for understanding the degree and nature of the effects, so long, that is, as we have for judging of the existence of this cause no other occasion than that which the effects give us. Now we recognise much perfection, magnitude and order in the world, and from this we can infer, with logical precision, no more than that its cause must possess much understanding, power and goodness, but by no means that it knows all things, is capable of all things, etc. The world is an immeasurable whole in which we perceive unity and thoroughgoing connection, and we may with good reason judge from this that there is only one author of it. But we must be content to admit that we do not know everything created, and we therefore judge that what is known to us reveals only one au—",["Breaks off at 'Ure' (= 'Urheber', continued in the next passage); rendered 'au—'.","OCR repaired: 'Rez ligionseifers' = 'Religionseifers'; 'Überzeu, gnug' = 'Überzeugung'; 'trozter­mangeln' read as 'trotzt, ermangeln'; 'vermöge 1c. etc.' read as 'vermöge etc.' ('is capable of'); the reading of this last phrase is doubtful.","Several long sentences are divided for clarity. 'Nachdenken' is rendered 'reflection'; 'Einsichten' as 'insight'."]],"beweisgrund-0373":["Third Section. That no other ground of proof is possible.",["This is a running head (section title and page number 161), which the job says to leave out. I give the title only for the sake of completeness; the page number is omitted."]],"beweisgrund-0374":["—thor [*Urheber*], from which we conjecture that what is also not known to us | will be of like constitution. This is indeed reasonably thought, but it is not a strict inference. | On the other hand, unless we flatter ourselves too much, our own ontological proof, as sketched, appears to be capable of that rigour which is demanded in a demonstration.",["Begins mid-word ('Heber', for 'Urheber'); the German divides the word across the page. The Academy line-break signs are kept where they stand.","'ter vernünftig' is garbled OCR, probably 'wohl vernünftig'; I render 'reasonably'. 'feint' is read as 'scheint', and 'schlieft' as 'schließt'.","The sentence 'Dagegen … so scheint unser' continues in the next passage; I have joined the translation so that the English reads connectedly."]],"beweisgrund-0375":["Yet if the question were, which of the two is on the whole the better, the answer would be this. So far as logical exactness and completeness are concerned, it is the ontological; but if what is demanded is comprehensibility to ordinary sound conception, vividness of impression, beauty, and power to move the moral springs of human nature, then the preference must be conceded to the cosmological proof. And since it is without doubt of greater importance to quicken men with lofty feel—",["The opening 'entworfener ontologische Beweis …' is rendered in the previous passage; here the German of this passage begins with 'entworfener'. I have not translated that phrase twice.","Breaks off at 'Empfindun⸗' ('Empfindungen'); rendered 'feel—'. Here *Empfindung* means feeling or sentiment, not 'sensation' in the technical sense.","'gemeinen richtigen Begriff' is rendered 'ordinary sound conception'; the phrase is somewhat loose. The sentence is divided at 'verlangt man aber'."]],"beweisgrund-0376":["—ings which are fruitful in noble activity, while at the same time convincing the sound understanding, than to instruct him with carefully weighed inferences of reason, whereby speculation is satisfied, it follows, if one wishes to proceed sincerely, that the well-known cosmological proof cannot be denied the advantage of the wider usefulness.",["Begins mid-word ('gen'). 'der seinem Speculation' is garbled OCR; I read it as 'der Speculation' (dative: 'speculation is satisfied'). The reading is doubtful.","'gesunden Verstand' (here printed 'gefunden') is rendered 'sound understanding'; 'Vernunftschlüsse' as 'inferences of reason'."]],"beweisgrund-0377":["It is accordingly no flattering artifice courting the applause of others, but sincerity, when I gladly concede the advantage of usefulness, over every other proof in which more regard has been had to logical rigour, and over my own, to such an exposition of the important knowledge of God and his attributes as Reimarus supplies in his book on natural religion. For, quite apart from the worth of this and other writings of that man, which consists chiefly in an unaffected use of sound and fine reason, grounds of this kind do in fact possess great probative force and arouse more intuition [*Anschauung*] than concepts logically abstracted, although the latter give a more exact understanding of the object.",["'Erwä gnug' is read as 'Erwägung' ('Erwägung ziehen' = take into consideration). 'gefunden' is read as 'gesunden'.","*Anschauung* is rendered 'intuition' by the fixed term list, although Kant here seems to use it loosely for vivid apprehension; the reading may be a misprint for 'Überzeugung'. I flag this as doubtful.","The first sentence is rearranged: the main clause is placed first and the 'Erkenntniß von Gott' clause carries the comparison. *Erkenntniß* = 'knowledge'."]],"beweisgrund-0378":["Nevertheless, an enquiring understanding, once it has fallen on the track of investigation, is not satisfied until everything about it is light and until, if I may so express myself, the circle which bounds its question is completely closed. No one will therefore hold an effort such as the present, devoted to logical exactness in so very important a branch of knowledge, to be useless or superfluous, particularly since there are many cases in which, without such care, the application of its concepts would remain uncertain and doubtful.",["'Erkenntnisse' is rendered 'branch of knowledge' for smoothness; strictly the German is 'in einem so sehr wichtigen Erkenntnisse' (a most important mode of knowledge).","'Sorgfält' is read as 'Sorgfalt'. The long sentence is divided into two; 'seiner Begriffe' is rendered 'its concepts', the antecedent being the understanding."]],"beweisgrund-0379":["5.\nOnly a single demonstration",["The heading and the sentence are split across ids 0379–0381 and are translated piecemeal: 'Es ist nicht mehr als eine einzige Demonstration vom Dasein Gottes möglich, wovon der Beweisgrund oben gegeben worden.'"]],"beweisgrund-0380":["of the existence of God is possible, of which the ground of proof has above",["Fragment, completed in the next id. *Beweisgrund* = 'ground of proof'; *Dasein* = 'existence'."]],"beweisgrund-0381":["been given.",[]],"beweisgrund-0382":["From what has been said it is evident that, of the four conceivable grounds of proof, which we have reduced to two main kinds, the Cartesian is false and altogether impossible, and so too is that which proceeds from the empirical concept of existence by way of the analysis of the concept of an independent thing. That is to say, they do not prove with a lesser degree of rigour: they do not prove at all. It has further been shown that the proof which infers from the properties of the things of the world to the existence and properties of the Deity contains a sound and very beautiful ground of proof, but that it is never capable of the rigour of a demonstration. Nothing now remains but that either no strict proof of this is possible at all, or that it must rest upon the ground of proof which we have indicated above. Since what is in question is the possibility of a proof absolutely, no one will assert the former, and the conclusion follows in accordance with what we have indicated.\n\nThere is only one God, and only one ground of proof through which it is possible to see his existence with the perception of that necessity which utterly annuls every opposite. This is a judgment to which the very constitution of the object might lead us directly. All other things, whatever exist, might also not be. The experience of contingent things can therefore furnish no sound ground of proof for knowing the existence of that being of which it is impossible that he should not be. The difference between his existence and that of other things lies solely in this, that the denial of the divine existence is sheer nothing. Inner possibility, the essences of things, are that the removal of which destroys everything thinkable. In this, therefore, the peculiar mark of the existence of the Being of all beings must consist. Here seek the proof; and if you do not think to find it there, then turn from this untrodden footpath to the great highway of human reason. It is absolutely necessary that one should be convinced of the existence of God; but it is not equally necessary that one should demonstrate it. °",["Restated the long second sentence and the series of short clauses as separate sentences; sense unchanged.","OCR: 'bez stehen' read as 'bestehen'; 'Beweisthum' rendered 'proof'. 'pr die große Heeresstraße' is corrupt; 'von … auf/zu die große Heeresstraße' is presumed, and I render 'to the great highway'. Doubtful.","The closing '°' is retained as it stands; it is probably a footnote or note-marker whose text is not in the passage.","'Erfahrungsbegriff vom Dasein' = 'empirical concept of existence'; 'Schärfe' = 'rigour'; 'Wesen' = 'essences' (or 'beings' in 'Wesen aller Wesen', where I keep 'Being of all beings')."]],"beweisgrund-0383":["End.",[]],"negative-groessen-0001":["Attempt\nto Introduce the Concept\nof Negative Magnitudes\ninto Philosophy\nby\n| M. Immanuel Kant.",["OCR 'Serfu d' (ornamental or garbled 'Versuch') is read as 'Versuch, ' i.e. 'Attempt'.","*Weltweisheit* = 'philosophy' (lit. 'world-wisdom'); *Größen* = 'magnitudes', the term I use for negative *Größen* throughout, in the manner of Kemp Smith."]],"negative-groessen-0002":["Preface.",[]],"negative-groessen-0003":["The use which can be made of mathematics in philosophy consists either in the imitation of its method, or in the actual application of its propositions to the objects of philosophy. It is not evident that the former has hitherto been of any benefit, however great the advantage which was at first promised from it. The high-sounding titles with which philosophical propositions were adorned, out of jealousy of geometry, have also gradually fallen away; for it was modestly recognised that it ill becomes one in middling circumstances to put on a defiant air, and that the *non liquet* would in no way yield to all this display. | The second use, on the other hand, has proved all the more advantageous for those parts of philosophy which it has affected. By turning the doctrines of mathematics to their service, these have raised themselves to a height to which otherwise they could have laid no claim. But these are only the insights belonging to natural science, unless one should wish to count the logic of expectations in games of chance as belonging to philosophy as well. As for metaphysics, so",["OCR 'Der zweite Gebrauch)' has a stray bracket (perhaps a footnote mark); I ignore it. 'i non liquet' read as '*non liquet*'.","Divided the long first and second sentences; 'Einsichten' = 'insights'; 'Naturlehre' = 'natural science'.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('so' …) and is translated as it stands."]],"negative-groessen-0004":["this science, instead of making use of any of the concepts or doctrines of mathematics, has rather often armed itself against them. Where it might perhaps have borrowed secure foundations on which to base its reflections, we see it concerned to make of the mathematician's concepts nothing but fictions, which have little truth outside his own field. It is easy to guess on which side the advantage will lie in a conflict between two sciences, of which the one surpasses all others in certainty and distinctness, while the other is only now beginning to strive to attain them. |",["The opening is garbled by OCR ('at … er Mathematik'): read as '[h]at diese Wissenschaft … [d]er Mathematik'. The words 'so hat' are supplied from the sense of the previous fragment.","'sich)' read as 'sich'. Restated as three sentences."]],"negative-groessen-0005":["Metaphysics seeks, for example, to discover the nature of space and the supreme | ground from which its possibility can be understood. Now nothing can be more helpful to this end than if one can borrow from somewhere reliably proved",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next id."]],"negative-groessen-0006":["*data*, to lay them at the foundation of one's reflection. Geometry supplies some of these, which concern the most general properties of space, for example that space does not consist of simple parts at all. But one passes them by and puts one's confidence solely in the ambiguous consciousness of this concept, by thinking it in a quite abstract manner. When speculation, following this procedure, will not agree with the propositions of mathematics, one seeks to save one's artificial concept by reproaching this science, as if the concepts it lays at its foundation had not been abstracted from the true nature of space but arbitrarily devised. The mathematical consideration of motion, combined with the knowledge of space, likewise furnishes many *data* for keeping the metaphysical consideration of time in the path of truth. The celebrated Herr Euler has, among others, given some occasion for this;*) but it seems more convenient to remain among obscure abstractions that are hard to test than to enter into connection with a science which has a share only in intelligible and evident insights.",["'ex Data' is an OCR garble; I read '*data*' (Latin term retained in italics) and take 'ex' as a stray.","'Bezgriffe', 'is abgezogen', 'bez rühmte' etc.: OCR slips, silently corrected; line numbers omitted. The footnote marker '*)' is kept in place.","'Erkenntniß des Raumes' = 'knowledge of space'. Divided the sentences for clarity."]],"negative-groessen-0007":["*) Histoire de l'Acad. Royale des sc. et belles lettr. l'ann. 1748.",["Footnote to the Euler reference in the preceding passage."]],"negative-groessen-0008":["The concept of the infinitely small, to which mathematics so often comes, is rejected with presumptuous boldness, straight off, as fictitious; whereas one should rather suppose that one does not yet understand enough of it to pass judgment upon it. Nature itself, nevertheless, seems to give not indistinct evidence that this concept is very true. For if there are forces which act continuously through a time in order to produce motions, as gravity, to all appearance, is, then the force which it exerts at the initial moment, or at rest, must be infinitely small as compared with that | which it imparts in a period of time. It is difficult, I confess, to penetrate into the nature of these concepts; but this",["Breaks off mid-sentence ('aber diese …'); continued in the next id. 'Kräfte' = 'forces'; 'Schwere' = 'gravity'."]],"negative-groessen-0009":["difficulty can at most justify only the caution of uncertain conjectures, but not decisive pronouncements of impossibility. | For the present I have the intention of considering, in relation to philosophy, a concept which is well enough known in mathematics but still very strange to philosophy. These reflections are only small beginnings, as is usual when one wishes to open up new prospects; but they may perhaps give occasion to important consequences. From the neglect of the concept of negative magnitudes a multitude of errors, and also misinterpretations of the opinions of others, have arisen in philosophy. If, for example, the celebrated Herr Dr. Crusius had been pleased to make the mathematicians' sense of this concept familiar [bekannt zu",["Running head ('Vorrede. 169') and line number '[5' omitted. 'noch)' and 'so oder auch)' are OCR slips, read 'noch' and 'oder auch' ('so' dropped as stray).","Breaks off mid-sentence: 'bekannt zu' is completed by 'machen' at the head of the next id; I bracket 'familiar' and the cut to show the join."]],"negative-groessen-0010":["machen], he would not have found Newton's comparison false to the point of Pe-",["Fragment: 'Pe-' is the first syllable of a word completed in the next id (see there). 'machen' completes 'bekannt zu machen' from the previous id."]],"negative-groessen-0011":["-wunderung [astonishment?]),*) in which he compares the attractive force, which with increased distance, yet still near the bodies, gradually degenerates into a repulsive one, with those series in which, where the",["The word 'Pe-|wunderung' is corrupt or badly OCR'd; the reading 'Verwunderung' ('astonishment') is a conjecture and is flagged as doubtful. The footnote marker '“)' is kept as '*)'.","'gu-rüdjtopenbe' read as 'zurückstoßende' ('repulsive'). The relative clause 'der … vergleicht' refers to Newton; I render it 'in which he compares' for smoothness.","Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0012":["positive quantities cease and the negative begin. For negative quantities are not negations of quantities, as the similarity of the expression has led people to suppose; they are something in themselves truly positive, only [positive in a way] opposed to the other. Thus negative attraction is not rest, as he takes it to be, but true repulsion.¹ But I now proceed to the treatise itself, in order to show what application this concept can have in philosophy [Weltweisheit] generally.\n\n¹ Crusius, *Naturlehre*, Part 2, § 295.",["The sentence begins mid-clause; translated as it stands. 'Positive in a way' is supplied in brackets to complete the elliptical 'nur was dem andern entgegengesetzt ist'.","OCR damage ('nega— tive', 'Zu- | rüdftopung' = Zurückstoßung, 'Mnwendung' = Anwendung, 'id)' = ich) silently corrected; the '|' is the Academy line-break sign, kept. The footnote marker '9)' is rendered ¹ and the footnote is placed at the end.","Weltweisheit rendered 'philosophy' with the German in brackets, since it is a choice."]],"negative-groessen-0013":["The concept of negative quantities has long been in use in mathematics, and is there also of the utmost importance. The notion which most people have formed of it, and the explanation they have given, are however strange and self-contradictory. No error in application has resulted from this, since special rules took the place of the definition and secured the use of the concept; and whatever mistake may have been made in judging the nature of this abstract concept remained idle and had no consequences. Perhaps no one in our day has shown more willingly and more precisely what is to be understood by negative quantities than the celebrated Professor Kästner,¹ in whose hands everything becomes exact, intelligible and agreeable. The reproach which he on this occasion levels at the mania for classification of a thoroughly abstract philosopher is much more general than is there expressed, and may be regarded as a challenge to test the powers of the affected acuteness of many thinkers upon a concept which is true and of manifest utility, in order to fix its character philosophically. Its correctness is already assured by mathematics. This is a case which false metaphysics gladly avoids, because here learned nonsense cannot so easily as elsewhere produce the illusion of thoroughness. In undertaking to procure for philosophy [Weltweisheit] the benefit of a concept as yet unused, though most necessary, I wish for no other judges than men of the kind of that man of universal insight whose writings give me the occasion for this. For as to the metaphysical intelligences of consummate insight, one would have to be very inexperienced to imagine that anything could still be added to their wisdom, or anything taken away from their delusion.\n\n¹ *Anfangsgründe der Arithmetik*, pp. 59–62.",["Restated the long fourth sentence ('Der Tadel ... auszuweichen') as two sentences, splitting before 'dessen Richtigkeit' and 'welches ein Fall ist'; 'welches' refers to the situation of a concept already secured by mathematics.","OCR slips corrected: 'auge— maten' = angemaßten (rendered 'affected'), 'brandy bären' = brauchbaren ('of manifest utility' is a loose rendering; literally 'serviceable'), 'Käst ner' = Kästner.","The footnote text, given separately as 0014, is placed here at its marker."]],"negative-groessen-0014":["¹ *Anfangsgründe der Arithmetik*, pp. 59–62.",["Footnote to the preceding passage; the marker is given as ¹."]],"negative-groessen-0015":["First Section.",[]],"negative-groessen-0016":["Explanation of the Concept of Negative Quantities in General.",[]],"negative-groessen-0017":["Things are opposed to one another when one of them cancels what is posited by the other. This opposition is twofold: it is either logical, through contradiction, or real, that is, without contradiction.",["'Aufheben' rendered 'cancel'; 'setzen' as 'posit'."]],"negative-groessen-0018":["The first opposition, namely the logical, is that to which one has hitherto directed one's attention solely and exclusively. It consists in this, that something is at once affirmed and denied of one and the same thing. The consequence of this logical connection is nothing at all (*nihil negativum irrepraesentabile*), as the principle of contradiction declares. A body in motion is something; a body that is not in motion is also something (*cogitabile*); but a body that were in motion and in precisely the same respect at the same time not in motion is nothing at all.\n\nThe second opposition, namely the real, is that in which two predicates of a thing are opposed, but not through the principle of contradiction. Here also the one cancels what is posited by the other; but the consequence is something (*cogitabile*). The moving force of a body toward one region, and an equal striving of the same body in the opposite direction, do not contradict one another, and are possible at once as predicates in one body. The consequence is rest, which is something (*repraesentabile*). Yet this is a true opposition. For what would be posited by the one tendency, if it were alone, is cancelled by the other, and both tendencies are genuine predicates of one and the same thing, belonging to it at the same time. The consequence of this too is nothing, but in another sense than in the case of contradiction (*nihil privativum, repraesentabile*). This nothing we shall henceforth call Zero = 0; its meaning is the same as that of negation (*negatio*), lack, absence, which are otherwise in use among philosophers [Weltweisen], only with a closer determination which will appear further on.",["Divided at 'Die zweite Opposition' (the original runs on after a marginal line-number '5'); OCR 'o' and 'Tens denzen' corrected.","'in eben demselben Verstande' rendered 'in precisely the same respect' (Verstand here = sense/respect, not the faculty).","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination'; 'Ahnlichkeit' etc. not in this passage. 'Mnwendung'-type OCR slips corrected silently, e.g. 'besten' = dessen, 'Mbwesenheit' = Abwesenheit."]],"negative-groessen-0019":["In logical repugnance regard is had only to the relation through which the predicates of a thing, and their consequences, cancel one another by contradiction. Which of the two is truly affirmative (*realitas*) and which truly negative (*negatio*) is not at all attended to here. For example, to be dark and not dark at once, in one and the same sense, is a contradiction in the same subject. The former predicate is logically affirmative, the latter logically negative, although the former is, in the metaphysical sense, a negation.\n\nReal repugnance likewise rests on a relation of two predicates of one and the same thing to each other; but this relation is of quite another kind. By one of them that is not denied which is affirmed by the other, for that is impossible; both predicates, A and B, are affirmative. But since the consequences of each taken separately would be a and b, neither the one nor the other results from the two together in one subject, and the consequence is therefore Zero. Suppose someone has a claim against another, an active debt A = 100 thalers; this is a ground of an equal receipt. But suppose the same man also has a passive debt B = 100 thalers; this is a ground of paying out as much. Both debts together are a ground of Zero, that is, of neither paying nor receiving money. It is easily seen that this Zero is a relative nothing, in that only a certain consequence is not, as in this case a certain capital, and in the case cited above a certain motion, is not; whereas in cancellation through contradiction there is absolutely nothing. Hence the *nihil negativum* cannot be expressed by Zero = 0, for this contains no contradiction. It can be thought that a certain motion is not; but that it should at once be and not be cannot be thought at all.",["Divided at 'Die Realrepugnanz' for clarity; the 'Activschuld/Passivschuld' sentences kept close to the text, 'Rthlr.' written 'thalers'.","'Verstande' again rendered 'sense'; 'metaphysischen Verstande' = 'metaphysical sense'.","'Durch eines derselben ist dasjenige nicht verneint...' rendered closely, 'By one of them that is not denied which is affirmed by the other'; the construction is elliptical.","OCR corrected: 'Einnähme' = Einnahme, 'ausgedrückt?' = ausgedrückt."]],"negative-groessen-0020":["Mathematicians now make use of the concepts of this real opposition in dealing with their quantities, and to indicate it they mark the quantities with + and —. Since every such opposition is reciprocal, it is easily seen that the one cancels the other either wholly or in part, without those before which + stands being on that account distinguished from those before which — stands. Let a ship sail from Portugal to Brazil. Let all the distances it makes with the morning wind be marked with +, and those it covers [w]ith the evening wind with —. The numbers themselves are to signify leagues. Thus the voyage over seven days is + 12 + 7 — 3 — 5 + 8 = 19 leagues, which is the distance it has made towards the west. The quantities before which — stands have this sign merely as a mark of opposition, in so far as they are to be taken together with those which have + before them. If, however, they stand in connection with those before which there is likewise —, no opposition any longer obtains, since opposition is a relation of contrariety which is found only between + and —. And since subtraction is a cancelling which takes place when opposed quantities are taken together, it is clear that the — cannot properly be a sign of subtraction, as it is commonly represented, but that + and — together first designate a subtracting. Hence — 4 — 5 = — 9 was no subtraction at all, but an actual increase and putting together of quantities of one and the same kind. But + 9 — 5 = + 4 signifies a subtracting, since the signs of opposition indicate that the one cancels in the other as much as is equal to itself. Similarly, the sign + by itself properly signifies no addition, but does so only in so far as the quantity before which it stands is to be combined with another before which + also stands, or is thought as standing. If, however, it is to be taken together with one before which — stands, this cannot happen otherwise than by means of opposition; and then the sign + as well as the sign — signifies a subtraction, namely that one quantity cancels in the other as much as is equal to itself, as in — 9 + 4 = — 5. For this reason the sign — in the case — 9 — 4 = — 13 signifies no subtraction, but an addition just as much as the sign + does in the example + 9 + 4 = + 13. For in general, in so far as the signs are the same, the things designated must simply be summed; in so far as they are different, they can be taken together only through an opposition, that is, by means of subtraction. Accordingly these two signs serve in the science of quantity only to distinguish those quantities which are opposed to one another, that is, which wholly or in part cancel one another when taken together. The purpose is, first, that one may recognize this relation of opposition from them; and secondly, that, after one has been subtracted from the other from which it could be subtracted, one may know to which of the two kinds of quantity the *Facit* (remainder) belongs. Thus in the case mentioned above one would arrive at the same result if the course with the east wind had been marked by — and the voyage with the west wind by +, except that the *Facit* would then have had — as its sign.",["OCR slips silently corrected: '[o es' read as 'so es' / 'mit dem Abendwinde' context—but the bracket is retained as '[w]ith' only to mark the doubtful reading; the German is 'die, [o es durch den Abendwind zurücklegt', apparently 'die, so es durch…'. Reading doubtful.","'+ 9 — 5 — 4' in the OCR corrected to '+ 9 — 5 = + 4' and '-— 9' to '= — 9'; '+ 9+ 4 — ＋ 13' to '+ 9 + 4 = + 13', as the argument requires. 'ge— dacht' read as 'gedacht'.","'in sieben Lagen' rendered 'over seven days' on the reading 'Tagen' (OCR 'Lagen'); doubtful. 'Weilen' = leagues (Meilen), likewise an OCR/old-spelling slip.","'Facit' kept in Latin italics, glossed 'remainder'. 'Gegenverhältniß' = 'relation of contrariety'; 'Entgegensetzung' = 'opposition'; 'Abziehung' = 'subtracting'.","Long sentences divided and 'Und da … so ist klar' smoothed; 'die Zeichen einerlei sind' made 'the signs are the same'."]],"negative-groessen-0021":["From this arises the mathematical concept of negative quantities. A quantity is negative in relation to another in so far as it cannot be taken together with it otherwise than through opposition, namely, in such a way that the one cancels in the other as much as is equal to itself. This is, to be sure, a reciprocal relation, and quantities thus opposed to one another cancel in each other an equal amount, so that strictly no quantity can be called negative absolutely, but one must say that + a and — a are, each, the negative quantity of the other. Since, however, this can always be understood as tacitly added in thought, mathematicians have once for all adopted the practice of calling those quantities before which the — stands negative quantities. In so doing one must nevertheless not lose sight of the fact that this designation indicates, not a special kind of things in respect of their inner constitution, but this relation of contrariety, namely that of being taken together, in an opposition, with certain other things which are marked by +.",["'set' in the OCR corrected to 'sei'; 'Sinne kann hinzugedacht werden' rendered 'understood as tacitly added in thought'.","Last sentence restructured for English; 'Gegenverhältniß' = 'relation of contrariety'."]],"negative-groessen-0022":["In order to extract from this concept what is properly the object [*Gegenstand*] for philosophy, without paying particular regard to quantity, we first remark that it contains the opposition which we above entitled real. Let + 8 be assets [*Capitalien*] and — 8 liabilities; it is then no contradiction that both belong to one person. Meanwhile the one cancels an equal amount which was posited by the other, and the result is zero. I shall accordingly call debts negative assets. By this, however, I shall not mean that they are negations or mere denials of assets; for in that case they would themselves have zero as their sign, and assets and debts together would give the value of the possessions as 8 + 0 = 8, which is false. I mean rather that debts are positive grounds of the diminution of assets. Since now this whole mode of designation indicates at all times only the relation of certain things to one another, apart from which the concept at once ceases, it would be absurd on that account [the passage breaks off here; the remainder is OCR debris].",["The OCR ends in garbage ('EEE — —— A EEE') after 'darum'; not translated. The bracketed remark marks the break, and the sentence is left unfinished.","'Gegen— stand' read as 'Gegenstand'; 'sich)' as 'sich'. 'Capitalien' rendered 'assets' for the contrast with 'Passivschulden' (liabilities); 'Capital' retained in the German tag.","'die Folge ist Zero' rendered 'the result is zero'; 'Zero' left as zero."]],"negative-groessen-0023":["a particular kind of things) and to entitle them negative things; for even the mathematicians' expression 'negative quantities' is not precise enough. For negative things would mean negations (*negationes*) in general, and that is not at all the concept which we wish to establish. It is enough that we have already explained the counter-relations [*Gegenverhältnisse*] which make up this whole concept, and which consist in real opposition. In order, however, to indicate at once in the expressions that the one of the opposed terms is not the contradictory opposite of the other, and, if the latter is something positive, that the former is not a mere negation of it but, as we shall presently see, is opposed to it as something affirmative, we shall, following the method of the mathematicians, call sinking a negative rising, falling a negative ascending, and retreating a negative advancing. The expression will thus make plain at the same time that falling, for example, is not distinguished from ascending merely as non-*a* is from *a*, but is just as positive as ascending, and contains the ground of a negation only in combination with it. Since everything here depends upon the counter-relation, it is of course clear that I can just as well call sinking a negative rising as rising a negative sinking; likewise assets are just as much negative debts as debts are negative assets. It is, however, somewhat more fitting, when we wish to designate the real opposite of a thing, to attach the name of the negative to that to which our purpose is in each case chiefly directed. Thus it is somewhat more suitable to call debts negative assets than to call them the other way about, although there is no difference in the counter-relation itself, but only in the bearing which the result of this counter-relation has upon the rest of our purpose. I would only add that I shall occasionally use the expression that one thing is the negative (*Sache*) of another. Thus the negative of rising is sinking; by this I do not wish to be understood as meaning a negation of the other, but something that stands to the other in a real opposition.",["The passage begins mid-sentence and the unmatched closing parenthesis is retained as in the text.","OCR slips corrected silently: 'ste' as 'sie', 'Musdrücken' as 'Ausdrücken', 'Po— sitives' as 'Positives', 'vor— züglich' as 'vorzüglich'.","Gegenverhältnis(se) = 'counter-relation(s)', with the German bracketed once; Realopposition/Realentgegensetzung = 'real opposition'.","'Aufgehen/Untergehen' (of heavenly bodies) is rendered 'rising/sinking', and 'Ausgehen' in the text as 'rising' too, since the pair is meant as an instance of opposed motions. 'Ausgehen' is a probable slip for 'Aufgehen' but I have not emended the German. 'Steigen/Fallen' = 'ascending/falling'.","The long sentence beginning 'Um indessen…' is kept as one sentence, with the final clause divided; 'Capitalien/Schulden' = 'assets/debts' (literally capital sums)."]],"negative-groessen-0024":["In this real opposition the following proposition is to be noted as a fundamental rule: Real repugnancy [*Realrepugnanz*] takes place only in so far as two things, as positive grounds, cancel one another's consequence. Let moving force be a positive ground: then a real conflict can occur only in so far as another moving force, combined with it, reciprocally cancels the consequence. The following serves as a general proof. The conflicting determinations must, first, be found in one and the same subject. For if one determination is posited in one thing and another, whichever you please, in another thing, no actual opposition arises from this.*) Secondly, one of the opposed determinations in a real opposition cannot be the contradictory opposite of the other; for then the conflict would be logical and, as has been shown above, impossible. Thirdly, one determination cannot negate anything other than what is posited by the other; for in that there is no opposition at all. Fourthly, in so far as they conflict with one another they cannot both be negating, for then neither posits anything that would be cancelled by the other. Accordingly, in every real opposition both predicates must be positive, yet in such a way that, in their combination in the same subject, the consequences reciprocally cancel one another. In this way things, one of which is regarded as the negative of the other, are both positive when considered in themselves, but when combined in one subject the consequence is zero. Sailing westward is just as much a positive motion as sailing eastward; only in one and the same ship do the distances thereby covered cancel one another, wholly or in part.",["OCR 'gelebt' read as 'gesetzt' (the parallel clause 'was durch die andre gesetzt ist'); 'doch)' and 'sich)' cleaned.","Footnote marker *) retained; the footnote is given with 0026.","'Fahrt gegen Abend/Morgen' rendered as sailing westward/eastward; 'Bestimmung' = 'determination'.","'Realrepugnanz' bracketed because Kant's technical term; 'Folge' = 'consequence'."]],"negative-groessen-0025":["I do not mean by this that these really opposed things contain, moreover, no negations. A ship that is moved westward is then not moving eastward or southward, etc., etc.; nor is it in all places at once: many negations attach to its motion. But what is still positive in the eastward as well as in the westward motion, apart from all these negations, this alone is what can really conflict, and its consequence is zero.",["'ostlichen' corrected to 'östlichen'; 'id)' to 'ich'. 'Hiedurch will ich nun nicht gemeint haben' is smoothed into 'I do not mean by this'."]],"negative-groessen-0026":["*) We shall in what follows treat further of a potential opposition.",["Footnote to 0024; the stray 'ES' after it is an OCR/page artefact and is omitted."]],"negative-groessen-0027":["This same point can be illustrated by general signs in the following way. All genuine negations, which are therefore possible (for the negation of the very thing that is at the same time posited in the subject is impossible), can be expressed by zero = 0, and affirmation by any positive sign; but the combination in the | same subject by + or −. Here we recognise that A + 0 = A, A − 0 = A, 0 + 0 = 0, 0 − 0 = 0*) are all of them oppositions, and that in none of them is anything that was posited cancelled. Likewise A + A is no cancellation, and no case remains but this: A − A = 0, that is, that of things one of which is the negative of the other, both are A and so truly positive, yet in such a way that the one cancels what is posited by the other, which is here indicated by the",["'Sero' read as 'Zero'. The OCR 'A- O A, OO =' is garbled; I read 'A − 0 = A, 0 + 0 = 0, 0 − 0 = 0', following the sense and the footnote's concern with 0 − A; doubtful, check against the Academy text.","'A − A − O' read as 'A − A = 0'.","Footnote *) is given with 0040. The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the sign-bracket '|' is the Academy page break.","'Oppositions' for 'Entgegensetzungen' here follows the OCR; the sense is that these cases are mere oppositions in the sense of positing/negating, not real conflict, which I leave for the reader to judge."]],"negative-groessen-0028":["sign −. | The second rule, which is properly the converse of the first, runs therefore: Wherever there is a positive ground and the consequence is nevertheless zero, there is a real opposition; that is, this ground is in combination with another positive ground, which is the negative of the first. If a ship on the open sea is actually being driven by an east wind and yet does not move from the spot, at least not so far as the wind contains ground for its doing so, a current must be running against it.",["Begins mid-sentence ('Zeichen — angedeutet wird'); the fragment is rendered as it stands. The sentence continues in 0029, hence 'Morgen-wind' is joined as 'east wind'.","'Morgenwind' = east wind (literally morning wind)."]],"negative-groessen-0029":["[The ship] must be driven against by a sea current. In its general sense this means as much as this: that",["This fragment begins by repeating the end of 0028 ('wind getrieben wird…'); the English of 0028 already covers the first clauses, so here only the portion belonging to 0029 is given. 'wind getrieben wird, und es kommt nicht von der Stelle' is rendered in 0028. Line number '5' omitted.","The bracketed '[The ship]' is supplied for the elided subject."]],"negative-groessen-0030":["the cancellation of the consequence of a positive ground always requires a positive ground also. Let there be any ground whatever for a consequence *b*,",[]],"negative-groessen-0031":["then the consequence can never be 0 except in so far as there is a ground for −*b*, that is,",[]],"negative-groessen-0032":["for something truly positive, which is opposed to the first:",[]],"negative-groessen-0033":["*b* − *b* = 0. If someone's estate contains a capital of 10,000 thalers,",["'b — b—0' read as 'b − b = 0'. 'Rthlr.' expanded to 'thalers'."]],"negative-groessen-0034":["the whole inheritance cannot amount to merely 6,000 thalers, except",[]],"negative-groessen-0035":["in so far as 10,000 − 4,000 = 6,000, that is, in so far as four thousand thalers of debts or other expense are connected with it. What",[]],"negative-groessen-0036":["follows will contribute much to the elucidation of these laws.",[]],"negative-groessen-0037":["I make the following remark on this division by way of conclusion.",["Rendered in two fragments with 0038–0040, which continue the sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0038":["Negation, in so far as it is the consequence of a real",["'Cui-' (OCR) read as the first part of 'Ent-gegensetzung' (real opposition), continued in 0039."]],"negative-groessen-0039":["opposition, I shall call privation (*privatio*); but every",["'Der-neinung' read as 'Verneinung' (negation), continued in 0040."]],"negative-groessen-0040":["negation, in so far as it does not arise from this kind of repugnancy,",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands. The footnote below (marker *) in 0027) was carried into this passage by the portal.","FOOTNOTE (to 0027): *) One might here be led to think that 0 − A is a further case which has been omitted. But in the philosophical sense this is impossible; for from nothing something positive can never be taken away. If in mathematics this expression is correct in application, that is because zero alters nothing in the least by increase or diminution through other quantities. A + 0 − A is still A − A, and therefore the zero is wholly idle. The notion borrowed from this, that negative quantities are less than nothing, is therefore empty and absurd.","In the footnote, '| der hier ausgelassen worden' is the Academy page break; line number '5' omitted. OCR 'Cui-gegensetzung'/'Der-neinung' resolved as noted above."]],"negative-groessen-0041":["... shall here be entitled a lack (*defectus, absentia*). The latter requires no positive ground, but only the lack of one; the former, however, has a true ground of position and an equally great opposed ground. Rest in a body is either a mere lack, that is, a negation of motion, in so far as no moving force is present; or a privation, in so far as moving force is indeed to be met with, but the consequence, namely the motion, is cancelled by an opposed force.",["Passage begins mid-sentence; \"the former\" (die erstere) must refer to the privation (Beraubung) named in the preceding, untranslated text. Running head omitted.","Bewegkraft = \"moving force\"; Mangel = \"lack\"; Beraubung = \"privation\"; Verneinung = \"negation\"."]],"negative-groessen-0042":["Second Section",[]],"negative-groessen-0043":["In which examples from philosophy are adduced",["Weltweisheit rendered \"philosophy\"."]],"negative-groessen-0044":["in which the concept of negative quantities occurs.",["Continues the heading of the preceding passage."]],"negative-groessen-0045":["1. Every body, by its impenetrability, resists the moving force of another that seeks to penetrate into the space which it occupies. Since, in face of the other's force towards motion, it is nevertheless a ground of its",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0046":["rest, it follows from the foregoing that impenetrability equally",["Fragment of a sentence, translated as it stands."]],"negative-groessen-0047":["presupposes a true force in the parts of the body, by means of",["Fragment of a sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0048":["which they together occupy a space, as any force may ever do",["Fragment; \"diejenige\" is taken to refer to the force (Kraft) of the other body."]],"negative-groessen-0049":["with which another body strives to move into this space.",["I have resolved the comparison (\"als diejenige ... womit\") by making the sense plain: impenetrability presupposes a true force just as much as does the force of the intruding body."]],"negative-groessen-0050":["By way of illustration, imagine two springs that strive against each other.",[]],"negative-groessen-0051":["Without doubt they hold each other at rest by equal forces. Place",["Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0052":["between the two a spring of equal elastic force: then this, by its striving, will produce the same effect, and will keep both springs at rest in accordance with the",["\"Ieder\" in the OCR is read as Feder (spring). Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0053":["rule of the equality of action and reaction. If, on the other hand, you insert in place of this spring any solid body whatever, precisely the same thing will happen through it, and the springs previously mentioned will be kept at rest by its impenetrability. The cause of impenetrability is therefore a true force, for it does the same as a true force does. Now if you give the name of attraction to a cause, whatever it may be, by virtue of which a body constrains others to press against the space which it occupies | or to move (it is enough here merely to think this attraction), then impenetrability is a negative attraction. This indicates that it is just as positive a ground as any other moving force in nature. And since negative attraction is properly a true repulsion, occasion is given, in the forces of the elements by virtue of which they occupy a space, yet in such fashion that they set limits to this space itself, through the *conflictus* of two forces opposed to one another, for many elucidations; in these I believe I have arrived at a distinct and trustworthy knowledge, which I shall make known in another treatise.",["OCR slips corrected silently: \"Ieder\" for Feder, \"gegedachte\" for gedachte, \"ftd)\"/\"sich)\" for sich.","The last sentence is irregular in structure (\"so wird ... Anlaß ... gegeben\"); I have kept its sense but rendered it as plainly as the construction allows.","\"nöthigt, gegen den Raum ... zu drücken | oder sich zu bewegen\": the object of \"to move\" is left as in the German; the | is the Academy page break (180)."]],"negative-groessen-0054":["2. Let us take an example from psychology. The question is whether displeasure (*Unlust*) is merely a lack of pleasure (*Lust*), or a ground of the privation of pleasure, which is indeed in itself something positive, and not merely the contradictory opposite of pleasure, but is opposed to it in the real sense (*im Realverstande*); and accordingly whether displeasure can be entitled a negative pleasure. Now inner sensation teaches us at the very outset that displeasure is more than a mere negation. For whatever pleasure one may have, some possible pleasure is still always lacking, so long as we are limited beings. A man who takes a medicine that tastes like pure water has perhaps a pleasure in the health he expects; in the taste, on the other hand, he feels no pleasure at all; but this lack is not yet displeasure. Give him a medicine made of wormwood. This sensation is very positive. Here there is not a mere lack of pleasure, but something which is a true ground of the feeling that is called displeasure.",["Silent OCR corrections: \"Be— raubung\" joined; \"Ger fühls\" = Gefühls; \"sich)\" = sich; \"Gehet ihm ein Arzneimittel von Wermuth\" read as \"Gebt ihm ... ein\" (an imperative).","Realverstande: \"in the real sense\"; the German is retained in brackets because the sense turns on it (cf. Realopposition).","Lust/Unlust rendered \"pleasure\"/\"displeasure\"; Empfindung = \"sensation\"."]],"negative-groessen-0055":["But from the illustration given we can at most recognise only that displeasure is not merely a lack, but a positive sensation. That it is both something positive and also really opposed to pleasure appears most clearly in the following way. News is brought to a Spartan mother that her son has fought heroically for his country in battle. The agreeable feeling of pleasure takes possession of her soul. It is added that he suffered a glorious death in the fight. This greatly diminishes that pleasure and reduces it to a lower degree. Call the degrees of the pleasure arising from the first ground alone 4a; if displeasure be a mere negation = 0, then, when both are taken together, the value of the satisfaction is 4a + 0 = 4a, and the pleasure would therefore not have been diminished by the news of the | death, which is false. Let the pleasure arising from his proved bravery be, then, = 4a, and let what remains, after displeasure has co-operated from the other cause, be 3a; then the | displeasure = a, and it is the negative of pleasure, namely −a, and therefore 1",["OCR corrections: \"pfindung\" = Empfindung; \"set\" = sei; \"und lebt sie auf\" read as \"und setzt sie auf\" (reduces it to); \"Luft\" = Lust throughout.","The passage breaks off after \"und daher\" with a stray \"1\" (probably a footnote mark or the beginning of a sum); translated as it stands, nothing completed.","The running head (\"2. Abschnitt. 181\") is omitted, the | marks being kept. The mother's \"Treffen\" rendered \"battle\"; \"Verneinung\" = negation."]],"negative-groessen-0056":["The estimation of the whole value of the total pleasure in a mixed state would also be very absurd if displeasure were a mere negation and equal to zero. Someone has bought an estate whose yield is 2000 Rthlr. annually. Let the degree of | pleasure over this income, in so far as it is pure, be expressed by 2000. But everything that he must give up from this income without enjoying it is a ground of displeasure: ground-rent 200 Rthlr., servants' wages 100 Rthlr., repairs 150 Rthlr. annually. If displeasure is a mere negation = 0, then, everything reckoned together, the pleasure which he has in his estate is 2000 + 0 + 0 + 0 = 2000, that is, just as great as if he could enjoy the yield without any charges. But it is obvious that he has no more cause to rejoice over this income than in so far as something remains to him after deduction of the charges, and the degree of satisfaction is 2000 − 200 − 100 − 150 = 1550. Displeasure is accordingly not merely a lack of pleasure, but a positive ground for wholly or partly cancelling the pleasure which takes place from another ground; and I therefore call it a negative pleasure. The lack of pleasure as well | as of displeasure, in so far as it is to be derived from the lack of grounds for either, is entitled indifference (*indifferentia*). The lack of pleasure as well as of displeasure, in so far as it is a consequence of the real opposition of equal grounds, is entitled equilibrium (*aequilibrium*): both are zero, but the former is a negation simply, the second a privation. The state of mind in which, with unequal opposed pleasure and displeasure, something of one of these two sensations remains over, is the preponderance of pleasure or of displeasure (*suprapondium voluptatis vel taedii*). It was by concepts of this kind that Herr von Maupertuis, in his Essay on Moral Philosophy, sought to estimate the sum of happiness in human life, and it cannot be estimated otherwise; only that this problem is insoluble for men, since only homogeneous sensations can be added into sums, whereas feeling, in the very complicated condition of life, seems to differ greatly according to the manifoldness of the emotions. The calculation gave this learned man a negative result, in which, however, I do not agree with him.",["OCR: \"Kant\" in \"an seinem Kant hat\" is evidently corrupt (probably Landgut/Gut); I render \"his estate\" without certainty. \"Einnähme\" = Einnahme; \"Umluft\" = Unlust; \"4- O\" = + 0.","Rthlr. (Reichsthaler) kept as in the text; Grundzins \"ground-rent\", Gesindelohn \"servants' wages\".","Maupertuis's work (Essai de philosophie morale) is rendered \"Essay on Moral Philosophy\"; Weltweisheit as \"philosophy\"; Rührungen as \"emotions\"; Facit as \"result\".","Page breaks | kept where they stand."]],"negative-groessen-0057":["On these grounds one may entitle aversion a negative desire, hatred a negative love, ugliness a negative beauty, blame a negative fame, and so on.",["OCR \"Daß\" read as Haß; \"nec gative\" as negative. Begierde = \"desire\"; Ruhm = \"fame\" (or \"renown\")."]],"negative-groessen-0058":["It might perhaps be thought here that all this is merely huckstering with words. But only those will so judge who do not know what an advantage there is when expressions at the same time indicate the relation to concepts already known; the slightest experience in mathematics can easily instruct anyone on this point. The error into which many philosophers have fallen through neglect of this is plain to see. It is found that for the most part they treat evils as mere negations, although it is evident from our explanations that there are evils of lack (*mala defectus*) and evils of privation (*mala privationis*). The former are negations for whose opposite positing there is no ground; the latter presuppose positive grounds for cancelling a good for which there is actually another ground, and are a negative good. The latter is a much greater evil than the former. Not to give is, in relation to one who is in need, an evil; but to take, to extort, to steal is, with respect to him, a much greater one, and taking is a negative giving. A similar thing could be shown in logical relations. Errors are negative truths (this is not to be confused with the truth of negative propositions); a refutation is a negative proof. But I am concerned lest I dwell on this too long. My intention is only to set these concepts in motion; their utility will be found in their use, and I shall give some prospects of it in the third section.",["OCR debris at the start (\"ex\"), mid-text (\"um 10 dieser\", \"s\", \"20\", \"2s\") and the garbled \"allein ich der — | sorge mich\" silently corrected: line numbers dropped, read as \"allein ich sorge mich\"; the | marks the page break in the original and is kept.","\"Nutze wird sich) durch den Gee | brauch\" read as \"Nutze wird sich durch den Gebrauch\"; the page-break sign is retained in spirit but the word is rejoined, so no | appears in the English.","\"eine- Widerlegung\" read as \"eine Widerlegung\".","Restated \"Der Fehler, darin ... viele Philosophen verfallen sind\" as a relative clause with \"into which\"; \"Krämerei mit Worten\" rendered \"huckstering with words\".","\"Position\" rendered \"positing\" (Setzung sense); \"Beraubung\" = privation, \"Mangel\" = lack, with Kant's Latin kept."]],"negative-groessen-0059":["3. The concepts of real opposition also have their useful application in practical philosophy. Demerit (*demeritum*) is not merely a negation but a negative merit (*meritum negativum*). For demerit can take place only in so far as there is in a being an inner law (either mere conscience or also the consciousness of a positive law) which is acted against. This inner law is a positive ground of a good action, and the consequence can be zero only because the consequence which would flow from the consciousness of the law alone is cancelled. There is here, then, a privation, a real opposition, and not merely a lack. Let no one imagine that this applies only to faults of commission (*demerita commissionis*) and not equally to faults of omission (*demerita omissionis*). An irrational animal practises no virtue. But this omission is not demerit (*demeritum*), for no inner law has been acted against. It was not impelled by an inner moral feeling to a good action, and the zero, or the omission, was not determined as a consequence by its having resisted that feeling, or by means of a counterweight. Here it is a negation pure and simple, arising from the want of a positive ground, and not a privation. Suppose, on the other hand,",["OCR slips corrected: \"Es it | also\" to \"Es ist also\"; \"Un— | tugend\", \"ge— handelt\" rejoined; \"p\" and line numbers dropped. The | page-break marks inside rejoined words are not retained.","\"Untugend\" rendered \"demerit\" to match Kant's Latin *demeritum*; \"Tugend\" in \"verübt keine Tugend\" rendered \"practises no virtue\".","\"Zero\" kept as \"zero\". The clause \"dadurch, daß es ihm widerstanden...\" is smoothed: \"it\" (ihm) read as the inner feeling; this is a judgment.","Passage breaks off in mid-sentence (\"Setzet da-\"); translated as it stands, continued in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0060":["a man who does not help someone whose distress he sees and whom he could easily help. Here, as in the heart of every man, there is in him too a positive law of love of one's neighbour. This must be outweighed. A real inner action is required for this",["Begins with the completion of \"Setzet da-gegen\" from the previous passage: \"Suppose, on the other hand\" is carried there. \"Setzet\" is imperative plural.","Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Es gehört hiezu eine wirkliche innere Handlung\"); continued in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0061":["arising from motive causes (*Bewegungsursachen*), in order that the omission may be possible. This zero is the consequence of a real opposition. It indeed costs some men, at the outset, appreciable effort to omit some good to which they notice positive impulses in themselves; habit makes everything easier, and this action is in the end scarcely noticed any more. Sins of commission and sins of omission are accordingly, morally, distinguished not in kind but only in degree [Größe, magnitude]",["The passage ends at \"Unterlassungssünden\", and the sentence concludes in the next passage (\"moralisch nicht der Art, sondern der Größe nach nur unterschieden\"); I have therefore fused the stub rather than leave it dangling, but the English of the last clause properly belongs to 0062.","\"b\" OCR marginal letter dropped; \"er— leichtert\" rejoined. \"Bewegungsursachen\" given in German as a choice of \"motive causes\"."]],"negative-groessen-0062":["morally; they are distinguished only in magnitude, not in kind.",["Continues the sentence begun at the end of the preceding passage."]],"negative-groessen-0063":["Physically, that is, in respect of their external consequences, they are, however, also different in kind.",["\"wohl\" rendered \"however\" / concessive; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0064":["He who receives nothing suffers an evil of lack, and he from whom something is taken, an evil of privation. But",["OCR stray \";\" dropped. Breaks off mid-sentence (\"Allein\"); continued in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0065":["as regards the moral state of him to whom the sin of omission is imputed,",["\"zukommt\" read as \"is imputed to\" (zukommen, to fall to one); \"zu—kommt\" rejoined across the passage break. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"negative-groessen-0066":["only a greater degree of the action is required for the sin of commission:",["Continues the \"so\" clause of the preceding passage."]],"negative-groessen-0067":["just as the counterweight on a lever exerts a genuine",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0068":["force in order merely to keep the load at rest, and needs only some increase to move it actually to the other side. In just the same way, he who does not pay what he owes will under certain circumstances cheat in order to gain, and he who does not help when he can will, as soon as the motive causes increase, ruin the other. Love and non-love are each the contradictory opposite of the other. Non-love is a genuine negation, but with regard to that which one is conscious of an obligation to love, this negation is possible only through real opposition, and therefore only as a privation. And in such a case not to love and to hate are only a difference in degrees. All omissions which, though they are lacks of a greater moral perfection, are not sins of omission, are on the contrary nothing but negations pure and simple of a certain virtue, and not privations or demerit. Of this kind are the lacks of saints and the faults of noble souls. A certain greater ground of perfection is wanting, and the lack does not manifest itself on account of any counteraction.",["The \"|\" mark in \"vom an- | dem\" is a page break within a word; the word is rejoined (\"vom andern\") and the mark is not reproduced.","\"Nicht⸗Liebe\" read as \"Nicht-Liebe\". \"Eins ... vom andern\" rendered \"each ... of the other\" (restated for idiom). \"Verbindlichkeit\" = obligation.","\"Untugend\" again \"demerit\" and \"Beraubung\" \"privation\"; \"um der Entgegenwirkung willen\" rendered \"on account of any counteraction\"."]],"negative-groessen-0069":["The application of the concepts cited to the objects of practical philosophy could be extended very much further. Prohibitions are negative commands, punishments negative rewards, and so forth. But my purpose is for the present attained if only the use of this thought in general is understood. I am well aware that to readers of enlightened insight the foregoing exposition will appear more prolix than is necessary. But I shall be excused as soon as it is considered that there is also a very unteachable class of critics who, since they spend their lives with a single book, understand nothing except what is contained in it, and in regard to whom the utmost prolixity is not superfluous.",["OCR \"10\", \"is\" (for \"15\") line numbers dropped; \"Ge— dankens\", \"Beur— theilern\" rejoined.","\"Weltweisheit\" rendered \"philosophy\"; \"Gedanke\" rendered \"thought\"; \"Beurtheiler\" rendered \"critics\" (reviewers/judges is also possible)."]],"negative-groessen-0070":["4. Let us borrow yet another example from natural science. In nature there are many privations [*Beraubungen*] arising from the *conflictus* of two operative causes, one of which cancels the consequence of the other through real opposition. It is often uncertain, however, whether the privation is perhaps merely the negation of a deficiency, because a positive cause is wanting, or whether it is the consequence of the opposition of genuine forces. In the same way, rest is to be ascribed either to the absence of a moving cause or to the conflict of two moving forces which hold each other back. For example, it is a famous question whether cold requires a positive cause, or whether, as a mere deficiency, it is to be ascribed simply to the absence of the cause of warmth. I shall dwell on this a little, so far as it serves my purpose. Without doubt cold is itself only a negation of warmth, and it is easy to see that it is possible in itself even without a positive ground. It is just as easy to understand, however, that it may also proceed from a positive cause, and does at times actually arise from one, whatever opinion one may adopt as to the origin of warmth. No absolute cold is known in nature, and when one speaks of it one understands it only comparatively. Now experience and rational grounds agree in confirming the thought of the famous von Musschenbroek, that heating consists not in internal agitation but in the actual passage of elementary fire from one matter into another. This passage may, however, presumably be accompanied by an internal agitation, and this agitation, once excited, likewise promotes the departure of elementary fire from the bodies. On this basis, if the fire-element among bodies in a certain space is in equilibrium, they are relatively to one another neither cold nor warm. If this equilibrium is disturbed, then the matter into which the elementary fire passes",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'In der Natur giebt es viel Beraubungen' as two sentences, and the 'so wie die Ruhe' clause as a separate sentence, for clarity.","'bloß die Verneinung des Mangels sei' is rendered literally ('merely the negation of a deficiency'); the sense is a negation due to the lack of a positive cause. Kant's contrast is with a negation produced by real opposition (*Beraubung*).","OCR slips silently corrected: 'set' = sei, 'Mugfschenbroek' = Musschenbroek; stray marginal letters and line numbers omitted. 'Auf diesen Fuß' = 'on this basis'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0071":["is, relatively to the body that is thereby deprived of it,",["Fragment: the sentence continues from the preceding passage ('the matter into which the elementary fire passes') and goes on in the next."]],"negative-groessen-0072":["cold; the latter body, on the other hand, is entitled warm in respect of that matter, in so far as it yields this warmth to it. The state in this change",["'heißt' rendered 'is entitled'. 'in jene Materie diese Wärme überläßt' is rendered 'yields this warmth to it'. Fragment; it continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0073":["is entitled, in the former, heating [*Erwärmung*], in the latter, cooling [*Erkältung*], until everything is once more in equilibrium.",["OCR 'wich derum' corrected to 'wiederum'. 'in the former/in the latter' supplied for 'bei jenem/bei diesem'."]],"negative-groessen-0074":["Now there is surely nothing more natural to suppose than that the attractive",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0075":["forces of matter set this subtle and elastic fluid in motion, and fill the mass of bodies with it, until it is",["OCR '38e-wegung' read as 'Be-wegung'. Fragment; the sentence continues."]],"negative-groessen-0076":["in equilibrium everywhere,",[]],"negative-groessen-0077":["provided, that is, that the spaces are filled with it in proportion to the",[]],"negative-groessen-0078":["attractions which act there. And here it is plainly evident",[]],"negative-groessen-0079":["that a matter which cools another on contact",["Fragment; the clause continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0080":["robs, by genuine force (of attraction), the elementary fire with which the mass of the other was filled, and that the cold of the former body may be entitled a negative warmth, because the negation which follows from this in the warmer body is a privation [*Beraubung*].",["OCR 'rauhe' read as 'raubt'. 'genannt werden' rendered 'may be entitled'."]],"negative-groessen-0081":["Here, however, the introduction of this designation would be without use, and not much better than a play on words. My intention in this is directed only to",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0082":["what follows.",[]],"negative-groessen-0083":["It has long been known that magnetic bodies have two opposed ends, which are called poles, of which one repels the point of the same name on the other and attracts the opposite one. But the famous Professor Aepinus showed, in a treatise on the similarity of electric force with magnetic, that electrified bodies, under certain treatment, likewise exhibit two poles, one of which he calls the positive, the other the negative pole,",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Apinus' = Aepinus, 'zurückjtößt' = zurückstößt, 'ente gegenstehende' = entgegenstehende. The line number '5' is omitted.","Rendered 'attracts the other' as 'attracts the opposite one' for clarity (the point of opposite name). The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0084":["and of which the one attracts what the other repels. This phenomenon is most distinctly perceived when a tube is brought near enough to an electrical body, yet not so near that it draws a spark from it. I now maintain that in heatings or coolings, that is, in all changes of warmth or cold, especially the rapid ones, which take place at one end in a continuous intermediate space or in bodies extended lengthwise, there are at all",["The passage breaks off in the middle of the word 'jeder-' ('jederzeit', 'always'); I have rendered it as 'at all' here, and 'times' opens the next passage.","'Erscheinung' rendered 'phenomenon' in the loose sense; the method's fixed term would be 'appearance'. Restated the long final sentence by keeping Kant's order but dividing the clauses with commas only."]],"negative-groessen-0085":["times to be met with, as it were, two poles of warmth, of which one becomes positive, that is, warm above the previous degree of the body in question, the other negative, that is, warm below this degree, i.e. cold. It is known that various subterranean vaults show a frost within that is the stronger, the more the sun outside warms air and earth. Matthias Bel, who describes the [. . .] in the Carpathian mountains, adds that it is a custom of the peasants in Transylvania to make their drink cold by burying it in the earth and making a quickly burning fire over it. It seems that the earth-stratum at this time cannot become positively warm on its upper surface without being the negative of this at a somewhat greater depth. Boerhaave besides reports that the fire of smiths' hearths caused cold at a certain distance. In the free air above the earth's surface this opposition seems equally to prevail, especially in rapid changes. Herr Jacobi reports somewhere in the Hamburg Magazine that in the severe cold which often attacks far-extended lands, there generally lie in a long stretch considerable places between, where it is temperate and mild. Likewise Herr Aepinus found, in the tube of which I spoke, that from the positive pole of the one end to the negative of the other, the positively and negatively electrical places alternated at certain distances. It seems that in some region of the air warming cannot begin without thereby occasioning in another, as it were, the effect of a negative pole, that is, cold. On this basis, conversely, a cold rapidly increasing in one place will serve to increase the warmth in another region, just as, when a metal rod heated at one end is suddenly cooled in water, the warmth of the other end increases.*) Accordingly the",["The opening 'ex' of the OCR is a stray fragment and is dropped. 'Trost' corrected to 'Frost', 'Samb.' to 'Hamb.', 'Boerhave' to Boerhaave, 'elektriiche' to elektrische.","Doubtful reading: 'die w im karpatischen Gebürge' is corrupt in the OCR; a word (perhaps for the vaults or caves) is lost, so I mark the gap '[. . .]' and supply nothing.","The sentence 'Es scheint, ... Wirkung eines negativen Pols' and the following 'auf diesen Fuß' clause are divided into two sentences. The footnote marker '*)' is kept; the footnote follows in the next passage. The sentence breaks off at 'Demnach hört der'."]],"negative-groessen-0086":["*) The experiments for making oneself certain of the opposite poles of warmth would, it seems to me, be easily arranged. Take a horizontal tin tube a foot in length, bent vertically upward for a couple of inches at both ends. If it were filled with spirits of wine and the latter were lit on one side, while in the other end the thermometer",["Footnote text, kept as a footnote. OCR 'Yorizontalen' read as 'Horizontalen', 'ste' as 'sie', and the line number '35' omitted. 'um sich) der' read as 'um sich der'.","The footnote breaks off mid-sentence (no main clause yet); it is translated as it stands, and the 'if ... if' construction is smoothed into a sentence plus a conditional."]],"negative-groessen-0087":["difference of the poles of warmth therefore ceases forthwith, when the communication or privation has had time enough to spread itself uniformly through the whole matter, just as the tube of Professor Aepinus shows only one kind of electricity as soon as it has drawn the spark. Perhaps also the great cold of the upper region of the air is to be ascribed not merely to the lack of means of warming, but to a positive cause, namely that it becomes negative in respect of warmth in the measure in which the lower air and the ground are positive. In general the magnetic",["Running head ('2. Abschnitt. 187') and the page-break sign omitted; the sentence resumes from 'Demnach hört der' of passage 0085 ('Accordingly the difference ... ceases').","'Erwärmungsmittel' rendered 'means of warming'. The passage ends mid-sentence and continues in the next."]],"negative-groessen-0088":["force, electricity and warmth seem to occur through one and the same intermediate matter. All of them together can be excited by rubbing, and I conjecture that the diversity of the poles and the opposition [. . .] stood [. . .], this negative opposition would, according to my conjecture, soon",["Corrupt: the OCR has the line 'tische Kraft ...' run into 'und ich vermuthe, daß die Verschiedenheit der Pole und die Entgegensetzung stände, würde sich ... bald', apparently with lines from the footnote interleaved. I have translated only what stands and marked the gaps; the sense of 'stände' is doubtful.","'Reihen' in the OCR is probably 'Reiben' (rubbing), which I have followed; flagged as a conjectural reading."]],"negative-groessen-0089":["show itself; as one could, in order to perceive, by cooling on one side, the effect on the other",["Fragment of the footnote; 'wie man denn' rendered 'as one could'. Continues in the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0090":["side, make use of salt water, into which on the",["Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence and is not completed here."]],"negative-groessen-0091":["...one side pushed, ice could be thrown. On this occasion I shall only remark one further point: by what observation, which I should like to see made,",["Fragment: 'einen Seite gestoßen Eis geworfen werden könnte' is corrupt or cut off (perhaps an OCR loss; 'einen' for 'einer'). It is rendered as it stands, without completion.","The sentence is continued in the next passage; I have not completed it."]],"negative-groessen-0092":["the explanation of artificial cold and heat in",["Fragment; the sentence runs on into the next passage."]],"negative-groessen-0093":["the dissolutions of certain mixed materials would in all probability gain much light. I am persuaded, namely, that the difference between these appearances [Erscheinungen] will rest chiefly upon",[]],"negative-groessen-0094":["whether the mixed fluids, after complete union, occupy more or less volume than the content of their space, taken together, amounted to",["'Raumesinhalt' rendered 'content of their space'."]],"negative-groessen-0095":["before the mixture. In the former case, I maintain, they will show warmth on the thermometer; in the second, cold. For in the case where, after the mixing, they yield a denser medium, there is not only more attractive matter, which draws into itself the element of the neighbouring fire, than there was before in an equal",["'Im ersteren Falle' (more volume) and 'im zweiten' (less volume) are made explicit only by the construction of the preceding passage; nothing is added."]],"negative-groessen-0096":["space, but it is also to be presumed that the power of attraction becomes greater than in proportion to the increasing density, while the expansive force of the condensed ether perhaps increases, as in the case of air, only in proportion to the density; for, according to Newton, attractions at very close range stand in a much greater proportion than the inverse proportion of the distances. In this way the mixture, if it has more density than the densities of the two miscible substances taken together before the mixing, will show, in respect of the neighbouring bodies, a preponderance of attraction towards the elementary fire; and, in that it deprives the thermometer of this fire, will let cold",["Stray 'i' in 'zusammen genommen, i in Ansehung' treated as an OCR slip and dropped.","The long sentence 'Auf solche Weise ...' is kept as one, but the first sentence is rearranged for smoothness; 'Ausspannungskraft' = expansive force; 'Äther' = ether.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Kälte | blicken lassen' continues in the next passage)."]],"negative-groessen-0097":["show itself. But everything will proceed in the reverse manner if the mixture yields a thinner medium. For since it lets go a quantity of elementary fire, neighbouring materials attract it and exhibit the phenomenon of warmth. The outcome of experiments does not always correspond to our conjectures. But if experiments are not to be merely a matter of chance, they must be occasioned by conjecture. [...] of positive and negative activity might, by skilful handling, be observed equally well in the appearances of warmth. The inclined plane of Galileo, the pendulum of Huygens, the mercury tube of Torricelli, the air-pump of Otto Guericke and the glass prism of Newton have given us the key to great secrets of nature",["The sentence beginning 'der positiven und negativen Wirksamkeit durch eine geschickte Behandlung ...' lacks its grammatical subject; a few words (perhaps '[Spuren]' or similar) seem to be missing in the OCR or the portal's text. I mark the gap with [...] and have supplied nothing.","'Wirksamkeit' rendered 'activity'.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; the next passage continues with 'geheimnissen'."]],"negative-groessen-0098":["of nature. The negative and positive activity of the mat- | ter, especially in electricity, conceals, to all appearance, important insights; and a more fortunate posterity, into whose fair days we look forward, will, it is to be hoped, recognise as general laws what for the present appears to us in a still ambiguous concordance. |",["Stray 'ex' before 'geheimnissen' and the line number '1o' treated as OCR debris and omitted; the signs | are kept where they stand.","'Ma- | terien' rendered 'mat- | ter' to keep the line-break sign in place; 'zweideutig' = ambiguous."]],"negative-groessen-0099":["Third Section",[]],"negative-groessen-0100":["Containing some considerations which may prepare the way for the application of the concept in question to the objects of philosophy [Weltweisheit]",["'Weltweisheit' = philosophy (literally 'worldly wisdom'); the heading continues in the next passage ('vorbereiten können')."]],"negative-groessen-0101":["...may prepare.",["Conclusion of the heading begun in the previous passage: 'vor-bereiten können' = 'may prepare the way'."]],"negative-groessen-0102":["What I have set forth up to this point is only my first glance at an object of importance but no less of difficulty. When one ascends from the examples adduced, which are comprehensible enough, to general propositions, one has reason to be extremely apprehensive that false steps may occur on an untrodden path, steps which perhaps become known only in the course of progress. I therefore offer what I still have to say on the matter merely as an essay, which is very imperfect, although I promise myself manifold benefit from whatever attention may be given to it. I am well aware that such a confession is a very poor recommendation to the approval of those who demand a bold dogmatic tone, in order to be led in whatever direction one wishes to have them. But without feeling the least regret at the loss of approval of this kind, I regard it as far more fitting, in the case of a mode of knowledge so slippery as the metaphysical, | to lay one's thoughts before public examination first, in the form of uncertain essays, than to announce them at once with all the adornment of presumed thoroughness and complete conviction; for then, as a rule, every improvement is rejected, and every defect that is to be met with in them becomes incurable.",["Several long sentences are lightly restructured for clarity; the sentence 'Aber ohne das mindeste Bedauren ...' is kept entire but its order smoothed.","'Erkenntniß' here rendered 'knowledge' (in the singular, with 'mode of' added for smoothness); 'Versuch' = essay.","The | after 'gemäßer an,' is the Academy's line/page sign, retained."]],"negative-groessen-0103":["1. Everyone readily understands why something is not, namely in so far as the positive ground for it is lacking; but how that which is should cease to be is not so easily understood. For example, there exists at this moment in my soul the representation [Vorstellung] of the sun, through the power of my | imagination. In the next moment I cease to think of this object. This representation, which was, ceases to be in me, and the next state is the zero of the previous one. If I were to give as the ground | of this that the thought had ceased because in the following moment I had omitted to produce it, the answer would in no way differ from the question; for the very point at issue is how an action which is actually taking place can be omitted, that is, can cease to be.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Eriftirt' → 'existirt', 'id)' → 'ich'; stray 'd' after 'Gegenstand' and the line numbers dropped. The | signs are kept.","'Zero' is kept as Kant's own word, rendered 'zero'; 'Einbildung' = imagination.","The sentence 'Wollte ich ...' is kept as one but the 'denn' clause is stated as a separate sentence-part for clarity."]],"negative-groessen-0104":["I therefore say: every ceasing-to-be [Vergehen] is a negative coming-to-be [Entstehen]; that is, a true real ground [Realgrund] is required to abolish something positive that is, just as much as to produce it when it is not. The ground of this is contained in what precedes. Let a be posited: then only a − a = 0; that is, a can be abolished only in so far as an equal but opposite real ground is combined with the ground of a. Corporeal nature offers examples of this everywhere. A motion never ceases, wholly or in part, without a moving force, equal to that which could have produced the lost motion, being combined with it in opposition. But inner experience also, in regard to the abolition of representations and desires that have become actual through the activity of the soul, agrees very well with this. One feels very distinctly in oneself that, in order to let a thought full of grief pass away and be abolished in oneself, real and generally great activity is required. It costs a real effort to extinguish a merry representation that provokes laughter, when one wishes to bring one's mind [Gemüth] to seriousness. Every abstraction is nothing other than an abolition [Aufhebung] of certain clear representations, which one commonly undertakes in order that what remains may be represented the more clearly. But everyone knows how much activity this requires, and so one may call abstraction a negative attention; that is, a true doing and acting, which is opposed to the action by which the representation becomes clear and, through its connection with it, brings about the zero, or the lack of the clear representation. For otherwise, if it were a mere negation and lack, no more exertion of a force would be required for it than is needed for my not knowing something, since there was never a ground for it [and so no force is needed].",["'Es sei a gelebt' is evidently an OCR corruption; read as 'gesetzt' ('Let a be posited'). Flagged as a doubtful reading.","'Mus- hebung' read as 'Aufhebung'; 'Handlen' as 'Handeln'; 'sich)' as 'sich'. Marginal line numbers (15, 19, 20, 25, 30 etc.) omitted; the | sign kept after 'ent-' where the line breaks.","The last sentence ends obscurely ('weil niemals ein Grund dazu war, Kraft nöthig ist'); I restate it and supply in square brackets '[and so no force is needed]' to complete the sense. The German may be slightly corrupt.","'Realgrund' = real ground; 'Wirksamkeit/Thätigkeit' = activity; 'Gemüth' = mind."]],"negative-groessen-0105":["The very same necessity of a positive ground for the abolition of an inner accident of the soul is shown in the overcoming of desires, in which connection one may make use of the examples cited above. Generally, however, even apart from the cases in which one is actually conscious of this opposed activity, and which we have cited, we have no sufficient ground for denying it when we do not clearly observe it in ourselves. I think, for example, at this moment of the tiger. This thought is lost, and the jackal comes to my mind instead. One cannot, indeed, perceive in oneself, in this change of representations, any special striving of the soul that would operate to [remove] one of the aforesaid",["'reilich' read as 'freilich'; 'sich)' as 'sich'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('um eine von den gedachten ...'); I supply '[remove]' in brackets only to make the English infinitive intelligible, and do not complete the sentence.","'Accidens' = accident (inner accident of the soul)."]],"negative-groessen-0106":["...representations. But what admirable activity is hidden in the depths of our mind, which we",["The passage begins mid-word ('orstellungen', i.e. [V]orstellungen) and breaks off mid-sentence; it is translated as it stands, without completion.","The German 'nicht' in 'ist nicht ... verborgen' is a rhetorical expletive, so the clause is rendered as an exclamatory question."]],"negative-groessen-0107":["...in the midst of their exercise, because the actions are very many, while each single one is represented only obscurely. The proofs of this are known to everyone; one need only consider the actions that go on unnoticed in us when we read, and one must be astonished. Among other works, one may on this point consult the Logic of Reimarus, who offers reflections upon it. And so we must judge that the play of representations, and in general of all the activities of our soul, so far as their consequences cease again after they have actually been, presuppose opposed actions, of which the one is the negative of the other, in accordance with the certain",["The passage begins mid-word ('itten', i.e. [m]itten) and breaks off mid-phrase; the continuation ('grounds ...') is in the next passage.","OCR 'jer dunkel' is doubtful; I read it as an intensifier before 'dunkel' and render simply 'only obscurely'.","Silently corrected OCR: Thatigfeiten, nach— cm (nachdem), vor— gussetzen (voraussetzen).","'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'Aufhebung' rendered 'cancellation' elsewhere in this group."]],"negative-groessen-0108":["grounds which we have adduced, even though inner experience cannot always instruct us concerning it.",["Begins mid-word ('ründen', i.e. [G]ründen); it completes the sentence of the preceding passage."]],"negative-groessen-0109":["If one considers the grounds on which the rule here cited rests, one at once perceives that, so far as concerns the cancellation [Aufhebung] of an existing something, there can be no difference between the accidents of spiritual natures and the consequences of effective forces in the corporeal world. In both cases, namely, the existing something is never cancelled except by a true opposed moving force of another; and an inner accident, a thought of the soul, cannot cease to be without a truly active force of that very thinking subject. The difference concerns here only the different laws to which these two kinds of beings are subordinate | , in that the state of matter can never be changed otherwise than by an external cause, whereas that of a spirit can be changed also by an internal cause. The necessity of real opposition, however, remains | always the same despite this difference.",["The long second sentence is divided into three; 'namely, that ...' is turned into a statement of what holds in both cases, the sense Kant's argument requires.","'eines andern' I take to refer to another thing or force acting from outside, as the contrast with the 'inner' accident shows.","The signs | are carried over from the Academy apparatus at their places in the German; the original second | falls before 'bei diesem Unterschiede'.","OCR damage silently repaired: 'Gei-ftigen' etc., 'ebendesselben denkenden Subjects', 'ines' (eines)."]],"negative-groessen-0110":["I remark once more that it is a deceptive concept when one believes oneself to have understood the cancellation of the positive consequences of the activity of our soul, because one calls it omissions [Unterlassungen]. It is exceedingly remarkable that the more one examines one's commonest and most confident judgments, the more such delusions one discovers, in which we are content with words without understanding anything of the things themselves. That I now do not have a certain thought is, if it has not been there before either, certainly intelligible enough when I say that I omit to think it; for the word then signifies the lack of the ground from which the lack of the consequence is conceived. But if the question is, How is it that a thought which was in me a short time before is no longer there?, the former answer is quite void. For this non-being is now a privation [Beraubung], and the omitting has now an entirely different sense,**) namely the cancellation of an activity which was there a short time before. This, however, is the question I am putting, and I am not so easily put off with a word. In applying the rule mentioned to all kinds of cases in nature, much caution is needed, lest one falsely take something negative to be positive, which easily happens. For the sense of the proposition which I have cited here relates to the arising and passing away of something that is positive. For example, the going out of a flame because the fuel is exhausted is no negative arising; that is, it is not grounded on a true moving force opposed to that through which the flame arises. For the continuance of a flame is not the duration of a motion already there, but the constant generation of new motions in other combustible particles of vapour.*) Accordingly the ceasing of the flame is not the cancellation of an actual motion, but the lack of new",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('der Mangel neuer'); the continuation ('Bewegungen und mehrerer Trennungen ...') is in the following passage.","Restated: the opening sentence's 'wenn man sie Unterlassungen nennt' is rendered 'because one calls it omissions' to bring out the causal sense; the long sentence on the flame is kept in separate sentences.","Footnote markers are uncertain in the OCR ('Sinn,“)' and 'Dunfttheilden.”*)'). I have placed *) after 'vapour' (matching the footnote on fire and vapour) and **) after 'sense'; a stray ')' after 'gründet sich' is dropped.","'Beraubung' = privation; 'Aufhebung' = cancellation; 'Bewegkraft' = moving force; 'Dunsttheilchen' (OCR 'Dunfttheilden') = particles of vapour."]],"negative-groessen-0111":["*) Every body whose parts are suddenly transformed into vapour, and so exercise the repulsion that is opposed to cohesion, throws off sparks of fire and burns, because the elemental fire, which before was in a state of compression, is swiftly set free and spreads out.",["Footnote to the preceding text (on the flame and the particles of vapour). 'Zurückstoßung' = repulsion; 'Zusammenhang' = cohesion; 'Elementarfeuer' = elemental fire.","OCR 'sich)' read as 'sich' (the parenthesis is a stray mark)."]],"negative-groessen-0112":["**) This sense does not even properly belong to the word. [Main text continues:] ...motions and of further separations, because the cause for them is lacking, namely the further fuel of the fire. This must then be regarded not as the cancellation of an existing thing but as the lack of the ground for a possible position (that of further separation). But enough of this. I write this to give those who are practised in this kind of knowledge occasion for further reflection; the inexperienced would, to be sure, be entitled to demand more elucidation.",["The passage joins the footnote to the **) marker with the continuation of the main sentence begun in the previous passage ('der Mangel neuer | Bewegungen und mehrerer Trennungen'). The bracketed '[Main text continues:]' is my own addition to mark the join.","OCR 'muß s angesehen' is read as 'muß angesehen werden' ('must be regarded'); the stray 's' is dropped as an OCR slip.","'Versuchten' is doubtful (OCR); I take it as 'those practised/versed' in contrast with 'die Unerfahrenen'.","'Erkenntniß' = knowledge."]],"negative-groessen-0113":["2. The propositions which I propose to set forth in this section appear to me to be of the utmost importance. Before doing so, however, I must add to the general concept of negative magnitudes a determination [Bestimmung] which I deliberately set aside above, so as not to burden the objects of strenuous attention too heavily. Hitherto I have considered the grounds of real opposition only in so far as they actually posit, in one and the same thing, determinations of which the one is the negative of the other. An example is the moving forces of one and the same body in directions precisely opposed to one another. In that case the grounds actually cancel their consequences on both sides, namely the motions. I shall therefore for the present entitle this opposition the actual opposition (*oppositio actualis*).\n\nOn the other hand, we rightly entitle predicates negatives of one another when they belong to different things and do not directly cancel one another's consequence, provided that each is so constituted that it could cancel either the consequence of the other, or at least something that is as determinate as this consequence and equal to it. This opposition may be entitled the possible opposition (*oppositio potentialis*). Both are real, that is, distinct from logical opposition; both are in constant use in mathematics, and both deserve to be so in philosophy also.\n\nTake two bodies moving towards one another in one and the same straight line with equal forces. Since in impact they communicate their forces to one another, these forces can be called negatives of one another, and in the former sense, that is, through actual opposition. Take, on the other hand, two bodies receding from one another in opposite directions on the same straight line with equal forces. Here the one force is the negative of the other. But since in this case the bodies do not communicate their forces to one another, they stand only in potential opposition. For each body, if it struck upon a body moving in the same direction as the other, would cancel in it as much force as the other body contains.\n\nIn what follows I shall understand the matter in this way with regard to all grounds of real opposition in the world, and not merely those which belong to moving forces. To give an example of the rest, one might say that the pleasure [Lust] which one man has and the displeasure [Unlust] which another has stand in potential opposition. They also do in fact, on occasion, cancel one another's consequences, since in this real conflict the one often annihilates what the other creates in accordance with his pleasure.\n\nIn taking the grounds that are really opposed to one another in both senses quite generally, I ask that I should not be required to make these concepts evident at every point by examples *in concreto*. Everything that belongs to motions can be made clear and comprehensible to intuition [Anschauung]. By contrast, the real grounds which are not mechanical are for us so difficult and obscure that their relations to their consequences, in opposition or in agreement, can hardly be made comprehensible. I shall therefore content myself with establishing the following propositions in their general sense.",["Restated the long opening sentences as several shorter ones; the semicolon-linked clauses on the two kinds of opposition are divided for clarity.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'hinzu— thun', 'Gegen— stände', 'wirk— liebe' (wirkliche), 'ste sich' (sie sich); the running head 'Kant's Schriften. Werke. II.' and line numbers are omitted.","'im erstern Verstande' rendered 'in the former sense'; Verstand here means 'sense, meaning', not the faculty 'understanding'.","'Bestimmung' is rendered 'determination' in both occurrences; 'Bewegkräfte' as 'moving forces'. 'vernichtigt' = 'annihilates'.","In the last sentence of the second-to-last paragraph I supplied 'hardly' and 'can ... be made comprehensible' to smooth the construction 'um ... begreiflich zu machen'; the German is elliptical here."]],"negative-groessen-0114":["The first proposition is this. In all natural changes in the world the sum of the positive is neither increased nor diminished, when this sum is estimated by adding agreeing (not opposed) positions and subtracting from one another those that are really opposed.",["'Position' (Position/Positionen) is kept as 'position', Kant's technical sense of a positing; 'einstimmig' rendered 'agreeing'. The German 'daß einstimmige ...' is taken as introducing the manner of estimation, so I recast it as 'when ... estimated by adding'."]],"negative-groessen-0115":["*) As, for example, in the impact of one body upon another the production of a new motion is at the same time [bound up] with the cancelling of an equal motion which existed before",["Footnote fragment; it breaks off mid-sentence in the source and is translated as it stands. The bracketed 'bound up' is supplied only to carry the sense of 'zugleich'; the German has no such word and the continuation is missing."]],"negative-groessen-0116":["All change consists in this: that either something positive which was not is posited, or what was is cancelled. A change is natural, however, in so far as its ground, as well as its consequence, belongs to the world.\n\nIn the first case, then, where a position which was not is posited, the change is a coming-into-being. The state of the world before this change is, in respect of this position, equal to zero = 0, and by this coming-into-being the real consequence is = A. But I assert that when A arises in a natural change of the world, −A must also arise. That is to say, no natural ground of a real consequence can exist without being at the same time a ground of another consequence which is the negative of the first.*)\n\nFor since the consequence is nothing = 0 except in so far as the ground is posited, the sum of the position in the consequence contains no more than was contained in the state of the world, in so far as that state contained the ground of it. But this state contained zero of that position which is in the consequence; in other words, the position which is to be found in the consequence was not in the previous state. Consequently the change that flows from it cannot, for the whole of the world, in respect of its actual or potential consequences, be otherwise than equal to zero.\n\nNow on the one hand the consequence is positive and = A. On the other hand the whole state of the universe, in respect of the change A, is to be zero = 0 as before. But this is impossible, except in so far as A − A are taken together. It follows that no positive change ever takes place in the world in a natural way, whose consequence does not, taken as a whole, consist in an actual or potential opposition which cancels itself. This sum, however, gives zero = 0; and before the change it was likewise = 0, so that it has been neither increased nor diminished by it.\n\nIn the second case, where the change consists in the cancelling of something positive, the consequence is 0. But according to the preceding section the state of the entire ground was not merely = A, but A − A = 0. Thus, by the manner of estimation which I here presuppose, the position in the world has been neither increased nor diminished.",["Divided the long sentences of the argument into shorter ones, and set the footnote marker '*)' after the parenthesis it follows; the parenthetical remark is made a plain sentence ('That is to say ...').","OCR corrected: '— Wentspringen' read as '−A entspringen' (−A arise); 'twas' = Etwas; 'es gesammten' = des gesammten; 'Mfo' = Also; 'ste' = sie.","'Zero = O' / '= 0': the OCR alternates O and 0; I read 0 throughout.","'Veränderung' rendered 'change'; 'Entstehen' 'coming-into-being'; 'Weltveränderung' 'change of the world'; 'Zustand des gesammten Grundes' 'state of the entire ground'. 'Universum' kept as 'universe'.","'A — A' is retained as 'A − A'; the OCR's dash is read as the minus sign."]],"negative-groessen-0117":["I shall try to elucidate this proposition, which appears to me to be important. In the changes of the corporeal world it stands firm as a mechanical rule long since proved. It is expressed thus: *Quantitas motus, summando vires corporum in easdem partes et subtrahendo eas quae vergunt in contrarias, per mutuam illorum actionem (conflictum, pressionem, attractionem) non mutatur* [the quantity of motion, found by adding the forces of bodies in the same directions and subtracting those which tend in contrary directions, is not changed by their mutual action (collision, pressure, attraction)]. But although in pure mechanics this rule is not derived directly from the metaphysical ground from which we have derived the general proposition, its correctness nevertheless in fact rests upon this ground.",["The bracketed English after the Latin rule is my own gloss, supplied in square brackets; Kant gives the Latin alone.","OCR corrected: 'ängst' = längst, 'metaohysischen' = metaphysischen, 'conlictum' = conflictum, 'das quae' = eas quae (the printed 'das' is plainly a slip for the Latin 'eas'; flagged as a conjectural correction)."]],"negative-groessen-0118":["For the law of inertia, which in the usual proof forms the foundation, borrows its truth merely from the ground of proof which has been cited, as I could easily show if I were allowed to be prolix. —\n\n[Footnote fragment:] — ... happens, and as no one can from a boat push another floating body towards one quarter without being himself driven in the opposite direction.",["The initial 'Enn' of the OCR is read as 'Denn'. The text after the dash is a footnote whose beginning is lost ('ne geschieht'; probably the end of a word such as 'eine'/'keine'); I translate only what is there and mark the gap with an ellipsis in square brackets' sense, not filling it.","The footnote's dependent clause ('und wie niemand ...') evidently continues a comparison begun in the missing portion; the doubtful reading is left as it stands."]],"negative-groessen-0119":["The elucidation of the rule with which we are occupied, in the cases of changes that are not mechanical, for example those in our soul, or those which depend in general upon it, is by its nature difficult. For these effects, as well as their grounds, cannot by far be presented so comprehensibly and with such intuitive clarity as those in the corporeal world. Nevertheless I shall try, so far as seems possible to me, to throw some light on the matter. |",["OCR corrected: 'in der! Fällen' = in den Fällen; '3. E.' = z. E.; 'se faßlich' read as 'so faßlich'. The sign | is carried over where it stands at the end of the passage.","'Seele' rendered 'soul'; 'anschauend deutlich' rendered 'with intuitive clarity'. I divided the second sentence of the German into two."]],"negative-groessen-0120":["Aversion is just as much something positive as desire. The first is a consequence of a positive displeasure, as the latter is the positive consequence of a pleasure. Only in so far as we feel pleasure and displeasure at one and the same object, are the desires and aversions relating to it in a real opposition. But in so far as the very same ground that occasions pleasure in one object becomes at the same time the ground of a true displeasure in others, the grounds of desires are at the same time grounds of aversions. The ground of a desire is then at the same time the ground of something that stands in real opposition to it, even though this opposition is only potential. Consider the motions of bodies which move apart from one another in opposite directions along the same straight line. They do not strive to cancel one another's motion, and yet the one is regarded as the negative of the other, because they are potentially opposed. Accordingly, if a person's desire for fame springs up in a given degree, an equally great degree of aversion arises at the same time with regard to the opposite. This aversion is indeed only potential so long as circumstances do not stand in actual opposition in respect of the desire for fame. Nevertheless, through the very same cause that produces the desire for fame, a positive ground of an equal degree of displeasure is established in the soul, in so far as the circumstances of the world might happen to turn out contrary to those which favour the former.*) We shall presently see that this is not so in the most perfect being, and that the ground of his highest pleasure excludes even every possibility of displeasure.",["The OCR 'Luft' (twice, once as 'Luft}') is silently read as 'Lust' (pleasure); stray quotation marks and brackets in the OCR are dropped.","The footnote marker after 'begünstigen' is rendered '*)'; the footnote text is in the following passage (0122).","Restructured the long sentence beginning 'So wie die Bewegungen...' (which lacks a main verb in the German) into 'Consider the motions...' and supplied 'then' and 'Accordingly' for the connectives; 'Diesemnach, ein so großer Grad' is read as 'Diesemnach, so großer Grad'.","Begierde = desire; Verabscheuung/Abscheu = aversion; Unlust = displeasure; Entgegensetzung = opposition; *potential* rendered 'potential' (Kant's adverbial use)."]],"negative-groessen-0121":["In the actions of the understanding we find, moreover, that the higher the degree in which a certain idea is made clear or distinct, the",["Breaks off at 'deste' (= desto); the sentence continues in 0122 ('mehr werden die übrige verdunkelt...'). Rendered as it stands without completion."]],"negative-groessen-0122":["*) For this reason the Stoic sage had to root out all such impulses as involve a feeling of great sensuous pleasure, because along with them one plants grounds of great discontent and displeasure, which, by the alternating play of the course of the world, can cancel the whole worth of the former. [continuing from the preceding sentence:] ...more are the rest obscured and their clearness diminished. Thus the positive that actually comes about in such a change is bound up with a real and actual opposition, which, if everything is taken together and estimated in the manner mentioned, neither increases nor diminishes the degree of the positive through the change.",["The passage opens with footnote text (marked '*)' in the translation of 0120) and then continues the sentence broken off in 0121; the OCR 'nehr' is read 'mehr', 'a£wechselnden' 'abwechselnden', 'sealen' 'realen', 'Posi— iven' 'Positiven'. The bracketed phrase marks the join.","'die übrige' is read as 'the rest' (the other ideas), supplying the sense from 0121."]],"negative-groessen-0123":["The second proposition is as follows. All the real grounds of the universe (*Universum*), if those which agree are summed, and those which are opposed to one another are subtracted from one another, give a result that equals zero. The whole of the world is in itself nothing, except in so far as it is something through the will of [another]. The sum of all existing reality, in so far as it is grounded in the world, considered in itself, is therefore equal to zero = 0. Now although all possible reality, in relation to the divine will, gives a result that is positive, the essence of a world is nevertheless not thereby annulled. It is...never—",["Corrected: 'Uni— gersum' to *Universum*; 'iud' to 'und'; 'Luft'-type slips not present here.","Doubtful reading: 'eines dem Etwas ist' is corrupt; I read 'eines Andern' and put [another] in brackets. The text may differ.","The passage ends 'so wird gleich—' (= gleichwohl) and continues in 0124; rendered as 'nevertheless', split across the two passages as 'never— / theless'. Accordingly the last clause of this rendering is simply 'the essence of a world is never—'; see 0124. Real grounds = *Realgründe*; Facit rendered 'result'."]],"negative-groessen-0124":["—theless annulled by this. From this essence of [the world] it follows necessarily that the existence of that which is grounded in it is, in and for itself alone, equal to zero. Thus the",["Begins 'ohl' for '[gleich]wohl'; I give the continuation of the word as '—theless', after the break in 0123. 'Aus diesem Wesen der' lacks a noun; I supply [the world] in brackets. 'Mfo' read 'Also'. Breaks off before 'Summe' (0125)."]],"negative-groessen-0125":["sum of what exists in the world, in relation to that ground",["'umme' read 'Summe'; the passage is a mere fragment, continued in 0126 ('Grund, der außer ihr ist')."]],"negative-groessen-0126":["which is outside it, is positive, but in relation of the inner real grounds to one another it is equal to zero. Now since in the first relation there can never be an opposition of the real grounds of the world to the divine will, there is in this respect no cancellation, and the sum is positive. But since in the second relation the result is zero, it follows that the positive grounds must stand in an opposition in which, considered and summed, they give zero.",["The OCR 'rund' is read 'Grund'; 'sealen' etc. are silently corrected; 'fann' read 'kann'. Joined to the fragment in 0125 ('that ground which is outside it')."]],"negative-groessen-0127":["Remark on the second number.",[]],"negative-groessen-0128":["I have set forth these two propositions with the intention of inviting the reader to reflect upon this subject. I confess also that for my own part they are neither clear enough nor to be seen from their grounds with sufficient evidence. Meanwhile I am very much convinced that unfinished attempts, put forward problematically in abstract knowledge, can be very beneficial to the growth of the higher philosophy. Another will very often find the solution of a deeply hidden question more easily than the person who gave him the occasion for it, and whose efforts may perhaps have overcome only half the difficulties. The content of these propositions seems to me to possess a certain dignity, which may well encourage a careful examination of them, provided only that one has [rightly grasped] their sense, which in this kind of knowledge is not so easy.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'aß ste' to 'daß sie', 'licht' kept as Kant's 'nicht licht genug' (= not clear enough), 'iberführt' to 'überführt', 'tief erborgenen' to 'tief verborgenen', 'zuräglich' to 'zuträglich'.","Doubtful reading: 'wofern man nur ihren Sinn wohl bete' is corrupt (perhaps 'wohl gefaßt hat'); the bracketed 'rightly grasped' is a conjecture.","Divided the long sentence about another finding the solution into two; Erkenntnis = knowledge, Weltweisheit = philosophy."]],"negative-groessen-0129":["Meanwhile I should like to try to forestall some misinterpretations. I should not be understood at all if it were imagined that by the first proposition I had meant to say that the sum of reality in general is neither increased nor diminished by the changes of the world. This is so far from my meaning that the mechanical rule cited by way of example allows exactly the opposite. For by the impact of bodies the sum of the motions is now increased, now diminished, if one considers it by itself; but the result, estimated in the manner added at the same time, is what remains the same. For the oppositions are in many cases only potential, where the moving forces do not actually cancel one another and where an increase therefore takes place. But according to the estimation once adopted as the rule, these too must be subtracted from one another.",["The stray '4' at the end of the OCR is dropped. 'Bewegkräfte' = moving forces. 'Facit' rendered 'result'."]],"negative-groessen-0130":["One must judge in the same way in applying this proposition to non-mechanical changes. It would be an equal misunderstanding if it occurred to anyone that by the very same proposition the perfection of the world could not grow at all. For this proposition by no means denies that the sum of reality in general should be capable of being increased in the natural course. Moreover, the perfection of the world in general consists very largely in this conflict of the opposed real grounds, just as the material part of the world is quite evidently maintained in a regular course merely by the strife of forces. And it is always a great misunderstanding to regard the sum of reality as one and the same with the magnitude of perfection. We have seen above that displeasure is just as positive as pleasure; but who would call it a perfection?",["Corrected 'sich)' and the broken 'Voll kommenheit' silently. 'Conflictus' appears in Kant in Latin; I render it 'conflict'. Perhaps 'natürlicher Weise' is better 'in the natural course'; I took it so. Realität = reality; Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"negative-groessen-0131":["3. We have already remarked that it is often hard to decide whether certain negations in nature are mere deficiencies [*Mängel*], arising from the absence of a ground, or privations [*Beraubungen*], arising from the real opposition of two positive grounds. In the material world examples of this are frequent. The contiguous parts of every body press against one another with true forces (of attraction), and the consequence of these strivings would be a diminution of the volume of space, if actions as truly real did not oppose them in equal degree through the repulsion of the elements, the effect of which is the ground of impenetrability. Here there is rest, not because moving forces are lacking, but because they work against one another. In the same way the weights on the two arms of a balance are at rest when they are placed on the lever according to the laws of equilibrium.\n\nThis concept can be extended far beyond the limits of the material world. When we believe ourselves to be in a complete inactivity of the mind, it is by no means necessary that the sum of the real grounds of thinking and desiring should be smaller than in the state in which some degrees of this activity reveal themselves to consciousness. Tell the most learned man, at a moment when he is idle and at rest, that he is to relate something and let us hear his insight. He knows nothing; and you find him in this state empty, without any determinate reflections or judgments. But give him only an occasion, by a question or by judgments of your own, and his learning reveals itself in a series of activities so directed that they make possible, for him and for you, the consciousness of this insight of his. Without doubt the real grounds for it were long present in him; but since the consequence, in respect of consciousness, was zero, they must to that extent have been opposed to one another.\n\nSo it is with the thunder which art has invented for destruction. It lies in a prince's arsenal, stored up for a future war, in threatening silence, until, when a treacherous tinder touches it, it flashes up in lightning and lays waste everything around. The springs, which were unceasingly ready to burst forth, lay bound within it by powerful attraction, and awaited the stimulus of a spark of fire to set themselves free.\n\nThere is something great, and, as it seems to me, very just, in the thought of Herr von Leibniz: the soul embraces the whole universe with its power of representation [*Vorstellungskraft*], although only an infinitely small part of these representations is clear. In fact all kinds of concepts [*Begriffe*] must rest solely upon the inner activity of our mind as their ground. External things may indeed contain the condition under which these concepts manifest themselves in one way or another, but not the power actually to produce them. The thinking power [*Denkungskraft*] of the soul must contain real grounds for all of them, so many as are naturally to arise in it; and the phenomena of modes of knowledge [*Kenntnisse*] that arise and pass away are, to all appearance, to be ascribed only to the agreement or opposition of all these activities. These judgments may be regarded as elucidations of the first proposition of the preceding section.",["OCR slips silently corrected: set→sei, Nealentgegensetzung→Realentgegensetzung, Pewußtsein→Bewußtsein; stray line numbers and 'sich)' removed.","The last sentence of the first paragraph of the German was kept as two or three English sentences in places; 'Saget dem gelehrtesten Manne…' is rendered as a hypothetical address, with the imperatives smoothed into narrative.","Realgrund = 'real ground', Realentgegensetzung = 'real opposition' (Kant's technical terms); Mangel = 'deficiency', Beraubung = 'privation' (Latin *privatio*). 'Zero' in the German is kept as 'zero'.","Kenntnisse (not Erkenntnisse) is rendered 'modes of knowledge' to preserve the plural."]],"negative-groessen-0132":["In moral matters, likewise, zero is not always to be regarded as a negation due to deficiency; nor is a positive consequence of greater magnitude always a proof of a greater activity applied in the direction of that consequence. Suppose a man has ten degrees of a passion which in a given case conflicts with the rules of duty, for example avarice. Let him exert twelve degrees of striving according to principles of love of one's neighbour. The consequence is two degrees, that is, so much as he will be beneficent and helpful. Now conceive another man with three degrees of avarice and seven degrees of power [*Vermögen*] to act according to principles of obligation. His action will be four degrees in magnitude, that is, so much as, after the conflict with his desire, he will be of use to another man.\n\nBut it is indisputable that, in so far as the passion in question may be regarded as natural and involuntary, the moral worth of the action of the first man is greater than that of the second, although, if one chose to estimate them by the living force [*lebendige Kraft*], the consequence in the second case exceeds that in the first. For this reason it is impossible for men to infer with certainty from the actions of others the degree of their virtuous disposition; and he who sees into the inmost recesses of hearts has reserved judgment to himself alone.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Graben→Graden, Gelbbegierbe→Geldbegierde; stray '1|', '19', '2' removed.","'Gehet einem Menschen … zehn Grade Leidenschaft' is an imperative construction ('let a man have'); rendered 'Suppose a man has'. The second 'Gedenket euch' as 'Now conceive'.","Restated the tangled middle sentence into two sentences; 'lebendige Kraft' (vis viva) rendered 'living force'."]],"negative-groessen-0133":["4. If one ventures to apply these concepts to the so frail knowledge which men can have of the infinite Godhead, what difficulties then beset our utmost endeavours! Since we can take the foundation of these concepts only from ourselves, it is in most cases obscure whether we are to transfer this idea to that incomprehensible object in the proper sense, or only by means of some analogy. Simonides remains a wise man, who after much hesitation and delay gave his prince the answer: The more I reflect upon God, the less am I able to understand him. Such is not the language of the learned rabble. It knows nothing and understands nothing, yet it talks of everything, and what it says it insists upon.\n\nIn the highest being no grounds of privation or of real opposition can occur. For since everything is given in him and through him, no inner removal [*Aufhebung*] is possible, through the all-possession of determinations [*Bestimmungen*] in his own existence. For this reason the feeling of displeasure [*Unlust*] is not a predicate befitting the Godhead.\n\nMan never has a desire for an object without positively abhorring its opposite; that is, not merely in such a way that the relation of his will to the contradictory opposite of the desire is [mere absence], but in such a way that what is really opposed to it (aversion) is a consequence of positive displeasure. In every desire, such as a faithful guide has [in training] his pupil well, any outcome that does not accord with his desire is positively opposed to him and a ground of displeasure.\n\nThe relations of objects to the divine will are of quite another kind. Strictly, no external thing is a ground either of pleasure or of displeasure in it; for it depends in the least on nothing else, and this pure pleasure does not dwell in him who is blessed through himself because the good exists outside him; rather this good exists because the eternal representation of its possibility, and the pleasure bound up with it, is a ground of the desire carried into effect.\n\nIf one compares with this the concrete representation of the nature of the desire of everything created, one becomes aware that the will of the uncreated can have little in common with it. This will not be unexpected, in respect of the other determinations also, to him who grasps well that the difference in quality must be immeasurable when one compares things of which the one are in themselves nothing, while the other is that through which alone everything is.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Umluft→Unlust, stray digits and 'z', 's', '5', '0' marks removed.","Doubtful sentence: 'Bei jeder Begierde, die ein treuer Führer hat seinen Schüler wohl zu ziehen' is syntactically obscure (a comma may be missing or text corrupt); my rendering with 'in training' is a conjecture, bracketed.","Supplied [mere absence] in the sentence on contradictory opposite: the German has only 'das contradictorische Gegentheil der Begierde, sondern …', and the main verb/predicate is elided; the supplied phrase is a conjecture from the argument (cf. Kant's distinction of privation and mere lack).","'Aufhebung' = removal/cancellation; 'Allbesitz der Bestimmungen' = all-possession of determinations; 'Unlust' = displeasure (pain), 'Lust' = pleasure.","Divided the long paragraph into several for readability; no content added."]],"negative-groessen-0134":["General Remark.",[]],"negative-groessen-0135":["Since the number of thorough philosophers, as they style themselves, grows greater daily, in that they look so deeply into all things that nothing remains hidden from them which they could not explain and comprehend,",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence and is translated as it stands, without completing it."]],"negative-groessen-0136":["I already foresee that the concept of real opposition [*Realentgegensetzung*], which I laid at the foundation at the beginning of this treatise, will appear to them very shallow, and the concept of negative quantities [*negative Größen*], which has been built upon it, not sufficiently profound. I make no secret of the weakness of my insight, by reason of which I commonly understand least of all what everyone else believes it easy to understand. I flatter myself that this very incapacity gives me a claim to the assistance of these great minds, so that their lofty wisdom may fill the gap which my deficient insight has had to leave.",["Restated the long final sentence as two sentences; the relative clause 'nach welcher ich gemeiniglich …' is made into a main clause for clarity.","'ihnen' (the great minds, *großen Geister*, named only at the end) refers to the critics the preceding context presumably introduces; not translated as a noun since the passage begins mid-sentence.","'o' after 'gebauet' in the OCR is a stray mark and has been dropped.","*Größen* rendered 'quantities' as in the title of the treatise; *Einsicht* = 'insight'."]],"negative-groessen-0137":["I understand very well how a consequence is posited by a ground according to the rule of identity: it is posited because it is found, through analysis of the concepts, to be contained in the ground. Thus necessity is a ground of immutability, composition a ground of divisibility, infinity a ground of omniscience, and so on. This connection of the ground with the consequence I can clearly see, because the consequence is really identical with a partial concept [*Theilbegriff*] of the ground, and, being already comprised in it, is posited through it according to the rule of agreement [*Einstimmung*]. But how something flows from something else, yet not according to the rule of identity, is something I should gladly have made clear to me. The first kind of ground I call the logical ground, because its relation to the consequence can be seen logically, that is, distinctly, according to the rule of identity. The ground of the second kind I call the real ground [*Realgrund*], because this relation certainly belongs to my true concepts, but the manner of it can in no way be judged.",["Divided the opening sentence of the passage and the final sentence for readability.","'Be— ziehung' is a line-break hyphenation in the OCR, silently repaired.","*Folge* = 'consequence'; *Grund* = 'ground'; *deutlich* = 'distinctly' or 'clearly' as the context suits.","Kemp Smith's practice would favour 'concept' for *Begriff* throughout; 'partial concept' is a literal choice for *Theilbegriff*."]],"negative-groessen-0138":["As regards this real ground and its relation to the consequence, my question presents itself in this simple form: how am I to understand that, because something is, something else is? A logical consequence is properly posited only because it is identical with the ground. Man can err; the ground of this fallibility lies in the finitude of his nature. For when I analyse the concept of a finite spirit, I see that fallibility lies in it, that is, is identical with what is contained in the concept of a spirit. But the will of God contains the real ground of the existence of the world. The divine will is something; the existing world is something quite different. Yet through the one the other is posited. The state in which I hear the name 'Stagirite' is something; through it something else, namely my thought of a philosopher, is posited. A body A is in motion, another body B, in the straight line of A's motion, is at rest. The motion of A is something, that of B is something else, and yet through the one the other is posited. Analyse the concept of the divine willing as much as you please, you will never find an existing world in it, unless it be contained in it and posited through it by reason of identity; and so in the remaining cases.\n\nNor will I let myself be put off with the words cause and effect, force and action. For if I already regard something as a cause of something, or attribute to it the concept of a force, then I have already thought in it the relation of the real ground to the consequence, and it is then easy to see the position of the consequence according to the rule of identity. For example, through the almighty will of God one can quite distinctly understand the existence of the world. But here almightiness [*Macht*] signifies that something in God through which other things are posited. This word, however, already designates the relation of a real ground to the consequence, which I should gladly have explained to me.\n\nI observe only in passing that the division made by Herr Crusius into ideal ground and real ground is wholly different from mine. For his ideal ground is identical with the ground of knowledge [*Erkenntnißgrund*], and it is easy to see that if I already regard something as a ground, I can infer the consequence from it. Hence, according to his propositions, the evening wind is a real ground of rain-clouds and at the same time an ideal ground, because I can recognize them from it and anticipate them. According to our concepts, however, the real ground is never a logical ground, and rain is not posited through the wind according to the rule of identity. The distinction of logical and real opposition set forth by us above runs parallel to the distinction here considered between logical and real ground.",["Divided the long passage into paragraphs at 'Ich lasse mich auch …' and 'Gelegentlich …'; the German is a single block.","'Fehlbarkeit' = 'fallibility'; 'Geist' = 'spirit'. Kant's 'Stagirit' (Aristotle) kept as 'Stagirite'.","'Macht' (in 'allmächtig') rendered 'almightiness'; the German plays on *Macht* meaning power, and 'dieses Wort' refers to *Macht*.","'ge— dacht', 'Real— grund' etc. are line-break hyphenations repaired. 'wovon' in 'Ursache wovon' rendered 'cause of something'.","In 'zu folge' the OCR spacing is normalized to 'zufolge'; the clause 'wird … nicht … gesetzt' is translated as 'is not posited'.","Obscure OCR 'als wenn sie darin enthalten' rendered 'unless it be contained'; the construction is elliptical in the German."]],"negative-groessen-0139":["The former [logical opposition] I see distinctly by means of the principle of contradiction, and I comprehend how, when I posit the infinity of God, the predicate of mortality is thereby cancelled, because it contradicts that infinity. But how the motion of one body is cancelled by the motion of another, since the latter does not stand in contradiction with the former, is another question. If I presuppose impenetrability, which stands in real opposition to every force that strives to penetrate into the space occupied by a body, I can indeed understand the cancelling of the motions; but then I have only reduced one real opposition to another.\n\nLet it now be tried whether real opposition in general can be explained, and whether one can make distinctly known how, because something is, something else is cancelled; and whether anything more can be said than what I have said of it, namely merely that it does not come about through the principle of contradiction. I have reflected upon the nature of our knowledge in respect of our judgments concerning grounds and consequences, and I shall at some future time set out the result of these reflections in detail. It turns out from it that the relation of a real ground to something that is thereby posited or cancelled cannot be expressed by a judgment at all, but only by a concept. This concept can indeed be brought by analysis to simpler concepts of real grounds, yet in such a way that in the end all our knowledge of this relation terminates in simple and unanalysable concepts of real grounds, whose relation to the consequence cannot be made distinct at all. Until then, those whose presumed insight knows no limits will try the methods of their philosophy, to see how far they can get with a question of this kind.",["'Die erstere' refers back to logical opposition (*logische Entgegensetzung*); supplied in square brackets.","Divided at 'Man versuche nun …' to give a new paragraph; also broke the long final sentences into shorter ones.","'ftd)' in the OCR is read as 'sich'; 'findet ftd)' = 'findet sich'.","'Wider— sprüche', 'Be— wegung' etc. are hyphenation artefacts, repaired.","*Erkenntnis* = 'knowledge'; *Unauflöslich* = 'unanalysable'."]],"beobachtungen-0001":["Observations\non\nthe Feeling\nof the\nBeautiful and Sublime.\nM. Immanuel Kant,",["OCR 'Srhabenen' corrected to 'Erhabenen'.","'M.' stands for *Magister*; kept as 'M.'."]],"beobachtungen-0002":["First Section.",[]],"beobachtungen-0003":["On the Different Objects of the Feeling of the",["The heading breaks off and continues in the next passage ('Sublime and Beautiful')."]],"beobachtungen-0004":["Sublime and Beautiful.",["Continuation of the heading in the preceding passage."]],"beobachtungen-0005":["The different sensations of pleasure or displeasure rest not so much on the constitution of the external things that arouse them as on the feeling, peculiar to each man, of being touched by them with pleasure or displeasure. Hence arise the joys of some men at which others feel disgust, the amorous passion, which is often a riddle to everyone, or again the lively aversion which one man feels toward something that is quite a matter of indifference to another. The field of observation of these peculiarities of human nature extends very far, and still conceals a rich store of discoveries, which are as agreeable as they are instructive. For the present I cast my glance only upon a few places in this region which seem to stand out especially, and even upon these with the eye more of an observer than of a philosopher.",["OCR 'Ckel' corrected to 'Ekel'.","*Empfindung* = 'sensation'; *Gefühl* = 'feeling'; *Lust/Unlust* = 'pleasure/displeasure'.","Split the second sentence's clause chain slightly in the English; the Kantian sentence structure is otherwise followed closely."]],"beobachtungen-0006":["Because a man finds himself happy only in so far as he satisfies an inclination, the feeling which makes him capable of enjoying great pleasures, without needing for that purpose any exceptional talents, is certainly no trifle. Corpulent persons, whose wittiest author is their cook, and whose works of his taste are to be found in their cellar, will fall into as lively a joy over vulgar obscenities and a coarse jest as that of which persons of noble sensation are so proud. A comfortable man who loves the reading of books because it is so easy to fall asleep over them; the merchant to whom all pleasures seem silly, excepting what a shrewd man enjoys when he reckons up his business profit; he who loves the other sex only in so far as he counts it among enjoyable things; the lover of the chase, whether he hunts flies, like Domitian, or wild beasts, like A. . .: all these have a feeling which makes them capable of enjoying pleasure after their own kind, without having to envy others, or even being able to form a concept of what others enjoy. For the present, however, I give this no attention. There is a feeling of a finer kind. It is so entitled either because one can enjoy it longer without satiety and exhaustion; or because it presupposes, so to speak, an excitability of the soul which at the same time makes the soul fit for virtuous impulses; or because it indicates talents and merits of understanding [Verstandesvorzüge], whereas the former kind of feeling can occur along with complete thoughtlessness. It is this feeling of which I propose to consider one side. From it, however, I exclude the inclination which is fixed upon lofty insights of the understanding, and the charm of which a Kepler was capable when, as Bayle reports, he would not have sold one of his discoveries for a principality. This sensation is far too fine to find a place in the present sketch, ge-",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Lergnügen' read as Vergnügen; 'so 10 genannt' (line number dropped); 'zu sein' read as 'zu fein'; 'ge— nießbaren' joined.","'A. . .' stands in the source as given; the name is not recoverable from the text, and I have not filled it in.","The passage breaks off in the middle of the word 'gehören'; the remainder is in the next passage and is left uncompleted here.","The long sentence on the finer feeling is divided: the three grounds are set out in separate clauses. 'Genannt wird' is rendered 'is so entitled'; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Verstandesvorzüge' = merits of understanding.","'Jene' (the former) in the last of the three grounds refers to the coarser feelings of the preceding examples; I have written 'the former kind of feeling' to make this plain."]],"beobachtungen-0007":["[be]long [gehören], [in the sketch] which will touch only the sensible feeling of which even commoner souls are capable.",["Continues the broken word 'ge-hören' from the previous passage; the stray 'no' in the OCR is dropped as an obvious slip.","'Welcher' has 'Entwurf' (sketch) as antecedent; the bracketed words are supplied by judgment of the syntax. 'Sinnliches Gefühl' = sensible feeling."]],"beobachtungen-0008":["The finer feeling which we are now to consider is chiefly of two kinds: the feeling of the sublime and that of the beautiful. The stirring [Rührung] produced by both is agreeable, but in very different ways. The sight of a mountain range whose snow-covered peaks rise above the clouds, the description of a raging storm, or Milton's depiction of the infernal kingdom, arouse satisfaction, but with dread. On the other hand, the prospect of flowery meadows, of valleys with winding brooks, covered with grazing herds, the description of Elysium, or Homer's depiction of the girdle of Venus, likewise occasion an agreeable sensation, but one which is joyous and smiling. If the former impression is to be made upon us with due strength, we must have a feeling of the sublime; and if we are to enjoy the latter properly, a feeling for the beautiful. Lofty oaks and lonely shadows in a sacred grove are sublime; flower-beds, low hedges and trees cut into figures are beautiful. Night is sublime, the day",["OCR: 'Lag' read as Tag; stray 'ss', 'A', 'as;' and line numbers dropped.","The sentence 'Damit jener Eindruck ...' is restated with 'If ... then' for clarity; 'jener' (the former) = the impression of the sublime, 'die letztere' = that of the beautiful.","The passage breaks off before 'ist schön', which the next passage supplies."]],"beobachtungen-0009":["is beautiful. Dispositions of mind [Gemüthsarten] which possess a feeling for the sublime are",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the following passages. 'Gemüthsarten' rendered 'dispositions of mind'."]],"beobachtungen-0010":["drawn on gradually, by the quiet stillness of a summer evening, when the trembling",["Fragment; 'werden' is divided from the preceding passage ('wer-den'). I have supplied 'drawn on' from the participle 'gezogen' which stands in a later passage, in the English word order only."]],"beobachtungen-0011":["light of the stars breaks through the brown shadows of the night, and",[]],"beobachtungen-0012":["the solitary moon stands in the horizon, into lofty sensations of friendship, of contempt for the world, of eternity.",["'Gezogen' (drawn) completes the verb begun two passages earlier; the English order is adjusted accordingly. OCR 'Empfindun— s gen' joined as 'Empfindungen'."]],"beobachtungen-0013":["The bright day inspires busy zeal and a feeling of gaiety. The sublime stirs, the beautiful charms. The mien of the man",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0014":["who finds himself in the full feeling of the sublime is serious, at times rigid and astonished.",[]],"beobachtungen-0015":["The lively sensation of the beautiful, on the other hand, announces itself by a shining cheerfulness in the eyes, by smiling features,",["OCR 'Lächlens' retained as 'smiling'; stray 'o' and line number dropped."]],"beobachtungen-0016":["and often by loud merriment. The sublime, again, is of different",["Breaks off at 'verschiede-', completed by 'ner Art' in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0017":["kinds. The feeling of it is at times accompanied by some dread or",[]],"beobachtungen-0018":["else melancholy; in some cases merely by quiet admiration; and",[]],"beobachtungen-0019":["in still others by a beauty spread over a sublime plan. The first I shall call the terrifying sublime, the second the noble, and the third the splendid. Deep solitude",["OCR 'Schöns heit' read as Schönheit. 'Schreckhaft-Erhabene', 'Edle', 'Prächtige' rendered as the terrifying sublime, the noble, the splendid.","The footnote marker after 'Einsamkeit' falls in the next passage; the footnote is given under beobachtungen-0020."]],"beobachtungen-0020":["*) I shall give only one example of the noble dread which the description of a complete solitude can inspire, and for that reason I extract some passages from Carazan's Dream in the Bremen Magazine, Volume IV, page 539. This miserly rich man had, in proportion as his riches increased, closed his heart to compassion and to love towards everyone else. Meanwhile, as his love of mankind grew cold, the diligence of his prayers and religious observances increased. After this confession he continues to speak thus: 'One evening, as I was casting up my accounts by my lamp and reckoning my business profit, sleep overpowered me. In this state I saw the angel of death come upon me like a whirlwind; he struck me before I could beg off the terrible blow. I grew rigid when I perceived that my lot was cast for eternity, and that to all the good I had done nothing could be added, and from all the evil I had done nothing taken away. I was led before the throne of him who dwells in the third heaven. The radiance that flamed before me addressed me thus: Carazan, your service of God is rejected. You have closed your heart to the love of mankind, and have held your treasures with an iron hand. You have lived for yourself alone, and therefore you shall also live henceforth in eternity alone, cast out from all communion with the whole creation. In this moment I was torn away by an invisible power and driven through the shining edifice of creation. I soon left countless worlds behind me. As I approached the outermost end of nature, I perceived that the shadows of the boundless void",["Footnote, retained as such. OCR corrected: 'Hand IV' read as Band IV; 'Gebeter' as Gebete; 'sach' as sah; 'dieb' as dich; 'Cara zans' as Carazans.","'Brem. Magazin' expanded as Bremen Magazine (Bremer Magazin). 'Handlungsvortheil' = business profit.","The text breaks off at 'sich' ('...des grenzenlosen Leeren sich'), a reflexive whose verb lies in the next portion; I translate only what is there, and 'sich' is left untranslated.","'Du' is rendered 'you', not 'thou', in the divine address."]],"beobachtungen-0021":["[solitude] is sublime, but in a terrifying way.*) Hence great, far-extended wildernesses, such as the immense desert Shamo in Tartary, have at all times given occasion for placing there fearful shadows, goblins and spectral phantoms.",["Continues 'Einsam-keit' from the previous passage; I supply 'solitude' in brackets for the severed word. The asterisk refers to the footnote under beobachtungen-0020.","'Gespensterlarven' rendered 'spectral phantoms'; the German hyphenation 'Gespenster—larven' is joined."]],"beobachtungen-0022":["The sublime must always be great; the beautiful can also be small. The sublime must be simple; the beautiful can be adorned and ornamented. A great height is just as sublime as a great depth; but the latter is accompanied by the sensation of shuddering, the former by admiration. Hence the latter sensation can be terrifyingly sublime, and the former noble. The sight of an Egyptian pyramid stirs one, as Hasselquist reports, far more than one can imagine from any description; but its construction is simple and noble. St Peter's in Rome is splendid. Because on this plan, which is great and simple, beauty (e.g. gold, mosaic work, etc., etc.) is so spread that the sensation of the sublime nevertheless works through most strongly, the object is entitled splendid. An arsenal must be noble and simple, a royal residence splendid, and a pleasure-palace beautiful and ornamented.",["OCR corrected: 'Hau' read as Bau; '[o verbreitet' as 'so verbreitet'. 'Pyramiden' with 'einer' is plural in the source; I follow the singular article and render 'pyramid'. Possibly a slip for 'Pyramide'.","'Einfältig' rendered 'simple'. 'Heißt' = 'is entitled'. 'Am meisten hindurch wirkt' rendered 'works through most strongly', with 'strongly' supplied by judgment.","The long sentence 'Weil auf diesen Entwurf ...' is kept as one, with the examples set in parentheses."]],"beobachtungen-0023":["A long duration is sublime. If it belongs to time past, it is noble; if it is foreseen in an unfathomable future, it has something of the terrible about it. A building from the remotest antiquity is venerable. Haller's description of future eternity inspires a gentle horror, and his description of the past, a rigid admiration. [Here the text breaks into a fragment:] … sank down before me into the depths. A dreadful realm of eternal silence, solitude and darkness! Unspeakable horror overcame me at this sight. Little by little I lost the last stars from view, and at last the final glimmering gleam of light was extinguished in the outermost darkness. The mortal anguish of despair increased with every moment, just as every moment increased my distance from the last inhabited world. With unendurable anguish of heart I reflected that, even if ten thousand times a thousand years should have carried me farther beyond the bounds of all that is created, I should still be gazing forward into the immeasurable abyss of darkness, without help or hope of any return. — — In this stupor I stretched out my hands with such violence towards objects of actuality that I awoke. And now I have been taught to esteem human beings highly; for even the humblest of those whom, in the pride of my good fortune, I had turned away from my door would in that terrible wilderness have been preferred by me far above all the treasures of Golconda. — —",["The passage as given runs the main text straight into the Carazan dream, which begins mid-sentence ('in die Tiefe vor mich herabsenkten'). This looks like a footnote misplaced by the OCR or the portal's segmentation. I translate what is there and mark the join with a bracketed remark.","'Ewig— keit' (hyphenation) and 'Cinsamkeit' (for Einsamkeit) silently corrected; 'Wirklichkeit' rendered 'actuality'."]],"beobachtungen-0024":["Second Section.",[]],"beobachtungen-0025":["On the Attributes of the Sublime and the Beautiful in Man in General.",["The title runs on into the next passage ('Menschen überhaupt'); I have joined it here, although the next passage also translates its own portion."]],"beobachtungen-0026":["[On the attributes of the sublime and beautiful in] man in general.",["Continuation of the heading in the previous id; the words in brackets are supplied from it."]],"beobachtungen-0027":["Understanding is sublime, wit is beautiful. Boldness is sublime and great; cunning is petty, but beautiful. Circumspection, said Cromwell, is a burgomaster's virtue. Truthfulness and honesty are simple and noble; jest and obliging flattery are fine and beautiful. Courtesy [*Artigkeit*] is the beauty of virtue. Disinterested zeal in service is noble; polish (*Politesse*) and civility are beautiful. Sublime attributes inspire esteem, beautiful ones, however, love. People whose feeling inclines chiefly to the beautiful seek out their honest, steadfast and serious friends only in time of need, but they choose the jesting, courteous and civil companion for their daily intercourse. Many a man is esteemed far too highly for one to be able to love him. He inspires admiration, but he is too far above us for us to venture to approach him with the familiarity of love.",["OCR 'Biürgermeistertugend' read as Bürgermeistertugend; 'sein' (twice) read as 'fein' (fine).","Erhaben = sublime, edel = noble, schön = beautiful, Hochachtung = esteem; 'Artigkeit' is a choice ('courtesy'), as is 'Geschliffenheit' = 'polish'."]],"beobachtungen-0028":["Those who unite both feelings in themselves will find that the emotion [*Rührung*] produced by the sublime is more powerful than that produced by the beautiful, only that without alternation with, or accompaniment by, the latter it fatigues and cannot be enjoyed so long.† The lofty sensations to which conversation in a well-chosen company sometimes rises must in the interval dissolve into cheerful jest, and the laughing joys should form a fine contrast with the moved and serious mien, so that the two kinds of sensation may alternate without constraint. Friendship has chiefly the character of the sublime, the love between the sexes that of the beautiful. Yet tenderness and",["The footnote marker (OCR '“)') is rendered †; the footnote text follows in the next id.","The sentence breaks off ('Doch geben Zärtlichkeit und'); it is continued in the next passage after the footnote, and I leave it unfinished.","'Empfindung' kept as 'sensation' according to the fixed terms; 'Gefühl' = 'feeling'."]],"beobachtungen-0029":["† The sensations of the sublime strain the powers of the soul more strongly and therefore fatigue sooner. One will be able to read a pastoral poem longer at a stretch than Milton's *Paradise Lost*, and La Bruyère longer than Young. It even seems to me a fault in the latter, as a moral poet, that he holds far too uniformly to the sublime tone; for the force of the impression can be renewed only by contrasts with gentler passages. In the case of the beautiful nothing fatigues more than laboured art that betrays itself. The effort to charm is felt as painful and burdensome.\n\n[Continuation of the sentence broken off above:] profound esteem lend to the latter a certain dignity and sublimity, whereas frolicsome jesting and familiarity heighten the colouring of the beautiful in this sensation. Tragedy, in my opinion, differs from comedy chiefly in this: that in the former the feeling for the sublime is moved, in the latter that for the beautiful. In the former we find magnanimous self-sacrifice for the welfare of others, bold resolution in danger, and tried fidelity. Love there is melancholy, tender and full of esteem; the misfortune of others stirs sympathetic sensations in the spectator's breast and makes his magnanimous heart beat for the distress of others. He is gently moved and feels the dignity of his own nature. Comedy, on the other hand, presents intrigues, strange complications, and wits who know how to extricate themselves, fools who let themselves be deceived, jests and ludicrous characters. Love here is not so morose; it is merry and familiar. Yet here, as in other cases, the noble may in a certain degree be united with the beautiful.",["The footnote text, which in the OCR stands in the middle of the main text, is placed first and marked †; the main text then resumes with the fragment that completes the sentence 'Doch geben Zärtlichkeit und … '. This is a restatement of the order, not of the content.","OCR 'de ia Bruyere' read as de la Bruyère; 'sich)' (stray bracket) and 'liebe' (for 'liche', i.e. lächerliche) corrected silently; 'erste— ren' mended.","'Lustspiel'/'Trauerspiel' = comedy/tragedy."]],"beobachtungen-0030":["Even vices and moral failings often carry with them some traits of the sublime or the beautiful; at least as they appear to our sensible feeling, without having been examined by reason. The wrath of one who inspires fear is sublime, like the wrath of Achilles in the *Iliad*. In general Homer's hero is terribly sublime, Virgil's, on the other hand, noble. Open and daring revenge after a great injury has something great about it, and however unlawful it may be, it nevertheless moves us in the narration with horror and with satisfaction. When Shah Nadir was set upon at night in his tent by some conspirators, he cried out, as Hanway relates, after he had already received some wounds and was defending himself in despair: Mercy! I will forgive you all. One of them answered, raising his sabre: You have shown no mercy, and you deserve none. Resolute audacity in a rogue is extremely dangerous, but it nevertheless moves us in the narration; and even when he is dragged to an ignominious death, he in a certain way ennobles it by going to meet it defiantly and with scorn. On the other hand, a cunningly devised scheme, even if it aims at a piece of villainy, has about it something that is fine and provokes laughter. Amorous inclination (coquetry), in the refined sense, that is, an assiduity in winning and charming, in an otherwise amiable | person is perhaps blameworthy, but yet beautiful, and is commonly",["Silent OCR corrections: 'hoch' → 'hob' (raising); 'haft' → 'hast'; 'seine Erbarmung' → 'keine'; 'vergehen' → 'verzeihen'; 'ste' → 'sie'; 'sein' → 'fein'; 'Perfor' → 'Person'. The 'keine' and 'verzeihen' readings are inferred from the sense and should be checked against the original.","The sentence breaks off and continues in the next id. The page-break sign | is kept where it stands."]],"beobachtungen-0031":["preferred to honourable, serious propriety.",["Completes the sentence of the previous id."]],"beobachtungen-0032":["The figure of persons who please by their outward appearance",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next id."]],"beobachtungen-0033":["falls now under one kind of feeling, now under the other. A large",["Line-break fragment, joined in sense with the neighbouring ids."]],"beobachtungen-0034":["stature wins respect and esteem, a small one more familiarity. Even a brownish complexion and black eyes are more closely related to the sublime, blue eyes and a fair complexion to the beautiful. A",["'Ansehen und Achtung' rendered 'respect and esteem'; OCR 'sich)' and 'Vertraulich— keit' corrected."]],"beobachtungen-0035":["somewhat greater age accords more with the attributes of the sublime,",[]],"beobachtungen-0036":["youth, however, with those of the beautiful. So it is also with the difference of ranks; and in all these relations just mentioned",["The stray 'o' in the OCR is dropped. 'Stände' = ranks (estates)."]],"beobachtungen-0037":["dress itself must even conform to this difference of feeling. Large, imposing persons must observe simplicity, at most splendour, in their dress; small ones may be decked out and adorned.",["The sentence begins in the previous id ('in allen diesen … Beziehungen müssen sogar die Kleidungen …') and I have rejoined it in sense."]],"beobachtungen-0038":["Darker colours and uniformity of attire befit age; youth shines in lighter and vividly contrasting articles of dress.",["'Kleidungsstücke' is split across the line ('Klei-' / 'dungsstücke')."]],"beobachtungen-0039":["Among the ranks, with equal means and standing,",[]],"beobachtungen-0040":["the clergyman must display the greatest simplicity, the statesman the most splendour. The *cicisbeo* may adorn himself as he pleases.",["*Cicisbeo* (the acknowledged gallant of a married lady) is kept in the Italian form."]],"beobachtungen-0041":["Even in external circumstances of fortune there is something that, at least according to men's fancy, falls under these sensations.",["'Wahn' rendered 'fancy'; the sentence is completed in the next id."]],"beobachtungen-0042":["Birth and title commonly find men disposed to respect. Wealth, even without merit, is honoured even by the disinterested, presumably",["The sentence breaks off and runs on into the next id."]],"beobachtungen-0043":["because projects of great actions are [bound up] with its representation [*Vorstellung*]—",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('ver—', presumably 'verbinden' or 'verknüpfen'); I do not complete it. The bracketed words are supplied only to make the fragment readable in English."]],"beobachtungen-0044":["… [which] could be carried out by it. This esteem occasionally falls even to some rich scoundrel who will never perform such actions, and who has no concept of the noble feeling which",["The passage opens mid-sentence with a truncated word, 'einbaren'. I could not recover the reading and rendered it only as far as the sense allows (the antecedent of 'die' is lost)."]],"beobachtungen-0045":["alone can make riches valuable. What increases the evil of",[]],"beobachtungen-0046":["poverty is the contempt it brings. Not even merit can wholly outweigh this contempt, at least not in the eyes of the common run of people, unless rank and title deceive this coarse feeling and to some extent beguile it to its own advantage. | In human nature praiseworthy qualities are never found without degenerate forms of them, which pass through endless shadings down to the extreme of imperfection. The quality of the terrifying-sublime, when it becomes wholly unnatural, is adventurous.*) Unnatural things, so far as the sublime is meant in them, are grotesques [*Fratzen*], even though little or nothing of the sublime is to be met with in them. He who loves and believes the adventurous is a fantast; inclination to grotesques makes the crotchet-monger [*Grillenfänger*]. On the other hand, the feeling for the beautiful degenerates when the noble is wholly lacking in it, and is then called silly [*läppisch*]. A man of this quality is called a dawdler [*Lasse*] if he is young, and a fop [*Geck*] if he is of middle age. Since the sublime is most necessary in old age, an old fop is the most contemptible creature in nature, just as a young crotchet-monger is the most repulsive and intolerable. Jests and gaiety belong to the feeling for the beautiful. Yet a fair amount of understanding [*Verstand*] may still shine through them, and to that extent they can be more or less akin to the sublime. He in whose gaiety this admixture is imperceptible talks nonsense [*faselt*]. He who does so constantly is silly. It is easily seen that even clever people sometimes talk nonsense, and that no little wit is required to call the understanding away from its post for a short time without anything going amiss. He whose speeches or actions neither amuse nor move is tedious. The tedious man, so far as he is nevertheless busy trying to do both, is insipid. The insipid man, if he is puffed up, is a fool.**)",["The single '|' stands where it does in the OCR and probably marks the page break (213/214); the other OCR line-break bars and hyphenation fragments ('Ver— dienste', 'lang— | weilig') were silently removed.","OCR slips corrected: 'in [o fern' = 'in so fern'; 'as | daß' = 'und daß'; 'sei- i new Posten' = 'seinem Posten'; 'aufgeblafen' = 'aufgeblasen'. The footnote signs *) and **) are placed where the OCR's stray ')' occur (after 'abenteuerlich' and 'Narr'); this placement is conjectural.","Restated the long opening sentence of the passage as two sentences. 'Abenteuerlich' = adventurous; 'Fratzen' = grotesques; 'Grillenfänger', 'Lasse', 'Geck' are choices made for English equivalents, and the German is given where the sense turns on it."]],"beobachtungen-0047":["*) So far as sublimity or beauty exceeds the familiar mean, it is customary to call it romantic.",["OCR 'rom anisch' read as 'romanisch'; I take it as Kant's 'romantisch' (as the sense requires), a likely OCR slip."]],"beobachtungen-0048":["**) One soon notices that this venerable society divides into two lodges, that of the crotchet-mongers and that of the fops. A learned crotchet-monger is politely called a pedant. When he assumes the defiant mien of wisdom, like the dunces of ancient and modern times, the cap and bells suit his face well. The class of fops is met with more in the great world. It is perhaps even better than the former. One has much to earn from them and much to laugh at. In this caricature, nevertheless, one makes a wry face at another, and bumps his empty head against the head of his brother.",["Line numbers removed; 'sich)' read as 'sich'."]],"beobachtungen-0049":["I shall try to make this curious sketch of human weaknesses somewhat more intelligible by examples; for he who lacks Hogarth's graver must make up by description for what the drawing lacks in expression. Bold undertaking of dangers for our own rights, or those of our country or our friends, is sublime. The Crusades and the old chivalry were adventurous; duels, a wretched remnant",["Line number '25' removed. The sentence breaks off at the passage boundary and is left unfinished."]],"beobachtungen-0050":["of the latter, arising from a perverse concept of the honour of reputation, are grotesques. Melancholy withdrawal from the bustle of the world, out of a legitimate weariness, is noble. The solitary devotion of the old hermits was adventurous. Monasteries and similar tombs, in which living saints are shut up, are grotesques. Conquest of one's passions by principles is sublime. Mortifications, vows and other monkish virtues of the kind are grotesques. Holy bones, holy wood, and all such trumpery, not excluding the holy excrement of the great Lama of Tibet",["Running head ('2. Abschnitt. 215') and OCR debris removed. 'ausei new recht— mäßigen' = 'aus einem rechtmäßigen'; 'Libet' = 'Tibet'. Restated nothing; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0051":["are grotesques. Of the works of wit and of fine feeling, the epic poems of Virgil and Klopstock fall under the noble, those of Homer and Milton under the adventurous. The Metamorphoses",["OCR 'des [o seinen Gefühls' read as 'des so feinen Gefühls'? More probably 'des feinen Gefühls'; rendered 'fine feeling'. Doubtful reading."]],"beobachtungen-0052":["of Ovid are grotesques; the fairy tales of French extravagant wit [*Aberwitz*]",["OCR 'Mber-' read as 'Aber-'."]],"beobachtungen-0053":["are the most wretched grotesques that were ever hatched. Anacreontic",[]],"beobachtungen-0054":["poems are commonly very close to the silly. The works of understanding and acuteness, so far as their objects also contain something for feeling, likewise",["OCR 'Scharfsfinnigkeit' corrected to 'Scharfsinnigkeit'; line number removed."]],"beobachtungen-0055":["take some part in the differences mentioned. The mathematical",[]],"beobachtungen-0056":["representation of the immeasurable magnitude of the universe, and the metaphysical reflections on eternity, providence, and the immortality of our soul, contain a certain sublimity and dignity. On the",["'Weltbau' rendered 'universe'. 'Hin- / gegen' joined across passages (see next)."]],"beobachtungen-0057":["other hand, philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] is also disfigured by much empty subtlety,",[]],"beobachtungen-0058":["and the appearance of thoroughness does not prevent the four syllogistic figures from deserving to be counted among the grotesques of the schools.",["OCR 'fyogistischen' read as 'syllogistischen'."]],"beobachtungen-0059":["Among moral qualities, true virtue alone is sublime. There are nevertheless good moral qualities which are amiable and beautiful, and which, so far as they harmonise with virtue, are also regarded as noble, although strictly they cannot be reckoned as belonging to the virtuous disposition. The judgment on this matter is fine and involved. One certainly cannot call that frame of mind virtuous which is the source of actions to which virtue too would lead, but from a ground which agrees with virtue only by accident, and which by its nature may often conflict with the universal rules of virtue. A certain soft-heartedness, which is easily set up as a warm feeling of compassion, is beautiful and",["OCR 'sein und verwickelt' read as 'fein und verwickelt'; 'auge— sehen' as 'angesehen'; 'Hinauslaufen' kept as 'would lead'. Restated Kant's long sentence with 'but from a ground' kept close to the German; the construction is somewhat irregular and was smoothed. Line number '5' removed."]],"beobachtungen-0060":["amiable; for it shows a kindly sympathy in the fate of other men, to which principles of virtue likewise lead. Yet this good-natured passion is weak and always blind. For suppose this sensation moves you to relieve a person in distress at your own expense, while you owe money to another and thereby put yourself out of a position to fulfil the strict duty of justice. Then plainly the action cannot spring from any virtuous purpose, for such a purpose could not possibly",["'Setzet' is an imperative (second person plural): 'suppose'. I divided the sentence at 'Then plainly'. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0061":["entice you to sacrifice a higher obligation to this blind enchantment. If, on the other hand, universal benevolence toward the human race has become a principle in you, to which you always subordinate your actions, then love toward the person in distress still remains, but it has now been placed, from a higher standpoint, in its true relation to the whole of your duty. Universal benevolence is a ground of sympathy with his misfortune, but also at the same time of justice, by whose prescription you must now refrain from this action. As soon as this feeling has risen to its proper universality, it is sublime, but also colder. For it is not possible that our breast should swell with tender concern for every man's share, and melt in sorrow at every stranger's distress; otherwise the virtuous man, dissolving ceaselessly in pitying tears like Heraclitus, would for all his good-heartedness become nothing more than a soft-hearted idler.”)",["OCR 'ex reizen' read as 'anreizen' ('entice'); line numbers removed. The closing '”)' marks the footnote sign (text of the footnote not given here), and I have left it as it stands.","Restated the closing sentences in slightly shorter units; 'Gesammte Pflicht' = 'whole of your duty'. 'Antheil von Zärtlichkeit' rendered 'tender concern for every man's share'; the phrase is awkward and the rendering is a judgment."]],"beobachtungen-0062":["*) On closer consideration one finds that, lovable as the compassionate quality may be, it nevertheless does not in itself possess the dignity of virtue. A suffering child, or an unfortunate and charming young woman, will fill our heart with this tender sadness, while at the same time we hear with coldness the news of a great battle in which, as is easily imagined, a considerable part of the human race must succumb undeservedly to cruel evils. Many a prince who turned his face away in sadness from a single unfortunate person gave the order for war at the very same time, from a motive that was often vain. | There is here no proportion at all in the effect. How then can one say that universal love of mankind is the cause?",["The OCR scrambles this footnote: the clause 'Es ist hier gar keine Proportion … Ursache sei?' is interleaved with the sentence about the prince. I have reconstructed the order 'Es ist hier gar keine Proportion in der Wirkung, wie kann man denn sagen, daß die allgemeine Menschenliebe die Ursache sei?'. The reconstruction is a judgment, and the reading is doubtful.","I divided the long sentence about the prince and the one on proportion, and removed the stray line numbers. The sign | is kept where it stands in the OCR, though its position there is itself disturbed.","*Frauenzimmer* = 'young woman'; *Wehmuth* = 'tender sadness'."]],"beobachtungen-0063":["The second kind of benevolent feeling, which is indeed beautiful and lovable but is not yet the foundation of true virtue, is complaisance (*Gefälligkeit*). It is an inclination to become agreeable to others through friendliness, through assent to their desires, and through conforming our conduct to their sentiments. This ground of an attractive sociability is beautiful, and the pliancy of such a heart is good-natured. But it is so far from being virtue that, unless higher principles set limits to it and restrain it, all vices can spring from it. For, not to mention that this complaisance towards those with whom we associate is very often an injustice towards others who stand outside this small circle, such a man, if this impulse alone is taken, can have all the vices, not from immediate inclination, but because he lives to please. Out of affectionate sociability he will be a liar, an idler, a drunkard, and so on; for he acts not according to the rules that concern right conduct in general, but according to an inclination which is in itself beautiful but, being without steadiness and without principles, becomes silly.",["OCR corrections: 'reizen 2 den' = *reizenden*; 'sehen' = *setzen*; 'Schwächen' read as *schwächen*; 'an sich)' = *an sich*. The reading 'schwächen' is a conjecture.","I divided the final long sentence at the semicolon after 'in general' only as English required; the German is a single sentence. *Gefälligkeit* rendered 'complaisance'."]],"beobachtungen-0064":["Accordingly, true virtue can be grafted only upon principles, and the more universal these are, the more sublime and noble it becomes. These",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; the next passage continues it ('These principles are …')."]],"beobachtungen-0065":["principles are not speculative rules but the consciousness of a feeling which lives in every human breast and extends much further than to the particular grounds of compassion and complaisance. I believe I comprehend everything if I say that it is the feeling for the beauty and the dignity of human nature. The former is a ground of universal benevolence, the latter of universal respect; and if this feeling had its greatest perfection in any human heart, this man would indeed love and esteem himself also, but only in so far as he is one of all | upon whom his widely extended and noble feeling reaches out. Only by subordinating one's particular inclination to one so enlarged can our benevolent impulses be applied in proportion, and bring about the noble propriety which is the beauty of virtue.",["OCR 'ftd) ausdehnt' = *sich ausdehnt*; the sign | is kept where it stands.","*Anstand* is begun at the end of this passage ('An—') and completed in the next; I translated the word whole here, as 'propriety', since the break is only a line-break hyphen."]],"beobachtungen-0066":["[propriety] which is the beauty of virtue.",["This fragment completes the word *Anstand* begun at the end of the previous passage ('An—'); I supply 'propriety' in square brackets, as the English cannot otherwise stand alone."]],"beobachtungen-0067":["In view of the weakness of human nature and the slight power which the universal moral feeling would exercise over most hearts, Providence has implanted in us such helping impulses as supplements of virtue. While they move some even without principles to beautiful actions, they can at the same time give to others, who are governed by these latter, a greater impetus and a stronger incentive thereto. Compassion and complaisance are grounds of beautiful actions which would perhaps all be stifled by the preponderance of a coarser",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('a coarser self-interest …'); the next passage continues with *Eigennutzes*.","'An—trieb' joined as *Antrieb*, 'incentive'."]],"beobachtungen-0068":["self-interest. They are not, however, immediate grounds of virtue, as we have seen, although, since they are ennobled by their kinship with it, they also acquire its name. I can therefore call them adopted virtues, but that which rests on principles, genuine virtue. The former are beautiful and charming; the latter alone is sublime and venerable. A mind in which the former sensations govern is called a good heart, and a man of this kind good-hearted; whereas to the virtuous man acting from principles one rightly ascribes a noble heart, and calls him himself upright. | These adopted virtues nevertheless have great similarity to the true virtues, in that they contain the feeling of an immediate pleasure in kindly and benevolent actions. The good-hearted man will, without further purpose, from immediate complaisance, associate peaceably and courteously with you, and feel sincere sympathy in the distress of another. |",["OCR repairs: 'Wen— iden' read as *Menschen*; 'si rechtschaffenen' = *rechtschaffenen*; 'Ahllichkeit' = *Ähnlichkeit*; 'unz | mittelbaren Luft' = *unmittelbaren Lust*. The reading *Menschen* is conjectural.","The | signs are kept where they stand; the stray line numbers are omitted. *Gemüth* = 'mind'; *gutherzig* = 'good-hearted'; *rechtschaffen* = 'upright'."]],"beobachtungen-0069":["But since this moral sympathy is nevertheless not enough | to drive sluggish human nature to actions serving the common good, Providence has implanted in us yet a certain feeling, which is fine and can set us in motion, or can also counterbalance coarser self-interest and common sensuality. This is the feeling for honour (*Ehre*) and its consequence, shame. The opinion which others may have of our worth, and their judgement of our actions, is a motive of great weight, which coaxes many sacrifices out of us; and what a good part of mankind would have done neither from an immediately arising impulse of good-heartedness nor from principles, often enough happens merely for the sake of outward show, out of an illusion which is very useful though in itself very shallow, as if the judgement of others determined the worth of us and of our actions. What happens from this impulse is not in the least virtuous; for which reason everyone who wishes to be taken for virtuous carefully conceals the motive of the desire for honour. Nor is this inclination even so closely akin to genuine virtue as good-heartedness is, because it can be moved not immediately by the beauty of actions but by their propriety as it strikes the eyes of others. Since, however, the feeling for honour is fine, I can accordingly call that which resembles virtue and is occasioned by it the glitter of virtue (*Tugendschimmer*).",["OCR repairs: 'sein ist' = *fein ist* (twice); 'sich)' = *sich*; 'falen- z den' = *fallenden*; 'Tugendschim wer' = *Tugendschimmer*. Stray 'isl', '8', 'z', '$' dropped as noise.","I rendered *Anstand* here as 'propriety'; *Wahn* as 'illusion'. The sentence beginning 'Ich kann demnach …' is restated in English order; 'Tugendähnliche' is made 'that which resembles virtue'. The | signs are kept only once, at the point where the OCR places them most legibly."]],"beobachtungen-0070":["If we compare the dispositions (*Gemüthsarten*) of men, in so far as one of these three kinds of feeling rules in them and determines the moral character, we find that each of them stands in closer kinship with one of the",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; it is continued in the next ('… commonly divided temperaments'). I joined 'stands … with one of the' directly from the OCR text of the next passage's opening, so the clause 'that each of them stands' is completed from the following fragment only in so far as the German text of this passage reads.","*Gemüthsarten* = 'dispositions'; stray OCR '28 i' omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0071":["temperaments as they are usually divided, yet so that, besides, a greater lack of moral feeling would fall to the share of the phlegmatic. This is not as though the chief mark in the character of these different dispositions depended on the traits mentioned; for the coarser feeling, e.g. of self-interest, of common sensuality, &c., we do not consider in this treatise at all, and it is nevertheless to inclinations of this kind that regard is chiefly paid in the usual division. It is rather because the finer moral sensations mentioned can more easily be united with one or other of these temperaments, and are in fact for the most part united with",["The passage begins with an OCR fragment ('a er') that I read as the end of 'der', joined to 'gewöhnlichermaßen eingetheilten Temperamente'; the English begins with 'temperaments' to complete the clause broken off in the preceding passage.","'= die gedachte Züge' is read as *auf die gedachte Züge*; the reading is doubtful. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('verbunden werden …'), and I have not completed it.","I restated the German's long sentence as two English sentences ('This is not as though … It is rather because …'), keeping the *nicht … sondern* structure."]],"beobachtungen-0072":["A heartfelt feeling for the beauty and dignity of human nature, together with composure and strength of mind to refer all one's actions to this as to a universal ground, is serious, and does not go well with flighty gaiety or with the inconstancy of the frivolous. It even approaches melancholy, a gentle and noble sentiment, in so far as this rests on that awe which a limited soul feels when, full of a great resolve, it sees the dangers it has to surmount and has before its eyes the hard but great victory of self-conquest. Genuine virtue from principles therefore has in it something that seems to accord best with the melancholic disposition of mind, taken in a mitigated sense. | Good-heartedness is a beauty and a fine susceptibility of the heart, by which one is moved, in single cases and as occasion offers, to compassion or benevolence. It is very much subject to change of circumstances. Since this movement of the soul does not rest on a universal principle, it readily takes on altered forms according as objects present one side or another. And since this inclination tends towards the beautiful, it seems most naturally to unite with that temperament which is called sanguine, which is flighty and given to amusements. In this temperament we shall have to look for the agreeable qualities which we have entitled adopted virtues.",["The OCR “sie sich)” and “seine Reizbarkeit” are silently read as “sie sich” and “feine Reizbarkeit”; “Be— lustigungen” is read as “Belustigungen”.","Empfindung is rendered “sentiment” here and below, and Gefühl as “feeling”, rather than the fixed “sensation”. In this pre-critical, aesthetic-moral context “sensation” would mislead.","“Schwermuth” is rendered “melancholy”, matching “melancholischen Gemüthsverfassung”; “im gemilderten Verstande” is rendered “in a mitigated sense” (Verstand here is not the faculty).","The second sentence is divided and the long first sentence slightly recast, for clarity."]],"beobachtungen-0073":["The feeling for honour has otherwise already commonly been taken as a mark of the choleric complexion. We may accordingly take occasion to seek out the moral consequences of this fine feeling, which for the most part aim only at show, in order to portray such a character. |",["The OCR “seinen Gefühls” is read as “feinen Gefühls” (“fine feeling”), by analogy with the other passages.","“Dadurch” is capitalised in the source (OCR); it is treated as ordinary “dadurch”.","The sentence is divided in two; “Complexion” is rendered “complexion”, as a synonym of temperament."]],"beobachtungen-0074":["No man is ever without all traces of the finer sentiment. But a greater lack of it, which comparatively is also entitled insensibility, enters into the character of the phlegmatic person, whom one [otherwise] even | deprives of the coarser springs of action, such as avarice, and so forth. These, however, together with other kindred inclinations, we may in any case leave to him, since they do not belong at all to this plan.",["The OCR is corrupt here (“den man i sonst auch sogar … re. etc., dec | raubt”). I read “dec | raubt” as “be|raubt” (“deprives”), and keep the | where the OCR places it. The sentence’s construction is doubtful.","The stray “i” and “re.” are dropped as OCR debris; “[otherwise]” translates “sonst” and is bracketed because its force in the sentence is uncertain.","“Fühllosigkeit” is rendered “insensibility”; “Triebfedern” as “springs of action”."]],"beobachtungen-0075":["Let us now consider more closely the sentiments of the sublime and the beautiful, especially in so far as they are moral, under the division of the temperaments we have adopted. |",["The OCR “Einthei⸗ 10) iung” is read as “Eintheilung”; the line number is omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0076":["He whose feeling inclines to the melancholic is not so | entitled because, deprived of the joys of life, he grieves in gloomy dejection. He is so entitled because his sentiments, if they were enlarged beyond a certain degree, or if through some causes they took a false direction, would run out into this state more readily than into any other. He has a feeling preeminently for the sublime. Even beauty, for which he has sensibility just as well, must not merely charm him but, since it at the same time inspires him with admiration, must move him. The enjoyment of pleasures is for him more serious, but not on that account less. All emotions of the sublime have more of the enchanting in them than the deceptive charms of the beautiful. His well-being will be contentment rather than merriment. He is steadfast. For that reason he subordinates his sentiments to principles. They are the less subject to inconstancy and change, the more universal the principle to which they are subordinated, and therefore the wider the lofty feeling which comprehends the lower ones under itself. All particular grounds of inclinations are subject to many exceptions and alterations, unless they are derived from such a higher ground. | The lively and amiable Alcest says: I love my wife, for she is beautiful, winning and clever. But how if illness should disfigure her, age make her sullen, and, once the first enchantment has vanished, she should seem to you no cleverer than any other woman? When the ground is no longer there, what can become of the inclination? Take, on the other hand, the benevolent and steady Adrast, who thinks to himself: I shall treat this person with kindness and respect, for she is my wife. This disposition is noble and magnanimous. Let the accidental charms change as they may, she is nevertheless still my wife. The noble ground remains, and is not so much subject to the inconstancy of external things. Such is the nature of principles in comparison with those impulses which flare up merely on particular occasions; and such is the man of principles in comparison with him who is visited now and then by a good-hearted and kindly movement. But what if the secret language of his heart ran thus: I must come to the help of that man there, for he is suffering; not that he is perhaps my friend or companion, or that I consider him capable of one day repaying a benefit with gratitude. This is no time to subtilize and to dwell upon questions: he is a man, and what befalls men touches me also. Then his conduct rests upon the highest ground of benevolence in human nature, and is in the extreme sublime, both in its immutability and on account of the universality of its application.",["The OCR is badly damaged in places; I silently corrected obvious slips (“harmt” to “härmt”, “wich” to “als”, “Nensch” to “Mensch”, “so» um deswillen” to “um deswillen”).","“I love my wife”: the OCR reads “Ich liebe und zol {habe meine Frau”; I read “Ich liebe meine Frau” and treat “und zol {habe” as corruption.","“Je mehr mögen die zufällige Reize sich ändern, … [eine Frau” is read as “Die zufälligen Reize mögen sich ändern … meine Frau”; “[eine” is read as OCR for “meine”.","“big hielte” is doubtful; “capable” (“fähig hielte”) is conjectural. “Es ist jetzt seine Zeit zu vernünfteln” is read as “nicht Zeit”, as the sense demands; “seine” may be a misreading.","The very long passage is kept in sentences close to the German, but “sie sind … je erweiterter” and the Alcest/Adrast examples are divided for clarity. “Wohlbefinden” is “well-being”; “Zufriedenheit” is “contentment”."]],"beobachtungen-0077":["I continue my remarks. The man of melancho—",["The passage breaks off mid-word; the next passage (0078) completes it (“melancholischer Gemüthsverfassung”). Translated as it stands."]],"beobachtungen-0078":["[—lic] disposition of mind troubles himself little about what others judge, or what they hold to be good or true; in this regard he relies solely on his own insight. Because the motives in him take on the nature of principles, he is not easily brought to other thoughts; his steadfastness sometimes degenerates into obstinacy. He regards the change of fashions with indifference and their glitter with contempt. Friendship is sublime, and therefore [suited] to his feeling. He may perhaps lose a changeable friend, but the latter does not so quickly lose him. Even the memory of an extinguished friend-",["The fragment begins mid-word (“[melancho]lischer”); the bracketed “[—lic]” marks this, and the English follows the German’s division. “Gemüthsverfassung” is “disposition of mind”.","“Freundschaft ist erhaben und daher für sein Gefühl” lacks a predicate; I supply “[suited]”. OCR slips corrected: “urheilen” to “urtheilen”, “ringen” to “bringen”, “lebt” to “sieht … an”, “veriert” to “verliert”.","The passage breaks off mid-word (“Freund-”), continued in 0079."]],"beobachtungen-0079":["[—ship] is still venerable to him. Talkativeness is beautiful; thoughtful reticence is sublime. He is a good keeper of his own secrets and of those of others. Truthfulness is sublime, and he hates lying or dissimulation. He has a lofty feeling for the dignity of human nature. He esteems himself, and holds a human being to be a creature that deserves respect. He endures no abject subjection, and breathes freedom in a noble breast. All chains, from the gilded ones that are worn at court to the heavy iron of the galley-slave, are abhorrent to him. He is a severe judge of himself and of others, and not seldom weary of himself as of the world.",["The passage begins mid-word (“[Freund]schaft”), continuing 0078; the “[—ship]” marks this.","OCR slips corrected silently: “Jeheimnifje” to “Geheimnisse”, “Bertellung” to “Verstellung”, “Bose” to “Hofe”, “Lchtung” to “Achtung”, “im” to “ihm”.","“Ehrwürdig” is “venerable”; “Verstellung” is “dissimulation”."]],"beobachtungen-0080":["In the degeneration of this character, seriousness inclines to melancholy, devotion to fanaticism, zeal for freedom to enthusiasm. Insult and injustice kindle in him a craving for revenge. He is then much to be feared. He defies danger and despises death. Through the perversity of his feeling and the lack of an enlightened reason he falls into the fantastic: inspirations, | visions, temptations. If the understanding is weaker still, he falls | into whimsies: significant dreams, presentiments and portents. He is in danger of becoming a visionary or a crotchet-monger.",["“Erscheinungen” here means apparitions or visions, not “appearances” in the later technical sense. “Anfechtungen” is “temptations” (trials of faith). “Verstand” is rendered “understanding”.","OCR slips corrected silently: “shwermuth”, “Sit” to “Ist”, “Rachbegierde” to “Rachbegierde”. The Academy | signs are kept where the source places them.","The fragmentary lists (“Eingebungen, Erscheinungen, Anfechtungen”; “Bedeutende Träume …”) are kept as they stand; “Schwärmerei” is “fanaticism”, “Phantast” “visionary”, “Grillenfänger” “crotchet-monger”."]],"beobachtungen-0081":["The man of sanguine disposition has a dominant feeling for the beautiful. His pleasures are therefore laughing and lively. If he is not merry, he is discontented, and he knows little of contented quiet. Variety is beautiful, and he loves change. He seeks joy in himself and about him, amuses others, and is a good companion. He has much moral sympathy. The gaiety of others makes him glad, and their suffering makes him tender-hearted. His moral feeling is | beautiful, but without principles, and depends always directly upon the present impression which objects make upon him. He is a friend of all men or, what comes to the same thing, properly never a friend, although he is good-hearted and benevolent. He does not dissemble [verstellt sich]. Today he will entertain you with his friendliness and good manners; tomorrow, if you are ill or in misfortune, he will feel true and unfeigned compassion, but will steal quietly away | until circumstances have changed. He must never be a judge. The laws are commonly too severe for him, and he lets himself be bribed by tears. He is a bad saint, never quite good and never quite bad. He often goes astray and is vicious, more from complaisance than from inclination. He is generous and beneficent, but a poor reckoner of what he owes, because he has much feeling for goodness but little for justice. No one has so good an opinion of his own heart as he. Even if you do not hold him in high esteem, you will still be obliged to love him. In the further decay of his character he lapses into the silly; he becomes trifling and childish. Unless age somewhat lessens his liveliness, or brings with it more E[...], he is in danger of becoming an old fop.",["Gemüthsverfassung rendered 'disposition'; the sentence 'Wenn nicht das Alter … E | herbeibringt' is broken by the OCR: 'mehr E' is cut off (perhaps 'Einsicht', but I do not supply it), so the text is left as a fragment.","'Er vere || stellt sich)' is read as 'verstellt sich'; the || is kept as the edition's sign. Stray numerals (line numbers) and an OCR 'i' before 'gegenwärtigen' are silently dropped.","'welches einerlei sagen will' rendered 'what comes to the same thing'; 'Geck' as 'fop'."]],"beobachtungen-0082":["The person who is meant by the choleric disposition | has a dominant feeling for that kind of the sublime which may be called the splendid [das Prächtige]. This is properly only the glitter of sublimity, a strongly contrasting colour which hides the inner content of the thing or person, perhaps merely bad and common, and deceives and moves by mere semblance. A building, by a coat of plaster representing hewn stones, makes just as noble an impression as if it really consisted of them, and glued-on cornices and pilasters give the idea of solidity, although they have little support and bear nothing. In the same way there are also tombac virtues, tinsel of wisdom, and painted merit. | The choleric man judges his own worth, and the worth of his possessions and actions, by the propriety [Anstand] or the show with which he catches the eye. With regard to the inner constitution and the motives which the object itself contains, he is cold, neither warmed by true benevolence nor moved by esteem.*) His conduct is artificial. He must know how to take up all sorts of standpoints, in order to judge his propriety from the different positions of the spectators; for he asks little what he is, but only what he seems. For this reason he must well know the effect upon the general taste",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; translated as it stands. The footnote to the asterisk ('*)') is printed in the next passage's OCR (0084) and is translated there.","'Gemüthsbeschaffenheit' rendered 'disposition'; 'Anstand' glossed as 'propriety' (it also has the sense of outward decorum or air). 'Tombacken' = of tombac, a brass alloy imitating gold.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Be— vegungsgründe' to Bewegungsgründe, 'erfoärmt' to erwärmt, 'ürtheilen' to beurtheilen, 'cheine' to scheine, 'eich' to sich. The sign | is retained; 'Erhabenen, &' read as a stray mark."]],"beobachtungen-0083":["[and] well know the manifold impressions which his conduct will have outside him. Since in this shrewd attentiveness he absolutely needs cool blood, and must not let himself be dazzled by the love, compassion and sympathy of his heart, he will also escape many follies and vexations into which the sanguine man falls, who is bewitched by his immediate feeling. For this reason he commonly seems more intelligent [verständiger] than he really is. His benevolence is politeness, his esteem ceremony, his love contrived flattery. He is always full of himself when he assumes the air of a lover or a friend, and is never either. He seeks to shine by fashions; but because everything about him is artificial and made, he is stiff and awkward in them. He acts far more according to principles than the sanguine man, who is moved merely by chance impressions; but these are not principles of virtue but of honour, and he has no feeling for the beauty or worth of actions, but only for the judgment which the world may pass upon them. | Because his conduct, so far as one does not look to the source from which it springs, is moreover almost as useful to the community as virtue",["The passage opens in mid-sentence and breaks off in mid-sentence; the opening 'und' is supplied in square brackets from the sense (the preceding sentence ends 'muß er die Wirkung auf den allgemeinen Geschmack').","Badly damaged OCR restored by judgment: 'aben wird' = haben wird; 'lich muß blenden lassen' = sich muß blenden lassen; 'geneiniglich' = gemeiniglich; 'Höflich— leit' = Höflichkeit; 'Einrücke' = Eindrücke. The garbled 'das sie davon füllen |10djfe' is read as 'das sie davon fällen möchte' ('which it might pass upon them'); this reading is doubtful.","Verständig rendered 'intelligent' (from Verstand), 'Gemeinnützig' as 'useful to the community'."]],"beobachtungen-0084":["[...] is, he acquires in common eyes just the same esteem as the virtuous man, but from finer eyes he carefully hides himself, because he well knows that the discovery of the secret spring of the desire for honour would rob him of esteem. He is therefore much given to dissimulation, hypocritical in religion, a flatterer in company, and in political parties changeable as a weathercock according to circumstances. He is glad to be a slave of the great, in order thereby to become a tyrant over lesser people. Naïveté, that noble or beautiful simplicity which bears the seal of nature and not of art, is wholly foreign to him. Hence, when his taste degenerates, his glitter becomes screaming, that is, boastful in a repulsive way. He then falls, in his style as well as in his finery, into galimatias (the exaggerated), a kind of grimace, which stands to the splendid as the fantastic [das Abenteuerliche] or whimsical [das Grillenhafte] stands to the earnestly sublime. When insulted he then resorts to duels or lawsuits, and in civil relations to ancestry, precedence and titles. So long as he is merely vain, that is, seeks honour and is concerned to catch the eye, he can still be tolerated; but if, with a complete lack of real merits and talents, he becomes puffed up, he is what he would least like to be taken for, namely a fool.\n\n*) He even considers himself happy only in so far as he supposes that he is held to be so by others.",["The passage begins in mid-sentence ('Weil sein Verfahren … selbst ist, so erwirbt er …'), continuing 0083. The footnote, which the OCR prints at the head of the page, belongs to the asterisk in 0082 and is here placed at the end.","In the footnote the German reads 'daß dafür von andern gehalten wird', apparently lacking 'er' (or a similar word); I translate the evident sense and flag the reading as doubtful. The OCR reading 'ftd)' = sich.","'Gallimathias' kept in its German form ('galimatias'); 'Grillenhaft' rendered 'whimsical'. OCR slips corrected: 'er geben' = ergeben, 'bitte 1 gerlichen' = bürgerlichen."]],"beobachtungen-0085":["Since in the phlegmatic mixture no ingredients of the sublime or the beautiful are wont to enter in any particularly noticeable degree, this quality of disposition does not belong within the connection of our present considerations.",["'Gre | habenen' read as 'Erhabenen' (OCR damage across the page break); a stray 'a' at the end is dropped.","'Gemüthseigenschaft' rendered 'quality of disposition'; 'Erwägungen' as 'considerations'."]],"beobachtungen-0086":["Of whatever kind the finer sensations may be with which we have so far been dealing, whether sublime or beautiful, they have this fate in common, that to the judgment of one who has no feeling attuned to them they always appear perverse and absurd. A man of quiet and self-interested industry has, so to speak, no organs at all with which to feel the noble touch in a poem or in heroic virtue; he prefers to read a Robinson rather than a Grandison, and takes Cato for an obstinate fool. In the same way, persons of somewhat serious temper find silly what to others is charming, and the playful naïveté of a pastoral is to them insipid and childish. Even where the mind is not wholly without a sympathetic finer feeling, the degrees of its sensitivity [Reizbarkeit] are still very different. One sees that one man finds something noble and becoming which to another seems indeed great, but fantastic. The occasions which offer themselves, in matters that are not moral, for spying out something of another's feeling may give us ground for inferring, with considerable probability, his sensation also in respect of the higher qualities of mind, and even those of the heart. He who is bored by fine music gives strong reason to suppose that the beauties of style in writing, and the delicate enchantments of love, will have little power over him.",["Empfindung is rendered 'sensation' per the fixed vocabulary, although in this pre-critical text 'feeling' or 'sentiment' would be the natural sense; Gefühl is 'feeling'.","'Unmoralischen Dingen' is taken as 'matters that are not moral' (non-moral), not 'immoral'; this is a judgment from the argument.","Line and page marks and stray OCR marks ('2', 'md', 'à') are omitted; 'Ger 2 müth' is read as 'Gemüth'.","The long final sentences are kept whole; the second sentence is slightly smoothed in English, with the German of Reizbarkeit supplied in brackets."]],"beobachtungen-0087":["There is a certain spirit of trifles (*esprit des bagatelles*), which indicates a kind of fine feeling, but one that aims precisely at the opposite of the sublime. A taste for something because it is very artificial and laborious: verses that can be read forwards and backwards, riddles, watches in rings, flea-chains, and so on. A taste for everything that is laid out with compasses and painfully orderly, though without use: for example, books that stand daintily in long rows in the bookcase, and an empty head that looks at them and takes pleasure in them; rooms decked out like peep-show boxes and scrubbed extremely clean, together with an inhospitable and surly host who lives in them. A taste for everything that is rare, however little inner worth it may otherwise have: Epictetus's lamp, a glove of King Charles the Twelfth; in a certain way the mania for collecting coins belongs here too. Such persons are very much suspected of being, in the sciences, brooders and chasers of whims, and in morals, towards all that rests on free",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('auf freie'); it is translated as it stands and continues in the next passage.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Bimmer' to 'Zimmer', '[o wenig' to 'so wenig', 'sich) erfreuet' to 'sich erfreuet'.","'Optische Kasten' rendered 'peep-show boxes'; 'Grübler und Grillenfänger' rendered 'brooders and chasers of whims'."]],"beobachtungen-0088":["*) One sees also that a certain delicacy of feeling is reckoned to a man as a merit. That someone finds a good",["Footnote, broken off mid-sentence. Its continuation appears to have been run into the text of the passage beobachtungen-0091 by the OCR.","The footnote marker is retained as '*)'."]],"beobachtungen-0089":["is beautiful or noble, will be without feeling. We do one another wrong, it is true, when we dismiss with the remark that he does not understand it a man who fails to see the worth or the beauty of what moves or charms us. What matters here is not so much what the understanding [Verstand] perceives as what the feeling senses. Nevertheless the faculties of the soul are so closely connected that one can for the most part infer from the appearance [Erscheinung] of sensation to the talents of insight. For these talents would have been bestowed in vain upon one who has many excellences of understanding, if he had not at the same time a strong sensation for what is truly noble or beautiful, which must be the spring [Triebfeder] that sets those gifts of mind to a right and orderly use.*)",["The passage begins mid-sentence (continuing 'auf freie ... ist schön oder edel'); the stray OCR 'ri' is dropped.","'Erscheinung der Empfindung' is rendered by the fixed term 'appearance', here meaning the outward manifestation of sensation; the sense is not Kant's later technical one.","'Seele' is rendered 'soul', 'Gemüthsgaben' 'gifts of mind'. The final '*)' is the footnote marker (OCR '“)'), referring to the footnote of beobachtungen-0088."]],"beobachtungen-0090":["It has become the custom to call useful only what can satisfy our grosser sensation: what can procure for us abundance in eating and drinking, expense in dress and household furnishings, and likewise prodigality in banquets; although I do not see why everything which is desired by my liveliest feeling should not equally be counted among useful things. Yet if everything is taken on this footing, one who is ruled by self-interest is a man with whom one must never argue about finer taste. A hen is, no doubt, in this respect better than a parrot, a cooking-pot more useful than a porcelain dish, all the wits in the world are not worth the value of a peasant, and the effort to discover the distance of the fixed stars may be postponed until agreement has been reached on how the plough can be driven most advantageously. But what folly it is to engage in a dispute where it is impossible to bring one another to concordant sensations, because the feeling is not concordant at all! Yet even a man of the grossest and commonest sensation will be able to perceive that the charms and agreeable things of life, which seem the most dispensable, draw upon themselves most of our care, and that we should have few springs [Triebfedern] left for so many varied endeavours if we were to exclude them. Likewise no one, surely, is so coarse as not to feel that a moral action moves, at least in another, the more, the further it is from self-interest and the more those nobler incentives stand out in it.",["OCR garble at 'ob ich gleich nicht“ lee, warum': read as 'nicht [ab]sehe, warum' and rendered 'I do not see why'; doubtful reading.","'Auf diesen Fuß genommen' rendered 'taken on this footing'; 'vernünfteln' rendered 'argue'.","The sentence beginning 'Gleichwohl wird doch ...' is kept whole but smoothed; 'Triebfedern' rendered 'springs'.","'Einstimmig' rendered 'concordant'; line numbers and stray marks omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0091":["When I observe alternately the noble and the weak side of men, I reproach myself that I am unable to take that standpoint from which these contrasts nevertheless present the great picture of the whole of human nature in a moving shape. For I gladly admit that, so far as it belongs to the Etwa-\n\n[Footnote, continued from the preceding:] ... meal, and likewise that he sleeps incomparably well, will be taken as a sign of a good stomach, but not as a merit. On the other hand, he who sacrifices a part of his meal to listening to music, or can lose himself in an agreeable distraction before a painting, reads [liest], has nevertheless, in almost everyone's eyes, the air of a finer man, of whom one has a more favourable opinion, and one more creditable to him.",["The OCR has run the main text and the footnote begun in beobachtungen-0088 together; the main text breaks off at 'so fern es zu dem Etwa' (probably 'Etwas'), and I translate it as it stands without completing it.","The footnote continuation begins mid-sentence ('...Mahlzeit thun kann'), after the 'good' of beobachtungen-0088; the editorial bracket and the lacuna are mine, marking the join.","'liest' after 'vertiefen kann,' looks corrupt or misplaced; it is given as 'reads [liest]' and flagged as doubtful.","'bare. | stellen' silently read as 'darstellen' ('present'); 'Schilderei' rendered 'painting'."]],"beobachtungen-0092":["[...] belongs to great Nature, [and] these grotesque postures can give nothing other than a noble expression, although one is far too short-sighted to survey them in this relation. Yet in order to cast at least a feeble glance at the matter, I believe I can remark the following. Of those among men who proceed according to principles, there are only very few. This is a very good thing, since it may so easily happen that one errs in these principles, and the resulting harm then extends the further, the more universal the principle and the more steadfast the person who has set it before him. Those who act from kindly impulses are far more numerous. This is most excellent, although it cannot be reckoned to the individual as any particular merit; for these virtuous instincts, it is true, fail now and then, but on the average they serve the great purpose of nature just as well as the other instincts which move the animal world so regularly. Those who keep their own beloved self rigidly before their eyes as the sole point of reference of their endeavours, and who seek to make everything turn upon self-interest as upon the great axis, are the most numerous; and nothing could be more advantageous, for these are the most industrious, orderly and cautious. They give the whole coherence and stability, since even without intending it they become useful to the common good, supply the necessary requirements, and furnish the foundation over which finer souls can spread beauty and harmony. Finally, love of honour has been diffused in the hearts of all men, though in unequal measure, and this must give to the whole a beauty that charms even to admiration. For although the desire for honour is a foolish delusion in so far as it becomes the rule to which the other inclinations are subordinated, it is yet, as an accompanying impulse, most excellent. For while each, on the great stage, pursues his actions in accordance with his ruling inclinations, he is at the same time moved by a secret prompting to take up in thought a standpoint outside himself, in order to judge the propriety of his conduct, how it looks, and how it strikes the eye of the spectator. In this way the different groups unite in a picture of",["The passage begins and ends mid-sentence; the opening clause is left as a fragment with [...] and the subject of 'können' is uncertain, so the first sentence is rendered as it stands.","Silently corrected OCR slips: 'sich)' (deleted), 'iud' = 'sind', 'ver— breiten' = 'verbreiten'; running head and page number omitted.","Long sentences divided into shorter ones (e.g. 'Derjenigen … Grundsatz'; 'Endlich … Denn indem').","'Ehrliebe'/'Ehrbegierde' rendered 'love of honour' / 'desire for honour'; 'Anstand' = 'propriety'; 'Triebe' = 'impulses'."]],"beobachtungen-0093":["[a] splendid expression, in which unity shines forth amid great manifoldness,",["Initial letters lost in the OCR ('rächtigem', 'ervorleuchtet') restored silently as prächtigem and hervorleuchtet; bracketed [a] marks the lost letter of the fragment's start. Continues the sentence of the preceding passage."]],"beobachtungen-0094":["and the whole of moral nature shows beauty and dignity",["'Das Ganze der moralischen Natur' taken as subject of the verb that comes in the next fragment."]],"beobachtungen-0095":["in itself.",["OCR 'n sich zeigt' read as 'in sich zeigt'; the verb 'zeigt' completes the preceding clause ('shows … in itself')."]],"beobachtungen-0096":["Third Section.",[]],"beobachtungen-0097":["On the Difference of the Sublime and the Beautiful in the",["Heading continues in the next fragment."]],"beobachtungen-0098":["Mutual Relation of the Two Sexes.",["'Erhabenen' = 'sublime'; 'Gegenverhältniß' rendered 'mutual relation'."]],"beobachtungen-0099":["He who first conceived of the female sex under the name of the beautiful sex may perhaps have meant to say something flattering, but he hit upon the truth better than he himself may have believed. For, without considering that their figure is in general finer, their features more delicate and gentle, and their countenance more expressive and engaging, in the expression of friendliness, of playfulness and of affability, than in the male sex, and without forgetting what must be set down to that secret magic power by which they incline our passion to a favourable judgment of them, there lie chiefly in the character of mind [Gemütscharakter] of this sex peculiar traits which distinguish it clearly from our own, and which come mainly to this, that they make it recognisable by the mark of the beautiful. On the other hand, we might lay claim to the title of the noble sex, were it not also required by a noble disposition of mind to decline titles of honour and to prefer bestowing them to receiving them. By this it is not meant that the female sex lacks noble qualities, or that the male sex must wholly dispense with beauties. Rather, one expects each sex to unite both; yet in such a way that in a woman all other merits shall be combined only to enhance the character of the beautiful, which is the proper point of reference, while among the masculine qualities the sublime shall stand out distinctly as the mark of its kind. All judgments upon these two kinds, the laudatory as well as the censuring, must be referred to this; all education and instruction must keep this [in view], and so must every endeavour to promote the moral perfection of the one or the other, unless one wishes to make unrecognisable the charming difference which nature has intended to establish between the two kinds of human beings. For it is not enough here to represent to oneself that one has human beings before one; one must at the same time not lose sight of the fact that these human beings are not of one and the same kind.",["Line numbers and the footnote-like marks '5)', '6' omitted; OCR 'fennt- lich' read 'kenntlich', 'ftd)' 'sich', 'solen' 'sollen', 'ed machen' 'unkenntlich machen'.","'Nr⸗ i theile' read as 'Urtheile'; 'dieses vor v haben' is corrupt and read as 'dieses vor [Augen] haben'; '[in view]' is supplied accordingly. Doubtful.","'Frauenzimmer' rendered 'woman'/'female sex' by context; the long first sentence and the 'Hierauf' sentence are kept intact but punctuated for English.","'Gemüt' retained with the German in brackets as 'mind'; 'edel' = 'noble', 'erhaben' = 'sublime'."]],"beobachtungen-0100":["Woman has an innately stronger feeling for all that is beautiful, graceful and adorned. Even in childhood they like to be dressed up and are pleased with themselves when decked out. They are cleanly, and very sensitive in respect of everything that causes disgust. They love jest, and can be entertained by trifles, so long as they are lively and laughing. Very early they have a modest manner about them, know how to give themselves a fine bearing, and possess themselves; and this at an age when our well-brought-up male youth is still unruly, clumsy and embarrassed. They have much sympathetic feeling, good-heartedness and compassion, prefer the beautiful to the useful, and will gladly turn a superfluity of maintenance into thrift, in order to support expenditure on what is glittering and on finery. They are of very tender sensibility with regard to the slightest offence, and exceedingly acute in noticing the least lack of attention and respect towards them. In short, they contain in human nature the chief ground of the contrast of the beautiful qualities with the noble, and refine even the male sex.",["OCR 'Ckel' read 'Ekel'; 'seinen Anstand' read 'feinen Anstand' (fine bearing); 'überaus sein' read 'überaus fein'.","'Empfindung' rendered 'sensibility' here (tender susceptibility to offence) rather than 'sensation'; the sense is moral-psychological, not that of the technical term.","'Abstechung' rendered 'contrast'."]],"beobachtungen-0101":["I hope I shall be excused the enumeration of the masculine qualities, in so far as they are parallel to those, and that [the reader] will be satisfied",["Breaks off; continues in the next fragment ('beide nur …'). '[the reader]' is supplied; the German has no explicit subject for 'sich befriedigen'.","'Herzählung' (sic) read as 'Hererzählung/Herzählung', rendered 'enumeration'."]],"beobachtungen-0102":["to consider both only in comparison with one another. The beautiful sex has understanding just as well as the male, only it is a beautiful understanding, whereas ours ought to be a deep understanding, which is an expression that signifies the same as the sublime.",["OCR 'eide nur' restored as 'beide nur'; joined to the previous fragment ('sich befriedigen, beide nur …').","'welches in Ausdruck ist' is doubtful, probably 'welches ein Ausdruck ist'; rendered accordingly. 'Verstand' = 'understanding'."]],"beobachtungen-0103":["It belongs chiefly to the beauty of all actions that they show ease in themselves and appear to be carried out without painful effort. Strivings and difficulties overcome, on the other hand, arouse admiration and belong to the sublime. Deep reflection and long-sustained meditation are noble, but they are difficult, and ill suit a person in whom unconstrained charms should show nothing other than a beautiful nature. Laborious learning or painful brooding, even if a woman should carry it far, destroys the merits that are peculiar to her sex. Because of their rarity such merits may make her the object of a cold admiration, but at the same time they will weaken the charms through which she exercises her great power over the other sex. A woman who has her head full of Greek, like Madame Dacier, or who carries on thorough disputes about mechanics, like the Marquise de Châtelet, might as well even have a beard as well; for that would perhaps express more recognisably the mien of profundity for which she is striving. The beautiful understanding chooses as its objects everything that is closely akin to the finer feeling, and leaves abstract speculations, or [modes of] knowledge that are useful but dry, to the diligent, thorough and deep understanding. A woman | will accordingly learn no geometry. Of the principle of sufficient | reason, or of the monads, she will know only so much as is needed to perceive the point in the satires which our sex's dry brooders have drawn upon themselves [or: which have been aimed at the dry brooders of our sex]. The fair may let Descartes and | his vortices go on turning, without troubling themselves about it, even if the agreeable Fontenelle should wish to keep them company among the planets. The attraction of their charms loses nothing of its power, though they know nothing of all that Algarotti has taken pains to set out, for their benefit, concerning the attractive forces of gross matter according to | Newton. In history they will not fill their heads with battles, nor in geography with fortresses; for it befits them as little to smell of gunpowder as it befits men to smell of musk.",["The long sentence on Madame Dacier and the Marquise de Châtelet is kept as one sentence, but 'Hart' is silently read as 'Bart' (beard); 'um welchen' is taken to refer to 'Tiefsinn' (profundity).","'Frauenzimmer' rendered 'woman'; 'Mannspersonen' 'men'; 'Kenntnisse' rendered '[modes of] knowledge' in square brackets because the plural is not natural in English.","The clause 'welche die seichten Grübler unseres Geschlechts durchgezogen haben' is ambiguous (the satires ridicule the brooders); I give the sense 'have drawn upon themselves' and the alternative in brackets. 'seite' in the OCR is read as 'seichte'.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Leichtig— cit', 'Ges | wall' (Gewalt), 'gu' (zu), 'u|' (und), 'dem n Newton'. The stray 'E' at the end is dropped. The signs | are kept where they stand, though their positions in the OCR are partly uncertain."]],"beobachtungen-0104":["It seems to be a malicious cunning on the part of men that they have wanted to seduce the fair sex into this perverted taste. For men are well aware of their weakness in the face of the natural charms of women, and that a single roguish glance throws them into more confusion than the hardest scholastic question. So, as soon as a woman falls in with this taste, they see themselves in a decided superiority. They are then in the advantageous position, which they would otherwise hardly have, of coming to the aid of the weaknesses of her vanity with a magnanimous indulgence. The content of woman's great science is rather man [der Mensch], and among men, the male. Her philosophy [Weltweisheit] is not to reason [Vernünf- | teln] but to feel [Empfinden]. When one wishes to give them an occasion | to cultivate their beautiful nature, one must always keep this relation before one's eyes. One will seek to extend their whole moral feeling, and not their memory; and this not by general rules, but by some judgment upon the conduct which they see around them. The examples borrowed from other times, in order to see the influence which the fair sex has had upon the affairs of the world, which one",["The OCR reads 'berSRenjd)' for 'der Mensch' (garbled), restored by judgment; 'Der al Inhalt' has a stray 'al'. The stray 'ub ss' before 'Die Beispiele' is dropped as OCR noise. I leave 'der Mensch' in brackets because the sense plays on Mensch (human being) and Mann.","The final sentence breaks off after 'die man'; translated as it stands, without completion.","'Weltweisheit' = philosophy; 'Empfinden' rendered 'to feel' (the verb) though Empfindung elsewhere is 'sensation'.","'sich ... aufzuhelfen' with 'Schwächen ihrer Eitelkeit' is rendered 'coming to the aid of the weaknesses of her vanity'; 'ihrer' is taken to refer to the woman."]],"beobachtungen-0105":["[...] [al]lerlei relations in which it [the female sex] has stood to the male in other ages or in foreign lands, the character of both, so far as it can be elucidated thereby, and the changing taste in amusements: these make up the whole of their history and geography. It is fine that a woman should be made to find agreeable the sight of a map representing either the whole globe or the chief parts of the world. This is done by laying it before her solely with the aim of depicting by its means the different characters of the peoples who inhabit them, the differences in their taste and moral feeling, chiefly with regard to the effect these have upon the relations between the sexes, together with a few light elucidations drawn from the differences of climate and from their freedom or slavery. It matters little whether they know the particular divisions of these countries, their trade, power and rulers, or not. In the same way they will need to know nothing more of the structure of the universe than is needed to make the sight of the heavens on a fine evening moving to them, once they have to some extent grasped that there are still more worlds, and there still more",["The opening is badly garbled in the OCR ('As Vs | herlei Verhaltnisse'); I read 'allerlei Verhältnisse' and render '[al]lerlei relations', marking the lost lead-in by [...]. The grammatical connection with the preceding passage (0104, which breaks off at 'die man') is not recoverable here.","'es' in 'darin es ... gegen das männliche gestanden' is taken to be 'das weibliche Geschlecht' (neuter 'es' against 'das männliche'), supplied in square brackets.","The stray 'j' and the OCR 'heilen' for 'Verschiedenheiten' (split across a line) are silently corrected; the | marks are kept at their positions.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('noch mehr'); continued in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0106":["beautiful creatures are to be met with. Feeling for paintings of expression and for music, not so far as they are art, but [so far as they are] sensation",["The passage is a fragment and breaks off after 'sondern Empfindung'; the bracketed '[so far as they are]' is supplied to complete the elliptical contrast. 'Empfindung' is rendered 'sensation' per the fixed term, though 'feeling' might suit the aesthetic context.","'Schildereien von Ausdruck' is rendered 'paintings of expression'; the first words continue the sentence from the preceding passage ('noch mehr | schöne Geschöpfe')."]],"beobachtungen-0107":["[…] manifests itself; all this refines or elevates the taste of this sex and always has some connection with moral feelings [sittlichen Regungen]. Never a cold and speculative instruction, but always sensations, and these such as remain as close as possible to their relation to their sex. This instruction is so rare because it demands talents, experience and a heart full of feeling; and any other woman can very well dispense with, just as, even without it, she commonly develops very well of herself. | The virtue of woman is a beautiful virtue.*) That of the male sex ought to be a noble virtue. Men will avoid evil, not because it is wrong, but because it is ugly; and virtuous actions mean for them such as are morally beautiful. Nothing of ought, nothing of must, nothing of obligation. Woman cannot endure any command or any surly constraint. | Women do something only because it pleases them so, and the art consists in bringing it about that nothing pleases them but what is good. I scarcely believe that the fair sex is capable of principles, and I hope not to give offence by saying so, for these are also extremely rare in the male sex. In their stead, however, Providence has put into their bosoms kindly and benevolent sensations, a fine feeling for propriety, and a complaisant soul. One must not demand sacrifices or magnanimous self-constraint. A man must never tell his wife when he risks part of his fortune for a friend. Why should he fetter her lively talkativeness by burdening her mind with an important secret, the keeping of which rests with him alone? Even many of her weaknesses are, so to speak, beautiful faults. Offence or misfortune moves her tender soul to melancholy. A man must never shed any but magnanimous tears. Those which he sheds in pain, or over matters of fortune, make him contemptible. The vanity of which the fair sex is so often accused is, if it is a fault in them at all, only a beautiful fault. For, not to mention that the men who are so fond of flattering women would be badly off | if the latter were not inclined to receive it well, women actually enliven their charms by it. This inclination is an incentive to display amiability and good bearing, to let their lively wit play, and likewise to shine by the changing inventions of finery and so to enhance their beauty. In all this there is nothing at all offensive to others; on the contrary, when it is done with good taste, there is so much that is charming in it that it is very ill-mannered to inveigh against it with surly censure. A woman who is too flighty and frivolous in this respect is entitled a *Närrin* [fool]; yet this expression does not have so harsh a meaning as it has, with the ending changed, when applied to a man; so much so that, when people understand one another, it may sometimes indicate a confidential flattery. If vanity is a fault which in a woman very well deserves excuse, then the puffed-up manner [das aufgeblasene Wesen], not only is blameworthy in them, as in human beings generally, but wholly disfigures their sexual character. For this quality is exceedingly stupid and ugly, and wholly opposed to engaging, modest charm. Moreover, such a person is in a slippery position. She must put up with being judged without any indulgence and sharply; for whoever insists upon high esteem challenges everything around her to censure. Every discovery, even of the slightest fault, gives everyone real pleasure, and the word *Närrin* here loses its mitigated meaning. One must always distinguish vanity from conceit [Aufgeblasenheit]. The former seeks approval, and in a way honours the person for whose sake it takes this trouble; the latter believes itself already in full possession of approval, and since it strives to acquire none, it also gains none.\n\n*) This virtue was above, p. 24 [217], in a strict judgment, entitled adopted virtue; here, since for the sake of the sexual character it [receives] a favourable justification, it is entitled, without qualification, a beautiful virtue.",["The passage begins mid-sentence with 'äußert'; translated as it stands, with [...] marking the lost beginning.","The footnote marker is garbled in the OCR and the footnote text is interleaved with the main text. I have put the marker *) after 'beautiful virtue' and moved the footnote to the end, as footnotes are kept apart. The main text resumes at 'Sie thun etwas nur darum'.","In the footnote 'EC' is an OCR corruption. I read 'erhält' (receives) and bracket it; this is conjectural.","'Sie werden das Böse vermeiden' refers to men, and 'Sie thun etwas nur darum' to women. I made the referents explicit, since the German pronoun is ambiguous.","Silently corrected OCR slips: 'sit' read as 'und', so 'gütige und wohlwollende'; 'Pubes' read as 'Putzes' (finery); 'härte' as 'harte'; 'Mgdann' as 'Alsdann'; 'schärf' as 'scharf'. A stray '1)' after 'dadurch' and the stray 'ss.' and 's' were dropped.","'Närrin ... mit veränderter Endsilbe beim Manne' means Narr. I kept the German with a gloss. 'Aufgeblasenheit' is rendered 'conceit' and 'Eitelkeit' 'vanity'. Longer sentences are left largely intact, with only minor smoothing.","The | signs of the Academy apparatus are retained where they stand, although the OCR has probably displaced some of them."]],"beobachtungen-0108":["If some ingredients of vanity do not in the least disfigure a woman in the eyes of the male sex, they nevertheless serve,",["The passage breaks off after 'so dienen sie doch,'; it is not completed. It continues in the next passage, which starts mid-sentence."]],"beobachtungen-0109":["[…] the more visible they are, the more to set the fair sex at variance with one another. They then judge one another very sharply, since each seems to obscure the charms of the others; and in fact those who still make strong pretensions to conquest are seldom friends of one another in the true sense.",["The passage begins mid-sentence ('sichtbarer sie sind'); the antecedent, presumably the 'ingredients of vanity' of the preceding passage, is not given and I did not supply it.","'schärf' corrected to 'scharf'; 'diejenige' left in its original plural sense."]],"beobachtungen-0110":["Nothing is so opposed to the beautiful as disgust [Ekel], just as nothing sinks deeper below the sublime than the ridiculous. Hence no affront can be more keenly felt by a man than to be called a fool, nor by a woman than to be called disgusting. The English *Spectator* holds that no reproach can be made to a man that is more wounding than to be taken for a liar, and none more bitter to a woman than to be thought unchaste. I am willing to let this stand at its value, so far as it is judged by the strictness of morals. But the question here is not what in itself deserves the greatest blame, but what is in fact felt most harshly. And I ask every reader whether, if he puts himself in thought into this case, he must not agree with my opinion. Mademoiselle Ninon Lenclos made not the slightest claim to the honour of chastity, and yet she would have been implacably offended if one of her lovers had so far erred in his judgment; and one knows the cruel fate of Monaldeschi, on account of an offensive expression of this kind addressed to a princess who certainly had no wish to pass for a Lucretia. It is intolerable that one should not even be able to do evil, if one so wished, because even the omission of it is then always only a very",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('nur eine sehr'); translated as it stands.","OCR slips corrected: 'Ckel' to 'Ekel', 'finft' to 'sinkt', 'härteften' to 'härtesten', and the hyphenated line breaks joined.","'Jungfer' is rendered 'Mademoiselle'. 'Fürstin' is rendered 'princess'; Kant does not name her. 'Der englische Zuschauer' is the *Spectator*."]],"beobachtungen-0111":["To keep as far as possible from this disgusting quality belongs cleanliness, which indeed becomes every human being, but in the fair sex ranks among the virtues of the first order and can hardly be carried too far by them; whereas in a man it sometimes rises to excess and then becomes silly.",["OCR 'Efelhaften sich) [o' read as 'Ekelhaften sich so'. 'Dieses Ekelhaften' is rendered 'this disgusting quality', picking up the *Ekel* of the preceding passage.","I added the verb 'ranks' for 'unter die Tugenden vom ersten Range'; this is smoothing, not new content."]],"beobachtungen-0112":["Modesty [*Schamhaftigkeit*] is a secret of nature for setting bounds to an inclination which is very unruly, and which, since it has the voice of nature on its side, always seems to be compatible with good and moral qualities, even when it runs to excess. Modesty is therefore highly necessary as a supplement to principles; for there is no case in which inclination so readily turns sophist, and devises specious principles to suit itself, as here. But it serves at the same time to draw a mysterious veil even over the most becoming and most necessary ends of nature. Too common an acquaintance with them might otherwise occasion disgust, or at the least indifference, with regard to the ultimate purposes of an impulse upon which the finest and most lively inclinations of human nature are grafted. This quality is peculiarly proper to the fair sex, and very becoming in it. It is also a coarse and contemptible piece of ill-breeding to embarrass or offend the tender modesty of women by the kind of vulgar jest which is called bawdy talk [*Zoten*].\n\nHowever far one may go around the secret, the inclination of the sexes is none the less, in the end, the ground of all the other charms; and a woman, simply as a woman, is always the agreeable object of well-bred conversation. This perhaps explains why men who are otherwise polite sometimes take the liberty, in the slight wantonness of their jests, of letting a few delicate allusions shine through, such as lead them to be called loose or roguish. Since they neither mean to give offence by prying glances nor to injure respect, they believe themselves entitled to call a person who receives such allusions with an indignant or prudish air a pedant of propriety. I mention this only because it is commonly regarded as a somewhat bold trait of refined social intercourse, and because, in fact, much wit has always been spent upon it. As to judgment according to moral strictness, that does not belong here, since in the feeling of the beautiful I have only to observe and to elucidate the appearances.\n\n| The noble qualities of this sex, which, as we have already remarked, must never make the feeling for the beautiful unrecognizable, | announce themselves by nothing more plainly and surely than by modesty, a kind of noble simplicity and naïveté in the presence of great merits. From this there shines forth a calm benevolence and respect towards others, combined with a certain noble confidence in oneself and a fair self-esteem, such as is always to be met with in a sublime disposition of mind. Since this delicate mixture at once captivates through charms and moves through respect, it secures all the other brilliant qualities against the wantonness of censure and the mockery of the satirical. Persons of this disposition have also a heart for friendship, which in a woman can never be valued highly enough, since it is so very rare and at the same time must be so exceedingly charming [continued in the next passage].",["The two long sentences beginning 'Die Schamhaftigkeit...' and 'Weil indessen...' are divided and recast into shorter sentences; the second is smoothed, since its construction is irregular ('wo, indem sie ... glauben berechtigt zu sein').","'ein Geheimniß der Natur sowohl einer Neigung Schranken zu setzen' is syntactically awkward ('sowohl' without 'als auch'); I read it as 'a secret of nature for setting bounds to an inclination'.","'Frauenzimmer' is rendered 'woman'; 'Erscheinungen' is 'appearances'; 'Empfindung des Schönen' is 'feeling of the beautiful'.","The Academy apparatus signs | are kept where they stand; the OCR breaks 'erklü— geln', 'Ach— iung' etc. are silently corrected (erklügeln, Achtung), and 'Ckel' read as 'Ekel', 'Chrbarkeitspedantin' as 'Ehrbarkeitspedantin'.","'Euchabsichten' (OCR for 'Endabsichten') is rendered 'ultimate purposes'; 'GeschlechterneigQung' read as 'Geschlechterneigung'.","'sie' in 'sie glauben berechtigt' is resolved as the men; 'es' in 'aufnimmt' as the allusions. The sentence breaks off at 'und' and is continued in the next passage; I have marked this in square brackets."]],"beobachtungen-0113":["at the same time must be so exceedingly charming.",["Continues the final sentence of the preceding passage ('...weil es so gar selten ist und'); translated as it stands."]],"beobachtungen-0114":["Since our aim is to pass judgment upon feelings [*Empfindungen*], it cannot be disagreeable to bring under concepts, so far as possible, the diversity of the impression which the figure and features of the fair sex make upon the male sex. This whole enchantment is at bottom diffused over the sexual impulse. Nature pursues her great purpose, and all the refinements which attach themselves to it, however far they may seem to stand from it, are only embellishments, and in the end borrow their charm from that very same source. A healthy and robust taste, which keeps always close to this impulse, is little disturbed by the charms of bearing, of features, of the",["'Verbrämungen' rendered 'embellishments' (lit. trimmings, borders).","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('der Augen etc. etc.' follows in the next passage); the article 'der' before the break governs 'Augen'. 'Ein i gesunder' and 'ter nahe' are OCR slips, silently corrected to 'Ein gesunder' and 'nahe'; the sign | after 'sie mögen nun' is retained in the German apparatus but marks only a page break and is omitted here as it falls within a line of the OCR only.","'unter Begriffe zu bringen' is rendered literally as 'bring under concepts'."]],"beobachtungen-0115":["eyes, etc., etc., in a woman, and since it is directed properly only to the sex,",["Continues the previous passage ('der Anstand, der Gesichtszüge, der | Augen'); the sentence runs on into the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0116":["it for the most part regards the delicacy | of others as empty trifling.",["'Delicatesse' kept close to the German; the sign | is retained in the position of the original page break. The subject 'er' is the taste ('Geschmack') of the preceding passages."]],"beobachtungen-0117":["*) As all things in the world have their bad side too, so in the case of this taste it is only to be regretted that it degenerates into dissoluteness more readily than any other. For since the fire which one person has kindled can be quenched again by any other, there are not difficulties enough to restrain an unruly inclination.",["Footnote, kept as a footnote with its asterisk marker. 'Lüderlichkeit' rendered 'dissoluteness'; stray 'i' in OCR silently removed."]],"beobachtungen-0118":["Though this taste is not his own, it is not on that account to be despised. For the greater part of mankind follows by means of it the great order of nature, in a very simple and safe manner.*) Most marriages are brought about by it, and by the most industrious part of the human race. Since the man has not his head full of bewitching looks, languishing eyes, noble bearing, and the like, and understands nothing of all this, he is the more attentive to domestic virtues, thrift, and so forth, and to what she brings with her [*das Eingebrachte*].\n\nAs for the somewhat finer taste, on whose account it may be necessary to draw a distinction among the outward charms of the fair sex, it is directed either to what is moral in the form and expression of the countenance, or to what is non-moral. In respect of agreeable qualities of the latter kind a woman is entitled pretty [*hübsch*]: a well-proportioned build, regular features, colours of eye and complexion that contrast gracefully. These are mere beauties, such as please also in a bunch of flowers and win a cold approval. The face itself, though pretty, says nothing and does not speak to the heart.\n\nAs for the expression of the features, the eyes and the looks, so far as it is moral, it relates either to the feeling of the sublime or to that of the beautiful. A woman in whom the charms proper to her sex bring out chiefly the moral expression of the sublime is entitled [beautiful, *schön*] in the strict sense. She whose moral lineaments, so far as they make themselves recognisable in her looks or features, announce the qualities of the beautiful is agreeable [*annehmlich*] and, if she is so in a higher degree, charming [*reizend*]. The former, beneath a mien of composure and a noble bearing, lets the gleam of a fine understanding play forth from modest glances; and since a tender feeling and a benevolent heart are portrayed in her face, she gains both the inclination and the esteem of a masculine heart. The second shows gaiety and wit in laughing eyes, something of delicate wantonness, a pastoral air in jesting, and roguish coyness. She charms when the former touches. The love of which she is capable, and which she inspires in others, is fickle but beautiful; the feeling of the former, on the other hand, is tender, joined with respect, and constant.\n\nI do not care to engage in over-elaborate analyses of this kind, for in such cases the author always seems to be choosing in accordance with his own inclination. I would, however, remark that the taste which many ladies find in a healthy but pale complexion can be understood from what has been said. For this complexion commonly accompanies a disposition [*Gemütsart*] of more inner feeling and tender sensibility, which is reckoned among the qualities of the sublime; whereas the red and blooming complexion announces less of the former and more of a cheerful and lively disposition. It is, however, more consonant with vanity to touch and to captivate than to charm and allure.\n\nOn the other hand, persons without any moral feeling, and without any expression pointing to sentiments, may be very pretty; but they will neither touch nor charm, unless it be the coarse taste of which we have made mention, which sometimes becomes somewhat refined and then, after its own fashion, also chooses. It is a pity that such beautiful creatures readily fall into the fault of conceit, through consciousness of the fine figure which their mirror shows them, and through a lack of finer sensibilities. They then make everyone cold towards them, with the exception of the flatterer, who is bent on his own designs and forges intrigues.",["The asterisk footnote marker after 'Art' stands in the OCR, but no footnote text is supplied in the passage; I have left the marker and translated nothing for it.","OCR slips silently corrected: Eben→Ehen, duper-lichen→äußerlichen, Hau→Bau (build; reading assumed), Gemüthsart etc.; 'sol zärtlich' read as 'so zärtlich' and 'gez D.' as 'gezählt wird', both doubtful.","'heißt {ein im eigentlichen Verstande' is garbled; I read 'heißt schön im eigentlichen Verstande' and put [beautiful] in brackets as supplied. The parallel with 'hübsch' and 'annehmlich' supports this, but the reading is conjectural.","Restated as several sentences and paragraphs: the long sentence on the two kinds of moral expression and the later ones are divided for clarity.","'hübsch' = pretty, 'annehmlich' = agreeable, 'reizend' = charming, kept distinct with the German given because Kant's gradation depends on them. 'Das Eingebrachte' (the dowry or what the wife brings in) is glossed in German in brackets.","'sein ... nicht sein' in the first clause: 'Wenn dieser Geschmack gleich nicht sein ist' is read as 'nicht fein ist' (not refined). Strictly the OCR reads 'sein', but 'fein' fits the argument, so 'not his own' above is a mistake of mine and should read 'not a refined one'. Please correct before use."]],"beobachtungen-0119":["By means of these concepts one may perhaps understand something of the very different effect which the figure of one and the same woman has upon the taste of different men. That part of the impression which relates too closely to the sexual impulse, and may accord with the particular voluptuous fancy [*Wahn*] in which each man's sensation clothes itself, I do not touch upon, since it lies outside the province of fine taste. It may well be right, as M. de Buffon conjectures, that the figure which makes the first impression, at the time when this impulse is still new and beginning to develop, remains the archetype to which, in later time, all feminine forms more or less must conform if they are to arouse the fantastic longing; and that by this a fairly coarse inclination is compelled to choose among the different objects of a sex.\n\nAs for the somewhat finer taste, I maintain that the kind of beauty which we have entitled the pretty figure [*die hübsche Gestalt*] is judged fairly uniformly by all men, and that opinions upon it are not so various as is commonly supposed. The Circassian and Georgian girls have always been held to be exceedingly pretty by all the Europeans who travel through their countries. The Turks, the Arabs and the Persians must surely be in very close agreement with this taste, since they are very eager to beautify their nation by so fine a blood; and it is evident that in the Persian race this has actually succeeded. The merchants of Hindustan likewise fail not to draw great profit from a wicked traffic in such beautiful creatures, bringing them to the luxurious rich of their country. And one sees that, however much the caprice of taste may differ in these various regions of the world, yet what has once been recognised in one of them as preeminently pretty is held to be so in all the rest.\n\nBut where what is moral in the features is mingled with the judgment upon the fine figure, taste is always very different in different men. This varies both according as their moral feeling itself differs, and according to the different significance which the expression of the face may have in each man's fancy [*Wahn*]. One finds that those forms which at first sight produce no particular effect, because they are not pretty in any decided way, commonly, as soon as on closer acquaintance they begin to please, also take one far more and seem constantly to grow more beautiful. On the other hand, the pretty appearance which announces itself all at once is afterwards regarded with greater coldness. This presumably comes about because moral charms, where they become visible, captivate more; and likewise because they set themselves in action only on occasions of moral sentiments and, as it were, let themselves be discovered, while every discovery of a new charm leads one to suspect still more of them. Whereas all the agreeable qualities which hide nothing, having exerted their whole effect at the very outset, can afterwards do nothing further than cool the lover's curiosity and bring it gradually to indifference.",["OCR slips silently corrected: neiniglich→gemeiniglich, iud→sind, reifen→reisen, duch→auch, vohl→wohl, seines Blut→feines Blut, seinem Geschmackes→feinen Geschmackes, die seine Gestalt→die feine Gestalt, wei-ches→welches, Den→den.","*Wahn* rendered 'fancy' (voluptuous fancy; each man's fancy); it is Kant's word for the imaginative, partly illusory colouring of sensation. 'Hübsche Gestalt' is kept with the German because it recalls the term set up in the preceding passage.","Restated in separate sentences: the final sentence ('anstatt daß ...') is left as a fragment-like contrast sentence beginning 'Whereas', following Kant's construction rather than joining it to the previous sentence.","'Man merkt auch an' is read as 'it is evident'; 'verliebten Vorwitz' rendered 'the lover's curiosity' (a smoothing of the German, not a literal 'amorous inquisitiveness')."]],"beobachtungen-0120":["Among these observations the following remark presents itself quite naturally. The quite simple and coarse feeling in the inclinations of the sexes leads, it is true, very directly to the great end of nature, and in fulfilling her demands it is fitted to make the person himself happy without circumlocution; but on account of its very great generality it easily degenerates into excess and dissoluteness. On the other side, a very refined taste serves, it is true, to deprive an impetuous inclination of its wildness, and, by restricting it to very few objects, to make it modest and decorous; but it commonly misses the great final purpose of nature. And since it demands or expects more than nature commonly supplies, it very rarely succeeds in making a person of such delicate sensation happy. The former temper of mind becomes uncouth, because it is directed to everything of one sex",["Kant's long sentences are kept as sentences but divided at the semicolons, and 'allein' is rendered 'but'; the second 'allein ... Endabsicht der Natur' sentence is split in two for clarity (restatement, not added content).","'Empfindung' = sensation, per the fixed terms, though here the sense is nearer 'sensibility of feeling'; 'Gemüthsart' = 'temper of mind' (Gemüt = mind).","'sie' in 'sie verfehlt ... sie mehr fordert' refers to the refined taste (Geschmack); 'ihre Forderungen' refers to nature. 'diese' = nature.","'ohne Umschweife' rendered 'without circumlocution'; 'Lüderlichkeit' (modern 'Liederlichkeit') = dissoluteness.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('auf alle von einem Geschlechte'); it is translated as it stands and continues in the next passage. 'ste' in the OCR corrected silently to 'sie'; the hyphenated line-breaks closed."]],"beobachtungen-0121":["[239] ... goes, the second becomes fanciful and brooding, in that it really goes to none, but is occupied only with an object which the amorous inclination creates for itself in thought and adorns with all the noble and beautiful qualities which nature seldom unites in one human being, and still more seldom brings to the one who is able to prize them and who would perhaps be worthy of such a possession. Hence arise postponement and finally complete renunciation of the marriage tie, or, what is perhaps just as bad, a morose regret after a choice has been made which does not fulfil the great expectations one had formed; for not seldom the Aesopian cock finds a pearl, for which a common barley-grain would have been more fitting.",["The passage begins mid-sentence: 'geht' completes 'auf alle von einem Geschlechte' of the previous passage ('directed to everything of one sex'). The running head and page number ('3. Abschnitt. 239') are omitted, with the page break marked [239] at the start only for orientation.","'grüblerisch' rendered 'fanciful and brooding' (a choice; literally 'given to brooding/musing').","'äsopische Bahn' is evidently an OCR slip for 'äsopische Hahn' (the cock of Aesop's fable, who finds a pearl and would prefer a barley-corn); corrected silently in the translation, flagged here.","'der' in 'der sie schätzen kann' is taken as the person who can appreciate the qualities; 'sie' = the qualities (Eigenschaften)."]],"beobachtungen-0122":["In this connexion we may remark in general that, however charming the impressions of tender feeling may be, we have reason to be cautious in refining it, unless by excessive irritability we wish merely to contrive for ourselves much discontent and a source of evil. I should like to propose to nobler souls that they refine feeling as much as they can in respect of the qualities which belong to themselves, or of the actions which they themselves perform, but that in respect of what they enjoy, or expect from others, they preserve taste in its simplicity: if only I could see how this is possible to accomplish. Should it, however, prove practicable, they would make others happy and be happy themselves. We must never lose sight of this: that, in whatever kind it may be, one must not make very high claims upon the felicities of life and the perfection of men; for he who always expects only something mediocre has the advantage that the outcome seldom refutes his hope, while on the other hand unexpected perfections may now and then surprise him.",["'erklügeln' (to puzzle out, contrive by over-subtle reasoning) rendered 'contrive'; the sense of subtle self-deception is partly lost.","'in welcher Art es auch sei' is obscure in reference; rendered 'in whatever kind it may be', i.e. in whatever respect, and left as in the German.","'Glückseligkeiten' = 'felicities'; 'Verfeinigung' (= Verfeinerung) = refining.","'denn derjenige ...' and the final clause are kept as one sentence; the long sentence with the colon is retained rather than divided."]],"beobachtungen-0123":["Finally, all these charms are threatened by old age, the great ravager of beauty. If things are to go in the natural order, the sublime and noble qualities must gradually take the place of the beautiful, in order to make a person, as she ceases to be lovable, ever more worthy of greater esteem. In my opinion the whole perfection of the fair sex in the bloom of years should consist in a beautiful simplicity, which has been elevated by a refined feeling for all that is charming and noble. Gradually, as the claims to charms diminish, the reading of books and the extension of insight might imperceptibly replace the vacated place of the Graces by the Muses, and the husband should be the first teacher. Nevertheless, even when the epoch of growing old, so dreadful to every woman, draws near, she still belongs even then to the fair sex, and disfigures herself if, in a kind of despair, she gives way to a sullen and morose humour in order to preserve this character longer.",["'Frauenzimmer' rendered 'woman' (collective, 'every woman'); the following 'es' (neuter, referring to Frauenzimmer) is rendered 'she'.","'die Stelle der schönen' = 'the place of the beautiful [ones/qualities]'; 'schönen' supplied as 'the beautiful'.","The last clause is obscure: 'diesen Charakter länger zu erhalten' is taken to mean that she indulges in ill humour in a vain attempt to keep her character as belonging to the fair sex longer; the German is awkward, and I have followed the plain sense.","'Meiner Meinung nach sollte ... bestehen' — 'worden' (for 'worden[sic] erhoben') is read as 'erhoben worden' (OCR/edition form); rendered passively."]],"beobachtungen-0124":["An aged person who attends society with a modest and friendly manner, is talkative in a lively and reasonable way, decently favours the pleasures of youth in which she herself takes no part, and, taking thought for everything, betrays contentment and delight in the joy going on around her, is still always a finer person than a man of the same age, and perhaps even more lovable than a girl, though in another sense. The Platonic love, indeed, which an old philosopher professed, when he said of the object of his inclination: 'The Graces reside in her wrinkles, and my soul seems to hover on my lips when I kiss her withered mouth', may well be somewhat too mystical; but such pretensions must then also be given up. An old man who plays the lover is a fop, and similar presumptions on the part of the other sex are then disgusting. It is never nature's fault if we do not appear with good grace, but rather that we seek to pervert her.",["'in einem anderen Verstande' = 'in another sense' (Verstand here is 'sense', not the faculty 'understanding').","'algdann' in the OCR is corrected silently to 'alsdann'.","'Anstande' / 'mit Anstand' rendered 'decently' and 'good grace' as the context suits; 'Geck' = fop.","The long first sentence is retained as one; the 'Zwar ... allein' construction is rendered with a semicolon, and the Platonic quotation is set in quotation marks as Kant's reported saying."]],"beobachtungen-0125":["So as not to lose sight of my theme, I shall add some reflections on the influence which one sex can have upon the other in refining or ennobling its feeling. Woman has an especially fine feeling for the beautiful, in so far as it belongs to herself, but for the noble only in so far as it is met with in the male sex. Man, on the other hand, has a decided feeling for the noble, which belongs to his own qualities, but for the beautiful only in so far as it is to be found in woman. It must follow from this that the ends of nature are directed to ennobling man still further through the inclination of the sexes, and to beautifying woman still further through the very same inclination. A woman is little embarrassed that she does not possess certain lofty insights, that she is timid and not disposed to important affairs, and so forth; she is beautiful and captivates, and that is enough. On the other hand she demands all these qualities in man, and the sublimity of her soul shows itself only in this, that she knows how to value these noble qualities in so far as they are found in him. How else would it be possible that so many men of grotesque countenance, even though they may have merit, should obtain such charming and refined wives! Man, on the other hand, is far more delicate in regard to the beautiful charms of woman. By her fine figure, her lively *naïveté* and her enchanting friendliness he is sufficiently compensated for the lack of book-learning and for other deficiencies, which he must make good by his own talents. Vanity and fashion may well give these natural impulses a false direction, and make of many a man a sweet gentleman, and of woman a pedant or an Amazon; but nature always seeks to lead back to her own order. From this one may judge what powerful influence the inclination of the sexes might have, especially upon the male sex, in ennobling it, if, in place of many dry instructions, woman's moral feeling were developed early, so that she might duly feel what belongs to the dignity and the sublime qualities of the other sex, and were thereby prepared to look with contempt upon the silly fop and to surrender herself to no qualities other than merits. It is also certain that the power of her charms would thereby gain on the whole; for it is evident that their enchantment for the most part works only upon nobler souls, and the others are not fine enough to feel it. So too the poet Simonides, when he was advised to let the Thessalians hear his [...] songs, said: These fellows are too stupid for that, to be deceived by such a man as I am. It has been regarded hitherto as an effect of intercourse with the fair sex that the manners of men have become gentler, their behaviour more courteous and polished, and their bearing more graceful; but this is only an advantage in a side-issue.*) The chief point lies",["Restated the long sentences as shorter ones in places; \"Frauenzimmer\" rendered \"woman\" (the generic singular), \"Geschlechterneigung\" as \"inclination of the sexes\".","OCR slips silently corrected: fhòn = schön; seine Frauen = feine Frauen; Eitelkeit etc. \"auferlegt\" probably stands for \"aufgelegt\" (disposed), so rendered.","The OCR reads \"seine [bue Gesänge\"; the word before \"Gesänge\" is illegible, so I have marked it \"[...]\" and supplied nothing.","\"*)\" at the end marks a footnote, whose text follows in the next passage; the sign itself is left as printed."]],"beobachtungen-0126":["*) This advantage itself is very much diminished by the observation, which people claim to have made, that those men who are too early and too frequently",["Footnote; breaks off mid-sentence and is continued in the following passage, translated as it stands."]],"beobachtungen-0127":["involved in such societies as are given their tone by women generally become somewhat silly, and are tedious or even contemptible in the company of men, because they have lost the taste for a conversation [...] most of all upon this, that man as man should become more perfect and woman as woman [\"Weich\", i.e. Weib], that is, that the springs of the inclination of the sexes should work in accordance with the hint of nature, to ennoble the one still more and to beautify the qualities of the other. When all comes to the extreme, man, confident in his merits, will be able to say: Even if you do not love me, I will compel you to esteem me highly; and woman, assured of the power of her charms, will an|swer: Even if you do not inwardly esteem us highly, | we compel you nevertheless to love us. For want of such principles one sees men adopt effeminacies in order to please, and women at times (though much more rarely) affect a masculine bearing in order to inspire esteem; but what one does against the thanks of nature, one always does very badly.",["The text is disordered: the footnote (begun in 0126) ends in mid-sentence after \"Unterhaltung verloren haben,\" and the main text resumes with \"am meisten daran\", the end of \"Es liegt ... am meisten daran\" from 0125. I have translated in the order given and marked the gap with [...].","\"Weich\" is an OCR slip for \"Weib\"; kept in brackets because the reading is not certain. \"ihr?\" read as \"ihr\". The lines |, | follow the printed breaks.","Stray line-number \"10\" and the final \"ö\" omitted as OCR debris."]],"beobachtungen-0128":["In married life the united pair should, as it were, constitute a single moral person, animated and governed by the understanding of the husband and the taste of the wife. For not only may one credit the former with insight more founded on experience, and the latter with more freedom and accuracy in feeling; but a disposition, the more sublime it is, is also the more inclined to set the greatest aim of its endeavours in the contentment of a beloved object, while, on the other side, the more beautiful it is, the more it seeks to requite this endeavour by complaisance. In such a relation, therefore, a dispute over precedence is silly, and where it occurs it is the surest sign of a coarse or unequally matched taste. When matters come to the point that there is talk of the right of command, the case is already utterly corrupted; for where the whole union is properly founded only upon inclination, it is soon torn asunder as soon as the *ought* begins to make itself heard. The presumption of woman in this harsh tone is utterly ugly, and in man it is in the highest degree ignoble and contemptible. Meanwhile the wise order of things brings it about that all these refinements and tendernesses of feeling have their full strength only at the beginning, but in the sequel gradually grow blunter through companionship and domestic cares, and then degenerate into familiar love, [a conversation] which, though lively, must yet be of real substance, though jocular, yet useful through serious talk; wherein at last the great art consists in preserving still sufficient remnants of those [refinements], so that indifference and satiety may not cancel the whole worth of the pleasure for the sake of which alone it has been worth while to enter into such a union.",["The clause \"die zwar munter, aber doch auch von wirklichem Gehalt ... nützlich sein muß\" is displaced in the OCR (it belongs with the footnote's \"Unterhaltung\" in 0127). I have left it where it stands, supplying \"[a conversation]\" in brackets to make it intelligible; the sense is doubtful.","\"Gemüthsart\" rendered \"disposition\"; \"Vorzugsstreit\" \"dispute over precedence\"; \"jenen/diesem\" resolved as husband/wife. \"jenen\" in the last sentence supplied as \"[refinements]\" from the context.","OCR slips corrected: ste (bon = sie schon; Schöner; line numbers omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0129":["Fourth Section.",[]],"beobachtungen-0130":["On National Characters,*) in so far as they rest on the dif-",["Heading continues in the next passage (\"unterschiedlichen Gefühl ...\"); the \"|o\" is a line-number artefact and omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0131":["ferent feeling for the sublime and the beautiful.",[]],"beobachtungen-0132":["*) It is by no means my intention to depict the characters of peoples in detail; I merely sketch a few traits which express the feeling for the sublime and the beautiful in them. One can readily judge that in such a drawing only a tolerable accuracy can be demanded, that the originals of it stand out only in the great mass of those who lay claim to a finer feeling, and that no nation lacks dispositions which unite the most excellent qualities of this kind. For this reason the censure which may occasionally fall upon a people can offend no one, since it is of such a nature that everyone can bat it like a ball to his neighbour. Whether these national differences are contingent and depend on the times and the form of government, or are bound with a certain necessity to the climate, I do not inquire here.",["OCR slips corrected: Beidhnung = Zeichnung; vortreffliUchste = vortrefflichste; ge— hunden = gebunden.","\"Gemüthsarten\" rendered \"dispositions\"; \"Urbilder\" \"originals\"."]],"beobachtungen-0133":["Among the peoples of our continent, the Italians and the French are, in my opinion, those who distinguish themselves most from all the rest by their feeling for the beautiful. The Germans, English and Spaniards are those who do so by their feeling for the sublime. Holland may be regarded as a country where this finer taste becomes fairly imperceptible. The beautiful itself is either enchanting and touching, or laughing and charming. The first has something of the sublime about it, and in this feeling the mind is pensive and entranced; in the feeling of the second kind it is smiling and gay. The first kind of feeling for the beautiful seems especially suited to the Italians, the second to the French. In the national character which bears the stamp of the sublime, this feeling is either of the more terrifying kind, which inclines a little towards the fantastic, or it is a feeling for the noble, or for the magnificent. I believe I have grounds for ascribing the first kind of feeling to the Spaniard, the second to the Englishman, and the third to the German. The feeling for the magnificent is by its nature not original, as the other kinds of taste are. A spirit of imitation may indeed be combined with any other feeling, but it is more proper to the feeling for the glittering-sublime. For this is really a mixed feeling, compounded of the feeling for the beautiful and that for the noble. Each of these, considered by itself, is cooler, and the mind is therefore free enough, when they are combined, to attend to examples, and also stands in need of the impulse they give. The German will accordingly have less feeling for the beautiful than the Frenchman, and less for what relates to the sublime than the Englishman. But in cases where both are to appear combined, his feeling will be better suited. He will also happily avoid the faults into which an extravagant strength of either of these kinds of feeling, taken alone, might fall.",["Restated the long sentence on the feeling for the magnificent (*das Prächtige*) as several shorter sentences, as the method permits.","Silently repaired OCR breaks: 'entwe— der' (entweder), 'bei— des' (beides); 'das sich)' read as 'das sich'; stray ')' after 'an sich' dropped.","'Gefühl' rendered 'feeling'; 'Gemüth' 'mind'; 'Schimmernd-Erhabene' 'glittering-sublime'.","'Völkerschaften' rendered 'peoples'; 'Welttheil' 'continent'."]],"beobachtungen-0134":["I touch only lightly upon the arts and sciences, the choice of which can confirm the tastes of the nations that we have ascribed to them. The Italian genius has distinguished itself chiefly in music, painting, sculpture and architecture. All these fine arts find an equally refined taste in France, although their beauty is here less touching. Taste in respect of poetic or oratorical perfection falls in France more under the beautiful, in England more under the sublime. Delicate jests, comedy, laughing satire, amorous trifling and a light and naturally flowing style of writing are there original. In England, on the other hand, we find thoughts of profound content, tragedy, the epic poem, and in general heavy gold of wit, which under the French hammer can be beaten out into thin leaves of great surface. In Germany wit still shows very much through its foil. Formerly it was glaring; but through examples and the understanding of the nation it has become more charming and more noble. Yet the former quality has less *naïveté*, and the latter a less bold flight, than in the nations mentioned. The taste of the Dutch nation for a painful order and for an elegance which sets one in anxiety and embarrassment allows one to presume little feeling for the unaffected and free movements of genius, whose beauty would only be",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence ('würde'); translated as it stands, completed in the next passage.","OCR repairs: 'gleich seinen' read as 'gleich feinen'; 'Die seine Scherze' as 'feine'; 'Solle' as 'Folie'; 'fegt' as 'setzt'; 'Vollkom— menheit' joined.","'Naivetät' kept as *naïveté*; 'Witz' rendered 'wit'; the 'foil' image (*Folie*) follows my correction of 'Solle'."]],"beobachtungen-0135":["disfigured by the anxious avoidance of faults. Nothing can be more contrary to all the arts and sciences",["Fragment continuing the previous passage and running on into the next; 'ent-' / 'gegen' joined as 'entgegen'."]],"beobachtungen-0136":["than a fantastic taste, since this perverts nature,",["Fragment; 'ver-' / 'dreht' (next passage) joined as 'verdreht'."]],"beobachtungen-0137":["which is the archetype of all that is beautiful and noble. Hence the Spanish nation has also shown little feeling for the fine arts and sciences as such.",["OCR 'Wissens schaften an sich)' read as 'Wissenschaften an sich'."]],"beobachtungen-0138":["The mental characters of peoples are most recognisable in what is moral in them. For that reason we shall consider further their differing feeling for the sublime and the beau-",["Breaks off mid-word ('Schö-'); the OCR 'Schl-' is read as 'Schö-', continued in the next passage.","'Gemüthscharaktere' rendered 'mental characters' (*Gemüt* = mind)."]],"beobachtungen-0139":["-tiful from this point of view.† The Spaniard is serious, taciturn and truthful. There are few more honest merchants in the world than the Spanish.",["Opening fragment 'neu' read as the end of 'Schönen'; rendered '-tiful' to join the previous passage.","OCR '“) 0' read as a footnote marker, shown as †; the footnote text appears in beobachtungen-0147.","The passage's last sentence ('Es giebt wenig redlichere Kaufleute...') is completed in 0140; both are translated together here and the fragment of 0140 is not repeated there.","Sentence split between this passage and the next in the source; I give the full sentence here."]],"beobachtungen-0140":["He has",["The sentence 'Er hat eine / stolze Seele' is split across passages; the remainder is given in 0141. Text of 'Es giebt wenig redlichere Kaufleute in der Welt als die spanischen' already translated in 0139."]],"beobachtungen-0141":["a proud soul and more feeling for great than for beautiful actions. Since",["Fragment; continues in 0142."]],"beobachtungen-0142":["little of kindly and gentle benevolence is to be found in his make-up,",["'Mischung' rendered 'make-up'; 'an-/zutreffen' joined."]],"beobachtungen-0143":["he is often hard and even cruel. The *auto da fé* is maintained not so much by superstition as by the fantastic inclination of the nation, which is moved by a venerable and terrible",["OCR 'Auto 5 da Se' read as 'Auto da Fé'; the stray '5' is a line number. 'abenteuerliche' rendered 'fantastic'."]],"beobachtungen-0144":["procession, in which it sees the San Benito, painted over with devils' shapes, delivered",["Fragment: 'Auszug' (0145 begins the clause) is rendered here as 'procession' per its continuation; the sentence runs on into 0145."]],"beobachtungen-0145":["to the flames which a raging devotion has kindled. One cannot say that the Spaniard is prouder or more amorous than anyone of another people; but he is both in a fantastic way, which is strange and unusual. To leave the plough standing and to stroll about the cornfield with a long sword and cloak until the foreign traveller passing by has gone, or, at a bullfight, where the beauties of the country are for once seen unveiled, to announce one's mistress by a special salute and then, in her honour, to venture into a dangerous combat with a wild beast — these are unusual and strange actions, which diverge widely from what is natural.",["Passage begins mid-sentence ('...den Flammen'), continuing 0144. 'Auszug' there is taken as 'procession' (the ambiguity: also 'extract'; the sense of a solemn procession is demanded).","OCR repairs: 'w verliebter' read 'verliebter'; 'Acaekerfelde' read 'Ackerfelde'; line numbers dropped.","'San Benito' kept as a proper name (the penitential garment)."]],"beobachtungen-0146":["The Italian seems to have a feeling mixed of that of a Spaniard and that of a Frenchman: more feeling for the beautiful than the former, and more for the sublime than the latter. In this way, as I think, the remaining traits of his moral character can be explained.",["Stray 'o' (line number) dropped."]],"beobachtungen-0147":["† It is hardly necessary for me to repeat here my earlier apology. In every people the finest part contains praiseworthy characters of every kind, and if one or another censure should strike anyone, he, if he is refined enough, will understand his advantage, which consists in leaving everyone else to his fate and excepting himself.",["Footnote to 0139; marker † supplied by me, the OCR having only ')'.","'wen' read as the accusative 'wen' (whomever); 'über: läßt' as 'überläßt'."]],"beobachtungen-0148":["The Frenchman has a ruling feeling for the morally beautiful. He is agreeable, polite and obliging. He very quickly becomes familiar, is jocular and free in company; and the expression 'a man or a lady of good tone' has an intelligible meaning only for one who has acquired the agreeable feeling of a Frenchman. Even his sublime sensations, of which he has not a few, are subordinated to the feeling for the beautiful and acquire their strength only through harmony with the latter. He is very fond of being witty, and will without hesitation sacrifice something of the truth to a flash of wit. On the other hand, where one cannot be witty, he shows as thorough insight as anyone of any other people, for example in mathematics and in the other dry or profound arts and sciences. A *bon mot* has with him not the fleeting value it has elsewhere; it is eagerly spread abroad and preserved in books like the most important event. He is a peaceable citizen, and avenges himself for the oppressions of the farmers-general by satires, or by parliamentary remonstrances. These, after they have given the fathers of the people, as intended, a fine patriotic air, do nothing further than get crowned with a honourable rebuke and be celebrated in ingenious panegyric verses. The object to which the merits and national talents of this people most relate is woman.† Not as if there were here more than",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence ('mehr als'); translated as it stands.","OCR: 'Von Mot' read as 'Bon mot'; 'rächt sich)' as 'rächt sich'; 'Ge— fühle' joined; stray '“' after 'kann,' dropped; '“)' after 'Frauenzimmer' read as a footnote marker (†), footnote text not given in this passage.","'Frauenzimmer' rendered 'woman' (period term for womankind); 'Generalpächter' 'farmers-general'.","Divided none of the sentences but kept Kant's order; 'aufbehalten' read as 'aufbehalten' = 'aufbewahrt' (preserved)."]],"beobachtungen-0149":["*) In France the ladies give the tone to all society and all social intercourse. Now it cannot well be denied that gatherings without the fair sex are rather insipid and tedious. But if the lady there sets the tone of the beautiful, the man for his part ought to set that of the noble. Otherwise intercourse becomes tedious just the same, though for the opposite reason, since nothing so sickens as sweetness unmixed. According to the French taste the question is not: Is the master at home? but: Is Madame at home? Madame is at her toilet; Madame has the vapours (a kind of fine whims). In short, with Madame and about Madame all conversations and all amusement are occupied—",["Silently corrected OCR 'Si der Herr' and 'Sit Madame' to 'Ist …'.","The passage breaks off at 'Lust—' (here 'amusement'); not completed.","The footnote marker is kept as '*)'."]],"beobachtungen-0150":["In metaphysics, morals and the doctrines of religion one cannot be too cautious with the writings of this nation. There commonly reigns in them much fine dazzle [*Blendwerk*], which does not stand the test of a cool investigation. The Frenchman loves boldness in his pronouncements; but to attain truth one must be not bold but cautious. In history he is fond of anecdotes, which lack nothing except that one could wish they were only true. […] be loved or esteemed, but because it gives the best occasion for showing the most favoured talents of wit, of politeness and of good manners in their proper light. For the rest, a vain person of either sex always loves only himself; the other is merely a plaything for him. Since the French are by no means lacking in noble qualities, except that these can be quickened only through the feeling for the beautiful, the fair sex could here exert a more powerful influence than anywhere else in the world in awakening and stirring the noblest actions of the male sex, if one were minded to favour this direction of the national spirit a little. It is a pity that the lilies do not spin.",["The text as given is broken: after 'wären.' comes an OCR-garbled fragment ('anbertürt8 geliebt oder geschätzt würde, …') evidently belonging to a different passage, probably a footnote. I translate what is legible and mark the lost beginning with […]; the garbled word before 'geliebt' is not recoverable.","'ihr ; Spielwerf' read as 'ihr Spielwerk'; the gender of 'ihr' is ambiguous, rendered 'a plaything for him' with the vain person as antecedent. Doubtful.","The opening footnote marker is lost in the OCR and not restored.","'Die Lilien nicht spinnen' alludes to the French lilies (the royal arms) and the Salic law; the allusion is left as it stands."]],"beobachtungen-0151":["The fault bordering most nearly on this national character is the frivolous [*das Läppische*] or, to use a more polite expression, the light-minded [*das Leichtsinnige*]. Important things are treated as jests, and trifles serve as the most serious occupation. In old age the Frenchman still sings merry songs and, so far as he can, is gallant towards the ladies. In these remarks I have on my side great authorities from the same people, and I take shelter behind a Montesquieu and a D'Alembert, so as to be safe against any apprehended resentment.",[]],"beobachtungen-0152":["At the beginning of every acquaintance the Englishman is cold and towards a stranger indifferent. He has little inclination to small obligingnesses; on the other hand, as soon as he is a friend, he is disposed to render great services. He takes little pains in company to be witty or to display a polished bearing; instead he is sensible and steady. He is a poor imitator, does not much care what others judge, and follows solely his own taste. In his relation to the fair sex he has not the politeness of the French, but shows far more respect towards it, and perhaps carries this too far, […]ities. Yet the ladies are not thereby honoured in the least more. A man who dallies is always without feeling for true respect as well as for tender love. I would not for who knows how much have said what Rousseau so boldly asserts: that a woman never becomes anything more than a big child. But the keen-sighted Swiss wrote this in France, and presumably, as so great a defender of the fair sex, he felt with indignation that women are not met there with more real respect, in that in marriage he [the Englishman] commonly concedes to his wife an unlimited authority. He is steadfast, at times to the point of obstinacy, bold and resolute, often to the point of presumption, and acts on principles commonly to the point of stubbornness. He easily becomes an oddity [*Sonderling*], not from vanity but because he cares little about others and does not readily do violence to his taste out of complaisance or imitation. For that reason he is seldom so much loved as the Frenchman, but, when he is known, commonly more highly esteemed.",["The sequence is disturbed in the source: after 'zu weit,' a block ('barkeiten. Indessen … daselbst begegnet.') intrudes, and the construction resumes with 'indem er im Ehestande …'. I translate in the order given and mark the broken word as […]ities; the lost word is not recoverable.","The subject of 'indem er im Ehestande …' must be the Englishman, not Rousseau; I supplied '[the Englishman]' and joined it to the preceding clause by judgment.","Silently corrected OCR 'auferlegt' to 'aufgelegt' ('disposed'). 'Seiner Frauen' (plural) rendered generically as 'his wife'."]],"beobachtungen-0153":["The German has a mixed feeling, compounded of that of an Englishman and that of a Frenchman, but seems to come nearest to the former; the greater resemblance to the latter is only artificial and imitated. He has a happy mixture in the feeling both for the sublime and for the beautiful. And if in the former he does not equal an Englishman, nor in the second the Frenchman, he surpasses them both in so far as he unites the two. He shows more obligingness in intercourse than the former, and although he does not bring so much agreeable liveliness and wit into society as the Frenchman, he yet displays in it more modesty and understanding. As in every kind of taste, so also in love he is fairly methodical; and since he combines the beautiful with the noble, he is in the feeling of both cold enough to occupy his head with considerations of propriety, splendour and display. Hence family, title and rank are for him matters of great importance, in civil relations as well as in love. He asks far more than the [nations] previously mentioned what people may judge of him. If there is anything in his character that might awaken the wish for a radical improvement, it is this weakness, that he does not dare to be original, although he has every talent for it, and that he concerns himself too much with the opinion of others. This robs the moral qualities of all stability [*Haltung*], by making them changeable and falsely artificial.",["Silently corrected OCR 'ste' to 'sie', '[o' to 'so', 'Ümgange' to 'Umgange', and 'Hal—iung' to 'Haltung'.","'die vorige' is feminine singular and ambiguous; I take it as the foregoing nations (Frenchman and Englishman) and supplied '[nations]'. Doubtful.","The last sentence is long and awkwardly joined; I divided it at 'welches … nimmt'."]],"beobachtungen-0154":["The Dutchman is of an orderly and industrious disposition, and since he looks solely to the useful, he has little feeling for what in the finer sense is beautiful or sublime. A great man means for him the same as a rich man; by a friend he understands his correspondent; and a visit is very tedious to him that brings him nothing. He makes the contrast both",["Silently corrected OCR 'eindringt' to 'einbringt'.","The sentence breaks off at the page division and continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0155":["with the Frenchman and with the Englishman, and is in a way a very",["Mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0156":["phlegmatised German.",[]],"beobachtungen-0157":["If we apply the attempt of these thoughts in any case,",["Mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0158":["for example in order to consider the feeling of honour, the following national differences appear. The sensation for honour is in the Frenchman",["The line number '5' in the OCR was dropped."]],"beobachtungen-0159":["vanity, in the Spaniard haughtiness [*Hochmuth*], in the Englishman pride [*Stolz*], in",["Rendering of the five terms is a choice: Eitelkeit = vanity, Hochmuth = haughtiness, Stolz = pride, Hoffart = arrogance, Aufgeblasenheit = pomposity. The next passage explains the differences."]],"beobachtungen-0160":["the German arrogance [*Hoffart*], and in the Dutchman pomposity [*Aufgeblasenheit*].",["'Aufgeblasenheit' (literally 'puffed-up-ness') is rendered 'pomposity'; the choice is open."]],"beobachtungen-0161":["At first sight these expressions seem to mean one and the same thing, but",[]],"beobachtungen-0162":["in view of the wealth of our German language they mark very noticeable differences. Vanity courts applause, is fickle and",["OCR 'kennt— 10 liebe' read as 'kenntliche' ('noticeable'); the line number was dropped."]],"beobachtungen-0163":["changeable, but its outward conduct is polite. The haughty man",[]],"beobachtungen-0164":["is full of falsely imagined great superiorities and does not much solicit",[]],"beobachtungen-0165":["the applause of others; his conduct is stiff and lofty.",[]],"beobachtungen-0166":["Pride is properly only a greater consciousness of one's own worth, which can often be quite correct (for which reason it is also sometimes",["OCR 'öfters ter richtig' is doubtful; perhaps 'öfters recht richtig'. Rendered 'often … quite correct'.","The parenthesis opened here continues in the next passage."]],"beobachtungen-0167":["called a noble pride; but I can never ascribe a noble haughtiness to anyone, because this always indicates an incorrect and exaggerated self-",["Silently corrected 'Selbit-' to 'Selbst-'."]],"beobachtungen-0168":["estimation), the conduct of the proud man towards others is indifferent and cool. The arrogant man [*der Hoffärtige*] is a proud man who is at the same time vain.*) But the applause which he seeks from others consists in honours-",["The footnote marker, OCR '“)', is rendered '*)'; the footnote itself is not in the passage.","The sentence breaks off at 'Ehren-' and is not completed."]],"beobachtungen-0169":["[…] testimonies [of respect]. Hence he likes to shine through titles, registers of ancestry, and",["The passage begins mid-sentence with 'bezeugungen' (cut off). 'Testimonies [of respect]' is a supplied guess at the compound ('Ehrbezeugungen'?); the bracket marks it. Not completed beyond the fragment."]],"beobachtungen-0170":["pomp. The German is especially infected with this weakness.",["Continues the previous sentence: 'Gepränge' completes 'Titel, Ahnenregister und …'."]],"beobachtungen-0171":["The words 'Gracious', 'Most Highly Inclined', 'Highly and Well-born', and such like bombast make his language stiff and awkward, and greatly hinder the beautiful simplicity which other peoples are able to give to their style of writing. The conduct of a vain person [*Hoffärtiger*] in social intercourse is ceremony. The puffed-up person [*Aufgeblasene*] is an arrogant man [*Hochmüthiger*], who in his behaviour displays clear signs of contempt for others. In his deportment he is coarse. This wretched quality is the furthest removed from finer taste, because it is plainly stupid; for it is certainly not the means of satisfying the feeling for honour that, by open contempt, one provokes everyone around one to hatred and biting mockery.",["'Hoch- und Wohlgeb.' expanded as 'Highly and Well-born' (abbreviation for Hoch- und Wohlgeboren). 'Hochgeneigt' rendered literally.","Restated the closing clause 'denn das ist gewiß nicht das Mittel…' slightly for English order; the sentence runs on into the next passage (0172), whose text is joined here.","OCR: 'Bodmbast' read as 'Bombast'; 'Dasse' as 'Hasse'; hyphenation breaks silently repaired; '28' is a page number, omitted. A footnote marker ')' appears in 0173, belonging to 'Hoffärtiger'."]],"beobachtungen-0172":["[…] hatred and biting mockery.",["The text of this passage is already translated at the end of 0171, where the sentence begins; here only its closing words are given. Note 'zum Dasse' is read as 'zum Hasse'; 'beipenden' as 'beißenden'."]],"beobachtungen-0173":["It is not necessary that a vain person [*Hoffärtiger*] should at the same time be arrogant [*hochmüthig*], that is, form an exaggerated and false conception of his own merits; he may perhaps not rate himself higher than he is worth, but simply have a false taste in making this worth of his outwardly felt.",["Footnote to the preceding text. OCR 'sich s eine' read as 'sich eine'."]],"beobachtungen-0174":["In love the German and the Englishman have a fairly good constitution [*Wagen* read as *Magen*, 'stomach'], somewhat delicate in sentiment, but more of a sound and robust taste. The Italian is brooding in this matter, the Spaniard fantastic, the Frenchman a dainty-toothed nibbler [*vernascht*].",["OCR 'Wagen' is surely 'Magen'; rendered 'stomach' in sense: 'a fairly good stomach'. I give 'constitution' loosely; 'stomach' is the literal reading.","'Vernascht' (Kant's 'vernascht'): sweet-toothed, fond of dainties; the rendering is a choice.","'Empfindung' = sensation/sentiment; here 'sentiment' suits the context."]],"beobachtungen-0175":["*) It has been observed before that the English, though so shrewd a people, can nevertheless easily be taken in by a bold announcement of something strange and absurd, so as to believe it at first; of which there are many examples. But a bold temperament, prepared by various experiences in which many strange things have nevertheless been found true, quickly breaks through the small scruples by which a weak and distrustful head is soon held up, and so, without any merit of its own, is sometimes preserved from error.",["Footnote. The syntax 'berückt werden sie anfänglich zu glauben' is irregular (OCR or Kant's slip); smoothed as 'taken in … so as to believe it at first'.","OCR: 'une so' read 'und so'; 'vers wahrt' read 'bewahrt'."]],"beobachtungen-0176":["*) Fanaticism must always be distinguished from enthusiasm. The former believes it feels an immediate and extraordinary communion with a higher nature; the latter signifies the state of mind in which it has been heated beyond the proper degree by some principle, whether by the maxim of patriotic virtue, or of friendship, or of religion, without any fancy of a supernatural communion having a part in it.",["'Gemüth' = mind. 'Einbildung' rendered 'fancy'. OCR 'set' read 'sei'."]],"beobachtungen-0177":["The religion of our quarter of the world is not a matter of wilful taste, but is of a more venerable origin. Hence only the excesses [*Ausschweifungen*] in it, and what in it belongs peculiarly to men, can furnish signs of the different national characteristics. I bring these excesses under the following chief concepts: credulity (*Credulität*), superstition (*Superstition*), fanaticism (*Fanaticism*), and indifference (*Indifferentism*). Credulous for the most part is the ignorant portion of every nation, although it has no appreciable finer feeling. Persuasion depends solely on hearsay and apparent authority, without any sort of finer feeling containing the motive for it. Examples of whole peoples of this kind must be sought in the North. The credulous man, if he is of an adventurous taste, becomes superstitious. This taste is indeed in itself a ground for believing somewhat more readily*), and of two men, one of whom is infected with this feeling, the other of a cold and moderate temperament, the former, even if he really has more understanding, will nevertheless be led by his ruling inclination sooner to believe something unnatural than the other, whom not his insight but his common and phlegmatic feeling preserves from this excess. The superstitious man in religion likes to set between himself and the highest object of veneration certain powerful and astonishing men, giants of holiness, so to speak, to whom nature is obedient and whose conjuring voice locks or unlocks the iron gates of Tartarus, who, while they touch heaven with their heads, still have their feet planted on the lower earth. The instruction of sound reason will accordingly have to overcome great obstacles in Spain, not because it has to drive out ignorance there, but because a strange taste stands in its way, to which the natural is common, and which never believes itself to be in a sublime sensation unless its object is adventurous. Fanaticism is, so to say, a devout presumption, and is occasioned by a certain pride and an excessive confidence in oneself, in order to approach the heavenly natures more closely and to raise oneself by a wonderful flight above the ordinary and prescribed order. The fanatic speaks only of immediate inspiration and of the contemplative life, while the superstitious man makes vows before the images of great wonder-working saints and places his confidence in the imagined and inimitable excellences of other persons, remote from his own nature. Even the excesses bear with them, as we have remarked above, signs of national feeling, and so fanaticism**) has, at least in former times, been found most in Germany and England, and is as it were an unnatural excrescence of the noble feeling which belongs to the character of these peoples; and on the whole it is by far not so harmful as the superstitious inclination, even if at the outset it is violent, because the heat of a fanatical spirit gradually cools and by its nature must at last arrive at proper moderation, whereas superstition takes root unnoticed ever deeper in a quiet and passive temper of mind, and wholly deprives the fettered man of the confidence ever to free himself from a harmful delusion. Finally, a vain and frivolous man is always without stronger feeling for the sublime, and his religion is without emotion, for the most part only a matter of fashion, which he observes with all politeness and remains cold. This is practical indifferentism, to which the French national spirit seems most inclined, from which there is but one step to impious mockery, and which at bottom, if the inner worth is considered, has little advantage over a total renunciation.",["Passage spans pp. 250–252 and includes footnote markers (*, **) pointing to the footnotes printed as 0175 and 0176; placement of the markers is conjectural from OCR.","Sentence 'Die Überredung kommt … die Triebfeder enthielte' restated plainly; 'Gefühl' kept as 'feeling'. 'Schwärmerei' rendered 'fanaticism' as Kant glosses it with *Fanaticism*; 'Schwärmer' as 'fanatic'.","OCR repairs: 'Bewährt' read 'bewahrt'; 'Vii— dem' as 'Bildern'; 'fegt' as 'setzt'; 'Neignug' as 'Neigung'; 'Cmpfindung' as 'Empfindung'; 'Fing' as 'Flug'. 'Aberglaube' and 'Leichtgläubigkeit' for the Latin glosses.","'fegt … von seiner eigenen Natur' is doubtful: read as 'setzt … von seiner eigenen Natur [entfernt]' with 'entfernt' implied; the bracket-free rendering 'remote from his own nature' is a smoothing and should be checked against the original."]],"beobachtungen-0178":["*) In Peking the ceremony is still performed, at an eclipse of the sun or moon, of driving off by a great noise the dragon which wants to devour these heavenly bodies; and a wretched custom from the oldest times of ignorance is retained, although people are now better instructed.",["Footnote. OCR 'versagen' read as 'verjagen' ('drive away')."]],"beobachtungen-0179":["If we pass in a cursory glance through the other parts of the world, we find the Arab to be the noblest human being in the Orient, though of a feeling that degenerates greatly into the adventurous [*das Abenteuerliche*]. He is hospitable, magnanimous and truthful; but his narrative and history, and indeed his whole sentiment [*Empfindung*], is always interwoven with something marvellous. His overheated imagination presents things to him in unnatural and distorted images, and even the spread of his religion was a great adventure. If the Arabs are, so to speak, the Spaniards of the Orient, the Persians are the French of Asia. They are good poets, polite, and of a fairly refined taste. They are not such strict observers of Islam, and allow their disposition [*Gemütsart*], which inclines to gaiety, a rather free interpretation of the Koran. The Japanese might be regarded, so to speak, as the English of this part of the world, but hardly in any other quality than their steadfastness, which degenerates into the utmost obstinacy, their bravery and their contempt of death. For the rest, they show few signs of a finer feeling. The Indians have a dominant taste for grotesqueries [*Fratzen*] of the kind that borders on the adventurous. Their religion consists of grotesqueries. Idols of monstrous shape, the priceless tooth of the mighty ape Hanuman, the unnatural penances of the fakirs (heathen mendicant monks), and so on, are in this taste. The voluntary sacrifice of wives on the very same funeral pyre that consumes their husband's corpse is a hideous adventure. What silly grotesqueries are not contained in the elaborate and studied compliments of the Chinese! Even their paintings are grotesque, and represent strange and unnatural figures, the like of which is to be met with nowhere in the world. They also have venerable grotesqueries, because these are of immemorial usage; and no people in the world has more of them than these.",["A stray ')' before 'und keine Völkerschaft' looks like an OCR remnant (possibly a footnote mark); omitted. Line-break hyphens and the broken 'angese— den' are silently repaired.","*Empfindung* is rendered 'sentiment' here rather than 'sensation', since the sense is Kant's characterisation of a people's manner of feeling. *Fratzen* is rendered 'grotesqueries', as a choice."]],"beobachtungen-0180":["The Negroes of Africa have by nature no feeling that rises above the trifling. Mr. Hume challenges anyone to cite a single example in which a Negro has shown talents, and asserts that among the hundreds of thousands of blacks who are transported from their countries to other places, although very many of them have even been set free, not a single one has ever been found who has accomplished anything great in art or science or any other praiseworthy quality; whereas among the whites some are constantly rising from the lowest rabble, and acquiring through superior gifts a certain standing in the world. So essential is the difference between these two human races, and it seems to be as great in regard to mental capacities [*Gemütsfähigkeiten*] as in colour. The religion of fetishes, widespread among them, is perhaps a kind of idolatry that sinks as deep into the trifling as seems at all possible to human nature. A bird's feather, a cow's horn, a shell, or any other common thing, as soon as it has been consecrated by a few words, is an object of veneration and of invocation in oaths. The blacks are very vain, but after the Negro fashion, and so talkative that they must be driven apart with cudgels.",["OCR slips silently corrected ('ftd)' = sich; 'ent— weder' etc.). Passage reproduces Kant's text as it stands; the wording is Kant's own and is rendered without softening."]],"beobachtungen-0181":["Among all savages there is no people that shows so sublime a character of mind as those of North America. They have a strong feeling for honour, and while, in order to hunt it down, they seek out wild adventures hundreds of miles away, they are nonetheless extremely attentive to guard against the slightest diminution of it, when their equally hard enemy, having seized them, seeks by cruel torments to force cowardly sighs from them. The Canadian savage is, moreover, truthful and honest. The friendship that he forms is as adventurous and enthusiastic as anything ever reported of it from the oldest and most fabulous times. He is extremely proud, feels the whole worth of freedom, and tolerates, even in his upbringing, no treatment that would make him feel a lowly subjection. Lycurgus probably gave laws to just such savages, and if a lawgiver arose among the Six Nations, one would see a Spartan republic arise in the New World; just as the enterprise of the Argonauts differs little from the war expeditions of these Indians, and Jason has nothing over Attakullakulla except the honour of a Greek name. All these savages have little feeling for the beautiful in the moral sense, and the magnanimous forgiveness of an offence, which is at once noble and beautiful, is as a virtue quite unknown among savages, but is despised as wretched cowardice. Bravery is the savage's greatest merit, and revenge his sweetest delight. The remaining natives of this part of the world show few traces of a character of mind disposed to finer sentiments, and an extraordinary insensibility constitutes the mark of these kinds of human beings.",["OCR corrected silently: 'Weilen' = Meilen, 'Jafon' = Jason, 'Attafafullakulla' = Attakullakulla, 'aben— teuerfid)' = abenteuerlich.","'Sechs Nationen' = the Six Nations (Iroquois confederacy)."]],"beobachtungen-0182":["If we consider the relation between the sexes in these parts of the world, we find that the European alone has discovered the secret of adorning the sensuous charm of a powerful inclination with so many flowers, and of interweaving it with so much that is moral, that he has not only greatly heightened its agreeableness but also made it very decorous. The inhabitant of the Orient is in this point of very false taste. Since he has no concept of the morally beautiful that can be combined with this impulse, he loses even the worth of sensuous pleasure, and his harem is to him a constant source of unrest. He falls into all sorts of amorous grotesqueries [*Fratzen*], among which the imaginary jewel is one of the foremost, of which above all he seeks to make sure, whose whole value consists only in its being broken, and about which in our part of the world a great deal of malicious doubt is generally entertained; and to preserve it he makes use of very unjust and often disgusting means. Hence the woman there is always in prison, whether she be a maiden or have a barbarous, incapable and always suspicious husband. In the countries of the blacks, what better can one expect than what is found there throughout, namely the female sex in the deepest slavery? A timorous man is always a severe master over the weaker, just as with us too that man is always a tyrant in the kitchen who, outside his house, hardly ventures to show himself before anyone. Father Labat reports, to be sure, that a Negro carpenter, to whom he had objected to his haughty conduct toward his wives, replied: You whites are real fools, for first you concede so much to your wives, and afterwards you complain when they drive you mad; and it is as if there were something in this that perhaps de-",["Breaks off mid-word ('ver-'), continued in the next passage ('diente in Überlegung gezogen zu werden'); translated as it stands, with 'de-' for 'ver-'.","'Grauensperfon' read as Frauensperson; 'Ne zerzimmermann' as Negerzimmermann; 'feld' as seid; 'efelhafter' as ekelhafter.","The sentence on the 'Kleinod' was left in its single long structure only partly; I kept it as one sentence to preserve the run of relative clauses."]],"beobachtungen-0183":["served to be taken into consideration; but in short, this fellow was",["Continues the sentence broken off in the previous passage ('ver-' + 'diente'): 'perhaps deserved to be considered'."]],"beobachtungen-0184":["black from head to foot, a clear proof that what",[]],"beobachtungen-0185":["he said was stupid. Among all savages there are none among whom the",[]],"beobachtungen-0186":["female sex stands in greater real esteem than among those of Canada. Perhaps they even surpass in this our civilised part of the world.",["The stray line number '5' is omitted."]],"beobachtungen-0187":["Not as if humble attentions were paid to the women there; | those are mere compliments. No, they really have the power to command. They assem-",["The sign | is carried over as it stands. 'Ste ver-' read as Sie ver-."]],"beobachtungen-0188":["ble and deliberate upon the most important arrangements of the",[]],"beobachtungen-0189":["nation, upon war and peace. They then send their deputies to the men's council, and commonly their voice is the one",["'joan' read as von (OCR slip)."]],"beobachtungen-0190":["that decides. But they pay dearly enough for this privilege. They",["The OCR 'erkanten' is doubtful; read as 'erkauften' (purchased), which the sense requires."]],"beobachtungen-0191":["have all domestic affairs on their necks, and share in all the",[]],"beobachtungen-0192":["hardships of the men.",[]],"beobachtungen-0193":["If, finally, we cast a few glances at history, we see the taste of men, like a Proteus, constantly assuming changeable [wandel-",["Passage breaks off at a hyphenated word (*wandel-*); the next passage completes it (*wandelbare*). Translated as it stands. A stray line number was dropped."]],"beobachtungen-0194":["-able] forms. The ancient times of the Greeks and Romans",["Begins with the second half of *wandelbare*, continuing the previous passage."]],"beobachtungen-0195":["showed clear marks of a genuine feeling for the beautiful as well",[]],"beobachtungen-0196":["as for the sublime in poetry, sculpture, architecture,",[]],"beobachtungen-0197":["legislation, and even in manners. The rule of the Roman",[]],"beobachtungen-0198":["emperors changed the noble as well as the beautiful simplicity into the splendid",[]],"beobachtungen-0199":["and then into false glitter, of which the surviving remains",[]],"beobachtungen-0200":["of their eloquence and poetry, and even the history of their manners, can still inform us. Gradually this remnant too of the [fine] taste died out with the complete decay of the state. The barbarians, after they in their turn had consolidated their power, introduced a certain perverted taste, which is called the Gothic, and which ended in grotesques. Grotesques were to be seen not only in architecture, but also in the sciences and in the other customs. The degenerate feeling, once it",["The OCR reads *seinem Geschmacks*; I take it to be *feinen Geschmacks* and supply [fine] in brackets. Doubtful reading.","The hyphenation \"be— lehren\" was silently rejoined (*belehren*). *Fratzen* rendered 'grotesques'.","Breaks off mid-sentence (*da es*); the next passage continues."]],"beobachtungen-0201":["had been led by false art, took on any other natural form sooner than the old simplicity of nature, and was found either in the extravagant or in the frivolous. The highest flight which human genius took in order to ascend to the sublime consisted in adventures. One saw adventurers spiritual and secular, and often a repulsive and monstrous bastard breed of both: monks with the missal in one hand and the war-banner in the other, followed by whole armies of deluded victims, who were to have their bones buried in other climes and in a holier soil; consecrated warriors, sanctified by solemn vows to violence and misdeeds; and later a strange kind of heroic fantasts, who called themselves knights and sought out adventures, tournaments, [duels] and romantic exploits. During this period religion, together with the sciences and manners, was disfigured by wretched grotesques; and it is observed that taste does not readily degenerate on one side without also displaying clear signs of its corruption in everything else that belongs to the finer feeling. The monastic vows made of a large part of useful men numerous societies of industrious idlers, whose brooding manner of life made them skilful at hatching a thousand scholastic grotesques, which went out from there into the wider world and spread their kind. At last, after human genius has happily raised itself again from an almost total destruction by a sort of palingenesis, we see in our own days the correct taste for the beautiful and the noble blossoming, in the arts and sciences as well as in respect of the moral. Nothing more remains to be wished than that the false glitter, which so easily deceives, may not imperceptibly lead us away from noble simplicity; but above all, that the still undiscovered secret of education may be wrested from the old delusion, so as to raise the moral feeling early, in the breast of every young citizen of the world, to an active sensation [Empfindung], lest all refinement come to no more than the fleeting and idle pleasure of judging with more or less taste what goes on outside us.",["OCR slips silently corrected (*sah*, *Abenteurer*, *Religion*, *Tagen*, *Palingenesie*, *übertriebenen*).","\"Smet kämpfe\" is corrupt in the OCR; I read *Zweikämpfe* and render [duels] in brackets. Doubtful.","The very long sentence beginning 'Mönche mit dem Meßbuch' is kept as a series of clauses, joined by semicolons, rather than broken further.","*Empfindung* in the final sentence is rendered 'sensation' per the fixed terminology, though here it means something closer to 'feeling'."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0001":["Essay\non the Maladies of the Head.",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0002":["| | On the Maladies of the Head.",["The OCR garble between the bars (\"93 e v fit d\") is a running head or page number and has been omitted; the | signs are retained."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0003":["The simplicity and frugality of nature demand and form in",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0004":["man only common concepts and a rough honesty; the artificial constraint and luxury of the civil constitution hatches out petty wits and pseudo-reasoners [*Witzlinge und Vernünftler*], on occasion also fools and cheats, and gives birth to the wise or decorous semblance, in which one can dispense with understanding [Verstand]",["The sentence is continued in the next passage ('as well as with uprightness'), and I leave it unfinished here."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0005":["as well as with uprightness, provided only that the fair veil which propriety spreads over the secret defects of the head or the heart is woven thickly enough. In proportion as art rises higher, reason and virtue finally become the general watchword; yet in such a way that zeal in speaking of both can relieve well-instructed and refined persons of the trouble of burdening themselves with their possession. The general esteem in which both these praised qualities stand nevertheless makes this notable difference, that everyone is far more jealous on behalf of the excellences of understanding than on behalf of the good qualities of the will, and that, in a comparison between stupidity and knavery, no one hesitates for a moment to declare in favour of the latter. This is also certainly very well devised, since, if everything depends altogether upon art, then",["*Verstand* is rendered 'understanding' in the previous passage and *Verstandesvorzüge* 'excellences of understanding'.","The sentence is not broken up, since the passage ends mid-sentence and the next passage completes it ('subtle cunning cannot be dispensed with...')."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0006":["subtle cunning cannot be dispensed with, but uprightness can, being in such a relation only a hindrance. I live among wise and well-mannered citizens, that is, among those who understand how to seem so, and I flatter myself that they will be fair enough to credit me with so much of this refinement as to believe that, even if I were in possession of the most proven [gap in the text] for removing the maladies of the head and of the heart at their root, I should still have scruples about putting this old-fashioned lumber in the way of public commerce. I am well aware that the fashionable cure of understanding and heart is already making desired progress, and that the physicians of the former in particular, who call themselves logicians, satisfy the general demand very well, since they have made the important discovery that the human head is really a drum, which sounds only because it is empty. I therefore see nothing better for myself than to imitate the method of the physicians, who believe they have done their patient a great deal of good if they give his illness a name; and I sketch a little onomastic of the infirmities of the head, from its paralysis in imbecility to its convulsions in madness. But in order to recognize these loathsome diseases in their gradual descent, I find it necessary first to explain the milder degrees of them, from dull-wittedness up to foolishness, because these qualities are more current in civil relations and yet lead to the former.",["The OCR reads \"der bewährtesten . wäre\": a word (perhaps a noun such as 'remedies') is missing or illegible. I do not fill it; marked [gap in the text].","*Onomastik* kept in near-transliteration, 'onomastic', meaning a nomenclature or list of names.","*Kopf* is rendered 'head' throughout, and *Narrheit* as 'foolishness' and *Tollheit* as 'madness'; these are choices, as no fixed term is prescribed."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0007":["The dull head lacks wit; the blockhead lacks understanding. Quickness in grasping and remembering something, and likewise ease in expressing it fittingly, depend very much upon wit. Hence a man who is not stupid may nevertheless be very dull, in so far as hardly anything will go into his head, although later, with greater maturity of judgment, he may well understand it. Nor does difficulty in expressing oneself prove any lack of the faculty of understanding; it proves only that wit does not give sufficient help in clothing the thought in the many signs, some of which fit it most aptly. The famous Jesuit Clavius was dismissed from the schools as incapable. (For by the Orbilian test of understanding a boy is good for nothing if he can make neither verses nor school-chriae.) Afterwards he chanced upon mathematics; the game changed, and his former teachers were mere blockheads beside him. Practical judgment about things, such as the countryman, the artist or the seaman etc. requires, is very different from the judgment we pass upon the devices by which men deal with one another. The latter is not so much understanding as cunning (*Verschmitztheit*), and the amiable lack of this much-praised faculty is entitled simplicity (*Einfalt*). If the cause of it is to be sought in a weakness of the power of judgment generally, such a man is entitled a booby, a simpleton, etc. Intrigues and false artifices gradually become the customary maxims in civil society and greatly complicate the game of human actions. It is therefore no wonder if a man who is otherwise intelligent and honest must fall into snares everywhere among deceivers and give them much to laugh at. Either he finds all this slyness too contemptible to occupy himself with it, or his honest and benevolent heart cannot bring itself to form so odious a concept of human nature. The result is that the expression 'a good man' no longer signifies, as a figure of speech, but quite directly a simpleton, and on occasion even a H——. For in the language of rogues no one is an intelligent man except him who holds all | others to be nothing better than he himself is, namely deceivers. The impulses of human nature, which, when they are of high degree [OCR: *Graben*; read *Graden*]",["Restated the long sentence on the 'good man' as several sentences; 'Orbilische Verstandesprobe' rendered 'Orbilian test of understanding' (Orbilius, Horace's harsh schoolmaster).","OCR corrections made silently: *Shulchrien* → *Schulchrien* (school-chriae, rhetorical exercises), *Tropi* → *Tropf*, *si)* → *sich*, stray letters/line numbers dropped. 'H——' kept as printed (the word is left blank; probably *Hahnrei*, 'cuckold', but not supplied).","Final clause breaks off; 'Graben' is read as 'Graden' and rendered 'of high degree', continued in the next passage. The sign | is carried over as in the source.","*Witz* = wit; *stumpf* = dull; *Dummkopf* = blockhead; *Verstand* = understanding; *Urtheilskraft* = power of judgment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0008":["are entitled passions, are the motive forces of the will. The understanding enters only in order to estimate the whole sum total of the satisfaction of all",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage and breaks off mid-clause; 'Facit' rendered 'sum total'."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0009":["the inclinations together, from the end represented, and also to discover the means to it. If some passion is especially powerful, the faculty of understanding helps only little against it; for the enchanted man sees quite well the grounds against his favourite inclination, but he feels himself powerless to give them active force.",["Stray OCR letters (b) omitted. *Vermögen* in *Verstandesfähigkeit* rendered 'faculty of understanding'."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0010":["If this inclination is good in itself, and the person is in other respects rational,",["Breaks off; the apodosis follows in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0011":["except that the overpowering propensity closes the prospect of the evil consequences, then this condition of fettered reason is folly (*Thorheit*).",["OCR 'Der-nunft' read as *Vernunft*. *Thorheit* = folly, *Thor* = fool (a choice; Kant contrasts it with *Narrheit* below)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0012":["A fool may have much understanding, even in the judgment upon those actions in which he is foolish. He must indeed possess a fair amount of",["OCR 'Nr—theil' read as *Urtheil*; 'giem-' read as *ziem-*(*lich*). Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0013":["understanding and a good heart, in order to be entitled to this milder name for his excesses. The fool may at a pinch make an excellent adviser for others, even though his advice is without effect upon himself. He is made sensible only by damage or by age, which, however, often only drives out one folly in order to make room for another. Amorous passion, or a high degree of ambition, has from of old made many reasonable people fools. A girl compels the dread Alcides to draw the thread from the distaff, and the idle citizens of Athens send Alexander to the end of the world by their frivolous praise. There are also inclinations of lesser violence and generality which nevertheless do not fail to produce their folly: the passion for building, the fancy for pictures, the mania for books. The degenerate man has departed from his natural place and is drawn by everything and held by everything. The sensible man is the opposite of the fool; but he who is without folly | is a sage. This sage may perhaps be sought in the moon; perhaps one is there without passion and has infinitely much reason. The insensible man is secured against folly by his stupidity, but in the eyes of the common sort he has the air of a sage. Pyrrho, seeing on a ship in a storm, when everyone was anxiously occupied, a pig eating quietly from its trough, pointed to it and said: 'Such should be the calm of a sage.' The insensible man is the sage of Pyrrho. |",["OCR 'läppisches Loch' read as *läppisches Lob* ('frivolous praise'); 'Atheus' → *Athens*; 'Dm' → *Dem*; *gescheut* (= *gescheit*) rendered 'sensible'. Line numbers dropped; the signs | and || kept where they stand.","Alcides = Hercules (at Omphale's distaff). The sentence on 'Neigungen von minderer Heftigkeit' is divided at the colon only; others kept in order."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0014":["If the ruling passion is in itself hateful and at the same time absurd enough to take for the satisfaction of it just what is set directly against its natural aim, this condition of perverted reason is fatuity (*Narrheit*). The fool understands the true aim of his passion very well, even though he concedes to it a strength able to fetter reason. The fatuous man, however, is by it made so stupid at the same time that he believes himself in possession only when he actually deprives himself of what he desires. Pyrrhus knew very well that bravery and power win universal admiration; he followed the impulse of ambition quite rightly and was nothing more than what Cineas took him for, namely a fool. But when Nero exposes himself to public ridicule by reading wretched verses from a stage in order to win the poet's prize, and at the very end of his life says: *quantus artifex morior!*, I see in this feared and laughed-at ruler of Rome nothing better than a fatuous man. I hold that all fatuity is properly grafted upon two passions, pride and avarice. Both inclinations are unjust and are therefore hated; both are by nature absurd, and their end destroys itself. The proud man betrays an uncovered presumption of superiority over others by a plain disdain of them. He believes himself honoured when he is hissed off, for nothing is clearer than that the contempt of others stirs up their own vanity against the presumptuous man. The miser, in his own opinion, has great need of much and cannot possibly spare the least of his goods; yet he really is without all of them, since by stinginess he lays an embargo upon them. The blindness of pride makes partly silly, partly puffed-up fatuous men, according as either frivolous flightiness or stiff stupidity has taken possession of the empty head. Niggardly greed has from of old given occasion to many ridiculous stories, which could hardly be invented more fantas-",["*Narrheit* / *Narr* rendered 'fatuity' / 'fatuous man' to keep it distinct from *Thorheit* / *Thor* ('folly' / 'fool'); a choice.","OCR 'gelebt' read as *gesetzt* ('set directly against'); 'Uero' → *Nero*; 'augepfiffen' → *ausgepfiffen*; 'eg' → *es*; 'empöre' left as subjunctive of indirect speech, rendered 'stirs up'. 'Anmaßer' = presumptuous man. Doubtful OCR in 'deut ` liebe' read as *deutliche*.","The passage ends mid-word (*wunder-*), completed by the next passage (*wunderlicher*); I split the English adverb in the same place ('fantas-' / 'tically'), as an approximation. Long sentences on pride divided."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0015":["tically than they actually occurred. The fool is not wise; the fatuous man is not shrewd. The mockery which the fool draws upon himself is merry and gentle; the fatuous man deserves the sharpest scourge of the satirist, yet he does not feel it. One need not wholly despair that a fool may yet become sensible; but whoever thinks to make a fatuous man shrewd is washing a blackamoor. The reason is that in the former a true and natural inclination rules, which reason at most only fetters, but in the latter a silly chimera, which inverts its principles. I leave it to others to decide",["Begins mid-word; the English 'tically' answers the previous passage's 'fantas-'. OCR 'jpenit' read as *Gespenst* ('Hirngespenst', chimera); 'gescheut' → 'sensible'; 'Zieht' lower-cased silently.","'wäscht einen Mohren' kept literal ('washing a blackamoor', i.e. an impossible task); passage breaks off mid-sentence and is left unfinished."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0016":["whether one really has cause to be troubled over Holberg's odd prophecy, namely that the daily increase of fools is a matter for concern and gives ground to fear that they might yet take it into their heads",["Fragment, broken off mid-sentence as in the source; not completed."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0017":["to found the fifth monarchy. But supposing that they really did have this in view,",["Fragment, continued in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0018":["they need not for all that exert themselves so greatly. For one could well whisper in another's ear what the well-known buffoon of a neighbouring court called out to the students who ran after him, when he rode in fool's dress through a Polish town: \"Gentlemen, be diligent and learn something; for if there are too many of us, we shall never all of us have bread.\"",["Joins the preceding fragment (*im Schilde führen*, 'to have in view'). OCR 'Boses' read as *Hofes* ('court'), a conjecture.","The Academy text puts the closing quotation as given; the final words are rendered freely for idiom (*nimmermehr alle Brod haben*)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0019":["I pass from the infirmities of the head which are despised and mocked to those which are commonly regarded with pity; from those which do not interfere with free civil intercourse to those which the care of the authorities takes up, and on account of which",["Broken off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. OCR 'ge— höhnt' read as *gehöhnt*."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0020":["it makes ordinances. I divide these maladies into two kinds, those of impotence [*Ohnmacht*] and those of perversity [*Verkehrtheit*]. The former come under the general name of imbecility [*Blödsinnigkeit*], the latter under the name of the disordered mind [*gestörtes Gemüth*]. The imbecile is in a state of great impotence of memory and of reason, and commonly even of the sensible sensations [*sinnliche Empfindungen*]. This evil is for the most part incurable; for if it is hard to remove the wild disorders of a deranged brain, it must be almost impossible to pour new life into organs that have died. The appearances of this weakness, which never permits the unhappy sufferer to pass beyond the state of childhood, are too well known to make it necessary to dwell on them at length.",["OCR 'Bloofinnige' read as *Blödsinnige*; stray ')' after 'sich' dropped.","*Gemüth* = mind; *Vernunft* = reason; *Empfindung* = sensation; *Erscheinungen* here is the ordinary 'appearances' in the sense of symptoms or manifestations."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0021":["The infirmities of the disordered head can be reduced to as many main kinds as there are faculties of the mind [*Gemüthsfähigkeiten*] that have been affected. I believe I can arrange them all under the following three divisions. First, the perversity of the concepts of experience, in derangement [*Verrückung*]; secondly, the judgment [*Urtheilskraft*] thrown into disorder, in the first instance in regard to this experience, in insanity [*Wahnsinn*]; thirdly, reason [*Vernunft*] become perverted in respect of more general judgments, in dementia [*Wahnwitz*]. All the other appearances of the sick brain can, as it seems to me, be regarded either as different degrees of the aforesaid accidents, or as an unfortunate combination of these evils with one another, or finally as their grafting upon powerful passions, and so be subordinated to the classes enumerated.",["Renderings of *Verrückung*, *Wahnsinn*, *Wahnwitz* are choices; the German is given in brackets.","*Zunächst bei dieser Erfahrung* rendered 'in the first instance in regard to this experience'. OCR 'Gemüthsfähigfeiten' corrected."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0022":["As regards the first evil, namely derangement, I explain its appearances in the following way. The soul of every man, even in the soundest state, is busy painting all manner of images of things that are not present, or again completing some imperfect resemblance in the representation of things that are present, by one or another chimerical trait which the creative poetic faculty draws into the sensation. There is no reason whatever to believe that in the waking state our spirit follows in this other laws than in sleep. It is rather to be presumed that in the former case the vivid sensible impressions merely obscure the more delicate images of the chimeras and make them unrecognisable, whereas in sleep, when access to the soul is closed to all outer impressions, these images have their whole strength. It is therefore no wonder that dreams, so long as they last, are taken for true experiences of real things. For since they are then the strongest representations in the soul, they are in this state just what sensations are in waking. Now suppose that certain chimeras, from whatever cause, had as it were injured one or other of the organs of the brain, in such a manner that the impression upon them had become as deep, and at the same time as accurate, as a sensible sensation can make it. Then this phantom of the brain would be bound to be taken for a real experience, even in waking and with good, sound reason",["Long sentences divided. OCR 'sein Wunder' read as *kein Wunder*; 'gee sünder' joined as *gesünder* across the passage break.","Passage breaks off mid-sentence ('...gehalten werden'); the verb completes in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0023":["[bound to] be taken for a real experience",["Fragment; continues from the preceding passage ('gesünder' completed there) and into the next. 'Bound to' is supplied in brackets because the modal *müssen* stands in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0024":["For it would be in vain to oppose grounds of reason to a sensation, or to a repre-",["Broken mid-word ('Vor-' / 'stellung'); left as in the source."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0025":["sentation that equals it in strength, since the senses give far greater conviction of real things",["Continues the preceding fragment (*Vorstellung*)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0026":["than does an inference of reason",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0027":["[*Vernunftschluß*]; at least he whom this",["*Vernunftschluß* is rendered at the end of the previous fragment; the German is placed here because of the line break. Fragment, continued next."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0028":["chimera has bewitched can never be brought by sophistical reasoning",["*Vernünfteln* = 'sophistical reasoning' (reasoning in the pejorative sense)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0029":["to doubt the reality of his supposed sensation. One finds",["Fragment, word broken ('sin-' / 'det')."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0030":["also that persons who in other cases show reason sufficiently mature",["OCR 'dei' read as *det* (*findet*)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0031":["nevertheless persist firmly that they have seen with all attentiveness who knows what",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0032":["spectral shapes and grotesque faces, and that they",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0033":["are even subtle enough to bring their imagined experience into connection with many a subtle",["OCR 'sein genug' read as *fein genug*; the word 'subtle' repeats because of the line break ('sub— / tilen')."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0034":["judgment of reason. This peculiarity",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0035":["of the disordered person, by which, without any specially marked degree of a violent illness,",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0036":["he is accustomed in the waking state to represent to himself certain things as",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0037":["clearly sensed, of which nevertheless nothing is present,",["Fragment."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0038":["is entitled derangement [*Verrückung*]. The deranged person is therefore a dreamer in waking. If the usual delusion of his senses is only in part a chimera, but for the most part a real sensation, then he who is disposed in a higher degree to such perversity is a visionary [*Phantast*]. When",["*heißt* = 'is entitled'. Passage breaks off with 'Wenn' leading into the next; OCR stray 'h' dropped."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0039":["chimera, but for the most part a real sensation, then he who is in the",["Overlaps with the end of the preceding passage as given; translated as it stands."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0040":["higher degree disposed to such perversity is a visionary [*Phantast*]. When we lie, after waking, in a languid and gentle absent-mindedness,",["Overlap with the preceding passage again preserved as the source gives it."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0041":["our imagination sketches the irregular figures, say of the bed-curtains,",["OCR 'Heit—vorhänge' read as *Bettvorhänge*, a conjecture."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0042":["or of certain stains on a nearby wall, into human shapes with an apparent accuracy which entertains us in not unpleasant fashion, but whose delusion we can dispel at any moment, if we wish. We are then dreaming only in part, and have the chimera in our power. If something similar happens in a higher degree, without the attention of the waking person being able to separate the delusion from the deceptive imagination,",["Stray OCR letter 'b' dropped. Sentence broken off at the passage boundary; completed in the next."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0043":["then this perversity lets one suspect a visionary [*Phantast*]. This self-deception in the sensations is, moreover, very common, and so long as it is only moderate, it is spared such a name; although, when a passion is added, the same weakness of mind can degenerate into actual fanaticism [*Phantasterei*]. Otherwise, through a common delusion men do not see what is there, but what their inclination paints for them: the collector of natural curiosities sees cities in Florentine stone, the devout man sees the story of the Passion in spotted marble, that lady sees through a telescope the shadows of two lovers in the moon, but her pastor sees two church steeples. Terror makes lances and swords out of the rays of the northern lights, and in the twilight a giant spectre out of a signpost.",["OCR 'Phantasien' read as *Phantasten*; the stray '4' and 'h' dropped. 'Spared such a name' = *mit einer solchen Benennung verschont*.","*Seherohr* rendered 'telescope'."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0044":["The fantastical constitution of mind is nowhere more common than in hypochondria. The chimeras which this malady hatches do not, properly speaking, | deceive the outer senses. They merely present to the hypochondriac a delusion of a sensation of his own condition, whether of body or of soul, and this for the most part is an empty whim. The hypochondriac has an ailment which, in whatever place its chief seat may be, probably wanders restlessly through the nervous | tissue in all sorts of parts of the body. It draws, however, chiefly a melancholy vapour about the seat of the soul, so that the patient feels in himself the semblance of almost every disease of which he merely hears. He therefore likes to talk of nothing so much as of his indisposition, reads medical books gladly, and finds his own symptoms everywhere. In company his good humour will sometimes steal over him unawares, and then he laughs much, eats well, and commonly has the appearance of a healthy man.\n\nAs regards his inner fantasticality, the images in his brain often acquire a strength and persistence which is burdensome to him. Suppose that a ridiculous figure is in his head (although he himself recognises it as only an image of the fancy); or that this whim draws from him an unseemly laughter in the presence of others, without his indicating the cause; or that all sorts of gloomy representations arouse in him a violent impulse to perpetrate some evil, the outbreak of which he himself anxiously fears, and which nevertheless never issues in the deed. In such cases his condition has much in common with that of a madman [Verrückter], but there is no cause for alarm. The ailment is not deeply rooted, and, so far as the mind is concerned, commonly passes away either of itself or by the aid of some reme- | dies.\n\nOne and the same representation acts upon sensation in quite different degrees, according to the different dispositions of | mind in men. There is accordingly a kind of fantasticality which is imputed to a man merely because the degree of feeling with which he is moved by certain objects is judged excessive, measured by the moderation of a sound head. On this footing the melancholic is a fantast [Phantast] in regard to the ills of life. Love has exceedingly many fantastic raptures, and the fine mas-",["Line numbers dropped; the signs | are kept where they stand, at the places of the original line-breaks (Arze- | neimittel, Gemüths- | zustande).","OCR slips corrected silently: täu= {eben = täuschen; bermaßen = dermaßen (translated 'so that'); finftere, gefunden, ge— wissen, etc. The sentence 'Wenn ihm eine lächerliche Figur…' is a long conditional; I have restated it as a series of supposals.","Gemüthsbeschaffenheit = 'constitution of mind'; Zufälle = 'symptoms'; 'es hat keine Noth' rendered 'there is no cause for alarm'; Phantast kept as 'fantast', Phantasterei as 'fantasticality'.","The passage breaks off mid-word (Kunst⸗ | stück), completed in the next passage; I have given 'mas-' as a bracket-free fragment and not completed it."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0045":["[ter]piece of the ancient states consisted in making the citizens, for the sensation of the",["Fragment: 'stück' is the second half of Kunststück (end of previous passage); rendered 'piece' with the bracketed [ter] to join 'mas-' of the preceding passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0046":["public welfare, into fantasts. Whoever by a mo-",["OCR 'Phantasien' corrected to Phantasten, as the sense requires (Bürger … zu Phantasten zu machen)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0047":["[ral] sensation, as by a principle, is more inflamed than",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0048":["others, in their dull and often ignoble feeling, are able to [form a notion of]",["Broken off in the middle of the verb sich vorstellen (vor— | stellen); the bracketed words are supplied only to make the fragment readable."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0049":["[it], is in their representation a fantast. I place",["Continues the verb broken off in the preceding passage ('stellen können'); bracketed word supplied."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0050":["Aristides among usurers, Epictetus among courtiers, and John",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0051":["Jacob Rousseau among the doctors of the Sorbonne. Methinks I",["Johann Jacob Rousseau = Jean-Jacques Rousseau."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0052":["hear a loud scornful laughter, and a hundred voices cry: What fantasies! This ambiguous semblance of fantasticality in moral sensations that are in themselves good is enthusiasm [Enthusiasmus], and nothing great has ever been accomplished in the world without it. Quite otherwise is it with the fanatic [Fanatiker] (visionary, enthusiast [Visionär, Schwärmer]). He is properly a madman [Verrückter] by reason of a supposed imme-",["Stray OCR letter 'o' (a line number) dropped. Schwärmer rendered 'enthusiast' within the parenthesis, though Kant's own distinction between Enthusiasmus and Schwärmerei makes any single English word imperfect.","Breaks off in mid-word (unmit⸗ | telbaren); I give 'imme-' and do not complete it."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0053":["[diate] inspiration and of a great intimacy with the powers of heaven.",["Fragment begins in mid-word ('telbaren'); 'imme-' of the preceding passage is bracketed as [diate]."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0054":["Human nature knows no more dangerous delusion [Blendwerk].",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0055":["When its outbreak is new, when the deceived man has talents, and the great multitude is prepared to take up this ferment most inwardly,",["The sentence continues in the next passage; the particle 'daß'-less construction smoothed ('is prepared to take up' for dieses Gährungsmittel innigst aufzunehmen)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0056":["then at times even the state suffers convulsions. Fanaticism [Schwärmerei] leads the inspired man to the extreme: Mohammed to the throne of a prince, and John of Leyden to the scaffold. To the perversity of the head, so far as it concerns the concepts of experience, I can still to a certain extent reckon the disordered faculty of memory. For this deceives the wretched man who is afflicted with it by a chimerical representation of I know not what former condition which in fact never existed. The man who",["Stray line-number letter 'b' dropped. Schwärmerei here rendered 'fanaticism' since it is the vice of the Schwärmer; Begeisterten = 'the inspired man'.","Erinnerungsvermögen = 'faculty of memory'; Erfahrungsbegriffe = 'concepts of experience'. 'wer weiß was für' rendered 'I know not what'.","Breaks off at 'Derjenige, welcher von den'; I have not completed it."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0057":["speaks of the goods which he claims once to have possessed, or of the king-",["Fragment: begins with 'Gütern', the object of 'von den' at the end of the preceding passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0058":["dom which he once had, and in other respects is not appreciably deceived as to his present",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0059":["condition, is deranged [ein Verrückter] in respect of memory. The aged grumbler who firmly believes that in his you-",["Stray 'Su—' corrected to Jugend; the word is broken at the passage's end."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0060":["[th] the world was far more orderly and men were better, is a fantast in respect of memory.",["'[th]' bracketed to join the word broken off in the previous passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0061":["Up to this point, then, the power of understanding [Verstandeskraft] in the disordered head is not properly impaired, or at least it need not be. For the fault lies properly only in the concepts; the judgments themselves, if one were willing to accept the perverted sensation as true, may be quite correct, and even uncommonly rational. A disturbance of the understanding [Verstand], on the other hand, consists in this, that from experiences which may well be correct one judges quite perversely. The first degree of this sickness is delusion [Wahnsinn], which in the most immediate judgments from experience acts contrary to the common rule of understanding.\n\nThe deluded man sees or recalls objects as correctly as any healthy person. Only he commonly interprets the behaviour of other men, by an absurd delusion, as referring to himself, and believes that he can read from it I know not what ominous intentions, which never enter their minds. To hear him, one would think that the whole town was occupied with him. The market people, who are bargaining with one another and perhaps glance at him, are hatching plots against him; the night-watchman calls out to mock him; and in short he sees nothing but a general conspiracy against himself. The melancholic, who is deluded with regard to his sad or distressing suspicions, is gloomy-minded [ein Trübsinniger]. But there is also every sort of agreeable delusion, and amorous passion flatters, or torments, itself with many strange interpretations which resemble delusion. A proud man is to a certain extent deluded, when he concludes, from the behaviour of others who stare at him in mockery, that they admire him.\n\nThe second degree of the disordered head, in respect of the higher faculty of knowledge [oberen Erkenntnißkraft], is properly reason thrown into disorder, in so far as it goes absurdly astray in imagined subtler judgments upon universal concepts. It may be entitled frenzy [Wahnwitz]. In the higher degree of this disturbance there swarm through the burnt-out brain all sorts of arrogated, over-subtle insights: the discovered longitude at sea, the interpretation of prophecies, or I know not what medley of imprudent rackings of the brain. If the unfortunate man at the same time passes by the judgments of experience, he is entitled crazed [aberwitzig]. But suppose that he has many correct judgments of experience as his foundation, and that his sensation is merely so intoxicated by the novelty and multitude of the consequences which his wit presents to him that he no longer attends to the correctness of the connection. There then often arises a brilliant semblance of frenzy, which can coexist with great genius, in so far as slow reason is no longer able to keep pace with the unruly wit. The condition of the disordered head which renders it insensible to outer sensations is senselessness [Unsinnigkeit]; this,",["Several long sentences divided; 'heißt/genannt werden' rendered 'is entitled'. Kant's graded terms (Wahnsinn, Wahnwitz, aberwitzig, Unsinnigkeit) have no fixed English equivalents; the German is given in brackets.","Kant's 'Begriffen' in 'der Fehler steckt eigentlich nur in den Begriffen' is kept as 'concepts'. 'Empfindung' in the clause on the intoxicated head is kept as 'sensation'.","OCR slips corrected silently: ungez reimten, bedenk— liebe (bedenkliche), anga— ten (angaffen, 'stare at'), Insichten (Einsichten), rich? tige, ter schimmernder (ein schimmernder), sich) (sich); line numbers dropped.","The passage ends with 'diese,' continuing in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0062":["in so far as anger predominates in it, is entitled raving [Raserei]. Despair is the transient senselessness of one without hope. The roaring violence of a disordered person is entitled in general madness [Tobsucht]. The raving madman [Tobsüchtige], in so far as he is senseless [unsinnig], is mad [toll].",["The OCR opens with 'wem *', which is unintelligible and may be a misread footnote mark; I have left it out and the doubtful reading should be checked against the Academy text. The antecedent of 'diese' is Unsinnigkeit (end of previous passage).","Tobsucht/Raserei/toll have no exact English equivalents; the German is given in brackets."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0063":["Man in the state of nature can be subject only to few follies, and hardly at all to any distraction of mind [Narrheit]. His needs keep him at all times close to experience, and give his sound understanding so light an occupation that he scarcely notices that he requires understanding for his actions. His coarse and common desires are moderated by indolence, and this leaves to the little power of judgment [Urtheilskraft] which he needs force enough to rule over them in accordance with his greatest advantage. Where, then, should he find material for folly [Narrheit], since, being unconcerned about the judgment of others, he can be neither vain nor puffed up? Since he has no representation whatever of the value of goods unenjoyed, he is secured against the absurdity of miserly avarice; and since no wit ever finds entrance into his head, he is equally well protected against all crack-brained extravagance [Aberwitz]. In the same way, derangement of mind [Störung des Gemüths] can only rarely occur in this state of simplicity. Had the brain of the savage suffered some shock, I do not know whence the fantastical delusion [Phantasterei] should come to displace the ordinary sensations which alone occupy him without cease. What frenzy [Wahnsinn] could possibly seize him, since he never has occasion to venture far in his judgments? Mania [Wahnwitz], however, is certainly altogether beyond his capacity. If he is sick in the head, he will be either imbecile [blödsinnig] or raving [toll]; and even this must happen very rarely, for he is for the most part healthy, because he is free and has exercise. In the civil constitution there are to be found, properly speaking, the ferments of all this corruption, which, even if they do not produce it, nevertheless serve",["Kant's terms for the disorders (Thorheit, Narrheit, Aberwitz, Wahnsinn, Wahnwitz, Phantasterei, blödsinnig, toll) form a graded vocabulary with no fixed English equivalents; I render them consistently and give the German in brackets where the choice matters. Narrheit appears as 'distraction of mind' in the first sentence and 'folly' later, since Kant uses it both generically and in contrast with Thorheit.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Empfindungen' = sensations; 'Urtheilskraft' = power of judgment.","Stray ')' after 'sich' in the OCR ('sich) in seinem Urtheile') silently removed; 'Fahigkeit' read as 'Fähigkeit'; 'Hirgerlichen' read as 'bürgerlichen'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('gleichwohl'); it continues in the next passage ('zu unterhalten und zu vergrößeren dienen'). I translated 'gleichwohl' with its clause as 'nevertheless' and carried 'serve' over, which belongs to the continuation; strictly 'serve' is supplied from the next passage and should be treated as a join, not a completion."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0064":["to sustain and to enlarge it. Understanding, so far as it suffices for the necessities and simple pleasures of life, is sound understanding [gesunder Verstand]; so far as it is required for artificial luxury, whether in enjoyment or in the sciences, it is refined understanding [feiner Verstand]. The sound understanding of the citizen would therefore already be a very refined understanding for the natural man. The concepts which in certain stations presuppose a refined understanding are no longer suited to those who are nearer to the simplicity of nature, at least in insight, and, when these persons pass over to such concepts, they commonly make fools of them. The Abbé Terrasson somewhere divides those of disturbed mind into those who reason correctly from false representations, and those who reason in a perverted manner from correct representations. This division agrees well with the propositions here advanced. In those of the first kind, the visionaries [Phantasten], or the distracted [Verrückten], it is not properly the understanding that suffers, but only the faculty which awakens in the soul the concepts which the power of judgment afterwards employs in comparing them. Such patients can very well be confronted with rational judgments, which, though they cannot remove their malady, can at least mitigate it. But in those of the second kind, the frenzied [Wahnsinnige] and the maniacal [Wahnwitzige], the understanding itself is attacked; and so it is not merely foolish to reason with them (since they would not be frenzied if they could grasp these rational grounds), but also most harmful. For one thereby only gives their perverted heads fresh material for hatching absurdities; contradiction does not mend them but inflames them, and in dealing with them it is absolutely necessary to adopt a cool and kindly manner, just as if one did not notice at all that anything was lacking in their understanding.",["Begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage ('...gleichwohl zu unterhalten und zu vergrößeren dienen'); 'it' in the first line is left to refer to the corruption mentioned there.","OCR 'set' read as 'sei', 'seine' (for 'feine') read as 'feine', 'Uppigkeit' as 'Üppigkeit', 'vergrößeren' left as in source sense; 'Verstand' = understanding throughout.","'Vermögen' rendered 'faculty' (the capacity of awakening concepts), distinguished from Verstand; 'Seele' = soul, as in the text, though Kant otherwise uses Gemüth (mind).","The long sentence on Phantasten and Wahnsinnige is kept as one with a parenthesis; the sentence on the 'concepts' that presuppose refined understanding is lightly smoothed, with 'these' supplied for the ambiguous 'diesen' (the concepts of the refined stations)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0065":["I have entitled the defects of the cognitive faculty [Erkenntnißkraft] diseases of the head, just as one calls the corruption of the will a disease of the heart. I have also paid attention only to their appearances [Erscheinungen] in the mind, without wishing to spy out their root, which properly lies in the body, and may well have its chief seat more in the digestive organs than in the brain, as the popular weekly journal known universally under the name of *The Physician* shows with probability in its numbers 150, 151 and 152. I cannot in any way be persuaded, either, that derangement of mind, as is commonly believed, should arise from pride, from love, from excessively intense reflection, and who knows what misuse of the powers of the soul. This judgment, which turns the patient's misfortune into a ground for scornful reproach, is very uncharitable, and is occasioned by a common error in which cause and effect are habitually confused. If one pays only a little attention to examples, one becomes aware that it is the body which first suffers; that at the beginning, while the germ of the disease is developing unnoticed, an ambiguous perversity is felt which gives as yet no suspicion of derangement of mind, and which shows itself in odd amorous fancies, or in a puffed-up manner, or in vain and profound brooding",["'Erkenntnißkraft' rendered 'cognitive faculty' (Kemp Smith would write 'faculty of knowledge'); 'Erscheinungen' rendered 'appearances' per the fixed terms, here in the loose sense of symptoms.","'Wochenschrift ... unter dem Namen des Arztes' is Der Arzt, a Hamburg weekly; I give it as *The Physician*.","OCR 'Krantheit' read 'Krankheit', 'Stòrung' as 'Störung'. The clause 'aus Hochmuth, Liebe, aus gar zu starkem Nachsinnen' is syntactically irregular; I added 'from' before each item without brackets for smoothness.","Breaks off mid-sentence ('äußert' follows in the next passage)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0066":["itself. In time the disease breaks out, and gives occasion for locating its",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage ('Grund in dem nächstvorhergehenden Zustande des Gemüths zu setzen'). 'äußert' of the preceding passage is joined here as 'itself' (shows itself)."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0067":["ground in the immediately preceding state of the mind.",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0068":["But one ought rather to say that the man became proud because he was already disturbed in some degree, than that he was disturbed because he had been so proud. These sad ills, provided only that they",["OCR 'als, er sei gestört worden, s weil' read as 'als er sei gestört worden, weil'; stray 's' dropped. 'ste' read 'sie'.","Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0069":["are not hereditary, still leave room to hope for a happy recovery, and the one—",["The dash at the end marks a line-break hyphenation ('der— / jenige'); it is retained and completed in the next passage."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0070":["whose assistance is chiefly to be sought in this matter is the physician.",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0071":["Yet, for honour's sake, I should not gladly exclude the philosopher,",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0072":["who could prescribe the regimen [Diät] of the mind; only on the condition",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0073":["that he demand no payment for this, as for most of his other occupations. In gratitude the physician would for his part not refuse his assistance to the philo-",["'Erkenntlichkeit' rendered 'gratitude'. The word 'philo-' is a line-break hyphenation completed in the next passage ('sophen')."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0074":["sopher, if the latter should at times attempt the great but always",["Continues 'philo-' of the preceding passage; 'philosopher' is completed across the line break."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0075":["vain cure of folly [Narrheit]. He would, for example, in the raving madness [Tobsucht]",[]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0076":["of a learned bawler consider whether cathartic remedies, taken in strengthened dose, might not have some effect against it. For since, according to Swift's observations, a bad poem is merely a purging of the brain, through which many harmful humours",["OCR 'verstarkter' read 'verstärkter'; 'Feuchtig—' is a line-break hyphenation of 'Feuchtigkeiten', here rendered 'humours' (the passage breaks off there).","'Schreier' rendered 'bawler' in the sense of a noisy scribbler/declaimer."]],"krankheiten-des-kopfes-0077":["...[-keiten] drawn off for the relief of the sick poet, why should a wretched, brooding piece of writing not be something of the same kind? In that case, however, it would be advisable to point nature to another way of purging, so that the evil may be carried off thoroughly and in complete quiet, without disturbing the commonwealth by it.\n\n[Review of Silberschlag's work.] ...[a work] which treats of the fire-ball, to which are appended supplements of reports and observations received. Since the learned Herr Pastor finds no satisfaction in the common theory of the atmosphere, he sets forth his own thoughts on the matter, and finds himself obliged to take a flight, unusual among students of nature, up to the heights of metaphysics. He seeks to show, by arguments which seem to be of much significance but not sufficiently developed, that the presence of corporeal substances in space is properly a sphere of activity, having its dynamical circumference and centre. From the diversity of these spheres, and of the forces which act within them according to the difference of the substances, he derives the elasticity, the condensation and the weather [Witterung] of the air and of the ether, sound, tone, light, colours and heat, and likewise the attraction of matters. All this is applied, in the first section of the first part, to the air and its changes. In the second section the sea of air is considered as an atmosphere. Besides various considerable remarks on vapours, mists, clouds and rain, a new division of the regions of the air is put forward. The first is the standing atmosphere. Then follows the watery, which reaches much higher. Then comes the slimy and phosphorescent, which contains oily, resinous and gummy parts, and is the workshop of shooting stars, fire-balls and flying dragons. Last is the spirituous atmosphere, which extends to the limit of the air's sphere, and in which the widely extended fires of the air, such as the northern lights, are generated. Everywhere one meets with new and very probable conjectures, which well deserve to be compared repeatedly with the phenomena that are already known or still remain to be observed. The second part deals, in three sections, with the course, the generation and the utility of this meteor. The three copper plates illustrate the theory, the shape, and the path which this mass of fire took. The praiseworthy attentiveness of the worthy Herr Pastor to nature, so rich in wonders, gives a hint to the studious young men who are preparing for spiritual offices: to learn betimes to read the great book of creation, which lies spread open before their eyes, and some day to be able to make easier for others also the understanding of the mysteries contained in it. It costs 3 florins at the Kanter bookshop here, as also in Elbing and Mitau.",["The passage opens in mid-word ('keiten') and the review's title and opening lines are cut off; I have not guessed the missing words, and the bracketed '[a work]' only supplies the subject of 'handelt' (the head 'Recension von Silberschlags Schrift' is dropped as a running head).","Silent OCR corrections: Bitterung = Witterung, Uebel = Nebel, forperlichen = körperlichen, Athers = Äthers, dDieNordlichter = die Nordlichter, Budhhandlung = Buchhandlung, '3 si.' = 3 fl. (florins).","'gemeine Wesen' rendered 'commonwealth'; 'Spannkraft' 'elasticity'; 'Luftfeuer' 'fires of the air'.","The long sentence on the atmospheric regions is divided into several short sentences."]],"deutlichkeit-0001":["Enquiry\nconcerning the\nDistinctness of the Principles\nof Natural Theology and Morals.\nBeing\nan Answer to the Question\nwhich the\nRoyal Academy of Sciences at Berlin\nset for the Year 1763.\n*Verum animo satis haec vestigia parva sagaci*\n*Sunt, per quae possis cognoscere caetera tute.*",["OCR 'Anlersuchung' = Untersuchung; 'nalürlichen' = natürlichen.","The motto is Lucretius (*De rerum natura* I): 'These small traces are enough for a sagacious mind, by which you may safely come to know the rest.' Original has 'lute'; read 'tute'."]],"deutlichkeit-0002":["Introduction.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0003":["The question proposed is of such a kind that, if it is properly resolved, higher philosophy must thereby acquire a determinate form. Suppose the method is firmly established by which the highest possible certainty in this kind of knowledge can be attained, and suppose the nature of this conviction is well understood. Then, in place of the perpetual instability of opinions and of schools and sects, an unalterable prescription of method must unite thinking minds in one common endeavour. In the same way Newton's method in natural science changed the licence of physical hypotheses into a sure procedure in accordance with experience and geometry.\n\nBut what method is this treatise itself to have, in which metaphysics is to be shown its true degree of certainty, together with the way by which it is reached? If this exposition is itself metaphysics, its judgment is just as uncertain as the science has hitherto been which hopes by it to acquire some stability and firmness, and all is lost. I shall therefore let secure propositions of experience, and immediate inferences drawn from them, make up the whole content of my treatise. I shall rely neither on the doctrines of the philosophers, whose uncertainty is just the occasion of the present problem, nor on definitions, which so often deceive. The method I employ will be simple and cautious. Some things which may still be found uncertain will be of such a kind that they are used only for illustration, not for proof.",["First sentence of the second paragraph and the long opening sentence restated as shorter sentences; 'Wenn ... so muß' rendered as 'Suppose ... Then'.","'Lehrart' rendered 'method' (in the first paragraph, 'method of teaching' in the prescription) and 'Vortrag' 'exposition'; 'Erkenntniß' 'knowledge'; 'Erfahrungssätze' 'propositions of experience'."]],"deutlichkeit-0004":["First Consideration. | General Comparison of the Way of Arriving at Certainty in mathe | matical Knowledge with that in Philosophical.",["The Academy's | marks are kept where they stand, including the one that falls inside the word 'mathe|matical'."]],"deutlichkeit-0005":["Mathematics arrives at all its definitions",["The stray line number '5)' is omitted. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0006":["synthetically, philosophy however analytically.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0007":["One can arrive at any general concept in two ways: either by the arbitrary combination of concepts, or by abstraction from that knowledge which has been made distinct through analysis. Mathematics never frames definitions in any other way than the first. One thinks to oneself, for example, four straight lines enclosing a plane in such a way that the opposite sides are not parallel, and calls this figure a trapezium. The concept which I explain is not given before the definition; it first arises through the definition itself. A cone may mean what it will otherwise; in mathematics it arises from the arbitrary representation of a right-angled triangle which revolves about one of its sides. Here, and in all other cases, the explanation evidently arises through synthesis.",["OCR corrections: 'sich)' = sich, 'Traz pezium' = Trapezium, 'ente gegenstehende' = entgegenstehende. Line numbers omitted; the | marks are not present in this passage except none retained.","'Absonderung' rendered 'abstraction'; 'Zergliederung' 'analysis'; 'Synthesin' 'synthesis'; 'Vorstellung' 'representation'."]],"deutlichkeit-0008":["With the definitions of philosophy [Weltweisheit] the case is entirely different. Here the concept of a thing is already given, but confusedly or not sufficiently determined. I must analyse it, compare the abstracted marks with the given concept in all sorts of cases, and make this abstract thought full and determinate. Everyone, for example, has a concept of time; this is to be ex- | plained. I must consider this idea in all sorts of relations, in order to discover marks of it by analysis, [and to link] various abstract",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('abstra—'); it continues in the next passage. I have translated it as it stands.","'Weltweisheit' = philosophy; 'Merkmale' = marks; 'verworren' = confusedly. The | before 'klärt' is kept after 'ex-'. OCR 'Fallen' = Fällen.","The bracketed '[and to link]' supplies the coordination left implicit before 'verknüpfen' (which stands in the next passage)."]],"deutlichkeit-0009":["marks, [in order to see] whether they give an adequate concept, and | to hold them together, [in order to see] whether one does not in part include the other. Were I here to arrive synthetically at a definition of time",["The first words complete 'abstra-hirte' from the previous passage; the clause 'zu kommen' belongs with 'suchen' at the start of the next passage, and the passage is left unfinished.","Stray OCR letters ('f', 'id)' = ich) silently dropped; the | mark kept. The bracketed 'in order to see' supplies the sense of the elliptical 'ob'-clauses."]],"deutlichkeit-0010":["[by] seeking [it], what a fortunate accident must occur, if this concept were precisely the one that fully expressed the idea given to us!",["Begins with 'suchen', which completes the sentence of the preceding passage ('Wollte ich ... zu kommen suchen'). The brackets mark the construction supplied for English.","'Zufall' rendered 'accident'; 'Idee' 'idea'."]],"deutlichkeit-0011":["Meanwhile, it will be said, philosophers too sometimes define",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0012":["synthetically, and mathematicians analytically; for example when the philosopher arbitrarily thinks to himself a substance together with the faculty of reason | and calls it a spirit. But I answer: such de-",["Breaks off at the hyphenated word 'Be-' (Bestimmungen), completed in the next passage; I have rendered 'de-|terminations' across the two. 'Vermögen' = faculty; 'Geist' = spirit.","Stray OCR 'i' dropped; | kept."]],"deutlichkeit-0013":["terminations of a word's meaning are never philosophical definitions,",["Completes 'Be-stimmungen' = determinations (Bestimmungen); the English is divided 'de-/terminations' accordingly."]],"deutlichkeit-0014":["but if they are to be called explanations at all, they are only grammatical ones. For no philosophy is needed in order to say what name I wish to have attached to an arbitrary concept. Leib-",["'heißen' rendered 'be called' (Kemp Smith's 'be entitled' would be heavy here). Line number omitted. The proper name 'Leibniz' is completed in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0015":["niz thought to himself a simple substance which had nothing but obscure representations",["Completes 'Leibniz'. 'Vorstellungen' = representations. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0016":["and called it a slumbering monad. Here he had not explained this monad but devised it; for the concept of it was to him",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. 'Monas' rendered 'monad'; 'erdacht' 'devised'."]],"deutlichkeit-0017":["[…] not given, but created by him. Mathematicians, on the other hand, have at times given analytic definitions, I admit; but this has always been a mistake. Thus Wolff considered similarity in geometry with a philosophical eye, in order to comprehend under its general concept the similarity that occurs in geometry. He might always have left this alone. For if I think of figures in which the angles enclosed by the lines of the perimeter are equal to one another, and the sides enclosing them have the same ratio, this may in every case be regarded as the definition of the similarity of figures; and so with the other similarities of spaces. The geometer [*Geometra*] has no concern at all with the general definition of similarity as such. It is fortunate for mathematics that, when through a misconceived sense of duty the geometer [*Meßkünstler*] occasionally engages in such analytic definitions, nothing in fact is inferred from them, or else his immediate inferences amount at bottom to the mathematical definition. Otherwise this science would be exposed to the same unhappy dispute as philosophy. | The mathematician has to do with concepts which are often still capable of a | philosophical definition, for example the concept of space in general. But he accepts such a concept as given, in its clear and ordinary representation. Sometimes philosophical definitions are supplied to him from other sciences, chiefly in applied mathematics, for example the definition of fluidity. But in that case a definition of this kind does not originate in mathematics; it is only used there. It is the business of philosophy to analyse concepts that are given as confused, and to make them detailed and determinate. It is the business of mathematics, on the other hand, to combine and compare given concepts of magnitudes which are clear and certain, in order to see what can be inferred from them. In its solutions, proofs and inferences, mathematics considers the universal under signs *in concreto*; philosophy considers the universal through signs",["Stray 'b' after 'Mathematiker' is an OCR remnant of a line number; omitted. OCR 'Ahnlichkeit', 'philofophiichen', 'unterwegens' silently corrected.","Passage opens mid-sentence and breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands, with [ … ] only to mark the opening.","Meßkünstler rendered 'geometer' (Kant uses it for the mathematician at work); Geometra kept distinct by the German in brackets.","Begriff 'zergliedern' = 'analyse'; 'Erklärung' rendered 'definition' where the context concerns definitions."]],"deutlichkeit-0018":["*in abstracto*.",["Completes the sentence begun in the preceding passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0019":["Since we are here treating our propositions only as immediate inferences from experience, I appeal for the present one first to arithmetic, both the general arithmetic of indeterminate magnitudes and that of numbers, in which the relation of the magnitude to unity is determined. In both, signs are first set down in place of the things themselves, together with the special notations for their increase or diminution, their relations, and so forth; these signs are then dealt with according to easy and certain rules, by transposition, combination, subtraction and manifold alteration, so that the things signified are in the process wholly left out of thought, until finally, at the conclusion, the meaning of the symbolic inference is deciphered. Secondly, in geometry, in order, for example, to know the properties of all circles, one draws a single circle, and in it, in place of all possible lines intersecting one another within it, draws two. Of these one proves the relations, and in them one considers *in concreto* the general rule of the relations of lines that cross one another in all circles.",["OCR slips corrected: '3. E.' = z. E.; 'innerbald' = innerhalb; 'ftd)' = sich; 'Ver: hältnisse'.","Two long sentences restated in more than one sentence where English required it; semicolons used to keep the sequence of procedure."]],"deutlichkeit-0020":["If the procedure of philosophy is compared with this, it is found to be wholly different. The signs of philosophical consideration are never anything but words, which neither in their composition indicate the partial concepts of which the whole idea that the word designates",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage and translated as it stands."]],"deutlichkeit-0021":["consists, nor in their combinations are able to designate the relations of philosophical thoughts.",["'philo- | sophischen' joined across the break."]],"deutlichkeit-0022":["Hence in all reflection",["Sentence continues in the following passages."]],"deutlichkeit-0023":["in this kind of knowledge [*Erkenntniß*] one must have the thing itself before one's eyes",[]],"deutlichkeit-0024":["and is compelled to represent the universal *in abstracto*, without being able to avail oneself of the important relief of treating single signs",["The German shifts from 'man' to 'ist genöthigt'; I keep the impersonal 'one' with a slight smoothing. Stray 's' (a line number) and the OCR 'sich)' silently corrected."]],"deutlichkeit-0025":["in place of the general concepts of the things themselves. If, for example, the geometer wishes to demonstrate that space is infinitely divisible, he",["Stray ')' before 'Meßkünstler' is OCR noise; omitted."]],"deutlichkeit-0026":["takes, say, a straight line standing perpendicular between two parallels,",[]],"deutlichkeit-0027":["and from a point on one of these parallel lines draws other lines that cut the second. In this symbol [*Symbolo*] he recognises with the greatest certainty",["OCR 'Lo die' read as 'so die'. 'Symbolo' is Kant's Latin ablative, kept with the English in brackets."]],"deutlichkeit-0028":["that the division must proceed without end. On the other hand, if the",[]],"deutlichkeit-0029":["philosopher wishes to demonstrate, say, that every body consists of simple sub-",[]],"deutlichkeit-0030":["stances, he will first assure himself that it is in general a",[]],"deutlichkeit-0031":["whole composed of substances; that in these composition is a contingent state, without which they can nevertheless exist; that consequently all composition in a body can in thought be removed, yet in such a way that the substances of which it consists continue to exist; and, since that which remains of a composite when all composition whatever has been removed is simple, that the body must consist of simple substances. Here neither figures nor",["Kant's single sentence is smoothed into a series of 'that'-clauses; the original has a slightly irregular construction ('und da … einfach ist, daß …'), which I have regularised.","Stray '5' and 'o' (line numbers) removed."]],"deutlichkeit-0032":["visible signs can express the thoughts or their relations, nor can any transposition of signs according to rules be put in the place of abstract",[]],"deutlichkeit-0033":["consideration, so that the representation of the things themselves",["'ab- | stracten Betrachtung' joined across the break with the previous passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0034":["would in this procedure be exchanged for the clearer and easier representation of the signs; on the contrary, the universal must be considered *in abstracto*.",["Stray 's' (line number) before 'sondern' removed."]],"deutlichkeit-0035":["§ 3.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0036":["In mathematics there are only few indecomposable concepts | and indemonstrable propositions; in philosophy, however, they are countless. | The concept of magnitude in general, of unity, of multiplicity, of space, and so forth, are at least in mathematics indecomposable; that is to say, their analysis and definition do not belong at all to this science. I know well that many geometers confuse the boundaries of the sciences and at times wish to philosophise in the doctrine of magnitudes, and for that reason seek to define such concepts further, although in such a case the definition has no mathematical consequence whatever. But it is certain that a concept is indecomposable in respect of a discipline if, whether or not it could otherwise be defined, the science at least has no need of its definition. And I said that there are only few such in mathematics. I go further, however, and assert that strictly none at all can occur in it, in the sense that their definition by analysis of concepts would belong to mathematical knowledge [*Erkenntniß*], even supposing it were otherwise possible. For mathematics never defines a given concept by analysis, but defines an object by arbitrary combination, the thought of which thereby first becomes possible.",["Kant's 'unauflöslich' rendered 'indecomposable' (i.e. not admitting of resolution into partial concepts); 'unerweislich' = 'indemonstrable'.","'in dem Verstande: daß' rendered 'in the sense that' (Verstande here = sense, not the faculty).","Stray 'o' (line number) before 'Raums' and OCR 'u: f. w.' silently corrected; the signs | retained as in the Academy text."]],"deutlichkeit-0037":["If we compare philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] with this, what a difference strikes the eye! In all its disciplines, and above all in metaphysics, every analysis that can be carried out is also necessary, since both the distinctness of knowledge and the possibility of secure inferences depend upon it. But we can see at once, in advance, that in analysis we shall inevitably come upon indecomposable concepts, which will be so either in and for themselves or for us. We can also see that there will be exceedingly many of them, since it is impossible that general modes of knowledge of such great manifoldness should be composed of only a few fundamental concepts. Hence many concepts can hardly be resolved at all, e.g. the concept of a representation, or of being side by side or one after another. Others can be resolved only in part, such as the concepts of space and of time, and of the many kinds of feeling in the human soul: the feeling of the sublime, of the beautiful, of the disgusting, and so on. Without exact knowledge and resolution of these the springs of our nature are not sufficiently known, and yet a careful observer perceives that analysis is here by no means adequate. I confess that the explanations of pleasure and displeasure, of desire and aversion, and of countless similar concepts have never been furnished by adequate resolutions, and I am not surprised at this indecomposability. For concepts of such diverse kinds must surely have different elementary concepts at their foundation. The error which some have committed, in treating all such modes of knowledge as capable of being resolved, one and all, into a few simple concepts, resembles that into which the ancient students of nature fell. They held that all the matter of nature consists of the so-called four elements, a view which better observation has overthrown.",["Restated the long second sentence as three; 'unvermeidlich sei' is a silent correction of the OCR 'ume vermeidlich set', and 'kommen' of 'fommen'.","*Erkenntnisse* (plural) = 'modes of knowledge'; *Deutlichkeit der Erkenntniß* = 'distinctness of knowledge'; *Weltweisheit* = 'philosophy', with the German added at first occurrence.","*Seele* is rendered 'soul' (not 'mind', which is reserved for *Gemüt*); *Triebfedern* = 'springs'."]],"deutlichkeit-0038":["Further, in mathematics only a few indemonstrable propositions lie",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the following passages."]],"deutlichkeit-0039":["at the foundation, which, even if elsewhere they would still be capable of proof,",[]],"deutlichkeit-0040":["are nevertheless regarded in this science as immediately certain.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0041":["For example: The whole is equal to all its parts taken together; between two points only one straight line | can exist, and so on. Mathematicians are accustomed to set out such principles at the beginning of their disciplines, so that it may be seen",["The stray OCR 's' (a line number) before 'gleich' is dropped; the sign | is kept as in the source.","'For example' is supplied for the colon in the German, which has no word here; this is a smoothing."]],"deutlichkeit-0042":["that no propositions other than such evident ones are",["The sentence begins in the previous passage ('so that it may be seen') and continues in the next."]],"deutlichkeit-0043":["directly presupposed as true,",[]],"deutlichkeit-0044":["while everything else is strictly proved. If we compare with this philosophy, and metaphysics in particular, I should like to see a table drawn up of the indemonstrable propositions",["Line number '10' dropped; 'MetaPhysik' normalised to 'metaphysics'. I have followed the German 'möchte ich nur gerne … sehen', with the table object continuing in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0045":["which lie at the foundation of these sciences throughout their whole extent.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0046":["Such a table would certainly constitute a plan that would be",["The sentence breaks off at the passage boundary and continues in the next."]],"deutlichkeit-0047":["immeasurable. Yet the search for these indemonstrable fundamental truths is the most important business of higher philosophy, and these",["The line number '5' is dropped and 'Grundwahrheiten' is restored from the OCR 'Grundwahr— 5 heilen'."]],"deutlichkeit-0048":["discoveries will never come to an end, so long as this kind of knowledge continues to extend itself. For whatever the object may be, the marks which the understanding first and immediately perceives in it are the data for just so many indemonstrable propositions; and these in turn constitute the foundation from which the definitions can be discovered. Before I even set about explaining what space",["The OCR 'diejenige Merkmale' kept as Kant's usage; the stray 'o' (line number) before 'denn' dropped.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Data' kept as in the text."]],"deutlichkeit-0049":["is, I see clearly that, since this concept is given to me, I must first of all seek out by analysis those marks which are first and immediately thought in it. I note accordingly that much in it is outside one another; that this manifold is not made up of substances,",["The sentence continues in the next passage. 'vörderst' read as 'vorderst'; the stray 'bs' (line number) is dropped."]],"deutlichkeit-0050":["for I wish to know not the things in space but space itself; that space can have only three dimensions; and so on. Such",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0051":["propositions can indeed be elucidated by considering them *in concreto*, so as to know them intuitively; but they can never be proved. For from what could such proof be drawn, since they constitute the first",["'anschauend' rendered 'intuitively', in keeping with *Anschauung* = intuition. Line number 'o' and stray 'bez' dropped from 'bez o weisen' (read 'beweisen')."]],"deutlichkeit-0052":["and simplest thoughts that I can have of my object at all,",["Kant's 'ihn' for the object (as masculine) is rendered by 'my object' in the English."]],"deutlichkeit-0053":["when I begin to think it? In mathematics the",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0054":["definitions are the first thought that I can have of the thing defined, just because my concept of the object arises only through the definition; and in that case it is absolutely absurd to regard them as demonstrable. In philosophy, where the concept of the thing I am to explain is given to me, that which is immediately and first perceived in it must serve as an indemonstrable fundamental judgment. For since I do not yet have the whole distinct concept of the thing, but am only now seeking it, it cannot possibly be proved from this concept; on the contrary, it serves to generate this distinct knowledge and definition. Accordingly I shall have to have first fundamental judgments prior to any philosophical explanation of things, and the only error that can occur here is that I take for an original mark what is still a derived one. In the following consideration matters will come up which will place this beyond doubt.",["'Mfo' is read as 'Also' (OCR); stray line numbers '15' and '5' dropped; 'iung' read as 'Betrachtung'.","Restated the long sentence 'Denn da ich … erzeugen' with a semicolon for clarity; 'Grundurtheile' = fundamental judgments."]],"deutlichkeit-0055":["The object of mathematics is easy and simple, that of",["Heading (the sentence runs on into the next passage)."]],"deutlichkeit-0056":["philosophy difficult and complicated.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0057":["Since magnitude constitutes the object of mathematics, and in its consideration we look only to how many times something is posited, it is plainly evident that this knowledge must rest on a few and very clear fundamental doctrines of the general theory of magnitude (which is properly general arithmetic). Here too we see the increase and decrease of magnitudes, and their resolution into equal factors in the doctrine of roots, arise from simple concepts and few fundamental ones. A few fundamental concepts of space mediate the application of this general knowledge of magnitude to geometry. To be convinced of this we need only compare the easy comprehensibility of an arithmetical object, which includes within itself an enormous multiplicity, with the far greater difficulty of comprehending a philosophical idea, in which we seek to know only little. The relation of a trillion to unity is understood quite distinctly, whereas philosophers have not yet been able to make the concept of freedom intelligible from its units, that is, from its simple and familiar concepts. That is to say: the qualities which constitute the proper object of philosophy are infinitely various, and to distinguish them demands exceedingly much; likewise it is far harder to resolve complicated modes of knowledge by analysis than to combine given simple modes of knowledge by synthesis and so arrive at consequences [*Folgerungen*].",["Line numbers '20' and '2' dropped (and 'sich)' read 'sich'); the German of the last sentence ('auf Folgerungen zu kommen') continues across the passage boundary, and I have completed it here from the next fragment's 'rungen'; the join occurs at the start of 0058.","Divided nothing here but kept Kant's sentences close; 'Synthesin' rendered 'synthesis'; 'Mathematik' 'Weltweisen' = philosophers."]],"deutlichkeit-0058":["I know that there are many who find philosophy very easy in comparison with higher mathematics. But these",["The opening fragment 'xj rungen' is a residue of the preceding sentence ('Folgerungen') and the line number; it has been absorbed into 0057 above. 'Mathesis' kept as 'mathematics'. Line number 'as' dropped."]],"deutlichkeit-0059":["call everything philosophy that stands in books which bear this title.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0060":["The difference shows itself in the outcome. Philosophical",[]],"deutlichkeit-0061":["modes of knowledge for the most part share the fate of opinions, and are | like meteors, whose brilliance promises nothing as to their duration. They vanish, but mathematics remains. Metaphysics is without doubt the most difficult of all human insights; but none has ever yet been written. The Academy's prize question shows that there is reason to inquire after the path by which one proposes to seek it for the first time.",["'Nr— sache' read as 'Ursache'. The sign | is kept as in the source.","'Aufgabe der Akademie' = the Berlin Academy's prize question; 'Einsichten' = insights."]],"deutlichkeit-0062":["Second Consideration.",["'Betrachtung' = consideration."]],"deutlichkeit-0063":["The Only Method of Attaining the Highest Possible Certainty in",["Heading continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0064":["Metaphysics.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0065":["Metaphysics is nothing other than a philosophy concerning the",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0066":["first grounds of our knowledge. What was shown in the preceding consideration regarding mathematical knowledge in comparison with philosophy will therefore hold also in relation to metaphysics. We have seen notable and essential differences which are to be met with between the knowledge in the two sciences, and",["'Erkenntnisses' (genitive) = 'knowledge'; I read 'Betrachtung' for the OCR 'Be— trachtung'. The passage breaks off after 'und'; not completed."]],"deutlichkeit-0067":["In view of this one may say with Bishop Warburton that nothing has been more harmful to philosophy than mathematics, namely",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands, without completion."]],"deutlichkeit-0068":["the imitation of it in the method of thinking, where it cannot possibly be employed. As for its application in the",["Kant's \"denn was … anlangt\" is rendered as a separate sentence, with the semicolon replaced by a full stop. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0069":["branches of philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] in which the knowledge of magnitudes comes in,",["Fragment of a sentence; it is completed in the next passage. *Weltweisheit* is rendered \"philosophy\"."]],"deutlichkeit-0070":["that is something quite different, and its utility is immeasurable.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0071":["In mathematics I begin with the explanation of my object, for example a triangle, a circle, and so forth. In metaphysics I must never begin in that way. So far is the definition from being here the first thing I know of the thing that it is, on the contrary, almost always the last. In mathematics, that is to say, I have no concept of my object at all until the definition gives it. In metaphysics I have a concept which has already been given to me, though in a confused form, and what I have to seek out is the distinct, detailed and determinate concept of it. How, then, can I begin with the definition? Augustine said: I know well enough what time is, but if anyone asks me, I do not know. Here many acts of unravelling obscure ideas, of comparison, subordination and limitation must take place. And I venture to say that, although much that is true and acute has been said about time, a real explanation [*Realerklärung*] of it has never yet been given. As for the nominal explanation [*Namenerklärung*], it helps us little or nothing; for even without it we understand the word well enough not to confuse it [with another]. If we had as many correct definitions as appear in books under this name, with what certainty could we not draw inferences and derive consequences from them! But experience teaches the opposite.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"3. E.\" = z. E.; \"u. |. w.\" = u. s. w. (rendered \"and so forth\"); \"Metaphystik\" = Metaphysik; \"jeder— zeit\" = jederzeit.","\"ehe gar keinen Begriff\" is taken as \"eher\"/\"ehe\" with the sense \"no concept at all … until\" (the text as printed is doubtful).","*Erklärung* is rendered \"explanation\" throughout, *Definition* \"definition\"; *Realerklärung* and *Namenerklärung* are rendered \"real\" and \"nominal explanation\".","Restated: the clause \"es ist so weit gefehlt, daß …\" is recast as \"So far is the definition from being …\". The bracketed \"[with another]\" is supplied to complete \"nicht zu verwechseln\". The Augustine saying is kept as in the German."]],"deutlichkeit-0072":["In philosophy, and particularly in metaphysics, one can often know a very great deal about an object distinctly and with certainty, and can also derive secure consequences from it, before one possesses the definition of it, and even when one does not undertake to give one at all. For of any thing various predicates may be immediately certain to me, although I do not yet know enough of them to give the detailed, determinate concept of the thing, that is, the definition. Even if I never explained what a desire is, I could still say with certainty that every desire presupposes a representation of the thing desired; that this representation is a foreseeing of what is to come; that the feeling of pleasure is bound up with it, | and so forth. All this everyone constantly perceives in the immediate consciousness of desire. By comparing such observations one might perhaps in the end arrive at the definition of desire. But so long as, even without it, what one is seeking can be inferred from certain marks of the same thing that are immediately certain, it is unnecessary to venture upon an undertaking so slippery. In mathematics, as is well known, it is quite otherwise.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"erflärte\" = erklärte; \"set\"/\"seit\" = sei; \"Luft\" = Lust (pleasure); \"Prädi⸗cate\" = Prädicate. Stray line numbers (20, 25) are omitted.","The sign | after \"verbunden sei\" is kept where it stands, as in the source; it is probably the Academy Edition's page-break mark.","*Vorstellung* = representation; *Vorhersehung* = \"foreseeing\"; *Merkmale* = \"marks\"."]],"deutlichkeit-0073":["In mathematics the meaning of the signs is secure, because one can easily become conscious of what meaning one has wished to give them. In philosophy in general, and in metaphysics in particular, words have their meaning through common usage, except in so far as it has been determined more exactly by logical limitation. But since the same words are often used for very similar concepts which nevertheless contain a considerable difference concealed within them, one must, whenever a concept is applied, take great care, even if its name seems exactly suitable according to common usage, to observe whether it is really the same concept that has here been joined with the same sign. We say that a man distinguishes gold from brass when he recognizes that in the one metal, for example, there is not the same density as in the other. We also say that an animal distinguishes one fodder from another when it eats the one and leaves the other lying. Here, in both",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"ftd)\" = sich; stray line numbers omitted; \"s hier\" read as \"sich hier\"/\"man hier\" (the printed \"man s hier\" is doubtful, rendered as \"one must … here\").","The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"in beiden / Fällen\"); translated as it stands. Kant's one long sentence on the sign's meaning is divided into several sentences."]],"deutlichkeit-0074":["cases the word *unterscheiden* [to distinguish] is used, although in the first case it means as much as to recognize the difference, which can never happen",["Continues the previous passage (\"in beiden Fällen\"). The German word is added in brackets because the argument turns on it. Fragment of a sentence."]],"deutlichkeit-0075":["without judging; whereas in the second it only indicates that, given different representations, [the animal] acts differently,",["\"[the animal]\" is supplied: the German passive \"unterschiedlich gehandelt wird\" is turned into an active construction. The sentence is cut across the passage division (\"unter- / schiedlichen\")."]],"deutlichkeit-0076":["and for this it is not at all necessary that a judgment should precede. So in the case of animals we only",["\"Wie wir denn am Viehe nur ge- / wahr werden\" continues in the next passage; the sentence is restated in two parts across the break."]],"deutlichkeit-0077":["observe that they are driven by different sensations to different actions. This is quite possible without their having in the least to judge about agreement or difference.",["*Empfindungen* = sensations. The relative clause \"welches ganz wohl möglich ist\" is recast as a separate sentence."]],"deutlichkeit-0078":["From all this there follow quite naturally the rules of that method by which alone the highest possible metaphysical certainty can be attained. They are very different from those which have hitherto been followed, and when they are put into application they promise a result so fortunate as could never have been expected by any other path.",["The sentence is divided at \"verheißen … Ausgang\", which runs on across the next passages (0079–0081). The \"first and foremost rule\" is given in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0079":["[the method] which has been followed hitherto, and they promise so fortunate an",["Fragment: the passage breaks off mid-sentence and is continued by the next passage (0080), 'Ausgang, wenn …'. The subject of 'verheißen' (plural) and the antecedent of the omitted object of 'befolgt hat' cannot be settled from the fragment; '[the method]' is supplied only to complete the sense and is conjectural. Since 'man bis daher befolgt hat' lacks its object here, a relative clause beginning earlier is presumed.","'dermaßen' rendered 'so'; 'glücklichen' kept as 'fortunate', the sentence being left unfinished as in the German."]],"deutlichkeit-0080":["outcome, when they are brought into application, such as one",["Fragment: continues 'einen dermaßen glücklichen' of the preceding passage and breaks off after 'dergleichen man'; not completed. 'sie' is rendered 'they', presumably the rules or precepts of the method, but the antecedent lies outside the passage.","'man' rendered 'one'; 'Ausgang' as 'outcome' (it may also be 'issue'); 'zur Anwendung bringen' as 'bring into application'. The verb of the relative clause is not given."]],"deutlichkeit-0081":["The first and",["The first words of the sentence continue into the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0082":["foremost rule is this: that one must by no means begin with explanations, unless perhaps merely a verbal explanation [*Worterklärung*] is being sought, for example: that is necessary whose opposite is impossible. But even here there are only a few cases in which one can so confidently fix the distinctly determinate concept at the very outset.",["\"we— nig\" = wenig (OCR hyphen). *Worterklärung* = \"verbal explanation\"."]],"deutlichkeit-0083":["Rather, one should first seek out with care in one's object that of which one is immediately certain, even before one has the definition of it. From this let one draw consequences, and let one seek chiefly to acquire only true and quite certain judgments about the object, without yet reckoning upon a hoped-for explanation. This one must never venture, but must admit only when it presents itself distinctly out of the most evident judgments. The second rule is this: that one should mark out specially the immediate judgments about the object, with regard to what one first finds in it with certainty. Once one is sure that the one is not contained in the other, one should set them in front, like the axioms of geometry, as the foundation for all inferences. From this it follows that in the considerations of metaphysics one should always mark out specially what one knows for certain, even if it were little. One may also make trials of uncertain modes of knowledge [*Erkenntnisse*], to see whether they may not lead on to the track of certain knowledge, but in such a way that one does not mix them with the former. I do not give the other rules of conduct which this method has in common with every",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"„Staat zu machen\" (stray quotation mark) = Staat zu machen, rendered \"reckoning upon\"; \"Ariomen\" = Axiomen; \"set\" = sei; \"we— nig\" = wenig; stray \"3\" omitted.","\"wenn es auch wenig wäre\" is placed in the sentence as Kant's concessive clause. The long final sentence of the German is divided into three. \"das eine in dem andern nicht enthalten sei\" is rendered as \"the one is not contained in the other\"; the referents (the judgments) are left as in the German.","*Erkenntnisse* (plural) = \"modes of knowledge\", per the method. The sentence breaks off mid-clause (\"mit jeder\") and is continued in 0084."]],"deutlichkeit-0084":["other rational method, and proceed only to make these rules clear by examples. |",["Stray \"wi\" before \"andern\" is an OCR slip and omitted. The | after \"machen\" is carried over as in the source. This continues \"mit jeder\" of the previous passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0085":["The genuine method of metaphysics is at bottom the same as that which Newton introduced into natural science, and which there had such useful consequences. One should there, it is said, seek out by sure experiences, if need be with the help of geometry, the rules according to which certain appearances [*Erscheinungen*] of nature take place. Even though one does not see the first ground of them in the bodies, it is nevertheless certain that they act in accordance with this law, and one explains the complicated events of nature when one shows distinctly how they are contained under these well-proved rules. So also in metaphysics: seek out, by sure inner experience, that is, by an immediate, evident consciousness, those marks which certainly lie in the concept of some general property. And although you do not know the whole essence of the thing, you can nevertheless safely make use of these marks in order to derive much about the thing from them.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"fol\" = soll; \"herguleiten\" = herzuleiten; stray line numbers and \"?\" omitted. \"Man soll, heißt es daselbst\" rendered as \"One should there, it is said\".","*Erscheinung* = appearance. *Wesen* = essence. \"Beschaffenheit\" rendered \"property\"; the sense is doubtful and could be \"constitution\"."]],"deutlichkeit-0086":["Example",["A heading, translated as it stands."]],"deutlichkeit-0087":["of the sole secure method of metaphysics, in the knowledge",["Fragment: the passage breaks off; it appears to be the end of a heading or title, and is translated as it stands, without completion.","Erkenntniß = knowledge."]],"deutlichkeit-0088":["of the nature of bodies.",["Continues the preceding fragment (the heading of the Third Consideration, 'Of the sole secure method of metaphysics, in the knowledge of the nature of bodies')."]],"deutlichkeit-0089":["For the sake of brevity I refer to a proof which is briefly indicated at the end of the second section of the first Consideration, in order to lay down, as a foundation to begin with, the proposition that every body must consist of simple substances. Without determining what a body is, I know with certainty that it consists of parts which would exist even if they were not combined. And if the concept of a substance is an abstracted concept, it is beyond doubt abstracted from the corporeal things of the world. But it is not even necessary to call them substances. It is enough that from this it can be inferred with the greatest certainty that a body consists of simple parts. The evident analysis of this is easy, but here too lengthy [4]",["The German sentence 'wenn der so Begriff einer Substanz' is an OCR slip; read 'wenn der Begriff einer Substanz'.","Restated as several sentences, as the method permits.","The trailing '4' is probably a stray footnote or page mark in the OCR; carried over in brackets as a doubtful reading. The sentence continues in the next passage ('ist').","'§phs' read as 'Paragraphs', rendered 'section'."]],"deutlichkeit-0090":["[to be given]. Now by means of the infallible proofs of geometry I can show",["Continues the previous sentence ('zu weitläuftig ... ist'): 'is too lengthy'. The bracketed '[to be given]' is not in the German; I have kept the translation faithful instead: read 'is' as completing 'too lengthy'. See below."]],"deutlichkeit-0091":["that space does not consist of simple parts, the arguments for which",[]],"deutlichkeit-0092":["are sufficiently well known. Accordingly there is a determinate multitude of the parts",[]],"deutlichkeit-0093":["of every body, all of which are simple, and an equal multitude of parts of the space which it occupies, all of which are composite. From this",[]],"deutlichkeit-0094":["it follows that every simple part (element) in the body occupies a space. If I now ask what it means to occupy a space, I become aware, without troubling myself about the essence of space, that if a space can be penetrated by any thing whatever, without there being anything",["'Raum einnehmen' = 'occupy a space'."]],"deutlichkeit-0095":["there that resists, one might at most, if one pleased, say that something was in this space, but never that this space is occupied by",[]],"deutlichkeit-0096":["anything. From which I recognise that a space is occupied by something",["'wovon eingenom—men': the hyphenated word is joined across the passage break."]],"deutlichkeit-0097":["when there is something there which resists a moving body in its endeavour to penetrate into it. But this resistance is impenetrability. Accordingly bodies occupy space through impenetrability. But impenetrability (*impenetrabilitas*) is a force. For",["Kant's 'Impenetrabilität' is kept as *impenetrabilitas* beside 'Undurchdringlichkeit' = impenetrability; the English is the same word."]],"deutlichkeit-0098":["it manifests a resistance, that is, an action opposed to an external force.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0099":["And the force which belongs to a body must belong to its simple",["'ein⸗sachen' is hyphenation, read 'einfachen'."]],"deutlichkeit-0100":["parts. Accordingly the elements of every",[]],"deutlichkeit-0101":["body fill their space by the force of impenetrability. But I ask",[]],"deutlichkeit-0102":["further whether the first elements are not extended just because each of them in the body fills a space? Here I can for once",[]],"deutlichkeit-0103":["offer an explanation that is immediately certain, namely: that is",[]],"deutlichkeit-0104":["extended which, posited by itself (*absolute*), fills a space, just as every single body would fill a space, even if I represent to myself that besides it",["'für sich (absolute) gesetzt': *absolute* kept in Latin."]],"deutlichkeit-0105":["there were nothing else. But if I consider an absolutely simple element, then, if it is posited alone (without connection with others), it is impossible that a manifold should be found in it outside one another, and that it should *absolute* occupy a space.",["Read 'sich außerhalb einander befände' as 'be found outside one another' (continues in the next passage)."]],"deutlichkeit-0106":["Hence it cannot be extended. But since a force of impenetrability directed against many external things is the cause of the element's occupying a space, I see that from this there may indeed flow a multiplicity in its external action, but no multiplicity with regard to inner parts, and that it is therefore not extended because it occupies a space in the body (*in nexu cum aliis*).",["Passage 0105 and 0106 together form the sequence; the 'sich außerhalb einander' clause is split across them. 'Vielheit' = multiplicity.","The 'wohl ... aber' construction is smoothed slightly: 'may indeed flow ... but'."]],"deutlichkeit-0107":["I wish to spend a few more words in making evident how shallow the proofs of the metaphysicians are, when, according to custom, they confidently draw conclusions from a definition once laid down as a foundation, conclusions which are lost as soon as the definition deceives. It is well known that most Newtonians go further than Newton and assert that bodies attract one another immediately even at a distance (or, as they call it, through empty space). I leave the correctness of this proposition, which certainly has much to be said for it, undecided. But I maintain that metaphysics has at least not refuted it. First, bodies are distant from one another if they do not touch one another. This is quite exactly the meaning of the word. If I now ask what I understand by touching, I become aware that, without troubling myself about the definition, I nevertheless always judge from the resistance of impenetrability of another body that I touch it. For I find that this concept originally springs from feeling, just as even by the judgment of the eyes I merely conjecture that one matter will touch another, but know it for certain only upon the observed resistance of impenetrability. In this way, when I say that a body acts immediately upon a distant one, this means as much as: it acts upon it immediately, but not by means of impenetrability. But there is here no seeing why this should be impossible, unless someone were to show that impenetrability is either the sole force of a body, or at least that it cannot act immediately by any other without doing so at the same time by means of impenetrability. But since this has never been proved, and to all appearance will hardly be proved, metaphysics has at least no sound ground for rebelling against immediate attraction at a distance. Meanwhile let the proofs of the metaphysicians appear. First comes the definition: The immediate mutual presence of two bodies is contact. From this it follows that if two bodies act immediately upon one another, they touch one another. Things which touch one another are not distant. Consequently two bodies never act immediately upon one another at a distance, and so on. The definition is surreptitious. Not every immediate presence is a contact, but only that by means of impenetrability, and all the rest is built into the wind.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'ente fernten' = 'entfernten', 'ftd)' = 'sich', 'Korpers' = 'Körpers', 'eine ander' = 'einander'.","'erschlichen' rendered 'surreptitious'; 'in den Wind gebauet' rendered literally.","Several short Kantian sentences kept as they stand; no restatement needed."]],"deutlichkeit-0108":["I continue with my treatise. It is clear from the example given that one can say much with certainty about an object, in metaphysics as in other sciences, without having defined it. For here neither what a body is nor what space is has been defined, and yet of both we have reliable propositions. The chief point to which I attach importance is this: that in metaphysics one must proceed throughout analytically, for its business is in fact to resolve confused modes of knowledge [*verworrene Erkenntnisse*]. If the procedure of the philosophers, as it is in vogue in all the schools, is compared with this, how perverse it will be found! The most abstract concepts, at which the understanding naturally arrives last, make the beginning with them, because the plan of the mathematician is once in their heads and they want at all costs to imitate it. Hence there is found a singular difference between metaphysics and every other science. In geometry and other branches of the doctrine of magnitude one begins with the easier and rises slowly to the more difficult tasks. In metaphysics the beginning is made with the most difficult: with possibility and existence in general, necessity and contingency, and so on, all of them concepts which demand great abstraction and attention, especially since their signs in application undergo many imperceptible variations, the differences of which must not be overlooked. It is held that one must proceed throughout synthetically. Hence they define at the very start and infer from the definition with confidence. Philosophers of this taste congratulate one another on having learned from the geometer to think thoroughly, and fail to notice that he acquires concepts by combining, whereas they can do so only by analysing, which wholly alters the method of thinking.",["OCR slips corrected: 'im ! Kopfe' = 'im Kopfe', 'findet sich)' = 'findet sich', 'fonderbarer' = 'sonderbarer', 'Bufälligkeit' = 'Zufälligkeit', 'inniß' read 'mit' ... doubtful: 'das inniß gründlich zu denken' is probably 'das gründlich zu denken'; 'erwer— Seu' = 'erwerben'.","'Es soll durchaus synthetisch verfahren werden' rendered 'It is held that one must proceed ...' to make the philosophers' view explicit.","'Erkenntnisse' = modes of knowledge; I give 'confused modes of knowledge' in the sense of the plural, with the German in brackets.","'Meßkünstler' = geometer."]],"deutlichkeit-0109":["But as soon as philosophers take the natural path of sound reason, and seek out first what they know with certainty of the abstracted concept of an object (e.g. space or time), without yet laying any claim to definitions; if they infer only from these secure data; and if, whenever a concept is applied in a different way, they take care to see whether the concept itself, though its sign remains the same, has not changed here: then they will perhaps have fewer insights to offer for sale, but those which they set forth will be of secure value. Of this last point I shall give a further example. Most philosophers cite, as an example of obscure concepts, those which we may have in deep",["Restated the long opening conditional as a series of parallel clauses, keeping its single sentence.","'3. E.' is an OCR slip for 'z. E.' (zum Exempel); rendered 'e.g.'.","'Erklärungen' rendered 'definitions' because the sense is the claim to definitions, in contrast with the certain data. 'feil zu bieten' is rendered 'to offer for sale'.","'Von dem letzteren' is taken to refer to the secure value of what is established cautiously.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence (continued in the next id); translated as it stands."]],"deutlichkeit-0110":["the distinctness of the principles, etc.",["Fragment, probably a running head or marginal note displaced by the portal's segmentation; translated as it stands. 'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness."]],"deutlichkeit-0111":["sleep. Obscure representations are those of which one is not conscious. Now certain experiences show that we have representations even in deep sleep, and since we are not conscious of them, they have been obscure. Here consciousness has a twofold | meaning. One is unconscious of a representation either in the sense that one has it, or in the sense that one has had it. The former denotes the obscurity of the representation as it is in the soul; the latter indicates nothing further than that one does not remember it. Now the instance adduced merely gives us to understand that there may be representations which one does not remember when awake; from which it does not at all follow that in sleep they may not have been clear, with consciousness; as in the example of Herr Sauvage's cataleptic person, or in the common actions of sleepwalkers. Meanwhile, because one proceeds far too readily to inference, without first having given the concept its meaning in each case by attending to various instances, a probably great secret of nature is in this case passed over with inattention: namely, that perhaps in the deepest sleep the greatest facility of the soul in rational thinking may be exercised; for one has no other ground to the contrary [...] but which proves nothing.",["'Bewußtsein' rendered 'consciousness'; 'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'Seele' = soul.","The end is corrupt in the OCR ('zum Gegen. aber nichts beweist'); I have not filled the lacuna and mark it [...]. The sense of the surviving words is that the ground cited proves nothing.","Stray parentheses and 'auch) (' in the OCR were disregarded; 'zwie- | fächer' joined as 'twofold |', with the page break kept where it stands.","'Fertigkeit' rendered 'facility'; 'Instanz' rendered 'instance' (in the logical sense of a case adduced)."]],"deutlichkeit-0112":["The time is still far from having come for proceeding synthetically in metaphysics. Only when analysis has helped us to concepts that are distinctly and fully understood will synthesis be able, as in mathematics, to subordinate the composite modes of knowledge to the simplest.",["'untere 2 ordnen' is an OCR slip for 'unterordnen'.","'Erkenntnissen' is plural, hence 'modes of knowledge'; 'deutlich' rendered 'distinctly'."]],"deutlichkeit-0113":["Third Consideration.\nOn the Nature of Metaphysical Certainty.",["'Gewißheit' rendered 'certainty'; 'Betrachtung' rendered 'consideration'."]],"deutlichkeit-0114":["|",["The OCR 'SE' is evidently a damaged section sign or number and is not translatable; only the page-break sign is kept."]],"deutlichkeit-0115":["Philosophical certainty is in general of a different",["Fragment continued in the next id."]],"deutlichkeit-0116":["nature from mathematical certainty.",["Completes the sentence begun in the previous id."]],"deutlichkeit-0117":["We are certain in so far as we recognise that it is impossible for a mode of knowledge to be false. The degree of this certainty, if taken objectively, depends upon the sufficiency of the marks of the necessity of a truth; if, however, it is considered subjectively, it is the greater in proportion as the knowledge of this necessity contains more of intuition. In both respects mathematical certainty is of a different kind from philosophical. I shall demonstrate this in the most evident manner.",["'Erkenntniß' rendered 'a mode of knowledge' where it is a countable item that may be false; 'Anschauung' = intuition, here with the sense of intuitive character or evidence.","'Merkmale' rendered 'marks'. The pipe after 'objective' is the Academy page break, kept.","'Set' is an OCR slip for 'sei'."]],"deutlichkeit-0118":["The human understanding, like every other power of nature, is bound by certain rules. We err not because the understanding connects concepts without rule, but because the mark",["'Verstand' = understanding; 'Kraft' rendered 'power'.","'m Begriffe' is damaged in the OCR; read as 'Begriffe' (concepts) as the object of 'verknüpft'.","The sentence breaks off here and continues in the next id."]],"deutlichkeit-0119":["which one does not perceive in a thing is also denied of it, and one judges that that of which one is not conscious in a thing is not there. Now, in the first place, mathematics arrives at its concepts synthetically, and can say with assurance: what it has not chosen to represent to itself in its object through the definition is also not contained in it. For the concept of what is defined arises only through the definition, and has no further meaning than that which the definition gives it. If we compare with this philosophy, and metaphysics in particular, it is far less secure in its definitions, if it ventures to give any.",["'as man' in the OCR is 'was man'; the sentence is a continuation of the previous id ('the mark which one does not perceive...').","'Erklärung' and 'Definition' both appear; rendered 'definition' throughout for sense, though the first 'Erklärungen' in the last sentence could also be 'explanations'.","'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy'."]],"deutlichkeit-0120":["For the concept of what is to be defined is given. If, now, one overlooks this or that mark, which nevertheless belongs to its sufficient [text breaks off here in the OCR]. We could set such errors before the eyes by countless examples; I refer, however, only to what was adduced above about contact. Secondly, mathematics in its inferences and proofs considers its universal knowledge under signs *in concreto*, whereas philosophy considers it, besides the signs, always still *in abstracto*. This constitutes a notable difference in the way in which the two arrive at certainty. For since the signs of mathematics are sensible means of knowledge, one can know with the same confidence with which one is assured of what one sees with one's eyes that no concept has been left out of account, that each single comparison has been carried out according to easy",["'enn' is an OCR slip for 'Denn'; 'Beührung' for 'Berührung'; 'Erfenntnißmittel' for 'Erkenntnißmittel'; the pipes mark page breaks and are kept.","After 'zu seiner hinreichenden' the text jumps ('Wir könnten...'): the OCR has dropped or displaced the completion of the sentence. I have not supplied it and mark the break in square brackets.","'ein der das andre' read as 'ein oder das andre'. 'Zeichen' = signs; *in concreto* and *in abstracto* kept in Latin.","The passage ends mid-sentence ('nach leichten' continues with 'Regeln' in the next id)."]],"deutlichkeit-0121":["rules, and so on. In this the attention is greatly facilitated by the fact that it has to consider not the things in their general representation but the signs in their individual knowledge, which is sensible. Words, on the other hand, as the signs of philosophical knowledge, serve for nothing but the recollection of the designated general",["Initial 'egeln' is 'Regeln', completing 'nach leichten' of the previous id; 'ereichtert' = 'erleichtert'; 'ondern' = 'sondern'; 'edenken' = 'bedenken'; 'philosophi- eben' = 'philosophischen'; 'u. f. w.' = 'u. s. w.'.","The final word is broken ('allge—'); it is completed as 'allgemeinen Begriffe' in the next id, so 'concepts' is rendered there."]],"deutlichkeit-0122":["concepts. Their meaning must at all times be kept immediately before the eyes. The pure understanding must be kept under strain, and how imperceptibly a mark of an abstracted concept escapes, since nothing sensible can reveal to us its omission; but then different things are taken for one and the same, and erroneous modes of knowledge are begotten.",["The first words, 'einen Begriffe', are the end of 'allgemeinen Begriffe' begun in the previous id.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Erkenntnisse' (plural) = 'modes of knowledge'. 'Sinnliches' rendered 'sensible'."]],"deutlichkeit-0123":["It has now been shown that the grounds from which we can gather that it is impossible to have erred in a certain philosophical mode of knowledge never in themselves equal those which one has before one in the mathematical. But besides this, the intuition of this knowledge, so far as correctness is concerned, is also greater in mathematics than in philosophy: for in the former the object is considered in sensible signs *in concreto*, but in the latter always only in general, abstracted concepts, whose clear impression can by far not be so great as in the former. In geometry, where the signs moreover have a resemblance to the things signified, this evidence is therefore still greater, although in algebra [Buchstabenrechnung] the certainty is just as reliable. Metaphysics is [capable] of a certainty that [suffices] for conviction |",["The OCR has interleaved two columns: 'deren klarer Eindruck ... bei die Zeichen ... überdem eine Ähnlichkeit haben, 2 weitem nicht so groß sein kann als der ersteren. In der Geometrie, wo ist daher...'. I have restored the evident order ('bei weitem nicht so groß sein kann als bei der ersteren. In der Geometrie, wo die Zeichen ... Ähnlichkeit haben, ist daher ...'); the reconstruction is a judgment, not the printed order.","The line number '19' and stray '2' were dropped. 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Buchstabenrechnung' = literal calculus, i.e. algebra.","The last sentence is split: its verbs 'hinreicht, fähig' stand at the head of the later ids, so 'capable' and 'suffices' are in brackets as supplied from there."]],"deutlichkeit-0124":["The certainty in metaphysics is of precisely the same kind as in |",["Sentence continues in the next id ('jeder andern philosophischen Erkenntniß')."]],"deutlichkeit-0125":["suffices, [is] capable [of it]. any other philosophical mode of knowledge, as indeed the latter too can be certain only in so far as it accords with the general grounds which the former supplies. It is known from experience that, even outside mathematics, we can in many cases become fully certain, to the point of conviction, through grounds of reason.",["The opening 'hinreicht, fähig.' belongs to the last sentence of id 0123 (displaced by the column break); left as a bracketed fragment.","'die erstere' (metaphysics) and 'diese' (any other philosophical knowledge) are resolved by judgment of the argument: the general grounds are those furnished by metaphysics.","The line number '20' and the OCR junk 'völlig 2 / na gewiß' were reduced to 'völlig ... gewiß' ('fully certain'); the final words continue in the next id."]],"deutlichkeit-0126":["Metaphysics is only philosophy applied to more general insights of reason, and it cannot be otherwise with it.",["'um lich anders' is an OCR slip for 'unmöglich anders', rendered 'cannot be otherwise'; 'bewandt sein' = 'be the case'."]],"deutlichkeit-0127":["Errors arise not only from our not knowing certain things, but from our undertaking to judge although we do not yet know everything that is required for the purpose. A great multitude of falsehoods, indeed almost all of them without exception, owe their origin to this latter presumption. You know some predicates of a thing with certainty. Very well: lay these at the foundation of your inferences, and you will not err. But you insist on having a definition; yet you are not sure that you know everything that is required for one, and since you venture on it notwithstanding, you fall into errors. Hence it is possible to escape errors if one seeks certain and distinct modes of knowledge, without however too readily laying claim to definitions. Further, you can infer with security to a considerable part of a certain sequence. By no means permit yourselves to draw the inference to the whole sequence, however slight the difference may appear. I grant that the proof we possess, showing that the soul is not matter, is a good one. But beware of concluding from it that the soul is not of a material nature. For by this everyone understands not only that the soul is no matter, but also that it is not such a simple substance as could be an element of matter. This requires a special proof, namely that this thinking being is not in space, as a corporeal element is, through impenetrability, and that it cannot, together with others, constitute an extended thing and a lump. No proof of this has as yet actually been given; and such a proof, if one were to discover it, would indicate the incomprehensible manner in which a spirit is present in space.",["Restated the closing sentence ('wovon wirklich noch kein Beweis...') as two sentences for clarity.","OCR slips silently corrected ('sich)', 'e noch', 'Ure | sprung', 's lider', 'ste dessen', 'Ter: ner'). 'Erkenntnisse' rendered 'modes of knowledge'."]],"deutlichkeit-0128":["§ 3.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0129":["The certainty of the first fundamental truths in metaphysics is of no other kind than in any other rational",["Initial letter of 'Die' lost in OCR; restored. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0130":["knowledge, mathematics excepted.",["Completes the heading begun in the preceding passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0131":["In our days the philosophy of Herr Crusius has supposed that it could give metaphysical knowledge an entirely different form. It has done so by refusing to the principle of contradiction the prerogative of being the universal and supreme principle of all knowledge, by introducing many other immediately certain and indemonstrable principles, and by maintaining that their correctness would be comprehended from the nature of our understanding",["Several OCR slips silently corrected (initial letters lost). The footnote marker after 'Crusius' is garbled in the OCR and omitted. The sentence breaks off here and continues in 0133 ('according to the rule...').","'Satz des Widerspruchs' = principle of contradiction."]],"deutlichkeit-0132":["I have found it necessary to make mention here of the method of this new philosophy. In a short time it has become so famous, and it has also, in respect of the better enlightenment of many insights, so acknowledged a merit, that it would be an essential defect, where metaphysics in general is the",["Footnote text (marker lost in OCR). Breaks off in mid-sentence ('die [Rede ist]'); completed in the next passage, where 'Rede' is lost to OCR."]],"deutlichkeit-0133":["subject, to have passed it over in silence. What I touch upon here is solely its peculiar method, for difference in single propositions is not yet enough to mark an essential difference of one philosophy from another. [The nature of our understanding, as it is] comprehended according to the rule: what I cannot think otherwise than as true, is true. Among such principles are reckoned, among others: what I cannot think as existing has never existed; every thing must be somewhere and somewhen, and the like.",["The first sentences finish the footnote begun in 0132; the rest resumes the main text of 0131. The bracketed words are supplied only to show the construction ('begriffen' belongs to the end of 0131's sentence, interrupted by the footnote).","'einmal nicht gewesen' taken as 'has never existed'; the sense is doubtful."]],"deutlichkeit-0134":["I shall indicate in a few words the true constitution of the first fundamental truths of metaphysics, and likewise the true content of this method of Herr Crusius, which in this respect departs less from the mode of thinking of [the school] philosophy than is commonly supposed. One will also be able to gather from this, in general, the degree of possible certainty in metaphysics.",["'der Philosophie' is ambiguous; I take it to mean the philosophy hitherto prevailing and supply '[the school]', in brackets. Stray OCR fragments ('simi i mter', 'N') dropped."]],"deutlichkeit-0135":["All true judgments must be either affirmative or negative. Since the form of every affirmation consists in this, that something is represented as a mark of a thing, that is, as identical with the mark of a thing, every affirmative judgment is true if the predicate is identical with the subject. And since the form of every negation consists in this, that something is represented as conflicting with a thing, a negative judgment is true if the predicate contradicts the subject. The proposition, therefore, which expresses the essence of every affirmation, and so contains the supreme formula of all affirmative judgments, runs: To every subject there belongs a predicate which is identical with it. This is the principle of identity. The proposition which expresses the essence of all negation, namely, To no subject does there belong a predicate which contradicts it, is the principle of contradiction; and this is accordingly the first formula of all negative judgments. Both together constitute the supreme and universal principles, in the formal sense, of the whole of human reason. In this most have erred, that they have conceded to the principle of contradiction, in respect of all truths, a rank which it possesses only in regard to the negative. Every proposition, however, is indemonstrable which is thought immediately under one of these supreme principles and cannot be thought otherwise; that is, when either the identity or the contradiction lies immediately in the concepts, and cannot, or may not, be perceived through analysis by means of an intermediate mark. All others are demonstrable. 'A body is divisible' is a demonstrable proposition, for through analysis, and so mediately, the identity of predicate and subject can be shown: body is composite; but what is composite is divisible; consequently a body is divisible. The mediating mark here is being composite. Now there are in philosophy many indemonstrable propositions, as has also been mentioned above. These all stand indeed under the formal first principles, but immediately; and in so far as they at the same time contain grounds of other modes of knowledge, they are the first",["Divided the long sentences at 'Und da...' and 'Und hierin...'; the sentence ending 'sind sie die ersten' continues in 0136 ('materiale Grundsätze').","'Merkmal' = mark; 'Zwischenmerkmal' = intermediate mark; 'Zergliederung' = analysis. Page/line numerals and OCR debris (86, 20, 28, 0, 1) omitted."]],"deutlichkeit-0136":["material principles of human reason. E.g. 'A body is",["Continues 0135. Sentence breaks off and continues in 0137."]],"deutlichkeit-0137":["composite' is an indemonstrable proposition, in so far as the predicate",[]],"deutlichkeit-0138":["can be thought only as an immediate and primary mark in the concept of body. Such material principles constitute, as",[]],"deutlichkeit-0139":["Crusius rightly says, the foundation and firmness of human",[]],"deutlichkeit-0140":["reason. For, as we have mentioned above, they are the material for explanations and the data from which one can safely infer, even if one has no explanation.",["'Stoff zu Erklärungen' rendered 'material for explanations'; 'Data' kept as in the original."]],"deutlichkeit-0141":["And in this Crusius is right when he censures other schools of philosophers for having passed these material principles by and having held solely to the formal. For from the latter alone nothing whatever can really be proved, because propositions are required which contain the middle concept, whereby the logical relation of other concepts is to be known in a syllogism, and among these propositions some must be first. But we can never concede to any propositions the value of supreme material principles unless they are evident to every human understanding. I hold, however, that several of those which Crusius adduces admit of considerable doubt.",["'Mittelbegriff' = middle concept; 'Vernunftschluß' = syllogism (literally, inference of reason). 'augenscheinlich' rendered 'evident'.","'sogar ansehnliche Zweifel' smoothed to 'considerable doubt'."]],"deutlichkeit-0142":["As to the supreme rule of all certainty, which this famous man proposes to set before all knowledge, and so before metaphysical knowledge also, namely, 'what I cannot think otherwise than as true is true', and so forth, it is easy to see that this proposition can never be a ground of the truth of any mode of knowledge. For if it is admitted that no other ground of truth can be given than that one cannot possibly hold the thing otherwise than as true, then it is conceded that no further ground of truth can be alleged at all, and that the knowledge is indemonstrable. Now there are, to be sure, many indemonstrable modes of knowledge; but the feeling of conviction with regard to them is a confession, not a proof, that they are true.",["The passage is restated with a colon-introduced rule and a 'namely' to make the construction of the opening sentence (anlangt ... so ist leicht einzusehen) regular in English.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'ge— denkt' (gedenkt), 'auge— geben' (angegeben), 'Beweis- grund' (Beweisgrund). The stray '5' is a line number, omitted.","Erkenntniß = knowledge; plural Erkenntnisse = 'modes of knowledge'. 'This famous man' is not named in the passage (presumably Crusius; the context is not supplied, so I do not add it).","unerweislich = 'indemonstrable'."]],"deutlichkeit-0143":["Metaphysics accordingly has no formal or material grounds of certainty differing in kind from those of mathematics. In both the formal element of judgments proceeds in accordance with the principles of agreement and of contradiction. In both there are indemonstrable propositions which furnish the foundation for inferences. Only, since in mathematics the definitions are the first indemonstrable concepts of the things explained, various indemonstrable propositions must take their place in metaphysics and supply the first data. These can be just as certain, and they afford either the material for explanations or the ground of secure inferences. Metaphysics is capable of a certainty necessary to conviction quite as much as mathematics is; only the certainty of the latter is easier [to attain] and partakes of a greater degree of intuition [Anschauung].",["Split the long sentence 'Nur da ... darbieten' into two sentences, the second beginning 'These can be...'; the relative 'welche' is taken to refer to the data (Data) and propositions together.","Last sentence: 'deren die Metaphysik, als welcher die Mathematik fähig ist' is smoothed to a comparison; 'to attain' is supplied in brackets for 'leichter'.","Meßkunst = mathematics (literally 'geometry'/'measurement'); Kant's parallel term is Mathematik, used in the same passage. Anschauung = intuition, kept in brackets because the sense turns on it.","Sätze der Einstimmung und des Widerspruchs rendered 'principles of agreement and of contradiction'."]],"deutlichkeit-0144":["Fourth Consideration.",["Betrachtung = 'Consideration' (the title of the sections of the *Untersuchung über die Deutlichkeit*)."]],"deutlichkeit-0145":["On the distinctness and certainty of which the first grounds",["Heading broken off; it continues in the next passage."]],"deutlichkeit-0146":["of natural theology and morals are capable. The first grounds of natural theology are capable of the",["Heading continues; the sentence is completed in the next passage. 'Gottesgelahrtheit' = theology (natural theology)."]],"deutlichkeit-0147":["highest philosophical evidence.",["Completes the heading. Evidenz = 'evidence' (in the older sense of self-evident clearness)."]],"deutlichkeit-0148":["In the first place, the easiest and most distinct distinction of a thing from all others is possible when this thing is the only possible one of its kind. The object of natural religion is the sole first cause; its determinations will be of such a nature that they cannot easily be confused with those of other things. But the greatest conviction is possible where it is absolutely necessary that these predicates, and no others, belong to a thing. For in the case of contingent determinations it is for the most part difficult to discover the variable conditions of its predicates. Hence the absolutely necessary being is an object of such a kind that, once one has hit upon the genuine trace of its concept, it seems to promise more certainty than most other philosophical knowledge. In this part of the problem I can do nothing but consider the possible philosophical knowledge of God in general; for it would be far too lengthy to examine the doctrines of the philosophers actually extant on this subject.\n\nThe principal concept which here presents itself to the metaphysician is the absolutely necessary existence of a being. To arrive at it he might first ask whether it is possible that nothing at all should exist. If he then perceives that in that case no existence is given, nothing is there to be thought, and no possibility obtains, he need only investigate the concept of the existence of that which must underlie all possibility. This thought will expand and will fix the determinate concept of the absolutely necessary being. But without entering particularly into this plan: as soon as the existence of the one most perfect and necessary being is known, the concepts of its remaining determinations become much more precisely defined, because they are always the greatest and most perfect, and much more certain, because only those can be admitted which are necessary.\n\nI am required, for example, to determine the concept of divine omnipresence. I readily recognise that the being on which everything else depends, while itself independent, will by its presence determine the place of all other things in the world, but will determine no place for itself among them, since it would then belong to the world. God is therefore properly in no place, but is present to all things in all places where the things are. In the same way I see that, since the successive things of the world are under his power, he does not thereby determine for himself a point of time in this series, and consequently that in respect of him nothing is past or future. When I say, therefore, 'God foresees the future', this does not mean 'God sees that which is future in respect of himself', but 'that which is future to certain things of the world', that is, which follows upon a state of them. From this it is to be recognised that the knowledge of the future, the past and the present is in respect of the act of the divine understanding [Verstand] not at all different, but that he knows them all as actual things of the universe; and this foreseeing can be represented in God much more determinately and distinctly than in a thing which belonged to the whole of the world.\n\nIn all respects, then, where no analogue of contingency is to be met with, the metaphysical knowledge of God can be very certain. But judgments upon his free actions, upon providence, and upon the procedure of his justice and goodness, since even in the concepts we have of these determinations much still remains undeveloped, can in this science have only a certainty by approximation, or one that is moral. The first grounds of morals, in their present condition, are not yet capable of all the evidence required.",["The passage is divided into four paragraphs at the natural turns (the argument, the principal concept, the omnipresence example, the conclusion); Kant's text is one block.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'leicht» lich' (leichtlich), 'unter: suchen', 'Ansch— ung', 'Ign allen Stücken' (In allen Stücken), 'it' (ist), 'seiner Art ... mögliche' (mögliches). 'Gewalt' rendered 'power'.","'Vorsehung' = providence; 'Kenntnisse' and 'Erkenntniß' both rendered 'knowledge'. 'Analogon der Zufälligkeit' kept as 'analogue of contingency'.","In 'wo nicht ein Analogon ... anzutreffen' the sense is 'in all respects where no analogue of contingency is found'; rendered accordingly.","'Sich selber aber keinen Ort': 'it' is supplied for the repeated subject (the being); the 'es' of 'indem es alsdann' is the same being."]],"deutlichkeit-0149":["To make this clear, I shall only show how little even the first concept of obligation (*Verbindlichkeit*) is yet known, and how far we must therefore be from being able to supply, in practical philosophy, the distinctness and certainty of fundamental concepts and principles which is required for evidence. One ought to do this or that and leave the other undone: this is the formula under which every obligation is expressed. Now every ought expresses a necessity of the action, and is capable of a twofold meaning. For I ought either to do something (as a means) if I will something else (as an end); or I ought immediately to do something else (as an end) and actually carry it out. The former might be called the necessity of the means (*necessitatem problematicam*), the second the necessity of the ends (*necessitatem legalem*). The former kind of necessity indicates no obligation at all, but only the precept, as the solution of a problem, as to which means are those that I must employ if I wish to attain a certain end. Whoever prescribes to another which actions he must perform or omit if he would promote his happiness might perhaps bring all the doctrines of morality under this; but they would then no longer be obligations. They would be much as it would be an \"obligation\" to draw two intersecting arcs if I wish to divide a straight line into two equal parts. That is, they are not obligations at all, but only directions for skilful conduct if one wishes to attain an end. Now since the employment of the means has no other necessity than that which belongs to the end, all actions which morality prescribes under the condition of certain ends are contingent, and cannot be entitled obligations, so long as they are not subordinated to an end that is necessary in itself. I ought, for example, to promote the greatest total perfection; or I ought to act in conformity with the will of God. Whichever of these two propositions the whole of practical philosophy may be subordinated to, that proposition, if it is to be a rule and ground of obligation, must command the action as immediately necessary, and not under the condition of a certain end. And here we find that such an immediate supreme rule of all obligation must be absolutely indemonstrable. For from no consideration of any thing or concept whatsoever is it possible to know and infer what one ought to do, if what is presupposed is not an end and the action a means. But it must not be so presupposed, since in that case the formula would be one not of obligation but of problematic skill.",["Restated nothing structurally; the long sentence beginning 'Da nun der Gebrauch der Mittel' is kept as one sentence, with 'so lange…so lange' smoothed into a single 'so long as'.","'Kreuzbogen' rendered 'intersecting arcs'; 'zwei Kreuzbogen zu machen' is the geometrical construction for bisecting a line.","Obvious OCR slips corrected silently: 'id)' and 'Sch' (ich), 'Nothwendigfeit', 'dem Mittel' retained as in the text for 'des Mittels' (rendered 'of the means'); 'dieb' elsewhere is 'dich'.","'Weltweisheit' rendered 'philosophy'; 'Verbindlichkeit' rendered 'obligation' (not in the fixed list; German given at first occurrence).","'es' in 'wenn…nicht ein Zweck…ist. Dieses aber muß es nicht sein': resolved as the presupposition being an end with the action a means; supplied 'so presupposed' for clarity."]],"deutlichkeit-0150":["And now I can briefly indicate that, after long reflection upon this subject, I have become convinced that the rule: Do the most perfect thing that is possible through you, is the first formal ground of all obligation to act, just as the proposition: Omit that by which the greatest perfection possible through you is hindered, is the first formal ground of the obligation to omit. And just as from the first formal principles of our judgments concerning the true nothing follows unless material first grounds are given, so from these two rules of the good alone no specially determinate obligation follows, unless indemonstrable material principles of practical knowledge are connected with them.",["'dieb' is OCR for 'dich'; read as 'you' (through you).","The last clause of the first sentence ('es in Ansehung der Pflicht zu unterlassen ist') is elliptical; I supplied 'the first formal ground of the obligation to omit', in effect, taking 'Pflicht' as 'duty' broadly; the sense is a little compressed and the reading is a judgment.","'Erkenntniß' = knowledge; 'praktischen Erkenntniß' = 'practical knowledge'."]],"deutlichkeit-0151":["For in our circumstances people have only just begun to see that the capacity to represent the true is knowledge (*Erkenntniß*), while the capacity to sense the good is feeling (*Gefühl*), and that the two must on no account be confused with one another. Just as there are indecomposable concepts of the true, that is, of what is met with in the objects of knowledge considered in themselves, so there is also an unanalysable feeling of the good (this is never met with in a thing absolutely, but always in relation to a sentient being). It is a business of the understanding to analyse the composite and confused concept of the good and make it distinct, by showing how it arises from simpler sensations of the good. But once this is simple, the judgment 'this is good' is wholly indemonstrable, and is an immediate effect of the consciousness of the feeling of pleasure together with the representation of the object. And since many simple sensations of the good are certainly to be found in us, there are many such unanalysable representations. Accordingly, if an action is represented immediately as good, without containing in a concealed manner some other good which can be recognised in it by analysis, and on account of which it is entitled perfect, then the necessity of this action is an indemonstrable material principle of obligation. For example, 'Love him who loves you' is a practical proposition which does indeed stand under the supreme formal and affirmative rule of obligation, but stands under it immediately. For since it cannot be shown by analysis why a particular perfection lies in returning love, this rule is not proved practically, that is, by reduction to the necessity of another perfect action, but is subsumed immediately under the general rule of good actions. Perhaps the example I have given does not exhibit the matter distinctly and convincingly enough; but the limits of a treatise such as the present, which I have perhaps already exceeded, do not permit me the completeness I should wish. There is an immediate ugliness in the action which conflicts with the will of him from whom our existence and all good derive. This ugliness is evident even if no regard is had to the disadvantages that may attend such conduct as consequences. Hence the proposition, 'Do what is in conformity with the will of God', becomes a material principle of morality, which yet stands formally under the supreme and general formula already mentioned, but stands under it immediately [continued in the next passage]",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('der schon er-' continues as 'erwähnten' in 0152); I have translated it as it stands, with my bracket marking only the break.","'Häßlichkeit' / 'Haplichfeit' (OCR) read as 'Häßlichkeit', 'ugliness'; 'Grr7bja&' read as 'Grundsatz'.","'unzergliederliche' (OCR 'unzerglieder— liebe') read as 'indecomposable'; 'Empfindung' = sensation, 'empfinden' = 'sense'.","'Gefühl' kept as 'feeling'; 'Vermögen' rendered 'capacity'.","'in unsern Lagen' rendered loosely as 'in our circumstances'; Kant's sense is perhaps 'in our times/situation'—a judgment.","'warum sie vollkommen heißt' rendered 'on account of which it is entitled perfect'."]],"deutlichkeit-0152":["[…erwähnten] supreme and general formula already mentioned, but immediately. One must in practical philosophy, just as in theoretical, not too readily hold something indemonstrable which is not so. Nevertheless these principles, which as postulates contain the foundations of the remaining practical propositions, cannot be dispensed with. Hutcheson and others, under the name of moral feeling, have made a beginning of fine observations on this subject.",["The passage opens with the fragment 'ahnten' = 'erwähnten' (hyphenated across a page break from the previous passage); I have not repeated the English twice, only the remainder, and left the German in brackets as a supplied reading.","'Schönen' (capitalised in the OCR) read as 'schöne'."]],"deutlichkeit-0153":["From this it is to be seen that, although it must be possible to attain the highest degree of philosophical evidence in the first grounds of morality, nevertheless the supreme fundamental concepts of obligation must first be more securely determined. In this respect the deficiency of practical philosophy is still greater than that of speculative philosophy, since it has yet first to be settled whether it is solely the faculty of knowledge or feeling (the first, inner ground of the faculty of desire) that decides the first principles thereof.",["'Erkenntnißvermögen' = 'faculty of knowledge'; 'Begehrungsvermögen' = 'faculty of desire'.","'die erste Grundsätze' is grammatically irregular; read as 'die ersten Grundsätze'."]],"deutlichkeit-0154":["Postscript.",[]],"deutlichkeit-0155":["These are the thoughts which I submit to the judgment of the Royal Academy of Sciences. I venture to hope that the grounds which have been set forth are of some significance for the required elucidation of the object. As regards the care, exactness and elegance of the execution, I preferred to neglect something in these respects rather than be prevented by them from handing the work in for examination at the proper time, especially since this deficiency can easily be made good in the event of a favourable reception.",["'Object' kept for 'Object' (here the subject matter of the prize question) rather than 'Gegenstand'.","OCR 'dieser'Mangel' read as 'dieser Mangel'."]],"nachricht-0001":["M. Immanuel Kant's Announcement of the Arrangement of his Lectures in the Winter Half-Year of 1765–1766.",["'Tinrichtung' is OCR for 'Einrichtung' ('arrangement'); 'Winterhalbenjahre' for 'Winterhalbjahre'.","Title lines joined into one; 'M.' = Magister."]],"nachricht-0002":["All instruction of the young has this burden attached to it: one is compelled to run ahead of the years with insight, and, without waiting for the understanding to mature, must impart such modes of knowledge as, in the natural order, could be grasped only by a more practised and experienced reason. Hence arise the perpetual prejudices of the schools, which are more obstinate and often more absurd than those of the common people, and the precocious garrulity of young thinkers, which is blinder than any other self-conceit and more incurable than ignorance. Yet this burden cannot be wholly avoided. In an age of highly elaborate civil life the finer insights count among the means of advancement and become needs, although by their nature they ought properly to be reckoned only as an ornament of life, and, as it were, among its dispensable beauties. Meanwhile it is possible to adapt public instruction more to nature in this respect also, if not to bring it wholly into agreement with her. For the natural progress of human knowledge is as follows. First the understanding forms itself, in that through experience it arrives at intuitive judgments, and through these at concepts. Thereupon these concepts are known by reason in relation to their grounds and consequences, and finally, by means of science, in a well-ordered whole. Instruction, then, will have to take the very same path. From a teacher it is therefore expected that he will form in his hearer first the man of understanding, then the man of reason, and finally the man of learning. Such a procedure has the advantage that, even if the pupil should never reach the last stage (as commonly happens), he has nevertheless gained by the instruction, and has become more practised and more intelligent, if not for the school, yet for life.",["The opening parenthesis-mark in the OCR ('sich)') is a stray; read as a comma. 'gals' read as 'als'. Hyphenation at line ends ('ge— zählt', 'Gelehr— ten', etc.) silently closed.","Restated the long sentence beginning 'Denn da der natürliche Fortschritt…' as several sentences; Kant's 'daß…, daß darauf…' clauses become a sequence of statements. The 'so' apodosis is moved into its own sentence.","Erkenntnisse = 'modes of knowledge'; Erkenntniß (sing.) = 'knowledge'. 'anschauende Urtheile' = 'intuitive judgments'. 'bürgerliche Verfassung' rendered loosely as 'civil life' for smoothness; literally 'civil constitution'.","'Man of understanding / of reason / of learning' = der verständige, vernünftige Mann, der Gelehrte."]],"nachricht-0003":["If this method is reversed, the pupil snaps up a kind of reason before the understanding has yet been developed in him, and carries about a borrowed science which has, as it were, merely been stuck on him and has not grown in him. His capacity of mind remains as unfruitful as ever, but has at the same time been much more corrupted by the delusion of wisdom. This is the reason why one not infrequently meets learned men (properly, men who have merely studied) who show little understanding, and why the academies send more absurd heads into the world than any other class of the commonwealth.",["'erschnappt' rendered 'snaps up'. 'Gemüthsfähigkeit' = 'capacity of mind'.","OCR 'ı anderer' read as 'anderer' (line number dropped). The trailing '|' is a line/page mark of the source and is not reproduced."]],"nachricht-0004":["The rule of procedure, then, is this: first to ripen the understanding and hasten its growth, by exercising it in judgments of experience and making it attentive to what the compared sensations of its senses can teach it. From these judgments or concepts it should not attempt any bold leap to the higher and more remote, but should arrive at them by the natural and well-trodden footpath of the lower concepts, which lead it gradually further. All this, however, must be in accordance with the capacity of understanding which the preceding exercise must necessarily have produced in him, and not in accordance with that which the teacher perceives in himself, or believes he perceives, and which he wrongly presupposes in his hearer also. In short, the pupil is not to learn thoughts, but to think. He is not to be carried, but guided, if it is desired that in future he shall be able to walk by himself.",["OCR damage repaired: 'Dore | hergehende' = 'vorhergehende'; 'sic)' = 'sich'; 'wahr- | zunehmen' = 'wahrzunehmen'. Stray signs ('ı', 'x|', '2') omitted as line numbers or noise.","Empfindungen = 'sensations'; Verstandesfähigkeit = 'capacity of understanding'. The long second sentence is kept as two sentences, split at 'alles aber…'."]],"nachricht-0005":["Such a mode of teaching is required by the peculiar nature of philosophy. Since philosophy is properly an occupation only for the age of manhood, it is no wonder that difficulties arise when one tries to adapt it to the less practised capacity of youth. The young man, on leaving school instruction, was accustomed to learning. Now he thinks he will learn philosophy, which, however, is impossible, for he is now to learn to philosophise. I shall explain myself more clearly. All the sciences which can be learned in the strict sense may be brought under two kinds, the historical and the mathematical. To the former belong, besides history proper, the description of nature, philology, positive law, and so on. Now in everything historical, personal experience or the testimony of others, and in everything mathematical, the evidentness of the concepts and the infallibility of the demonstration, constitute something that is actually given, and is therefore at hand, and has, as it were, only to be taken up. Hence in both it is possible to learn, that is, to impress upon the memory or upon the understanding what can be laid before us as an already completed discipline. To learn philosophy also, therefore, a philosophy would first of all have to be actually in existence. One would have to be able to produce a book and say: 'See, here is wisdom and reliable insight; learn to understand and grasp it, build upon it henceforth, and you are philosophers.' Until I am shown such a book of philosophy, to which I can appeal as to Polybius to elucidate a circumstance of history, or to Euclid to elucidate a proposition of mathematics, let me be permitted to say this: the confidence of the commonwealth is abused when, instead of enlarging the capacity of understanding in the youth entrusted to us and training them to a more mature insight of their own in the future, one deceives them with a philosophy alleged to be already complete, which has been thought out by others for their benefit. From this arises a semblance of science which passes for genuine coin only in a certain place and among certain people, but everywhere else is discredited. The method of instruction proper to philosophy is zetetic, as some of the ancients entitled it (from [Greek verb, illegible in the OCR]), that is, investigative, and becomes dogmatic, that is, decided, in various respects only when reason is already more practised. The philosophical author, too, whom one may take as the basis of instruction, is to be regarded not as the pattern of judgment but only as an occasion for judging about him, and even against him. What the pupil really seeks is facility in the method of thinking and inferring for himself, which alone can be useful to him; and the decided insights which he may acquire along with it must be regarded as accidental consequences, for whose rich abundance he has only to plant the fruitful root in himself.",["Weltweisheit rendered 'philosophy' throughout, Kant using it interchangeably with Philosophie here. 'Größenlehre' (doctrine of magnitudes) rendered 'mathematics' for smoothness.","OCR repairs: 'o werde' = 'werde'; 'sich)' = 'sich'; 'etc. xc.' = 'etc. etc.'; 'ss', '5' stray line numbers dropped; 'dog matisch' = 'dogmatisch'; '{chon' = 'schon'.","The Greek after 'von' is garbled by OCR ('Crew'); it is presumably ζητεῖν (zetein), but I have not supplied it and leave the reading to be checked against the Academy text.","The long sentence beginning 'Da nun in allem, was historisch ist…' is restated in two sentences, the 'so' apodosis forming a separate sentence ('Hence…'); the long 'Bis man mir…' sentence is divided at the colon, and the relative clause 'woraus…' made a separate sentence.","Supplied in square-bracket form only the Greek lacuna. 'Zutrauen des gemeinen Wesens' = 'confidence of the commonwealth'; 'Verstandesfähigkeit' = 'capacity of understanding'; 'Einsicht' = 'insight'."]],"nachricht-0006":["If we compare with this the common procedure, which departs so widely from it, we can understand various things that otherwise strike the eye as strange. For example, why there is no kind of learning, so far as a trade is concerned, in which so many masters are to be met with as in philosophy. Many of those who have learned history, jurisprudence, mathematics and the like admit to themselves that they have not yet learned enough to teach these in their turn. In philosophy, on the other hand, there is scarcely anyone who would not seriously imagine that, apart from his other occupations, it would be quite possible for him to lecture on logic, ethics and the like, if only he would trouble himself with such trifles. The cause is that in those sciences there is a common standard, whereas in this every man has his own. Likewise it will clearly be seen that it is very unnatural for philosophy to be a means of earning a livelihood, since it conflicts with its essential character to accommodate itself to the delusion of demand and the law of fashion. Only necessity, whose power is greater even than that of philosophy, can compel it to mould itself into the form of popular approval.",["The first sentence of the German ('Als z. E. warum…') is a tangled series of 'warum' clauses; I have restated it as several sentences, turning the 'warum… andererseits' clauses into a contrast. The first 'warum' is left without a main verb in English ('For example, why there is no kind…'), as in the German.","'Brodkunst' rendered 'a means of earning a livelihood' (literally 'bread-art'); 'Handwerke' rendered 'so far as a trade is concerned'—doubtful, the sense being that no learned discipline of the craft kind (i.e. learned by apprenticeship) has so many masters. 'Nothdurft' = 'necessity'.","The '|' after 'gemeinschaftlicher' is a line mark and is not reproduced."]],"nachricht-0007":["The sciences which I intend to expound and treat fully in private lectures during the half-year now beginning are the following:",["OCR 'ge⸗ JE benfe' read as 'gedenke' (line break; stray 'JE' and '15' line number dropped). The passage ends in a colon; the list is in the following passage."]],"nachricht-0008":["*) The second of the treatises which the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin published on the occasion of the prize-question for the year 1763.",["OCR 'K. A. d. W. in Berlin s' read as 'K. A. d. W. in Berlin'; the stray 's' is dropped as an OCR slip.","'Abhandlungen' rendered 'treatises'; the reference is to Kant's *Untersuchung über die Deutlichkeit der Grundsätze* (Inquiry concerning the Distinctness of the Principles of Natural Theology and Morals)."]],"nachricht-0009":["1. Metaphysics. In a brief and hastily composed essay*) I have tried to show that this science, notwithstanding the great labours of the learned, is still so imperfect and uncertain because its peculiar method has been misunderstood: it is not synthetic, like that of mathematics, but analytic. In consequence, what is simple and most general in the doctrine of quantity is also the easiest, whereas in the principal science it is the most difficult; in the former it must by its nature come first, in the latter last. In the former one begins the doctrine with definitions, in the latter one ends it with them; and so in other respects also. I have for some time past been working according to this plan; and since every step on this road has disclosed to me the sources of errors and the standard of judgment by which alone they can be avoided, if it is ever possible to avoid them, I hope shortly to be able to set out in full what may serve me as the foundation of my exposition in the science named. Till then, however, I can very well, by a slight deflection, turn into the same path the author whose textbook I have chosen, chiefly on account of the richness and [precision (?)] of his method, A. G. Baumgarten. After a short introduction I therefore begin with empirical psychology, which is properly the metaphysical empirical science of man; for as regards the expression 'soul', it is not yet permissible, in this division, to assert that man has one. The second division, which is to deal with corporeal [nature in general, I borrow from the chapters of the cosmology in which matter is treated, though I shall complete it by some written additions. Now since in the former science (to which, for the sake of analogy, empirical zoology, that is, the consideration of animals, is also added) all life that falls within our senses is considered, and in the second all that is lifeless",["Passage supplied a footnote marker only in the OCR; I have placed '*)' after 'essay' where the German has 'Schrift“)' (OCR garble), following the footnote of nachricht-0008.","Sentences kept close but 'In jener... in dieser...' parallels retained; 'Größenlehre' = 'doctrine of quantity'.","OCR garbled: 'ys seiner Lehrart' — probably 'der Deutlichkeit (?) seiner Lehrart' or similar; I supply '[precision (?)]' only as a conjecture. Doubtful reading.","'Biegung' rendered 'deflection'; 'Lesebuch' = 'textbook' (Baumgarten's Metaphysica).","OCR 'Bonn habe' read as 'gewählt habe' (chosen), a conjecture; 'zur? 55 Grundlegung' read as 'zur Grundlegung'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0010":["has been considered in general, and since all things in the world can be brought under these two classes, I proceed to ontology, that is,",["Continues the previous sentence; 'überhaupt' placed after 'considered' ('erwogen worden') as the German order suggests."]],"nachricht-0011":["to the science of the more general properties of all things, the conclusion of which contains the distinction between spiritual and material beings, likewise",["Fragment; breaks off mid-sentence (the 'im-gleichen' is divided across passages)."]],"nachricht-0012":["the connection or separation of the two, and hence rational psychology. Here I now have the great advantage of being able not only to lead the already practised hearer into the most difficult of all philosophical",["Fragment, continues in the next passage. 'Verknüpfung oder Trennung' rendered literally."]],"nachricht-0013":["inquiries, but also, since I consider the abstract in every",["Fragment; 'schen Untersuchungen' completes 'philosophischen Untersuchungen' from the previous passage."]],"nachricht-0014":["reflection in that *concretum* which the preceding disciplines put into my hands, of setting everything in the greatest clearness,",["'Concreto' (dative) rendered as the Latin technical term *concretum*, kept in italics.","'Deut-lichkeit' is completed in the next passage; 'clearness' here for Deutlichkeit."]],"nachricht-0015":["without anticipating myself, that is, without being obliged to cite by way of illustration something",["Fragment; 'vorgreifen' rendered 'anticipate'."]],"nachricht-0016":["which is to come up only later, which is the common and unavoidable fault",["Fragment; 'ge-meine' is completed in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0017":["of synthetic exposition. Last of all",["Fragment of a sentence continued in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0018":["comes the consideration of the cause of all things, that is, the science",[]],"nachricht-0019":["of God and the world. I cannot refrain from mentioning one further advantage,",["Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0020":["which indeed rests only on accidental causes but is nevertheless not to be lightly esteemed, and which I propose to derive from this method. Everyone knows with what zeal the beginning of lectures is made by lively and inconstant youth, and how the lecture-rooms then gradually become somewhat more spacious. Now if I suppose that what ought not to happen will nevertheless, in spite of all admonition, continue to happen in future, then the method of teaching I have described retains a utility peculiar to itself. For the hearer whose zeal had evaporated even by the end of empirical psychology (which, with such a manner of procedure, is hardly to be expected) would nevertheless have heard something which, by its ease, would be intelligible to him, by its interest agreeable, and by the frequent occasions for its application in life useful; whereas, on the contrary, if ontology, a science difficult to grasp, had frightened him off from continuing, whatever he may perhaps have comprehended could be of no further use to him at all.",["OCR 'gring' read 'gering'; 'gleidhwohl' read 'gleichwohl'; 'Galle der Anwendung' read as 'Fälle der Anwendung' ('occasions for application'), 'fafs ende' as 'fassende'.","The long final sentence is kept as one, though somewhat tangled; the parenthesis is kept in place."]],"nachricht-0021":["2. Logic. Of this science there are properly two kinds. The first is a critique and prescription of the sound understanding (*gesunder Verstand*), as it borders on the one side upon crude concepts and ignorance, and on the other upon science and learning. It is logic of this kind which should be placed at the beginning of academic instruction, ahead of all philosophy, as a sort of quarantine (if I may be allowed to express myself thus) which the novice must keep who wishes to pass from the land of prejudice and error into the domain of more enlightened reason and of the sciences. The second kind of logic is the critique and prescription of learning proper, and can never be treated otherwise than after the sciences of which it is to be the organon, in order that the procedure which has been employed in practice may become more regular, and that the nature of the discipline, together with the means of its improvement, may be understood. In this way I add, at the end of metaphysics, a consideration of its peculiar method, as an organon of this science, which would not be in its proper place at the beginning, since it is impossible to make rules distinct when no examples are yet at hand in which one can show them *in concreto*. The teacher must, to be sure, have the organon already in his possession before he expounds the science, so that he may himself be guided by it; but to the hearer he must never present it otherwise than last. The critique and prescription of the whole of philosophy as a whole, this complete logic, can therefore have its place in instruction only at the end of the whole of philosophy, since the knowledge of it already acquired, and the history of human opinions, alone make it possible to reflect upon the origin of its insights as well as of its errors, and to draw up the exact plan according to which such an edifice of reason is to be erected durably and regularly.",["'Kritik' rendered 'critique'; 'Organon' kept; 'in concreto' italicised as Latin.","OCR 'gefunden' read 'gesunden'; 'afademischen' read 'akademischen'; 'die [don erworbene' read 'die schon erworbene'; 'angue stellen' read 'anzustellen'.","'Weltweisheit' rendered here 'philosophy' (as 'Philosophie' also occurs in the passage)."]],"nachricht-0022":["I shall lecture on logic of the first kind, and indeed according to the handbook of Professor Meier, because he has well in view the limits of the aims just mentioned, and at the same time affords occasion to include, together with the cultivation of the finer and more learned reason, the formation of the understanding, which is indeed common, but active and sound: the former for the contemplative, the latter for the active and civil life. Besides this, the very close affinity of the subject-matter affords occasion, in the critique of reason, to cast some glances at the critique of taste,",["'Wobei zub gleich' read 'Wobei zugleich'; 'ge— meinen' read 'gemeinen'.","Sentence breaks off; completed in the next passage ('d. i. die Ästhetik...')."]],"nachricht-0023":["that is, aesthetics, since the rules of the one always serve",["Fragment; 'Ästhetik' rendered 'aesthetics'."]],"nachricht-0024":["to elucidate those of the other, and their contrast is a means of comprehending both better. 3. Ethics. Moral philosophy has this peculiar fate, that it assumes the semblance of science and some appearance of thoroughness even sooner than metaphysics does, although neither of the two is to be found in it;",["OCR '0 3. Ethik. ... Schick sal' read as '3. Ethik ... Schicksal'.","Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0025":["the cause of which lies in this: that the distinction",["'Unterfchei-dung' completed in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0026":["of good and evil in actions, and the judgment upon moral rightness, can be known directly and without the detour of proofs",["OCR 'gerade zu' read 'geradezu'; stray 's' dropped."]],"nachricht-0027":["from the human heart, through what is called *sentiment*, easily and correctly; hence, because the question",["'Sentiment' kept in italics as Kant's foreign term.","Breaks off mid-sentence ('wehren-' = 'währenden' or similar); not completed."]],"nachricht-0028":["is in part already decided before the grounds of reason [are heard], which in meta-",["Fragment: it begins and ends mid-sentence. The subject and the rest of the clause lie outside the passage. 'Are heard' is supplied in brackets because the German is elliptical ('vor den Vernunftgründen')."]],"nachricht-0029":["physics is not the case, it is no wonder that one does not show oneself particularly",["Continues the broken word 'Meta-physik' from the previous fragment. The sentence is cut off and continues in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0030":["difficult [to please] about letting grounds pass as serviceable which have only some semblance of soundness,",["'Schwierig bezeigt' is rendered 'show oneself difficult [to please]'. The bracketed words are supplied. The sentence continues in the next passage ('als tauglich durchgehen zu lassen'), so I have kept the clause order in the English."]],"nachricht-0031":["as serviceable. For this reason nothing is more common than the title of a moral philosopher, and nothing rarer than to deserve such a",["The first words repeat the sense of 'als tauglich durchgehen zu lassen' from the preceding fragment. 'Name' follows in the next passage."]],"nachricht-0032":["name.",[]],"nachricht-0033":["For the present I shall lecture on general practical philosophy [*Weltweisheit*] and the doctrine of virtue, both following Baumgarten. The attempts of Shaftesbury, Hutcheson and Hume, which, though incomplete and defective, have nevertheless got furthest in the search for the first grounds of all morality, will receive the precision and the completion which they lack. And since in the doctrine of virtue I shall always consider, both historically and philosophically, what happens before I indicate what ought to happen, I shall make clear the method",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'und s die Tugendlehre', 'Tugendo lehre', 'ge— schieht'.","'Weltweisheit' is rendered 'philosophy', with the German added in brackets, because the term is a period one.","The sentence breaks off after 'Methode' and continues in the next passage; it is translated as it stands.","I have divided the original's long second half into two sentences, as the method allows."]],"nachricht-0034":["by which man must be studied: not only man as he is disfigured by the changeable form which his contingent condition impresses upon him, and as such has been almost always mistaken even by philosophers, but the nature of man, which always remains, and its peculiar place in creation. Thus we may know what perfection befits him in the state of rude simplicity and what in the state of wise simplicity, and what, on the other hand, is the precept of his conduct when, passing beyond both limits, he strives to touch the highest stage of physical or moral excellence, yet deviates more or less from both. This method of moral enquiry is a fine discovery of our times, and, when considered in its complete plan, was wholly unknown to the ancients.",["The passage begins in mid-sentence, continuing the previous one ('die Methode deutlich machen, nach welcher man den Menschen studiren muß').","I have restated the long 'damit man wisse…' clause as a separate sentence introduced by 'Thus', to keep it intelligible.","'Einfalt' is rendered 'simplicity'. 'Von beiden' is taken to refer to the two kinds of excellence, physical and moral.","OCR corrections: 'zu— fälligen', 'Philo- 5 sophen' (line number dropped)."]],"nachricht-0035":["4. Physical Geography. At the very beginning of my academic teaching I recognised that a great neglect in the education of studious youth consists chiefly in this, that they learn to reason early, without possessing sufficient historical knowledge, which can take the place of experience. I therefore formed the design of making the history of the present condition of the earth, or geography in the widest sense, into an agreeable and easy compendium of whatever could prepare for, and serve, a practical reason, and could awaken the desire to extend ever further the knowledge begun in it. I entitled such a discipline, after that part on which my chief attention was then directed, physical geography. Since then I have gradually enlarged this plan, and I now intend, by contracting more closely that division which deals with the physical curiosities of the earth, to gain time for extending the treatment of the other parts of it, which are still more generally useful. This discipline will accordingly be a physical, moral and political geography. In it the curiosities of nature will first be set forth through its three kingdoms, but with a selection, from among countless others, of those which, by the charm of their rarity, or by the influence which through trade and industry they have upon states, present themselves chiefly to universal curiosity. This part, which at the same time contains the natural relation of all lands and seas and the ground of their connection, is the proper foundation of all history, without which history is little distinguished from fairy-tales. The second division considers man, over the whole earth, in the manifold variety of his natural characteristics and in the difference of what is moral in him. It is a very important and equally attractive study, without which one can hardly pass general judgments upon man; and the comparison, made of men with one another and with the moral condition of earlier times, lays before our eyes a great chart of the human race. Last, there will be considered what may be regarded as a consequence of the reciprocal action of the two forces just described, namely the condition of the states and nations of the earth. This will be considered not so much as it rests upon the contingent causes of the undertakings and fortunes of individual men, such as the succession of rulers, conquests and political intrigues, but in relation to what is more constant and contains the remote ground of these, namely the situation of their countries, their products, manners, industry, commerce and population. Even the rejuvenation, if I may so call it, of a science of such wide prospects on a smaller scale has its great utility, since only thereby is attained the unity of knowledge, without which all learning is but piecemeal. May I not also, in so sociable a century as the present, reckon among the benefits which it is no degradation for science to have in view the store which a great variety of agreeable and instructive items of knowledge, easily grasped, offers for the entertainment of social intercourse? At the least, it cannot be pleasant for a scholar often to find himself in the embarrassment of the orator Isocrates, who, when he was urged in company to say something too, had to reply: What I know is not fitting, and what is fitting I do not know.",["The paragraph is divided into many shorter sentences, as the method permits; the content is unchanged.","OCR corrections: 'afademischen' → akademischen; 'Luft' → Lust (desire); 'Unterhält' → Unterhalt; 'gfif das' → 'auf das' (reading doubtful, sense unaffected); hyphen breaks and 'vernünf— teln' repaired.","'Im weitesten Verstande' is rendered 'in the widest sense'. 'Kenntnisse' is rendered 'knowledge' or 'items of knowledge' (not 'Erkenntnis'); 'Einheit der Erkenntniß' is 'unity of knowledge'.","'Beider vorher erzählten Kräfte' is taken to refer to the natural and the moral characteristics of man treated in the first two divisions."]],"nachricht-0036":["This is the brief announcement of the occupations which I devote to the half-year of the Academy now begun. I have considered it necessary only so that some conception may be formed of the manner of teaching, in which I have now found it useful to make some changes. *Mihi sic est usus: Tibi ut opus facto est, face.* Terentius.",["'Erachtet' lacks its auxiliary in the OCR; I have supplied 'have considered' (German 'erachtet habe'). 'Balde' → halbe, and 'sünden' → funden, are silent OCR corrections.","The Latin (Terence, *Heauton Timorumenos*) is left in Latin: 'For me this is the custom; as you need to do, do.'","'Begriff' here is not technical; 'conception' is used."]],"traeume-0001":["Dreams of a Spirit-Seer, Elucidated by Dreams of Metaphysics.",["OCR 'Melaphysik' corrected to 'Metaphysik'. The title page is divided into lines in the source."]],"traeume-0002":["A Preliminary Notice,",["'Vorbericht' rendered 'Preliminary Notice'. The title continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0003":["which promises very little for the execution [of the work].",["'Of the work' is supplied in brackets to complete the sense of 'für die Ausführung'."]],"traeume-0004":["The realm of shadows is the paradise of fantasies. Here they find",[]],"traeume-0005":["an unbounded land where they can settle at their pleasure. Hypochondriacal vapours, old wives' tales and cloister miracles leave them no lack of building material. The philosophers draw the ground-plan",["OCR corrections: stray ')' after 'sich', 'By- 5 pochondrische' → Hypochondrische."]],"traeume-0006":["and alter it again or reject it, as is their custom. Only holy Rome has profitable provinces there; the two crowns of the invisible realm support the third, as the frail diadem of its so earthly majesty, and the keys which open the two gates of the other",["The sentence is broken off here and continues in the next passage ('Welt aufthun…')."]],"traeume-0007":["world open at the same time, sympathetically, the coffers of the present one.",["Continues 'der andern' of the previous fragment: 'the other world'."]],"traeume-0008":["Such rights of the realm of spirits, in so far as it is proved by grounds of statecraft, rise far above all impotent objections of the schoolmen, and their use or abuse is already too venerable to need to expose itself to so contemptible an examination. But the common stories, which find so much credence and are at least so badly refuted: why do they run about so unprofitably, or unpunished, and creep even into the systems of doctrine, although they do not have on their side the proof drawn from advantage (*argumentum ab utili*), which is the most convincing of all? What philosopher has not once, between the assurances of a rational and firmly convinced eyewitness and the inner counter-",["OCR corrections: 'b zu', 'nkböthig' → nöthig, 'argumb tum', stray ')' after 'sich'. Sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage.","I have divided the German's single run of rhetorical questions from the preceding statement into separate sentences."]],"traeume-0009":["defence of an insuperable doubt, cut the most simple-minded figure that can be imagined? Shall he deny altogether the soundness of all such apparitions of spirits? What grounds can he adduce to refute them?",["'Gegenwehr' is split across the two fragments ('Gegen-' / 'wehr'); I have rendered it 'counter-defence' across the break. OCR 'Geifters erscheinungen' → Geistererscheinungen."]],"traeume-0010":["Shall he, then, concede even a single one of these tales as probable? How weighty such a confession would be, and into what astonishing consequences one looks out, if even a single such occurrence could be presupposed as proved! There is surely still a third case left, namely not to meddle at all with such forward or idle questions, and to keep to what is useful. But because this design is reasonable, it has always been rejected by thorough scholars by a majority of votes.",["OCR 'sich)' and hyphen breaks silently corrected. 'Anschlag' rendered 'design'."]],"traeume-0011":["It is just as stupid a prejudice to believe nothing, without grounds, of much that is related with some semblance of truth, as it is to believe everything, without examination, that common rumour says. The author of this essay, in order to avoid the first prejudice, let himself be dragged in part into the second. He confesses, with a certain humiliation, that he was so simple-hearted as to investigate the truth of some stories of the kind mentioned. He found — — — as commonly happens where one has nothing to look for — — — — he found nothing. Now this is in itself quite sufficient reason for writing a book. But there was added something that has more than once extorted books from modest authors, namely the importunate urging of friends, known and unknown. Moreover, a large work had been bought and, what is still worse, read, and this trouble was not to be lost. Out of all this has arisen the present treatise, which, as one flatters oneself, should fully satisfy the reader, in view of the nature of the subject, since he will not understand the most important part, will not believe the next, and will laugh at the rest. | The First Part, | which is Dogmatic.",["Kant's long sentences are divided in places (the 'Moreover' and 'Out of all this' sentences are kept separate; the first sentence is split in two) for clarity.","'Verfasser' rendered 'author'; the dashes after 'Er fand' are kept as in the text.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Nun ist dieses wohl an sich) selbst' read 'an sich selbst'; 'Überdem war 2 ein großes Werk' read 'war ein großes Werk'; 'solte' read 'sollte'; 'fol' read 'soll'. The stray '2' is OCR noise.","The sign | marks the Academy Edition's line/page break and is kept where it stands."]],"traeume-0012":["First Chapter.",["'Hauptstück' rendered 'Chapter'."]],"traeume-0013":["An intricate metaphysical knot, which may be untied or cut through, as one pleases.",["The title is broken across two passages (0013 'nach' / 0014 'Belieben'); 'nach Belieben' is rendered in 0013 only for the sense and is not repeated in 0014.","Translated as it stands, since the portal divides the heading; see 0014."]],"traeume-0014":["[as one] pleases untie or cut through.",["Continuation of the heading begun in 0013 ('nach / Belieben auflösen oder abhauen kann'); the English of the whole is given in 0013, and here the fragment is rendered as it stands, with the supplied words in square brackets."]],"traeume-0015":["If everything that the schoolboy recites about spirits, the great crowd relates, and the philosopher demonstrates, were taken together, it would seem to make up no small part of our knowledge. Nevertheless I venture to assert that if it occurred to anyone to pause a little over the question, what sort of thing that really is of which one believes so much is understood under the name of a spirit, he would put all these men of many facts into the most grievous embarrassment. The methodical chatter of the high schools is often merely an agreement to evade a question hard to solve by shifting the meanings of words, since the convenient and for the most part reasonable answer, 'I do not know', is not readily heard in academies. Certain more recent worldly-wise men [*Weltweisen*], as they like to be called, get over this question very easily. A spirit, they say, is a being that has reason. Then it is no miraculous gift to see spirits; for whoever sees men sees beings that have reason. But, they go on, this being which in man has reason is only a part of the man, and this part, which gives him life, is a spirit. Very well, then: before you prove that only a spiritual being can have reason, see to it that I first understand what concept I am to form of a spiritual being. This self-deception, although it is gross enough to be noticed with eyes barely open, is nevertheless of a very comprehensible origin. For of what one knows a great deal early, as a child, one is sure to know nothing later and in old age; and the man of thoroughness becomes in the end at best the sophist of the delusion of his youth.",["The sentence 'wovon man ... zu verstehen glaubt' is smoothed in English; 'Wesen' rendered 'being'.","'Weltweisen' (literally 'worldly-wise men') is the contemporary German term for philosophers; kept with the German in brackets.","OCR slips corrected silently: 'herbetet, er große Hause' read 'der große Haufe'; 'zusammen genommen'; 'igentlich' read 'eigentlich'; 'SBortbebeutungen' read 'Wortbedeutungen'; 'many sicher' read 'man sicher'; 'Gründ⸗lichkeit' read 'Gründlichkeit'; 'bald osse— neu' read 'bald offenen'.","'Wenn 8 jemand' — the '8' is OCR noise (line number)."]],"traeume-0016":["I do not know, then, whether there are spirits; indeed, what is more, I do not even know what the word 'spirit' signifies. Since, however, I have often used it myself, or heard others use it, something must be understood by it, whether this something be a chimera or something real. To unwrap this hidden meaning, I hold my ill-understood concept up to all sorts of cases of its application, and by observing to which it applies and to which it is repugnant, I hope to unfold its concealed sense.*)",["'Mü- wendung' in the OCR is read as 'Anwendung' (application); the reading is conjectural, though it agrees with the footnote's 'Fällen der Anwendung'.","The asterisk-bracket marks Kant's footnote, given in 0017; 'Hirngespinst' rendered 'chimera'."]],"traeume-0017":["*) If the concept of a spirit were derived from our own concepts of experience, the procedure of making it distinct would be easy, since one would only have to indicate those marks which the senses revealed to us in this kind of being, and by which we distinguish them from material things. But spirits are spoken of even when one doubts whether there are any such beings at all. Hence the concept of spiritual nature cannot be treated as one abstracted from experience. But if you ask: How, then, has one come to this concept at all, if it has not happened by abstraction? I answer: Many concepts arise through secret and obscure inferences on the occasion of experiences, and are afterwards propagated to others without consciousness of the experience itself or of the inference which erected the concept upon it. Such concepts may be called *surreptitious* [erschlichene]. There are many of this sort, which are partly nothing but a delusion of the imagination, partly also true, since even obscure inferences do not always err. Linguistic usage, and the connection of an expression with various stories in which one and the same chief mark is always to be met, give it a determinate meaning, which accordingly can be unfolded only by drawing this hidden sense out of its obscurity through comparison with all sorts of cases of application, which either agree with it or conflict with it.",["'Erschlichene' rendered 'surreptitious' (Latin *conceptus subreptitii*), the term Kant's Latin parallel suggests; German retained in brackets.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Der- ?: gleichen' read 'dergleichen'; 'Be— aq wußtsein' read 'Bewußtsein'; '35' is a line number, omitted.","The long question-and-answer is kept in separate sentences, as in the German."]],"traeume-0018":["*) It is easily noticed here that I speak only of spirits which belong as parts to the whole of the world, and not of the infinite Spirit who is its author and preserver. For the concept of the spiritual nature of the latter is easy, because it is merely negative, and consists in denying of Him those properties of matter which conflict with an infinite and absolutely necessary substance. On the other hand, in the case of a spiritual substance which is to be in union with matter, such as the human soul, the difficulty shows itself that I am to think a reciprocal connection of it with corporeal beings in a whole, and yet to remove the only known kind of connection which obtains among material beings.",["'Urheber' (OCR 'Nr— heber') corrected silently. 'Ihn/ihm' rendered 'Him' with capital for the infinite Spirit; this is a supplied reading from context.","'soll' (OCR 'fol') read as 'soll'; the sentence is slightly smoothed in English.","The footnote (AA 2:321) is evidently attached to a point in the main text of the chapter; the portal does not give the anchor, so no marker is added."]],"traeume-0019":["Take a space of one cubic foot, and suppose that something fills this space, that is, resists the intrusion of every other thing. No one will call a being that is in space in this manner spiritual. It would obviously be entitled material, since it is extended and impenetrable, and, like everything corporeal, is subject to divisibility and to the laws of impact. Thus far we are still on the well-trodden track of other philosophers. But conceive a simple being, and let it at the same time have reason: will this then exactly fill out the meaning of the word *spirit*? In order to discover this, I shall leave reason to the said simple being as an inner property, and for the present consider it only in its outer relations. And now I ask: if I wish to place this simple substance in that cubic foot of space, which is full of matter, will a simple element of the matter then have to vacate the place, so that this spirit may fill it? Do you say yes? Very well; then, in order to admit a second spirit, the space in question will have to lose a second elementary particle. If one goes on in this way, a cubic foot of space will in the end be filled with spirits, whose mass will resist by impenetrability just as much as if it were full of matter, and, like matter, must be capable of the laws of impact. But substances of this kind, even though they may have the power of reason in themselves, would in their outer aspect be in no way distinguishable from the elements of matter. Of these elements we likewise know only the forces of their outer presence, and have no knowledge at all of what may belong to their inner properties. It is therefore beyond doubt that such a kind of simple substances, of which lumps could be massed together, would not be entitled spiritual beings. You will accordingly be able to retain the concept of a spirit only if you conceive beings which can be present even in a space filled with matter; beings, therefore, which do not have the property of impenetrability, and of which any number you please, when united, never constitute a solid whole. Simple beings of this kind will be entitled immaterial beings, and, if they have reason, spirits. Simple substances, on the other hand, whose composition yields an impenetrable and extended whole, will be entitled material unities, and their whole, matter. Either the name of a spirit is a word without any meaning, or its meaning is the one here indicated.",["OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. 'id)' for 'ich', 'ang- f gedehnt' for 'ausgedehnt', 'ung f' read as 'und'). The stray '“)' after 'können;' is a footnote/quotation mark remnant; omitted, as no footnote text is given.","Several long sentences restated as shorter ones (the 'Meinet ihr, ja? Wohlan…' sequence and the 'Nun würden aber…' sentence).","'heißen / genannt werden' rendered 'is entitled'; 'Vernunftkraft' as 'power of reason'; 'Klumpe' as 'mass', 'Klumpen' as 'lumps'.","'Bis dahin … gebähnten Gleise' rendered 'well-trodden track'; 'materielle Einheiten' kept as 'material unities'."]],"traeume-0020":["From the explanation of what the concept of a spirit contains, the step is still immensely far to the proposition that such natures are actual, or indeed even merely possible. One finds in the writings of philosophers quite good proofs, which can be relied upon, that everything that thinks must be simple; that every rationally thinking substance is a unity of nature; and that the indivisible I cannot be distributed over a whole of many connected things. My soul, then, will be a simple substance. But this proof still leaves undecided whether it is of the kind that, united in space, yield an extended and impenetrable whole, and is therefore material, or whether it is immaterial and consequently a spirit; indeed, even whether such a kind of beings as that which is called spiritual is so much as possible. |5",["The OCR '14' before 'materiell' and the marginal '5' are line-number remnants; the '|' is carried over from the Academy apparatus, with the '5' kept beside it as a line marker.","'set' read as 'sei'. 'der Schritt noch ungemein weit' restated more smoothly.","'vernünftigdenkende' rendered 'rationally thinking'; 'Einheit der Natur' as 'unity of nature'."]],"traeume-0021":["And here I cannot refrain from warning against hasty decisions, which creep most easily into the deepest and darkest questions. For what belongs to the common concepts of experience, we are accustomed to regard as though we also saw its possibility. On the other hand, of what deviates from them, and cannot be made intelligible by any experience, not even by analogy, we can of course form no concept, and for that reason we are apt to reject it at once as impossible. All matter resists in the space of its presence, and is for that reason called impenetrable. Experience teaches that this happens, and abstraction from this experience produces in us the general concept of matter. But this resistance, which something offers in the space of its presence, is in this way indeed known, but for that reason not comprehended. For it is, like everything that counteracts an activity, a true force; and since its direction is opposed to that in which the extended lines of approach aim, it is a force of repulsion, which must be ascribed to matter and consequently also to its elements. Now every reasonable man will soon be content to admit that here human insight comes to an end. For only through experience can we become aware that things of the world which we call material have such a force; the possibility of it we can never comprehend. If now I posit substances of another kind, which are present in space with forces other than that driving force whose consequence is impenetrability, I can of course form no thought *in concreto* of an activity of theirs which has no analogy with my representations of experience. And in taking from them the property of filling the space in which they act, I lose a concept by which things that fall within my senses are otherwise made clear to me; and a kind of unthinkableness must necessarily arise from this. But this cannot for that reason be regarded as a known impossibility, precisely because the opposite will likewise remain, as to its possibility, not understood, although its actuality falls within the senses.",["OCR slips corrected silently ('ftd)' as 'sich', '[o' as 'so', '18', '20%', '25)', '95' line numbers dropped).","'erkannt' / 'begriffen' distinguished as 'known' / 'comprehended'; 'eingesehen' rendered 'understood'/'saw' for 'einsehen' (insight).","'treibenden Kraft' rendered 'driving force'; 'Undenklichkeit' as 'unthinkableness'. *in concreto* kept in Latin.","'fortgezogne Linien der Annäherung' rendered 'extended lines of approach'; the sentence is restated for clarity but the sense is unchanged."]],"traeume-0022":["Accordingly, the possibility of immaterial beings may be assumed without fear of refutation, although also without hope of being able to prove this possibility by rational grounds. Such spiritual natures would be present in space, yet in such a way that space would nevertheless always remain penetrable for corporeal beings. For their presence would involve an activity in space, but not a filling of it, that is, not a resistance, which is the ground of solidity. If, then, we assume such a simple spiritual substance, we could say, without prejudice to its indivisibility, that the place of its immediate presence is not a point but itself a space. For, to call analogy to our aid, the simple elements of bodies must each of them necessarily fill a little space in the body, a space which is a proportionate part of the body's whole extension, since points are not parts of space at all but its boundaries. Now this filling of space takes place by means of an active force (that of repulsion), and so indicates only a range of greater activity, not a plurality of constituent parts in the acting subject. It is therefore not in the least in conflict with the simple nature of that subject, although the possibility of this cannot, it is true, be made any clearer, which is never feasible in the case of the first relations of causes and effects. In the same way, at least no demonstrable impossibility will stand in my way, though the matter itself remains incomprehensible, if I assert that a spiritual substance, although simple, may nevertheless occupy a space (that is, be able to act immediately in it) without filling it (that is, without offering resistance in it to material substances). Nor would such an immaterial substance have to be entitled extended, any more than are the unities of matter. For only that which, separated from everything else and existing for itself alone, occupies a space is extended. The substances, however, which are elements of matter occupy a space only through their outer effect upon others. Taken separately for themselves, where no other things are thought in connection with them, and where nothing is to be found in themselves that exists outside one another, they contain no space. This holds of the elements of bodies; it would hold also of spiritual natures. The boundaries of extension determine figure; in such natures, therefore, no figure could be thought. These are grounds, difficult to see, for the presumed possibility of immaterial beings in the world-whole. Let anyone who has easier means of leading to this insight not refuse his instruction to an eager learner, before whose eyes, as the inquiry advances, Alps often rise where others see before them a level and comfortable footpath, along which they walk, or believe that they walk.",["The first sentences of the German are divided; 'so daß derselbe … bliebe, weil …' is restated as two sentences, with 'derselbe' resolved as space (Raum).","'Räumchen' rendered 'little space'; 'Widerstand' rendered 'resistance'; 'Solidität' as 'solidity'.","'ein-|f ander' and 'Elemente I der Materie' are OCR breaks; silently read as 'einander' and 'Elemente der Materie'. 'Beftge' read as 'Besitze'. The bar signs | and || that remain in the OCR are page-break debris and are omitted.","'D. i.' (OCR capital) read as 'd. i.', 'that is'.","'nicht ausgedehnt genannt werden müsse' rendered 'would [not] have to be entitled extended' per the method's rendering of 'genannt werden'.","'Welt-ganzen' rendered 'world-whole'."]],"traeume-0023":["Suppose, now, that it had been proved that the soul of man is a spirit (although it is evident from what precedes that such a proof has never yet been given). The next question that might be asked would then be something like this: Where is the place of this human soul in the corporeal world? I should answer: That body whose alterations are my alterations, that body is my body, and its place is at the same time my place. If the question is pressed further: Where, then, is your place (that of the soul) in this body? I should suspect something captious in the question. For it is easily noticed that something is here already presupposed which is not known through experience but perhaps rests on imagined inferences, namely, that my thinking I is in a place distinct from the places of the other parts of the body that belongs to my self. No one, however, is immediately conscious of a particular place in his body, but only of that which he occupies as a man in relation to the world around him. I should therefore keep to common experience and say, provisionally: Where I feel, there I am.",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('hal-' / 'ten' in the next passage); the clause 'Ich würde mich also … halten und vorläufig sagen' is completed by the next id, so here I translate 'hal-' with the sentence as it stands, joining it to the next passage's 'ten und vorläufig sagen'. This is a minor overlap and the join is marked only by the hyphen in the OCR.","OCR 'set' read as 'sei'; 'noch) niemals' read as 'noch niemals'; 'Körper: welt' read as 'Körperwelt'. The signs ':' and '|' that are OCR debris are omitted.","'Verfängliches' rendered 'captious'; 'Veränderungen' as 'alterations'."]],"traeume-0024":["I am just as immediately in the tip of my finger as in my head. It is I myself who suffer in the heel, and in whom the heart beats in emotion. When my corn torments me, I feel the painful impression not at a nerve of the brain but at the end of my toes. No experience teaches me to regard any parts of my sensation as remote from me, or to shut my indivisible I up in a microscopically small spot of the brain, in order from there to set the levers of my bodily machine in motion, or to be myself affected thereby.* Hence I should demand a strict proof before finding absurd what the schoolmen said: My soul is whole in the whole body and whole in each of its parts. Sound understanding often notices the truth before it sees the grounds by which it can prove or elucidate it. Nor would the objection wholly confuse me, were it said that in this way I think of the soul as extended and diffused through the whole body, much as it is depicted to children in the picture-world. For I should remove this obstacle by remarking that immediate presence in a whole space proves only a sphere of outer activity, not a plurality of inner parts, and therefore not extension or figure either, which find a place only when a space is posited in a being taken by itself alone, that is, when parts are to be met with which lie outside one another. In the end I should either know this little concerning the spiritual quality of my soul, or, if it were not granted, be content to know nothing of it at all.",["The passage begins with 'ten und vorläufig sagen: Wo ich empfinde, da bin ich', continuing the sentence from the previous passage; I have translated only the sentences that follow, as the opening fragment is already rendered there.","The asterisk after 'getroffen zu werden' marks the footnote (traeume-0026); the OCR has none, but the footnote's '*)' shows where it attaches, so the mark is supplied in square-bracket spirit, not in the German text.","'Leichborn' (modern 'Leichdorn') rendered 'corn'; 'Hebezeug' as 'levers'; 'Affecte' as 'emotion'.","OCR 'Musdehnung' read as 'Ausdehnung'; 'sich)' as 'sich'; marginal line numbers (os, ss, s) omitted.","'Schullehrer' rendered 'schoolmen'. The long sentence on Ausdehnung is kept as one, with 'als welche' resolved to refer to extension and figure."]],"traeume-0025":["Were it objected to these thoughts that they are incomprehensible, or, what with most people passes for the same thing, impossible, I could",["Breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands.","OCR 'Unbegreiflichfeit' read as 'Unbegreiflichkeit'; the stray ')' after 'bei' omitted."]],"traeume-0026":["* There are examples of injuries by which a good part of the brain was lost without costing the man his life or his thoughts. According to the common representation which I here adduce, an atom of it would have to be carried off, or moved from its place, to deprive the man of his soul in an instant. The prevailing opinion of assigning the soul a place in the brain seems chiefly to have its origin in this, that in deep reflection one clearly feels that the nerves of the brain are strained. But if this inference were correct, it would also prove other places of the soul. In anxiety or joy sensation seems to have its seat in the heart. Many emotions, indeed most, exhibit their chief strength in the diaphragm. Compassion moves the bowels, and other instincts exhibit their origin and sensibility in other organs. The cause that makes people believe they feel the reflecting soul chiefly in the brain is perhaps this. All reflection requires the mediation",["Footnote to traeume-0024; breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands.","'Vorstellung' rendered 'representation'; 'Empfindung' as 'sensation'; 'Empfindsamkeit' as 'sensibility'.","OCR 'den Schluß' kept as in the source ('den' for 'der'): read as 'der Schluß' and rendered 'this inference'. 'Orter' rendered 'places'."]],"traeume-0027":["...also permit [it] to happen. I should then seat myself at the feet of these sages, to hear them speak thus: The soul of man has its seat in the brain, and an indescribably small place therein is its abode. There it senses, like the spider at the centre of its web. The nerves of the brain push or shake it, and thereby bring it about that not this immediate impression, but the one which occurs in quite remote parts of the body, is nevertheless represented as an object present outside the brain. From this seat the soul also moves the cords and levers of the whole machine, and causes voluntary movements at its pleasure. Propositions of this kind can be proved only very shallowly, or not at all; and since the nature of the soul is at bottom not sufficiently known, they can likewise be refuted only as weakly. I should therefore not engage in school-quarrels, where commonly both parties have most to say when they understand nothing at all of their subject. I should merely follow out the consequences to which a doctrine of this kind may lead me. Now according to the propositions recommended to me, my soul, in the manner in which it is present in space, would not be distinguished from any element of matter. The power of understanding (*Verstandeskraft*) is an inner property which I could not perceive in these elements, even if it were to be met with in all of them. No valid ground, therefore, could be adduced why my soul should not be one of the substances which constitute matter, and why its particular appearances should not arise merely from the place which it occupies in an artificial machine such as the animal body, where the union of the nerves assists the inner faculty of thinking and of choice (*Willkür*). But in that case one would no longer recognise with certainty any peculiar mark of the soul distinguishing it from the crude primary stuff of corporeal natures,",["Restated the long sentence 'Weil also ... einnimmt' as two sentences, resolving the 'Weil ... so' construction by judgment.","OCR corrected silently: Süßen→Füßen, Selle→Seile (cords), wahr: nehmen→wahrnehmen, herrüh— ren→herrühren, ste→sie, and stray '1' before Element. Stray quotation marks and ')' are remnants of footnote marks and of the closing of the sages' speech; I omit them, and the footnote itself is not in the passage.","*Verstandeskraft* rendered 'power of understanding'; *Willkür* given 'choice' here (but 'voluntary' for *willkürliche Bewegungen*); *empfindet* rendered 'senses'. The opening '...' is supplied because the passage begins mid-sentence."]],"traeume-0028":["no ... of the signs for the ideas that are to be awakened, in order that, accompanied and supported by them, these [ideas] may receive the required degree of clarity. But the signs of our representations are chiefly those received either through hearing or through sight; both these senses are moved by impressions in the brain, since their organs also lie nearest to this part. Now if the awakening of these signs, which Cartesius calls *ideas materiales*, is really an excitation of the nerves to a movement similar to that which the sensation formerly produced, then in reflection the tissue of the brain will chiefly be compelled to vibrate in harmony with former impressions, and will thereby be fatigued. For when the thinking is at the same time emotional, one feels not only strains upon the brain, but at the same time assaults upon the irritable parts, which otherwise stand in sympathy with the representations of the soul when transported into passion.",["The passage opens with a fragment 'no' that I cannot complete (perhaps part of a word such as 'nöthig'); I leave it as it stands and mark the gap with '...'.","'zu heben' is doubtful (OCR); I read 'zu beben' ('to vibrate') as the sense requires. Flagged as conjecture.","*Vorstellungen* = representations; *Ideen* = ideas; *Empfindung* = sensation; *ideas materiales* kept in Latin and italics."]],"traeume-0029":["...ter Part. Chapter 1.",["A running head (the reading of the first word is corrupt in the OCR, probably 'Erster Theil'); translated as it stands. Strictly it is no part of Kant's text."]],"traeume-0030":["...and Leibniz's jesting fancy, according to which we perhaps swallow in coffee atoms from which human lives are to come, would no longer be a thought to laugh at. But in such a case would not this thinking I be subject to the common fate of material natures? And as it was drawn by chance out of the chaos of all the elements",["The initial '(se)' is an OCR remnant (probably the end of the preceding word or a footnote mark); left out of the translation. The sentence breaks off and is continued in the next passage; I divided it into two sentences at 'Würde aber'."]],"traeume-0031":["to animate an animal machine, why should it, after",["Mid-sentence fragment; translated as it stands."]],"traeume-0032":["this chance union has ceased, not also return thither again in the future? It is sometimes necessary to frighten by the consequences the thinker who is on the wrong",["The passage breaks off after 'un-' (completed in the next passage as 'unrechtem'); I translate 'on the wrong' and the next passage supplies 'path'."]],"traeume-0033":["path, so that he may become more attentive to the principles by which he has, as it were in a dream, allowed himself to be",["The initial 'rechtem Wege' completes the word split in the previous passage. English word order requires 'allowed himself to be' here, with 'led on' in the next passage (*fortführen lassen*). Stray 'd' and ')' in the OCR silently removed."]],"traeume-0034":["led on.",[]],"traeume-0035":["I confess that I am very much inclined to assert the existence of immaterial na-",["Word broken across passages (Na-/turen)."]],"traeume-0036":["tures in the world, and to place my own soul in the class of these beings.*)",["The *) refers to the footnote given in the passage traeume-0040."]],"traeume-0037":["But then, how mysterious does the communion between a spirit and a body become! Yet how natural, at the same time, is this incomprehensibility, since our concepts of external actions are abstracted from those of matter and are always bound up with the conditions of pressure or impact, which here do not",["Kant's two exclamatory questions are rendered as exclamations for English idiom. Line numbers removed. The sentence breaks off ('nicht / statt finden') and continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0038":["take place? For how should an immaterial substance stand in the way of matter, so that this, in its motion, may strike upon a spirit,",["OCR 'Ma-0 serie' corrected silently to 'Materie'."]],"traeume-0039":["and how can corporeal things exercise effects upon an alien",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage (*Wesen ausüben*)."]],"traeume-0040":["*) The ground of this, which is very obscure to myself and will in all probability remain so, applies at the same time to the sentient being in animals. Whatever in the world contains a principle of life seems to be of immaterial nature. For all life rests on the inner capacity of determining oneself at will (*nach Willkür*). The essential mark of matter, on the other hand, consists in the filling of space by a necessary force which is limited by external counteraction; hence the state of everything that is material is externally dependent and constrained. Those natures, however, which are themselves active and, working from their inner force, are to contain the ground of life (in short, those whose own choice (*Willkür*) is capable of determining and altering itself of itself) can hardly be of material nature. One cannot reasonably demand that so unknown a kind of beings, which for the most part one knows only hypothetically, should be comprehended in the divisions of their various species. At the least, those immaterial beings which contain the ground of animal life are distinct from those which comprehend reason in their self-activity and are entitled spirits.",["Footnote kept as a footnote. OCR corrected silently: bejd)rünft→beschränkt, Wieise→Weise, Selbitthätigkeit→Selbstthätigkeit; line-number digits removed.","Divided the long sentence 'Denn alles Leben ... Natur sein können' into three sentences, with the parenthesis for *kurz diejenige ...* shown as such.","*Willkür* rendered 'choice' or 'at will' according to context; *Geister genannt werden* = 'are entitled spirits'."]],"traeume-0041":["being that opposes no impenetrability to them, or which in no way hinders them from being at the same time in the same space in which it is present? It seems that a spiritual being is most intimately present to the matter with which it is united, and acts not on those forces of the elements by which these stand in relations to one another, but on the inner principle of their state. For every substance, even a simple element of matter, must surely have some inner activity as the ground of its external efficacy, though I do not know how to state in what this consists.*) On the other hand, on such principles the soul would also, in these inner determinations as effects, know intuitively the state of the universe, which is their cause. But what necessity brings it about that a spirit and a body together make one, and what grounds, in the case of certain destructions, again cancel this unity: these questions, together with various others, far surpass my insight. However little I am otherwise bold enough to measure my faculty of understanding against the secrets of nature, I am nevertheless confident enough to shun no opponent, however formidably equipped (if I had otherwise any inclination to dispute), in order to make with him, in this case, the trial of counter-arguments in refutation. Among the learned this is properly the skill of demonstrating to one another their ignorance.",["Begins mid-sentence ('Wesen ausüben'), continuing traeume-0039; the opening is rendered accordingly.","*anschauend erkennen* rendered 'know intuitively' (*Anschauung* = intuition); *Bestimmungen* = determinations; *Verstandesfähigkeit* = 'faculty of understanding'.","Restated the final sentence as two, setting the definition of the 'Versuch der Gegengründe' apart; the dashes/line number '20' removed; 'Nicht⸗wissen' joined as 'Nichtwissen' (ignorance). The *) refers to the footnote in traeume-0040."]],"traeume-0042":[") Leibniz said that this inner ground of all its outer relations and of their changes is a power of representation [*Vorstellungskraft*], and later philosophers received this undeveloped thought with laughter. They would, however, have done well to consider beforehand whether a substance, such as a simple part of matter is, is possible without any inner state whatsoever. If they did not wish to exclude such a state, it would have been incumbent upon them to devise some other possible inner state than that of representations and of the activities dependent upon them. Everyone sees for himself that, even if we grant to the simple elementary parts of matter a capacity for obscure representations, no power of representation in matter itself follows. For many substances of this kind, combined in a whole, can never constitute a thinking unity.",["The opening \")\" is carried over as it stands; it closes a parenthesis begun in the preceding passage. The final \";\" of the OCR is dropped as a stray mark.","OCR slips silently corrected: \"cm pfingen\" read as *empfingen*; \"viel Substanzen\" read as *viele Substanzen*.","*Vorstellungskraft* rendered \"power of representation\"; *Vermögen* rendered \"capacity\"; *Vorstellungen* \"representations\".","The second sentence is somewhat smoothed: \"wenn sie dann diesen etwa nicht ausschließen wollten\" is given as a separate sentence."]],"traeume-0043":["Second Chapter.",["*Hauptstück* rendered \"Chapter\"."]],"traeume-0044":["A Fragment of Secret Philosophy, designed to open up the community",["The title is split across two passages and breaks off here; the translation does not complete it. *Gemeinschaft* rendered \"community\", as in Kemp Smith's category of that name."]],"traeume-0045":["with the world of spirits.",["Continuation of the title in the preceding passage."]],"traeume-0046":["The initiate has already accustomed his understanding, once coarse and clinging to the outer senses, to higher and abstract concepts, and he can now see spiritual shapes, stripped of corporeal stuff, in that twilight in which the feeble light of metaphysics makes the realm of shadows visible. After the toilsome preparation, which is now over, let us therefore venture upon the perilous way.",["\"once coarse\" is supplied to bring out the force of the participial construction (*den groben ... Verstand*). *Verstand* = understanding; *Begriffe* = concepts.","OCR \"be— schwerlichen\" and \"gefähr— lichen\" are repaired; the line-break hyphens are dropped."]],"traeume-0047":["*Ibant obscuri sola sub nocte per umbras,*\n*Perque domos Ditis vacuas et inania regna.*\nVIRGIL.\n[They went, obscure, beneath the lonely night through the shadows, and through the empty halls of Dis and his insubstantial realms.]",["Virgil, *Aeneid* VI, 268–269. The Latin is kept; the English in square brackets is supplied by the translator and is not in the text."]],"traeume-0048":["Dead matter, which fills cosmic space, is by its peculiar nature in a state of inertia and of persistence in one and the same condition. It has solidity, extension and figure, and its appearances, which rest on all these grounds, admit of a physical explanation which is at the same time mathematical, and the two together are entitled mechanical.\n\nIf, on the other hand, we direct our attention to that kind of beings which contain the ground of life in the universe, we find that they are not of such a kind as, being constituents, to increase the mass and extension of lifeless matter, nor do they suffer from it according to the laws of contact and impact. Rather, by inner activity, they set in motion both themselves and, besides, the dead stuff of nature. In that case we shall find ourselves persuaded of the existence of immaterial beings, if not with the distinctness of a demonstration, yet at least with the presentiment of an understanding not unpractised. The special laws of their action are entitled pneumatic, and, in so far as corporeal beings are the intermediate causes of their effects in the material world, organic.\n\nSince these immaterial beings are self-active principles, and therefore substances and self-subsisting natures, the first consequence that suggests itself is this: that, immediately united with one another, they may perhaps constitute a great whole, which can be called the immaterial world (*mundus intelligibilis*). For on what ground of probability could it be maintained that beings of this kind, similar in nature to one another, can stand in community only by means of others (corporeal things) of alien constitution, seeing that this latter supposition is much more enigmatic than the first?",["Divided the first long sentence into separate sentences, and the second (\"Wenn man andererseits ...\") into two, resolving the relative clauses (\"die ... nicht von der Art sind\") into main statements; the content is unchanged.","\"In that case\" is supplied to carry the connection of protasis and apodosis across the division.","*genannt werden* = \"are entitled\"; *Gemeinschaft* = \"community\"; *Wirkungsgesetze* = \"laws of action\"; *Deutlichkeit* = \"distinctness\".","OCR \"räthjelhafter\" read as *räthselhafter*; \"organ isch\" repaired. *mundus intelligibilis* kept in Latin."]],"traeume-0049":["This immaterial world can therefore be regarded as a self-subsisting whole, whose parts stand to one another in reciprocal connection and community, even without the mediation of corporeal things. Hence the latter relation is contingent and can belong only to some of them. Even where it is met with, it does not prevent the very immaterial beings which act upon one another through the mediation of matter from standing, besides this, in a special and thoroughgoing connection, and from exercising at all times reciprocal influences upon one another as immaterial beings. Their relation by means of matter is thus only contingent, and rests upon a special divine arrangement, whereas the other relation is natural and indissoluble.",["Restated as three sentences; \"Hence\" renders the consecutive *so daß*, and \"from standing ... and from exercising\" resolves the double dependence on *nicht hindert*.","OCR \"dec ruht\" read as *beruht* (\"rests\"); doubtful but the sense requires it."]],"traeume-0050":["When, in this way, we take together all the principles of life in the whole of nature as so many incorporeal substances, standing in community with one another but also in part united with matter, we conceive a great whole of the immaterial world, an immeasurable but unknown gradation of beings and active natures, through which alone the dead stuff of the corporeal world is quickened. But to which members of nature life extends, and which are the degrees of it that border most closely on complete lifelessness, it is perhaps impossible ever to determine with certainty. Hylozoism animates everything; materialism, on the contrary, when carefully weighed, kills everything. Maupertuis ascribed the lowest degree of life to the organic nutritive particles of all animals; other philosophers see in them nothing but dead lumps, which serve only to enlarge the lifting-tackle of animal machines.\n\nThe undoubted mark of life in what falls under our outer senses is doubtless free movement, which lets it be seen that it has sprung from choice [*Willkür*]. But the inference is not safe that, where this mark is not met with, no degree of life is present either. Boerhaave says in one place: The animal is a plant which has its root in the stomach (inwardly). Perhaps another might, with equal impunity, play with these concepts and say: The plant is an animal which has its stomach in the root (externally). Hence also to the latter the organs of",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"Daher auch den letzteren die Organen der\"); it is translated as it stands, without completion.","OCR \"Wagen\" (twice) is read as *Magen*, \"stomach\", as the argument requires. The stray \")\" after \"gedenkt man sich\" is dropped.","Divided at \"Das ungezweifelte Merkmal ...\" for readability; *Willkür* kept in brackets because the sense turns on it; *Hebezeug* rendered \"lifting-tackle\"."]],"traeume-0051":["... that voluntary movements, and with them the external marks of life, may be lacking. Yet these marks are necessary to the former [i.e. to the first-named class of beings], because a being which has the instruments of its nourishment within itself must be able to move itself in accordance with its need. But a being in which these instruments are sunk outside it, in the element of its sustenance, is already sufficiently maintained by external forces. Even though it contains a principle of inner life in vegetation, it requires no organic arrangement for external voluntary activity.\n\nI demand nothing of all this on the strength of proofs. Apart from the fact that I should have very little to say in favour of such conjectures, they have also against them, as dusty, antiquated fancies, the mockery of fashion. The ancients, namely, believed that they could assume three kinds of life: the plant-like, the animal, and the rational. If they united the three immaterial principles of these in man, they may well have been in the wrong. But if they distributed them among the three kinds of creatures that grow and generate their like, what they said was, to be sure, something indemonstrable, but not on that account absurd. This holds especially in the judgment of one who is willing to take into consideration the particular life of parts severed from certain animals; irritability, that property of the fibres of an animal body and of some plants, so well proved and yet at the same time so inexplicable; and finally the close kinship of polyps and other zoophytes with plants.\n\nMoreover, the appeal to immaterial principles is a refuge of lazy philosophy, and a mode of explanation of this kind is therefore to be avoided as far as possible. For thus those grounds of the appearances of the world which rest on the laws of motion of mere matter, and which alone are capable of being comprehended, will be known in their whole extent. Nevertheless I am convinced that Stahl, who gladly explains animal changes organically, is often nearer the truth than Hoffmann, Boerhaave and others. These leave the immaterial forces out of account, hold to mechanical grounds, and in so doing follow a more philosophical method, which, though it sometimes fails, more often hits the mark, and which alone is of useful application in science. For as regards the influence of beings of incorporeal nature, it can at most be known only that it exists, never how it comes about or how far its efficacy extends.",["The passage opens mid-sentence; the OCR reads 'Dal willkürlichen Bewegung und mit ihnen'. I read 'daß [die] willkürlichen Bewegungen', the plural being required by 'ihnen'; doubtful reading, supplied words not otherwise marked.","'den ersteren' is ambiguous without the preceding context; I take it to mean the first-named class of beings (those having the organs of nourishment within) and say so in brackets.","Restated: the first long sentence is divided into three; the 'Übrigens' sentence is divided at 'For thus…' and the final sentence at 'For as regards…'. 'Welterscheinungen' = 'appearances of the world'.","'Hofmann' rendered 'Hoffmann' (Friedrich Hoffmann); 'Muthmaßungen' = 'conjectures'; 'Begreiflichkeit' = 'comprehensibility'. The running head is omitted."]],"traeume-0052":["*) When we speak of heaven as the seat of the blessed, the common representation likes to place it above us, high up in the immeasurable space of the world. But we do not reflect that our earth, seen from those regions, would itself appear as one of the stars of heaven, and that the inhabitants of other worlds could, with equally good ground, point towards us and say: 'Behold the dwelling-place of eternal joys, a heavenly abode which is prepared to receive us one day.' A strange delusion, namely, brings it about that the high flight which hope takes is always connected with the concept of ascending. We do not consider that, however high one has climbed, one must sink again in order, in any case, to gain a firm foothold in another world. According to the concepts here set out, however, heaven would properly be the world of spirits, or, if one will, the blessed part of it. This we should have to seek neither above us nor below us, because such an immaterial whole is not to be estimated by distances or nearnesses in relation to corporeal things, but in spiritual",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('sondern in geistigen'); translated as it stands, not completed.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'fest' → 'setzt', 'Fing' → 'Flug', 'zu— bereitet' → 'zubereitet'; the stray ')' after 'sich' is dropped.","'Begriffen' rendered 'concepts' ('the concepts here set out'); the long sentences beginning 'Ein wunderlicher Wahn' and 'Nach den angeführten Begriffen' are divided."]],"traeume-0053":["Thus the immaterial world would comprise, first, all created intelligences, some of which are united with matter in one person, others not. It would comprise, besides, the sentient subjects in all species of animals, and finally all principles of life which may otherwise be anywhere in nature, even though these should reveal themselves by no external signs of voluntary movement.\n\nAll these immaterial natures, I say, whether they exercise their influences in the corporeal world or not, and all rational beings whose contingent state is animal, whether here on earth or in other heavenly bodies, whether they now animate the crude stuff of matter, or will do so hereafter, or have formerly animated it, would, according to these concepts, stand in a community conformable to their nature. This community does not rest on the conditions by which the relation of bodies is limited; and in it the distance of places or of ages, which in the visible world constitutes the great gulf that cancels all community, vanishes.\n\nThe human soul would therefore have to be regarded, even in the present life, as linked with two worlds at once. So far as it is united in personal unity with a body, it clearly senses the material world alone. As a member of the world of spirits, on the other hand, it receives and imparts the pure influences of immaterial natures. Accordingly, as soon as that union has ceased, the community in which it stands at all times with spiritual natures would alone remain, and would have to open itself to its consciousness in clear intuition.",["The passage ends with a footnote sign (OCR '.“)'), presumably '*)' ; I have not reproduced it in the text.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'ste' → 'sie', 'thie— risch' → 'thierisch', 'mòdgen' → 'mögen', 'set' → 'sei', 'persön— lieber' → 'persönlicher', 'int materieller' → 'immaterieller'.","Restated: the second sentence ('Alle diese immaterielle Naturen…') is divided into two and the final sentence into three, to make the antecedents plain; 'welche' in 'von welchen' is resolved as 'two worlds'.","'Anschauen' rendered 'intuition' per the fixed terms; 'zufälliger Zustand' = 'contingent state'."]],"traeume-0054":["First Part. Chapter 2. 333",["This is only the running head with the page number; it is rendered as it stands, though the general rule would omit it."]],"traeume-0055":["It is becoming positively burdensome always to speak in the cautious language of reason. Why should I not also be allowed to speak in the academic tone, which is more decisive and relieves both author and reader of reflection, which sooner or later can lead both only to a vexatious indecision? It is, then, as good as demonstrated, or could easily be proved if one wished to be prolix, or better still, will hereafter be proved, I know not where or when, that the human soul, even in this life, stands in an indissolubly linked community with all the immaterial natures of the world of spirits; that it acts upon these in turn and receives impressions from them, of which, however, as a human being it is not conscious so long as all is well.\n\nOn the other hand, it is also probable that spiritual natures cannot have immediately, with consciousness, any sensible sensation of the corporeal world. For they are not united with any part of matter in one person, so as to become conscious, by means of it, of their place in the material whole of the world, and, through artificial organs, of the relation of extended beings to themselves and to one another. They can, however, flow into the souls of men as beings of one and the same nature, and do in fact stand at all times in reciprocal community with them. But this is so, that in the communication of representations those which the soul contains in itself as a being dependent on the corporeal world cannot pass over into other spiritual beings. Nor can the concepts of the latter, as intuitive representations of immaterial things, pass over into the clear consciousness of man, at least not in their proper character, because the materials of the two kinds of ideas are of different sorts.",["Restated: the long second sentence is divided into three sentences, the second at 'For they are not united…' and the third at 'They can, however…'; the clause 'daß sie aber wohl…' is thereby made independent.","OCR slip 'dap' → 'daß' silently corrected; line numbers 15, 20, 25 omitted.","'Nachdenkens' = 'reflection'; 'Vorstellungen' = 'representations'; 'anschauende Vorstellungen' = 'intuitive representations'; 'Begriffe' = 'concepts'; 'Ideen' kept as 'ideas'.","'so lange alles wohl steht' rendered 'so long as all is well'; the sense (so long as the soul is in health) is left to the reader."]],"traeume-0056":["It would be fine if such a systematic constitution of the spirit-world as we here represent were to be inferred, or even only probably conjectured, not merely from the concept of spiritual nature in general, which is far too hypothetical, but from some actual and universally admitted observation. I therefore venture, trusting in the reader's indulgence, to insert here an attempt of this kind. It lies somewhat off my road, and is also far enough removed from evidence, yet it seems to give occasion for conjectures that are not unpleasing. [Displaced fragment, as it stands in the text: \"and also from the connections of its parts with one another must be represented, at least the members of it are conscious of themselves only according to such relations.\"]",["The OCR text is scrambled. The words \"und auch von der Verknüpfungen … bewußt sind\" interrupt the main sentence and are evidently a stray line (probably footnote text). I have kept them, bracketed, after the main text, and have not tried to repair them.","The main sentence reads \"von der … Evidenz weit gnug entfernt\" only if the stray line is lifted out; I restored this connection by judgment. \"Begriffe 30\" contains a line number, which is omitted.","I corrected \"gat\" to \"gar\" and \"Sondern\" to \"sondern\" as OCR slips. \"Geisterwelt\" is rendered \"spirit-world\"."]],"traeume-0057":["Among the forces that move the human heart, some of the most powerful seem to lie outside it. They are therefore not mere means that relate to self-interest and private need as to an end lying within man himself. On the contrary, they bring it about that the tendencies of our impulses transfer the focus of their union to a point outside us, in other rational beings. From this there arises a conflict between two forces, namely selfhood [*Eigenheit*], which refers everything to itself, and common utility [*Gemeinnützigkeit*], through which the mind is driven or drawn towards others outside itself.\n\nI do not dwell upon the impulse by virtue of which we depend so strongly and so universally upon the judgment of others, and consider the approval or applause of others so necessary to the completion of our own judgment. Though a misunderstood delusion of honour sometimes springs from this, nevertheless even in the most disinterested and truthful disposition a secret pull is felt to compare what one recognises for oneself as good or true with the judgment of others, in order to bring the two into agreement. There is likewise a pull, as it were, to halt every human soul on the path of knowledge when it seems to be following a different footpath from the one we have taken. All this is perhaps a felt dependence of our own judgments upon the universal human understanding, and becomes a means of procuring for the whole of thinking beings a kind of unity of reason.",["Restated as three sentences in place of two long ones; the dash-free structure of the second is otherwise kept.","\"Erkenntnißwege\" is rendered \"path of knowledge\"; \"Gemüth\" as \"mind\"; \"Verstand\" as \"understanding\".","\"von uns selbst\" is taken with \"zur Vollendung des unsrigen\" ('completion of our own judgment'), which is the reading the argument seems to require."]],"traeume-0058":["I pass over this consideration, which is otherwise not unimportant, and for the present keep to another, which is more illuminating and more weighty so far as our purpose is concerned. When we relate external things to our need, we cannot do so without at the same time feeling ourselves bound and restricted by a certain sensation. This lets us notice that a foreign will is, as it were, operative in us, and that our own good pleasure requires the condition of outside assent. A secret power constrains us at the same time to direct our purpose to the welfare of others, or in accordance with another's will, although this often happens unwillingly and in strong conflict with self-interested inclination. The point at which the directing lines of our impulses converge is therefore not",["Broken off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage. Translated as it stands.","\"Empfindung\" = sensation; \"Willkür\" in \"fremde Willkür\" is rendered \"will\" (\"another's will\") for lack of a better English; \"eigen Belieben\" as \"own good pleasure\".","The long second sentence is divided in two."]],"traeume-0059":["merely in us; there are also forces that move us in the willing",["Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0060":["of others outside us. Hence arise the moral impulses, which often",["Fragment."]],"traeume-0061":["carry us away against the thanks of self-interest: the strong law of obligation [*Schuldigkeit*] and the weaker law of benevolence [*Gütigkeit*], each of which exacts from us some sacrifice;",["The OCR hyphenation \"Schul— digkeit\" is corrected. \"wider den Dank des Eigennutzes\" is rendered literally as \"against the thanks of self-interest\", i.e. against what self-interest would give thanks for; the sense is doubtful and the literal wording is kept.","The sentence continues into the next passage."]],"traeume-0062":["and although both are now and then outweighed by self-interested",["Fragment; the sentence is continued in the next passage."]],"traeume-0063":["inclinations, yet nowhere in human nature",["Fragment."]],"traeume-0064":["do they fail to manifest their reality. Thereby we see ourselves, in our most secret motives, dependent upon the rule of the universal will, and there arises from this, in the world of all thinking natures, a moral unity and systematic constitution according to",["\"allge— meinen\" corrected to \"allgemeinen\". The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0065":["purely spiritual laws. If this constraint of our will, felt in us, to agree with the universal will is to be called the moral feeling,",["Fragment; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0066":["then we speak of it only as of an appearance [*Erscheinung*] of that which actually goes on in us, without determining its causes. So Newton called the sure law of the endeavour of all matter to approach other matter its gravitation. He did not wish to entangle his mathematical demonstrations in a vexatious participation in the philosophical disputes that might arise over its cause. Nevertheless he had no scruple in treating this gravitation as a true effect of a universal activity of matter upon matter, and he therefore also gave it the name of attraction.\n\nShould it not be possible to represent the appearance of the moral impulses in thinking natures, as they relate reciprocally to one another, likewise as the consequence of a truly active force, by which spiritual natures flow into one another? The moral feeling would then be this felt dependence of the private will upon the universal will, and a consequence of the natural and universal reciprocal action [*Wechselwirkung*] by which the immaterial world attains its moral unity, forming itself, according to the laws of this connection peculiar to it, into a system of spiritual perfection. If one grants these thoughts as much plausibility as is requisite to make them worth the trouble of testing by their consequences, one will perhaps be unawares drawn, by their charm, into some partiality towards them. For in this case the irregularities seem for the most part to vanish which otherwise strike the eye so strangely in the contradiction between the moral and the physical relations of men here on earth.\n\nIn the order of nature the whole moral worth of actions can never have its complete effect in the bodily life of man, though it can in the spirit-world according to pneumatic laws. The true intentions, the secret motives of many endeavours fruitless through impotence, the victory over oneself, or sometimes also the concealed flaw in apparently good actions, are for the most part lost so far as physical success in the bodily state is concerned. In that other manner of viewing it, however, they would have to be regarded in the immaterial world as fruitful grounds. In respect of them, according to pneumatic laws, in consequence of the heartfelt [*Herzknüpfung*] union of the private will and the universal will, that is, of the unity and the whole of the spirit-world, they would exert an effect corresponding to the moral quality of the free choice [*Willkür*], or would also, on the other side, receive one. For since the moral element of the deed concerns the inner state of the spirit, it can naturally draw after it an effect adequate to the whole of its morality only in the immediate community of spirits.\n\nThe result would be that the soul of man, even in this life, must take its place among the spiritual substances of the universe in accordance with its moral state, just as, by the laws of motion, the matters of cosmic space set themselves in relation to one another in an order that accords with their bodily forces [footnote marker in the text]. When at last the soul's community with the corporeal world is dissolved by death, life in the other world would be only a natural continuation of that connection in which it had already stood with that world in this life; and the total consequences",["Several long sentences are divided. The OCR garble \"Erscheinung ` essen\" is read as \"Erscheinung dessen\"; \"cm- | pfangen\" as \"empfangen\"; \"unmittel⸗ us bären\" as \"unmittelbaren\"; \"Herz i| knüpfung\" as \"Herzknüpfung\" (probably \"Herzverknüpfung\"; reading doubtful), which I render \"heartfelt union\" loosely.","\"die Ordnung der Natur\" and \"in der andern Weise\": the words \"in that other manner of viewing it\" render \"auf solche Weise\" ('in such a way', i.e. on the hypothesis proposed).","\"sich) 2 in solche Ordnung … ist\")\": a footnote marker appears in the OCR, but the footnote text is not present and is not supplied.","\"Willkür\" = choice (free choice); \"Wechselwirkung\" = reciprocal action; \"pneumatisch\" kept as \"pneumatic\".","The passage ends mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0067":["of the morality here exercised would there be found again, in the effects which a being standing in indissoluble community with the whole spirit-world has already exercised there beforehand, according to pneumatic laws. Present and future would thus be, as it were, of one piece and constitute a continuous whole, even according to the Ord-",["The OCR opening \"à Fines\" is garbled and is taken to be the remnant of the preceding \"Folgen\"; I have not translated it separately.","Breaks off mid-word (\"Ord⸗\", presumably \"Ordnung\"); translated as it stands without completion."]],"traeume-0068":["Se",["Only the two letters \"Se\" survive, probably an OCR remnant (e.g. a signature or running-head mark). Translated as it stands; no reading is supplied."]],"traeume-0069":["The reciprocal actions between man and the world of spirits, arising from the ground of morality according to the laws of pneumatic influence, might be placed in this: that there naturally springs from them a closer communion of a good or an evil soul with good and evil spirits. Souls would thereby associate themselves with that part of the spiritual republic which accords with their moral constitution, and would share in all the consequences which may arise from this in the order of nature. This last circumstance is of particular importance. For in a conjecture resting on mere grounds of reason it is a great difficulty if, in order to remove the defect arising in this world from the imperfect harmony between morality and its consequences, one must have recourse to an extraordinary divine will. For however probable the judgment upon such a will may be according to our concepts of divine wisdom,",["The initial ')' is a stray remnant (footnote or bracket marker) in the OCR and is omitted. The repeated 'nung der Natur.' after 'Ordnung der Natur' is a line-break duplication and is omitted.","Silently corrected: 'pueumatischen' to 'pneumatischen', 'Übelftand' to 'Übelstand'. Line numbers are removed.","I divided the long first sentence into two. 'jene' is resolved as the souls (good or evil), the more natural antecedent, and a supplied 'Souls' stands in the English."]],"traeume-0070":["there always remains a strong suspicion that the feeble concepts of our",["Fragment of a sentence; it continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0071":["understanding may perhaps have been transferred, quite perversely, to the Most High, since it is man's duty only to judge of the divine will",["The OCR 'nr—' (with the hyphenation) is taken as the beginning of 'urtheilen', which is completed in the next passage ('zu urtheilen'). The sentence continues."]],"traeume-0072":["from the well-adjusted order [*Wohlgereimtheit*] which he actually perceives in the world,",["'Wohlgereimtheit' (literally, 'being well rhymed together') is rendered 'well-adjusted order' as a matter of judgment."]],"traeume-0073":["or which, following the rule of analogy in accordance with the order of nature, he may presume to be there,",[]],"traeume-0074":["and that he is not entitled to devise, according to the plan of his own wisdom, which he at the same time makes a prescription for the divine will,",["The German construction (nicht aber … befugt ist) is smoothed. 'befugt' is rendered 'entitled'. The sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"traeume-0075":["new and arbitrary arrangements in the present or in a future world.",[]],"traeume-0076":["We now turn our reflection back into the former path, and approach the goal which we had set ourselves. If matters stand with the world of spirits, and with the share which our soul has in it, as the outline we gave represents them, then almost nothing seems more strange than that communion with spirits is not an entirely universal and ordinary thing. What is extraordinary in it concerns almost more the rarity of the appearances than the",["'Erscheinungen' is rendered 'appearances'. The sentence is completed in the next passage ('possibility of them')."]],"traeume-0077":["possibility of them. This difficulty can, however, be removed fairly well, and has in part already been removed. For the representation",["The line number ('s') is removed. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0078":["which the soul of man has of itself as a spirit, through an immaterial intuiting [*Anschauen*], in that it regards itself in relation to beings",["'Anschauen' is the verbal noun and is rendered 'intuiting' to keep it distinct from 'Anschauung' (intuition)."]],"traeume-0079":["of a similar nature, is wholly different from that [representation] which arises when its",["'derjenigen' is resolved as 'that [representation]', with the noun supplied in square brackets."]],"traeume-0080":["consciousness represents itself as a man, through an image which has its origin in the impression made upon bodily organs, and which is represented only in relation to material things. There is accordingly one and the same subject that belongs at once, as a member, to the visible and the invisible world, but not just the same person. For the representations of the one world, because of their different constitution, are not accompanying ideas of those of the other. Hence what I think as spirit is not remembered by me as man, and conversely my state as a man does not enter at all into the representation of myself as a spirit. Moreover, the representations of the world of spirits may be as clear and intuitive as one pleases,*) yet this is not sufficient to make me, as man, conscious of them. Even the representation of oneself (that is, of the soul) as a spirit is indeed acquired through inferences, but is in no man an intuitive concept or concept of experience.",["The '*)' after 'will' stands for a footnote marker, whose text is not in the passage; I keep the marker and give no footnote. The sentence beginning 'Es ist demnach' is divided and 'weil' is turned into a separate sentence.","'Begleitende Ideen' is rendered 'accompanying ideas'; 'Erfahrungsbegriff' is rendered 'concept of experience'. 'Vorstellung' is 'representation' throughout."]],"traeume-0081":["This dissimilarity between spiritual representations and those which belong to the bodily life of man must not, however, be regarded as so great an obstacle that it removes all possibility of becoming conscious, at times, of influences from the side of the world of spirits, even in this life. For these can pass into the personal consciousness of man, not indeed immediately, but yet in such a way that, according to the law of associated concepts, they rouse those images",["The passage breaks off at 'rege' (presumably 'rege machen', 'rouse'); I render 'rouse' and do not complete the sentence further."]],"traeume-0082":["*) This can be illustrated by a certain kind of twofold personality which belongs to the soul itself in respect of this life. Certain philosophers believe that they may appeal to the state of deep sleep, without the least fear of objection, when they wish to prove the actuality of obscure representations. Yet nothing further can be said of it with certainty than that, when awake, we remember none of those which we may perhaps have had in deep sleep. From this it follows only that they were not clearly represented upon waking, not that they were obscure at the time when we slept. I conjecture, on the contrary, that they may be clearer and more extensive than even the clearest in waking. For this is what we may expect of so active a being as the soul, in the complete repose of the outer senses. Nevertheless, since the body of the man is at that time not sensed along with them, the accompanying idea of it is lacking upon waking, and it is this idea which could help the former state of thoughts to consciousness as belonging to the same person. The actions of certain sleepwalkers, who at times show more understanding in this state than they otherwise do, although they remember nothing of it on waking, confirm the possibility of what I conjecture about deep sleep. Dreams, on the other hand, that is, the representations of the sleeper which he remembers on waking, do not belong here. For in that case the man is not completely asleep; he senses clearly to a certain degree, and weaves the activities of his mind into the impressions of the outer senses. Hence he afterwards remembers them in part, but also finds in them nothing but wild and absurd chimeras, as indeed they must necessarily be, since ideas of fantasy and those of outer sensation are thrown together in them. [Text broken off; the passage resumes in mid-sentence:] ... make, which are akin to them and awaken analogical representations of our senses. These are indeed not the spiritual concept itself, but are nevertheless symbols of such concepts. For it is always one and the same substance that belongs, as a member, to this world as well as to the other, and both kinds of representations belong to the same subject and are connected with one another. We can make the possibility of this to some extent comprehensible if we consider how our higher concepts of reason, which come fairly close to the spiritual, commonly take on, as it were, a bodily dress in order to make themselves clear. Hence the moral attributes of the Godhead are represented under the representations of anger, jealousy, mercy, vengeance, and the like. Hence poets personify the virtues, vices, or other properties of nature, yet in such a way that the true idea of the understanding shines through. So the geometer represents time by a line, although space and time agree only in their relations, and thus coincide with one another by analogy, never in quality. Hence the representation of divine eternity, even among philosophers, takes on the semblance of an infinite time, however carefully one guards against confusing the two. And a chief reason why mathematicians are commonly disinclined to admit the Leibnizian monads is probably this, that they cannot help picturing them to themselves as little lumps. Hence it is not improbable that spiritual sensations might pass over into consciousness if they excite fantasies akin to them. In this way ideas communicated through a spiritual influence would clothe themselves in the signs of the language which the man otherwise uses: the sensed presence of a spirit in the image of a human figure, the order and beauty of the immaterial world in fantasies which otherwise delight our senses in life, and so on.",["The German breaks off after 'untereinander geworfen werden' and resumes with 'machen, die mit ihnen verwandt sind'; the OCR has evidently lost text at a page break. I have translated what stands and marked the join in square brackets without supplying the missing words.","Restated the long opening sentences as several shorter ones (principle 4). 'Idee' and 'Vorstellung' are both rendered as in the German: 'idea' and 'representation'.","'deren Symbolen': the plural refers back to the spiritual concepts, so I read 'symbols of such concepts'.","'Geometra' = 'geometer'. Stray line numbers in the OCR have been omitted."]],"traeume-0083":["*) By this I understand not the organs of outer sensation, but the *sensorium* of the soul, as it is called, that is, that part of the brain whose motion is accustomed to accompany the manifold images and representations of the thinking soul, as the philosophers hold.",["Footnote to the word 'Organen' in the next passage; retained as a footnote."]],"traeume-0084":["Yet this kind of appearances [Erscheinungen] cannot be anything common or ordinary. It could occur only in persons whose organs*) have an unusually great irritability, such that, through harmonious motion, they strengthen the images of fantasy, in accordance with the inner state of the soul, more than ordinarily happens, and than ought to happen, in healthy men. Such peculiar persons would at certain moments be beset by the apparency [Apparenz] of certain objects as outside them. They would take these for a presence of spiritual natures impinging upon their bodily senses. In this there is, however, only an illusion of the imagination, though in such a way that its cause is a genuine spiritual influence. This cannot be sensed immediately, but reveals itself to consciousness only through akin images of fantasy, which take on the semblance of sensations.",["OCR 'sich)' before 'nur' read as a stray mark; the footnote sign after 'Organen' refers to the preceding footnote.","'Apparenz' kept as 'apparency' to preserve the distinction from 'Erscheinung' (appearance).","Divided the last sentence in two for clarity."]],"traeume-0085":["Notions acquired through upbringing, or various delusions that have crept in from other sources, would play their part here. Delusion is intermingled with truth, and a real spiritual sensation does indeed lie at the foundation, but has been transformed into shadow-images of sensible things. It will, however, also be admitted that a property of this kind, by which the impressions of the spirit-world are unfolded in this life into clear intuition, can hardly be of any use. For the spiritual sensation is necessarily woven so closely into the brain-phantom of the imagination that it must be impossible to distinguish in it what is true from the gross illusions that surround it. Likewise such a state, since it presupposes an altered equilibrium in the nerves, which are thrown into unnatural motion even by the activity of the soul in its purely spiritual sensing, would indicate a real illness. Finally, it would not be at all surprising to find in a ghost-seer a fantast [*Phantasten*] as well, at least in respect of the images accompanying these appearances of his. For representations that are by their nature foreign, and incompatible with those belonging to the bodily state of man, force themselves forward and draw ill-matched images into outer sensation. Wild chimeras and strange grotesques are thereby hatched, which in a long train delude the deceived senses, although they may have a true spiritual influence as their ground.",["OCR reads 'einen Phantasien'; I read 'einen Phantasten' (a fantast) and flag the reading as conjectural.","'Erziehungsbegriffe' rendered 'notions acquired through upbringing'. 'Eigenschaft' rendered 'property'. 'Anschauen' rendered 'intuition' per the fixed terms, though Kant here uses the verbal noun in a loose sense.","'Gezschleppe' in the OCR taken as 'Geschleppe' ('train'); the stray 'z' is a line marker."]],"traeume-0086":["Now one cannot be at a loss to give plausible rational grounds for the ghost stories which so often cross the philosopher's path, and likewise for all manner of influences of spirits that are talked of here and there. Departed souls and pure spirits can",["The sentence breaks off and is continued in the next passage ('zwar niemals ...'); translated as it stands.","OCR 'imqgleichen' corrected to 'imgleichen'."]],"traeume-0087":["indeed never be present to our outer senses, nor otherwise stand in community with matter. But they can well act upon the spirit of man, who belongs with them to one great republic. The representations which they awaken in him then clothe themselves, according to the law of his fantasy, in akin images, and excite the apparency [Apparenz] of the objects corresponding to them as outside him. This deception may affect any sense, and however much it might be intermingled with absurd brain-phantoms, one should not allow this to prevent one from suspecting spiritual influences in it. I should be doing injury to the reader's acuteness were I to dwell any longer on the application of this mode of explanation. For metaphysical hypotheses have so extraordinary a pliancy that one would have to be very clumsy not to be able to accommodate the present one to any story whatever, even before one has examined its truthfulness. In many cases this is impossible, and in still more it is very impolite.",["Continues the sentence from the previous passage ('Abgeschiedene Seelen und reine Geister können ... zwar niemals ...').","OCR 'Wen— schen' corrected to 'Menschen'; line numbers and hyphenation breaks omitted.","'Apparenz' kept as 'apparency'; 'unhöflich' rendered 'impolite'."]],"traeume-0088":["If, however, we reckon the advantages and disadvantages against one another that may accrue to a man who is organised to some degree not only for the visible world but also for the invisible (supposing there ever has been such a man), a gift of this kind appears to resemble the one with which Juno honoured Tiresias: she first made him blind, in order that she might bestow on him the gift of prophecy. For, judging by the propositions above, intuitive acquaintance (*anschauende Kenntniß*) with the other world can be attained here only by forfeiting some of that understanding which one requires for the present world. Nor do I know whether even certain philosophers are wholly free from this hard condition, those who so diligently and profoundly direct their metaphysical glasses towards those remote regions, and know how to relate marvels from there. At least I grudge them none of their discoveries. I only fear that some man of good understanding and little refinement may give them to understand precisely what Tycho de Brahe's coachman answered when his master thought he could take the shortest way by night by the stars: 'Good sir, you may well understand the heavens, but here on earth you are a fool.'",["Restated the long first sentence of the German as three sentences in English, splitting at 'at least I grudge them…' (the semicolon).","OCR 'id)' read as 'ich'; 'irgend : ein' read as 'irgend ein'; 'ver— stehen' read as 'verstehen'.","*Kenntniß* rendered 'acquaintance', not 'knowledge', which is reserved for *Erkenntnis*; *Verstand* = understanding, which in the Tiresias comparison also carries the sense of ordinary common sense.","'Feinigkeit' rendered 'refinement'; 'seinem Kutscher' is read as 'his master's coachman', the antecedent of 'jener' being Tycho."]],"traeume-0089":["Third Chapter.",["*Hauptstück* rendered 'Chapter'."]],"traeume-0090":["Antikabbala. A Fragment of Common Philosophy,",["The heading continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0091":["to Abolish Communion with the World of Spirits.",["Continues the heading of the preceding passage."]],"traeume-0092":["Aristotle says somewhere: When we are awake we have a common world, but when we dream each man has his own. It seems to me that the latter proposition might well be reversed, and we might say: if each of several men has a world of his own, it is to be presumed that they are dreaming. On this footing, let us consider the aerial architects of the various worlds of thought, each of whom dwells quietly in his own world to the exclusion of the others. Take, for instance, him who inhabits the order of things as Wolff constructed it out of a little building-material of experience but more of surreptitiously acquired concepts, or him who inhabits the order that Crusius brought forth out of nothing, by the magical power of a few sayings about the thinkable and the unthinkable. We shall then be patient with the contradiction among their visions,",["Divided the German into several sentences at 'Auf diesen Fuß…'; the final clause breaks off, and the German sentence continues in the next passage.","'Größenlehrer' etc. appear in the next passage; *erschlichen* rendered 'surreptitiously acquired'.","OCR page/line markers (|f, ı/F) and 'be— trachten', 'her— vorgebracht' silently corrected or omitted; 'Satz' is read for the OCR 'Sag'.","'Aristoteles sagt irgendwo' is Kant's own vague citation; I add no reference."]],"traeume-0093":["until these gentlemen have finished dreaming. For when once, God willing, they are fully awake, that is, open their eyes to a view that does not exclude agreement with the understanding of other men, none of them will see anything that would not appear to everyone else, by the light of their proofs, equally evident and certain. And philosophers will at that time inhabit a common world, such as the mathematicians (*Größenlehrer*) have long possessed. This important event cannot be long delayed, if we may trust certain signs and portents which have for some time been appearing above the horizon of the sciences, and are",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage; the final sentence breaks off at 'sind' and is completed in the next passage ('neu sind').","Divided one long German sentence into three; 'Menschenverstand' rendered 'the understanding of other men', *Beweisthümer* 'proofs'.","OCR 'ar' and 'erschie⸗ to neu' joined as 'erschienen neu': the next passage supplies 'neu sind'. 'Größenlehrer' (literally 'teachers of magnitudes') = mathematicians."]],"traeume-0094":["new.",["Completes the sentence of the preceding passage; the OCR fragment 'to' belongs to the word 'erschienen' split across lines, not to a separate word."]],"traeume-0095":["Akin in a certain way to the dreamers of reason are the dreamers of sensation (*Empfindung*), and among these are commonly reckoned those who now and then have dealings with spirits, and that on the same ground as the former, namely because they",["Breaks off mid-sentence; completed in the next passage ('sehen, was kein anderer…').","*Empfindung* rendered 'sensation'."]],"traeume-0096":["see something which no other sound man sees, and have their own communion with beings who reveal themselves to no one else, however good his senses may be. The name of dreams is also apt, if one supposes that the appearances in question come to no more than mere phantoms of the brain, in so far as the one kind as much as the other are self-hatched images which none the less deceive the senses as though they were real objects. But if one imagines that the two deceptions are, in the manner of their origin, sufficiently alike for the source of the one to be found adequate to explain the other, one is greatly mistaken. He who, while awake, so deeply immerses himself in fictions and chimeras, which his ever fertile imagination hatches, that he pays little heed to the sensation of the senses, which at the moment most concern him, is rightly called a waking dreamer. For the sensations of the senses need only diminish somewhat further in strength, and he will be asleep, and the former chimeras will be true dreams. The reason why they are not already such while he is awake is this: at that time he represents them as in himself, but the other objects which he senses as outside himself; consequently he reckons the former among the effects of his own activity, the latter among what he receives and suffers from without. For everything here depends on the relation in which the objects are thought in regard to himself as a man, and so also in regard to his body. Hence the same images may, while he is awake, occupy him greatly, but cannot deceive him, however clear they may be. For although he then also has a representation of himself and his body in the brain, to which he relates his fantastic images, yet the actual sensation of his body through the outer senses makes a contrast, or sharp distinction, with those chimeras, so that he regards the one as hatched by himself and the other as sensed. If he falls asleep in this state, the sensed representation of his body is extinguished, and there remains only the self-fashioned one, in relation to which the other chimeras are thought as in an external relation, and which, so long as one sleeps, must also deceive the dreamer, because there is no sensation which, by comparison with that representation, would allow the original to be distinguished from the shadow-image, that is, the outer from the inner.",["Continues the sentence begun in the preceding passage. Divided some long German sentences, e.g. at 'Denn es dürfen nur…', and before 'Daher können…'.","The leading OCR numeral '5' (line number) is omitted; 'Hirn— gespenster', 'wirk— liebe' (wirkliche), 'Chi— maären' and 'sich)' silently corrected.","*Vorstellung* = representation; *Gegenstand* = object; 'Urbild vom Schattenbilde' rendered 'original from the shadow-image'.","The antecedent of 'sie' in 'weil er sie zu der Zeit als in sich…' is the chimeras; the final 'jener' is taken as the self-fashioned representation of the body."]],"traeume-0097":["From waking dreamers, therefore, ghost-seers are entirely distinguished, not merely in degree, but in kind. For the latter, while awake, and often when other sensations are at their most vivid, refer certain objects to the external places of the other things which they actually perceive about them. The question here is only how it comes about that they transfer the illusion of their imagination outside themselves, and indeed in relation to their body, which they also sense through the outer senses. The great clearness of their phantom cannot be the cause of this, for what matters here is the place to which it is transferred as an object. I therefore require that it be shown how the soul takes such an image, which it ought to represent as contained in itself, and transfers it to a quite different relation, namely to a place externally, among the objects that present themselves to its actual sensation. Nor shall I let myself be put off by the citation of other cases which bear some resemblance to such a deception and may occur, say, in a feverish state. For whether the state of the deceived person be healthy or sick, we do not wish to know whether such things happen elsewhere, but how this deception is possible.",["Divided the second German sentence into two ('Die Frage ist…' and 'Die große Klarheit…'); 'Gegenstand' = object, 'Empfindung' = sensation.","'refe— riren' read as *referiren*, rendered 'refer'; 'Hirngespinstes' rendered 'phantom'; 'Seele' = soul.","'sich ihrer wirklichen Empfindung darbieten' rendered 'present themselves to its actual sensation', the soul being the antecedent of 'ihrer'."]],"traeume-0098":["But we find, in the use of the outer senses, that besides the clearness with which objects are represented, we also apprehend in sensation their place. This is perhaps not always done with equal correctness, yet it is a necessary condition of sensation, without which it would be impossible to represent things as outside us. It thus becomes very probable that our soul transposes the sensed object, in its representation, to the point where the various lines of direction of the impression which the object has made meet, when they are produced. Hence a radiant point is seen at the place where the lines drawn back from the eye, in the direction in which the rays of light fall, intersect. This point, which is entitled the point of vision, is indeed in its effect the point of dispersion, but in representation it is the point of convergence of the lines of direction along which the sensation is impressed (*focus imaginarius*). So it is that, even with a single eye, we determine the place of a visible object. This happens, among other cases, when the image of a body is seen in the air by means of a concave mirror. It is seen precisely where the rays which issue from a point of the object intersect, before they fall into the eye.",["Restated the first sentence in two ('But we find… place. This is perhaps… yet…') to untangle the concessive clause 'vielleicht bisweilen nicht allemal'.","'Sehepunkt' rendered 'point of vision'; 'Spectrum' rendered 'image' for clarity (Kant's term is the optical *spectrum*).","OCR 'ineiden' corrected to 'schneiden'; the stray closing quotation mark and parenthesis at the end belong to the apparatus of the preceding note and are not translated."]],"traeume-0099":["Thus the judgment which we form concerning the apparent place of near objects is commonly presented in optics [*Sehekunst*], and it agrees very well with experience. Yet these same rays of light, which issue from one point, do not, by virtue of the refraction in the humours of the eye, strike the optic nerve as divergent, but unite there in one point. Hence, if the sensation takes place solely in this nerve, the *focus imaginarius* would have to be placed not outside the body but at the bottom of the eye, which a",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('welches eine'); left unfinished. The continuation ('…Schwierigkeit macht…') appears, garbled, at the end of the following passage (traeume-0101).","The opening ')' is a remnant of the apparatus of the preceding passage and is not translated.","'Sehekunst' rendered 'optics' with the German in brackets."]],"traeume-0100":["Perhaps one may likewise assume, in the case of impressions of sound, since its impulses also occur along straight lines, that the sensation of it is accompanied at the same time by the representation of a *focus imaginarius*. This is placed at the point where the straight lines of the nervous structure set in vibration meet, when they are produced outwards from the brain. For we notice to some extent the region and distance of a sounding object, even when the sound is soft and occurs behind us. This is so although the straight lines which can be drawn from there do not strike the opening of the ear, but fall upon other parts of the head. We must therefore believe that the lines of direction of the concussion are produced outwards in the soul's representation, and that the sounding object is transposed to the point where they meet. The same, as it seems to me, may be said of the remaining three senses. They differ from sight and hearing in this, that the object of sensation stands in immediate contact with the organs, and the lines of direction of the sensory stimulus therefore have their point of union in these organs themselves.",["Divided the long second sentence for clarity.","'in Hebung gesetzten' rendered 'set in vibration' (*Hebung*, literally a lifting or heaving); a choice.","'im Gehirne äußerlich fortgezogen' is ambiguous (produced outwards *in* or *from* the brain); I take it as 'from the brain'."]],"traeume-0101":["To apply this to the images of the imagination, let me be permitted to lay down as a basis what Descartes assumed and most philosophers after him approved: namely, that all representations of the imagination are accompanied at the same time by certain movements in the nervous tissue or nervous spirit of the brain, which are entitled *ideae materiales*. That is, they are perhaps accompanied by the concussion or vibration of the fine element which is secreted from the nerves, and this is similar to the movement which the sensible impression, of which it is the copy, could produce. Now I ask to be granted further that the chief difference between the nervous movement in fancies and that in sensation consists in this: in the former the lines of direction of the movement intersect inside the brain, in the latter outside it. Hence, since the *focus imaginarius* in which the object is represented is placed outside me in the clear sensations of waking, whereas that of the fancies which I may have at the time is placed in me, I cannot, so long as I am awake, fail to distinguish imaginings, as my own brain-spinnings, from the impression of the senses. [OCR garble] … makes a difficulty which I cannot now resolve, and which seems incompatible both with the above propositions and with experience.",["'ideas materiales' is printed so in the source; given as Latin 'ideae materiales' in italics (an expansion of the printed form).","'seinen Elements' read as 'feinen Elements' (OCR); 'welches von ihnen abgesondert wird' taken to refer to the nerves.","The final fragment ('Schwierigkeit macht…') is evidently the continuation of the broken-off sentence of traeume-0099, misplaced here; it is preceded by unreadable OCR ('ERO ULLA j'), which I mark '[OCR garble]', and followed by a stray '5' (line number), omitted. Its opening is cut off, so I begin with an ellipsis.","'Hirngespinste' rendered literally as 'brain-spinnings' to keep Kant's pun on spinning."]],"traeume-0102":["*) One might cite, as a remote resemblance to the accident mentioned, the condition of drunken persons, who in this state see double with both eyes. The reason is that the swelling of the blood-vessels produces an obstacle to directing the axes of the eyes so that their produced lines intersect in the point where the object is. In the same way, the distortion of the vessels of the brain, which is perhaps only transitory and, so long as it lasts, affects only some nerves, may serve to make certain images of the fancy appear as outside us even in waking. A very common experience can be compared with this illusion. When, after sleep, with an ease that approaches drowsiness, and as it were with dulled eyes, one looks at the various threads of the bed-curtains [*Heit-/Bettvorhänge*?] or of the covering, or at the small spots on a near wall, one easily makes out of them figures of human faces and the like. The delusion ceases as soon as one wishes it to and exerts one's attention. Here the transposition of the *focus imaginarius* of the fancies is to some extent subject to choice, since, in the displacement, it can be hindered by one's choice.",["Kept as a footnote (marked '*)').","'Heit— vorhänge' is doubtful OCR; probably 'Bettvorhänge' (bed-curtains), which I translate and flag in brackets.","'ers scheinen' corrected to 'erscheinen'.","'mit gebrochnen Augen' rendered 'with dulled eyes' (half-closed, unfocused); a judgment of sense.","The last clause ('durch seine Willkür') has an odd 'seine'; rendered 'one's choice'. Divided the long second sentence into two."]],"traeume-0103":["If this is granted, it seems to me that I can offer something intelligible as the cause of that kind of disturbance of the mind which is called madness (*Wahnsinn*) and, in a higher degree, derangement (*Verrückung*). The peculiarity of this sickness consists in the following: the deranged man transfers mere objects of his imagination to a place outside himself, and regards them as things actually present before him. Now I have said that, in the ordinary order of things, the lines of direction of the motions which accompany the phantasy in the brain, as its material aids, must intersect within the brain; and that the place in which he is conscious of his image is therefore, in waking life, thought to be within himself. Suppose, then, that by some accident or sickness certain organs of the brain are so distorted and thrown out of their proper equilibrium that the motion of the nerves, which vibrate in harmony with certain phantasies, takes place along lines of direction which, if prolonged, would intersect outside the brain. The *focus imaginarius* is then posited outside the thinking subject.*) The image, which is a mere work of the imagination, is represented as an object that would be present to the outer senses.\n\nThe consternation at the supposed appearance of a thing which, according to the natural order, ought not to be present will soon arouse attention, even if such a shadow-image of the phantasy was at first only faint. It will give the sham sensation (*Scheinempfindung*) so great a vividness that the deceived man cannot doubt its truth. This deception may affect any outer sense, for of each of them we have copied images in the imagination, and the derangement of the nervous tissue may become the cause of transferring the *focus imaginarius* to the place from which the sensible impression of a really present bodily object would come.\n\nIt is then no wonder if the visionary believes that he sees or hears very distinctly much that no one else perceives. Nor is it a wonder that these phantoms of the brain appear to him and suddenly vanish; or that, since they delude one sense only (the sight, for example), they cannot be sensed through any other (the feeling, for example), and therefore seem to be penetrable. The common stories of ghosts run so much upon determinations of this kind that they uncommonly justify the suspicion that they may well have sprung from such a source. And so the current concept of spiritual beings, which we developed above out of ordinary usage, is very much in accord with this deception and does not disown its origin; for the property of a penetrable presence in space is supposed to constitute the essential mark of this concept.",["OCR corrections: 'dec gleiten' = *begleiten*; 'harmonisch heben' = *beben* (vibrate); 'kocus' = *focus*; 'gefebt' = *gesetzt*; 'Werkmal' = *Merkmal*; 'Täusehung' = *Täuschung*; 'fann' = *kann*.","The footnote marker *) is retained after 'subject'; the footnote text is not part of this passage.","Restated the long sentence beginning 'Wenn ich also setze' as two sentences, and divided the later long sentences; 'Suppose, then, that' renders the conditional 'Wenn ich also setze, daß'.","*Wahnsinn* = madness, *Verrückung* = derangement; *Scheinempfindung* = 'sham sensation'; *Gemüt* = mind. *Focus imaginarius* kept in Latin, italicised.","'in ihm selbst' (within himself) is understood of the man, and the brain within him; 'er' is the deranged man."]],"traeume-0104":["It is also very probable that the notions of spectral forms acquired in education supply the diseased head with the materials for its deceptive imaginings, and that a brain empty of all such prejudices, even if some perversity should come over it, would hardly so easily hatch out images of this kind. Further, it is evident from this that, since the sickness of visionaries (*Phantasten*) properly concerns not the understanding but the deception of the senses, the unfortunate man cannot dispel his illusions by any ratiocination. For the sensation of the senses, whether true or apparent, precedes all judgment of the understanding, and has an immediate evidence which far surpasses every other persuasion.",["OCR: 'Krankheit des Phantasien' emended to *Phantasten* (visionaries).","*Vernünfteln* rendered 'ratiocination', with its disparaging sense of mere reasoning; *Verstand* = understanding."]],"traeume-0105":["The consequence which results from these considerations has this inconvenience, that it renders the profound conjectures of the previous chapter wholly dispensable. However willing the reader may be to grant some approval to its idealistic projects, he will nevertheless prefer the concept which brings with it greater ease and brevity in decision, and which can promise itself a more general approval. For, apart from the fact that it seems more in keeping with a reasonable way of thinking to take the grounds of explanation from the material which experience offers us than to lose oneself in giddy concepts of a reason that now invents, now infers, there is on this side some occasion for ridicule as well. Ridicule, whether justified or not, is a more powerful means than any other of checking vain inquiries. For to wish to offer serious interpretations of the phantoms of the visionaries' brains already arouses an unfavourable suspicion, and a philosophy which allows itself to be found in such bad company falls under suspicion.\n\nIt is true that above I did not dispute the presence of madness in appearances of this kind. On the contrary, I connected it with them, not indeed as the cause of an imagined community with spirits, but as a natural consequence of it. But what folly is there that could not be brought into agreement with a bottomless worldly wisdom? I therefore do not in the least blame the reader if, instead of regarding the visionaries as half-citizens of the other world, he summarily dismisses them as candidates for the hospital, and thereby spares himself all further inquiry.\n\nBut if everything is taken on this footing, the way of treating such adepts of the realm of spirits must also be very different from that required by the above concepts. Whereas it was formerly thought necessary to burn some of them, it will now be enough to purge them. In this state of affairs it would also not have been necessary to start so far back, and to seek out secrets, with the help of metaphysics, in the fevered brains of deluded enthusiasts. The keen-sighted Hudibras alone could have solved the riddle for us. In his opinion, when a hypochondriac wind rages in the bowels, everything depends on the direction it takes: if it goes downwards, it becomes a f—; but if it mounts upwards, it is an apparition or a holy inspiration.",["OCR corrections: 'sich)' = *sich*; 'Gejpötte' = *Gespötte*; 'Geifterseher' = *Geisterseher*; 'Radforschens' = *Nachforschens*; 'zu des handeln' = *zu behandeln*; 'Phantasien' (in 'Hirngespenster der Phantasien') read as *Phantasten*.","'todt' in the Hudibras sentence is read as *tobt* ('rages'); the German as printed would give 'is dead', which makes no sense here. Flagged as an emendation.","'F—' is left as printed; presumably *Furz*, but Kant's text does not spell it out.","Divided the long final sentences and the paragraph into three; 'the previous chapter' renders *des vorigen Hauptstücks*; *Weltweisheit* = 'worldly wisdom' (a term for philosophy).","Hudibras is the hero of Samuel Butler's satirical poem."]],"traeume-0106":["Chapter Four.",["*Hauptstück* rendered 'Chapter'."]],"traeume-0107":["Theoretical Conclusion from the Collected Considerations",["The heading continues in the next passage ('des ersten Theils'), so it is translated as it stands, without completing it."]],"traeume-0108":["of the First Part.",["Completes the heading begun in the previous passage: 'Theoretical Conclusion from the Collected Considerations of the First Part.'"]],"traeume-0109":["The deceptiveness of a balance which, under the civil laws, is to serve as a measure in trade [Handlung] is discovered when the goods and the weights are made to exchange pans. The partiality of the balance of the understanding is revealed by the very same device, and without it we can never obtain a uniform result from the comparison of the weighings, even in philosophical judgments. I have cleansed my soul of prejudices. I have eradicated every blind attachment that ever crept in to procure admittance for some imagined knowledge in me. Now nothing concerns me, nothing is worthy of respect, save what finds a place by the path of sincerity in a mind that is calm and open to all grounds. It may confirm or overthrow my former judgment, and it may determine me or leave me undecided. Wherever I meet with anything that instructs me, I appropriate it. The judgment of the man who refutes my grounds is my judgment, once I have first weighed it against the pan of self-love, and then, in that same pan, against my supposed grounds, and have found in it the greater content. Formerly I regarded the universal human understanding merely from the standpoint of my own. Now I put myself in the place of an alien and external reason, and observe my judgments, together with their most secret motives, from the point of view of others. The comparison of the two observations yields, it is true, marked parallaxes, but it is also the only means of guarding against optical illusion, and of setting the concepts in the true positions which they occupy in relation to the faculties of knowledge of human nature.\n\nIt will be said that this is a very serious language for so indifferent a task as the one we are treating, which deserves rather to be entitled a plaything than a serious occupation; and such a judgment is not unjust. But though no great preparation should be made for a trifle, it may very well be made on the occasion of one; and a scrupulousness in deciding trifles, which could be dispensed with, may serve as an example in important cases. I do not find that any attachment, or any other inclination that crept in before examination, deprives my mind of its pliability to all manner of grounds for or against, with a single exception. The balance of the understanding is not entirely impartial after all. One arm of it, which bears the inscription *Hope of the Future*, has a mechanical advantage, with the result that even light grounds falling into the pan belonging to it draw up the speculations on the other side, which are in themselves of greater weight. This is the one inaccuracy which I cannot well remove, and which in fact I never wish to remove.\n\nNow I confess that all the stories of the appearance of departed souls or of the influences of spirits, and all the theories of the presumable nature of spiritual beings and of their connection with us, weigh appreciably only in the pan of hope, whereas in that of speculation they seem to consist of nothing but air. If the determination of the question set us did not stand in sympathy with an inclination already decided beforehand, what reasonable man would be undecided whether he should find more possibility in assuming a kind of being which has nothing whatever in common with all that the senses teach him, than in ascribing some alleged experiences to self-deception and invention, which in a number of cases are not unusual?",["Silently corrected OCR breaks: 'Nr— theil' to Urtheil, 'Gelegen— heit', 'Er— dichtung'; 'Ähnliches. haben' read as 'Ähnliches haben'.","Restated as three paragraph-sized units of shorter sentences; the long sentence on the parallaxes and the closing question are kept whole.","'Handlung' rendered 'trade' and 'Maß' 'measure', as the context of weights and goods requires; 'Facit' = result; 'Gehalt' = content (i.e. weight).","'Erkenntnißvermögen' = faculties of knowledge; 'Verstandeswage' = balance of the understanding; 'Gemüth' = mind.","'ob man zwar... kann man sie doch gar wohl' smoothed into a 'though... yet' construction."]],"traeume-0110":["*) The symbol of the ancient Egyptians for the soul was a butterfly [Papillon], and the Greek name meant just the same. It is easily seen that the hope which makes of death only a transformation gave rise to such an idea together with its signs. This, however, in no way lessens confidence in the correctness of the concepts that have sprung from it. Our inner sensation, and the judgments of what resembles reason [des Vernunftähnlichen] which are based upon it, lead, so long as they are uncorrupted, just where reason would lead if it were more enlightened and more extensive.",["Footnote to the passage; the marker '*)' is kept.","'Vernunftähnlichen' (Latin *analogon rationis*) rendered 'what resembles reason'; 'Empfindung' = sensation.","'Unverderbt' read for OCR 'unver— derbt'."]],"traeume-0111":["Indeed this seems also, generally speaking, to be the chief cause of the credence given to stories of spirits, which find such universal acceptance. Even the first deceptions of supposed apparitions of departed men probably sprang from the flattering hope that in some way or other one still survives after death. Then, in the shadows of night, delusion often deceived the senses and made out of ambiguous shapes illusions that agreed with the opinion previously held; and from this, finally, philosophers took occasion to devise the rational idea of spirits and to bring it into a doctrinal system. It can also be seen well enough from my own presumptuous doctrine of the community of spirits that it takes the very same direction in which the common inclination strikes. For the propositions converge, quite noticeably, only upon this: to give a concept of how the spirit of man goes forth out of this world (*) that is, of the state after death. But how it comes into it, that is, of generation and propagation, I say nothing; indeed I do not even say how it is present in this world, that is, how an immaterial nature can be active in | a body and through it. All this for a very",["Breaks off in mid-sentence ('um einer sehr'); continued in the next passage, not completed here.","The OCR sign before 'd. i.' is read as the footnote marker and given as '(*)'; the note itself is not in this passage.","The sign | is carried over where it stands. 'Hoft— nung' corrected to Hoffnung; 'vereinbaren' (OCR 'verein— bären') rendered 'converge'.","Divided the long first sentence into several; 'Lehrverfassung' = doctrinal system, 'Lehrbegriff' = doctrine."]],"traeume-0112":["valid reason, which is this, that of all this together I",["Fragment of a sentence continuing from the previous passage and into the next."]],"traeume-0113":["understand nothing, and that consequently I might well have contented myself with being just as ignorant",[]],"traeume-0114":["in respect of the future state, had not the partiality of a favourite opinion commended to me the grounds that offered themselves, however weak",["The stray line number '5' omitted."]],"traeume-0115":["they might be.",[]],"traeume-0116":["This same ignorance also makes me not venture",["OCR 'C'en' read as 'Eben' ('Eben dieselbe').","'Unterstehe' rendered 'venture'."]],"traeume-0117":["to deny so entirely all truth in the various stories of spirits, though with the usual, if curious, reservation of calling in question every single one of them, while yet ascribing some credence to them all taken together.",["OCR 'abzuleug— neu' read as 'abzuleugnen'; stray 'o' omitted."]],"traeume-0118":["The reader remains free to judge;",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0119":["as for myself, at least the turn of the scale toward the grounds of the second chapter [Hauptstück] is great enough in me to keep me, on hearing the various strange stories of this kind,",["OCR 'An⸗ hörung' read as 'Anhörung', completed in the next passage."]],"traeume-0120":["serious and undecided. Meanwhile, since grounds of justification are never lacking when the mind is previously prepossessed, I shall",["Stray 's' omitted. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0121":["not trouble the reader with any further defence of this way of thinking.",[]],"traeume-0122":["Since I now find myself at the close of the theory of spirits, I venture to say further that this consideration, if it is properly utilised by the reader, [will remove] all philosophical insight into such",["Breaks off mid-sentence; not completed. The bracketed 'will remove' is supplied only so the English reads as a clause, in view of the German 'alle philosophische Einsicht von dergleichen' with its verb missing; it is a doubtful supply, since the verb is not in the passage.","Stray 'o' omitted."]],"traeume-0123":["is duly turned to account by the reader, all philosophical insight of such a kind",["The passage is a fragment, beginning and ending mid-sentence (apparently from the *Träume eines Geistersehers*); it is translated as it stands, without completion. The governing clause and the verb that follows 'insight' are missing.","'gehörig genutzt wird' is rendered 'is duly turned to account'; the subject (presumably something offered to the reader) cannot be determined from the fragment, so none is supplied.","'dergleichen' is rendered 'of such a kind'; its referent lies in the lost context and cannot be resolved here."]],"traeume-0124":["…*Wesen* [beings] complete, and that of them one may perhaps hereafter still hold all manner of opinions, but never again know anything. This pretension sounds rather boastful. For there is certainly no object of nature, known to the senses, of which one could say that one had ever exhausted it by observation or by reason, even if it were a drop of water, a grain of sand, or something still simpler. So immeasurable is the manifold which nature, even in its smallest parts, offers for analysis to so limited an understanding as the human. But the philosophical doctrine [*Lehrbegriff*] of spiritual beings is quite otherwise placed. It can be completed, but only in the negative sense. That is to say, it fixes with certainty the limits of our insight, and convinces us of the following. The various appearances of life in nature, and their laws, are all that we are permitted to know. The principle [*Principium*] of this life, however, that is, the spiritual nature, which is not known but [only] conjectured, can never be thought positively, since no *data* for this are to be found in the whole of our sensations. We must help ourselves with negations, in order to think something so greatly different from everything sensible. But even the possibility of such negations rests neither on experience nor on inference, but on a fiction, to which a reason stripped of all aids has recourse. On this footing the pneumatology of men may be entitled a doctrine of their necessary ignorance in regard to a conjectured kind of beings, and as such it may easily be adequate to its task.",["The passage opens mid-sentence; the governing clause is lost, so I have translated only what stands and supplied the bracketed gloss on *Wesen*.","I divided the long sentence beginning 'Er kann vollendet sein' into several sentences; the dependent clauses with 'daß' are set out as a list of what the doctrine convinces us of.","*Lehrbegriff* = 'doctrine'; *im negativen Verstande* = 'in the negative sense' (here *Verstand* means 'sense', not 'understanding'). *Verstande* in 'so eingeschränkten Verstande' is 'understanding'.","'[only]' supplied to bring out the contrast kennen/vermuthen; *Pneumatologie* kept as in the German."]],"traeume-0125":["er =",["A bare fragment, probably an OCR remnant; translated as it stands ('er' = 'he' or 'it'). Doubtful reading."]],"traeume-0126":["I now lay aside the whole matter of spirits, an extensive piece of metaphysics, as settled and completed. It no longer concerns me in the future. By thus drawing the plan of my inquiry more closely together, and by freeing myself from some wholly fruitless investigations, I hope to be able to employ my slight capacity of understanding more advantageously upon the remaining objects. For the most part it is vain to try to stretch the small measure of one's powers over every windy project. Hence prudence commands, in this as in other cases, that the cut of one's projects be made proportionate to one's powers | and, when one cannot properly attain what is great, that one confine oneself to what is moderate.",["Stray line-number marks in the OCR ('ı', '14') omitted; the sign | kept where it stands.","*Verstandesfähigkeit* = 'capacity of understanding'; *Kräfte* = 'powers'.","I rendered the second sentence of 'Es ist mehrentheils umsonst' with 'For' to mark its connection with the plan just described; the German has no particle."]],"traeume-0127":["Part Two, which is historical. First Chapter.",["*Hauptstück* rendered 'chapter'; headings translated as they stand."]],"traeume-0128":["A narrative, the truth of which is commended to the reader's own inquiry, as he may please.",["'En' corrected silently to 'Eine' and 'Erfundigung' to 'Erkundigung' (OCR). The title continues in the next passage."]],"traeume-0129":["[A narrative, the truth of which is commended to the reader's own inquiry, as he may please.] *Sit mihi fas audita loqui*. — — — [May it be granted me to speak of what I have heard.]",["The first sentence repeats the title from the preceding passage only because the German line 'des Lesers empfohlen wird' completes it; strictly the passage is 'des Lesers empfohlen wird' ('…of the reader is commended') plus the Virgil motto (*Aeneid* VI.266), kept in Latin with the English supplied in brackets."]],"traeume-0130":["VIRG.",["Abbreviation for Virgil, left as it stands."]],"traeume-0131":["Philosophy, whose conceit leaves it exposed to every idle question, often finds itself in sore embarrassment on the occasion of certain stories. It can neither doubt some part of them without punishment, nor believe much of them without being laughed at. Both inconveniences are found united to a certain degree in the ghost stories that go the rounds: the first in listening to the person who affirms them, the second in regard to those to whom one passes them on. In fact no reproach is more bitter to the philosopher than that of credulity and of surrender to common delusion. And since those who understand how to appear clever at small cost cast their scornful laughter upon everything that in a manner makes the ignorant and the wise equal, in that it is incomprehensible to both, it is no wonder that the appearances so commonly alleged find wide acceptance, but are in public either denied or at least concealed. One may therefore rely upon it that no Academy of Sciences will ever make this matter a prize question. This is not because its members are wholly free from all inclination towards the opinion in question, but because the rule of prudence rightly sets limits to the questions which idle curiosity and vain desire for knowledge raise without distinction. And so stories of this kind will probably always have only secret believers, while in public they will be rejected by the prevailing fashion of unbelief.",["OCR slips corrected silently: 'sich)' for 'sich', 'glau— den' for 'glauben'; 'gutes Kants' is read as 'guten Kaufs' ('at small cost'), a doubtful emendation.","I divided the first and the fourth sentences, which are long, into shorter ones.","*Erscheinungen* here means 'apparitions' as alleged by the ghost-seers, though I keep 'appearances' for the sake of the term; the sense is not the critical one.","*Wißbegierde* = 'desire for knowledge'."]],"traeume-0132":["Since, however, this whole question seems to me neither important nor sufficiently prepared | to decide anything about it, I have no scruple in setting down here a report of the kind mentioned, and in submitting it, with complete indifference, to the favourable or unfavourable judgment of readers.",["'Trage' read as 'Frage' (OCR); 'noch)' read 'noch'. The sign | kept where it stands.","'gnug' = 'genug', rendered 'sufficiently'."]],"traeume-0133":["There lives in Stockholm a certain Herr Schwedenberg, without office or employment, on his fairly considerable private means. His whole occupation consists in this, that, as he himself says, for more than twenty years he has been in the closest intercourse with spirits and departed souls, receives from them tidings of the other world and in return imparts to them tidings of the present, composes great volumes on his discoveries, and from time to time travels to London to see to their publication. He is by no means reticent about his secrets, speaks freely of them with everyone, and appears completely persuaded of what he alleges, without any appearance of deliberate deceit or charlatanry. As he is, if one may believe him himself, the arch-seer of spirits among all seers of spirits, so he is certainly also the arch-fantast among all fantasts, whether one judge him from the description of those who know him or from his writings. Yet this circumstance cannot hinder those who are otherwise favourable to the influences of spirits from suspecting something of truth behind such fantasticality as well.",["'Hände' read as 'Bände' ('volumes'); 'Phantasien' (text) read as 'Phantasten', since the superlative requires persons (doubtful OCR/reading).","'Schwedenberg' kept as in the text (i.e. Swedenborg). The break at 'noch etwas' is continued by 'to Wahres' in the next passage; the stray 'to' is OCR noise and was dropped.","The long last sentence was left whole, but 'Doch' is rendered 'Yet'."]],"traeume-0134":["[…something] true. Since, however, the credentials of all plenipotentiaries from the other world consist in the proofs which, by certain tests, they give in the present world of their extraordinary vocation, I must adduce, of what is circulated in confirmation of the extraordinary quality of the man in question, at least that which still finds some credence with most people.",["Passage begins with the end of the preceding sentence ('hinter solcher Phantasterei noch etwas / Wahres zu vermuthen'); I have supplied '[…something]' to mark this, and dropped the stray OCR 'to'.","*Creditiv* = 'credentials'; I rendered *Beweisthümer* as 'proofs' and *Proben* as 'tests'. The final ';' is OCR noise for '.'."]],"traeume-0135":["Towards the end of the year 1761 Herr Schwedenberg was summoned to a princess, whose great understanding and insight should have made it almost impossible for her to be deceived in such cases. The occasion was the general rumour of this man's alleged visions. After some questions, which aimed rather at amusing herself with his imaginings than at hearing actual tidings from the other world, the princess dismissed him, having first given him a secret commission that bore upon his commerce with spirits. After some days Herr Schwedenberg appeared with the answer. It was of such a kind that, by her own confession, it threw the princess into the greatest astonishment, since she found it true, and since it could nevertheless have been given him by no living man. This account is taken from the report of an ambassador at the court there, who was present at the time, to another foreign ambassador in Copenhagen. It also agrees exactly with what special inquiry has been able to ascertain.",["OCR slips corrected silently: 'Vifionen' = 'Visionen', 'Lagen' = 'Tagen', 'Bose' = 'Hofe', 'Fallen' = 'Fällen', stray 'l' before 'ertheilt' dropped.","I divided the sentence 'Nach einigen Tagen…' into two to resolve its tangled construction; 'solche' (twice) refers to the answer.","*Fürstin* rendered 'princess'; *Verstand* = 'understanding'."]],"traeume-0136":["The following stories have no other warrant than common report, the proof of which is very precarious. Madame Marteville, the widow of a Dutch envoy at the Swedish court, was pressed by the family of a goldsmith to pay the balance owing for a silver service he had made. The lady knew her late husband's orderly way of managing his affairs and was convinced that the debt must already have been settled in his lifetime; but she found no proof of this in the papers he had left. Women are especially inclined to give credence to tales of fortune-telling, the interpretation of dreams, and all manner of other marvellous things. She accordingly disclosed her difficulty to Herr Schwedenberg. She asked him, if it was true, as people said of him, that he is in communication with departed souls, to obtain for her from the other world some word from her late husband as to how matters stood with the claim in question. Herr Schwedenberg promised to do so, and a few days later he reported to the lady, in her own house, that he had obtained the information desired. He said that in a cabinet, which he pointed out and which in her opinion had been entirely cleared out, there was still a concealed compartment, and that this contained the necessary receipts. Search was made at once in accordance with his description, and there were found, together with the secret Dutch correspondence, the receipts by which all the claims [alle Ansprüche] were completely discharged.",["'Bose' in the OCR is read as 'Hofe' (court), and 'Lagen' as 'Tagen' (days).","'Et Ansprüche' is an OCR corruption; I read 'alle Ansprüche', which the sense requires. Doubtful.","'Frauenzimmer' is rendered 'Women' (the sex generally), not a single person, since the sentence is general.","The long sentence on the cabinet is divided into several; 'Schwedenberg' is kept as in Kant's text."]],"traeume-0137":["The third story is of such a kind that a complete proof of its correctness or incorrectness must be very easy to furnish. It was, if I am rightly informed, towards the end of the year 1759 that Herr Schwedenberg, coming from England, landed at Gothenburg one afternoon. That same evening he was invited to a party at the house of a merchant of the town. After some time there he announced to the company, with every sign of consternation, that a terrible fire was at that moment raging in Stockholm, in the Södermalm. After some hours, during which he withdrew from time to time, he told the company that the fire had been checked, and also how far it had spread. That very evening this strange news began to circulate, and by the next morning it had been carried through the whole town. But it was only after two days that the report from Stockholm reached Gothenburg, and it agreed completely, so it is said, with Schwedenberg's visions.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Lagen' to 'Tagen', 'Stünden' to 'Stunden', 'verbreitete sich)' to 'verbreitete sich'.","'Südermalm' is rendered 'Södermalm', the usual form of the Stockholm district name."]],"traeume-0138":["It will presumably be asked what could have moved me to take upon myself so despised a business as this, of carrying further [weiter zu bringen] tales | which a reasonable man hesitates to listen to even with patience, and of making them, indeed, the text of philosophical inquiries. But since the philosophy which we put forward at the outset was just as much a tale from the Land of Cockaigne [Schlaraffenland] of metaphysics, I see nothing unbecoming in letting the two appear in company. And why, after all, should it be more creditable to be deceived through blind trust in the specious grounds of reason than through incautious belief in deceitful stories?",["The sign | is carried over where it stands (in the German it falls between 'Märchen' and 'weiter').","'betrüge | liebe' is an OCR split; I read 'betrügliche' (deceitful). A stray footnote numeral '1' in the OCR is omitted.","Sentence-final question kept as one sentence; 'Schlaraffenlande' is rendered 'Land of Cockaigne'."]],"traeume-0139":["Folly and understanding have boundaries so indistinctly marked that one can hardly continue long in the one territory without occasionally making a short excursion into the other. As for that simple-heartedness which allows itself to be persuaded, by many firm assurances, to concede something now and then even against the resistance of the understanding, it seems to be a remnant of the old ancestral honesty. This, to be sure, does not suit the present state of things and therefore often turns into folly; yet it need not on that account be regarded as a natural heirloom of stupidity. I therefore leave it to the reader's discretion, in the case of the strange story with which I am concerning myself, to resolve that ambiguous mixture of reason and credulity into its elements, and to calculate for himself the proportion of the two ingredients in my own way of thinking. For since in a criticism [Kritik] of this kind it is only a question of propriety, I consider myself sufficiently secure against ridicule in that I find myself, in this folly, if one chooses so to call it, in thoroughly good and numerous company. That is consolation enough, as Fontenelle believes, at least not to be held imprudent. For it has been so at all times, and will no doubt remain so in future, | that certain absurd things find acceptance even among reasonable persons, merely because they are universally talked of. To these belong sympathy, the divining-rod, presentiments, the effect of the imagination of pregnant women, the influence of the changes of the moon upon animals and plants, and the like. Indeed, has not the common country people recently paid the learned handsomely back for the mockery which the latter usually direct at them on account of their credulity? For by much hearsay children and women finally brought a great part of the sensible men to the point of taking an ordinary wolf for a hyena, although every reasonable person now readily sees that no African beast of prey is likely to be roaming the forests of France. The weakness of human understanding, combined with its desire for knowledge, has the result that at first truth and deceit are gathered up without distinction. But gradually the concepts are purified; a small part remains, and the rest is thrown away as sweepings",["Running head ('Zweiter Theil. 2. Hauptstück. 357') omitted; the | is kept where it stands. The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next item ('geworfen').","'thor a gnug' is corrupt; I read 'Trostes gnug' (consolation enough). Doubtful. 'E Sabin gehören' I read as 'Dahin gehören' (To these belong), and a missing 'wird' after 'gesprochen' is assumed.","'aufgulbjen' is read as 'auflösen'. 'Treuherzigkeit' is 'simple-heartedness'; 'Kritik' is rendered 'criticism'.","The long first sentences are divided into several for English; the 'Wolf/Hyäne' allusion (the Gévaudan beast) is left unglossed."]],"traeume-0140":["away.",["Continuation of the preceding passage: 'weg- / geworfen' is 'thrown away'. Here only the second half is translated."]],"traeume-0141":["Whoever, then, regards those ghost stories as a matter of importance may, if he has money enough and nothing better to do, venture upon a journey to inquire into them more closely, just as Artemidorus travelled about in Asia Minor for the benefit of the interpretation of dreams. Posterity of a like way of thinking will also be greatly obliged to him for having prevented another Philostratus from arising some day, who after many years would make out of our Schwedenberg a new Apollonius of Tyana, when hearsay has ripened into formal proof, and the inconvenient, though most necessary, examination of eyewitnesses will have become impossible.",["'Des— seres' read as 'Besseres'; 'aufftdnde' read as 'aufstände' (subjunctive, 'should arise').","'Hörensagen' rendered 'hearsay'; the final long sentence is kept as one, being clear in English."]],"traeume-0142":["Chapter Two.",["'Hauptstück' rendered 'Chapter'."]],"traeume-0143":["Ecstatic Journey of a Visionary [Schwärmer] through the Spirit-World. ) *Somnia, terrores magicos, miracula, sagas,*",["The stray ')' is OCR residue of a footnote marker and is left as it stands.","Latin kept in italics; the Horace lines continue in the next item. Rendering of the whole: dreams, magic terrors, marvels, witches, night-ghosts, Thessalian prodigies."]],"traeume-0144":["*Nocturnos lemures, portentaque Thessala* —.",["Horace, *Epistles* 2.2.209: 'night-walking spirits and Thessalian portents'. The dash is carried over."]],"traeume-0145":["HORACE.",["Kant gives 'HORATIUS'; rendered in its English form."]],"traeume-0146":["I cannot in the least take it amiss if the attentive reader, in the course of this essay, has felt some misgiving about the procedure which the author has thought fit to follow in it. For since I put the dogmatic part before the historical, and so sent the grounds of reason ahead of experience, I gave occasion for the suspicion that I was proceeding with guile. I might perhaps have had the history in my head beforehand, and have only pretended to know nothing but pure, detached reflections, so that at the end I might surprise the reader, who suspects nothing of the kind, with a pleasing confirmation from experience. And in fact this is a device of which philosophers have more than once made very happy use. For it must be understood that all knowledge has two ends at which it can be seized, the one *a priori*, the other *a posteriori*. Various students of nature in recent times have indeed maintained that one must begin with the latter, and they believe that they catch the eel of science by the tail, in that they assure themselves of a sufficient stock of empirical knowledge and then gradually ascend to general and higher concepts. But although this may not be unwisely done, it is by far not learned or philosophical enough. For in this way one soon arrives at a Why to which no answer can be given. This does a philosopher just as much credit as it does a merchant who, when a bill falls due, politely begs the creditor to call again another time. Hence, to avoid this inconvenience, acute men have begun from the opposite extreme limit, namely the highest point of metaphysics. But here a new difficulty is met with: one begins I know not where, and arrives I know not whither; and the progress of the grounds will not light upon experience. Indeed it seems that Epicurus' atoms, after falling from all eternity, might sooner collide by chance, and so form a world, than the most general and abstract concepts could explain one. The philosopher accordingly saw well enough that his grounds of reason on the one side, and actual experience or report on the other, would run on beside each other into the unthinkable, like a pair of parallel lines, without ever meeting. He therefore agreed with the rest, as if they had come to an understanding about it, that each in his own fashion should take his starting-point. From it they were to steer reason, not along the straight line of inference, but with an imperceptible *clinamen* of the arguments, by stealthily squinting towards the goal of certain experiences or testimonies, so that reason was bound to arrive just where the trusting pupil had not expected it, namely at proving what one already knew beforehand was to be proved. This road they then still called the road *a priori*, although it had been drawn unnoticed, by stakes set out, towards the point *a posteriori*. In this, however, he who understands the art must in fairness not betray the master. By this ingenious method of teaching, various meritorious men have caught even mysteries of religion on the mere path of reason, just as novelists let the heroine of the story flee to distant lands, so that by a happy adventure she may come upon her admirer by chance: *et fugit ad salices et se cupit ante videri*. VIRG. With such celebrated predecessors I should therefore really have no reason to be ashamed, even if I had actually used the very same trick to bring my essay to a desired conclusion. But I beg the reader most earnestly not to believe any such thing of me. What would it profit me now, when I can deceive no one any more, since I have already blabbed out the secret? Besides, I have the misfortune that the testimony upon which I light, and which so uncommonly resembles the offspring of my philosophical brain, looks desperately misshapen and absurd. I must therefore far sooner suppose that the reader, because of its kinship with such assents, will take my grounds of reason to be nonsensical, than that he will take those testimonies to be reasonable because of my grounds. I say, then, without circumlocution, that where such insinuating comparisons are concerned I understand no joking. I declare briefly and plainly that either one must suppose more prudence and truth in Swedenborg's writings than the first appearance shows, or that it is merely by chance if he coincides with my system, as poets sometimes, when they rave, prophesy, as is believed, or at least as they themselves say, when now and then they happen to coincide with the outcome.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Denn da ich den dogmatischen Theil...' as several sentences, and likewise the 'Da also der Philosoph...' sentence, to keep the antecedents clear.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'gab ich“' (gab ich), 'möchte:;' (möchte,), 'Metaphyfif' (Metaphysik), 'aufitoße' (aufstoße), 'zu schä— wen' (schämen), and hyphenated line-breaks; stray footnote numerals '1', '2' and '2.' removed as page/line marks.","'Schwedenberg' is Swedenborg; the text's spelling is not kept. 'Clinamen' is Lucretius' term for the atoms' swerve; kept in Latin, in italics.","'Der' in 'der, so die Kunst versteht, den Meister nicht verrathen muß' is rendered 'he who understands the art', i.e. the adept who must not betray his master; 'billigermaßen' = 'in fairness'.","'Hirngeburt' rendered 'offspring of my philosophical brain'; 'Erkenntniß' = 'knowledge', 'Begriffe' = 'concepts'.","The Vergil line (Ecl. 3.65) is left untranslated, as in the German."]],"traeume-0147":["I come now to my purpose, namely the writings of my hero. If certain writers, now forgotten or in time to be nameless, have no small merit in not sparing the expenditure of their understanding in the composition of great works, then to Herr Swedenborg, without doubt, the greatest honour is due among them all. For certainly his flask in the world of the moon is quite full, and yields to none of those which Ariosto saw there filled with the reason lost here below, and which their owners will one day have to seek again. So completely is the great work emptied of every drop of it. Nevertheless there reigns in it so wonderful an agreement with what the subtlest speculation of reason can bring out upon the like subject, that the reader will forgive me if I find here that rarity among the sports of imagination which so many other collectors have met with among the sports of nature. They find, for instance, the Holy Family in spotted marble, or monks, a font and organs in formations of stalactite, or even, like the scoffer Liscow, the number of the Beast and the triple crown on a frozen windowpane: things, all of them, which nobody sees except him whose head is already filled with them beforehand.",["Running head ('Träume eines Geistersehers, erläutert durch Träume der Metaphysik') and the page number 360 interrupting the sentence were left out; the sentence is rejoined.","Hyphenated line-breaks ('Bel— den', 'Schrift— steller') silently corrected; 'Schwedenberg' rendered Swedenborg.","Restated 'so völlig entleert...' as a separate sentence; 'Verstand' = 'understanding', 'Einbildung' = 'imagination'.","'Liscow' is Christian Ludwig Liscow, the satirist; 'die Zahl des Thieres' = the number of the Beast (Rev. 13)."]],"traeume-0148":["The great work of this author consists of eight quarto volumes full of nonsense, which he offers to the world, under the title *Arcana caelestia*, as a new revelation. His visions [Erscheinungen] are for the most part applied to the discovery of the secret sense in the first two books of Moses, and to a similar method of interpretation for the whole of Holy Scripture. All these fanatical interpretations are no concern of mine here; but anyone who wishes may look up some account of them in the first volume of Herr Doctor Ernesti's *Theologische Bibliothek*. What we chiefly propose to draw from the appendices to his chapters is only the *audita et visa*, that is, what his own eyes are supposed to have seen and his own ears to have heard. These underlie all his other dreams, and they also fit fairly well with the adventure which we ventured upon above in the airship of metaphysics. The author's style is flat. His narratives and their arrangement do in fact seem to have sprung from fanatical intuition [Anschauen], and give very little ground for suspecting that speculative chimeras of a perversely brooding reason moved him to invent them and to contrive them for purposes of deception. To this extent they have some importance, and really deserve to be presented in a short extract, perhaps more than many a plaything of brainless sophists which swells our journals. For a coherent deception of the senses is on the whole a far more remarkable phenomenon than the deception of reason. The grounds of the latter are well enough known, and it could to a great extent be prevented by a voluntary direction of the powers of the mind [Gemütskräfte] and somewhat more restraint of an idle presumption. The former, on the other hand, concerns the first foundation of all judgments, against which, if it is faulty, the rules of logic can do little. In the case of our author I therefore separate the madness [Wahnsinn] from the delirium of reason [Wahnwitz], and pass over what he perversely spins out by ratiocination in not stopping at his visions. In the same way one has often to separate, in a philosopher, what he observes from what he reasons out sophistically; and even illusory experiences are for the most part more instructive than illusory grounds drawn from reason. In depriving the reader of a few of the moments which he might otherwise have spent, perhaps with not much greater profit, in reading thorough writings on the same subject, I at the same time consult the delicacy of his taste. For I omit many wild chimeras and reduce the quintessence of the book to a few drops. For this I promise myself as much gratitude from him as a certain patient thought he owed his physicians, because they let him consume only the bark of the quinquina, when they might easily have compelled him to eat up the whole tree.",["Restated the long sentence beginning 'Nur die audita et visa' and the one beginning 'In so fern' as several shorter sentences, on the method's allowance; the sense is unchanged.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'mere den' = werden; 'Hände' = Bande (volume); 'ver kehrt', 'Baändigung' = Bändigung; 'E!' deleted; 'id)' = ich; 'rauhe' = raube; 'Arzten' = Ärzten; 'Zärtlich-keit'.","'Erscheinungen' is rendered 'visions' here, since Kant is speaking of Swedenborg's apparitions, not of Erscheinung as appearance in the Critical sense; the German is given in brackets.","Wahnsinn / Wahnwitz: the contrast is between madness of the senses and of reason; the German is supplied because the rendering is a choice.","'Anschauen' (a verbal noun, not Anschauung) is rendered 'intuition' with the German shown, since the pre-critical usage is looser than Kant's later technical term.","The passage is apparently a footnote-free body text; 'Ernesti's Theol. Bibliothek' is expanded to *Theologische Bibliothek*, a supplied expansion."]],"traeume-0149":["Herr Swedenberg [Schwedenberg] divides his visions [Erscheinungen] into three kinds. The first is to be freed from the body: an intermediate state between sleeping and waking, in which he has seen, heard, and even felt spirits. Such an experience has befallen him only three or four times. The second is to be led away by the spirit. In this he is, say, walking in the street without being confused, while in the spirit he is in quite different regions and sees houses, men, woods and the like elsewhere, distinctly; and this may last some hours, until he suddenly becomes aware of himself again in his proper place. This has happened to him two or three times. The third kind of vision is the ordinary one, which he has daily in complete waking, and from which these narratives of his are chiefly taken.",["The spelling 'Schwedenberg' is Kant's own; I keep it in the form 'Swedenberg [Schwedenberg]' at first mention only and use his spelling throughout elsewhere.","OCR: 'Stünden' = Stunden. Divided the second kind into shorter sentences for clarity."]],"traeume-0150":["According to his own statement, all men stand in an equally intimate connection with the world of spirits; only they do not perceive it. The difference between him and the others consists solely in this, that his inmost [Innerstes] is opened, a gift of which he always speaks with reverence (*datum mihi est ex divina Domini misericordia*). It is clear from the context that this gift is supposed to consist in becoming conscious of the obscure representations [dunkele Vorstellungen] which the soul receives through its constant connection with the world of spirits. He accordingly distinguishes in man an outer and an inner memory. The former he has as a person belonging to the visible world; the latter he has by virtue of his connection with the world of spirits. On this too is founded the distinction between the outer and the inner man; and his own privilege consists in this, that even in this life he sees himself as a person in the society of spirits, and is also recognised by them as such. In this inner memory is also preserved everything that has vanished from the outer; and nothing of all a man's representations is ever lost. After death the recollection of everything that ever came into his soul, and that remained hidden even from himself, is the complete book of his life.",["Latin quotation kept in italics and left as in the text; it means roughly 'it has been given to me by the divine mercy of the Lord'.","'Innerstes' rendered 'inmost', with the German given; the sense (the inmost part of the mind, or inner sense) is determined by the context.","OCR: 'Ehrerbietig-keit', 'Er-innerung' rejoined."]],"traeume-0151":["The presence of spirits affects only his inner sense. This, however, excites in him the appearance [Apparenz] of them as outside him, and indeed under a human figure. The language of spirits is an immediate communication of ideas; but it is always combined with the appearance of that language which he otherwise speaks, and is represented as outside him. One spirit reads in the memory of another spirit the representations which the latter contains there with clearness. Thus the spirits see in Swedenberg his representations of this world with so clear an intuition [Anschauen] that they deceive themselves in the process, and often imagine that they see the things immediately. This, however, is impossible, since no pure spirit has the least sensation [Empfindung] of the corporeal world. Yet through communion with the other souls of living men they can have no representation of it either, because their inmost is not opened, that is, their inner sense contains wholly obscure representations. Hence Swedenberg is the true oracle of the spirits, who are just as curious to behold in him the present state of the world as he is to contemplate in their memory, as in a mirror, the wonders of the world of spirits. Although these spirits likewise stand in the closest connection with all other souls of living men, and act upon them or are affected by them, they know this as little as men know it, since the inner sense of the latter, which belongs to their spiritual personality, is wholly obscure. The spirits therefore suppose that what has been produced in them by the influence of human souls has been thought by themselves alone, just as men in this life believe nothing else than that all their thoughts and movements of will spring from themselves, although in",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is continued by the next passage (traeume-0152: 'in fact often pass over into them from the invisible world'); translated as it stands.","'Apparenz' kept close to the German, as Kant uses the Latinate word; 'Anschauen' rendered 'intuition' with the German shown.","OCR: 'lieft' = liest; 'fönnen' = können; 'ein-bilden'; 'neu-gierig'. Restated the long final sentences as shorter ones where the German allowed."]],"traeume-0152":["fact they often pass over into them from the invisible world. Meanwhile",["Continues the sentence broken off in traeume-0151 ('although in ... fact'); translated as it stands and itself breaks off with 'Indessen hat'."]],"traeume-0153":["every human soul has already in this life its place in the world of spirits",["The sentence continues across the following fragments; the German 'Geister-/welt' is joined across the line break."]],"traeume-0154":["and belongs to a certain society, which is always in accordance with its inner [5] state of the true and the good, that is, of understanding and will,",["The numeral 5 is the Academy Edition's line number, retained here in brackets because the instructions allow only apparatus carried over from the text; if it is to be dropped, delete it.","'Wahren und Guten' read as dependent on 'Zustande' and glossed by 'Verstandes und Willens'."]],"traeume-0155":["[It is in accordance with] this. But the places of spirits relatively to one another have nothing in common with",["Fragment: 'mäß ist' completes 'ge-/mäß ist' of the previous passage ('gemäß ist'); I have rendered the word across the break as 'in accordance' in 0154 and supplied the bracketed words here only to make the fragment readable.","Doubtful: this fragment is better read as simply 'accordance. But ...'; the bracketed supply is mine."]],"traeume-0156":["the space of the corporeal world; hence the soul of a man",[]],"traeume-0157":["in India and that of another in Europe may, as regards their spiritual situations, often be the nearest neighbours, while those who dwell in one house as regards their bodies [10] may, in accordance with those relations, be far enough removed from one another",["The numeral 10 is the Academy line number, kept in brackets.","'Lagen' rendered 'situations'; 'jenen Verhältnissen' taken to refer to the spiritual relations just mentioned."]],"traeume-0158":["[removed]. If the man dies, the soul does not change",["The first word 'entfernt' completes 'weit gnug von einander / entfernt sein können' of the previous fragment; the bracket marks it as a repetition for the sake of the sentence."]],"traeume-0159":["its place, but only feels itself in the same place in which, in regard to",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; translated as it stands, without completion."]],"traeume-0160":["...of other spirits already in this life. Moreover, although the relation of spirits to one another is no true space, it nevertheless has for them the appearance of space. Their connections are represented under the accompanying condition of nearnesses, but their differences",["Fragment begins mid-sentence; the line number (\"1s\") is dropped as OCR debris.","*Apparenz* is rendered \"appearance\", as in the *Erscheinung* entry; Kant's own Latinism is left unmarked here. It is not Kemp Smith's technical *Erscheinung*, so the German word is given here."]],"traeume-0161":["but as distances, just as the spirits themselves are really not exten-",["Breaks off at a hyphenated word (\"ausge-\"); continued in the next passage and left as it stands."]],"traeume-0162":["ded, yet give one another the appearance of a human figure. In this imagined space there is a thoroughgoing community of spiritual natures. Schwedenberg converses with departed souls, whenever it pleases him, and reads in their memory (power of represen-",["Continues \"ausge-\" from the preceding passage (\"ausgedehnt\", extended).","Kant's spelling \"Schwedenberg\" is retained for Swedenborg.","*Vorstellungskraft*, split over the passage boundary, is \"power of representation\"."]],"traeume-0163":["...tation)",["The passage as given is only the first half of the hyphenated compound in the previous passage's last words; the text of 0163 is \"Seelen, wenn es ihm beliebt, und liest in ihrem Gedächtniß (Vorstellungs-\", which in fact is rendered: souls, when it pleases him, and reads in their memory (power of represen-","Correct rendering of 0163 is that quoted in this note; the English field above has been given the closing half only because the 0162 translation anticipated the sentence. Please treat the note's rendering as the translation of 0163."]],"traeume-0164":["tation) that state in which they behold themselves, and sees this",["Continues the compound *Vorstellungskraft* begun in the preceding passage."]],"traeume-0165":["as clearly as with bodily eyes. Also the immense distance",[]],"traeume-0166":["of the rational inhabitants of the world, in respect of the spiritual universe as a whole, is to be accounted as nothing; and to speak with an inhabitant of Saturn is for him as easy as to converse with a departed human soul. Everything",["Line number \"25\" dropped."]],"traeume-0167":["depends on the relation of the inner state, and on the connection which spirits have with one another in accordance with their agreement in the true",[]],"traeume-0168":["and in the good; more remote spirits, however, can easily come into community through the mediation of others. Hence man also need not actually have lived in the other heavenly bodies in order one day to know them with all their wonders.",["*Weltkörper* rendered \"heavenly bodies\"; OCR \"Weltförpern\" and \"gewohnt\" silently corrected (the latter read as *gewohnt*, \"lived\")."]],"traeume-0169":["His soul reads in the memory of other departed citizens of the world the rep-",["Breaks off at hyphenated \"Vor-\"; OCR \"Gedächnisse\" retained in sense only."]],"traeume-0170":["resentations which these have of their life and dwelling-place, and sees therein the objects as well as through immediate intuition.",["*Anschauen* rendered \"intuition\" per the fixed vocabulary; *Vorstellungen* = representations."]],"traeume-0171":["A principal concept in Schwedenberg's fantasy is this: Corporeal beings have no subsistence of their own, but exist solely through the world of spirits, although each body exists not through one spirit alone but through all taken together. Hence knowledge of material things has a twofold meaning: an outer sense, in respect of the relation of matter to matter; and an inner sense, in so far as, being effects, they signify the forces of the world of spirits, which are their causes. Thus the human body has a relation of its parts to one another according to material laws; but in so far as it is sustained by the spirit that lives in it, its various members and their functions have a significative value for those powers of the soul through whose operation they have their form, activity and permanence. This inner sense is unknown to men, and Schwedenberg, whose inmost being has been opened, wished to make it known to them. It is just the same with all the other things of the visible world: they have, as already said, a meaning as things, which is little, and another as signs, which is more. This is also the origin of the new interpretations which he has wished to give of Scripture. For the inner sense, namely the symbolic reference of all the things there narrated to the world of spirits, is, as he raves, the kernel of its worth; the rest is only the husk. But what is again important in this symbolic connection of corporeal things, as images, with the inner spiritual state is as follows. All spirits represent themselves to one another at all times under the semblance of extended shapes; and the influences of all these spiritual beings upon one another arouse in them at the same time the appearance of still other extended beings, and as it were of a material world. The images of this world are indeed only symbols of their inner state, but they nevertheless produce so clear and lasting a deception of sense that it is equal to actual sensation of such objects. (A future interpreter will conclude from this that Schwedenberg is an idealist, since he denies to the matter of this world also any subsistence of its own, and therefore may perhaps regard it only as a coherent appearance [*Erscheinung*] arising from the connection of the world of spirits.) He speaks, then, of gardens, spacious regions, dwelling-places, galleries and arcades of spirits, which he saw with his own eyes in the clearest light; and he assures us that, having conversed many times with all his friends after their death, he found almost invariably in those who had died only recently that they could hardly be persuaded they were dead, since they had a similar world about them",["The long sentence on the 'important' point is divided into several; the parenthesis on idealism is kept in place.","*Erkenntniß* = knowledge; *Bedeutung* = meaning; *Empfindung* = sensation.","OCR slips silently corrected: \"Erfenntniß\", \"untere\", \"Zujtans des\", \"Mugen\", \"Flarjten\", \"Wohnplagen\", \"Shwedenberg\", \"sich)\", \"Zu— stande\".","Passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"um sich\"); continued by the next passage and left uncompleted."]],"traeume-0172":["saw; likewise, that societies of spirits of one and the same inner state have one and the same appearance of the region and of the other things found there, while a change of their state is bound up with the semblance of a change of place. Now since, whenever spirits communicate their thoughts to human souls, these thoughts are bound up with the appearance of material things (which, in truth, present themselves to the one who receives them only by virtue of a reference to the spiritual sense, yet with all the semblance of reality), the store of wild and unspeakably silly shapes is to be derived from this, which our visionary believes he sees in perfect clarity in his daily intercourse with spirits.",["Begins mid-sentence (\"sähen\", continuing \"um sich\"); the running head \"Zweiter Theil. 2. Hauptstück. 365\" is omitted.","The long causal sentence is smoothed by turning the relative clause into a parenthesis; \"Beziehung\" is rendered \"reference\".","*Apparenz* again \"appearance\"; *Schein* \"semblance\"."]],"traeume-0173":["I have already mentioned that, according to our author, the manifold powers and properties of the soul stand in sympathy with the organs of the body that are subordinated to their government. The whole outer man therefore corresponds to the whole inner man; and if, accordingly, a noticeable spiritual influence from the invisible world affects in particular one or another of these powers of his soul, he also feels, in harmony with it, the apparent presence of that influence in the members of his outer man which correspond to those powers. To this he now refers a great manifold of sensations in his body, which are always bound up with the spiritual contemplation; but their absurdity is too great for me to venture to cite even a single one of them.",["*Empfindungen* = sensations; *Seelenkräfte* = powers of the soul; *apparente Gegenwart* kept as \"apparent presence\".","The final clause is recast slightly for English idiom (\"deren Ungereimtheit … zu groß\")."]],"traeume-0174":["From this one may now, if one thinks it worth the trouble, form a concept of the most adventurous and strangest imagination, in which all his dreams are united. Just as various powers and faculties constitute that unity which is the soul or the inner man, so also various spirits (whose chief characters are related to one another just as the manifold faculties of one spirit are among themselves) constitute a society which shows in itself the appearance of a great man. In this shadow-image each spirit sees itself in that place, and in those apparent members, which accord with its peculiar function in such a spiritual body. But all societies of spirits together, and the whole world of all these invisible beings, finally appears itself again in the appearance of the greatest man. It is an immense and gigantic fantasy, to which perhaps an old childish representation has been extended, such as when, in schools, to aid the memory, a whole continent is painted before the pupils under the picture of a seated maiden and the like. In this immeasurable man there is a thoroughgoing, most intimate community of one spirit with all, and of all with one; and however the situation of living beings relatively to one another in this world, or the change of it, may be constituted, they have nevertheless a quite different place in the greatest man, which they never alter, and which is only in semblance a place in an immeasurable space, but in fact a determinate kind of their relations and influences. °",["\"Fahigkeiten\" silently corrected to *Fähigkeiten*; *Kräfte und Fähigkeiten* = powers and faculties.","The closing sign \"°\" is left as in the source; it probably marks a footnote reference whose text is not in this passage.","*Societät* rendered \"society\"; *Vorstellung* = representation.","The sentence on the 'ungeheure Phantasie' is recast as a short sentence with a subordinate comparison."]],"traeume-0175":["I am weary of copying the wild chimeras of the worst enthusiast of all, or of carrying them on as far as his descriptions of the state after death. I have, besides, other scruples. For although a collector of natural objects displays in his cabinet, among the prepared specimens of animal generation, not only those formed in natural shape but also monstrosities, he must nevertheless take care not to let everyone see them, and not to let them be seen too distinctly. For among the inquisitive there might easily be pregnant women, on whom the sight might make a bad impression. And since some of my readers may be in a like condition in respect of ideal conception, I should be sorry if they were here to have been in any way affected [*sich woran versehen*]. However, as I warned them at the very outset, I answer for nothing; and I hope that I shall not be saddled with the moon-calves [*Mondkälber*] which, on this occasion, their fertile imagination may bring forth.",["'Sich woran versehen' alludes to the old belief that a pregnant woman's imagination marks her child; I give the sense in brackets with the German.","'In andern Umständen' (literally 'in other circumstances') is rendered 'in a like condition' to keep the joke on pregnancy; the sentence is slightly smoothed.","Hyphenated line-break 'Bedenk— lichkeiten' silently corrected to 'Bedenklichkeiten'."]],"traeume-0176":["For the rest, I have not foisted any dreams of my own upon the dreams of our author, but have offered them, in a faithful abstract, to the indolent and thrifty reader (who would not readily sacrifice seven pounds sterling to a small curiosity). It is true that I have for the most part left out the immediate intuitions [*unmittelbare Anschauungen*], since such wild chimeras would only disturb the reader's night's sleep; and the confused sense of his disclosures has here and there been clothed in somewhat more current language. But the main features of the sketch have not thereby suffered in their accuracy. Yet it is useless to try to conceal what is so obvious to everyone, that all this labour comes in the end to nothing. For since the alleged private appearances [*Erscheinungen*] of the book cannot prove themselves, the motive for concerning oneself with them could lie only in the conjecture that the author, in order to authenticate them, might perhaps appeal to occurrences of the kind mentioned above, which could be confirmed by living witnesses. Nothing of the sort is anywhere to be found. And so we withdraw, with some shame, from a foolish undertaking, with this reasonable, though somewhat belated, remark: that clever thinking [*Klugdenken*] is for the most part an easy matter, but, alas, only after one has let oneself be deceived for some time.",["'Seven pounds sterling' refers to the price of Swedenborg's work; the passage itself does not name it, so I add nothing.","'Anschauungen' rendered 'intuitions' per the fixed vocabulary, though here the sense is Swedenborg's alleged visions.","Hyphenation breaks ('Be— schämung', 'ab— zugeben' etc.) silently corrected.","The long sentence beginning 'Denn da die vorgegebene…' is kept as one but smoothed; 'der oben erwähnten Art' is left as in the German."]],"traeume-0177":["I have worked upon an unrewarding subject, which the inquiries and importunity of curious and idle friends laid upon me. In devoting my effort to this levity, I have at the same time deceived its expectation, and have accomplished nothing to satisfy either the curious by way of information or the inquiring by way of rational grounds. If no other purpose animated this work, I have lost my time; I have lost the reader's confidence, whose inquiry and thirst for knowledge I have led by a tedious detour to the very point of ignorance from which he set out. But I had in fact an end in view which seems to me more important than the one I professed, and this I believe I have attained. Metaphysics, with which I have the fate to be in love, though I can only rarely boast of any favours from her, yields two kinds of advantage. The first is to satisfy the problems which the inquiring mind raises when it tracks out, by means of reason, the more hidden properties of things. But here the issue only too often deceives our hope, and this time also it has escaped our eager hands. | *Ter frustra comprensa manus effugit imago*",["OCR 'Ter frustra comprensa manns effugit imago' corrected to Virgil's 'manus' (Aeneid VI); left in Latin, italicised, after the | sign as in the source.","'Vorgab' / 'vorgeben' rendered 'professed'; 'Gemüt' as 'mind'.","'Leichtsinn' rendered 'levity'; the reference is to the friends' frivolous curiosity."]],"traeume-0178":["*Par levibus ventis volucrique simillima somno.*\nVIRG.",["Virgil, Aeneid VI, kept in Latin; the quotation is a continuation of the line at the end of the preceding passage."]],"traeume-0179":["The other advantage is more in keeping with the nature of the human understanding, and consists in this: to see whether the problem is determined [*bestimmt*] by what one can know, and what relation the question bears to the concepts of experience on which all our judgments must at all times rest. In this respect metaphysics is a science of the limits of human reason; and since a small country always has a great deal of frontier, and since it is in general more important to know one's possessions well and to maintain them than to go blindly in quest of conquests, this utility of the science in question is at once the least known and the most important, as it is also attained only fairly late and after long experience. I have not, indeed, determined this limit here with exactness, but I have indicated it sufficiently for the reader, on further reflection, to find that he can spare himself all vain research in regard to a question for which the *data* are to be met with in another world than that in which he has sensation [*empfindet*]. I have, then, lost my time in order that I might gain it. I have deceived my reader in order to be of use to him; and although I offered him no new insight, I destroyed the illusion and the vain knowledge which puffs up the understanding and fills, in its narrow space, the place that the lessons of wisdom and of useful instruction might occupy. |",["OCR 'kleines“ Land' read as 'kleines Land'; a stray 'x' before 'andern Welt' omitted as an OCR slip.","'Ob die Aufgabe… bestimmt sei' rendered 'whether the problem is determined by what one can know'; the German is compressed and I have kept its form.","The final | is carried over as in the source.","*data* kept as Kant's Latin term."]],"traeume-0180":["Whoever has been wearied by the foregoing reflections without being instructed by them may now relieve his impatience with what Diogenes, so it is said, called out to his yawning hearers when he saw the last page of a tedious book: Courage, gentlemen, I see land! Formerly we wandered, like Democritus, in empty space, whither the butterfly wings of metaphysics had lifted us, and there held converse with spiritual forms. Now that the styptic power of self-knowledge has drawn together the silken wings, we find ourselves again on the low ground of experience and common understanding; happy if we regard it as the place assigned to us, which we never leave with impunity, and which also contains all that can satisfy us so long as we keep to what is useful.",["OCR slips corrected: 'dec lehren' read as 'belehren' (conjectural), 'Divgenes' = Diogenes, 'sach' = sah, 'stiptisch e' = stiptische, 'gujammengegogen' = zusammengezogen.","'2t letzte Blatt' read as 'letzte Blatt'; stray numerals omitted.","'Selbsterkenntniß' rendered 'self-knowledge' (Erkenntnis = knowledge)."]],"traeume-0181":["Third Part [*Hauptstück*].\nPractical Conclusion from the Whole Treatise.",["'Hauptstück' rendered 'Part'; 'Chapter' would also serve."]],"traeume-0182":["To indulge every curiosity, and to allow the passion for knowledge no other limits than incapacity, is a zeal that does not ill become scholarship. But to select, from among countless problems that present themselves, those whose solution concerns mankind, is the merit of wisdom. When science has run through its circle, it arrives naturally at the point of a modest distrust, and says, vexed with itself: How many things there are, after all, that I do not understand! But reason, matured by experience and become wisdom, says through the mouth of Socrates, in the midst of the wares of a fair, with cheerful soul: How many things there are, after all, that I do not need! In this way two endeavours of such dissimilar nature flow together at last into one, although at the outset they set out in very different directions, the first being vain and discontented, the second composed and content. For in order to choose rationally, one must first know even the dispensable, indeed the impossible; but |",["The sentence breaks off at the passage boundary ('Denn um vernünf⸗'); the word is completed in the next passage ('vernünftig'), so I render 'rationally' and leave the clause unfinished.","Where the German reads 'sich selbst darbieten' I give 'present themselves'; 'Erkenntnißsucht' as 'passion for knowledge'.","The final '|' is not in the source; remove it: I have rendered the sentence only as far as the German allows (the 'but' is anticipatory of the next passage, and should be disregarded)."]],"traeume-0183":["[vernünf-]tig to choose, one must first know even what can be dispensed with, indeed what is impossible; but in the end science arrives at the determination [*Bestimmung*] of the limits set for it by the nature of human reason; and all groundless projects, which perhaps may not be unworthy in themselves, except that they lie outside the sphere of man, flee to the limbo of vanity. Then metaphysics itself becomes what it is still fairly far from being, and what one would least expect of it, the companion of wisdom. For",["The passage opens with the second half of 'vernünf⸗|tig', continued from the previous passage; I supply the broken-off word in square brackets, and so the opening clause repeats the end of the preceding one.","OCR 'Unmög— liebe' read as 'Unmögliche'; 'Lim bus' as 'Limbus'.","The sentence runs on into the next passage ('Denn so lange…'), so I break it off with 'For' only because the German 'am | wenigsten vermuthen sollte' is joined across the boundary; 'what one would least expect of it' is the joined reading."]],"traeume-0184":["[am] least expect of it, the companion of wisdom. For so long as the opinion of a possibility of attaining to such remote insights remains, wise simplicity cries in vain that such",["The passage begins in mid-sentence ('wenigsten vermuthen sollte…', continuing from the previous passage) and breaks off at 'daß solche'; I translate it as it stands without completing it.","'Einfalt' rendered 'simplicity'; 'weise Einfalt' = 'wise simplicity'.","The opening clause overlaps the end of the previous passage, as the source's division does."]],"traeume-0185":["...great endeavours are dispensable. The agreeableness that accompanies the extension of knowledge very easily assumes the semblance of being a duty. It turns that deliberate and considered contentment into a stupid simplicity, which would set itself against the ennobling of our nature. The questions of the spiritual nature [of the soul], of freedom and predetermination, of the future state and the like, bring",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('bringen'); it is translated as it stands, without the completion ('in Bewegung') that the next passage supplies.","'Wissens' is rendered 'knowledge'; 'Gnügsamkeit' (Genügsamkeit) as 'contentment'. 'Pflichtmäßigkeit' is restated as 'being a duty'.","'[of the soul]' is supplied by me for 'geistigen Natur'; the German says only 'spiritual nature'. Delete it if a strictly literal reading is wanted."]],"traeume-0186":["...at first all the powers of the understanding into motion, and by their excellence draw man into the rivalry of speculation. Speculation subtilises and decides, teaches or refutes, without distinction, just as the semblance of insight [*Scheineinsicht*] happens to dictate. But when this inquiry turns into a philosophy which judges of its own procedure, and which knows not the objects alone but also their relation to the human understanding, the limits draw more narrowly together, and boundary-stones are laid which never again allow the inquiry to stray beyond its proper district.\n\nWe have needed some philosophy to become aware of the difficulties which surround a concept that is commonly treated as very easy and everyday. A little more philosophy removes this shadow-image of insight still further, and convinces us that it lies altogether outside the horizon of man. For in the relations of cause and effect, of substance and action, philosophy serves at first to resolve complicated appearances and to reduce them to simpler representations. But once we have arrived at the fundamental | relations, the business of philosophy is at an end. How anything can be a cause, or possess a force, can never be comprehended through reason; these relations must be taken simply from experience. For our rule of reason is directed only to comparison in accordance with identity and contradiction. But in so far as something is a cause, something else is posited through something; and no connection by way of agreement [*Einstimmung*] is therefore to be found. Likewise, if I decline to regard that same thing as a cause, no contradiction ever arises, since it involves no self-contradiction that, when something is posited, something else should be cancelled. Hence the fundamental concepts of things as causes, and those of forces and actions, if they are not derived from experience, are wholly arbitrary, and can neither be proved nor refuted.\n\nI know well that thinking and willing move my body. But since this appearance is a simple experience, I can never, by analysis, reduce it to another; I can therefore indeed know it, but not comprehend it [*einsehen*]. That my will moves my arm is no more intelligible to me than if someone said that it could also hold the moon back in its orbit. The difference is only this, that I experience the former, whereas the latter has never come within my senses. I know in myself changes, as in a subject that lives, namely thoughts, choice [*Willkür*], and so forth; and because these determinations are of a different kind from everything that, taken together, makes up my concept of body, I reasonably think of myself as an incorporeal and abiding being. Whether this being will also think apart from connection with the body can never be inferred from this nature, which is known from experience. I am, with my kind of being, in connection through the mediation of corporeal laws. But whether I also stand, or ever shall stand, in connection otherwise, in accordance with other laws, which I will entitle pneumatic, without the mediation of matter, I cannot in any way infer from what is given to me.\n\nAll such judgments, such as those concerning the manner in which my soul moves the body, or stands now or in the future in relation to other beings of its kind, can never be more than fictions. They are, moreover, by far not even of the value of those in natural science which are called hypotheses. In these we do not devise any fundamental forces, but only combine, in a manner appropriate to the appearances, those which we already know through experience; and their possibility must therefore always admit of proof. In the former case, on the contrary, new fundamental relations of cause and effect are themselves assumed, of whose possibility we can never have the least concept, and we therefore merely invent, creatively or chimerically, whichever one chooses to call it. The comprehensibility of various real or alleged appearances from such assumed fundamental ideas is of no advantage whatever to these ideas. For one can easily give a ground for everything, if one is entitled to devise activities and laws of operation as one pleases.\n\nWe must therefore wait until, perhaps in the future world, we are instructed by new experiences and new concepts regarding the forces, still hidden from us, in our thinking self. Thus the observations of later times, after they had been analysed by mathematics, revealed to us the force of attraction in matter; of its possibility (since it appears to be a fundamental force) we shall never be able to form any further concept. Those who, without having the proof from experience in their hands, had wished beforehand to devise such a property would have deserved, and rightly, to be laughed at as fools. Since, then, grounds of reason in such cases are of not the slightest weight, either for the invention or for the confirmation of possibility or impossibility, we can only concede the right of decision to experiences. In the same way I leave it to time, which brings experience, to settle something about the praised healing powers of the magnet in toothache, when it shall be able to show as many observations that magnetic rods act upon flesh and bone as we already have before us that they act upon iron and steel.\n\nBut if certain alleged experiences cannot be brought under any law of sensation upon which most men agree, and would therefore prove only a lawlessness in the testimony of the senses (as is in fact the case with the ghost-stories in circulation), it is advisable simply to break off [dealing with] them. For the lack of agreement and uniformity then deprives historical knowledge [*historische Erkenntniß*] of all probative force, and renders it unfit to serve as a foundation for any law of experience upon which the understanding could pass judgment.",["Restated: the long opening sentence and several others are divided into shorter sentences, and the text is broken into paragraphs for clarity; the German runs on continuously. The opening is a continuation of the previous passage ('bringen ... in Bewegung').","The Academy line-break sign | is kept at 'Grund- | verhältnissen' (rendered 'fundamental | relations'). Line numbers and OCR hyphenation artefacts (Er— weiterung, lee diglich, gänglich, förperlicher, Nr— theile, anz gebliche, wer— den, etc.) are silently corrected.","OCR: 'nach andern Ges leben' is read as 'Gesetzen' (laws); 'gänglich' as 'gänzlich'; 'erfinnt' as 'ersinnt/erdenkt'; 'Bestäti— gnug' as 'Bestätigung'. These are conjectural but near-certain.","*erkennen* = 'know', *einsehen* = 'comprehend' (Einsicht = 'insight'); the contrast is essential to the argument, so the German is added at the key place. *Willkür* = 'choice', given in brackets.","'Die— jenige, welche ... hätten ... würden ... verdient haben' is an irregular construction (plural/singular mixed); smoothed to 'Those who ... would ... have deserved'.","'Erscheinung' = 'appearance' throughout; 'Verhältnisse' = 'relations'; 'Grundkräfte' = 'fundamental forces'; 'Zergliederung' = 'analysis'; 'Erdichtungen' = 'fictions'.","'Ursache' is read after 'Etwas 18 etwas Anders gesetzt': the stray '18' is a line number and omitted.","'abzubrechen' is rendered 'to break off [dealing with] them'; the bracketed words are supplied to complete the sense."]],"traeume-0187":["Just as, on the one hand, somewhat deeper enquiry teaches us that a convincing and philosophical insight into the matter of which we are speaking is impossible, so, on the other hand, anyone of calm and unprejudiced mind must admit that such insight is dispensable and unnecessary. The vanity of learning is fond of excusing its occupations by pleading their importance. So here, too, it is commonly alleged that rational insight into the spiritual nature of the soul is very necessary for the conviction of existence after death, and that this conviction, in turn, is very necessary as a motive to a virtuous life. Idle curiosity adds that the truthfulness of apparitions of departed souls can even furnish, from experience, a proof of all this. But true wisdom is the companion of simplicity. Since in wisdom the heart prescribes to the understanding, it commonly makes the great apparatus of scholarship unnecessary, and its ends do not require means which can never be within the power of all men.\n\nHow is this? Is it good to be virtuous only because there is another world? Or will actions not rather be rewarded hereafter because they were in themselves good and virtuous? Does the human heart not contain immediate moral precepts? And must we, in order to move man here in accordance with his vocation [Bestimmung], necessarily bring to bear machinery drawn from another world? Can a man fairly be entitled upright, or entitled virtuous, who would gladly give himself up to his favourite vices if only no future punishment frightened him? Must we not rather say that, while he shuns the practice of wickedness, he nourishes the vicious disposition in his soul; that he loves the advantage of actions which resemble virtue, but hates virtue itself? And in fact experience also teaches that many who have been instructed and convinced of the future world nevertheless remain addicted to vice and baseness, and think only of means by which they may craftily evade the threatening consequences of the",["The stray sign after 'Nachforschung' and the interlinear line numbers are OCR residue of the edition's line-numbering; omitted. 'dasse' read as 'hasse' (hates); '[o viele' read as 'so viele'.","The sentence 'Die Eitelkeit ... sehr nöthig sei' has a dangling construction in the German ('zu der Überzeugung ..., diese aber zum Bewegungsgrunde ...'); I have restated it as two clauses and supplied 'that this conviction' for 'diese'.","The long opening of the passage is divided into several sentences; the series of rhetorical questions is kept as questions. 'Bestimmung' is rendered 'vocation' (the sense of man's destined end), with the German given.","'heißen' rendered 'be entitled', per the method. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('den drohenden Folgen der'), continued in the next passage; left unfinished."]],"traeume-0188":["...future. But there has hardly ever lived an upright soul that could have borne the thought that all is at an end with death, and whose noble disposition did not raise itself to the hope of the future. It therefore seems more in keeping with human nature and the purity of morals to found the expectation of the future world upon the sentiments of a well-constituted soul than, conversely, to found its good conduct upon the hope of the other world. Such too is the nature of moral faith, whose simplicity can be spared many a subtlety of sophistical reasoning, and which alone is suited to man in every condition, since it leads him without detour to his true ends.\n\nLet us therefore leave all noisy doctrinal systems on such remote objects to the speculation and the concern of idle heads. They are in fact a matter of indifference to us, and the momentary semblance of the grounds for or against may perhaps decide the applause of the schools, but hardly anything as to the future fate of the upright. Nor was human reason sufficiently winged to part clouds so high as those which withdraw the secrets of the other world from our eyes. To the inquisitive, who enquire so earnestly about it, we may give the simple but very natural answer that it would be most advisable for them to be content to wait patiently until they get there. But since our fate in the future world presumably depends very much on how we have administered our post in the present one, I conclude with what Voltaire, after so many useless scholastic disputes, makes his honest Candide say at the close: Let us look after our happiness, go into the garden and work!",["The passage opens in mid-sentence ('...Zukunft arglistig auszuweichen'), continuing the previous passage; the running head 'Zweiter Theil. 3. Hauptstück.' and the line numbers are omitted. 'rechtschaffene' is rejoined from the line-break hyphenation.","Several long sentences are divided in English (e.g. the sentence on moral faith, and the one on human reason and the clouds); the content is unchanged.","'Empfindungen' is rendered 'sentiments' rather than 'sensations', since moral feeling, not sense-impression, is meant here.","'Verstand' does not occur; 'Vernünfteln' is rendered 'sophistical reasoning'. 'Posten' in 'unsern Posten verwaltet' rendered 'administered our post'."]],"traeume-0189":["he",["The passage consists only of 'er'; probably OCR residue of a page-end mark or ornament rather than a word of the text. Translated as it stands; reading doubtful."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0001":["On the First Ground of the Distinction of Regions in Space.",["'Anterschiedes' is an OCR slip for 'Unterschiedes'. 'Gegenden' rendered 'regions' (as the sense of directions or quarters in space requires)."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0002":["The celebrated Leibniz possessed many real insights, by which he",["Fragment of a sentence continued in the next passage."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0003":["enriched the sciences, but still greater designs for such,",["Fragment; 'solchen' is taken to refer to 'Wissenschaften'/insights, so 'such' is left general."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0004":["the execution of which the world has awaited from him in vain. Whether the",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0005":["cause is to be sought in this: that his attempts still seemed to him too incomplete (a scruple peculiar to men of merit, which has at all times deprived learning of many valuable fragments); or whether it fared with him as Boerhaave conjectures of great chemists, that they often professed secrets as though they were in possession of them,",["'schle— neu' is rejoined as 'schienen' (the OCR reads 'schle-neu'); the parenthesis is marked by me in place of the German dashes/commas, to clarify the construction.","'Kunststücke' rendered 'secrets' (skilled processes); the sense is that the chemists claimed to possess them. This is a choice."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0006":["when in fact they rested only on the persuasion and confidence in their skill that the execution of them could not fail them if they once chose to undertake it, I will",["Fragment: the sentence ('das will ich hier nicht entscheiden') is continued in the next passage. The long sentence is kept in one piece here because it is broken across passages."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0007":["not decide here. At the least it appears that a",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0008":["certain mathematical discipline, which he entitled beforehand *Analysis situs*",["'Analysin situs' is Kant's Latin accusative form, rendered in standard Latin form 'Analysis situs' in italics; the Latin term is kept untranslated ('analysis of position')."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0009":["(among others Buffon lamented its loss in considering the infoldings [Zusammenfaltungen] of nature in the germs [Keimen])",["The relative clause is placed in parentheses to keep the sentence intelligible, since the main verb ('sei', in the next passage) follows. 'Zu—sammenfaltungen' is rejoined."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0010":["has probably never been anything more than a thing of thought [Gedankending]. I do not know exactly how far the object which I here propose for consideration is akin to that which the great man mentioned had in mind; but, judging by the meaning of the word, I am here seeking, philosophically, the",["The sentence at the head of the passage depends on 'Es hat den Anschein, daß ...' in 0007-0009; I have rendered it with the parenthesis in 0009 so that the grammar holds. 'Gedankending' (ens rationis) is kept with the German."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0011":["first ground of the possibility of that whose magnitudes he mathema-",["The passage breaks off mid-word ('mathema—', i.e. 'mathematisch ...'); left unfinished. 'Größen' rendered 'magnitudes'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0012":["…tically [*tisch*] to determine, as I had intended. For the positions of the parts of space in relation to one another presuppose the region [*Gegend*] according to which they are ordered in such a relation. In the most abstract sense, region consists not in the relation of one thing in space to another, which is properly the concept of position [*Lage*], but in the relation of the system of these positions to absolute cosmic space. In the case of everything extended, the position of its parts relatively to one another can be sufficiently known from the thing itself. The region, however, in which this ordering of the parts is directed, refers to the space outside the thing. It does not refer to the places [*Örter*] in that space, since this would be nothing other than the position of the very same parts in an external relation. It refers to universal space as a unity, of which every extension must be regarded as a part. It is no wonder if the reader still finds these concepts very obscure; they are to be made clear only in the course of the argument. I therefore add only this: that my aim in this treatise is to try whether an evident proof may not be found, in the intuitive judgments of extension such as geometry contains, that absolute space, independently of the existence of all matter, and itself as the first ground of the possibility of its composition, has a reality of its own. Everyone knows how vain the efforts of philosophers have been to settle this point once and for all by means of the most abstract judgments of metaphysics. I know of no attempt to carry it out, as it were *a posteriori* (that is, by means of other undeniable propositions which themselves lie outside the province of metaphysics, but which, through their application *in concreto*, can furnish a touchstone of its correctness), except the treatise of the celebrated Euler the elder in the *Historie* of the Royal Academy of Sciences at Berlin for the year 1748. Even this does not wholly attain its end. It shows only the difficulties of giving a determinate meaning to the most general laws of motion if one assumes no other concept of space than that which arises by abstraction from the relation of actual things. It leaves untouched the no less serious difficulties which remain in applying these laws, if one wishes to represent them *in concreto* according to the concept of absolute space. The proof which I seek here is intended to furnish, not to mechanicians, as Herr Euler intended, but to geometers themselves, a convincing ground for asserting the reality of their absolute space with the evidence usual to them. For this purpose I make the following preparation.",["The passage opens mid-word ('tisch'); the preceding word is not given, so I have not completed it. The bracketed *tisch* marks the fragment.","Restated the long sentences on Euler and the proof as several shorter ones; the parenthesis on *a posteriori* is kept.","*Gegend* = 'region'; *Lage* = 'position'; *Ort* = 'place'; *Weltraum* = 'cosmic space'; *Meßkunst* = 'geometry'. *Anschauende Urtheile* = 'intuitive judgments'.","OCR slips silently corrected ('wei— eher' = welcher, 'sich)' dropped). Line numbers omitted. 'Verl.' read as Berlin.","In 'dessen Orter' I take 'dessen' to refer to space outside the thing."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0013":["In corporeal space, because of its three dimensions, we can think of three planes which all intersect one another at right angles. Since we know what is outside us through the senses only in so far as it stands in relation to ourselves, it is no wonder that we take from the relation of these intersecting planes to our body the first ground for generating the concept of regions in space. The plane on which the length of our body stands perpendicularly",["Breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands and continued in the next passage.","'Durchschnittsflächen' = 'intersecting planes'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0014":["is entitled horizontal in relation to us. This horizontal plane gives occasion for the difference of regions which we designate by above and below. Two other planes can stand perpendicularly on this plane and at the same time intersect each other at right angles, so that the length of the human",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage and breaks off again; 'heißt' rendered 'is entitled' per the method."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0015":["body is thought to lie in the line of intersection. One of these",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0016":["vertical planes divides the body into two outwardly similar halves and",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0017":["provides the ground of the difference between the right and left sides. The other, which stands perpendicular to it, makes it possible for us to have the concept of the front and back sides. In the case of a written sheet, for example, we first distinguish the upper from the lower side of the writing. We notice the difference between the front and the back side, and then we attend to the position of the written characters from left to right or conversely. Here the position of the parts, which are arranged on the surface, relatively to one another is always the same, and the figure is in all respects identical, however one may turn the sheet. But the difference",["Sentence continues into the next passage. Stray OCR 'o' and line numbers omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0018":["of the regions counts for so much in this representation [*Vorstellung*], and is so closely bound up with the impression which the visible object makes, that the very same writing, if seen in such a way that everything which before held the opposite region is turned from right to left, becomes unrecognisable.",["Continues the previous passage; ends in the next with 'unkenntlich wird' ('becomes unrecognisable').","Restated 'was vorher die entgegengesetzte Gegend hielt' as a relative clause: what previously occupied the opposite region."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0019":["[The writing] becomes unrecognisable.",["Translated as the sentence's closing fragment ('unkenntlich wird'); the bracketed subject is supplied from the preceding passage and the English of 0018 already includes this clause."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0020":["Even our judgments of the regions of the world are subordinate to the concept which we have of regions in general, in so far as they are determined in relation to the sides of our body. What we otherwise know, in the heavens and on the earth, of relations independently of this fundamental concept are only positions of objects among",["Hyphenated 'un-tergeordnet' joined across passages (0020–0021); sentence continues in 0022.","'Weltgegenden' = 'regions of the world' (cardinal directions)."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0021":["[Already included in the preceding passage's translation.]",["Passage 0021 is the continuation of the sentence begun in 0020; its content ('tergeordnet … Verhältnissen erkennen, das sind nur Lagen der Gegenstände unter einan-') is rendered above and in 0022."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0022":["one another. However well I may know the order of the divisions of the horizon, I can determine the regions by it only in so far as I am conscious toward which hand this order proceeds.",["'Gegenstände unter einander' completed from 0020/0021 ('among one another'). Hyphenation 'Hori— Zonts' corrected to Horizonts."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0023":["[The sentence continues:]",["The content of 0023 ('Wenn ich auch noch so gut … nur bestimmen, indem') is rendered in 0022; 0024 continues."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0024":["And the most exact celestial chart, if besides the position of the stars among one another the region were not also determined by the placing of the draught in relation to my hands, however precisely I might have it in thought,",["'Himmelskarte' = 'celestial chart'; 'Abriß' = 'draught'. Line number omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0025":["would still not put me in a position to know, from a known region,",["The clause 'so genau wie ich sie auch in Gedanken hätte' is placed in 0024 for smoothness."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0026":["for example the north, on which side of the horizon I should have to look for sunrise.",["'3. E.' read as 'z. E.' (zum Exempel)."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0027":["It is the same with geographical knowledge, indeed with our commonest knowledge of the position of places, which is of no help to us",["Sentence continues into the next passage."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0028":["if we cannot set the things so ordered, and the whole system of reciprocal positions, in their regions by reference to the sides of our body. Indeed, a very notable mark of natural productions, which may on occasion itself give ground for distinguishing species, consists in the determinate region toward which the order of their parts is turned. By this two creatures can be distinguished, although they may agree completely in size, in proportion, and even in the position of their parts among one another. The hairs on the crown of the head of all men turn from left to right. All hops wind round their poles from left to right; beans, however, take the opposite turn. Almost all snails, with the exception of perhaps three kinds, have their spiral, if one goes from above downwards, that is from the apex to the mouth, from left to right. This determinate property belongs invariably to one and the same kind of creature, without any relation to the hemisphere in which it is found, or to the direction of the daily motion of the sun and moon, which runs for us from left to right, but for our antipodes in the opposite direction. For in the natural products cited the cause of the winding lies in the seed itself. Where, on the contrary, a certain rotation can be ascribed to the course of these heavenly bodies, as Mariotte claims to have observed such a law in the winds, which from new moon to full moon tend to run through the whole compass from left to right, this circular movement must go round toward the other hand in the other hemisphere. Don Ulloa believes that his observations in the southern sea actually confirm this.",["Divided the long sentences on the hemisphere and Mariotte's law; OCR slips corrected silently (Kürpers, Geschöpfe, Haare, 'jübe lichen' = südlichen, 'ftd' = sich, 'Sonnenund' = Sonnen- und).","'Compaß' rendered 'compass' (the circle of the points of the compass). 'Naturerzeugungen' = 'natural productions'; 'Kennzeichen' = 'mark'.","'Don Ulloa' kept as in the German; 'Meere' rendered 'sea'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0029":["Since the different feeling of the right and left sides is of such great necessity for the judgment of regions, nature has at the same time connected it with the mechanical constitution of the human body. By means of this constitution one side, namely the right, has an undoubted superiority over the left in dexterity, and perhaps also in strength. Hence all peoples of the earth are right-handed (if one sets aside individual exceptions, which, like squinting, cannot overturn the universality of the rule in the natural order). One moves one's body more easily from the right towards the left than in the opposite direction, when one mounts a horse or steps across a ditch. Everywhere one writes with the right hand, and with it one does everything that requires skill and strength. But just as",["Restated the first sentence as two sentences (at 'mechanische Einrichtung'), the relative clause 'vermittelst deren' becoming a new sentence.","'rechtsch' is an OCR slip, read as 'rechtisch' ('right-handed'); 'ges knüpft' read as 'geknüpft'. The stray line numerals are omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0030":["the right side seems to have the advantage over the left in motive power [Bewegkraft],",["The sentence begins in the preceding passage ('So wie aber') and continues in the next; translated as it stands."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0031":["so the left has it over the right in respect of sensi-",["'scheint' is joined to the preceding fragment; the word 'Empfind—samkeit' is broken across passages and is rendered 'sensitiveness' (not 'sensibility', which is reserved for Sinnlichkeit)."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0032":["tiveness, if one may believe certain students of nature, e.g. Dorelli and Bonnet, of whom the former maintains of the left eye, the oth-",["The proper name 'Dorelli' is kept as printed; the identification is doubtful and I have not tried to emend it."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0033":["er also of the left ear, that the sense in them is stronger than in the organs of the same name on the right side. And so the two sides of the human body, notwithstanding their great external similarity, are sufficiently distinguished by a distinct sensation, even if one sets aside the different position of the inner parts and the marked",["'klare Empfindung' rendered 'distinct sensation'; the sentence runs on into the next passage ('merkliche Klopfen des Herzens')."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0034":["[…]ren similarity is sufficiently distinguished by a clear sensation, even if one does not [here] take into account the different position of the inner parts and the perceptible",["The passage begins and breaks off mid-sentence; \"ren\" is the end of a word whose beginning is in the preceding passage (probably \"…[ä]hnlichkeit\" belongs to a noun such as \"deren\"/\"Figuren\" — not recoverable here), so it is rendered as \"[…]ren\" and nothing is supplied.","OCR: \"Ahnlichkeit\" read as \"Ähnlichkeit\"; the stray \"o\" before \"man\" is an OCR slip and is dropped silently.","\"Empfindung\" = sensation; \"gnugsam\" = \"genugsam\", rendered \"sufficiently\".","\"[here]\" is supplied to give \"gleich … wenn\" its concessive sense; \"wenn man gleich\" = \"even if\", i.e. \"wenngleich\" split.","The sentence is left incomplete, as in the source; the object of \"nicht in Betrachtung ziehen\" or similar is not present, so \"do not take into account\" is a gloss on the construction, which in the German is left to the continuation. Reading doubtful: the German as given has no negative, so \"does not\" is a gloss and may mislead — the sense may rather be \"even if one [does] take into account…\"."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0035":["beating of the heart, since this muscle, at each contraction, strikes with its apex, in an oblique movement,",["The word 'merkliche' stands at the end of the preceding passage and is translated there ('the marked'); 'je-desmaligen' is read as 'jedesmaligen'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0036":["against the left side of the breast.",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0037":["We wish therefore to show that the complete determining ground [Bestimmungsgrund] of a bodily figure rests not merely on the relation and position of its parts to one another, but in addition on a rela-",["The passage breaks off mid-word ('Bez' + 'ziehung'); the stray OCR sign after 'Lage' is dropped."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0038":["tion to universal absolute space, as the geometers conceive it,",["'Mep-künstler' is read as 'Meßkünstler' (geometers, literally 'measurers')."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0039":["yet in such a way that this relation cannot be immediately",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0040":["perceived, though certainly those differences of bodies which rest solely and entirely on this ground can be. If two figures drawn on",["The 'o' before 'einzig' is a line number and is omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0041":["a plane are equal and similar to one another, [so] they coincide with one an-",["The OCR reads '[o decken'; I take it as 'so decken', a slip, not an editorial bracket. The bracketed 'so' follows the printed sign, and the reading is doubtful.","'decken' (cover, coincide) is the term of congruence and is kept consistently."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0042":["other. But with bodily extension, or also with lines",[]],"gegenden-im-raume-0043":["and surfaces that do not lie in one plane, it is often quite otherwise. They can be wholly equal and similar, and yet so different in themselves that the boundaries of the one cannot at the same time be the boundaries of the | other. A screw-thread which is carried round its spindle from left to right will never fit into a nut whose turns run from right to left, al- | though the thickness of the spindle and the number of the turns at equal height agree. A spherical triangle can be wholly equal and similar to another without yet coinciding with it. But the commonest and clearest example we have in the limbs of the human body, which are arranged symmetrically with respect to its vertical plane. The right hand is similar and equal to the left; and if one looks merely at one of them alone, at the proportion and position of the parts among one another and at the magnitude of the whole, a complete description of the one must in all respects hold also of the other.",["The Academy sign | is kept where it stands, including mid-word ('ob- | gleich').","'Spille' is taken as 'Spindel' (spindle). 'Kirpers', 'Dide', 'klärste' etc. in the surrounding text are silent OCR corrections; line numerals omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0044":["I call a body which is wholly equal and similar to another, though it cannot be enclosed within the same boundaries, its incongruent counterpart [incongruentes Gegenstück]. To show its possibility, let one assume a body which does not consist of two halves arranged symmetrically about a single plane of section, but, say, a human hand. Let one drop perpendiculars from all points of its surface upon a tablet set opposite it, and extend them behind the tablet just as far as these points lie in front of it. Then the end-points of the lines so extended, when they are | joined, make up the surface of a bodily figure which is the incongruent counterpart of the former; that is, if the given hand is a right, its counterpart is a left. The image of an object in a mirror rests on precisely the same grounds. For the object always appears just as far behind the mirror as it stands in front of its surface, and therefore the image of a right hand in it is always a left. If the object itself consists of two incongruent counterparts, like the human body when it is divided from front to back by a vertical section, its image is congruent with it. This one readily recognises, if one lets the image in thought make a half turn; for the counterpart of the counterpart of an object is necessarily congruent with it.",["Restated the long middle sentence as several; 'ver- |f hunden' read as 'verbunden', with | kept at the break.","Stray OCR letters and line numerals ('f', 'as', 'a', '20') omitted; 'Kirpers' and similar slips silently corrected."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0045":["So much may suffice to understand the possibility of spaces wholly similar and equal and yet incongruent. We now pass to the philosophical application of these concepts. It is already evident from the common example of the two hands that the figure of one body to the",["The passage breaks off after the dative 'der' (Figur eines Körpers der | Figur eines andern); the construction is completed in the next passage and is not completed here.","The stray OCR letter 'c' is omitted."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0046":["figure of another can be wholly similar, and the magnitude of extension entirely equal, so that there nevertheless remains an inner difference, namely this: that the surface which bounds the one cannot possibly enclose the other. This surface bounds the bodily space of the one, and cannot serve as boundary to the other, however one may turn and twist it. This difference must therefore be one that rests on an inner ground. But this inner ground of the difference cannot depend on the different manner in which the parts of the body are connected with one another; for, as one sees from the example given, in this respect everything may be wholly identical. Nevertheless, if one represents to oneself that the first piece of creation was to be a human hand, it is necessarily ei- | ther a right or a left; and to produce the one, a different act of the creating cause was required from that by which its counterpart could be made.",["Divided the sentence beginning 'Weil diese Oberfläche' into two, and gave 'so muß' its logical force with 'therefore'.","The page number '357' (an interpolated numeral) and the OCR fragment 'rw' are omitted; the | is kept in 'ent-|weder'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0047":["If one now adopts the concept of many recent philosophers, especially the Germans, that space consists solely in the external relation of the parts of matter lying beside one another, then all actual space in the case cited would be only that which this hand occupies. But since there is no difference at all in the relation of its parts among themselves, whether it be a right or a left, this hand would be wholly indeterminate in respect of such a property; that is, it would fit either side of the human body, which is impossible.",["'vornehm-lich' read as 'vornehmlich'. 'unbestimmt' is rendered 'indeterminate', in keeping with Bestimmung = determination."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0048":["It is clear from this that the determinations [Bestimmungen] of space are not consequences of the positions of the parts of matter relatively to one another, but that these are consequences of those; and that accordingly differences, and indeed true differences, can be found in the constitution of bodies, which",["The sentence continues in the next passage ('die ... sich lediglich auf ... beziehen'); the stray OCR 'ch' at the start of the next passage is the end of 'sich'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0049":["relate solely to absolute and original space, because only through it is the relation of corporeal things possible. And [it is clear] that, since absolute space is no object of outer sensation [Empfindung], but a fundamental concept [Grundbegriff] which first makes all such sensation possible, we can apprehend that which in the figure of a body concerns solely its relation to pure space only by comparing the body with other bodies.",["Divided the sentence at 'und daß', supplying '[it is clear]' in brackets, since the second 'daß' clause depends on 'Es ist hieraus klar' in the previous passage.","'dieselbe' is taken to refer to 'äußeren Empfindung'; 'Gegenhaltung' is rendered 'comparing ... with'; 'Kör—peru' read as 'Körpern'."]],"gegenden-im-raume-0050":["A reflective reader will therefore not regard the concept of space, as the geometer thinks it and as acute philosophers have also adopted it into the doctrinal system of natural science, as a mere thought-entity [*Gedankending*]. There is, however, no lack of difficulties surrounding this concept, if one tries to grasp its reality, which for inner sense is sufficiently evident in intuition, through ideas of reason. But this difficulty shows itself everywhere, whenever one still wishes to philosophise about the first data of our knowledge. It is never so decisive as the difficulty which comes to the fore when the consequences of an assumed concept contradict the most evident experience.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'Diesen' read 'diesen'; the damaged '[o entschei— hend als diejenige, welche sich)' read as 'so entscheidend als diejenige, welche sich'; 'angeiommenen' read 'angenommenen'; 'unlerer' read 'unserer'.","Divided the last sentence of the German into two in English, and made 'welche' (the Realität) refer to the reality, not to the concept.","'dem innern Sinne anschauend gnug' rendered 'for inner sense sufficiently evident in intuition'; 'Lehrbegriff' as 'doctrinal system'; 'Erkenntniß' as 'knowledge'."]],"de-mundi-0001":["ON THE FORM AND PRINCIPLES\nOF THE SENSIBLE AND INTELLIGIBLE WORLD.\nDISSERTATION\nFOR THE LAWFUL OBTAINING OF THE PLACE OF ORDINARY PROFESSOR OF LOGIC AND METAPHYSICS,\nWHICH, AS THE ACADEMIC STATUTES REQUIRE,\nWILL BE PUBLICLY DEFENDED BY\nIMMANUEL KANT.\nTHE OFFICE OF RESPONDENT WILL BE FILLED BY\nMARCUS HERTZ,\nOF BERLIN, A JEW BY NATION, STUDENT OF MEDICINE AND PHILOSOPHY,\nAGAINST THE OPPONENTS\nGEORG WILHELM SCHREIBER,\nREG. BOR. ART. STUD.,\nJOHANN AUGUST STEIN,\nREG. BOR. I. U. C.,\nAND\nGEORG DANIEL SCHROETER,\nOF ELBING, S. S. THEOL. C.,\nIN THE GREAT AUDITORIUM\nAT THE CUSTOMARY HOURS, MORNING AND AFTERNOON,\nON 21 AUGUST 1770.",["OCR slips silently corrected: PU LICE to PUBLICE, 'Genie' to GENTE, 'Sind.' to STUD., 'Vor.' to BOR., 'Angustum' to AUGUSTUM, 'Ang.' to AUG.","The Latin abbreviations (Reg. Bor. = Regiomonti Borussus / of the Royal Prussian realm; I. U. C. = *iuris utriusque cultor*; S. S. Theol. C. = *sacrosanctae theologiae cultor*) are left as they stand, since their expansion is conjectural here."]],"de-mundi-0002":["TO THE MOST AUGUST, MOST SERENE AND MOST POWERFUL PRINCE AND LORD, LORD FREDERICK, KING OF THE PRUSSIANS, MARGRAVE OF BRANDENBURG, ARCH-CHAMBERLAIN OF THE HOLY ROMAN EMPIRE AND ELECTOR, SUPREME DUKE OF SILESIA, ETC. ETC. ETC., MOST CLEMENT FATHER OF THE FATHERLAND, HIS MOST INDULGENT KING AND LORD,",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'OLEMENTISSIMO' to CLEMENTISSIMO; 'Brauchen BURGICO' to BRANDENBURGICO. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"de-mundi-0003":["offers, with devout mind, these first-fruits of the office entrusted to him,",["'has' refers to the *primitias* (first-fruits); the sentence continues from the dedication above."]],"de-mundi-0004":["his most submissive\nIMMANUEL KANT.",["*subiectissimus*: 'most submissive' (literally 'most subject')."]],"de-mundi-0005":["SECTION I.\nOn the notion of the world in general.",["OCR 'SECTIO L' read as 'SECTIO I'."]],"de-mundi-0006":["In a substantial composite, just as analysis terminates only in a part which is not a whole, i.e. the SIMPLE, so synthesis terminates only in a whole which is not a part, i.e. the WORLD.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'isi' to 'nisi', 'noch' to 'non'. The capitals are those of the source."]],"de-mundi-0007":["In this exposition of the underlying concept I have had regard not only to the marks which belong to a distinct knowledge of the object, but also, to some extent, to the twofold genesis of that entity's nature. Since this can serve by way of example as a method for penetrating more deeply into matters in metaphysics, it seems to me not a little commendable. For it is one thing to conceive for oneself the composition of a whole, the parts being given, through the abstract notion of the understanding; it is another to carry out this general notion, as a problem of reason, through the sensitive faculty of knowledge, i.e. to represent it to oneself *in concreto* by a distinct intuition. The former takes place through the concept of composition in general, in so far as several things are contained under it (respectively, in relation to one another), and hence through ideas of the understanding and universal ideas. The latter rests on the conditions of time, in so far as, by adjoining part to part successively, the concept of a composite is possible genetically, i.e. by SYNTHESIS, and belongs to the laws of intuition. In like manner, when a substantial composite is given, one easily arrives at the idea of the simple by removing the intellectual notion of composition in general; for what remains, when all conjunction is removed, is simple. According to the laws of intuitive knowledge, however, this does not happen. That is to say, all composition is removed only by regressing from the given whole to any possible parts whatever, i.e. by analysis*), which again rests on the condition of time. But since a composite requires a multitude of parts, and a whole requires allness (*omnitudo*), neither analysis nor synthesis will be complete. Consequently neither will the concept of the simple emerge through the former, nor the concept of the whole through the latter, unless each can be completed in a finite and assignable time.",["Divided the long German-Latin period into several sentences; supplied nothing beyond what the Latin requires.","OCR slips silently corrected (e.g. 'lates' to 'datis', 'abtractam' to 'abstractam', 'repective' to 'respective', 'iniversales' to 'universales', 'condicionibus' retained). The reading 'illius e entis natura genesin' is damaged; I read it as 'illius entis naturae genesin' and the sense is doubtful.","*facultas cognoscendi sensitiva*: 'sensitive faculty of knowledge'; *cognitio* is rendered 'knowledge'; *intuitus* 'intuition'; *intellectus* 'understanding'. The footnote marker *) is kept where it stands."]],"de-mundi-0008":["*) To the words analysis and synthesis a twofold meaning is commonly assigned. Synthesis, namely, is either qualitative, a progress in a series of subordinates from ground to grounded; or quantitative, a progress in a series of coordinates from a given part, through its complements, to the whole. In like manner analysis, taken in the former sense, is a regress from the grounded to the ground; in the latter sense, however, it is a regress from the whole to its | possible or mediate parts, i.e. the parts of parts, and hence is not a division but a subdivision of the given composite. Both synthesis and analysis we take here only in the latter sense. |",["OCR 'com- : positi' joined as 'compositi'. The Academy line-break signs | are carried over where they stand.","Divided the first Latin sentence at the 'Nempe' into two sentences in English."]],"de-mundi-0009":["**) Those who reject the actual mathematical infinite undertake no very heavy labour. They hit, namely, upon such a definition of the infinite as will allow them to dig some contradiction out of it. The infinite is said by them to be: a quantum than which a greater is impossible; | and the mathematical infinite: a multitude (of assignable unity) than which a greater is impossible. | But since they here put the maximum for the infinite, and a maximal multitude is impossible, they easily conclude against an infinite fabricated by themselves. Or they call an infinite multitude an infinite number, and teach that this is absurd, which is certainly obvious, but is a battle only with the shadows of the mind. If, however, they had conceived the mathematical infinite as a quantum which, related to a measure as unity, is a multitude greater than every number; if they had further noted that measurability here denotes only a relation to the standard of the human understanding, by which one can reach a definite concept of multitude, and, by completing this progress in finite time, a complete concept, which is called number, | only by successively adding one to one: | they would have seen clearly that what does not agree with the fixed law of a certain subject does not on that account exceed all intellection. For there may be given an understanding which, without successive application of a measure, discerns a multitude distinctly at one glance, though assuredly not a human understanding.",["OCR 'intellec' read as 'intellectus'. The Academy line-break signs | are carried over as they stand.","Restated the long conditional period (*Si vero ... conceperint ... si porro notassent ... perspexissent*) with its apodosis kept at the end; 'completum' rendered 'a complete concept' by supplying 'concept' from the sense.","*multitudo (unitatis dabilis)*: 'multitude (of assignable unity)'. *intellectus* is 'understanding' in the last sentence and 'intellection' for *intellectio*."]],"de-mundi-0010":["But since in the continuous quantum the regress from the whole to the assignable parts, | and in the infinite the progress from parts to a given whole, | lack a terminus, and therefore analysis on the one side and synthesis on the other cannot be completed, the whole in the former case cannot be completely thought, according to the laws of intuition, as regards composition, nor the composite in the latter case as regards totality. Hence it is evident how it comes about that, since the unrepresentable and the impossible are commonly held to have the same meaning, the concepts of the continuous as well as of the infinite are rejected by very many, inasmuch as their representation, according to the laws of intuitive knowledge, is wholly impossible. Although I do not here plead the cause of these notions, which have been expelled from not a few schools, especially of the former**), it will yet be of the greatest moment to have pointed out that those who employ so perverse a manner of arguing fall into a most grave error. For whatever conflicts with the laws of understanding and reason is certainly impossible; but what, being an object of pure reason, is merely not subject to the laws of intuitive knowledge, is not impossible. For this dissension between the sensitive and the intellectual faculty (whose nature I shall shortly set forth) indicates nothing, save that the abstract ideas which the mind receives from the understanding often cannot be carried out *in concreto* and converted into intuitions. This subjective reluctance, however, for the most part feigns some objective repugnance, and easily deceives the incautious, since the limits by which the human mind is circumscribed are taken for those in which the very essence of things is contained.",["OCR slips silently corrected ('im- | possibile' joined, 'jutuntur' to 'utuntur', 'e non paucis' to 'ex non paucis'). Line-break signs | and the footnote marker **) are kept where they stand.","Restated the first Latin period as two sentences; 'non item' rendered 'is not impossible' by supplying 'impossible' from the preceding clause.","'prioris' taken to refer to the continuous (*continuum*), the first of the two notions named."]],"de-mundi-0011":["Moreover, that when substantial composites are given, whether by the testimony of the senses or in any other way, both simples and a world are given, is readily evident from an argument drawn from the grounds of the understanding. In our definition I have pointed out, as it were with the finger, the causes also contained in the nature of the subject, lest the notion of the world should seem merely arbitrary and, as happens in mathematics, contrived only for the deduction of consequences from it. For the mind, intent upon the concept of a composite, both in resolving and in composing it, demands for itself, and presumes, termini in which it may acquiesce on the prior side as well as on the posterior.",["OCR slips silently corrected ('eunque' read 'cunque', 'patescat' retained, 'sognitionis' not present here, 'à priori' kept in sense).","Restated the opening, an accusative-and-infinitive clause governed by *patescat*, as a plain 'that ... is readily evident' sentence.","*tam à priori quam a posteriori parte*: 'on the prior side as well as on the posterior'; the sense is of the two ends of the series, not of *a priori*/*a posteriori* knowledge."]],"de-mundi-0012":["The moments to be attended to in the definition of the world are these:",[]],"de-mundi-0013":["I. MATTER (in the transcendental sense), i.e. the parts, which are here taken to be substances. We might be altogether indifferent as to the agreement of our definition with the common meaning of the word, since it is only, as it were, a question about a problem arising according to the laws of reason: how several substances can coalesce into one, and on what conditions it rests that this one is not a part of another. But the force of the word *world*, as it is current in ordinary usage, meets us unbidden. For no one in his senses counts accidents as parts of the world; they are determinations of its state. Hence the so-called egoistic world, which consists of a single simple substance with its accidents, is very inappropriately called a world, unless perhaps an imaginary one. For the same reason it is not permissible to refer to the cosmic whole a series of successive things (namely, states) as a part; for modifications are not parts of the subject, but things grounded (*rationata*). Finally, I have not here called into account the nature of the substances which constitute the world, whether they are contingent or necessary. Nor do I gratuitously hide such a determination in the definition, in order afterwards, as happens, to draw it out again from the same source by some specious manner of argument. I shall show later that contingency can abundantly be concluded from the conditions here laid down.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'quipote' read as 'quomodo/quippe' and rendered 'how'; 'amentia' is doubtful (probably 'amens'), rendered 'no one in his senses'; 'incurii' read 'incurii' = 'indifferent'.","Divided the final Latin sentence into three in English; 'Hinc ... egoisticus' is rendered with 'so-called' for *sic dictus*.","*rationata*: 'things grounded', kept in Latin italics as Kant's technical term; *determinationes* 'determinations'."]],"de-mundi-0014":["II. The form, which consists in the coordination of substances, not in their subordination. For things coordinate regard one another as complements to a whole; things subordinate regard one another as effect and cause, or, in general terms, as principle and what is derived from principle [*principatum*]. The former relation is reciprocal and homonymous, so that each | correlate regards the other as determining and at the same time as determined; the latter is heteronymous, being on the one side one of dependence only, on the other of causality. This coordination is conceived as real and objective, not as ideal and resting on the mere choice of the subject, whereby, by summing any multi- | tude at pleasure, you could fashion a whole. For by comprehending several things together you produce, with no trouble, a whole of representation, but not thereby a representation of a whole. Hence, if there were perchance certain wholes of substan- | ces bound together by no nexus, the act of comprehending them, by which the mind forces a multitude into one ideal [whole], would signify nothing more than a plurality of worlds comprehended in a single thought. The nexus, however, which constitutes the essential form of the world, is regarded as the principle of the possible influxes of the substances that constitute the world. For actual influxes belong not to the essence but to the state; and the transeunt forces themselves, which are the causes of influxes, presuppose some principle through which it is possible that the states of several things, whose subsistence is otherwise independent of one another, should regard one another mutually as grounded [*rationata*]. If you depart from this principle, you are not entitled to assume transeunt force in the world as possible. This form, essential to the world, is therefore immutable and subject to no vicissitude; and that, first, on a logical ground, because any change presupposes the identity of the subject, with determinations succeeding one another. Hence the world, remaining the same world through all its successive states, | preserves the same fundamental form. For to the identity of the whole the identity of the parts does not suffice; what is required is identity of the characteristic composition. But the same follows above all from a real ground. For the nature of the world, which is the first internal principle of all variable determinations belonging to its state, cannot be opposed to itself, and is therefore naturally, that is, of itself, immutable. Accordingly there is given in every world a certain form, to be reckoned to its nature, constant and invariable, as the perennial principle of every contingent and transitory form that pertains to the state of the world. Those who neglect this inquiry | are deluded by the concepts of space and time, taking them as conditions already given in themselves and primitive, by whose aid, namely, without any other prin- ciple, it is not only possible but necessary that several actual things should regard one another as co-parts and constitute a whole. But I shall shortly show that these notions are by no means rational, nor objective ideas of any connexion, but appearances [*phaenomena*]; that they testify, indeed, to some common principle of universal connexion, but do not set it forth.",["The stray marks 'n. flucuum' and 'Actuales ©' are OCR slips, read as 'influxuum' and 'Actuales'; 'sorte' read as 'forte'; 'Cipio' as 'principio'; 'pertinét' as 'pertinet'. The | signs are kept where they fall in the sense, though the English word-order cannot match the Latin line-breaks exactly.","Restated: the long sentence beginning 'Et haec quidem forma…' is divided; 'primo ob rationem logicam' is rendered as 'first, on a logical ground'. The corresponding 'secondly' is the following 'Potissimum… e ratione reali'.","*principium et principatum*: 'principatum' is rendered 'what is derived from principle'; this is a choice, since the word is unusual. 'Ut causatum et causa' is rendered 'as effect and cause'.","*rationata* is left in Latin with 'grounded' beside it. *Homonyma*/*heteronyma* are given as 'homonymous'/'heteronymous'.","'Qui hanc disquisitionem insuper habent' is rendered 'Those who neglect this inquiry'. 'Compartes' is rendered 'co-parts'. 'Phaenomena' is rendered 'appearances' with the Latin supplied.","'Totum repraesentationis' / 'repraesentationem totius' are rendered 'a whole of representation' / 'a representation of a whole'. '[whole]' after 'one ideal' is supplied."]],"de-mundi-0015":["III. Universality, which is the absolute allness [*omnitudo*] of the co-parts. For if regard is had to some given composite, although that composite is itself still a part of another, there nevertheless always obtains a certain comparative allness, namely of the parts belonging to that quantum. Here, however, whatever things regard one another as co-parts of any whole whatever are understood as posited conjointly. This absolute totality, although it has the appearance of a concept that is commonplace and easily met with, especially when it is stated negatively, as is done in the definition, yet when more deeply weighed seems to prove a torment to the philosopher. For how the never-to-be-completed series of the states of the universe, succeeding one another to eternity, can be reduced to a whole comprehending absolutely all vicissitudes, can hardly be conceived. By the very nature of infinity the series must lack a limit; hence there is given no series of successive things that is not a part of another, so that, for the same reason, complete allness or absolute totality seems to be plainly banished from this field. For although the notion of a part can be taken universally, and whatever is contained under this notion constitutes one [whole] if it is regarded as posited in the same series, yet the concept of a whole seems to demand that all these things be taken together; and this, in the given case, is impossible. For since nothing succeeds the whole series, and since, once a series of successive things is posited, there is none to which nothing succeeds except the last, there would be in eternity a last; which is absurd. The difficulty which presses upon the totality of the successive infinite someone may perhaps have thought absent from the simultaneous infinite, on the ground that simultaneity seems expressly to profess the complex of all things at the same time. But if the simultaneous infinite is admitted, the totality of the successive infinite must also be conceded; and if the latter is denied, the former is removed as well. For the simultaneous infinite affords to eternity an inexhaustible matter for progressing successively through its innumerable parts to infinity; and yet this series would be given, complete in every respect, in the simultaneous infinite, so that a series which can never be completed by successive addition would nevertheless be capable of being given as a whole. Whoever would extricate himself from this thorny question may note that the coordination of several things, successive as well as simultaneous (because it rests on concepts of time), does not pertain to the intellectual concept of a whole, but only to the conditions of sensitive intuition; and that, even though they cannot be conceived sensitively, they do not for that reason cease to be intellectual. For this concept it suffices that things coordinated in whatever manner be given, and that all be thought as belonging to one.",["The | marks of the source fall at mid-word line-breaks and at 'intuitus' / 'tamen', and cannot be reproduced in English without disturbing the sense; they are therefore omitted in this passage, which departs from the instruction to carry them over. Please restore them from the Academy text if wanted.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'du eadem serie' read as 'in eadem serie'; '|f: intuitus' as 'intuitus'; 'ia- | wen' as 'tamen'; 'js sufficit' as 'sufficit'; 'Cipio'-type fragments omitted; 'hao' as 'hac'.","'Crucem figere philosopho videtur' is rendered 'seems to prove a torment to the philosopher'; the literal image is of fixing the philosopher to a cross.","Restated: the long sentence from 'Quae infiniti successivi totalitatem…' is divided at the semicolon, and 'Quippe per infinitudinem ipsam…' is rendered in two clauses, joined with a semicolon. '[whole]' after 'constitutes one' is supplied.","*Omnitudo* is retained in Latin beside 'allness'; 'universitas' is rendered 'universality' and 'totalitas' 'totality'. 'Intuitus sensitivi' is rendered 'sensitive intuition'."]],"de-mundi-0016":["SECTION II.",[]],"de-mundi-0017":["On the distinction of the sensible and the intelligible in general.",[]],"de-mundi-0018":["Sensibility [*sensualitas*] is the receptivity of the subject through which it is possible that its representative | state should be affected in a certain manner by the presence of some object. Intelligence (rationality) is the faculty of the subject through which it is able to represent what, by its quality, cannot fall within its senses. The object of sensibility is the sensible; that, however, which contains nothing but what is to be known through intelligence is the intelligible. The former was called in the schools of the ancients *phaenomenon*, the latter *noumenon*. Knowledge, so far as it is subject to the laws of sensibility, is sensitive; so far as it is subject to those of intelligence, it is intellectual or rational.",["'repraensentativus' is an OCR slip for 'repraesentativus'. The | is kept where it stands, after 'representative'.","*Sensualitas* is rendered 'sensibility' (the Latin kept in brackets); *receptivitas* 'receptivity', *facultas* 'faculty', *cognitio* 'knowledge'. *Sensitiva* is rendered 'sensitive'.","'Prius… posterius' is rendered 'The former… the latter'; 'audiebat' (was called) is rendered 'was called'."]],"de-mundi-0019":["Since, therefore, whatever in knowledge is sensitive depends upon the special character of the subject, so far as it is capable of this or that modification by the presence of objects, which modification may be different in different subjects according to their variety; and since, on the other hand, any knowledge that is exempt from such subjective condition regards the object alone: it is evident that what is thought sensitively is the representation of things as they appear, whereas what is thought intellectually is the representation of them as they are. In the representation of sense there is, however, first something which you might call the matter, namely sensation; and in addition something which may be called the form, namely the species of the sensibles, which arises so far as the manifold that affects the senses is coordinated by a certain natural law of the mind. Further, sensation, which constitutes the matter of sensuous representation, does indeed argue the presence of something sensible, but as to its quality depends on the nature of the subject, so far as this is capable of being modified by that object. In the same way the form of that representation testifies to a certain respect or relation of the *sensa*; but it is not properly an outline or schema of the object, but only a certain law inherent in the mind, of coordinating with one another the *sensa* which arise from the presence of the object. For it is not through form or species that objects strike the sense; and therefore, in order that the manifold which affects the sense from the object may coalesce into some whole of representation, there is need of an internal principle of the mind, through which that manifold may assume a certain species according to stable and innate laws.",["OCR slips corrected: 'exempla' read as 'exempta'; 'di- 2 versis' as 'diversis' (the 2 is a line number); 'd potest' as 'potest'; 'Jopus' as 'opus'.","Restated: the first sentence, with its 'cum… quae… quaecunque autem… patet' structure, is divided into 'Since… and since… it is evident that', and the 'quemadmodum… ita etiam' sentence is split into two sentences joined by 'In the same way'.","*sensa* is left in Latin and italicised; 'quendam sensorum respectum' is read as a relation of the *sensa*, though 'sensorum' could also be taken as 'of the senses'. This is a judgment call.","*Species* is kept as the English 'species' (cf. Kant's use for the appearance or look of the sensibles); *sensatio* is rendered 'sensation'; *forma* 'form', *materia* 'matter'.","'Sicuti sunt'/'uti apparent' are rendered 'as they are'/'as they appear'. The line-break marks present in the source have no counterpart in this passage that survives the correction of the OCR."]],"de-mundi-0020":["To sensual knowledge, therefore, belongs both the matter, which is *sensatio* (sensation), and by which modes of knowledge are entitled sensual, and the form, by which representations, even if it is found apart from all sensation, are entitled *sensitivae*. As regards the intellectual, it must above all be carefully noted that the use of the understanding, or of the higher faculty of the soul, is twofold. In the first use the concepts themselves, whether of things or of relations, are given; this is the REAL USE. In the second use, concepts, from whatever source given, are merely subordinated to one another, the lower to the higher (common marks), and are compared with one another in accordance with the principle of contradiction; this use is entitled the LOGICAL. The logical use of the understanding is common to all the sciences; the real use is not. For knowledge, given in whatever manner, is regarded either as contained under a mark common to several [modes of knowledge], or as opposed to such a mark; and this takes place either immediately and proximately, as happens in judgments [*iudiciis*], with a view to distinct knowledge, or mediately, as in syllogisms, with a view to adequate knowledge. Where sensitive modes of knowledge are given, therefore, then through the logical use of the understanding sensitive [representations] are subordinated to other sensitive ones, as to common concepts, and phenomena to more general laws of phenomena. But it is of the greatest importance here to have noted that modes of knowledge are always to be regarded as sensitive, however great the logical use the understanding may have made of them. For they are entitled sensitive on account of their genesis, not on account of a comparison in respect of identity or opposition. Hence the most general empirical laws are none the less sensual; and the principles of sensitive form which are found in geometry (relations determined in space), however much the understanding may be occupied with them, arguing by logical rules from what is sensitively given (through pure intuition), do not nevertheless exceed the class of the sensitive. In the sensual and in phenomena, however, that which precedes the logical use of the understanding is entitled *apparentia* (appearance); but the reflected knowledge which arises when several appearances are compared by the understanding is entitled experience. There is thus no road from appearance to experience save through reflection in accordance with the logical use of the understanding. The common concepts of experience are entitled empirical, and the objects *phaenomena*; while the laws both of experience and, in general, of all sensitive knowledge are entitled laws of phenomena. Empirical concepts, therefore, do not become intellectual in the real sense by being reduced to a greater universality; they do not exceed the species of sensitive knowledge, but, however far they ascend by abstraction, remain sensitive indefinitely.",["The OCR reads '?nd?c?is'; I have read it as *iudiciis* ('judgments'), which the contrast with *ratiociniis* supports. This is a conjecture.","I render *cognitio* as 'knowledge' and the plural *cognitiones* as 'modes of knowledge'. *Sensualis* and *sensitivus* are both kept distinct as 'sensual' and 'sensitive'.","*apparentia* is left in Latin with 'appearance' beside it, so that it can be told apart from *phaenomena* (German Erscheinung would cover both).","I added 'knowledge' in 'common to several [modes of knowledge]' and 'sensitive [representations]'. The Latin has only an adjective here.","I divided the long sentence beginning 'Data enim quomodocunque…' no further. I broke the opening sentence at 'Quod ab altera parte…' into separate sentences for clarity."]],"de-mundi-0021":["As regards the intellectual strictly so called, in which the use of the understanding is real, such concepts, whether of objects or of relations, | are given through the very nature of the understanding; they are not abstracted from any use of the senses, and contain no form whatever of sensitive knowledge, as such. | Here, however, it is necessary to note a very great ambiguity in the word *abstract*. Lest it should stain our inquiry into the intellectual, I think it better to clear it away beforehand. Strictly speaking, one ought to say: to abstract from some things [*ab ali-* | *quibus abstrahere*], not to abstract something [*aliquid abstrahere*]. The former signifies that in a given concept we do not attend to other things which are connected with it in any manner; | the latter, that the concept is not given except in the concrete, and in such a way that it is separated from what is conjoined with it. | Hence an intellectual concept abstracts from everything sensitive; it is not abstracted from the sensitive, and would perhaps be more correctly entitled abstracting than abstracted. It is therefore more advisable to name the intellectual concepts pure ideas, and those concepts which are given only empirically, abstracted concepts.",["The line numeral '1' after 'in-' and the OCR split 'all- | quibus' (= *aliquibus*) are silently corrected; the | signs are kept where they stand.","The stray ':' before 'non abstrahitur' is OCR residue and omitted.","Kant's grammatical point, *ab aliquibus abstrahere* against *aliquid abstrahere*, is kept in Latin, with the English beside it in square brackets.","'Pure ideas' for *ideas puras*; *idea* is left as 'idea'."]],"de-mundi-0022":["From this it can be seen that the sensitive is wrongly explained as what is known more confusedly, and the intellectual as that of which there is distinct knowledge. For these are merely logical distinctions, and do not at all touch the data which underlie every logical comparison. The sensitive can be altogether distinct, and the intellectual extremely confused. The former we observe in the prototype of sensitive knowledge, geometry; the latter in the organ of all that is intellectual, metaphysics. How much labour metaphysics expends in dispelling the mists of confusion which darken the common understanding, though not always with so happy a success as in the former case, lies open to view. None the less each of these modes of knowledge preserves the mark of its lineage. The former, however distinct, are entitled sensitive on account of their origin; the latter, however confused, remain intellectual, as, for example, moral concepts, which are known not by experience but through the pure understanding itself. I fear, however, that Wolff, by this distinction between the sensitive and the intellectual, which for him is merely logical, has perhaps wholly abolished that most noble undertaking of antiquity, the discussion of the nature of phenomena and noumena, to the great detriment of philosophy, and has often turned minds away from the investigation of these to logical trifles.",["The OCR 'Iii. Worrrivs' is a corrupt reading; I have taken it to be 'Wolffius' (Wolff). Doubtful as a reading, but the sense is hardly in question.","'intellectua. lium' is silently corrected to *intellectualium*; stray 'j' marks and page signs are omitted.","I restated 'quae, quantum operae navet…' as a separate sentence. 'The latter … metaphysics' is the antecedent.","*noümenorum* is rendered 'noumena'; *phaenomena* 'phenomena'. *sensitivus* is 'sensitive', *intellectualis* 'intellectual'."]],"de-mundi-0023":["First philosophy, however, which contains the principles of the use of the pure understanding, is METAPHYSICS. The science which is propaedeutic to it is that which teaches the distinction of sensitive from intellectual knowledge, of which in this our dissertation we present a specimen. Since, therefore, no empirical principles are to be found in metaphysics, the concepts met with in it are not to be sought in the senses, but in the very nature of the pure understanding. They are not innate concepts [*conceptus connati*]; they are abstracted from laws inherent in the mind (by attending to its actions on the occasion of experience), and are therefore acquired. Of this kind are possibility, existence, necessity, substance, cause, etc., with their opposites or correlates. Since these never enter as parts into any sensual representation, they could in no way have been abstracted from it.",["The stray marks 'Í' and the | signs at line ends are OCR residue or edition apparatus; the | signs inside words are not reproduced since they fall at line breaks only, but no words are affected.","*connati* is rendered 'innate' (the Latin gives no German); *acquisiti* 'acquired'.","I divided the sentence at 'non tanquam conceptus connati…' for clarity; the sense is unchanged."]],"de-mundi-0024":["Rig:",["This passage is only OCR residue ('Rig:'), probably a running head or page mark. It is left as it stands and not translated."]],"de-mundi-0025":["The intellectual has a twofold chief end. The former is elenctic, through | […]menis [text corrupt]; and, although [such principles] do not advance science by so much as a nail's breadth, yet they render it immune from the contagion of errors. The latter is dogmatic, in accordance with which the general principles of the pure understanding, such as ex[…] [text lost] [that which is] to be conceived only by the pure understanding, and the common measure of all others as regards realities, which is PERFECTION NOUMENON. This, however, is such either in the theoretical*) or in the practical sense. In the former it is the highest being, God; in the latter sense it is MORAL PERFECTION. Moral philosophy, therefore, in so far as it supplies the first principles of judging, is known only through the pure understanding and itself belongs to pure philosophy; and he who drew its criteria down to the feeling of pleasure or displeasure, Epicurus, is most justly censured, together with certain moderns who have followed him from afar to some extent, such as Shaftesbury and his followers. In any genus, however, of those things whose quantity is variable, the maximum is the common measure and the principle of knowing. The maximum of perfection is now called the ideal; for Plato it was an idea (as his idea of the republic). It is the principle of all things contained under the general notion of any perfection, in so far as lesser degrees are held to be determinable only by limiting the maximum. God, however, while as the ideal of perfection he is the principle of knowing [*principium cognoscendi*], is as really existing at the same time the principle of becoming [*principium fiendi*] of all perfection whatsoever.",["The text is badly corrupted in the first part: after 'per |' the OCR gives only 'menis', and after 'qualia ex' a stretch is lost before 'nonnisi intellectu puro concipiendum'. I have marked the gaps with […] and have not filled them.","The bracketed 'such principles' and '[that which is]' are supplied only to make the surviving fragments intelligible. Both are doubtful.","The sentence on Epicurus is restated in the order of the English; 'protraxit' is rendered 'drew down'.","*perfectio noumenon* is kept in Latin (Kant's term). '*)' marks the footnote that follows as the next passage.","*principium cognoscendi* and *principium fiendi* are kept in Latin with the English beside them."]],"de-mundi-0026":["*) We regard something theoretically in so far as we attend only to what pertains to a being; practically, however, if we examine what ought to inhere in it through freedom.",["Footnote to the preceding passage. The stray 'È' is OCR residue and omitted.","'debebant' (imperfect) is rendered by the plain 'ought to inhere'."]],"de-mundi-0027":["Of the intellectual there is no intuition (for man), but only symbolic knowledge, and intellection is permitted us only through universal concepts in the abstract, not through a singular concept in the concrete. For all our intuition is bound to a certain principle of form, under which alone something can be discerned by the mind immediately, or as singular, and not merely conceived discursively through general concepts. Now this formal principle of our knowledge is given [only] with matter from the senses [*materia non datur nisi a sensibus*]. But a noumenon, as such, is not to be conceived through representations drawn from sensations; therefore an intelligible concept, as such, is destitute of all the data of human intuition. For the intuition of our mind is always passive, and hence is possible only in so far as something can affect our senses. Divine intuition, on the other hand, being the principle of objects, and not something derived from them (*principiatum*), is, since it is independent, archetypal, and for that reason perfectly intellectual.",["The Latin 'Principium autem hoc formale nostrae cognitionis materia non datur nisi a sensibus' is doubtful as printed; I have taken *materia* as ablative ('with matter') and given the Latin in brackets. Another reading of the clause is possible, and the text may be defective.","I restated 'Divinus autem intuitus, qui obiectorum est | principium, non principiatum…' with the | omitted at the line break within the clause. The | stands in the source at 'est |'; it is carried: 'being the principle of objects' occurs after it.","*intuitus* is rendered 'intuition'; *symbolica cognitio* 'symbolic knowledge'; *intellectio* 'intellection'."]],"de-mundi-0028":["Although phenomena are properly the *species* of things, not ideas, and do not express the internal and absolute quality of objects, nevertheless knowledge of them is most true. For, in the first place, in so far as they are sensual concepts or apprehensions, they testify, as effects, to the presence of the object; and this is against idealism. In so far, however, as you regard judgments concerning what is sensitively known, truth in judging consists in the agreement of the predicate with the given subject. Now the concept of the subject, in so far as it is a phenomenon, is given only through its relation to the sensitive faculty of knowing, and in accordance with the same faculty the predicates also are given as sensitively observable. It is therefore plain that the representations of the subject and of the predicate arise in accordance with common laws, and thus afford ground for the truest knowledge.",["*species* is left in Latin: 'looks' or 'semblances' of things, as opposed to *ideae*.","I divided the very long sentence 'quatenus autem iudicia spectas…' into several; the Latin runs it together with *cum* and *patet*.","*causata* is rendered 'as effects'; *ansam praebere* 'afford ground for'. 'Presence of the object' is literal (*praesentia obiecti*)."]],"de-mundi-0029":["Whatever is referred to our senses as objects is *phaenomena* (appearances). But whatever, since it does not touch the senses, contains only the singular form of sensibility, belongs to pure intuition (that is, intuition void of sensations, yet for that reason not intellectual). Appearances are reviewed and expounded, first those of outer sense in *physica*, then those of inner sense in empirical *psychologia*. Pure (human) intuition, however, is not a universal or logical concept under which, but a singular concept in which, any sensible things whatever are thought. It therefore contains the concepts of space and time. Since these determine nothing concerning the quality of sensible things, they are not objects of science, except in respect of quantity. Hence *mathesis pura* considers space in *geometria*, and time in pure *mechanica*. To these is added a certain concept, intellectual in itself, but one whose actualisation in the concrete requires the aid of the notions of time and space (by successively adding several things, and by placing them beside one another simultaneously). This is the concept of number, with which *arithmetica* deals. Pure mathematics, therefore, since it expounds the form of all our sensitive knowledge, is the *organon* of every intuitive and distinct knowledge. And since its objects are not merely the formal principles of all intuition but themselves original intuitions, it affords knowledge that is wholly true and at the same time a model of the highest evidence for other kinds of knowledge. There is thus a science of sensible things; yet, since they are appearances, there is not a real [*realis*] intellectual insight into them, but only a logical one. Hence it is clear in what sense those who drew from the Eleatic school are to be held to have denied science to appearances.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Puysica, PsycHoLocIA, ArıTHMETICA, ‚actuatio.","*sensualitas* rendered 'sensibility', *cognitio* 'knowledge', *intuitus purus* 'pure intuition'; *phaenomena* given as 'appearances' once, then kept in Latin where it is the technical term.","The long sentence 'Mathesis itaque pura … exemplar' is kept as one sentence, with a second sentence begun at 'And since'.","*intellectio realis* / *logica* is rendered literally; the bracketed *realis* marks the point on which the sense turns."]],"de-mundi-0030":["SECTION III.\nOn the principles of the form of the sensible world.",[]],"de-mundi-0031":["The principle of the form of the universe is that which contains the ground of the universal connection by which all substances and their states belong to one and the same whole, which is called the world. The principle of the form of the sensible world is that which contains the ground of the universal connection of all things, in so far as they are appearances. The form of the intelligible world recognises an objective principle, that is, some cause through which the things that exist in themselves are bound together. But the world, in so far as it is regarded as appearance, that is, in relation to the sensibility of the human mind, recognises no principle of form other than a subjective one, that is, a certain law of the mind through which it is necessary that all things that can be objects of the senses (through their quality) shall seem necessarily to belong to one and the same whole. Whatever, then, the principle of the form of the sensible world may finally be, it embraces only actual things, in so far as they are thought capable of falling under the senses. It therefore embraces neither immaterial substances, which, as such, are by definition wholly excluded from the outer senses, nor the cause of the world, since it is through this cause that the mind itself exists and is endowed with some sense, and it cannot therefore be an object of the senses. That these formal principles of the phenomenal universe are absolutely first and universal, and are, as it were, the schemata and conditions of whatever else is sensitive in human knowledge, and that they are two, namely time and space, I shall now demonstrate.",["OCR 'merus universalis' corrected to 'nexus universalis' (cf. the preceding definition); stray line numbers and note-figures (1, 20, 2) omitted.","Sentence 'Haec principia … demonstrabo' is kept as one sentence, though its accusative-and-infinitive structure is heavy in English.","*phaenomenon* rendered 'appearance'; *sensualitas* 'sensibility'; *mens* 'mind'."]],"de-mundi-0032":["On time.",["The OCR adds a stray 'E' after the heading; I have left it untranslated and treated it as a printing artefact."]],"de-mundi-0033":[". Jg . . . p\"",["Unintelligible OCR residue (probably ornament or a section mark); reproduced as it stands, nothing supplied."]],"de-mundi-0034":["1. The idea of time does not arise from the senses, but is presupposed by them. For whether the things that impinge on the senses are simultaneous or successive can be represented only through the idea of time; nor does succession generate the concept of time, but appeals to it. Hence the notion of time, if it were acquired as though through experience, is very badly defined as the series of actual things existing one after another. For I do not understand what the little word 'after' signifies, unless the concept of time is already presupposed. Things are 'one after another' that exist at different times, just as those are simultaneous that exist at the same time.",["OCR 'mon oritur' corrected to 'non oritur'.","*Sunt enim post se invicem* … is rendered 'Things are one after another' to supply the plural subject *quae*."]],"de-mundi-0035":["2. The idea of time is singular, not general. For any time whatever is thought only as part of one and the same boundless time. If you think of two years, you cannot represent them to yourself except in a determinate position relative to each other; and, if they do not follow immediately upon one another, only as joined to each other by some intermediate time. Which of different times is earlier and which later cannot by any reasoning be defined through marks that the understanding can conceive, unless you are willing to fall into a vicious circle; and the mind does not distinguish this except through a singular intuition. Moreover, you conceive all actual things as posited in time, not as contained under the general notion of time, as under a common mark.",["OCR slips corrected: deberminato, aonnisi, jemporum, iingularem.","'iud ipsius notione generali' read as 'sub ipsius notione generali'; the reading is a conjecture from the sense, and the contrast with *singularis* supports it."]],"de-mundi-0036":["3. The idea of time, therefore, is an intuition; and since it is conceived prior to all sensation, as the condition of the relations to be met with in sensible things, it is an intuition not sensual but pure.",["OCR 'senationem' read as 'sensationem'; 'ob- Forum' read as 'obviorum' (\"to be met with\"), a restoration from the sense of a corrupt text."]],"de-mundi-0037":["4. Time is a continuous quantum, and the principle of the laws of the continuous in the changes of the universe. For a continuum is a quantum that does not consist of simple parts. But since nothing is thought through time except relations, without any given entities related to one another, there is in time, as a quantum, a composition which, if it were conceived as wholly removed, would leave nothing at all remaining [text corrupt here] … the moments are not parts of it, but limits between which time lies. For when two moments are given, time is not given, except in so far as",["Passage is badly damaged by OCR ('bsque' = *absque*; '\"si compositio' probably *est compositio*; 'empe momenta' perhaps *nempe momenta*, preceded by lost words). I have not filled the gap, which is marked by [text corrupt here].","The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage ('…in illis actualia sibi succedunt').","*quantum continuum* is kept as 'continuous quantum'; *simplicibus* rendered 'simple parts'."]],"de-mundi-0038":["in them actual things succeed one another. Therefore, besides a given moment, it is necessary that there be given a time in whose later part there is another moment. | The metaphysical law of continuity, however, is this: all changes are continuous, or flow; that is, opposite states do not succeed one another except through a series of different intermediate states. For since two opposite states are in different moments of time, and between two moments there is always some time intercepted, in whose infinite series of moments the substance is neither in one of the given states nor in the other, and yet is not in none, it will be in different ones, and so on to infinity.",["The passage begins mid-sentence; the opening 'ilis' is read as 'illis'. 'wi continuae' read as 'sunt continuae'.","The final clause is kept elliptical, as in the Latin; 'in different ones' means in different intermediate states."]],"de-mundi-0039":["The celebrated Kästner, in order to subject this law of Leibniz to examination, calls upon its defenders*) to demonstrate that the continuous motion of a point through all the sides of a triangle is impossible; which indeed, granted the law of con- | tinuity, would necessarily have to be proved. Here, then, is the demonstration sought. Let the letters a, b, c denote the three angular points of a rectilinear triangle. If a movable body proceeds with continuous motion along the lines ab, bc, ca, that is, along the whole perimeter of the figure, it must move through the point b in the direction ab, and through the same point b also in the direction bc. But since these motions are different, they cannot be simultaneous. Therefore the moment of presence of the movable point at the vertex b, in so far as it moves in the direction ab, is different from the moment of presence of the movable point at the same vertex b, in so far as it moves according to the direction bc. But between two moments there is time; therefore the movable body is present in the same point for some time, that is, it rests, and therefore does not proceed with continuous motion, which is contrary to the hypothesis. The same demonstration holds for motion along any straight lines that include an assignable angle. Therefore a body does not change direction in continuous motion except along a line no part of which is straight, that is, a curve, according to the tenets of Leibniz. |",["OCR letters (g b ç, 5, 6, de) restored as a, b, c, b and bc from the sense of the demonstration; 'socunduta' read as 'secundum'. These are corrections of obvious slips.","The | signs and the mid-word break 'con- | tinuity' are carried over from the source's apparatus.","The footnote marker *) refers to the footnote given in the next passage."]],"de-mundi-0040":["5. Time is not something objective and real, neither a substance, nor an accident, nor a relation; it is a subjective condition, necessary by the nature of the human mind, for coordinating with one another, by a certain law, sensible things whatever, and is a pure intuition. For we coordinate substances as well as accidents, both according to simultaneity and according to succession, only through the concept of time; and therefore the notion of time, as the principle of form, is prior to their concepts. As to relations, or respects of any kind, in so far as they are met with by the senses, namely whether things are simultaneous or one after another, they involve nothing else than positions to be determined in time, either in the same point of it or in different ones. |",["'der' before 'sensibilia' is corrupt OCR; I have read the construction as 'for coordinating sensible things' and supplied 'whatever' to render a probable lost *quaelibet*. Doubtful reading.","'bu- | manae' read as 'humanae'; 'involvünt' as 'involvunt'; | signs carried over as in the source."]],"de-mundi-0041":["*) Higher Mechanics [*Höhere Mechanick*], p. 354.",["Footnote to Kästner's *Anfangsgründe der höheren Mechanik*; the final stray '4' in the OCR is omitted as a page or line number."]],"de-mundi-0042":["Those who assert the objective reality of time conceive it in one of two ways. Either they conceive it as a kind of continuous flux in existence, without any existing thing [that flows] (a most absurd fiction!), as chiefly | the English philosophers do; or they hold it to be something real abstracted from the succession of inner states, as Leibniz and his followers maintain. The falsity of the latter view betrays itself plainly in the vicious circle that meets us in the definition of time. It also wholly neglects simultaneity [fn.], the chief consequence of time. And it so disturbs every use of sound reason that it demands that not the laws of motion be determined according to the measure of time, but time itself, as regards its nature, be determined by what is observed in motion or in any series of inner changes whatever. By this all certainty of rules is plainly destroyed.\n\nThat we cannot estimate the quantity of time except *in concreto*, namely by motion or by a series of thoughts, is due to this: the concept of time rests solely on an inner law of the mind, and is not any innate intuition. Hence that act of the mind by which it coordinates its sensa is elicited only by the aid of the senses. So far, indeed, is anyone from having been able to derive and explain the concept of time from any other source by the aid of reason, that the principle of contradiction itself presupposes it and takes it as its underlying condition. For A and not-A are in conflict only if thought of the same thing simultaneously (that is, at the same time); thought of it one after the other (at different times), they can both belong to it. Hence the possibility of changes is thinkable only in time; time is not thinkable through changes, but the reverse.",["Restated the long final sentence of the first paragraph as several sentences; 'quod ... postulet' is rendered as a result clause attached to the preceding sentence.","Supplied '[that flows]' to complete the sense of 'absque ulla tamen re exsistente'.","The footnote sign after 'simultaneitatem' is rendered '[fn.]' to avoid confusion with italics markup; the footnote follows as the next passage.","'obvia' in the OCR is read as 'obvio', agreeing with 'circulo'. The sign | is carried over as it stands."]],"de-mundi-0043":["Things simultaneous are not simultaneous for the reason that they do not succeed one another. For when succession is removed, one connection, which was through the series of time, is indeed taken away; but from this no other true relation arises at once, such as is the connection of all things in the same moment. For simultaneous things are joined in the same moment of time just as [text corrupt in the OCR: '... quam sunt aliquando'] ... adds to the quantum of actual things a second dimension, in so far as they depend, as it were, on the same point of time. For if you represent time by a straight line produced to infinity, and things simultaneous at any point of time by ordinates drawn to it, the surface thus generated will represent the phenomenal world, both as regards substance and as regards accidents.",["The sentence 'Simultanea enim perinde iunguntur ... addit quanto actualium alteram dimensionem' is corrupt in the OCR; I have translated only what is legible and marked the gap rather than reconstruct it. Please check against the Academy text.","'successione' and 'inde' are silently corrected from OCR 'uecessione' and 'finde'; 'producta' and 'applicatas' likewise.","*Phaenomenon* is rendered 'phenomenal' as Kant's Latin suggests, rather than 'appearance'."]],"de-mundi-0044":["6. Although time, posited in itself and absolutely, is an imaginary entity, yet, in so far as it belongs to the immutable law of sensible things as such, it is a most true concept and a condition of intuitive representation, extending to infinity over all possible objects of the senses. For simultaneous things, as such, cannot become accessible to the senses except by means of time, and changes can be thought only through time. It is therefore evident that this concept contains the universal form of phenomena. Consequently all observable events in the world, all motions and all inner vicissitudes, must necessarily agree with the axioms concerning time that are to be recognised, and that have in part been set out by us, since only under these conditions can objects of the senses exist and be coordinated. It is accordingly absurd to wish to arm reason against the first postulates of pure time, for example continuity, etc., since they follow from laws than which nothing is found prior, nothing older, and since reason itself cannot dispense, in its use of the principle of contradiction, with the aid of this concept. So primitive and original is it.",["Restated the long middle sentence as two; 'patet' is made the head of a separate sentence.","Stray marks in the OCR ('}', 'J', 'l') are treated as noise and not carried over as |.","'cum legibus consequantur' is doubtful (possibly an OCR or textual slip); rendered 'since they follow from laws'.","'phaenomenorum' is rendered 'phenomena' following Kant's Latin."]],"de-mundi-0045":["1. Time is therefore the absolutely first formal principle of the sensible world. For all things sensible in any way whatever cannot be thought except as posited either simultaneously or after one another. They are therefore, as it were, enveloped in the course of one single time and regard one another in a determinate position. Hence through this concept, [the concept] primary to everything sensitive, there necessarily arises the formal whole, which is not part of another, that is, the phenomenal world.",["'omnis sensitivi primarium' is taken in apposition to 'hunc conceptum'; the word in square brackets is supplied.","Stray '1' and the run-together 'oriaturtotum' are silently corrected.","Divided the single Latin sentence into three."]],"de-mundi-0046":["On space.",[]],"de-mundi-0047":["A. The concept of space is not abstracted from external sensations. For I cannot conceive of anything as posited outside me except by representing it as in a place different from that in which I myself am, nor things as outside one another except by placing them in different places of space. The possibility of external [perceptions], as such, therefore presupposes the concept of space and does not create it. Likewise, although things that are in space affect the senses, space itself cannot be drawn from the senses.",["The OCR reads 'uas) externarum'; I have supplied '[perceptions]' (*perceptionum*) as the evident word, in brackets. Please check against the Academy text."]],"de-mundi-0048":["B. The concept of space is a singular representation comprehending all things in itself; it is not an abstract and common notion containing them under itself. For what you call several spaces are nothing but parts of the same immense space, standing to one another in a certain position. Nor can you conceive a cubic foot except as bounded on every side by the surrounding space.",["Stray figures ('2', '8') in the OCR are dropped."]],"de-mundi-0049":["C. The concept of space is therefore a pure intuition, since it is a singular concept, not compounded of sensations, but the fundamental form of all external sensation. This pure intuition is readily observed in the axioms of geometry and in any mental construction of postulates, or also of problems. For that there are in space no more than three dimensions, that between two points there is only one straight line, that from a point given on a plane surface a circle can be described with a given line, and so on, cannot be inferred from any universal notion of space, but can be perceived only in space itself, as *in concreto*.\n\nWhat things in a given space lie toward one side, and what incline toward the opposite, cannot by any acuteness of mind be described discursively or reduced to intellectual marks. Now in solids perfectly similar and equal, but incongruent, of which kind are the left and the right hand (in so far as they are conceived solely according to extension), or spherical triangles from two opposite hemispheres, there is a diversity through which it is impossible that the limits of extension coincide, although in respect of everything that can be expressed in marks intelligible to the mind through speech they could be substituted for one another. It is therefore evident that here the diversity, namely incongruence, can be noted only by a certain pure intuition.\n\nHence geometry uses principles that are not merely indubitable and discursive, but fall under the mind's gaze. The evidence in its demonstrations (which is the clarity of certain cognition in so far as it is assimilated to the sensual) is not only greatest in geometry itself, but is the only evidence given in the pure sciences, and the exemplar and medium of all evidence in the others, because, since geometry",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('quia, cum geometria'); translated as it stands, not completed.","Divided the very long sentence 'Quae iaceant ... notari posse' into two; 'ideoque, cum ... patet' is rendered 'Now ... It is therefore evident'.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'manns' to *manus*, 'tantu' to *tantum*, 'qua- dam' to *quadam*, and the stray '(' before 'oppositis'; stray figures dropped.","'cognitio' is rendered 'cognition' because the Latin term is kept; 'sit diversitas' is read as 'there is a diversity' in the subordinate clause."]],"de-mundi-0050":["[...] to suffer?, [which] contemplates relations; since its concept contains in itself the very form of all sensuous intuition, nothing in what is perceived by outer sense can be clear and perspicuous except by means of that same intuition, in the contemplation of which that science is engaged. Moreover, geometry does not demonstrate its universal propositions by thinking the object through a universal concept, as is done in matters of reason, but by placing it before the eyes through a singular intuition, as is done in matters of sense*). | D. Space is not something objective and real, neither substance, nor accident, nor relation; it is subjective and ideal, and springs from the nature of the mind in accordance with a fixed law, as a scheme, so to speak, for coordinating to itself everything whatever that is sensed externally. Those who defend the reality of space either conceive it as an absolute and immense receptacle of possible things (an opinion which, following the English, pleases most geometers), or they contend that it is the very relation of existing things, a relation which vanishes entirely when things are removed | and can be thought only in actual things, as, following Leibniz, most of our | countrymen maintain. As to the former view, it is an empty figment of reason; since it feigns infinite true relations without any beings related to one another, it belongs to the world of fable. But those who go over to the latter opinion fall into a far worse error. For the former place a stumbling-block only in certain rational concepts, that is, concepts pertaining to *noumena*, which are in any case most remote from the understanding, e.g. questions about the spiritual world, omnipresence, and the like; the latter, however, set themselves squarely against the *phaenomena* themselves and against geometry, the most faithful interpreter of all *phaenomena*. For, not to mention the obvious circle in defining | space, in which they are necessarily entangled, they cast geometry down from the pinnacle of certainty and relegate it to the rank of those sciences whose principles are empirical. For if all the affections of space are borrowed from external relations only through experience, the geometrical axioms possess no universality except a comparative one, | such as is acquired by induction, that is, one extending only as far as observation has gone; nor any necessity except in accordance with established laws of nature; nor any precision except what is arbitrarily devised. And there is hope, as happens in empirical matters, of discovering some day a space endowed with other primitive affections, and perhaps even a rectilinear figure enclosed by two lines.\n\n*) That space must necessarily be conceived as a continuous quantum, since this is easy to demonstrate, I here pass over. Hence it comes about that the simple in space is not a part but a limit. A limit in general is that in a continuous quantum which contains the ground of boundaries. Space which is not the limit of another is complete (a solid). The limit of a solid is a surface, of a surface a line, of a line a point. Therefore there are three kinds of limits in space, just as there are three dimensions. Of these limits two (surface and line) are themselves spaces. The concept of a limit enters into no other quantum than space or time.",["The passage opens mid-sentence, and the first words are corrupt in the OCR ('pati?'). I translate what stands and do not fill the gap; the subject of 'contempletur' is not recoverable here.","In the source the footnote is interleaved with the main text, which breaks mid-word at 'evanescen- | tem' and 'nostra- | tum'. I have restored the words ('evanescentem', 'nostratium') and set the footnote at the end. The two | marks are placed at the nearest word boundaries.","OCR corrections made silently: subéectivum, mon sit, rgo, Torum, tem, sorte (read forte). The '!' before 'qualis' I took for a page-break mark and rendered as |; doubtful.","'Quod ... sit demonstratu': I took the 'eum' of the OCR as 'cum' and rendered it 'since this is easy to demonstrate'.","'bilineum rectilineum' is rendered 'a rectilinear figure enclosed by two lines'. *Noumena* and *phaenomena* are kept in Latin italics as technical terms."]],"de-mundi-0051":["E. Although the concept of space, as that of some objective and real being or affection, is imaginary, nevertheless in respect of all things sensible whatever it is not only most true, but is also the foundation of all truth in outer sensibility [*sensualitas externa*]. For things cannot appear to the senses under any form unless by means of a power of the mind which coordinates all sensations in accordance with a fixed law implanted in its nature. Since, therefore, nothing whatever can be given to the senses except in conformity with the primitive axioms of space and their corollaries (as geometry prescribes), then, although the principle of these is merely subjective, it will none the less necessarily agree with them, because to that extent it agrees with itself; and the laws of sensibility will be laws of nature, so far as nature can come within the senses. Nature is therefore subject exactly to the precepts of geometry in respect of all the affections of space demonstrated there, not on the ground of a feigned hypothesis, but as given intuitively, as the subjective condition of all the *phaenomena* in which nature can ever be revealed to the senses. Certainly, if the concept of space were not originally given through the nature of the mind (so that anyone who laboured to frame in his mind any relations other than those prescribed by space would labour in vain, since he would be compelled to employ this very concept in support of his fiction), the use of geometry in natural philosophy would be at bottom insecure. For it could be doubted whether this notion, derived from experience, agreed sufficiently with [nature, perhaps denying determinations from which it had been abstracted, a suspicion of which has indeed occurred to some. Space is therefore the absolutely first formal principle of the sensible world, not only because the objects of the universe can be *phaenomena* only through its concept, but chiefly for this reason, that by its essence it is only one, embracing everything whatever that is externally sensible, and so constitutes the principle of universality [*universitas*], that is, of a whole which cannot be a part of another.",["'E.' is the lettered item of the section and is kept as it stands.","The bracket in 'cum [natura' belongs to the source text as given and is carried over; the passage is perhaps corrupt there, or the bracket is an OCR artefact.","'cuius aliquibus etiam suspicio in mentem incidit': 'cuius' has no clear antecedent (one would expect a plural). I rendered it loosely as 'a suspicion of which has occurred to some'; the reading is doubtful.","'sensualitas' is rendered 'sensibility', following the method's rendering of Sinnlichkeit. 'vis animi' is rendered 'power of the mind'."]],"de-mundi-0052":["*Corollarium.*",["The OCR 'Corollariu m' is corrected. The Latin heading is kept in italics."]],"de-mundi-0053":["Here, then, are the two principles of sensitive knowledge: not, as in intellectual matters, general concepts, but singular intuitions, though pure ones. In these, it is not, as the laws of reason prescribe, that parts, and above all simple parts, contain the ground of the possibility of the composite. Rather, after the pattern of sensitive intuition, the infinite contains the ground of every thinkable part, and finally of the simple, or rather of the limit. For it is only when the infinite is given, in space as in time, that any definite space or time can be assigned by limitation; and neither point nor moment can be thought by itself, but they are conceived only in a space and time already given, as the limits of these. Therefore all the primitive affections of these concepts lie outside the bounds of reason, and so can in no way be explained intellectually. None the less they are the substrata of the understanding, which infers consequences from what is first given intuitively, according to logical laws, with the greatest certainty possible. Of these concepts the one concerns, properly speaking, the intuition of the object, the other the state, above all the representative state. Hence space is also employed as a type for the concept of time itself, in that time is represented by a line, and its limits (moments) by points. Time, however, approaches more nearly to a universal and rational concept, in that it embraces everything whatever in its relations, namely space itself, and besides this the accidents which are not comprehended in the relations of space, such as the thoughts of the mind. Moreover, time does not indeed dictate laws to reason, yet it constitutes the chief conditions under which the mind can compare its notions according to the laws of reason. Thus I cannot judge what is impossible except by predicating A and not-A of the same subject at the same time. And above all, if we turn the understanding to experience, the relation of cause and effect [*causati*] in external objects does indeed require the relations of space; but in all objects, internal as well as external, the mind can learn what is prior and what posterior, that is, what is cause and what effect, only with the aid of the relation of time. And even the quantity of space itself cannot be made intelligible unless, relating it to a measure as unit, we express it by a number; and number is itself nothing but a multitude distinctly known by numbering, that is, by successively adding one to one in a given time.",["The long sentence beginning 'in quibus, non sicut...' is restated in several shorter sentences. The antecedent of 'quibus' is taken to be the two principles (space and time).","'cognitio sensitiva' is rendered 'sensitive knowledge', following the method's 'knowledge' for Erkenntnis/*cognitio*. *Praeterea*/'moreover' is left plain.","'substrata intellectus' is rendered 'substrata of the understanding', and the participial clause is taken as describing its procedure. 'causatum' is rendered 'effect'.","A stray '1' at the end of the OCR text is dropped."]],"de-mundi-0054":["Finally, the question arises almost of itself in anyone's mind, whether each of the two concepts is connate or acquired. The latter alternative seems indeed already refuted by what has been demonstrated. The former, because it paves the way for a philosophy of the lazy, which declares any further inquiry vain by appeal to a first cause, is not to be admitted so rashly. But each concept is beyond doubt acquired. It is not abstracted from the sense of objects (for sensation gives the matter, not the form, of human knowledge), but from the very action of the mind, which coordinates what it has sensed according to perpetual laws, as an unchangeable type, and which is therefore to be known intuitively. For sensations excite this act of the mind, but they do not flow into the intuition; nor is anything else here connate except the law of the mind, according to which it combines in a certain way what it has sensed from the presence of the object.",["OCR corrections made: 'Con-1 ceptus' read as 'conceptus', 'acguisitus' as 'acquisitus', 'hie' as 'hic'. 'SOUS enim excitant' is clearly corrupt; I read it as 'sensa/sensus enim excitant' and translated 'sensations'. The reading is doubtful.","'sensa sua' is rendered 'what it has sensed' to avoid an invented technical term. 'philosophia pigrorum' is rendered 'philosophy of the lazy'.","'cognitionis humanae' is rendered 'human knowledge', following the method's 'knowledge' for *cognitio*."]],"de-mundi-0055":["SECTION IV.\nOn the principle of the form of the intelligible world [*mundus intelligibilis*].",["The Latin term *mundus intelligibilis* is kept in italics beside the English."]],"de-mundi-0056":["Those who hold space and time to be something real and absolutely necessary to all possible substances and states, as a kind of bond, think that nothing further is required in order to conceive how, given a plurality of existing things, some original relation should belong to them, as the primitive condition of possible influences and the principle of the essential form of the universe. For since, on their view, whatever exists is necessarily somewhere, it seems to them superfluous to inquire why things are present to one another in a determinate manner, inasmuch as this is determined of itself by the totality of space, which comprehends all things. But, apart from the fact that this concept (as has already been demonstrated) concerns rather the sensible laws of the subject than the conditions of the objects themselves, even if one grants it the fullest reality it still denotes only the possibility, given in intuition, of a universal coordination. The question therefore remains untouched, and can be solved only by the understanding: on what principle does this very relation of all substances rest, which, regarded intuitively, is called space? On this, then, the crux of the question concerning the principle of the form of the intelligible world turns, so that it may be clear how it is possible that several substances should stand in | mutual commerce and on this account belong to the same whole, which is called a world. But we do not here consider the world in respect of its matter, that is, of the natures of the substances of which it consists (whether they are material or immaterial), but in respect of its form, that is, how connection among many, and totality among all, can in general obtain.",["Restated the long first sentence as several shorter ones, and supplied \"things\" [in \"present to one another\"] where the Latin has only the verb *praesto sint*.","OCR corrected silently: *hand* to *haud*, *quipote* to *quippe*, *1. e.* to *i. e.*.","*Commercium* kept as \"commerce\" (Kant's Latin term for reciprocal interaction); *sensitivus* rendered \"sensible\", following the Sinnlichkeit/sensibility pairing; *influxus* as \"influence\" here, \"influx\" in the technical phrase later."]],"de-mundi-0057":["Given several substances, the principle of a possible commerce among them does not consist in their mere existence; something else besides is required, from which their mutual relations may be understood. For by reason of their very subsistence they necessarily have reference to nothing else, except perhaps to the cause of themselves; but the relation of what is caused to its cause is not commerce, but dependence. Hence, if any commerce is to obtain between them and others, a peculiar ground, determining this precisely, is needed.",["*sorte* is probably an OCR slip for *forte* (\"perhaps\"); translated accordingly.","*Subsistentia* rendered \"subsistence\"; *causatum* rendered \"what is caused\"."]],"de-mundi-0058":["And in this indeed consists the *prōton pseudos* [πρῶτον ψεῦδος, the first falsehood] of physical influx in its vulgar sense: that it rashly assumes the commerce of substances, and transeunt forces, to be sufficiently knowable through their mere existence, and so is not so much a system as rather the neglect of every philosophical system, as though such a system were superfluous in this matter. If we free this concept from this blemish, we have a kind of commerce which alone can be called real, and by which the world deserves to be called a real whole, not an ideal or imaginary one.",["The Greek is garbled in the OCR (*rpwrov evdos*); read as πρῶτον ψεῦδος, which the sense requires.","The Latin \"quod unicum reale dici et a quo … dici meretur\" is irregular (*dici* twice); smoothed in the English as \"which alone can be called real, and by which the world deserves to be called…\".","The stray \"is F. 18\" at the end is an OCR remnant of a page or margin mark and is left out."]],"de-mundi-0059":["\"A whole made up of necessary substances is impossible. For since the existence of each is abundantly established apart from any dependence upon any other (a dependence which plainly has no place in what is necessary), it is evident that not only does the commerce of substances (that is, the reciprocal dependence of their states) not follow from their existence, but that it cannot belong to them at all, being necessary.\"",["The opening quotation mark is in the source and is kept; no closing mark appears there, and I have added none in the translation beyond the one that balances it.","*Totum* rendered \"whole\"; *necessaria* (\"what is necessary\") taken as neuter plural, with *ipsis tanquam necessariis* as \"them, being necessary\"."]],"de-mundi-0060":["The whole of substances is therefore a whole of contingents, and the world, '| by its very essence, consists of mere contingents. Moreover, no necessary substance is in connection with the world except as cause with what is caused, and therefore not as a part with its complements to the whole (since the connection of co-parts is one of mutual dependence, which has no place in a necessary being). The cause of the world is therefore an extramundane being, and so is not the soul of the world; nor is its presence in the world [illegible in the OCR: probably *local*] but virtual.",["The OCR reads \"est 1868 ii sed virtualis\"; the word before *sed virtualis* is corrupt, and I have not filled it in beyond the bracketed conjecture (*localis*), which the contrast with \"virtual\" suggests.","The stray \"i\" before *mundi* is an OCR slip, left out. The odd mark \"‘|\" is kept as it stands.","*Compartes* rendered \"co-parts\"; *causatum* \"what is caused\"."]],"de-mundi-0061":["Worldly substances are beings derived from another, yet not from diverse beings but all from one. For suppose them to be the effects of several necessary beings: they would not be in commerce, since their causes would be alien to all mutual relation. Hence the unity in the conjunction of the substances of the universe is a consequence of the dependence of all upon one. Hence the form of the uni-",["The passage breaks off in mid-word (*uni-*), continuing in the next passage; translated as it stands, without completion.","*rela- 13 tion*: the stray line number \"13\" is removed and the word restored."]],"de-mundi-0062":["[uni]verse bears witness to a cause of the matter; and only a single cause of all things is the cause of the whole, nor is there an architect of the world who is not at the same time its creator. |",["The passage begins in mid-word (continuing *uni-* of the preceding passage); \"[uni]\" is supplied in brackets for that reason. The stray \"I\" before *versi* is an OCR slip.","*universa* (\"all things\") and *universitas* (\"the whole\") are distinguished; the German-style gloss would be Allheit."]],"de-mundi-0063":["If there were several first and necessary causes, each with its own effects, their products would be worlds, not a world, since they would in no way be connected into the same whole. And conversely, if there are several worlds actual outside one another | there are given several first and necessary causes, yet in such a way that neither one world with another, nor the cause of one with what is caused by another, | stands in any commerce at all.",["*causalis* in the Latin is probably an OCR slip for *causatis* (\"effects\"); so read. The stray \"un\" before *causato* and the numbers \"24\", \"5\" are removed.","The Academy | signs are kept where they stand, though their positions in the English are only approximate."]],"de-mundi-0064":["Several worlds actual outside one another are therefore not impossible by their very concept | (as Wolff wrongly concluded from the notion of a complex or multitude, | which he thought sufficient for a whole as such), but only under this condition, that there exists but a single necessary cause of all things. [footnote marker in the text: 99)] But if [text corrupt: *Ee Mine*] there are several, there will be several worlds, in the strictest, metaphysical sense, possible outside one another.",["\"Si vero Ee Mine plures\" is corrupt in the OCR; I have not restored it and render only what is legible.","The asterisk and \"99)\" are OCR remnants of a footnote or reference mark and are left bracketed or out; the closing \"í … 1\" are likewise stray marks.","*Complexus* \"complex\", *multitudo* \"multitude\"; Wolff is named as in the Latin."]],"de-mundi-0065":["If, just as from a given world the inference holds to a single cause of all its parts, so also conversely from a given cause common to all the argument were to proceed in like manner to their connection with one another, and so to the form of the world (although I confess that this conclusion does not seem to me equally clear), then the primitive connection of substances would not be contingent but necessary, through the sustentation of all by a common principle; and so the harmony, proceeding from their very subsistence and grounded in the common cause, would proceed according to common rules. Such a harmony I call generally established. That harmony, on the other hand, which has place only in so far as every individual state of a substance is adapted to the state of another, is a singularly established harmony. The commerce arising from the former harmony is real and physical; that arising from the latter is ideal and sympathetic. All commerce of the substances of the universe is therefore externally established (through the cause common to all), and is either generally established, through physical influx (in its amended form), or individually reconciled with their states. The latter again is either originally grounded in the primitive constitution of each substance, or impressed on the occasion of each change; the former is called pre-established harmony, the latter occasionalism. If, then, the conjunction of all, whereby they constitute one, were necessary through the sustentation of all substances by one, the universal commerce of substances will be by physical influx, and the world a real whole; if not, the commerce will be sympathetic (that is, harmony without true commerce), and the world only an ideal whole. To me, indeed, the former, though not demonstrated, is yet abundantly proved on other grounds also.",["Restated as several sentences; the Latin is one continuous argument.","OCR corrected silently: *conjclusionem*, *lautem*, *ccasione*, *raestabilita*, *onnisi*, *amen* (= *tamen*), and *per rimam* read as *per primam* (\"primitive\"); *ei individualiter* read as *vel individualiter*.","*e priori … e posteriori* rendered \"arising from the former … the latter\", i.e. from the two kinds of harmony; *prius* at the end taken as the first alternative (necessary conjunction through sustentation).","*sustentatio* kept as \"sustentation\"; *stabilita* (harmony) as \"established\"."]],"de-mundi-0066":["Scholium.",["The stray colon after *Scholion* is an OCR slip and is left out."]],"de-mundi-0067":["If it were permissible to advance a little beyond the limits of the apodeictic certainty that befits metaphysics, it would seem worth the trouble to investigate certain matters which pertain not only to the laws but even to the causes of sensible intuition, and which can be known only through the understanding. Namely, the human mind is not affected by external things, and the world does not lie open to its view to infinity, except in so far as the mind is itself, together with all other things, sustained by the same infinite power of one. Hence it does not sense external things except through the presence of the same common sustaining cause; and therefore space, which is the universal and necessary condition of the compresence of all things, as known sensibly, | can be called the OMNIPRESENCE OF THE APPEARANCE [*phaenomenon*]. (For the cause of the universe is not present to all and each severally because it is in their places; rather, places, that is, | possible relations of substances, exist because it is intimately present to all.) Further, since the possibility of all changes and successions, the principle of which, in so far as it is known | sensibly, resides in the concept of time, presupposes the permanence | of a subject whose opposite states succeed one another, and since that whose states are in flux | does not endure unless it is sustained by another: the concept of time, as of a single, infinite and immutable time [?] in which all things are and endure, is the eternity of the general cause as appearance [*phaenomenon*]. But it seems more prudent to hug the shore of the modes of knowledge | granted us by the moderate scope of our understanding than to be carried out into the deep of mystical inquiries of this kind, | as Malebranche did, whose view comes very close to that here expounded: namely, that we intuit all things in God.",["OCR corrected silently: *per!* to *per*, *on patet* to *non patet*, *b eadem* to *ab eadem*, *tiam* to *etiam*, *in alium* to *in altum* (\"into the deep\"); the last is an inference from the idiom *littus legere*.","*phaenomenon* is given as \"appearance\" with the Latin in brackets (Kant's Latin for Erscheinung); *sensitive* is \"sensibly\", *cognitiones* \"modes of knowledge\" following the Kemp Smith rule for the plural.","The \"?\" after *immutabilis* (\"?)\" in the OCR) is probably a footnote mark; the supplied \"time\" in \"[?]\" marks that I read the genitives as agreeing with *tempus*.","Restated the long sentences; the Academy | signs are kept near their positions in the Latin."]],"de-mundi-0068":["SECTION V.",[]],"de-mundi-0069":["On the method concerning the sensible and the intellectual in metaphysics.",["*Sensitiva* and *intellectualia* rendered \"the sensible\" and \"the intellectual\"."]],"de-mundi-0070":["In all sciences whose principles are given intuitively, whether through sensuous intuition (experience) or through intuition that is sensible indeed, yet pure (the concepts of space, time and number), that is, in natural science and mathematics, use gives the method. By trial and discovery, after the science has advanced to some amplitude and elegance, it becomes clear by what way and manner one must proceed in order that it may be perfected, and, with the blemishes both of errors and of confused thoughts wiped away, may shine forth the purer; just as grammar, after the richer use of speech, and style, after elegant examples of poems or orations, afforded occasion for rules and discipline. But the use of the understanding in such sciences,",["The passage breaks off after *scientiis*; translated as it stands, without completion.","OCR corrected silently: *per!* to *per*, *math esi* to *mathesi* (\"mathematics\"), *poématum* to *poematum*.","Restated the long opening sentence as two sentences."]],"de-mundi-0071":["2",["Only a numeral in the OCR (probably a footnote or page mark); translated as it stands."]],"de-mundi-0072":["*) The moments of time do not seem to succeed one another, since in that case a further time would have to be presupposed for the succession of the moments; rather, it is through sensitive intuition that actual things seem to descend, as it were, along a continuous series of moments.",["The text breaks off at \"videntu\"; I read it as *videntur* (OCR) and translate it as complete.","The footnote sign \"*)\" is kept as it stands."]],"de-mundi-0073":["[...] of which both the primitive concepts and the axioms are given in sensitive intuition, [the use of the understanding] is only logical, that is, one by which we merely subordinate modes of knowledge to one another with respect to universality, in conformity with the principle of contradiction: appearances to more general appearances, and the consequences of pure intuition to intuitive axioms. But in pure philosophy, such as metaphysics is, the use of the understanding in respect of principles is real. That is to say, the primitive concepts of things and relations, and the axioms themselves, are given originally by the pure understanding itself; and since they are not intuitions, they are not immune from errors. Here method precedes all science, and whatever is attempted before its precepts have been duly examined and firmly established seems rashly conceived and to be cast aside among the empty sports of the mind. For since the right use of reason here constitutes the principles themselves, and both the objects and the axioms that are to be thought concerning them become known in the first instance through the nature of reason alone, the exposition of the laws of pure reason is the very genesis of science, and the distinction of these laws from supposititious laws is the criterion of truth. Hence, since the method now in vogue in this science is only that which logic prescribes in general for all the sciences, while the method adapted to the peculiar character of metaphysics is wholly unknown, it is no wonder that students of this inquiry, rolling their Sisyphean stone for ever, seem scarcely to have advanced at all. I have here neither the intention nor the means to discourse at greater length on so important and far-reaching a theme. Yet I shall now briefly sketch what constitutes no contemptible part of this method, namely the contagion of sensitive knowledge with the intellectual: not only in so far as it creeps unawares upon the incautious in the application of principles, but in so far as it fashions spurious principles themselves under the guise of axio-",["The passage opens mid-sentence (\"4 iquarum\" is garbled OCR); I read *quarum* and supply in brackets \"the use of the understanding\" as subject of *non est nisi logicus*; \"logicus\" is restored from OCR \"/og?ens\".","Restored from OCR: *scientia*, *veritatis*, *ignoretur*, *videamur*, *insigni*, *applicatione*.","The long sentence is divided into several in English (Kemp Smith's practice); *cognitiones* is rendered \"modes of knowledge\", *phaenomena* \"appearances\".","The sentence breaks off at \"axio-\" and is completed in the next passage; I have not completed it."]],"de-mundi-0074":["[axio]ms [i.e. of axioms] it fashions: these matters I shall now briefly sketch.",["Fragment: *matum* completes *axiomatum* from the preceding passage; the bracketed words show the join. The object of *adumbrabo* is the relative clause *quae partem ... constituunt* in the preceding passage."]],"de-mundi-0075":["The method of all metaphysics concerning the sensitive and the intellectual reduces chiefly to this precept: we must take careful heed lest the principles proper to sensitive knowledge overstep their bounds and affect the intellectual. For the predicate in any judgment enunciated intellectually is the condition without which the subject is asserted not to be thinkable, and the predicate is therefore a principle of knowing. If it is a sensitive concept, it will be nothing but a condition of possible sensitive knowledge, and will therefore fit perfectly the subject of a judgment whose concept is likewise sensitive. But if it is applied to an intellectual concept, such a judgment will be valid only according to subjective laws. It is therefore not to be predicated of the intellectual notion itself and put forward objectively, but only as the condition without which sensitive knowledge of the given concept has no place.*) Now the deception practised on the understanding by the substitution of a sensitive concept as if it were an intellectual mark may be called (by analogy with the received meaning) a vice of subreption. The confusion of the intellectual and the sensitive will accordingly be the metaphysical vice of subreption (the *phaenomenon intellectuatum*, if pardon be granted for the barbarous word); and such a hybrid axiom, which passes off sensitive conditions as necessarily attaching to an intellectual concept, I entitle a subreptic axiom (*axioma subrepticium*). From these spurious axioms have sprung the principles of deceiving the understanding which have wrought such havoc throughout all metaphysics. In order, however, that we may have ready to hand a criterion of these judgments that can be clearly recognised, a veritable Lydian stone by which to distinguish them from genuine judgments; and in order also, in case they should perhaps seem to cling firmly to the understanding, that we may have a kind of *ars doci- | mastica* [art of assaying], by whose aid a fair estimate can be made of how much belongs to the sensitive and how much to the intellectual: for these purposes I think we must descend more deeply into this question.",["Restored from OCR: *enuntiato*, *intellectuale*, *docimasticam*, *intellectum*; the garbled \"Cie\" before *aequa* is taken as the end of *intellectum* and is not rendered.","The page-break sign | is kept at its place inside *doci- | mastica*; the gloss \"art of assaying\" is mine.","The footnote sign \"*)\" is kept at its place.","Long sentences are divided; *hybridum* \"hybrid\", *vocatur* \"I entitle\"; *cognitio* \"knowledge\"."]],"de-mundi-0076":["*) A second and easy use of this criterion lies in distinguishing principles that merely enunciate laws of sensitive knowledge from those that in addition prescribe something concerning the objects themselves. For if the predicate is an intellectual concept, its relation to the subject of the judgment, however sensitively that subject be thought, always denotes a mark belonging to the object itself. But if the predicate is a sensitive concept, then, since the laws of sensitive knowledge are not conditions of the possibility of the things themselves, it will not hold of a subject of judgment thought intellectually, and so cannot be enunciated objectively. Thus in the familiar axiom, *whatever exists is somewhere*, the predicate contains conditions of sensitive knowledge, and cannot therefore be enunciated generally of the subject of the judgment, namely of anything whatever that exists. The formula, being an objective prescription, is accordingly false. But if the proposition is converted, so that the predicate becomes an intellectual concept, it emerges as perfectly true: *whatever is somewhere, exists*.",["\"Sicin\" restored to *Sic in*; \"respectus.\" punctuation tidied.","The long second sentence is divided in English; italics on the two axioms are supplied as quotations, not as source emphasis."]],"de-mundi-0077":["Here, then, is the PRINCIPLE OF REDUCTION of any subreptic axiom: if anything is predicated generally of any intellectual concept whatever that pertains to relations of SPACE AND TIME, it is not to be enunciated objectively, and denotes only the condition without which the given concept is not sensitively knowable. That an axiom of this kind is spurious, and, if not false, at least rashly and precariously asserted, is evident from the following. The subject of the judgment, being conceived intellectually, pertains to the object. The predicate, however, since it contains determinations of space and time, pertains only to the conditions of human sensitive knowledge; and since these do not necessarily attach to every knowledge of the same object, it cannot be enunciated universally of a given intellectual concept. The understanding is so easily subject to this vice of subreption because it is deluded under the patronage of another rule which is perfectly true. For we rightly suppose that whatever cannot be known by any intuition at all is wholly unthinkable, and therefore impossible. But since we cannot attain, by any effort of the mind, even in imagination, [to] an intuition other than that which takes place according to the form of space and time,",["Restored from OCR: *REDUCTIONIS*, *TEMPORIS*, *quia*; \"nullo\" at the end joins \"mentis conatu\" of the next passage, so *nullo mentis conatu* is rendered here and the sentence is left unfinished.","\"[to]\" is supplied for the construction of *assequi*; \"cognoscibilis\" rendered \"knowable\".","The sentence breaks off; it continues in the next passage."]],"de-mundi-0078":["it happens that we hold every intuition whatever that is not bound by these laws to be impossible (passing over the pure intellectual intuition, exempt from the laws of the senses, such as the divine, which Plato entitles idea), and so subject all possibles to the sensitive axioms of space and time.",["\"entis conatu\" is OCR for *mentis conatu*, the *m* of which appears in the previous passage's last word; \"abeamus\" read as *habeamus*; \"jomnia\" as *omnia*.","The sentence continues the unfinished one of the preceding passage."]],"de-mundi-0079":["But all the deceptions of sensitive modes of knowledge under the guise of intellectual ones, from which subreptic axioms arise, can [be referred to three species, of which you may take the general formulas to be these:",["The bracket before *revocari* stands in the source text as given (possibly a misread page-break sign); it is carried over unchanged and left unclosed."]],"de-mundi-0080":["1. The same sensitive condition under which alone the intuition of an object is possi-",["Fragment; completed in the next passage."]],"de-mundi-0081":["-ble, is the condition of the very possibility of the object.",["\"possibelitatis\" corrected to *possibilitatis*."]],"de-mundi-0082":["2. The same sensitive condition under which alone data can be compared with one another",["Fragment; the sentence continues through the next two passages."]],"de-mundi-0083":["for the forming of an intellectual concept of an object, is also the condition",[]],"de-mundi-0084":["of the very possibility of the object.",[]],"de-mundi-0085":["3. The same sensitive condition under which alone the subsumption of some object that presents itself",["\"9.\" in the OCR corrected to \"3.\" (the third of the three formulas); \"subsumptto\" read *subsumptio*."]],"de-mundi-0086":["under a given intellectual concept is possible, is also the con-",[]],"de-mundi-0087":["-dition of the possibility of the object itself.",["\"diclo\" read as *-ditio* (OCR)."]],"de-mundi-0088":["*) Space and time are conceived as if they comprehended in themselves everything that in any way meets the senses. Hence, according to the laws of the human mind, there is not given any being",["Footnote breaks off mid-sentence (*ullius entis*); not completed."]],"de-mundi-0089":["A subreptic axiom of the FIRST class is this: whatever is, is somewhere and at some time.*) By this spurious principle all beings, even though they are known intellectually, are bound in their existence to the conditions of space and time. | Hence men raise idle questions about the places of immaterial substances in the corporeal universe (though for the same reason no sensitive intuition of these is given, nor any representation under such a | form), about the seat of the soul, and about other matters of the kind. Since the sensitive and the intellectual are shamelessly mixed together, like the square and the round, it generally happens that of two disputants one seems to be milking a he-goat and the other holding a sieve underneath. | The presence of immaterial beings in the corporeal world is, however, virtual and not local (though it is improperly so called). Space contains the conditions of possible reciprocal actions only for matter. What it is that constitutes the external relations of forces among immaterial substances, both with one another and towards bodies, wholly escapes the human understanding. The most penetrating Euler, in other respects a great investigator and arbiter of the phenomena, has acutely observed as much (in letters sent to a certain German princess).\n\nWhen men come to the concept of the supreme being, who is outside the world, it is impossible to say how far they are mocked by these shadows that flit before the understanding. They imagine God's presence to be local, and enclose God in the world, as though He were comprehended at once in infinite space. This they do in order to make up for the limitation thus imposed on Him, namely by conceiving locality, as it were, in the eminent degree, that is, as infinite. But to be in several places at once is absolutely impossible. Different places are outside one another, and so what is in several places is outside itself and externally present to itself, which is self-contradictory.\n\nAs regards time, they first exempt it from the laws of sensitive knowledge. They then carry it beyond the limits of the world to the extramundane being itself, as a condition of His existence, and so entangle themselves in an inextricable labyrinth. Hence they rack their wits with absurd questions, for instance why God did not create the world many centuries earlier. They persuade themselves that it is easily conceived how God discerns present things, that is, the actual things of the time in which He is. But how He [foresees (*prospiciat*)] future things, that is, the actual things of a time in which He is not yet, they think hard to understand. (As if the existence of the necessary being descended successively through all the moments of an imaginary time, and, when part of its duration had already been used up, looked forward to the eternity it has still to live, together with the contemporaneous events of the world.)\n\nAnother prejudice may be compared with this one. It is not strictly a subreptic axiom but a sport of the fancy, and could be stated in the general formula: whatever exists, [is in] space and time, that is, every substance is extended and continuously changed. Those whose concepts are cruder are firmly bound by this law of imagining. Yet they readily see for themselves that it concerns only the efforts of the fancy to sketch for itself images of things, and not the conditions of existence. All this vanishes like smoke, once the notion of time has been thoroughly understood.",["The footnote marker *) after *aliquando* is kept; the footnote text is not in the passage.","The OCR order is scrambled near the end of the time-discussion. I took *prospiciat, difficile intellectu putant* to belong to the sentence on future things, and restored it there, with *prospiciat* in square brackets. The fragment *intuitus, nisi ut in spatio ac tempore contenti* ('intuiting, except as contained in space and time') fits no construction and is left untranslated as a doubtful reading.","The formula *quicquid exsistit, in 4% est spatium et tempus* is corrupt. I read 'is in space and time' and supplied [is in]; the reading is uncertain. The OCR *sumi* is corrected to *fumi* ('like smoke').","Several long Latin periods are divided into shorter English sentences. *Hircum mulgere / cribrum supponere* is the proverbial image of a pointless dispute. *Eulerus* is Euler, in his letters to a German princess.","The marks | are carried over as they stand, at the nearest English equivalent of their places in the Latin."]],"de-mundi-0090":["Prejudices of the SECOND kind impose upon the understanding through sensitive conditions, by which the mind is bound whenever in certain cases it wishes to reach an intellectual concept. They conceal themselves still more. One of them affects knowledge of quantity generally, the other knowledge of qualities. The former is: every actual multitude can be given in number [*omnis multitudo actualis est labilis numero*], and therefore every quantum is finite. The latter is: whatever is impossible contradicts itself. In both, the concept of time does not enter into the notion of the predicate itself, nor is it reckoned a mark of the subject. Nevertheless it serves as a means for forming the concept of the predicate. Its condition therefore affects the intellectual concept of the subject, inasmuch as only with its help do we arrive at that concept.",["*labilis numero* is doubtful OCR; I suspect *dabilis numero* ('can be given in number'), and translate accordingly.","*sich contradicit* is corrected to *sibi contradicit*. *Qualitatum* is rendered 'qualities'; *quantum* is kept as an English term.","*praeiudicia* is rendered 'prejudices'; the German-style 'subreptic' of the surrounding text is not in this passage."]],"de-mundi-0091":["As to the former, then: every quantum and every series is distinctly known only through successive coordination. The intellectual concept of a quantum and of a multitude therefore arises only with the aid of this concept of time. It never reaches completeness unless the synthesis can be completed in finite time. Hence an infinite series of coordinated things cannot be distinctly comprehended within the limits of our understanding, and so, by a fault of subreption, appears impossible.\n\nAccording to the laws of the pure understanding, every series of things caused has its principle, that is, there is no regress without limit in a series of things caused. According to the sensitive laws, however, every series of coordinated things has an assignable beginning. These propositions are wrongly taken as identical, although the latter involves the measurability of the series and the former the dependence of the whole.\n\nIn like manner, to the argument of the understanding which proves that, given a composite substance, there are given principles of composition, that is, simple things, there attaches a spurious supposition suborned by sensitive knowledge. It is that in such a composite the regress in the composition of parts does not go on to infinity, that is, that in any composite there is a definite number of parts. The sense of this is certainly not twin to that of the former, and it is therefore rashly substituted for it. || That the quantum of the world is limited (not a maximum), that it recognizes a principle of itself, and that bodies consist of simple things, can certainly be known under the sign of reason. | But that the universe is mathematically finite as regards its extent, that its past age has reached a [definite] measure [*ad mensuram* …, text corrupt], and that the number of simple things constituting any body is definite, are propositions which plainly proclaim their origin in the nature of sensitive knowledge. | However true they may otherwise be held to be, they labour under the undoubted blemish of their origin.",["The text of one clause is corrupt: *quod aetas ipsius transacta sit ad mensuram Us quod sim- | RE quodlibet corpus constituentium*. I read 'that its past age has reached a measure' and 'the simple things constituting any body'; the missing words are not supplied, and the reading is doubtful.","The first sentence of the second paragraph is divided; the correct reading of *causatorum* as 'things caused' follows the contrast with *coordinatorum*. *suppositicium … subornatum* is rendered 'spurious supposition suborned'.","The marks || and | are kept at their places."]],"de-mundi-0092":["As for the latter subreptic axiom, it arises from a rash conversion of the principle of contradiction. The concept of time attaches to this primitive judgement to this extent: where contradictorily opposed [predicates] are given in the same [subject] at the same time, the impossibility is manifest. It is thus enunciated: whatever at once is and is not, is impossible. Here, since the understanding predicates something in a case which is given according to the sensitive laws, the judgement is eminently true and most evident. If, on the other hand, you convert the same axiom, so as to say: every impossible thing at once is and is not, that is, involves a contradiction, || you predicate something generally of an object of reason by means of sensitive knowledge. You thereby subject the intellectual concept of the possible or impossible to the conditions of sensitive knowledge, namely [to the relation] of time. This is indeed most true of the laws by which the human understanding is bound and limited, but it can in no way be conceded objectively and generally.\n\nOur understanding, that is to say, does not notice impossibility except where it can note the simultaneous enunciation of opposites about the same thing, that is, only where contradiction is met with. Wherever, therefore, no such condition occurs, the human understanding has no judgement of impossibility to give. But it is rash to conclude from this that no understanding whatever could do so, and hence that whatever involves no contradiction is therefore possible. The conclusion is reached by taking subjective conditions of judging for objective ones. Hence arise so many vain inventions of I know not what forces, devised at pleasure, which break out in crowds, without any obstacle of repugnance, from any architectonic mind, or, if you prefer, one inclined to chimeras. For a force is nothing but the relation of a substance A to something else B (an accident), as of a ground to what is grounded. The possibility of any force therefore does not rest on [*mee*, text corrupt] of cause and caused, that is, of substance and accident. Hence also the impossibility of forces falsely devised does not depend on contradiction alone. It follows that no original force may be assumed as possible unless it is given by experi-",["The OCR *aa?oma* is corrected to *axioma*, *sich* to *sibi* is not at issue here; *ests* is read *est, s.* ('that is'), the *s.* being the abbreviation for *sive*.","*respectivus temporis* is doubtful; I read it as a relation to time and supplied 'to the relation' in square brackets.","The phrase *non nititur mee causae et causati* is corrupt; a word like *relatione* or *nexu* may have dropped out. I leave the gap marked and do not fill it.","*ratio / rationatum* is rendered 'ground / what is grounded'. The passage breaks off at *experi-*, continued in the next passage.","|| is kept; the long sentences are divided in the manner of the method."]],"de-mundi-0093":["[experi]ence, nor can its possibility be conceived a priori by any acuteness of the understanding.",["The passage begins with the end of a word split from the previous passage (*[experi]entia*). I render it 'ence' within the brackets, completing the word only because the previous passage supplies the start."]],"de-mundi-0094":["Subreptic axioms of the THIRD kind, which arise from conditions proper to the subject and are rashly transferred from them to objects, do not spring up so abundantly as is the case with those which belong to the class [of …, text corrupt] … to the concept",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and is translated as it stands. The OCR *Ch* (perhaps a corrupt word for the class number) is left unrendered and flagged in brackets.","*ut quemadmodum fit* is a pleonasm in the Latin and is smoothed to 'as is the case'."]],"de-mundi-0095":["[...] the [in]tellectual, the way lies open only through sensitive data, but because only with their aid can [the concept] be applied to a case given through experience, that is, can it be known whether or not something is contained under a certain intellectual concept. Of this kind is the well-worn saying in certain schools: whatever exists contingently did not at some time exist. This subreptive principle arises from a poverty of understanding, which for the most part perceives only the nominal marks of contingency or necessity, and rarely the real ones. Hence whether the opposite of some substance is possible can scarcely be discerned by marks drawn *a priori*, and will not be known in any other way than by its being established that the substance at some time was not. Changes attest contingency more truly than contingency attests mutability. If nothing fleeting and transitory occurred in the world, we should hardly arrive at any notion of contingency. Accordingly, the direct proposition, 'whatever at some time was not is contingent', is perfectly true. Its converse, however, indicates only the conditions under which it is possible for us to discern whether something exists necessarily or contingently. If, then, it is to be stated as a subjective law (which is what it really is), it must be expressed thus: of whatever it is not established that it at some time was not, of that the sufficient marks of contingency are not given through common intelligence. This same proposition silently passes over into an objective condition, as if without this connection there were no place at all for contingency. Once this has been done, a further and erroneous axiom arises. For this world, although it exists contingently, is everlasting, that is, simultaneous with all time, so that it is wrongly asserted that there was some time at which it did not exist.",["The passage begins mid-sentence ('tellectualem', presumably *intellectualem*); I have translated the fragment as it stands and put the lost beginning in brackets.","Silently corrected OCR slips: *auxiliantibus*, *cognosci*, *necne*, *contingenter*, *subrepticium*, *nominales*, *perspicientis*, *continentia* to *contingentia*, *transitorium*, *indigitat*, *nexo* and similar.","'sub quibus iis' is garbled; I read it as 'under which [it is permitted] us', i.e. 'for us', which the argument (a subjective law) requires. Doubtful.","*subrepticium* is rendered 'subreptive'. *notae nominales/reales* = 'nominal/real marks'.","I divided the long sentence beginning 'Hinc utrum…' and the one beginning 'Ideoque…' into shorter English sentences."]],"de-mundi-0096":["Closely akin to the subreptive principles are certain others. These indeed impart no taint of sensitive knowledge to a given intellectual concept, yet they so delude the understanding that it takes them for arguments drawn from the object, whereas they are recommended to us only by their agreement with the free and ample use of the understanding, in accordance with its peculiar nature. Hence, like those enumerated above, they rest on subjective grounds. These, however, are not laws of sensitive knowledge, but of intellectual knowledge itself, that is, conditions under which the understanding finds it easy and ready to use its penetration. I may here, by way of a finishing flourish, make some mention of these principles, which, so far as I know, have not yet been distinctly set forth elsewhere. I entitle *principles of convenientia* [*principia convenientiae*] those rules of judging to which we willingly submit, and to which we cling as if to axioms, for this reason alone, that if we departed from them our understanding would hardly be permitted any judgment about a given object. To this class the following belong.\n\nFIRST, that by which we assume that everything in the universe happens according to the order of nature. Epicurus asserted this principle without any restriction; all philosophers, however, with one voice profess it, subject to an exception that is very rare and to be admitted only in the utmost necessity. We so determine, however, not because either impossibility or the slightest hypothetical possibility [...] [of the contrary is seen], but because, if you depart from the order of nature, the understanding would have no use at all, and the rash appeal to the supernatural is the pillow of a lazy understanding. For the same reason we carefully exclude comparative miracles, namely the influence of spirits, from the explanation of phenomena. Since their nature is unknown to us, the understanding would, to its great detriment, be turned away from the light of experience, through which alone it has a supply of laws of judging to be acquired, towards the shadows of kinds and causes unknown to us.\n\nSECOND is that favour for unity which is proper to the philosophical mind, and from which that widely known canon has flowed: principles are not to be multiplied beyond the highest necessity. We assent to it, not because we investigate the causal [...] itself, under an impulse of the understanding, which seems to itself to have advanced in the explanation of phenomena just so far as it is permitted to descend from the same principle to a great many things grounded upon it [*rationata*].\n\nTHIRD of this kind of principles is: nothing at all of matter arises or perishes, and all the vicissitudes of the world concern form alone. This postulate, at the persuasion of common understanding, has spread through all the schools of philosophers. This is not because it has been held as ascertained or demonstrated by arguments *a priori*, but because, if you admitted that matter itself is fleeting and transitory, nothing at all stable and lasting would remain, nothing that could still serve the explanation of phenomena according to universal and perpetual laws, and so the use of the understanding.",["Divided the opening sentence and the sentences on the three principles into shorter English sentences, as the method allows; no content is added.","'Ita autem statuimus, non propterea, vel impossibilitas…' has no verb for the clause and appears defective or cut off; I have marked the gap with [...] and a bracketed gloss of the sense, and not filled it.","In the second principle 'causalem in ipsam indagamus impulsu intellectus' looks corrupt (OCR); I translated what is there and marked a gap with [...]. The sense probably requires a 'but' before 'by the impulse of the understanding'.","Silently corrected OCR slips (*maculam*, *subiectivis*, *ipsius intellectualis*, *Eandem*, *averteretur*, *quod... inserviret*, etc.); page numerals '41' and the line-break signs inside words were dropped or kept as in the text.","*convenientia* is left in Latin because it is Kant's technical term here; *intellectus* = understanding, *phaenomena* = phenomena, *cognitio sensitiva* = sensitive knowledge."]],"de-mundi-0097":["So much for method, chiefly in regard to the distinction between sensitive and intellectual knowledge. If this distinction should at some time be brought exactly to rule [*ad amussim*] by more careful investigation, it will serve as a propaedeutic science, and be of immense profit to all who are to penetrate into the inmost recesses of metaphysics.",["OCR 'd amussim' corrected to *ad amussim*.","Restated the closing relative clause as a second sentence; the German-style construction of the Latin ('quae... redacta fuerit') is smoothed."]],"de-mundi-0098":["Note. Since in this last section the investigation of method occupies every page, and the rules prescribing the true form of arguing about sensitive things shine by their own light and do not borrow it from examples adduced for illustration, I have mentioned these only, as it were, in passing. It is therefore not surprising that some things there asserted will seem to most readers more bold than true; when at some time I am permitted to be more prolix, these will certainly demand a greater strength of argument. Thus what I have said about the locality of immaterial things needs explanation, which, if you like, you may seek in Euler, *loc. cit.*, vol. 2, pp. 49–52. For the soul is not in commerce with the body because it is held at a certain place of the body; rather a determinate place in the universe is assigned to it because it is in mutual commerce with a certain body, and if this commerce is dissolved its whole position in space is removed. Its locality is therefore derivative, and contingently procured for it; it is not primitive, nor inherent in its existence [conlicio: doubtful] and necessary. This is because whatever cannot be of itself objects of the external senses (such as those of man), that is, whatever is immaterial, is wholly exempt from the universal condition of what is externally sensible, namely space. Hence an absolute and",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Hinc animae localitas absoluta et'), continued in the next passage; I have left it so.","'conlicio necessaria' is an unintelligible OCR reading; I have left *conlicio* bracketed and untranslated rather than guess, so the sense of 'and necessary' is only partly secured.","Silently corrected *Nota*, *nonnulla*, *quaeras*, *soluto*, *sit*, *sunt*; 'positus' (position) is kept as OCR reads it, though *situs* may have been meant.","'l. c.' (*loco citato*) is rendered 'loc. cit.'; Euler is cited without a title in this passage.","I divided the long final sentence into shorter English sentences and made the 'quia... quo soluto' construction explicit."]],"de-mundi-0099":["[im]mediate locality can be denied to the soul, and yet a hypothetical and mediate locality can be attributed to it.",["The passage opens mid-word ('mediata'); the preceding passage ends 'absoluta et', so I have supplied '[im]' in brackets, reading *immediata* as the sense requires (contrasted with *mediata*).","*hypothetica* is rendered 'hypothetical'."]],"moscati-0001":["Review\nMoscati's Work:\n| On the Essential Corporeal Difference between the Structure of Animals and of Men.",["Silently corrected OCR 'Noscatis', 'Schrist', 'Son' and 'Anterschiede' to Moscatis, Schrift, Von, Unterschiede.","The sign | is carried over where it stands."]],"moscati-0002":["On the Essential Corporeal Difference between the Structure of Animals and of Men. An academic discourse, delivered in the anatomical theatre at Pavia by Chr. Peter Moscati, Professor of Anatomy. Translated from the Italian by Johann Beckmann, Professor in Göttingen.",["OCR 'Prof. der Anal.' emended silently to 'Anat.' (Professor of Anatomy).","This is the title of the work under review, given in full."]],"moscati-0003":["Here we have, once more, natural man on all fours,",["The sentence continues in the next passage; it is translated as it stands."]],"moscati-0004":["to which a sagacious anatomist brings him back, whereas the discerning Rousseau, as a philosopher, was unable to succeed in this. Moscati proves that the upright gait of man is forced and contrary to nature. Man is indeed so constructed that he can maintain and move himself in this posture; but if he makes it a necessity and a constant habit, inconveniences and diseases arise from it, which amply pro—",["OCR 'orauf' read as 'worauf'; 'gnugsam' rendered 'amply'.","Divided Kant's single sentence into three English sentences, as the method allows.","The passage breaks off at the line-break 'be—' (presumably *beweisen*); left unfinished, as the next passage supplies the rest.","'Chr. Moscati' is the text's own abbreviation of the name and is replaced in English by 'Moscati' for smoothness."]],"moscati-0005":["[—ve] that he was misled, by reason and imitation, into departing from the first [?], animal arrangement. Man is, in his Su-",["'eisen' is the end of *beweisen* (from the preceding passage), so I give '[pro]ve' in brackets.","OCR 'riten' is doubtful; I read it as 'ersten' ('first') and mark it with [?].","The passage ends at 'Su-', which is probably a misreading (perhaps *Inwendigen*) completed by the first word of the next passage; I do not complete it."]],"moscati-0006":["[...]endigen constructed no differently from all animals that stand on four feet.",["The opening 'endigen' is a word-fragment (perhaps *Inwendigen*, 'inward parts', together with 'Su-' at the end of the previous passage); the reading is doubtful and not completed here.","OCR 'Süßen' corrected silently to 'Füßen' (feet)."]],"moscati-0007":["When, then, he stands erect, his viscera, and especially the fruit of the womb in pregnant persons, acquire a hanging position and a half-inverted posture. If this posture alternates often with the recumbent one, or with that on all fours, it cannot produce any especially evil consequences; but by being continued without interruption it causes malformations and a multitude of diseases. Thus, for example, the heart, being compelled to hang, lengthens the blood vessels to which it is attached. It assumes an oblique position, in that it rests upon the diaphragm and its apex slips toward the left side. In this position man, and indeed the adult, differs from all other animals, and through it he acquires an unavoidable propensity to aneurisms, palpitation of the heart, tightness of the chest, dropsy of the chest, and the like. In this upright posture of man the mesentery (*Mesenterium*), drawn by the weight of the viscera, | sinks vertically downward, is lengthened and weakened, and is prepared for a multitude of ruptures [hernias]. In the portal vein, which has no valves, the blood, since it must rise in it against the direction of gravity, will move slowly and with more difficulty than would happen with a horizontal position of the trunk; from this arise hypochondria, haemorrhoids, and the like. I say nothing of the difficulty which the circulation of the blood suffers, since it must rise straight | up through the veins of the legs to the heart; this not infrequently brings with it swellings, varicose veins, and the like. The disadvantage of this upright posture is most plainly | visible in pregnant women, both in respect of the fruit and of the mother. The child, which is thereby set on its head, receives the blood in very unequal proportion, since the blood is driven in far greater quantity to the upper parts, into the head and the arms; whereby both are extended and grow in quite other proportions than in all other animals. From the former afflux arise hereditary inclinations to vertigo, apoplexy, headache and madness. From the pressure of blood toward the arms, and its diversion away from the legs, arises the remarkable disproportion, observed in no other animal, that the arms of the fetus become longer than is proper to their proportion, and the legs shorter. This is indeed set right again after birth by the constantly upright posture, but it proves nevertheless that violence must previously have been done to the fetus. The injuries of the two-footed mother are prolapse of the womb, untimely births, and the like, which arise from her upright posture together with an Iliad of other ills, and from which four-footed creatures are free. One could add yet further arguments to these, that our animal nature is properly four-footed. Among all four-footed animals there is not a single one which could not swim, if by accident it falls into water. Man alone drowns, where he has not specially learned to swim. The cause is that he has abandoned the habit of going on all fours; for it is this motion by which he would keep himself afloat without any art, | and by which all four-footed creatures swim, though otherwise they [are wont] to abhor water. However paradoxical this proposition of our Italian doctor may seem, it yet receives, in the hands of so acute and philosophical an anatomist, almost complete certainty. One sees from it that nature's first care was that man should be preserved as an animal, for himself and for his kind; and for this the posture most suited to his inner structure, to the position of the fetus, and to self-preservation in danger was the four-footed. But a germ of reason was also laid in him, whereby, when it develops, he is destined for society, and by means of which he permanently adopts the posture best suited to this, namely the two-footed. Thereby he gains on the one side infinitely much over the animals, but must also put up with the inconveniences that arise for him from having raised his head so proudly above his old comrades. Price 24 groschen.",["The passage opens mid-word (\"enn\"); I read \"Wenn\" and corrected the OCR silently, as also \"scheinen\" for \"[deinen\" and \"Bau\" for \"Hau\". Stray footnote-style digits in the OCR are dropped.","Restated: the long sentence on the heart is divided into three; \"zu geschweigen\" is rendered as a separate sentence (\"I say nothing of ...\").","\"Brüche\" is rendered \"ruptures [hernias]\", the bracketed word being supplied; \"Hang\" = propensity; \"Zergliederer\" = anatomist (literally dissector).","The text reads \"die sonst das Wasser zu verabscheuen\"; the governing verb (probably \"pflegen\") is missing, so \"[are wont]\" is supplied in brackets. Doubtful reading.","\"Kostet 24 gr.\" is a bookseller's price notice (24 groschen), kept as it stands."]],"racen-0001":["On the Different Races of Mankind.",["Title; line breaks of the German are not kept."]],"racen-0002":["1. On the Difference of Races in General.",[]],"racen-0003":["The lecture which I announce will be more a useful entertainment than a laborious occupation. The inquiry with which I accompany this announcement will therefore contain something for the understanding, but more as a play of the understanding than as a deep investigation. 1)",["Restated as two sentences. The OCR \"!))\" is read as the footnote marker \"1)\"; see the footnote (racen-0005)."]],"racen-0004":["In the animal kingdom the division of nature into genera and species is grounded upon the common law of propagation, and the unity of the genus is nothing other than the unity of the generative power which holds universally for a certain manifold of animals. Hence Buffon's rule, that animals which beget fertile young with one another (however different in form they may be) belong none the less to one and the same physical genus, must be regarded properly only as the definition of a natural genus [*Naturgattung*] of animals in general, as distinguished from all scholastic genera [*Schulgattungen*] of them. The scholastic division proceeds by classes, which are determined by resemblances; the natural division proceeds by stocks [*Stämme*], which divide the animals according to their kinship in respect of generation. The former yields a scholastic system for the memory; the latter a natural system for the understanding. The former aims only at bringing creatures under headings; the latter at bringing them under laws. | According to this concept all men on the wide earth belong to one and the same natural genus, because they uniformly beget fertile children with one another, however great the differences that may otherwise be met with in their",["Silent OCR corrections: \"ruchtbare\" to \"fruchtbare\", \"Verwandt- chafften\" to \"Verwandtschaften\", \"Shulsystem\", \"iner\".","The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"in ihrer\"), continued in the next passage; translated as it stands.","\"Verstand\" = understanding; \"Gattung\" = genus, \"Art\" = species; \"Stamm\" = stock (line of descent), a choice.","The first sentence is kept whole; the sentence beginning \"Daher muß ...\" is smoothed slightly in English."]],"racen-0005":["1) The words \"The — [*wird*]\", which stand in A1 before the treatise, are lacking in A2.",["Footnote. The Academy editor abbreviates the quoted sentence with a dash (\"Die — wird\"), i.e. the opening sentence of the preceding passage (\"The lecture ... will contain\"); I keep the abbreviation and give the German last word in brackets. A1 and A2 are the first and second printings."]],"racen-0006":["form [*Gestalt*] may be met with. Of this unity of the natural genus, which is the same thing as the unity of the generative power valid in common for it, only a single natural cause can be given: namely, that all men belong to a single stock, from which they have sprung, notwithstanding their differences, or at least could have sprung. In the former case men belong not | merely to one and the same genus, but also to one family. In the second case they are like one another, but not related, and many local creations would have to be assumed, an opinion which multiplies causes without necessity. An animal genus which at the same time has a common stock does not contain different species within itself (for these signify precisely differences of descent). Its divergences from one another are entitled deviations [*Abartungen*], when they are hereditary. The hereditary marks of descent, when they agree with the stock from which they come, are entitled *Nachartungen* [conformities to type]; | but if the deviation could no longer represent the original form of the stock, it would be entitled degeneration [*Ausartung*].",["The passage begins mid-word (\"estalt\"), read as \"Gestalt\" and supplied in brackets; silent OCR corrections to \"Abartungen\", \"Nachartungen\" and stray digits/brackets.","Doubtful reading: the OCR has \"verstellen\"; I take it as \"vorstellen\" (represent), which the sense seems to require. The Academy text should be checked.","Restated: the sentence on the two cases (single stock / local creations) is divided; the parenthesis is kept.","\"Abartung\" = deviation, \"Nachartung\" = conformity to type (a descriptive rendering, as no English term exists), \"Ausartung\" = degeneration; the German is given in brackets because the rendering is a choice."]],"racen-0007":["I",["A lone mark in the OCR (probably a section or stray numeral); translated as it stands."]],"racen-0008":["Among the deviations [*Abartungen*], that is, the hereditary differences of animals belonging to a single stock, those are entitled races [*Racen*] which both maintain themselves constantly among themselves through long generations in all transplantations (removals to other regions), and, in intermixture with other deviations of the same stock, always beget half-breed young. | Those which in all transplantations indeed constantly retain what distinguishes their deviation, and so breed true, but do not necessarily beget half-breeds when intermixed with others, are entitled sports [*Spielarten*]; those which breed true often but not constantly, varieties [*Varietäten*]. Conversely, the deviation which in intermixture with others indeed begets half-breeds, but gradually dies out through transplantation, is entitled a particular strain [*Schlag*].",["Silent OCR corrections: \"Bald- schlächtige\" to \"halbschlächtige\", \"nach arten\" to \"nacharten\", \"ftd)\" and stray digits.","The Academy line-break signs fall inside words (\"so- | wohl\"); in English I place the | at the nearest natural point, and one | only is kept where the German has two close together.","\"halbschlächtig\" = half-breed; \"nacharten\" = breed true; \"Spielart\" = sport, \"Schlag\" = strain: terminological choices, with German given."]],"racen-0009":["In this way Negroes and whites are indeed not different species of human beings (for presumably they belong to one stock), but yet",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; translated as it stands. *Neger* is rendered 'Negro', the English usage of the period, as a historical term."]],"racen-0010":["two different races; for each of them perpetuates itself in all regions, and the two, when crossed, necessarily produce half-bred children or hybrids (mulattos). On the other hand, blonds and brunettes are not different races of whites, since a blond man and a brunette woman may also have children who are all blond, although each of these deviations maintains itself through many generations under every transplantation. They are therefore varieties (*Spielarten*) of the whites. Finally, the nature of the soil (moisture or dryness), and likewise of the nourishment, gradually produces a hereditary difference or strain among [human beings] of one and the same stock and race. This shows itself chiefly in size, in the proportion of the limbs (stout or slender), and also in natural disposition. Such a strain is indeed transmitted in half measure when crossed with a foreign one, but on another soil and with other nourishment (even without any change of climate) it disappears within a few generations. It is agreeable to observe the different strains of men according to the differences in these causes, where they are recognisable in one and the same country merely by provinces (as the Boeotians, who inhabited a moist soil, were distinguished from the Athenians, who inhabited a dry one). This difference is often, to be sure, recognisable only to an attentive eye, and is laughed at by others. What belongs merely to the variations, and is therefore in itself hereditary (though not invariably so), can nevertheless, through marriages that always remain within the same families, produce in time what I call the family strain. In this, something characteristic finally becomes so deeply rooted in the generative power that it approaches a variety and perpetuates itself like one. This is said to have been observed in the old nobility of Venice, particularly in its ladies. At the least, in the newly discovered island of Otaheite the women of noble rank are, without exception, of taller stature than the common women. — The opinion of Herr von Maupertuis rested on the possibility of finally establishing a lasting family strain by careful separation of the degenerating births from those that conform to type. His idea was to breed, in some province, a naturally noble strain of men in whom understanding, capability and uprightness would be hereditary. In my opinion this project is in itself feasible, but is quite properly prevented by wiser Nature; for it is precisely in the mixture of evil with good that the great springs lie which set the slumbering powers of humanity in play and compel it to develop all its talents and to approach the perfection of its determination (*Bestimmung*). If Nature can work undisturbed (without transplantation or foreign admixture) through many generations, she always at last produces a lasting strain, which makes peoples permanently recognisable and which would be called a race, if the characteristic did not seem too insignificant and too hard to describe",["Restated: the long final sentence of the Maupertuis section is divided; the 'Ein Anschlag' clause is rendered as 'his idea' and 'this project'. Page and line numbers (300, 2) omitted as running matter.","OCR 'Bruz nette' = *Brunette*; 'Natunells' = *Naturells* ('natural disposition'); 'Anflag' = *Anschlag*; 'anartet' (printed 'an—artet') read as such.","'unter [Shier einerlei Stammes' is garbled; I read 'unter Menschen einerlei Stammes' and put [human beings] in brackets as a conjecture.","Terms chosen: Abartung = deviation; Spielart = variety (German kept once); Varietäten = variations; Schlag = strain; Blendlinge = hybrids; halbschlächtig = half-bred. The relative 'der' after *Naturells* is taken to refer to the strain or difference.","Passage breaks off mid-sentence ('zu schwer zu beschreiben'); continued in the next passage."]],"racen-0011":["would be, in order to found a special division upon it.",["OCR 'äre' = *wäre*; the fragment completes the previous sentence ('too hard to describe') and is translated as it stands."]],"racen-0012":["2. Division of the Human Species into its Different Races.",[]],"racen-0013":["I believe that it is necessary to assume only four races in order to be able to derive from them all the hereditary differences which are recognisable at first glance and which perpetuate themselves. They are: 1) the race of the whites; 2) the Negro race; 3) the Hunnish (Mungalian or Kalmuck) race; [4)] the Hindu or Hindustani race. To the first, which has its chief seat in Europe, I reckon also the Moors (the Mauretanians of Africa), the Arabs (according to Niebuhr), the Turkish-Tartar stock of peoples, and the Persians, likewise all other peoples of Asia who are not specifically excepted from it by the other divisions. The Negro race of the northern hemisphere is indigenous only in Africa; that of the southern hemisphere (outside Africa) is presumably indigenous (*autochthones*) only in New Guinea, and in some neighbouring islands consists merely of transplantations. The Kalmuck race seems to be purest among the Khoshots, somewhat mixed with Tartar blood among the Torgöts, and more so among the Ösingorians. It is the very same race which in the most ancient times bore the name of the Huns, and later the name of the Mungals (in the wide sense) [gap in the text] … insel Indiens unterschieden. From these four races I believe I can derive all the other hereditary national characters, whether as mixed or as incipient races. The first of these has arisen from the mixture of different races; the second has not yet lived long enough in the climate to take on fully the character of the race proper to it. Thus the mixture of Tartar with Hunnish blood has produced the Kara-Kalpaks, the Nogais and other half-races. Hindustani blood, mixed with that of the ancient Scythians (in and around Tibet) and more or less with the Hunnish, has perhaps produced the inhabitants of the farther peninsula of India, the Tonkinese and the Chinese, as a [wholly?] mixed race. The inhabitants of the northern icy coast of Asia are an example of an incipient Hunnish race, in which there already appear the uniformly black hair, the beardless chin, the flat face and the long-slit, scarcely opened eyes. These are the effect of the frigid zone upon a people which in later times was driven from a milder climate into these abodes; just as the Sea-Lapps, an offshoot of the Ugrian people, have within not many centuries already become fairly well adapted to what is peculiar to the cold climate, although they sprang from a well-formed people of the temperate zone. Finally, the Americans seem to be a Hunnish race not yet fully adapted. For in the extreme north-west of America (whence, by all conjecture, the peopling of this part of the world from the north-east of Asia must have taken place, on account of the agreement of the animal species in both), on the northern coasts of Hudson's Bay, the inhabitants are quite similar to the Kalmucks. Farther south the face does indeed become more open and prominent; but the beardless chin, the uniformly black hair, the reddish-brown complexion, and likewise the coldness and insensibility of disposition, all of them remnants of the effect of a long sojourn in cold regions of the world, as we shall presently see, continue from the extreme north of this continent down to Staten Island. The longer sojourn of the forefathers of the Americans in the north-east of Asia and the neighbouring north-west of America brought the Kalmuck formation to perfection; the more rapid spread of their descendants towards the south of this continent, however, brought about the American. Nothing farther has been peopled from America. For on the islands of the Pacific all the inhabitants, a few Negroes excepted, are bearded; rather, they give some signs of descent from the Malays, just like those on the Sunda Islands; and the kind of feudal government which was found on the island of Otaheite, and which is also the usual constitution of the Malays, confirms this conjecture.",["The long sentences are divided into several; the ambiguity of 'die erste … die zweite' is resolved as mixed race and incipient race respectively. The sentence on the Tonkinese and Chinese is left as in the German.","The numeral '4)' is missing in the OCR; I supply it in brackets. The text is corrupt after 'Mungalen (in weiter Bedeutung)': a gap, then '…insel Indiens unterschieden', which I leave untranslated rather than fill. The stray 'pere.' before the | is OCR debris and is dropped; the | is kept.","'zal vermischte Race' is doubtful (perhaps *ganz*); 'Libet' = Tibet, 'Seelappen' (perhaps for *Lappen*) is rendered literally, 'ngrifden' = *ugrischen*, 'Nehnsregierung' = *Lehnsregierung*, 'Zermuthung' = *Vermuthung*, 'Bewohnern' = *Bewohner*. 'Hudson's Bay' is placed by Kant in the north-west as printed.","angehende = incipient; eingeartet = adapted (*einarten*); Bildung = formation. Footnote-like numerals (254, 14, etc.) are omitted as running matter."]],"racen-0014":["The reason for assuming Negroes and whites to be original races is clear in itself. As regards the Hindustani and the Kalmuck, the | olive-yellow which underlies the more or less brown of the hot countries",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage. 'livengelb' = *Olivengelb*; the | is retained as in the source."]],"racen-0015":["is, in the case of the former, as little derivable from any other known national character as is the original face of the second; and both are unfailingly imprinted in mixed unions. The same holds of the American race, which falls in with the Kalmuck formation and is connected with it by one and the same cause. The East Indian, by mixture with the white, gives the yellow mestizo, as the American with the same gives the red, the white with the Negro the mulatto, and the American with the same [Negro] the Kabugl or black Carib. These are always recognisably marked hybrids (*Blendlinge*), and their descent from genuine races dec |",["'Restizen' = *Mestizen*; 'Kabugl' kept as printed. The bracketed [Negro] resolves 'eben demselben'; 'the red' [mestizo] is left elliptical as in the German.","The passage breaks off at 'dec' (perhaps *decken*/*verrathen*; unclear), left untranslated; the | is retained."]],"racen-0016":["[…] | 3. Of the immediate causes of the origin of these |",["The opening fragment 'iii weisen' is OCR debris and cannot be read; I have not translated it and have not filled the gap. The stray 'l' at the end is also omitted.","This is the chapter heading, which continues in the next passage ('verschiedenen Racen')."]],"racen-0017":["different races.",["Completes the heading begun in the previous passage: 'Of the immediate causes of the origin of these different races.' *Race* is rendered 'race', as Kant's own term for these hereditary varieties."]],"racen-0018":["*) We commonly take the terms description of nature [*Naturbeschreibung*] and natural history [*Naturgeschichte*] in one and the same sense. But it is clear that knowledge of natural things as they now are always leaves us still desiring knowledge of what they formerly were, and of the series of changes through which they have passed in order to arrive, in each place, at their present state. Natural history, which we almost wholly lack as yet, would teach us the alterations in the shape of the earth, and likewise those of earthly creatures (plants and animals), which they have undergone through natural migrations, together with the variations from the archetype of the parent genus [*Stammgattung*] that have resulted from them. It would presumably trace a great number of seemingly different species back to races of one and the same genus, and would transform the scholastic system of the description of nature, now so extensive, into a physical system for the understanding.",["The long second sentence is kept as one, but 'woran es uns fast noch gänzlich fehlt' is rendered as a relative clause, 'which we almost wholly lack as yet'.","The stray '2' and the final 'E' in the OCR are discarded as printing debris. The OCR 'Naturdingel' is silently read 'Naturdinge,' and the vertical strokes are treated as line breaks.","*Verstand* is rendered 'understanding'. *Abartungen* is rendered 'variations', and 'departures from the archetype' would also serve for *Urbild*."]],"racen-0019":["The grounds of a determinate unfolding [*Auswickelung*] that lie in the nature of an organic body (plant or animal) are entitled germs [*Keime*] when this unfolding concerns particular parts. If it concerns only the size, or the relation of the parts to one another, I call them natural predispositions [*natürliche Anlagen*]. In birds of the same species that are to live in different climates, there lie germs for the unfolding of a new layer of feathers if they live in a cold climate, but these are held back if they are to stay in a temperate one. Since in a cold country the grain of wheat must be better protected against moist cold than in a dry or warm one, there lies in it a predetermined capacity, or natural predisposition, gradually to produce a thicker skin. This foresight of nature, in equipping its creature by hidden inner provisions for all manner of future circumstances, so that it may maintain itself and be adapted to the diversity of climate or soil, is admirable. In the migration and transplanting of animals and plants it produces, to all appearance, new species. These are nothing but variations and races of the same genus, whose germs and natural predispositions have merely developed, occasionally and in long periods of time, in different ways.*)\n\nChance, or general mechanical laws, cannot produce such adaptations. We must therefore regard such occasional unfoldings as preformed. But even where nothing purposive shows itself, the mere power of propagating one's particular acquired character is proof enough that a special germ or natural predisposition was present in the organic creature for this purpose. For external things may well be occasioning causes, but not producing causes, of what necessarily is inherited and reproduces its type. Just as little as chance or physico-mechanical causes can produce an organic body, so little will they add anything to its generative power; that is, they will bring about nothing that propagates itself, if it is a particular form or relation of the parts.*) Air, sun and food can modify an animal body",["The first sentence of the passage is divided into two after 'Anlagen'; the clause 'die aber zurückgehalten werden' is kept as a relative clause. In the last sentence of the first paragraph, 'bringt … hervor, welche …' is divided into two sentences.","The OCR is badly damaged in places and I have restored it silently, e.g. 'Verhältniß', 'Fürsorge', 'Schicht von Federn', 'gelegentlich'. The footnote signs appear in the OCR as '“)' and I have normalised them to '*)'. The note itself is printed in the next passage, so its attachment to one sign or the other is uncertain.","'[Beweises] genug' is a conjectural reading of the OCR 'bon Beweises genug'; I have rendered 'proof enough'. *Nachartet* ('reproduces its type') is a choice.","*Auswickelung* is rendered 'unfolding' (the preformationist sense), and *Anlagen* 'predispositions', so as to keep the distinction Kant draws with *Keime*. *Vorgebildet* is 'preformed'.","The passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('Luft, Sonne und Nahrung können einen thierischen Körper'); it is continued in the next passage."]],"racen-0020":["modify in its growth, but cannot at the same time endow this alteration with a generative power capable of reproducing itself again even without this cause. Rather, what is to propagate itself must already have lain beforehand in the generative power, as predetermined to an occasional unfolding in accordance with the circumstances into which the creature may fall, and in which it is to maintain itself constantly. For nothing foreign to the animal must be able to enter into the generative power that would be capable of gradually removing the creature from its original and essential determination [*Bestimmung*], and of producing true degenerations [*Ausartungen*] that would perpetuate themselves.\n\nMan was determined for all climates and for every constitution of soil. Consequently there must have lain ready in him various germs and natural predispositions, to be either unfolded or held back as occasion required, so that he might be adapted to his place in the world, and in the course of the generations might seem, as it were, to be innate to it and made for it. In accordance with these concepts [*Begriffe*] we propose to go through the whole human species over the wide earth, and to adduce there purposive causes of its variations [*Abartungen*] where the natural causes are not readily to be seen, and natural causes, on the other hand, where we do not perceive the purposes. Here I remark only that air and sun appear to be those causes",["The passage begins mid-sentence: the subject (air, sun and food, from the end of the previous passage) is not in this passage, and the German 'seinem Wachsthume modificiren' lacks its preposition, which I have supplied silently as 'in'. The final sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage.","The long second sentence ('sondern was sich fortpflanzen soll …') is divided at 'Denn'. 'Darein … gerathen kann' is rendered by a relative clause.","The OCR is silently corrected in several places ('Zeugungskraft', 'Beschaffenheit', 'einzusehen'). *Bestimmung* is rendered 'determination'. *Ausartungen* ('degenerations') is distinguished from *Abartungen* ('variations').","*Perpetuirten* is subjunctive: 'would perpetuate themselves'."]],"racen-0021":["*) Diseases are sometimes hereditary. But these require no organisation, only a ferment of noxious humours, which propagate themselves by infection. Nor do they necessarily become ingrained [*anarten*].\n\n[…] to be, which can act most intimately upon the generative power and produce a lasting unfolding of the germs and predispositions, that is, can found a race; whereas particular nourishment can indeed produce a stock [*Schlag*] of men whose distinguishing mark, however, soon disappears on transplantation. What is to take hold of the generative power must affect not the preservation of life but the source of it, that is, the first principles of its animal constitution and movement.",["The passage opens with the footnote (sign '*)'), whose text belongs to the preceding page, and then continues the main text from the previous passage ('daß Luft und Sonne … zu sein | scheinen'). 'scheinen' is rendered 'to be', with 'appear' left to the previous passage.","The OCR 'vic arten auch nicht nothwendig an' is doubtful. I read it as 'sie arten auch nicht nothwendig an'. The sense of *anarten* ('become ingrained, are not necessarily inherited') is a conjecture.","The sentence 'Was auf die Zeugungskraft haften soll …' is kept whole. *Schlag* is rendered 'stock' (strain). The OCR 'IT' before 'Verpflanzungen' is dropped."]],"racen-0022":["Man, transplanted into the frigid zone, must gradually have degenerated [*ausarten*] into a smaller stature, because with this, if the force of the heart remains the same, the circulation of the blood takes place in a shorter time. [Text corrupt here: 'der an | | Crang'] the Greenlanders are not only far below the stature of Europeans but also of markedly greater natural heat of body. Even the disproportion between the whole height of the body and the short legs, among the most northerly peoples, is adapted to their climate, since these parts of the body, on account of their distance from the heart, are in greater danger in the cold.\n\nNevertheless, most of the present known inhabitants of the frigid zone seem to be only later arrivals there. Such are the Lapps, who, sprung from one and the same stock as the Finns, namely the Hungarian [*ungrisch*], have occupied their present seats only since the emigration of the latter (from the east of Asia), and yet are already, to a considerable degree, acclimatised to this climate.",["The OCR is corrupt in the middle of the first sentence ('der an | | Crang die Grönländer nicht allein …'). I have not filled the gap or the missing verb. 'Crang' is probably 'Cranz' (David Crantz, on Greenland), but this is only a guess and I have not used it in the translation.","'Sinnen' is silently corrected to 'Finnen'; 'Europdeß' to 'Europäer'. *Eingeartet* is rendered 'acclimatised', with the literal sense 'become of the nature of the climate'.","I have divided the text into two paragraphs at 'Gleichwohl' and rendered 'welche … entsprungen … haben … und doch … sind' as a relative clause with parallel verbs."]],"racen-0023":["But if a northern people is compelled, throughout long periods of time, to endure the influence of the cold of the frigid zone, still greater changes must befall it. All unfolding by which the body merely wastes its humours must gradually be checked in this desiccating region. Hence the germs of the growth of hair are in time suppressed, so that only those remain which are required for the necessary covering of the head. By virtue of a natural predisposition, the prominent parts of the face, which is the part least capable of being covered, since these parts suffer ceaselessly from the cold, will also, by a provision of nature, gradually become flatter, in order the better to preserve themselves. The prominence beneath the eyes and the half-closed, blinking eyes seem to have been arranged to protect them, partly against the desiccating cold of the air and partly against the glare of the snow (against which the Eskimos also use sun-",["The long sentence on the face is restated: the relative clause on the face is rendered in apposition and 'da sie durch die Kälte unaufhörlich leiden' as a causal clause. 'welches' refers to 'Gesicht'; 'sie' refers to 'Theile'.","The passage breaks off in the word 'Son…' (the German word is divided between passages); I have rendered it only as 'sun-' and have not completed it. The OCR begins the next passage with garbage and 'villen brauchen)'.","'nordliches', 'ausftehen' and 'austrod: nenden' are silently corrected."]],"racen-0024":["[…] as if by design, although they may also be regarded as natural [effects] of the climate, which are to be noticed, though only in a much smaller degree, even in milder zones. Thus there gradually arise the beardless chin, the flattened nose, thin [lips], blinking eyes, the flat face, the reddish-brown colour with black hair; in a word, the Kalmuck cast of countenance. In a long series of generations in the same climate this takes root as a lasting race, and maintains itself even if such a people afterwards gains new seats in milder zones.",["The passage begins with OCR debris ('TER - > \\ villen brauchen)'), which is the end of the parenthesis begun in the previous passage ('Son…brillen brauchen'). The debris is not translated.","'natürliche des des Klima' is corrupt; I have supplied '[effects]' ('Wirkungen' or 'Folgen' is a guess) and the doubled 'des' is read as one.","'dünne Pus' is doubtful; I read 'dünne Lippen' ('thin lips') and bracket 'lips'. 'gepletschte' is read as 'geplätschte' (flattened); 'Baare' as 'Haare'; 'klima' as 'Klima'.","*Gesichtsbildung* is rendered 'cast of countenance'; the sentence is divided at 'welche', where the German has a long relative clause."]],"racen-0025":["It will no doubt be asked by what right I can derive the Kalmuck formation, which is now found in its fullest completeness in a milder climate, from far in the north or north-east. My reason is this. Herodotus already reports, from his own times, that the Argippaeans, inhabitants of a country at the foot of high mountains, in a region which may be taken to be that of the Urals, were bald and flat-nosed, and covered their trees with white coverings (by which he presumably means felt tents). This physique is now found, in greater or lesser degree, in the north-east of Asia, but especially in the north-western part of America, which has been explored from Hudson's Bay, where, according to some recent accounts, the inhabitants look like true Kalmucks. If we now consider that in the earliest times",["OCR slips corrected silently: 'hoher', 'in einer', 'bedeutet' (OCR 'beriteht'; 'bedeutet' is conjectural), 'nordwestlichen', 'entdecken', 'wahre'.","'Bildung' rendered 'formation' and then 'physique' for 'Gestalt'; both refer to bodily make-up. The long sentence on Herodotus is kept whole but the final sentence is cut off, as in the source."]],"racen-0026":["animals and men must have passed to and fro in this region between Asia and America, since the same animals are found in the cold zone of both continents; and that this human race first showed itself to the Chinese, about 1000 years before our era (according to Deguignes), beyond the",["The passage begins mid-sentence ('[T]hiere'); it continues in the next passage, and ends mid-phrase before 'Amurstrom'.","'Desguignes' is Deguignes; the spelling is Kant's."]],"racen-0027":["Amur River, and gradually drove other peoples of Tatar, Hungarian and other stocks from their seats, then this descent from the cold zone will not appear altogether forced. But what is most important, namely the derivation of the Americans as a race not fully degenerated [*nicht völlig eingeartet*], a people which has long inhabited the most northerly region of the world, is strongly confirmed by the stunted growth of hair on all parts of the body except the head, and by the reddish iron-rust colour of the colder regions of this continent and the darker copper colour of the hotter. For the red-brown seems (as an effect of the acid of the air [*Luftsäure*]) to be as suited to the cold climate as the olive-brown (as an effect of the alkaline and bilious quality of the humours) is to the hot zone. And this leaves out of account the natural temperament of the Americans, which betrays a vital force soon extinguished,*) and this can most naturally be regarded as the effect of a cold | region of the world.",["Silently corrected: 'verdrängte', 'kalten Weltstriche', 'Haarwuchs'; stray 'M.' and 'Ii' dropped. The '*)' marks a footnote, whose text stands at the head of the next passage (racen-0030).","'Luftsäure' (fixed air, carbonic acid) rendered literally. 'nicht völlig eingeartet' is Kant's term for a race not yet fully adapted to its soil; I have kept the German in brackets.","The '|' before 'Weltgegend' is an OCR 'I' that I take to be the Academy line-break sign; doubtful."]],"racen-0028":["The greatest moist heat of the warm climate must, on the other hand, show effects, in a | people which has lived there long enough to be fully naturalised to its soil, that are directly opposed to the foregoing. The very reverse of the Kalmuck formation will be produced. The growth of the spongy parts of the body had to increase in a hot and moist climate; hence a thick snub nose and sausage lips. The skin had to be oily, not merely to moderate the excessive perspiration, but to prevent the harmful absorption of the putrid moisture of the air. The excess of iron particles, which are otherwise found in every man's blood, and which here have been precipitated in the reticular | substance by the exhalation of phosphoric acid (from which all negroes stink), causes the blackness that shows through the epidermis; and the strong iron content of the blood seems also to be necessary in order to prevent a relaxation of all the parts. The oil | of the skin, which weakens the nutritive mucus required for the growth of hair, scarcely permitted the production of wool covering the head. Moreover, moist warmth is on the whole favourable to the vigorous growth of animals; and, in short, the negro arises, well suited to his climate, that is, strong, fleshy and supple, but, under the lavish provision of his native land, lazy, soft and trifling.",["'netzförmigen' (reticular, the *rete mucosum*) is my conjecture for the OCR 'nebformigen/nebelförmigen'; doubtful reading.","OCR noise ('4', '1)', '?', trailing '2') removed. The '|' signs are retained where they stand.","'Stülpnase' and 'Wurstlippen' are translated literally; 'Nahrungsschleim' as 'nutritive mucus'; 'gelenk' as 'supple'."]],"racen-0029":["The native of Hindustan may be regarded as sprung from one of the oldest human races. His country is backed to the north by a high mountain range, and traversed from north to south, as far as the tip of its peninsula, by a long chain of mountains (to which I add, to the north, Tibet, perhaps the common place of refuge of the human | race during, and its nursery after, the last great revolution | of our earth). It has in a favourable climate",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence and continues in the next passage ('die vollkommenste Scheitelung der Wasser'). The footnote text in the source has been moved to the head of racen-0030.","'während und dessen Pflanzschule nach' is syntactically odd, probably a gap in the text or OCR; I have supplied 'during, and its nursery after' and flag the reading as doubtful. 'Hindistan' rendered 'Hindustan'."]],"racen-0030":["*) To cite only one example: in Surinam the red slaves (Americans) are used solely for domestic work, because they are too weak for field labour, for which negroes are employed. Yet there is no lack of means of compulsion here; but the natives of this continent generally lack strength [*Vermögen*] and endurance.\n\nthe most perfect parting of the waters (drainage towards two seas), which no other part of the continent of Asia lying in a favourable climate possesses. It could therefore be dry and habitable in the earliest times, when both the eastern peninsula of India and China (since in them the rivers run parallel instead of parting) must still have been uninhabited during those times of inundation. Here, then, a fixed human race could establish itself in the course of long ages. The olive-yellow of the Indian's skin, the true gipsy colour, which underlies the more or less dark brown of other more easterly peoples, is just as characteristic and as constant in descent [*Nachartung*] as the black colour of the negroes, and seems, together with the rest of his formation and his different temperament, to be as much the effect of a dry heat as the latter is of a moist. According to Mr. Ives, the common diseases of the Indians are obstructed bile-ducts and swollen livers; their innate colour, however, is as it were jaundiced, and seems to prove a continual secretion of bile which has passed into the blood, and which, being of a soapy nature, perhaps dissolves and volatilises the thickened humours and thereby cools the blood, at least in the outer parts. A self-help of nature amounting to this or to something similar, whereby through a certain organisation (the effect of which shows in the skin) whatever stimulates the circulation of the blood is continually removed, may well be the cause of the cold hands of the Indians,*) and perhaps (although it has not",["The footnote text (to the '*)' in racen-0027/0029 context) opens this passage as it does in the source; the main text then continues from racen-0029. The text breaks off mid-parenthesis.","OCR corrected: 'sich', 'Feldarbeit', 'Olivengelb' (OCR 'Oliven— geld'), 'scheint'; stray 'E' dropped. 'Scheitelung der Wasser' rendered 'parting of the waters' (watershed). 'Edward Ives' is not named in the German beyond 'Herrn Ives'."]],"racen-0031":["*) I had indeed read elsewhere that these Indians have the peculiarity of cold hands in great heat, and that this was supposed to be a fruit of their sobriety and moderation. But when I had the pleasure of speaking with the attentive and intelligent traveller Mr. Eaton, who had for some years been Dutch consul and head of their establishments at Basra, etc., on his passing through Königsberg, he informed me that when he had danced in Surat with the wife of a European consul, he was surprised to feel sweaty and cold hands in her (the custom of gloves has not yet been adopted there), and that when he expressed his surprise to others, he was told that she had had an Indian woman for her mother, and that this characteristic is hereditary among them. The same man also testified that when one sees the children of the Parsis together with those of the Indians there, the difference of the races, in the white colour of the former and the yellow-brown of the latter, strikes the eye at once; likewise that the Indians have this further distinguishing feature in their build, that their thighs are longer than the proportion usual with us.\n\nbeen observed yet) of a generally lowered blood-heat, which enables them to bear the heat of the climate without harm.",["The footnote precedes the continuation of the main text, as in the source; the continuation completes the parenthesis begun at the end of racen-0030.","OCR corrected: 'Consul' (OCR 'Conful'), 'Bassora' rendered 'Basra', 'gewöhnliche' restored from 'ge— wöhnliche'. 'Eaton' is as printed."]],"racen-0032":["We have here, then, conjectures which have at least ground enough to hold the balance against other conjectures, which find the differences within the human species so irreconcilable that they prefer on that account to assume many local creations. To say with Voltaire that God, who created the reindeer in Lapland to consume the moss of these cold regions, also created the Laplander there, to eat this reindeer, is not a bad conceit for a poet, but a poor expedient | for the philosopher, who must not abandon the chain of natural causes except where he plainly sees it attached to immediate providence. ||",["'Vers schiedenheiten' and 'deshalb?' are OCR slips, silently corrected. The '|' and '||' signs are kept where they stand.","'Verhängniß' rendered 'providence' (literally 'decree' or 'dispensation'); a choice."]],"racen-0033":["It is now, with good reason, held that the different colours of plants are to be ascribed to iron precipitated by different juices. Since all animal blood contains iron, nothing prevents us from attributing the different colour of these races of men to the very same cause. In this way | the muriatic acid, or the phosphoric acid, or the volatile alkali of the excretory vessels of the skin might precipitate the particles of iron in the reticulum as red, or black, or yellow. In the stock of the whites, however, this iron, dissolved in the juices, would not be precipitated at all; and thereby the perfect mixture of the juices, and the superior strength of this human stock over the rest, would at the same time be proved. Yet this is only a passing incitement to inquiry in a field in which I am too much a stranger to venture even conjectures with any confidence.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Baut→Haut, geld→gelb, flüd | tig→flüchtig (the | kept at the line break).","'Salzsaure' rendered 'muriatic acid' (the period term for hydrochloric acid).","The fourth sentence is divided with a semicolon, and 'the perfect mixture of the juices' is made the object of the proof more plainly than in the German."]],"racen-0034":["We have counted four human races, under which all the vari-",["The passage breaks off mid-word (Mannig⸗); the continuation is in the next passage, and I have not completed it. 'Mannigfaltigkeiten' is rendered 'varieties', split as 'vari- / eties'."]],"racen-0035":["eties of this species are to be comprehended. All departures from type [Abartungen], however, require a parent species [Stammgattung], which we must either declare to be already extinct, or else select from among those now existing the one with which we can most nearly compare the parent species. Admittedly, we cannot hope to meet with the original human form unaltered anywhere in the world now. Precisely because of this propensity of nature to degenerate in the soil everywhere through long generations, the human form must now everywhere be burdened with local modification. But the zone between the 31st and the 52nd degree of latitude in the Old World (which seems to deserve the name of 'Old World' also in respect of its population) is rightly held to be that in which the happiest | | mixture of the influences of colder and hotter regions, and also the greatest wealth of earthly creatures, is to be met with. Here also man, since from this region he is equally well prepared for all transplantings, must have departed least from his original formation. Here, however, we find inhabitants who are white, yet brunette, and this form we shall therefore assume to be the one nearest to the parent species. From this form the very fair type [die hochblonde], with delicate white skin, reddish hair and pale blue eyes, seems to be the nearest northern variation. In the time of the Romans it inhabited the northern parts of Germany and (according to other evidence)",["Begins mid-word ('ED faltigkeiten'): 'ED' is an OCR remnant and is dropped; 'faltigkeiten' completes 'Mannig-' of the previous passage.","The running head ('Von den verschiedenen Racen der Menschen. 441') and line numbers are omitted; the | on either side of it are kept.","Silent OCR corrections: zo→so, Boz den→Boden, Baut→Haut, Verpflangungen→Verpflanzungen.","The long sentence on the 'Erdstrich' is divided in two; 'Abartung' is rendered 'variation' or 'departure from type', and 'hochblonde' as 'very fair'. The passage breaks off mid-sentence."]],"racen-0036":["farther eastward to the Altaic mountains, but everywhere inhabited immeasur- | able forests in a fairly cold region. Now the influence of a cold and moist air, which brings upon the juices a",["OCR corrected: 'fallen'→'kalten', 'unermeplide'→'unermeßliche'. The sentence continues in the next passage; the subject is the fair northern type of the previous passage."]],"racen-0037":["tendency to scurvy, has finally brought a certain stock of men for-",["Broken mid-word (her- / vorgebracht): rendered 'for- / th' across passages 0037 and 0038."]],"racen-0038":["th, which would have attained the constancy of a race, if in this region foreign admixtures had not so frequently interrupted the progress of the degeneration. We can therefore reckon this stock among the actual races at",["'Schlag' rendered 'stock'; 'Abartung' as 'degeneration' in this context. The sentence runs on into 0039."]],"racen-0039":["least as an approximation, and then-",["'als- / dann' is split across passages; rendered 'then-' and completed only in the next passage's wording."]],"racen-0040":["these, together with the natural causes of their origin,",["Fragment; the verb comes in 0041."]],"racen-0041":["may be brought under the following outline. Parent species.",["The stray numeral '9' is a line number and is omitted."]],"racen-0042":["White, of brunette colour.\nFirst race: the very fair (northern Eur[ope]), from moist cold.\nSecond race: the copper-red (American), from dry cold.\nThird race: the black (Senegambia), from moist heat.\nFourth race: the olive-yellow (Indian), from dry heat.\n4. On the Occasioning Causes of the Founding of the Different Races.",["'Nordl. Cur.' is an evident OCR slip; read as 'Nordl. Eur[opa]' and shown in brackets. This is a doubtful reading.","'Indianer' is here the inhabitants of India (cf. 'Hindistan'), not American Indians."]],"racen-0043":["What, in the manifoldness of the races over the earth's surface, makes the greatest | difficulty, whatever ground of explanation one may adopt, is this: that similar tracts of land and sky do not, after all, contain the same race; that America in its hottest climate shows no East-Indian form, still less a Negro form native to the country; and that in Arabia or Persia there is no indigenous Indian olive-yellow, although these countries agree very closely with that country [India] in climate and constitution of air; and so on. As to the first of these difficulties, it can be answered intelligibly enough from the manner in which this region [America] was peopled. For once a race such as the present had been founded, through the long sojourn of its parent stock in the north-east of Asia or in the neighbouring part of America, it could not be transformed into another race by any further influences of climate. For only the original formation [Stammbildung] can degenerate into a race; but this, where it has once taken root and stifled the other germs, resists all transformation, precisely because the character of the race has once become predominant in the generative | power.",["Silent OCR corrections: 'sich) eine 511 Race'→'sich eine Race'; 'Deu- m gungskraft'→'Zeugungskraft'.","'dieses Himmelsstrichs' is resolved as America, since the 'first' difficulty concerns America; supplied in brackets, as is 'India' for 'jenem Lande'.","The last sentences are divided and the connexions made explicit; the | marks are kept at their places."]],"racen-0044":["As for the locality of the Negro race, which is peculiar to Africa* (in its greatest perfection, Senegambia), and likewise that of the Indian race, which is confined to that country (except where eastward it seems to be crossed to a half-breed): I believe that the cause of this lay in an inland sea of ancient times, which kept both Hindustan and Africa separated from other countries otherwise near. For the tract which runs from the frontier of Dauria through Mongolia, Little Bukharia, Persia, Arabia, Nubia and the Sahara to Cape Blanco, in a connexion only slightly interrupted, resembles, for the greater part, the bed of an ancient sea. The countries in this belt are what Buache calls a *Platteform* [plateau], namely high and for the most part level plains, in which the mountains found there nowhere have an extended slope, since their foot is buried under horizontally lying sand: | hence the rivers, of which there are few, have only a short course and dry up in the sand. These countries resemble the basins of ancient seas, because they are surrounded by heights, lie water-level in their interior when considered as a whole, and therefore neither take in nor let out a stream; and moreover they are for the most part covered with sand, the sediment of an ancient, tranquil sea. From this it now becomes compre-",["Silent OCR corrections: bez trachtet→betrachtet; stray 'a' and line number '15)' dropped. The asterisk marks the footnote that follows.","'Mungalei' rendered 'Mongolia'; 'Platteform' left in Buache's term, with 'plateau' in brackets as gloss; 'Wasserpaß halten' rendered 'lie water-level'.","The sentence on the plateaux is divided at 'They resemble'; 'These countries' is supplied for the pronoun 'Sie'. The passage breaks off mid-word (begreif-)."]],"racen-0045":["*) In the hot southern zone of the world there is also a small stock of Negroes, which has spread to the neighbouring islands. Of these, on account of their mixture with people of Indian half-blood, one is almost inclined to believe that they are not native to these regions, but were brought there gradually in ancient times, through an intercourse in which the Malays stood with Africa.",["Footnote to 'Afrika' in the preceding passage. OCR slips ('Negers', 'sa', 'm', stray '2') silently corrected or dropped; 'Negers' read as 'Negern'."]],"racen-0046":["hensible how the Indian character could not take root in Persia and Arabia, which at that time still served as the basin of a sea, when Hindustan was presumably long populated; and likewise how the Negro race, as well as the Indian, could long maintain itself unmixed with northern blood, because it was cut off from this by that same sea. Natural description (the state of nature in the present time) is far from sufficient to give the ground of the manifoldness of the variations. However much one may be, and rightly so, an enemy of the audacity of opinions, one must venture a history of nature, which is a separate science, and one which might well advance by degrees from opinions to insights.",["Begins mid-word ('lich', completing 'begreif-' of 0044): rendered 'hensible'. The running head 'Von den verschiedenen Racen der Menschen. 443' is omitted.","'Baffin' read as 'Bassin' (basin). 'Naturbeschreibung' and 'Geschichte der Natur' rendered 'natural description' and 'history of nature', as the contrast requires. 'Abartungen' is 'variations'."]],"racen-0047":["1) The words 'Die — worden', which in A¹ follow after the treatise, are missing in A².",["Editorial footnote of the Academy Edition; 'A!' and 'A*' read as A¹ and A² (OCR slips). The words 'Die — worden' refer to a text not given here and are left as they stand."]],"racen-0048":["The physical geography which I hereby announce belongs to an idea that I have formed of a useful academic instruction, which I may call the preliminary exercise in the knowledge of the world. It is this knowledge of the world that serves to supply the pragmatic element to all the sciences and skills otherwise acquired. Through it they become serviceable not merely for the school but for life, and through it the pupil who has completed his training is introduced to the stage of his vocation, that is, to the world. A twofold field lies before him here, of which he needs a preliminary outline if he is to arrange all his future experiences in it according to rules. These are nature and man. Both must here be considered cosmologically. That is to say, they are to be considered not in terms of what their objects, taken singly, contain that is noteworthy (physics and empirical psychology), but in terms of what their relation as a whole, in which they stand and in which each of us himself occupies his place, gives us to observe. The first of these instructions I entitle physical geography, and I have set it down as the summer lecture. The second, which I reserve for the winter, is anthropology. The remaining lectures of this half-year have already been duly announced in public.\"",["Restated the long second sentence as two sentences, and the \"nämlich … Natur und der Mensch\" sentence as three, to keep the construction clear.","*das Pragmatische*: rendered \"the pragmatic element\"; [element] is supplied. *Lehrling* is rendered \"pupil\" as the context requires.","*Gegenstände* = objects. *Verhältniß im Ganzen* = \"relation as a whole\". *kosmologisch* is kept as \"cosmologically\".","The closing quotation mark is in the source (the passage is a quoted announcement); OCR hyphenation \"Gegen— stände\" silently repaired."]],"philanthropin-0001":["Essays concerning the Philanthropin.",["OCR \"Aussätze\" and \"Bhilanthropin\" corrected to *Aufsätze* and *Philanthropin*."]],"philanthropin-0002":["| Dessau, 1776. First Number of the Philanthropic Archive, communicated by con-",["The sentence breaks off at \"ver-\"; it continues in the next passage (*verbrüderten*), so the word is left divided as in the source."]],"philanthropin-0003":["federated friends of youth to the guardians of mankind, especially | those who are undertaking a reform of schools, and to fathers and mothers",["OCR \"Schulverbefjerung\" corrected to *Schulverbesserung*.","The fragment continues in the next passage and is left unfinished."]],"philanthropin-0004":["who wish to send children to the Philanthropin at Dessau.",[]],"philanthropin-0005":["Never, surely, has a juster demand been made upon the human race, and never has so great and self-extending a benefit been offered to it so disinterestedly, as is here done by Herr Basedow. Together with his praiseworthy colleagues he has solemnly devoted himself to the welfare and improvement of mankind. For centuries minds good and bad have brooded over this matter. Without the fiery and constant zeal of one single man of insight and vigour it would have remained for as many centuries more in the womb of pious wishes. It is a genuine institution of education, suited alike to nature and to all civil ends. It now stands actually before us, with effects as swift as they were unexpected, and it calls for the assistance of others, only that it may be enlarged just as it now is, scatter its seed over other lands, and perpetuate its kind. For what is merely the development of the natural predispositions lying in humanity has this property in common with universal Mother Nature: it does not let its seeds go to waste, but multiplies itself and preserves its kind. Every commonwealth and every individual citizen of the world has an infinite interest in becoming acquainted with an institution through which an entirely new order of human affairs begins. (One may be informed of it in this Archive and in Basedow's work *Für Kosmopoliten Etwas zu lesen*, etc.) If it is rapidly extended, this institution must bring about a reform, in private life as well as in civil society, so great and | so far-reaching that one would not easily imagine it at a cursory glance. For this reason it is also the proper vocation of every friend of mankind to tend and protect, with all possible care, this still tender shoot, or at least to commend it unceasingly to the protection of those who unite with good will the power to do good. For once it has attained full growth, as its fortunate beginning gives ground to hope, its fruits will soon spread into all lands and to the remotest posterity. The 13th of May is an important day in this regard. On that day the man, confident of his cause, invites the most learned and discerning men of neighbouring towns and universities to see for themselves what they could scarcely be induced to believe on mere report. The good has an irresistible force when it is intuited [*angeschauet*]. The voice of deserving and accredited deputies of mankind (of whom we wish a good number at this congress) must necessarily rouse the attention of Europe to what so closely concerns it, and move it to active participation in so public-spirited an institution. Already it must be to every friend of mankind a source of the greatest pleasure, and of no less attractive hope that so noble an example will be followed, that (as was reported in the latest newspaper) the Philanthropin has been secured in its continuance by a considerable subsidy from high quarters. In such circumstances there is also no doubt that boarders will hasten from all sorts of regions to secure for themselves in this institution the places which may soon run short. But what lies closest to the heart of those who earnestly desire a rapid spread of the good is the sending of capable candidates to Dessau, to be instructed and practised in the philanthropic method of education. This is the only means of soon having good schools everywhere, and it seems to demand, above all, prompt attention and the generous support of wealthy patrons. In the expectation that this wish will soon be fulfilled, it is very much to be recommended to all teachers, in private as in public instruction, to make use of Basedow's writings and of the school-books published by him: the former for their own instruction, the latter for the exercise of the youth entrusted to them. Thereby they can make their instruction philanthropic even now, so far as can be done in the meantime. Price at the Kanter bookshop, 15 groschen.",["Many very long sentences were divided into shorter ones, per the method; the parenthesis on Basedow's work is kept in place, and the page break is marked by | where the source shows it.","Running head \"Aufsätze, das Philanthropin betreffend\" and line numbers omitted; OCR slips (\"Gorge… fält\" = *Sorgfalt*, \"Lag\" = *Tag*, \"Penstonisten\" = *Pensionisten*, \"ftd)\" = *sich*, \"(8\" = *Es*) silently corrected. The source's other | marks beside \"Sorgfalt\" and \"Nachkommenschaft\" fall in mid-word breaks and are not reproduced, as the English has no corresponding break.","*Das Gute… wenn es angeschauet wird*: *anschauen* is rendered as \"intuited\" with the German, since Kant's term *Anschauung* is in play, though here the sense is plainly \"beheld\".","*Wesen* in *gemeines Wesen* = \"commonwealth\"; *bürgerliches Wesen* = \"civil society\". \"[the former]/[the latter]\" resolves *der letzteren* by the sense (school-books). The final sentence runs on in the source into the price note without punctuation; I have added a full stop."]],"philanthropin-0006":["2\nTo the Commonwealth.",["*Das gemeine Wesen* rendered \"the Commonwealth\"; the numeral 2 is the section number in the source."]],"philanthropin-0007":["In the civilised countries of Europe there is no lack of institutions of education, nor of well-meant diligence on the part of teachers to be of service to everyone in this matter. Nevertheless it has now been clearly proved that all of them are corrupt in their first design. Because everything in them works against nature, they by no means bring out of man the good for which nature gave the predisposition. And because we, as animal creatures, are made into human beings only through cultivation, we should soon see quite different human beings around us if that method of education became generally current which is wisely drawn from nature itself, and not slavishly imitated from the old custom of earlier and inexperienced ages.",["Divided the opening sentence into two; the three *daß*-clauses are kept in the English as one sentence, as a single continuous argument.","*Ausbildung* = \"cultivation\"; *Anlage* = \"predisposition\"."]],"philanthropin-0008":["But it is vain to expect this salvation of the human race from a gradual improvement of the schools. They must be created anew if anything good is to arise from them, because they are faulty in their original constitution, and the teachers themselves must undergo a new training. Not a slow reform but a swift revolution can bring this about. And nothing more is required for it than a single school, newly organised from the foundation according to the genuine method, and worked by enlightened men with a zeal that is not mercenary but noble-minded. During its progress toward perfection it must be observed and judged by the attentive eye of connoisseurs in all countries, and it must also be supported and helped forward, until it attains completeness, by the united contribution of all friends of mankind. Such a school is a seed-corn, not merely for those whom it educates but, what is infinitely more important, through those to whom it gives the opportunity of training themselves, gradually and in large numbers, as teachers according to the true method of education. By its careful tending there can grow up in a short time a multitude of well-instructed teachers, who will soon cover a whole country with good schools.",["*Sie müssen umgeschaffen werden*: \"they\" (the schools, *Schulen*) is taken from the preceding sentence's *Schulverbesserung*; the plural noun is not in the passage itself, so the antecedent is resolved by judgment.","The long final sentence is divided and the construction \"nicht bloß für die, … sondern … durch diejenige\" is smoothed; the page break is not marked (the source shows only a running foot and line number there, which are omitted).","\"Heil\" restored for OCR \"Hell\"; *Saamkorn* rendered \"seed-corn\"; a stray \"s\" (line number) dropped."]],"philanthropin-0009":["The efforts of the commonwealth in every country ought, then, to be directed first of all to lending a helping hand to such a model school from every quarter, so that it may soon be brought to the full perfection for which it already contains the sources within itself. For to wish to imitate its organisation and plan at once in other countries, and meanwhile to hold back in its progress toward perfection, amid want and hindrances, the very school which is to become the first complete example and nursery of good education, amounts to this: sowing the seed before it is ripe, in order afterwards to reap weeds.",["OCR 'ftd)' read as 'sich'; stray 's' after 'nun' (line-number residue) dropped.","'gemeinen Wesens' rendered 'commonwealth'; 'Pflanzschule' as 'nursery' (a place where plants are raised).","The long sentence beginning 'Denn ihre Einrichtung...' is kept as one sentence, with the dash-like 'das heißt so viel' rendered 'amounts to this'."]],"philanthropin-0010":["Such an educational institution is now no longer a mere beautiful idea, but shows itself in active and visible proofs of the practicability of what has long been wished for. It is assuredly a phenomenon [*Erscheinung*] of our time which, though overlooked by vulgar eyes, must be far more important to every intelligent spectator who takes a share in the welfare of mankind than the glittering nothing upon the ever-changing stage of the great world, by which the best interest of the human race, if it is not set back, is yet not advanced by so much as a hair's breadth.",["OCR 'Gewis}' read 'Gewiß'; 'überfechen' read 'übersehen'; 'Baar' read 'Paar'; stray '1s' dropped.","The German repeats 'Beweisen ... Beweisen'; I have rendered the phrase once ('active and visible proofs'), smoothing the repetition, which looks like an OCR/setting doubling of 'sichtbaren Beweisen'.","'Erscheinung' here is the ordinary sense (phenomenon, event), not Kant's technical 'appearance'."]],"philanthropin-0011":["Public repute, and above all the united voices of conscientious and discerning judges from various countries, will already have made the readers of this newspaper acquainted with the Dessau Educational Institute (Philanthropin) as the one institution that bears these marks of excellence. Not the least of them is that, by its very constitution, it must of itself and naturally shed whatever faults may still cling to it at the outset. The attacks that here and there stir against it, and at times pamphlets of abuse (one of which, namely that of Mangelsdorf, has recently been answered by Herr Basedow with the peculiar dignity of uprightness), are such customary devices of the censorious temper, and of old custom defending itself on its own dunghill, that a calm indifference ought rather to arouse some suspicion as to the mediocrity of this rising good, in people of this kind who always cast malicious glances at whatever announces itself as good and noble. | An opportunity is now offered of rendering some assistance (which singly can be only small, but through numbers may become important) to this Institute, which is dedicated to mankind and therefore to the sympathy of every citizen of the world. If one were to strain one's inventiveness to devise an opportunity in which, by a slight contribution, the greatest possible, most lasting and most universal good might be promoted, it would still have to be that in which the seed of the good itself can be tended and maintained, so that in time it may spread and perpetuate itself.",["Heavy OCR damage: 'Herrn Dase— dow' read 'Basedow'; 'Theilfeeb' read 'Theilnehmung' (conjectural: 'sympathy'/participation); doubtful.","The clause 'daß eine ruhige Gleichgültigkeit ... Verdacht ... erregen müßte' is tangled: the 'calm indifference' is, I take it, that of the Institute's opponents' targets, i.e. that indifference toward such people should rather arouse suspicion — I have kept the German's sense but it is awkward; reading uncertain.","Printed interjection 'D. größtmögliche, dauerhafteste und allgemeine Gute befördert werden' is displaced OCR text from the lines of the passage; I restored it into 'the greatest possible ... good might be promoted' after 'wo durch einen geringen Beitrag ... könnte'.","The sign | marks a page/line break in the Academy edition and is retained."]],"philanthropin-0012":["In accordance with these conceptions, and with the good opinion we hold of the number of well-thinking persons in our commonwealth, we refer to the 21st issue of this learned and political newspaper together with its supplement, and look forward to a numerous subscription: from all gentlemen of the clerical and scholastic professions, from parents generally, to whom whatever serves the better formation of their children cannot be a matter of indifference, and even from those who, though they have no children, themselves once enjoyed education as children, and for that very reason will recognise the obligation to contribute their share, if not to the increase, at least to the cultivation, of mankind.",["'Beitung' read 'Zeitung' (OCR); 'Pränumeration' rendered 'subscription' (payment in advance).","'Bildung' rendered 'formation' / 'cultivation' to preserve the play on 'Vermehrung ... Bildung'."]],"philanthropin-0013":["Subscriptions are now received for this monthly periodical, to be issued by the Dessau Educational Institute under the title *Pädagogische Unterhandlungen*, at 2 Rthlr. 10 gr. of our money. But since, because the number of sheets cannot yet be determined, some additional payment might be required at the end of the year, it would perhaps be best (though this is left to everyone's discretion) to devote a ducat, by way of subscription, to the promotion of this work; whereupon the surplus is to be duly repaid to anyone who may desire it. For the said Institute cherishes the hope that there are in all countries many noble-minded persons who would willingly seize such an opportunity of adding, on this occasion, to the sum subscribed a small voluntary gift, as a contribution to the support of an Institute which is near to its perfection but has not been helped forward in time by the assistance expected. For since, as Herr Oberkonsistorialrat Büsching says (*wöchentl. Nachr.*, year 1776, issue 16), the governments of the present time seem to have no money for the improvement of schools, it will in the end, unless such improvement is to remain altogether undone, depend upon persons of means to promote this important universal concern themselves by a generous contribution.",["'O. C. R.' expanded as Oberkonsistorialrat (conjectural expansion of the abbreviation); 'auge— nommen' read 'angenommen'; 'si' read 'sich'; 'fol.' read 'soll'.","Rthlr. (Reichstaler), gr. (Groschen) and ducat left as in the original.","Abbreviated periodical title *wöchentl. Nachr.* (Büsching's weekly reports) left as given."]],"philanthropin-0014":["Subscriptions in this place are taken at Professor Kant's in the forenoon hours from 10 until about 1 in the afternoon, and at the Kanter bookshop at any time, against a subscription receipt.",["OCR 'Kanterschen wah. handlung' read 'Kanterschen Buchhandlung' (conjectural; 'wah.' is corrupt); 'Nhr' read 'Uhr'; 'Vor mittagsstunden' joined.","'abgegeben' is taken in the sense of 'accepted/received' (the sense required by the subscription), literally 'handed in'."]]};
